window.QBANK=[{"id": "PCON-01", "sub": "pollution-control", "subname": "Principles of pollution control (CPA, CGM, new technologies)", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.1", "pages": "243, 303-304", "url": "subtopic/pollution-control.html#PCON-01", "snippet": "A nuclear reprocessing plant on the coast discharges low-level liquid radioactive waste into the sea through a pipeline.", "text": "a nuclear reprocessing plant on the coast discharges low-level liquid radioactive waste into the sea through a pipeline. explain what is meant by critical pathway analysis (cpa). give three factors that would be considered in a cpa for this discharge. describe how critical group monitoring (cgm) would be used to assess the risk to the public from this discharge. suggest two types of environmental sample that would be collected to check the cpa predictions. predicting the route / movement a pollutant will take through the environment (based on its properties and features of the environment) to assess where it will become concentrated / which locations and organisms are most at risk, so monitoring can focus on those sites sea current velocity and direction properties of the radionuclides: half-life, solubility, tendency to adsorb onto sediments / bioaccumulate food chain pathways, eg uptake by seaweed, shellfish, fish eaten by people geology / hydrology of the coast; sediment deposition areas (mud flats) human activities in the area: fishing, seaweed collection, beach use identify the group of people whose lifestyle gives them the highest exposure (eg people who eat a lot of locally caught shellfish / fish, live near the mud flats, collect seaweed) monitor their exposure / the concentration of radioactive material in the foods they eat or their body if their exposure / risk is acceptably low, everyone else is assumed to be at even lower risk; if not, emissions are controlled cgm assesses exposure before health effects occur (not used to detect damage) seawater sediment / mud seaweed shellfish / molluscs / fish milk, vegetables or meat from nearby farms atmospheric dust soil", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PCON-01\" data-qid=\"PCON-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pollution-control.html\">Principles of pollution control (CPA, CGM, new technologies)</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.1 &middot; Genn pp. 243, 303-304 &middot; PCON-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A nuclear reprocessing plant on the coast discharges low-level liquid radioactive waste into the sea through a pipeline.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Explain what is meant by <b>Critical Pathway Analysis</b> (CPA).\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Give <b>three</b> factors that would be considered in a CPA for this discharge.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Describe how <b>Critical Group Monitoring</b> (CGM) would be used to assess the risk to the public from this discharge.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Suggest <b>two</b> types of environmental sample that would be collected to check the CPA predictions.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Predicting the route / movement a pollutant will take through the environment (based on its properties and features of the environment)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>To assess where it will become concentrated / which locations and organisms are most at risk, so monitoring can focus on those sites</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sea current velocity and direction</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Properties of the radionuclides: half-life, solubility, tendency to adsorb onto sediments / bioaccumulate</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Food chain pathways, eg uptake by seaweed, shellfish, fish eaten by people</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Geology / hydrology of the coast; sediment deposition areas (mud flats)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Human activities in the area: fishing, seaweed collection, beach use</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Identify the group of people whose lifestyle gives them the highest exposure (eg people who eat a lot of locally caught shellfish / fish, live near the mud flats, collect seaweed)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Monitor their exposure / the concentration of radioactive material in the foods they eat or their body</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>If their exposure / risk is acceptably low, everyone else is assumed to be at even lower risk; if not, emissions are controlled</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>CGM assesses exposure before health effects occur (not used to detect damage)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Seawater</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sediment / mud</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Seaweed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Shellfish / molluscs / fish</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Milk, vegetables or meat from nearby farms</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Atmospheric dust</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Soil</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 243, 303-304; AQA spec 3.4.3.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PCON-02", "sub": "pollution-control", "subname": "Principles of pollution control (CPA, CGM, new technologies)", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.1", "pages": "243-245", "url": "subtopic/pollution-control.html#PCON-02", "snippet": "Explain how the control of emission location and emission timing can reduce the severity of pollution. Use examples.", "text": " explain how the control of emission location and emission timing can reduce the severity of pollution. use examples. explain the polluter pays principle and suggest why it encourages pollution prevention. explain the precautionary principle as applied to the release of a new chemical. location: discharge into the sea / large water bodies where currents dilute and disperse the pollutant location: chimneys / emissions sited downwind of urban areas so pollutants are not carried over populations; avoiding valleys and enclosed water bodies where pollutants concentrate location: not discharging wastes onto permeable rock above an aquifer / not siting landfill on aquifers timing: restrict polluting activities (industrial combustion, vehicle use) during temperature inversions when dispersal is poor timing: discharge liquid effluent when river flow is high / on an ebb tide so it is diluted and carried away timing: pesticide spraying at night / on still days; noisy operations restricted to daytime; night-flight restrictions the organisation / person that causes the pollution is responsible for (paying for) the damage caused if the cost of preventing the pollution is less than the cost of the damage / fines, there is a financial incentive to prevent it; works best when penalties are high and escape is unlikely it is assumed that the waste will cause pollution / harm until research shows it is unlikely to so release is not permitted until it has been tested; lack of knowledge of a problem is not an excuse / does not reduce responsibility", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PCON-02\" data-qid=\"PCON-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pollution-control.html\">Principles of pollution control (CPA, CGM, new technologies)</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.1 &middot; Genn pp. 243-245 &middot; PCON-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain how the control of emission <b>location</b> and emission <b>timing</b> can reduce the severity of pollution. Use examples.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain the <b>polluter pays</b> principle and suggest why it encourages pollution prevention.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain the <b>precautionary principle</b> as applied to the release of a new chemical.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Location: discharge into the sea / large water bodies where currents dilute and disperse the pollutant</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Location: chimneys / emissions sited downwind of urban areas so pollutants are not carried over populations; avoiding valleys and enclosed water bodies where pollutants concentrate</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Location: not discharging wastes onto permeable rock above an aquifer / not siting landfill on aquifers</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Timing: restrict polluting activities (industrial combustion, vehicle use) during temperature inversions when dispersal is poor</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Timing: discharge liquid effluent when river flow is high / on an ebb tide so it is diluted and carried away</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Timing: pesticide spraying at night / on still days; noisy operations restricted to daytime; night-flight restrictions</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The organisation / person that causes the pollution is responsible for (paying for) the damage caused</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>If the cost of preventing the pollution is less than the cost of the damage / fines, there is a financial incentive to prevent it; works best when penalties are high and escape is unlikely</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It is assumed that the waste will cause pollution / harm until research shows it is unlikely to</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So release is not permitted until it has been tested; lack of knowledge of a problem is not an excuse / does not reduce responsibility</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 243-245; AQA spec 3.4.3.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PCON-03", "sub": "pollution-control", "subname": "Principles of pollution control (CPA, CGM, new technologies)", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.4.3.1", "pages": "244-245", "url": "subtopic/pollution-control.html#PCON-03", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the relationship between the cost of pollution control equipment at a factory and the percentage of a pollutant emission that is prevented.", "text": "figure 1 shows the relationship between the cost of pollution control equipment at a factory and the percentage of a pollutant emission that is prevented. describe the relationship shown in figure 1. use figure 1 to calculate the extra cost of increasing the pollution prevented from 90% to 99%. explain what is meant by the terms alara and batneec and how they would be applied by the factory. suggest why the control methods used originally by a factory may no longer be sufficient after several years. cost increases as the percentage prevented increases the relationship is not linear: cost rises slowly at first then very steeply / exponentially above about 80-90% 16.0 - 5.0 = 11.0 (£ million) alara: emissions should be as low as reasonably achievable it is rarely practical / affordable to reduce emissions to zero; low-level emissions may have acceptable impacts so an acceptable emission level is set batneec: best available technology not entailing excessive cost - choosing the most effective control equipment whose cost is not excessive the factory would install equipment that gives a high level of control (eg 80-90%) rather than paying £16 million for 99% industry / city may have expanded so there are more pollutant sources and total emissions have risen better understanding of the health / environmental effects so stricter standards are set improved technology becomes available (batneec changes) / cost of better control falls pollutant may have accumulated (persistent) so background levels are higher", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PCON-03\" data-qid=\"PCON-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pollution-control.html\">Principles of pollution control (CPA, CGM, new technologies)</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.1 &middot; Genn pp. 244-245 &middot; PCON-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the relationship between the cost of pollution control equipment at a factory and the percentage of a pollutant emission that is prevented.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,270.0 157.0,264.3 253.9,257.2 350.9,247.3 447.9,227.4 496.4,199.0 520.6,156.4 540.0,42.7\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"157.0\" cy=\"264.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"253.9\" cy=\"257.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"350.9\" cy=\"247.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"447.9\" cy=\"227.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"496.4\" cy=\"199.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"520.6\" cy=\"156.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"42.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">19.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">3.52</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">39.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">7.04</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">59.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">10.6</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">79.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">14.1</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">99</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">17.6</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Pollution prevented / %</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Cost / &amp;pound; million</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Describe the relationship shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Use Figure 1 to calculate the extra cost of increasing the pollution prevented from 90% to 99%.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain what is meant by the terms <b>ALARA</b> and <b>BATNEEC</b> and how they would be applied by the factory.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Suggest why the control methods used originally by a factory may no longer be sufficient after several years.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cost increases as the percentage prevented increases</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The relationship is not linear: cost rises slowly at first then very steeply / exponentially above about 80-90%</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>16.0 - 5.0 = 11.0 (&pound; million)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>ALARA: emissions should be As Low As Reasonably Achievable</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It is rarely practical / affordable to reduce emissions to zero; low-level emissions may have acceptable impacts so an acceptable emission level is set</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>BATNEEC: Best Available Technology Not Entailing Excessive Cost - choosing the most effective control equipment whose cost is not excessive</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The factory would install equipment that gives a high level of control (eg 80-90%) rather than paying &pound;16 million for 99%</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Industry / city may have expanded so there are more pollutant sources and total emissions have risen</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Better understanding of the health / environmental effects so stricter standards are set</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Improved technology becomes available (BATNEEC changes) / cost of better control falls</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pollutant may have accumulated (persistent) so background levels are higher</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 244-245; AQA spec 3.4.3.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PCON-04", "sub": "pollution-control", "subname": "Principles of pollution control (CPA, CGM, new technologies)", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.4.3.1, 3.4.3.3", "pages": "244, 303-304", "url": "subtopic/pollution-control.html#PCON-04", "snippet": "Table 1 shows four general approaches to pollution control.", "text": "table 1 shows four general approaches to pollution control. complete table 1 by giving one example of each approach. explain why prevention of production is generally preferable to post-release remediation. describe how each of the following new technologies helps to control pollution. production prevention: desulfurisation of fuels before combustion; low-temperature (fluidised bed) combustion to prevent nox; smokeless fuels prevention of release: electrostatic precipitators / bag filters / cyclones for smoke; catalytic converters; flue gas desulfurisation; bund walls; landfill liners post-release remediation: oil spill clean-up (booms, skimmers, dispersants, bioremediation); phytoremediation of metal-contaminated land; liming of acidified lakes alternative processes: electric vehicles instead of petrol / diesel; renewable energy instead of fossil fuels; pyrethroids instead of organochlorines; fuel cells instead of internal combustion engines remediation is often incomplete / cannot recover all the pollutant (eg oil spills, dispersed pollutants), so some damage still occurs prevention avoids the damage entirely and is usually cheaper than clean-up / avoids polluter-pays costs; moving from post-production treatment to non-release is a circular economy principle satellites detect and track the extent / movement of oil slicks over large areas, quickly and repeatedly, so clean-up can be directed and polluters identified gps tracking monitors ship positions so navigation errors / collisions / groundings are reduced and offshore shipping routes are enforced polymers with a high surface area adsorb metal ions from contaminated water / effluent so they can be removed and recovered plants that absorb heavy metals (hyperaccumulators) are grown on contaminated land, harvested and disposed of / metals recovered, cleaning the soil", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PCON-04\" data-qid=\"PCON-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pollution-control.html\">Principles of pollution control (CPA, CGM, new technologies)</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.1, 3.4.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 244, 303-304 &middot; PCON-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows four general approaches to pollution control.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Complete Table 1 by giving one example of each approach.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Production prevention</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Prevention of release</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Post-release remediation</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Alternative processes</td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain why prevention of production is generally preferable to post-release remediation.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Describe how each of the following new technologies helps to control pollution.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Production prevention: desulfurisation of fuels before combustion; low-temperature (fluidised bed) combustion to prevent NOx; smokeless fuels</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Prevention of release: electrostatic precipitators / bag filters / cyclones for smoke; catalytic converters; flue gas desulfurisation; bund walls; landfill liners</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Post-release remediation: oil spill clean-up (booms, skimmers, dispersants, bioremediation); phytoremediation of metal-contaminated land; liming of acidified lakes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Alternative processes: electric vehicles instead of petrol / diesel; renewable energy instead of fossil fuels; pyrethroids instead of organochlorines; fuel cells instead of internal combustion engines</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Remediation is often incomplete / cannot recover all the pollutant (eg oil spills, dispersed pollutants), so some damage still occurs</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Prevention avoids the damage entirely and is usually cheaper than clean-up / avoids polluter-pays costs; moving from post-production treatment to non-release is a circular economy principle</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Satellites detect and track the extent / movement of oil slicks over large areas, quickly and repeatedly, so clean-up can be directed and polluters identified</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>GPS tracking monitors ship positions so navigation errors / collisions / groundings are reduced and offshore shipping routes are enforced</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Polymers with a high surface area adsorb metal ions from contaminated water / effluent so they can be removed and recovered</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Plants that absorb heavy metals (hyperaccumulators) are grown on contaminated land, harvested and disposed of / metals recovered, cleaning the soil</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 244, 303-304; AQA spec 3.4.3.1, 3.4.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PCON-05", "sub": "pollution-control", "subname": "Principles of pollution control (CPA, CGM, new technologies)", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.4.3.1", "pages": "243-245", "url": "subtopic/pollution-control.html#PCON-05", "snippet": "Which of the following best describes the purpose of Critical Group Monitoring? Tick one box.", "text": " which of the following best describes the purpose of critical group monitoring? tick one box. explain why cpa can reduce the cost of an environmental monitoring programme. a new chemical has properties similar to pcbs (persistent, liposoluble chlorinated organic compounds). explain how knowledge of pcbs helps decisions about the new chemical. c monitoring can be focused on the sites predicted to be most at risk / where the pollutant will concentrate no need to sample sites that were never likely to be polluted; sampling also checks the accuracy of the predictions behaviour of new chemicals can be predicted from similar existing chemicals: it is likely to persist, bioaccumulate and biomagnify so acceptable emission limits, control methods and monitoring programmes can be set before release (precautionary principle)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PCON-05\" data-qid=\"PCON-05\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pollution-control.html\">Principles of pollution control (CPA, CGM, new technologies)</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.1 &middot; Genn pp. 243-245 &middot; PCON-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Which of the following best describes the purpose of Critical Group Monitoring? Tick one box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; To detect health damage in the population most affected by a pollutant</li><li>B&nbsp; To predict the route a pollutant will take through the environment</li><li>C&nbsp; To assess exposure of the people most at risk so that everyone else can be assumed to be safe</li><li>D&nbsp; To measure the concentration of a pollutant at the point of release</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain why CPA can reduce the cost of an environmental monitoring programme.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>A new chemical has properties similar to PCBs (persistent, liposoluble chlorinated organic compounds). Explain how knowledge of PCBs helps decisions about the new chemical.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b></b>&nbsp; C</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Monitoring can be focused on the sites predicted to be most at risk / where the pollutant will concentrate</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No need to sample sites that were never likely to be polluted; sampling also checks the accuracy of the predictions</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Behaviour of new chemicals can be predicted from similar existing chemicals: it is likely to persist, bioaccumulate and biomagnify</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So acceptable emission limits, control methods and monitoring programmes can be set before release (precautionary principle)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 243-245; AQA spec 3.4.3.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PCON-06", "sub": "pollution-control", "subname": "Principles of pollution control (CPA, CGM, new technologies)", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.4.3.1", "pages": "243-244", "url": "subtopic/pollution-control.html#PCON-06", "snippet": "Which of the following best describes the precautionary principle? Tick one box.", "text": " which of the following best describes the precautionary principle? tick one box. explain why the polluter pays principle works best when penalties are high and enforcement is reliable. give two choices about emission location that reduce the severity of pollution. a waste is assumed to cause pollution until research shows it is unlikely to do so if the cost of preventing damage is less than the (penalty for) damage there is a financial incentive to prevent pollution if polluters can escape their responsibilities / penalties are low, there is little incentive to comply marine discharges where currents dilute and disperse emissions emissions downwind of urban areas not discharging waste onto permeable rock above an aquifer", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PCON-06\" data-qid=\"PCON-06\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pollution-control.html\">Principles of pollution control (CPA, CGM, new technologies)</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.1 &middot; Genn pp. 243-244 &middot; PCON-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Which of the following best describes the <b>precautionary principle</b>? Tick <b>one</b> box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; A waste is assumed to cause pollution until research shows it is unlikely to do so</li><li>B&nbsp; The polluter must pay for any damage caused</li><li>C&nbsp; Emissions should be as low as reasonably achievable</li><li>D&nbsp; Only pollutants known to be toxic need to be controlled</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain why the polluter pays principle works best when penalties are high and enforcement is reliable.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Give <b>two</b> choices about emission location that reduce the severity of pollution.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b>A</b>&nbsp; A waste is assumed to cause pollution until research shows it is unlikely to do so</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>If the cost of preventing damage is less than the (penalty for) damage there is a financial incentive to prevent pollution</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>If polluters can escape their responsibilities / penalties are low, there is little incentive to comply</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Marine discharges where currents dilute and disperse emissions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Emissions downwind of urban areas</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Not discharging waste onto permeable rock above an aquifer</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 243-244; AQA spec 3.4.3.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PCON-07", "sub": "pollution-control", "subname": "Principles of pollution control (CPA, CGM, new technologies)", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.1, 3.4.3.3", "pages": "243-245, 303-304", "url": "subtopic/pollution-control.html#PCON-07", "snippet": "A nuclear fuel reprocessing plant discharges small amounts of radioactive material in liquid effluent to a coastal sea.", "text": "a nuclear fuel reprocessing plant discharges small amounts of radioactive material in liquid effluent to a coastal sea. explain why it cannot be assumed that the discharge will be diluted to insignificant levels. give four factors that would be included in a critical pathway analysis of this discharge. explain how the results of the analysis reduce the cost of environmental monitoring. explain why critical group monitoring measures exposure rather than damage to health. natural processes may concentrate the material (eg adsorption onto sediments, bioaccumulation and food-chain concentration) it may be carried to locations where it is hazardous, such as fishing grounds or beaches used by people physical state of the effluent (solution / solid) ocean currents / river flow effect of ph and oxygen on solubility bioaccumulation and food-chain concentration food sources and consumption by local people (eg shellfish, seaweed) half-lives of the isotopes meteorological conditions for any gaseous release nature of the environment - geology, vegetation monitoring focuses on the sites most at risk of contamination no need to sample sites that were never likely to be polluted; sampling checks the accuracy of the predictions it assesses the risk to the people most exposed because of their lifestyle before any health effects occur if their risk is acceptably low everyone else has a lower risk; if unacceptable, emissions are reduced to prevent future exposure", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PCON-07\" data-qid=\"PCON-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pollution-control.html\">Principles of pollution control (CPA, CGM, new technologies)</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.1, 3.4.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 243-245, 303-304 &middot; PCON-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A nuclear fuel reprocessing plant discharges small amounts of radioactive material in liquid effluent to a coastal sea.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Explain why it cannot be assumed that the discharge will be diluted to insignificant levels.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Give <b>four</b> factors that would be included in a Critical Pathway Analysis of this discharge.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Explain how the results of the analysis reduce the cost of environmental monitoring.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>Explain why Critical Group Monitoring measures exposure rather than damage to health.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Natural processes may concentrate the material (eg adsorption onto sediments, bioaccumulation and food-chain concentration)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It may be carried to locations where it is hazardous, such as fishing grounds or beaches used by people</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Physical state of the effluent (solution / solid)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ocean currents / river flow</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Effect of pH and oxygen on solubility</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bioaccumulation and food-chain concentration</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Food sources and consumption by local people (eg shellfish, seaweed)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Half-lives of the isotopes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Meteorological conditions for any gaseous release</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nature of the environment - geology, vegetation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Monitoring focuses on the sites most at risk of contamination</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No need to sample sites that were never likely to be polluted; sampling checks the accuracy of the predictions</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It assesses the risk to the people most exposed because of their lifestyle before any health effects occur</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>If their risk is acceptably low everyone else has a lower risk; if unacceptable, emissions are reduced to prevent future exposure</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 243-245, 303-304; AQA spec 3.4.3.1, 3.4.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PCON-08", "sub": "pollution-control", "subname": "Principles of pollution control (CPA, CGM, new technologies)", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 15, "flags": ["calc", "data", "ext"], "spec": "3.4.3.1, 3.4.3.3", "pages": "243-245, 303-304", "url": "subtopic/pollution-control.html#PCON-08", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows how the cost of controlling emissions from a factory changes with the proportion of the pollutant that is prevented from being released.", "text": "figure 1 shows how the cost of controlling emissions from a factory changes with the proportion of the pollutant that is prevented from being released. use figure 1 to calculate the extra cost of increasing control from 90% to 99% of the pollutant. explain how the terms alara and batneec are used when deciding how much pollution control is required. explain why the control methods that were originally adequate for a factory may no longer be sufficient. evaluate the principles and general strategies that can be used to decide how pollution should be controlled. 16.0 - 5.0 = £11 million it is rarely practical to reduce emissions to zero and the impact of low-level emissions may be acceptable, so an acceptable level must be decided alara: emissions should be as low as reasonably achievable batneec: the best available technology not entailing excessive cost is chosen to achieve this as industries expand or cities grow the number of pollutant sources increases so the total emissions / combined concentration rises even if each source is unchanged, and higher standards are needed critical pathway analysis: predicting movement of mobile persistent pollutants (state of effluent, wind, currents, geology, ph / oxygen and solubility, bioaccumulation, food sources, half-lives) so monitoring focuses on sites at risk; mainly radioactive discharges; checked by sampling critical group monitoring: exposure of the most-exposed group monitored before health effects; if their risk is acceptable everyone else is safer; emissions reduced if not emission control strategies: location (marine discharges, downwind of cities, not over aquifers) and timing (avoiding temperature inversions, low river flow) principles: polluter pays (incentive depends on penalties and enforcement), precautionary principle (assume harm until shown otherwise; ignorance no excuse), alara / batneec and the non-linear cost-efficiency relationship selection of methods: production prevention (desulfurisation), prevention of release (precipitators, catalytic converters), post-release remediation (oil spill clean-up, phytoremediation), alternative processes (electric vehicles, renewables, pyrethroids) evaluation: prevention usually cheaper and more effective than remediation; costs rise steeply near 100%; cpa relies on understanding of pathways; enforcement and international agreement needed for transboundary pollutants; judgement about acceptable risk is political as well as scientific justified conclusion", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PCON-08\" data-qid=\"PCON-08\" data-marks=\"15\" data-flags=\"calc data ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 8</span><span class=\"meta\">15 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pollution-control.html\">Principles of pollution control (CPA, CGM, new technologies)</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.1, 3.4.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 243-245, 303-304 &middot; PCON-08</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows how the cost of controlling emissions from a factory changes with the proportion of the pollutant that is prevented from being released.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,270.0 157.0,264.4 253.9,257.5 350.9,247.8 447.9,228.3 496.4,200.6 520.6,158.9 540.0,47.8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"157.0\" cy=\"264.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"253.9\" cy=\"257.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"350.9\" cy=\"247.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"447.9\" cy=\"228.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"496.4\" cy=\"200.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"520.6\" cy=\"158.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"47.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">19.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">3.6</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">39.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">7.2</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">59.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">10.8</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">79.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">14.4</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">99</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">18</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Proportion of pollution prevented / %</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Cost / &amp;pound; million</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.1</span>Use Figure 1 to calculate the extra cost of increasing control from 90% to 99% of the pollutant.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.2</span>Explain how the terms <b>ALARA</b> and <b>BATNEEC</b> are used when deciding how much pollution control is required.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.3</span>Explain why the control methods that were originally adequate for a factory may no longer be sufficient.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.4</span>Evaluate the principles and general strategies that can be used to decide how pollution should be controlled.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>16.0 - 5.0 = &pound;11 million</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It is rarely practical to reduce emissions to zero and the impact of low-level emissions may be acceptable, so an acceptable level must be decided</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>ALARA: emissions should be As Low As Reasonably Achievable</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>BATNEEC: the Best Available Technology Not Entailing Excessive Cost is chosen to achieve this</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>As industries expand or cities grow the number of pollutant sources increases</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So the total emissions / combined concentration rises even if each source is unchanged, and higher standards are needed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Critical Pathway Analysis: predicting movement of mobile persistent pollutants (state of effluent, wind, currents, geology, pH / oxygen and solubility, bioaccumulation, food sources, half-lives) so monitoring focuses on sites at risk; mainly radioactive discharges; checked by sampling</li><li>Critical Group Monitoring: exposure of the most-exposed group monitored before health effects; if their risk is acceptable everyone else is safer; emissions reduced if not</li><li>Emission control strategies: location (marine discharges, downwind of cities, not over aquifers) and timing (avoiding temperature inversions, low river flow)</li><li>Principles: polluter pays (incentive depends on penalties and enforcement), precautionary principle (assume harm until shown otherwise; ignorance no excuse), ALARA / BATNEEC and the non-linear cost-efficiency relationship</li><li>Selection of methods: production prevention (desulfurisation), prevention of release (precipitators, catalytic converters), post-release remediation (oil spill clean-up, phytoremediation), alternative processes (electric vehicles, renewables, pyrethroids)</li><li>Evaluation: prevention usually cheaper and more effective than remediation; costs rise steeply near 100%; CPA relies on understanding of pathways; enforcement and international agreement needed for transboundary pollutants; judgement about acceptable risk is political as well as scientific</li><li>Justified conclusion</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 243-245, 303-304; AQA spec 3.4.3.1, 3.4.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-01", "sub": "methodology", "subname": "Scientific methodologies & sampling design", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.7.1", "pages": "390-393", "url": "subtopic/methodology.html#RM-01", "snippet": "A student investigated whether the number of earthworms per m2 changes with distance from a hedge across an arable field.", "text": "a student investigated whether the number of earthworms per m2 changes with distance from a hedge across an arable field. explain why systematic sampling along a transect is more appropriate than random sampling for this investigation. describe how a preliminary study could be used to decide the distance between sampling sites and the number of samples at each site. describe how earthworms could be extracted from a 0.5 m x 0.5 m sample area and give two limitations of the method. there is an environmental gradient (distance from the hedge / changing conditions) so samples must be taken at regular intervals along it to detect the change random sampling might leave gaps / miss the pattern; systematic sampling avoids bias while covering the gradient sample at close intervals (eg every 1 m) first and see at what spacing the variations are still detected without unnecessary data; wider intervals (eg 4 m) may miss peaks and troughs take increasing numbers of samples at one site and calculate the running mean; the number needed is where the mean stops fluctuating / stabilises calculate standard deviation to assess scatter: more variable data need more samples test the method (extraction technique, timing) for reliability before the main study remove surface vegetation; pour an irritant solution (dilute mustard / detergent in water) onto the area until saturated (and around it), keep adding as it infiltrates collect worms that come to the surface for a standardised time; count / identify / weigh limitations: solution may not percolate impermeable soils or reach deep worms; worms may move sideways or downwards; some may die or not respond; slow-moving worms missed", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-01\" data-qid=\"RM-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/methodology.html\">Scientific methodologies &amp; sampling design</a> &middot; spec 3.7.1 &middot; Genn pp. 390-393 &middot; RM-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A student investigated whether the number of earthworms per m<sup>2</sup> changes with distance from a hedge across an arable field.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Explain why systematic sampling along a transect is more appropriate than random sampling for this investigation.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Describe how a preliminary study could be used to decide the distance between sampling sites and the number of samples at each site.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Describe how earthworms could be extracted from a 0.5 m x 0.5 m sample area and give <b>two</b> limitations of the method.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>There is an environmental gradient (distance from the hedge / changing conditions) so samples must be taken at regular intervals along it to detect the change</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Random sampling might leave gaps / miss the pattern; systematic sampling avoids bias while covering the gradient</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sample at close intervals (eg every 1 m) first and see at what spacing the variations are still detected without unnecessary data; wider intervals (eg 4 m) may miss peaks and troughs</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Take increasing numbers of samples at one site and calculate the running mean; the number needed is where the mean stops fluctuating / stabilises</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Calculate standard deviation to assess scatter: more variable data need more samples</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Test the method (extraction technique, timing) for reliability before the main study</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 4 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Remove surface vegetation; pour an irritant solution (dilute mustard / detergent in water) onto the area until saturated (and around it), keep adding as it infiltrates</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Collect worms that come to the surface for a standardised time; count / identify / weigh</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Limitations: solution may not percolate impermeable soils or reach deep worms; worms may move sideways or downwards; some may die or not respond; slow-moving worms missed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 390-393; AQA spec 3.7.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-02", "sub": "methodology", "subname": "Scientific methodologies & sampling design", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.7.1", "pages": "390-393", "url": "subtopic/methodology.html#RM-02", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the running mean of moth catches as a student increased the number of nights of light-trapping at one site.", "text": "table 1 shows the running mean of moth catches as a student increased the number of nights of light-trapping at one site. use table 1 to suggest the minimum number of nights needed to obtain a reliable mean. explain your answer. explain why moth surveys must be carried out at standardised times and weather conditions. distinguish between accuracy and precision of measurements. explain the importance of a null hypothesis and standardised techniques in a scientific investigation. about 8 nights the running mean fluctuates widely with few samples but stabilises at 21-22 from 8 nights onwards; extra samples do not change it significantly moth activity varies with temperature, wind, rain and season (adults only fly for part of the year) so catches vary independently of population size sampling at different conditions / times would make results not comparable timing between samples should be chosen to detect the temporal variation of interest (eg weekly through the season) accuracy: how close a recorded result is to the real value precision: the smallest interval between possible recorded values (eg a ruler marked in mm is more precise than one in cm) a null hypothesis (eg no difference / no correlation) can be tested statistically and accepted or rejected at a stated confidence level standardised techniques ensure results collected at different times, places or by different people are comparable other variables are controlled or monitored so their effect can be assessed; allows the study to be repeated / checked (reliability)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-02\" data-qid=\"RM-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/methodology.html\">Scientific methodologies &amp; sampling design</a> &middot; spec 3.7.1 &middot; Genn pp. 390-393 &middot; RM-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the running mean of moth catches as a student increased the number of nights of light-trapping at one site.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Number of nights</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>4</th><th>6</th><th>8</th><th>10</th><th>12</th><th>14</th></tr><tr><td>Running mean catch per night</td><td>31</td><td>18</td><td>24</td><td>21</td><td>22</td><td>22</td><td>21</td><td>22</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Use Table 1 to suggest the minimum number of nights needed to obtain a reliable mean. Explain your answer.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain why moth surveys must be carried out at standardised times and weather conditions.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Distinguish between <b>accuracy</b> and <b>precision</b> of measurements.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Explain the importance of a null hypothesis and standardised techniques in a scientific investigation.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>About 8 nights</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The running mean fluctuates widely with few samples but stabilises at 21-22 from 8 nights onwards; extra samples do not change it significantly</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Moth activity varies with temperature, wind, rain and season (adults only fly for part of the year) so catches vary independently of population size</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sampling at different conditions / times would make results not comparable</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Timing between samples should be chosen to detect the temporal variation of interest (eg weekly through the season)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Accuracy: how close a recorded result is to the real value</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Precision: the smallest interval between possible recorded values (eg a ruler marked in mm is more precise than one in cm)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A null hypothesis (eg no difference / no correlation) can be tested statistically and accepted or rejected at a stated confidence level</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Standardised techniques ensure results collected at different times, places or by different people are comparable</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Other variables are controlled or monitored so their effect can be assessed; allows the study to be repeated / checked (reliability)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 390-393; AQA spec 3.7.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-03", "sub": "methodology", "subname": "Scientific methodologies & sampling design", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.7.1, 3.7.2.2", "pages": "390-393, 419-420", "url": "subtopic/methodology.html#RM-03", "snippet": "A group of students wants to investigate the effect of vegetation cover on rain-splash soil erosion.", "text": "a group of students wants to investigate the effect of vegetation cover on rain-splash soil erosion. describe how they could carry out the investigation in the laboratory. give three variables, other than those you have standardised, that affect rain-splash erosion in the field. explain why it is easier to obtain valid results in the laboratory than in the field, and give one disadvantage of laboratory studies. trays of the same soil (texture, compaction, organic matter, depth) with different percentage vegetation cover (eg 0, 25, 50, 75, 100% grass), replicated same slope / gradient; simulated rain from a standard height at a standard rate and drop size (eg watering can / sprinkler) for a standard time collect the soil splashed / washed off (eg onto a sheet or into a trough at the base), dry and weigh it repeat each treatment several times and calculate means (and standard deviation) plot soil loss against cover; statistical test (spearman's rank / t-test) for significance rainfall intensity / raindrop size / height soil texture, compaction, organic matter, depth permeability of the material beneath gradient root binding all variables except the independent variable can be standardised in the laboratory; in the field uncontrollable variables (weather) must be measured instead laboratory conditions may not represent natural conditions so results may not apply directly to real ecosystems", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-03\" data-qid=\"RM-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/methodology.html\">Scientific methodologies &amp; sampling design</a> &middot; spec 3.7.1, 3.7.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 390-393, 419-420 &middot; RM-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A group of students wants to investigate the effect of vegetation cover on rain-splash soil erosion.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Describe how they could carry out the investigation in the laboratory.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Give <b>three</b> variables, other than those you have standardised, that affect rain-splash erosion in the field.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain why it is easier to obtain valid results in the laboratory than in the field, and give <b>one</b> disadvantage of laboratory studies.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Trays of the same soil (texture, compaction, organic matter, depth) with different percentage vegetation cover (eg 0, 25, 50, 75, 100% grass), replicated</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Same slope / gradient; simulated rain from a standard height at a standard rate and drop size (eg watering can / sprinkler) for a standard time</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Collect the soil splashed / washed off (eg onto a sheet or into a trough at the base), dry and weigh it</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Repeat each treatment several times and calculate means (and standard deviation)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Plot soil loss against cover; statistical test (Spearman's rank / t-test) for significance</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Rainfall intensity / raindrop size / height</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Soil texture, compaction, organic matter, depth</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Permeability of the material beneath</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Gradient</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Root binding</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>All variables except the independent variable can be standardised in the laboratory; in the field uncontrollable variables (weather) must be measured instead</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Laboratory conditions may not represent natural conditions so results may not apply directly to real ecosystems</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 390-393, 419-420; AQA spec 3.7.1, 3.7.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-04", "sub": "methodology", "subname": "Scientific methodologies & sampling design", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.7.1", "pages": "391-393", "url": "subtopic/methodology.html#RM-04", "snippet": "Which of the following best describes a representative sample? Tick one box.", "text": " which of the following best describes a representative sample? tick one box. describe how random sampling sites would be selected in an irregularly shaped meadow. explain why deliberately choosing sampling sites introduces bias. b number all possible sampling sites (eg on a grid map) select the sites using a random number generator / table; (regular shape: grid coordinates from random numbers) sites may be chosen for convenience or because they appear to support (or dismiss) the hypothesis so the sample does not represent the whole area and conclusions are invalid", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-04\" data-qid=\"RM-04\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/methodology.html\">Scientific methodologies &amp; sampling design</a> &middot; spec 3.7.1 &middot; Genn pp. 391-393 &middot; RM-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Which of the following best describes a <b>representative</b> sample? Tick one box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; A sample taken from the most convenient location</li><li>B&nbsp; A sub-sample that accurately reflects the complete data set</li><li>C&nbsp; The largest sample that can be collected</li><li>D&nbsp; A sample that supports the hypothesis</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Describe how random sampling sites would be selected in an irregularly shaped meadow.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain why deliberately choosing sampling sites introduces bias.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b></b>&nbsp; B</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Number all possible sampling sites (eg on a grid map)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Select the sites using a random number generator / table; (regular shape: grid coordinates from random numbers)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sites may be chosen for convenience or because they appear to support (or dismiss) the hypothesis</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So the sample does not represent the whole area and conclusions are invalid</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 391-393; AQA spec 3.7.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-16", "sub": "methodology", "subname": "Scientific methodologies & sampling design", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.7.1", "pages": "390-391", "url": "subtopic/methodology.html#RM-16", "snippet": "A student measured the pH of a stream with a meter that reads to 0.01 pH units but had not been calibrated. Which statement is correct? Tick one box.", "text": " a student measured the ph of a stream with a meter that reads to 0.01 ph units but had not been calibrated. which statement is correct? tick one box. explain what is meant by an anomalous result and how a student should decide whether to include it. explain why a preliminary study is carried out before the main investigation. the readings are precise but may not be accurate a result that differs from other results with which it was expected to be similar repeat the study / measurement: if it recurs it is a 'real' result caused by another variable; if not it was an error and can be excluded to test the reliability of the method / equipment to decide the sample size, number of samples, spacing or timing needed to detect the variation without collecting unnecessary data", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-16\" data-qid=\"RM-16\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/methodology.html\">Scientific methodologies &amp; sampling design</a> &middot; spec 3.7.1 &middot; Genn pp. 390-391 &middot; RM-16</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>A student measured the pH of a stream with a meter that reads to 0.01 pH units but had not been calibrated. Which statement is correct? Tick <b>one</b> box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; The readings are precise but may not be accurate</li><li>B&nbsp; The readings are accurate but not precise</li><li>C&nbsp; The readings are both accurate and precise</li><li>D&nbsp; The readings are reliable because the meter is electronic</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain what is meant by an <b>anomalous result</b> and how a student should decide whether to include it.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain why a preliminary study is carried out before the main investigation.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b>A</b>&nbsp; The readings are precise but may not be accurate</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A result that differs from other results with which it was expected to be similar</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Repeat the study / measurement: if it recurs it is a 'real' result caused by another variable; if not it was an error and can be excluded</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>To test the reliability of the method / equipment</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>To decide the sample size, number of samples, spacing or timing needed to detect the variation without collecting unnecessary data</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 390-391; AQA spec 3.7.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-17", "sub": "methodology", "subname": "Scientific methodologies & sampling design", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.7.1", "pages": "391-393", "url": "subtopic/methodology.html#RM-17", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the mean number of woodlice per pitfall trap calculated as more traps were included in the mean (a running mean).", "text": "figure 1 shows the mean number of woodlice per pitfall trap calculated as more traps were included in the mean (a running mean). use figure 1 to suggest the minimum number of traps needed to produce a reliable mean. give a reason for your answer. explain why the number of samples needed depends on the degree of scatter of the results. the student wants to compare woodlouse numbers in a woodland and in a grassland. describe how the sampling should be standardised. explain why the timing of sampling would matter if the student were investigating the activity of flying insects rather than woodlice. about 40-50 traps because the running mean stops changing / fluctuating with fewer than this the mean is unreliable widely scattered results (large standard deviation) mean a single sample may not be representative more samples are needed so that the effect of variability is averaged out and the mean is representative / significance can be assessed same type and size of pitfall trap, set for the same length of time traps emptied at the same time of day / same weather conditions in both habitats same number of traps and same method of positioning (random or systematic) in each habitat other variables that could affect results (eg rainfall, temperature) monitored or controlled flying insect activity changes with weather over minutes to weeks (temperature, wind) and diurnally samples on different occasions are needed to produce a mean that represents the typical value or to identify trends the interval between samples should match the rate at which the factor changes, found from a preliminary study", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-17\" data-qid=\"RM-17\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/methodology.html\">Scientific methodologies &amp; sampling design</a> &middot; spec 3.7.1 &middot; Genn pp. 391-393 &middot; RM-17</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the mean number of woodlice per pitfall trap calculated as more traps were included in the mean (a running mean).</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,55.7 96.9,118.2 133.8,87.9 170.8,107.5 244.6,98.6 318.5,102.1 392.3,100.4 466.2,100.4 540.0,100.4\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"55.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"96.9\" cy=\"118.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"133.8\" cy=\"87.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"170.8\" cy=\"107.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"244.6\" cy=\"98.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"318.5\" cy=\"102.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"392.3\" cy=\"100.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"466.2\" cy=\"100.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"100.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">5</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">18</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">2.8</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">31</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">5.6</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">44</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">8.4</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">57</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">11.2</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">70</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">14</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Number of traps used to calculate the mean</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Running mean number of woodlice per trap</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Use Figure 1 to suggest the minimum number of traps needed to produce a reliable mean. Give a reason for your answer.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain why the number of samples needed depends on the degree of scatter of the results.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>The student wants to compare woodlouse numbers in a woodland and in a grassland. Describe how the sampling should be standardised.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Explain why the timing of sampling would matter if the student were investigating the activity of flying insects rather than woodlice.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>About 40-50 traps</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Because the running mean stops changing / fluctuating with fewer than this the mean is unreliable</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Widely scattered results (large standard deviation) mean a single sample may not be representative</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>More samples are needed so that the effect of variability is averaged out and the mean is representative / significance can be assessed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Same type and size of pitfall trap, set for the same length of time</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Traps emptied at the same time of day / same weather conditions in both habitats</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Same number of traps and same method of positioning (random or systematic) in each habitat</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Other variables that could affect results (eg rainfall, temperature) monitored or controlled</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Flying insect activity changes with weather over minutes to weeks (temperature, wind) and diurnally</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Samples on different occasions are needed to produce a mean that represents the typical value or to identify trends</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The interval between samples should match the rate at which the factor changes, found from a preliminary study</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 391-393; AQA spec 3.7.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-21", "sub": "methodology", "subname": "Scientific methodologies & sampling design", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 15, "flags": ["data", "ext"], "spec": "3.7.1, 3.7.2.3", "pages": "390-393, 420-423", "url": "subtopic/methodology.html#RM-21", "snippet": "A student investigated whether the number of earthworms per m2 differs between an organic field and a conventionally farmed field. Table 1 shows the results.", "text": "a student investigated whether the number of earthworms per m2 differs between an organic field and a conventionally farmed field. table 1 shows the results. name the statistical test the student should use and explain why it is appropriate. the test gave p = 0.01. state the conclusion the student should draw. explain why the student cannot conclude that organic farming caused the higher earthworm numbers. evaluate the decisions that must be made when planning a fieldwork investigation so that valid conclusions can be drawn. t-test (or mann-whitney u if not normally distributed) comparing two means of counted / measured data from two independent samples the difference is statistically significant (less than 1% probability that it arose by chance) the null hypothesis (no difference between fields) is rejected; the organic field has significantly more earthworms a significant difference does not show a causal relationship other variables (soil type, moisture, ph, cultivation history) may differ between the two fields and cause the difference scientific methodology: identifying the topic, reviewing existing knowledge, formulating a testable hypothesis and null hypothesis, designing the study, analysing and drawing conclusions, planning further research sample location: random (grid coordinates / numbered sites) or systematic (transects, fixed intervals) to avoid bias; interval chosen so variation is detected without excess data sample timing: diurnal, seasonal, weather-related and long-term changes; sampling on different occasions; interval matched to rate of change sample size and number: larger and more samples for non-homogeneous variables; running mean and standard deviation show when the mean is reliable; preliminary study to decide standardisation and control of variables; accuracy, precision, reliability, representativeness; anomalies and repeats; calibration of equipment; appropriate techniques and risk management statistical analysis: choosing the test (spearman, chi-squared, t-test, mann-whitney), significance and p-values, error bars, correlation vs causation evaluation: trade-offs between cost, time, disturbance and reliability; limitations of sub-sampling; justified conclusion", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-21\" data-qid=\"RM-21\" data-marks=\"15\" data-flags=\"data ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">15 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/methodology.html\">Scientific methodologies &amp; sampling design</a> &middot; spec 3.7.1, 3.7.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 390-393, 420-423 &middot; RM-21</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A student investigated whether the number of earthworms per m<sup>2</sup> differs between an organic field and a conventionally farmed field. Table 1 shows the results.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Field</th><th>Number of samples</th><th>Mean earthworms per m<sup>2</sup></th><th>Standard deviation</th></tr><tr><td>Organic</td><td>10</td><td>48</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td>Conventional</td><td>10</td><td>31</td><td>12</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Name the statistical test the student should use and explain why it is appropriate.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>The test gave p = 0.01. State the conclusion the student should draw.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Explain why the student cannot conclude that organic farming <b>caused</b> the higher earthworm numbers.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>Evaluate the decisions that must be made when planning a fieldwork investigation so that valid conclusions can be drawn.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>t-test (or Mann-Whitney U if not normally distributed)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Comparing two means of counted / measured data from two independent samples</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The difference is statistically significant (less than 1% probability that it arose by chance)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The null hypothesis (no difference between fields) is rejected; the organic field has significantly more earthworms</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A significant difference does not show a causal relationship</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Other variables (soil type, moisture, pH, cultivation history) may differ between the two fields and cause the difference</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Scientific methodology: identifying the topic, reviewing existing knowledge, formulating a testable hypothesis and null hypothesis, designing the study, analysing and drawing conclusions, planning further research</li><li>Sample location: random (grid coordinates / numbered sites) or systematic (transects, fixed intervals) to avoid bias; interval chosen so variation is detected without excess data</li><li>Sample timing: diurnal, seasonal, weather-related and long-term changes; sampling on different occasions; interval matched to rate of change</li><li>Sample size and number: larger and more samples for non-homogeneous variables; running mean and standard deviation show when the mean is reliable; preliminary study to decide</li><li>Standardisation and control of variables; accuracy, precision, reliability, representativeness; anomalies and repeats; calibration of equipment; appropriate techniques and risk management</li><li>Statistical analysis: choosing the test (Spearman, chi-squared, t-test, Mann-Whitney), significance and p-values, error bars, correlation vs causation</li><li>Evaluation: trade-offs between cost, time, disturbance and reliability; limitations of sub-sampling; justified conclusion</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 390-393, 420-423; AQA spec 3.7.1, 3.7.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "BIOV-01", "sub": "biodiversity-value", "subname": "Importance of biodiversity: resources & ecosystem services", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.1.1-3.1.2.1.2", "pages": "10-15", "url": "subtopic/biodiversity-value.html#BIOV-01", "snippet": "Explain what is meant by biomimetics and describe three examples.", "text": " explain what is meant by biomimetics and describe three examples. explain why many plants produce chemicals that are useful as human medicines, and give two examples. explain why the discovery of medicines is a strong argument for conserving whole habitats rather than individual species. give one example of a wildlife species used in physiological research and the human health issue it helps to understand. copying the structural / functional adaptations of living organisms in the design of manufactured items aircraft wingtip fins copied from splayed wingtip feathers of soaring birds, reducing turbulence and fuel use humpback whale flipper tubercles copied on ship rudders and wind turbine blades (tighter turns, rotation at lower wind speeds) shark skin ridges reducing friction / bacterial adhesion (surface coatings, hospital surfaces) termite mound convection copied for natural ventilation of buildings; hollow strutted bird bones in lightweight roofs; gecko toe adhesion; burr hooks - velcro; lotus leaf self-cleaning glass; spider silk for airbags / body armour; bat / dolphin echolocation - ultrasound scanners plants produce toxic chemicals (eg alkaloids) to defend themselves against herbivores; in controlled doses these affect human physiology examples: morphine / codeine from poppies; quinine from cinchona bark (malaria); aspirin from willow bark; taxol from yew (cancers); diosgenin from mexican yam (contraceptives, cortisone) animal examples accepted: azt from a marine sponge (hiv), shark liver lipids (tumours) only a very small proportion of species have been studied for medicinal substances so unknown species may contain valuable chemicals conserving the habitat protects all its species, including those not yet investigated (and their interdependent relationships) squid giant nerve cells - nerve function / heart disease, stroke; marsupials (kangaroo, wallaby pouch) - embryo development; hippo skin secretion - sunscreen / burns; sea urchin embryos - teratogen testing; armadillo - leprosy; marine sponge proteins - transplant rejection", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"BIOV-01\" data-qid=\"BIOV-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/biodiversity-value.html\">Importance of biodiversity: resources &amp; ecosystem services</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.1.1-3.1.2.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 10-15 &middot; BIOV-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Explain what is meant by <b>biomimetics</b> and describe <b>three</b> examples.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain why many plants produce chemicals that are useful as human medicines, and give <b>two</b> examples.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain why the discovery of medicines is a strong argument for conserving whole habitats rather than individual species.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Give <b>one</b> example of a wildlife species used in physiological research and the human health issue it helps to understand.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Copying the structural / functional adaptations of living organisms in the design of manufactured items</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Aircraft wingtip fins copied from splayed wingtip feathers of soaring birds, reducing turbulence and fuel use</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Humpback whale flipper tubercles copied on ship rudders and wind turbine blades (tighter turns, rotation at lower wind speeds)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Shark skin ridges reducing friction / bacterial adhesion (surface coatings, hospital surfaces)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Termite mound convection copied for natural ventilation of buildings; hollow strutted bird bones in lightweight roofs; gecko toe adhesion; burr hooks - Velcro; lotus leaf self-cleaning glass; spider silk for airbags / body armour; bat / dolphin echolocation - ultrasound scanners</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Plants produce toxic chemicals (eg alkaloids) to defend themselves against herbivores; in controlled doses these affect human physiology</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Examples: morphine / codeine from poppies; quinine from cinchona bark (malaria); aspirin from willow bark; taxol from yew (cancers); diosgenin from Mexican yam (contraceptives, cortisone)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Animal examples accepted: AZT from a marine sponge (HIV), shark liver lipids (tumours)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Only a very small proportion of species have been studied for medicinal substances so unknown species may contain valuable chemicals</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Conserving the habitat protects all its species, including those not yet investigated (and their interdependent relationships)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Squid giant nerve cells - nerve function / heart disease, stroke; marsupials (kangaroo, wallaby pouch) - embryo development; hippo skin secretion - sunscreen / burns; sea urchin embryos - teratogen testing; armadillo - leprosy; marine sponge proteins - transplant rejection</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 10-15; AQA spec 3.1.2.1.1-3.1.2.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "BIOV-02", "sub": "biodiversity-value", "subname": "Importance of biodiversity: resources & ecosystem services", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.1.2.1.2", "pages": "15-17", "url": "subtopic/biodiversity-value.html#BIOV-02", "snippet": "Table 1 shows characteristics introduced into commercial crops from crop wild relatives (CWRs).", "text": "table 1 shows characteristics introduced into commercial crops from crop wild relatives (cwrs). explain why commercial crop varieties often lack the characteristics shown in table 1. explain what is meant by a vavilov centre (centre of diversity) and why these areas are conservation priorities. explain why a large population does not necessarily have a large gene pool, and why a small gene pool is a problem. give one wild plant species with potential as a new commercial food crop and the characteristic that makes it valuable. domesticated crops were bred from a small number of original plants so they have a small gene pool / low genetic diversity selective breeding for yield, appearance or shelf life may have lost other useful genes (eg taste, disease resistance) genetically similar plants share the same susceptibilities so a new disease or environmental change can affect the whole crop a region with a high concentration of the wild relatives of important crops - usually where the crop was first domesticated (eg wheat / barley in the middle east; potato, tomato in south america; rice in india; maize in central america) they hold the largest gene pools / most genetic variety for future crop breeding (disease resistance, drought and salt tolerance, yield) many are in regions where environmental degradation / habitat loss threatens the wild plants a large population may all descend from a few closely related individuals so they are genetically similar similar adaptations mean all are susceptible to the same change (disease, climate) greater risk of inbreeding so disadvantageous recessive genes are expressed (eg amelanism, poor muscle development in lion cubs); so species should be protected across their whole range to retain regional genes morama bean (drought resistant, soya-like protein); yeheb tree (drought resistant nuts, poor soils); potato bean (high protein); perennial maize / kernza (no annual replanting, less ploughing / erosion); salt-tolerant spinach relatives", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"BIOV-02\" data-qid=\"BIOV-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/biodiversity-value.html\">Importance of biodiversity: resources &amp; ecosystem services</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 15-17 &middot; BIOV-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows characteristics introduced into commercial crops from crop wild relatives (CWRs).</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Crop</th><th>Wild relative source</th><th>Characteristic introduced</th></tr><tr><td>Sugar cane</td><td>Wild sugar cane, Indonesia</td><td>Resistance to mosaic virus</td></tr><tr><td>Tomato</td><td>Single wild species</td><td>Resistance to nine major diseases</td></tr><tr><td>Rice / barley</td><td>Wild salt-marsh varieties</td><td>Salt tolerance</td></tr><tr><td>Oil palm</td><td>Wild palms, central Africa</td><td>25% higher yield</td></tr><tr><td>Wheat</td><td>Wild wheat</td><td>Better mycorrhizal associations / phosphate uptake</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain why commercial crop varieties often lack the characteristics shown in Table 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain what is meant by a <b>Vavilov centre</b> (centre of diversity) and why these areas are conservation priorities.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain why a large population does not necessarily have a large gene pool, and why a small gene pool is a problem.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Give <b>one</b> wild plant species with potential as a new commercial food crop and the characteristic that makes it valuable.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Domesticated crops were bred from a small number of original plants so they have a small gene pool / low genetic diversity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Selective breeding for yield, appearance or shelf life may have lost other useful genes (eg taste, disease resistance)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Genetically similar plants share the same susceptibilities so a new disease or environmental change can affect the whole crop</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A region with a high concentration of the wild relatives of important crops - usually where the crop was first domesticated (eg wheat / barley in the Middle East; potato, tomato in South America; rice in India; maize in Central America)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They hold the largest gene pools / most genetic variety for future crop breeding (disease resistance, drought and salt tolerance, yield)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Many are in regions where environmental degradation / habitat loss threatens the wild plants</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A large population may all descend from a few closely related individuals so they are genetically similar</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Similar adaptations mean all are susceptible to the same change (disease, climate)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Greater risk of inbreeding so disadvantageous recessive genes are expressed (eg amelanism, poor muscle development in lion cubs); so species should be protected across their whole range to retain regional genes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Morama bean (drought resistant, soya-like protein); yeheb tree (drought resistant nuts, poor soils); potato bean (high protein); perennial maize / Kernza (no annual replanting, less ploughing / erosion); salt-tolerant spinach relatives</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 15-17; AQA spec 3.1.2.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "BIOV-03", "sub": "biodiversity-value", "subname": "Importance of biodiversity: resources & ecosystem services", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.1.3", "pages": "18-19", "url": "subtopic/biodiversity-value.html#BIOV-03", "snippet": "Explain how living organisms provide each of the following ecosystem services.", "text": " explain how living organisms provide each of the following ecosystem services. explain why darwin's orchid of madagascar depends on a single moth species, and why such specialisation is both an advantage and a risk. explain why elephants are particularly important seed dispersers. photosynthesis and aerobic respiration balance o2 and co2 in a dynamic equilibrium; evapotranspiration adds water vapour forming clouds and rain; microbes drive the nitrogen and other biogeochemical cycles so wastes do not accumulate and nutrients are recycled detritivores, fungi and bacteria decompose dead organic matter releasing nutrients and forming humus that binds soil; organic acids weather rock forming new soil; roots bind soil, reducing erosion and regulating water flow / flooding species provide food, pollination (bees, moths, birds, bats - more reliable than wind, especially in forests), seed dispersal (animals carry larger seeds to suitable sites, faeces as fertiliser, gut passage stimulates germination) and habitat provision (trees as nest sites and canopy microclimate; mollusc shells for hermit crabs) its nectar is at the end of a 30 cm tube reached only by the long tongue of the sphinx moth, which carries pollen only between orchids of the same species advantage: no pollen wasted on other species and the moth has an exclusive food source; risk: if either species declines the other cannot survive they are not ruminants so seeds are not destroyed by repeated chewing / pass through intact; they eat the fruit of most tree species not territorial so they carry seeds over large distances / areas", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"BIOV-03\" data-qid=\"BIOV-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/biodiversity-value.html\">Importance of biodiversity: resources &amp; ecosystem services</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.1.3 &middot; Genn pp. 18-19 &middot; BIOV-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Explain how living organisms provide each of the following ecosystem services.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain why Darwin's orchid of Madagascar depends on a single moth species, and why such specialisation is both an advantage and a risk.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain why elephants are particularly important seed dispersers.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Photosynthesis and aerobic respiration balance O2 and CO2 in a dynamic equilibrium; evapotranspiration adds water vapour forming clouds and rain; microbes drive the nitrogen and other biogeochemical cycles so wastes do not accumulate and nutrients are recycled</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Detritivores, fungi and bacteria decompose dead organic matter releasing nutrients and forming humus that binds soil; organic acids weather rock forming new soil; roots bind soil, reducing erosion and regulating water flow / flooding</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Species provide food, pollination (bees, moths, birds, bats - more reliable than wind, especially in forests), seed dispersal (animals carry larger seeds to suitable sites, faeces as fertiliser, gut passage stimulates germination) and habitat provision (trees as nest sites and canopy microclimate; mollusc shells for hermit crabs)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Its nectar is at the end of a 30 cm tube reached only by the long tongue of the sphinx moth, which carries pollen only between orchids of the same species</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Advantage: no pollen wasted on other species and the moth has an exclusive food source; risk: if either species declines the other cannot survive</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They are not ruminants so seeds are not destroyed by repeated chewing / pass through intact; they eat the fruit of most tree species</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Not territorial so they carry seeds over large distances / areas</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 18-19; AQA spec 3.1.2.1.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "BIOV-04", "sub": "biodiversity-value", "subname": "Importance of biodiversity: resources & ecosystem services", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.1.2.1", "pages": "10-19", "url": "subtopic/biodiversity-value.html#BIOV-04", "snippet": "Discuss the reasons why the conservation of biodiversity is important for human society.", "text": " discuss the reasons why the conservation of biodiversity is important for human society. state what is meant by biodiversity. resources: wood, fibres (cotton, wool, silk), oils, fuels (wood, biofuels), food; new food species (morama bean, perennial cereals, eland, bison) better adapted to local conditions biomimetics: vehicle design, architecture, structures, adhesion, materials, ultrasound medicines from plants and animals (examples); most species unstudied - argument for habitat conservation physiological research using wildlife species; wildlife as pest control agents (ladybirds, encarsia, cactoblastis, bt) genetic resources: crop wild relatives, vavilov centres, gene pools ecosystem services: atmospheric composition, hydrological and biogeochemical cycles, soil maintenance, pollination, seed dispersal, habitat provision ethical / aesthetic / recreational arguments; the unknown value of species not yet investigated (precautionary principle) evaluation: which arguments are most persuasive to decision makers; economic valuation vs intrinsic value; judgement the variety and abundance of life (species, genes, habitats) in an area / on earth", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"BIOV-04\" data-qid=\"BIOV-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/biodiversity-value.html\">Importance of biodiversity: resources &amp; ecosystem services</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 10-19 &middot; BIOV-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Discuss the reasons why the conservation of biodiversity is important for human society.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>State what is meant by <b>biodiversity</b>.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Resources: wood, fibres (cotton, wool, silk), oils, fuels (wood, biofuels), food; new food species (morama bean, perennial cereals, eland, bison) better adapted to local conditions</li><li>Biomimetics: vehicle design, architecture, structures, adhesion, materials, ultrasound</li><li>Medicines from plants and animals (examples); most species unstudied - argument for habitat conservation</li><li>Physiological research using wildlife species; wildlife as pest control agents (ladybirds, Encarsia, Cactoblastis, Bt)</li><li>Genetic resources: crop wild relatives, Vavilov centres, gene pools</li><li>Ecosystem services: atmospheric composition, hydrological and biogeochemical cycles, soil maintenance, pollination, seed dispersal, habitat provision</li><li>Ethical / aesthetic / recreational arguments; the unknown value of species not yet investigated (precautionary principle)</li><li>Evaluation: which arguments are most persuasive to decision makers; economic valuation vs intrinsic value; judgement</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The variety and abundance of life (species, genes, habitats) in an area / on Earth</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 10-19; AQA spec 3.1.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "BIOV-05", "sub": "biodiversity-value", "subname": "Importance of biodiversity: resources & ecosystem services", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 5, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.1.1", "pages": "11", "url": "subtopic/biodiversity-value.html#BIOV-05", "snippet": "Give two wild species with potential as new food species and, for each, one characteristic that makes it useful.", "text": " give two wild species with potential as new food species and, for each, one characteristic that makes it useful. explain why indigenous species may be better for domestication than introduced livestock or crops. explain how species of the spinach family could help to solve a problem caused by irrigation. potato bean (n america): high protein content morama bean (southern africa): protein content similar to soya, drought resistant yeheb tree (somalia): edible nuts, drought resistant, grows on poor soils perennial maize (mexico) / kernza: no need to buy seed each year, less ploughing so less soil erosion american bison: graze grassland too poor for cattle common eland: browse and graze so can be kept with cattle without severe competition indigenous species are better adapted to local conditions such as climate, pests / diseases and soil so they have higher survival rates / productivity is not reduced, as it is when introduced species are kept in areas to which they are not adapted they grow in soils with a high salt content, so their genes could be used in breeding programmes to increase the salt tolerance of crops grown on soils salinised by irrigation", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"BIOV-05\" data-qid=\"BIOV-05\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/biodiversity-value.html\">Importance of biodiversity: resources &amp; ecosystem services</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.1.1 &middot; Genn pp. 11 &middot; BIOV-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Give <b>two</b> wild species with potential as new food species and, for each, one characteristic that makes it useful.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain why indigenous species may be better for domestication than introduced livestock or crops.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain how species of the spinach family could help to solve a problem caused by irrigation.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Potato bean (N America): high protein content</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Morama bean (southern Africa): protein content similar to soya, drought resistant</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Yeheb tree (Somalia): edible nuts, drought resistant, grows on poor soils</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Perennial maize (Mexico) / Kernza: no need to buy seed each year, less ploughing so less soil erosion</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>American bison: graze grassland too poor for cattle</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Common eland: browse and graze so can be kept with cattle without severe competition</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Indigenous species are better adapted to local conditions such as climate, pests / diseases and soil</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So they have higher survival rates / productivity is not reduced, as it is when introduced species are kept in areas to which they are not adapted</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They grow in soils with a high salt content, so their genes could be used in breeding programmes to increase the salt tolerance of crops grown on soils salinised by irrigation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 11; AQA spec 3.1.2.1.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "BIOV-06", "sub": "biodiversity-value", "subname": "Importance of biodiversity: resources & ecosystem services", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.1.2.1.1", "pages": "11-14", "url": "subtopic/biodiversity-value.html#BIOV-06", "snippet": "Biomimetics uses knowledge of the adaptations of other species to improve the design of manufactured items.", "text": "biomimetics uses knowledge of the adaptations of other species to improve the design of manufactured items. complete table 1 by adding the missing adaptation or application. explain how the design of termite mounds has been copied to reduce the environmental impact of large buildings. describe two ways in which studying the physiology of other species could help to solve human health problems. explain why bird wing bones are a useful model for engineers. ship rudders that turn in tighter circles / aerogenerator blades that start turning at lower wind speeds water-repelling surface of lotus flowers coatings for aircraft and ships that reduce fuel consumption / hospital coatings that bacteria cannot stick to hooked burrs on seeds that stick to animal fur the mound absorbs sunlight and warms; hot air inside rises, creating a convection current that draws stale air out of the lower parts (solar-driven ventilation) air blowing over the mound has a lower pressure which also draws air out copied in shopping complexes / office blocks to give natural ventilation and cooling without air conditioning, so less energy / electricity is used marsupials: young develop in the pouch so are easier to study than a human foetus, helping understanding of developmental problems hippopotamus skin secretes hipposudoric acid (natural sunscreen and antimicrobial) which may improve treatment of burns marine sponges produce proteins that prevent rejection of grafts, being developed to prevent rejection of transplanted organs they are light but strong: hollow with internal struts that prevent bending / breaking, copied in lightweight bridges and roofs", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"BIOV-06\" data-qid=\"BIOV-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/biodiversity-value.html\">Importance of biodiversity: resources &amp; ecosystem services</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.1.1 &middot; Genn pp. 11-14 &middot; BIOV-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Biomimetics uses knowledge of the adaptations of other species to improve the design of manufactured items.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Complete Table 1 by adding the missing adaptation or application.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Species and adaptation</th><th>Design application</th></tr><tr><td>Tubercles (bumps) on the flippers of humpback whales</td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Self-cleaning glass</td></tr><tr><td>Shark skin scales that reduce friction</td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Velcro</td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain how the design of termite mounds has been copied to reduce the environmental impact of large buildings.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Describe <b>two</b> ways in which studying the physiology of other species could help to solve human health problems.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Explain why bird wing bones are a useful model for engineers.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ship rudders that turn in tighter circles / aerogenerator blades that start turning at lower wind speeds</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Water-repelling surface of lotus flowers</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Coatings for aircraft and ships that reduce fuel consumption / hospital coatings that bacteria cannot stick to</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hooked burrs on seeds that stick to animal fur</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The mound absorbs sunlight and warms; hot air inside rises, creating a convection current that draws stale air out of the lower parts (solar-driven ventilation)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Air blowing over the mound has a lower pressure which also draws air out</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Copied in shopping complexes / office blocks to give natural ventilation and cooling without air conditioning, so less energy / electricity is used</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Marsupials: young develop in the pouch so are easier to study than a human foetus, helping understanding of developmental problems</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hippopotamus skin secretes hipposudoric acid (natural sunscreen and antimicrobial) which may improve treatment of burns</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Marine sponges produce proteins that prevent rejection of grafts, being developed to prevent rejection of transplanted organs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They are light but strong: hollow with internal struts that prevent bending / breaking, copied in lightweight bridges and roofs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 11-14; AQA spec 3.1.2.1.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "BIOV-07", "sub": "biodiversity-value", "subname": "Importance of biodiversity: resources & ecosystem services", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.1.3", "pages": "18-19", "url": "subtopic/biodiversity-value.html#BIOV-07", "snippet": "Explain why the ecosystem services provided by other species are often given a low conservation priority.", "text": " explain why the ecosystem services provided by other species are often given a low conservation priority. explain how soil organisms provide services that humans depend on. explain why pollination by insects is more useful than wind pollination to plants growing in forests. explain how seed dispersal by animals increases the survival of a plant's offspring. the processes (eg regulation of atmospheric gases by photosynthesis and respiration) act in a dynamic equilibrium and cancel each other out because there is no overall change their actions go unnoticed / their importance is not appreciated until they are lost invertebrates, fungi and bacteria break down / decompose dead organic matter, releasing nutrients for plant growth the organic matter and humus produced hold the soil together (reducing erosion) and regulate the water cycle, giving more even river flow and less flooding organic acids from decomposition aid the weathering of rock, producing more soil and releasing more nutrients microbes carry out the processes of the carbon, nitrogen and phosphorus cycles so wastes do not build up and nutrients are not depleted trees reduce wind velocity so wind pollination would be ineffective / unreliable over long distances insects search over long distances for flowers so dispersed populations can be pollinated, and less pollen needs to be produced (energy saving); specialised flowers reduce pollen being wasted on other species dispersal is not haphazard: animals live in the same habitat as the plant so are likely to carry seeds to locations where they can survive seeds can be carried longer distances than by wind and can be larger, containing more nutrients for the seedling seeds passing through the gut are deposited in faecal material which acts as a fertiliser, and passage through the intestine may stimulate germination", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"BIOV-07\" data-qid=\"BIOV-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/biodiversity-value.html\">Importance of biodiversity: resources &amp; ecosystem services</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.1.3 &middot; Genn pp. 18-19 &middot; BIOV-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Explain why the ecosystem services provided by other species are often given a low conservation priority.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Explain how soil organisms provide services that humans depend on.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Explain why pollination by insects is more useful than wind pollination to plants growing in forests.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>Explain how seed dispersal by animals increases the survival of a plant's offspring.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The processes (eg regulation of atmospheric gases by photosynthesis and respiration) act in a dynamic equilibrium and cancel each other out</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Because there is no overall change their actions go unnoticed / their importance is not appreciated until they are lost</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Invertebrates, fungi and bacteria break down / decompose dead organic matter, releasing nutrients for plant growth</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The organic matter and humus produced hold the soil together (reducing erosion) and regulate the water cycle, giving more even river flow and less flooding</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Organic acids from decomposition aid the weathering of rock, producing more soil and releasing more nutrients</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Microbes carry out the processes of the carbon, nitrogen and phosphorus cycles so wastes do not build up and nutrients are not depleted</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Trees reduce wind velocity so wind pollination would be ineffective / unreliable over long distances</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Insects search over long distances for flowers so dispersed populations can be pollinated, and less pollen needs to be produced (energy saving); specialised flowers reduce pollen being wasted on other species</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Dispersal is not haphazard: animals live in the same habitat as the plant so are likely to carry seeds to locations where they can survive</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Seeds can be carried longer distances than by wind and can be larger, containing more nutrients for the seedling</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Seeds passing through the gut are deposited in faecal material which acts as a fertiliser, and passage through the intestine may stimulate germination</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 18-19; AQA spec 3.1.2.1.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "BIOV-08", "sub": "biodiversity-value", "subname": "Importance of biodiversity: resources & ecosystem services", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 15, "flags": ["data", "ext"], "spec": "3.1.2.1", "pages": "10-19", "url": "subtopic/biodiversity-value.html#BIOV-08", "snippet": "Table 1 shows some medicines that were discovered in wild species.", "text": "table 1 shows some medicines that were discovered in wild species. explain why many plants produce chemicals that turn out to be useful medicines. explain why the medicines in table 1 provide an argument for conserving whole habitats rather than individual species. describe how one named species is being studied to help solve a human health problem other than by providing a medicine. discuss the extent to which the resources and ecosystem services provided by other species justify the conservation of biodiversity. plants produce chemicals (often alkaloids) that are toxic to the herbivores that would eat them in carefully controlled amounts these chemicals have beneficial physiological effects in humans only a very small proportion of species have been studied for medicinal substances, so it is not known which species will be useful conserving the habitat protects all the species in it, including those not yet studied and the species they depend on marsupials (kangaroo / wallaby): young develop in the pouch so are easier to study than a human foetus - developmental problems hippopotamus: hipposudoric acid skin secretion (sunscreen / antimicrobial) - burns treatment marine sponges: proteins preventing graft rejection - organ transplants resources: wood, fibres, oils, fuels, food; new food species adapted to local conditions (morama bean, yeheb, perennial maize, kernza, bison, eland) biomimetics: whale flipper tubercles, shark skin, termite-mound ventilation, bird bones, gecko adhesion, burrs / velcro, lotus self-cleaning, spider silk medicines and physiological research; genetic resources - crop wild relatives, vavilov centres, gene pools, inbreeding ecosystem services: atmospheric composition, hydrological cycle, biogeochemical cycles, soil maintenance; interspecies relationships - food, pollination, seed dispersal, habitat provision evaluation: services go unnoticed because of dynamic equilibrium; unknown future value; economic vs moral / intrinsic arguments; conservation also needed for aesthetic and cultural reasons justified conclusion", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"BIOV-08\" data-qid=\"BIOV-08\" data-marks=\"15\" data-flags=\"data ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 8</span><span class=\"meta\">15 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/biodiversity-value.html\">Importance of biodiversity: resources &amp; ecosystem services</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 10-19 &middot; BIOV-08</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows some medicines that were discovered in wild species.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Medicine</th><th>Source species</th><th>Use</th></tr><tr><td>Taxol</td><td>Yew tree</td><td>Cancers of the breast, ovary, lung, bladder and prostate</td></tr><tr><td>Quinine</td><td>Cinchona tree</td><td>Malaria</td></tr><tr><td>AZT</td><td>Caribbean marine sponge</td><td>HIV / AIDS</td></tr><tr><td>Diosgenin (steroids)</td><td>Mexican yam</td><td>Contraceptive pill, cortisone</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.1</span>Explain why many plants produce chemicals that turn out to be useful medicines.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.2</span>Explain why the medicines in Table 1 provide an argument for conserving whole habitats rather than individual species.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.3</span>Describe how <b>one</b> named species is being studied to help solve a human health problem other than by providing a medicine.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.4</span>Discuss the extent to which the resources and ecosystem services provided by other species justify the conservation of biodiversity.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Plants produce chemicals (often alkaloids) that are toxic to the herbivores that would eat them</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In carefully controlled amounts these chemicals have beneficial physiological effects in humans</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Only a very small proportion of species have been studied for medicinal substances, so it is not known which species will be useful</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Conserving the habitat protects all the species in it, including those not yet studied and the species they depend on</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Marsupials (kangaroo / wallaby): young develop in the pouch so are easier to study than a human foetus - developmental problems</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hippopotamus: hipposudoric acid skin secretion (sunscreen / antimicrobial) - burns treatment</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Marine sponges: proteins preventing graft rejection - organ transplants</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Resources: wood, fibres, oils, fuels, food; new food species adapted to local conditions (morama bean, yeheb, perennial maize, Kernza, bison, eland)</li><li>Biomimetics: whale flipper tubercles, shark skin, termite-mound ventilation, bird bones, gecko adhesion, burrs / Velcro, lotus self-cleaning, spider silk</li><li>Medicines and physiological research; genetic resources - crop wild relatives, Vavilov centres, gene pools, inbreeding</li><li>Ecosystem services: atmospheric composition, hydrological cycle, biogeochemical cycles, soil maintenance; interspecies relationships - food, pollination, seed dispersal, habitat provision</li><li>Evaluation: services go unnoticed because of dynamic equilibrium; unknown future value; economic vs moral / intrinsic arguments; conservation also needed for aesthetic and cultural reasons</li><li>Justified conclusion</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 10-19; AQA spec 3.1.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SUS-01", "sub": "dynamic-equilibria", "subname": "Dynamic equilibria, feedback & tipping points", "topic": "Sustainability", "tslug": "sustainability", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.6.1", "pages": "370-372", "url": "subtopic/dynamic-equilibria.html#SUS-01", "snippet": "State the Brundtland Commission (1987) definition of sustainable development.", "text": " state the brundtland commission (1987) definition of sustainable development. explain why activities such as slash-and-burn agriculture, fishing and sewage discharge can be sustainable with a small human population but not with a large one. explain why urban populations are often less aware of unsustainable exploitation than rural communities. describe two negative feedback mechanisms in natural systems and explain how human responses can override them. development that meets the needs of the present generation without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs impact depends on total population x per capita impact with few people, natural processes (forest regrowth, fish recruitment, aerobic decomposition / dilution) recover faster than the resource is used or the waste added larger populations exceed the rate of natural recovery: forest and soil do not regenerate, catches exceed msy, water deoxygenates urban areas are supported by resources brought in from elsewhere; when one area is depleted, exploitation simply moves (timber, fish, minerals, energy) so consumers see no shortage rural communities exploit local resources directly so the effects of over-exploitation become obvious quickly, encouraging cultural limits to need climate: warming increases evaporation, cloud and albedo; higher co2 increases photosynthesis and sequestration; hydrological cycle: more evaporation gives more precipitation; populations: density-dependent factors return numbers to carrying capacity in agriculture soil degradation, water shortage and loss of predators would reduce yields (negative feedback limiting the activity) humans respond with fertilisers, irrigation and pesticides to maintain yields, further damaging the natural systems that support production", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SUS-01\" data-qid=\"SUS-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/dynamic-equilibria.html\">Dynamic equilibria, feedback &amp; tipping points</a> &middot; spec 3.6.1 &middot; Genn pp. 370-372 &middot; SUS-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>State the Brundtland Commission (1987) definition of sustainable development.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain why activities such as slash-and-burn agriculture, fishing and sewage discharge can be sustainable with a small human population but not with a large one.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain why urban populations are often less aware of unsustainable exploitation than rural communities.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Describe <b>two</b> negative feedback mechanisms in natural systems and explain how human responses can override them.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Development that meets the needs of the present generation without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Impact depends on total population x per capita impact</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>With few people, natural processes (forest regrowth, fish recruitment, aerobic decomposition / dilution) recover faster than the resource is used or the waste added</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Larger populations exceed the rate of natural recovery: forest and soil do not regenerate, catches exceed MSY, water deoxygenates</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Urban areas are supported by resources brought in from elsewhere; when one area is depleted, exploitation simply moves (timber, fish, minerals, energy) so consumers see no shortage</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Rural communities exploit local resources directly so the effects of over-exploitation become obvious quickly, encouraging cultural limits to need</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 4 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Climate: warming increases evaporation, cloud and albedo; higher CO2 increases photosynthesis and sequestration; hydrological cycle: more evaporation gives more precipitation; populations: density-dependent factors return numbers to carrying capacity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>In agriculture soil degradation, water shortage and loss of predators would reduce yields (negative feedback limiting the activity)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Humans respond with fertilisers, irrigation and pesticides to maintain yields, further damaging the natural systems that support production</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 370-372; AQA spec 3.6.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SUS-02", "sub": "dynamic-equilibria", "subname": "Dynamic equilibria, feedback & tipping points", "topic": "Sustainability", "tslug": "sustainability", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.6.1", "pages": "371-372", "url": "subtopic/dynamic-equilibria.html#SUS-02", "snippet": "Explain why natural systems regulated by negative feedback rarely reach tipping points, and give an example of a natural tipping point in the geological past.", "text": " explain why natural systems regulated by negative feedback rarely reach tipping points, and give an example of a natural tipping point in the geological past. explain why diverse systems are more resistant to change than simple systems, using a natural and a human example. give three positive feedback mechanisms by which human-induced warming may become self-sustaining. negative feedback responds to any change by re-establishing the previous equilibrium only large unusual events overwhelm the feedback so a new equilibrium forms example: methane hydrate release at the end of the permian causing climate change and mass extinction in a diverse system each component is relatively less important so the loss / change of one has a small effect; alternative food sources and relationships buffer the change natural: coral reefs and tropical rainforests with complex food webs are stable; simple polar / desert systems fluctuate human: agroecosystems focus on a few crops and remove detritivores, pollinators and predators so are vulnerable to pests, disease, weather diverse technical systems: a society using a range of renewable energy resources is more secure than one relying on a single source melting permafrost releasing methane reduced albedo as ice / snow melt methane hydrate release forest and peat fires faster soil decomposition ocean acidification reducing sequestration cirrus cloud formation", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SUS-02\" data-qid=\"SUS-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/dynamic-equilibria.html\">Dynamic equilibria, feedback &amp; tipping points</a> &middot; spec 3.6.1 &middot; Genn pp. 371-372 &middot; SUS-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain why natural systems regulated by negative feedback rarely reach tipping points, and give an example of a natural tipping point in the geological past.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain why diverse systems are more resistant to change than simple systems, using a natural and a human example.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Give <b>three</b> positive feedback mechanisms by which human-induced warming may become self-sustaining.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Negative feedback responds to any change by re-establishing the previous equilibrium</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Only large unusual events overwhelm the feedback so a new equilibrium forms</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Example: methane hydrate release at the end of the Permian causing climate change and mass extinction</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In a diverse system each component is relatively less important so the loss / change of one has a small effect; alternative food sources and relationships buffer the change</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Natural: coral reefs and tropical rainforests with complex food webs are stable; simple polar / desert systems fluctuate</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Human: agroecosystems focus on a few crops and remove detritivores, pollinators and predators so are vulnerable to pests, disease, weather</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Diverse technical systems: a society using a range of renewable energy resources is more secure than one relying on a single source</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Melting permafrost releasing methane</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduced albedo as ice / snow melt</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Methane hydrate release</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Forest and peat fires</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Faster soil decomposition</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ocean acidification reducing sequestration</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cirrus cloud formation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 371-372; AQA spec 3.6.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SUS-10", "sub": "dynamic-equilibria", "subname": "Dynamic equilibria, feedback & tipping points", "topic": "Sustainability", "tslug": "sustainability", "papers": [2], "marks": 5, "flags": [], "spec": "3.6.1", "pages": "370", "url": "subtopic/dynamic-equilibria.html#SUS-10", "snippet": "Explain the difference between approaches to sustainable lifestyles based on moral belief and on enlightened self-interest.", "text": " explain the difference between approaches to sustainable lifestyles based on moral belief and on enlightened self-interest. explain why the overall environmental impact of humans depends on two main factors. give one activity that is sustainable with a small population but unsustainable if the population is too large. moral belief: protecting the environment is the 'right' thing to do regardless of benefit to people enlightened self-interest: protecting the environment is done because it causes fewer difficulties (for people) than not doing so total impact = population size x the individual (per capita) impact of each person per capita impact rises with affluence / access to energy, so both a larger population and a more affluent population increase the total impact slash and burn agriculture fishing discharge of sewage / biodegradable pollutants", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SUS-10\" data-qid=\"SUS-10\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/dynamic-equilibria.html\">Dynamic equilibria, feedback &amp; tipping points</a> &middot; spec 3.6.1 &middot; Genn pp. 370 &middot; SUS-10</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Explain the difference between approaches to sustainable lifestyles based on <b>moral belief</b> and on <b>enlightened self-interest</b>.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain why the overall environmental impact of humans depends on two main factors.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Give <b>one</b> activity that is sustainable with a small population but unsustainable if the population is too large.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Moral belief: protecting the environment is the 'right' thing to do regardless of benefit to people</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Enlightened self-interest: protecting the environment is done because it causes fewer difficulties (for people) than not doing so</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Total impact = population size x the individual (per capita) impact of each person</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Per capita impact rises with affluence / access to energy, so both a larger population and a more affluent population increase the total impact</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 1 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 1.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Slash and burn agriculture</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fishing</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Discharge of sewage / biodegradable pollutants</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 370; AQA spec 3.6.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SUS-11", "sub": "dynamic-equilibria", "subname": "Dynamic equilibria, feedback & tipping points", "topic": "Sustainability", "tslug": "sustainability", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.6.1", "pages": "371-372", "url": "subtopic/dynamic-equilibria.html#SUS-11", "snippet": "Table 1 lists some feedback mechanisms that affect the Earth's climate.", "text": "table 1 lists some feedback mechanisms that affect the earth's climate. complete table 1 by stating whether each mechanism is an example of negative or positive feedback. explain why natural systems regulated by negative feedback rarely reach tipping points. using an example, explain how a large unusual event can cause a tipping point. explain how the human response to negative feedback in agriculture can make food production less sustainable. negative positive negative positive the response to a change acts to re-establish / return the system to the previous equilibrium so the change does not become self-sustaining unless a large unusual event overwhelms the negative feedback eg the possible release of methane hydrate at the end of the permian era overwhelmed the negative feedback mechanisms, causing climate change and a mass extinction / a new equilibrium soil degradation, reduced water availability and loss of natural pest predators would reduce yields (negative feedback curtailing the activity) instead of reducing the activity, farmers use fertilisers, irrigation and pesticides, which further damage the natural systems that support food production", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SUS-11\" data-qid=\"SUS-11\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/dynamic-equilibria.html\">Dynamic equilibria, feedback &amp; tipping points</a> &middot; spec 3.6.1 &middot; Genn pp. 371-372 &middot; SUS-11</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 lists some feedback mechanisms that affect the Earth's climate.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Complete Table 1 by stating whether each mechanism is an example of <b>negative</b> or <b>positive</b> feedback.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Type of feedback</th></tr><tr><td>Higher temperatures increase cloud cover, raising albedo</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Melting permafrost releases methane</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Higher CO<sub>2</sub> levels increase photosynthesis and carbon sequestration</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Melting ice reduces albedo</td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain why natural systems regulated by negative feedback rarely reach tipping points.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Using an example, explain how a large unusual event can cause a tipping point.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Explain how the human response to negative feedback in agriculture can make food production less sustainable.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Negative</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Positive</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Negative</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Positive</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The response to a change acts to re-establish / return the system to the previous equilibrium</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So the change does not become self-sustaining unless a large unusual event overwhelms the negative feedback</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>eg the possible release of methane hydrate at the end of the Permian era</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Overwhelmed the negative feedback mechanisms, causing climate change and a mass extinction / a new equilibrium</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Soil degradation, reduced water availability and loss of natural pest predators would reduce yields (negative feedback curtailing the activity)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Instead of reducing the activity, farmers use fertilisers, irrigation and pesticides, which further damage the natural systems that support food production</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 371-372; AQA spec 3.6.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SUS-12", "sub": "dynamic-equilibria", "subname": "Dynamic equilibria, feedback & tipping points", "topic": "Sustainability", "tslug": "sustainability", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.6.1", "pages": "370-371", "url": "subtopic/dynamic-equilibria.html#SUS-12", "snippet": "Explain three reasons why the development of new human activities has rarely been restricted by their environmental impacts.", "text": " explain three reasons why the development of new human activities has rarely been restricted by their environmental impacts. explain why sedentary rural communities often exploit resources sustainably. explain why urban populations are often unaware that the resources they use are being depleted. explain why the growth of affluence has increased per capita environmental impacts. the new opportunity is overwhelmingly attractive (economic benefit) lack of knowledge / understanding of the impacts the impacts occur elsewhere or much later, so are not evident to those who benefit cultural belief that resources should be exploited to satisfy needs only problems of over-exploitation become obvious quickly as local supplies dwindle, so behaviour changes resources are brought in from other areas if one area is depleted, exploitation moves to another area (eg timber, fish, metals, energy) without the consumers noticing easier access to energy has made many new activities possible the scale of activities has greatly increased toxic wastes are produced that cannot be decontaminated by natural processes", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SUS-12\" data-qid=\"SUS-12\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/dynamic-equilibria.html\">Dynamic equilibria, feedback &amp; tipping points</a> &middot; spec 3.6.1 &middot; Genn pp. 370-371 &middot; SUS-12</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Explain <b>three</b> reasons why the development of new human activities has rarely been restricted by their environmental impacts.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain why sedentary rural communities often exploit resources sustainably.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain why urban populations are often unaware that the resources they use are being depleted.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Explain why the growth of affluence has increased per capita environmental impacts.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The new opportunity is overwhelmingly attractive (economic benefit)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lack of knowledge / understanding of the impacts</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The impacts occur elsewhere or much later, so are not evident to those who benefit</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cultural belief that resources should be exploited to satisfy needs only</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Problems of over-exploitation become obvious quickly as local supplies dwindle, so behaviour changes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Resources are brought in from other areas</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>If one area is depleted, exploitation moves to another area (eg timber, fish, metals, energy) without the consumers noticing</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Easier access to energy has made many new activities possible</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The scale of activities has greatly increased</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Toxic wastes are produced that cannot be decontaminated by natural processes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 370-371; AQA spec 3.6.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SUS-16", "sub": "dynamic-equilibria", "subname": "Dynamic equilibria, feedback & tipping points", "topic": "Sustainability", "tslug": "sustainability", "papers": [2], "marks": 15, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.6.1-3.6.3", "pages": "370-374", "url": "subtopic/dynamic-equilibria.html#SUS-16", "snippet": "State the Brundtland Commission definition of sustainable development.", "text": " state the brundtland commission definition of sustainable development. explain the difference between a negative feedback mechanism and a positive feedback mechanism, giving one example of each in the climate system. explain why a diverse ecosystem is more likely to resist change than an agroecosystem. evaluate the extent to which copying the principles of natural systems could make human activities more sustainable. development that meets the needs of the present generation without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs negative feedback resists change / returns the system towards its previous state eg increased temperature increases cloud cover and albedo, or higher co2 increases photosynthesis positive feedback increases the change / makes it self-sustaining eg melting permafrost releasing methane, declining albedo as ice melts, forest and peat fires many species with a complex food web so the loss of one has a small effect; agroecosystems have few species and lack detritivores, pollinators and pest predators that maintain stability principles of natural systems: dynamic equilibria and negative feedback; diverse systems resistant to change; low energy-density renewable energy (solar-driven hydrological, carbon and nitrogen cycles); low-temperature processes using enzymes; material cycles with wastes as raw materials; small number of abundant elements; biodegradable non-toxic wastes human systems: non-renewable energy, high temperatures (haber process, incineration, manufacturing), linear resource use, dispersal of minerals and mixtures that are hard to separate, persistent toxins (pcbs, organochlorines, toxic metals in electronics) applications: renewable energy and heat pumps, bioleaching, recycling and design for re-use, industrial symbiosis, organic and integrated agriculture, retaining hedgerows and pollinators, circular economy human responses that override feedback (fertilisers, irrigation, pesticides) and positive feedback triggered by human activity (permafrost, methane hydrates, fires); tipping points barriers: population size and affluence, attitudes of urban populations to distant resources, economics, need for international cooperation, existing infrastructure justified conclusion on how far natural principles can be applied", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SUS-16\" data-qid=\"SUS-16\" data-marks=\"15\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">15 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/dynamic-equilibria.html\">Dynamic equilibria, feedback &amp; tipping points</a> &middot; spec 3.6.1-3.6.3 &middot; Genn pp. 370-374 &middot; SUS-16</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>State the Brundtland Commission definition of sustainable development.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain the difference between a negative feedback mechanism and a positive feedback mechanism, giving <b>one</b> example of each in the climate system.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain why a diverse ecosystem is more likely to resist change than an agroecosystem.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Evaluate the extent to which copying the principles of natural systems could make human activities more sustainable.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Development that meets the needs of the present generation without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Negative feedback resists change / returns the system towards its previous state</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>eg increased temperature increases cloud cover and albedo, or higher CO<sub>2</sub> increases photosynthesis</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Positive feedback increases the change / makes it self-sustaining</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>eg melting permafrost releasing methane, declining albedo as ice melts, forest and peat fires</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Many species with a complex food web so the loss of one has a small effect; agroecosystems have few species and lack detritivores, pollinators and pest predators that maintain stability</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Principles of natural systems: dynamic equilibria and negative feedback; diverse systems resistant to change; low energy-density renewable energy (solar-driven hydrological, carbon and nitrogen cycles); low-temperature processes using enzymes; material cycles with wastes as raw materials; small number of abundant elements; biodegradable non-toxic wastes</li><li>Human systems: non-renewable energy, high temperatures (Haber process, incineration, manufacturing), linear resource use, dispersal of minerals and mixtures that are hard to separate, persistent toxins (PCBs, organochlorines, toxic metals in electronics)</li><li>Applications: renewable energy and heat pumps, bioleaching, recycling and design for re-use, industrial symbiosis, organic and integrated agriculture, retaining hedgerows and pollinators, circular economy</li><li>Human responses that override feedback (fertilisers, irrigation, pesticides) and positive feedback triggered by human activity (permafrost, methane hydrates, fires); tipping points</li><li>Barriers: population size and affluence, attitudes of urban populations to distant resources, economics, need for international cooperation, existing infrastructure</li><li>Justified conclusion on how far natural principles can be applied</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 370-374; AQA spec 3.6.1-3.6.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MON-01", "sub": "eco-monitoring", "subname": "Ecological monitoring & new technologies", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.5", "pages": "393-394, 400-402", "url": "subtopic/eco-monitoring.html#MON-01", "snippet": "A conservation team is planning to protect a population of an endangered antelope in a reserve.", "text": "a conservation team is planning to protect a population of an endangered antelope in a reserve. explain why each of the following features of the population should be measured before a conservation strategy is decided. describe how the population size of the antelope could be estimated. size: shows whether the population is viable / how urgent action is; sets a baseline to judge success; needed to estimate carrying capacity and sustainable off-take distribution: shows which habitats / areas are used and whether the population is fragmented into non-viable isolated groups needing corridors; identifies key areas to protect survival rate (mortality): identifies the life stage and causes of death (predation, disease, poaching) so management targets the limiting factor age structure: many young indicates a growing population; few young / ageing population indicates breeding failure or high juvenile mortality even if adults are numerous; k-selected species need breeding adults protected direct counts from vehicles / aircraft / drones along transects, or camera traps with individual recognition sub-sample counts in known areas scaled up to the whole reserve; mark-release-recapture (lincoln index) for smaller species", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MON-01\" data-qid=\"MON-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/eco-monitoring.html\">Ecological monitoring &amp; new technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.5 &middot; Genn pp. 393-394, 400-402 &middot; MON-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A conservation team is planning to protect a population of an endangered antelope in a reserve.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[8 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Explain why each of the following features of the population should be measured before a conservation strategy is decided.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Describe how the population size of the antelope could be estimated.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">8 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Size: shows whether the population is viable / how urgent action is; sets a baseline to judge success; needed to estimate carrying capacity and sustainable off-take</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Distribution: shows which habitats / areas are used and whether the population is fragmented into non-viable isolated groups needing corridors; identifies key areas to protect</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Survival rate (mortality): identifies the life stage and causes of death (predation, disease, poaching) so management targets the limiting factor</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Age structure: many young indicates a growing population; few young / ageing population indicates breeding failure or high juvenile mortality even if adults are numerous; k-selected species need breeding adults protected</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Direct counts from vehicles / aircraft / drones along transects, or camera traps with individual recognition</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sub-sample counts in known areas scaled up to the whole reserve; mark-release-recapture (Lincoln index) for smaller species</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 393-394, 400-402; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.5.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MON-02", "sub": "eco-monitoring", "subname": "Ecological monitoring & new technologies", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.6", "pages": "402-406", "url": "subtopic/eco-monitoring.html#MON-02", "snippet": "Explain how each of the following new technologies improves the validity of ecological research.", "text": " explain how each of the following new technologies improves the validity of ecological research. explain why new technologies allow the collection of 'more representative' data. tracking: continuous data on movements, territory size, migration routes and habitat use without disturbance, over large areas and long periods; acoustic tags in seawater; geolocators for small birds; more representative than occasional sightings dna: identifies individuals, gene pool size / inbreeding and regional populations (tracing ivory, timber, fish); edna detects rare or hidden aquatic species (great crested newt, invasive carp) from shed cells without capture image recognition: software identifies individuals from unique markings (tiger stripes, whale shark spots, fin damage) in camera-trap / drone / satellite images, giving population size, lifespan, social structure from large image sets quickly and objectively acoustic: bat detectors, hydrophones and sonograms record species that are hard to see (bats, cetaceans, insects), giving presence, abundance and activity continuously, day and night data collected continuously / over large areas / at night rather than at a few times and places a researcher can visit less disturbance so animals behave naturally; larger sample sizes; objective rather than subjective identification", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MON-02\" data-qid=\"MON-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/eco-monitoring.html\">Ecological monitoring &amp; new technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.6 &middot; Genn pp. 402-406 &middot; MON-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[8 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain how each of the following new technologies improves the validity of ecological research.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain why new technologies allow the collection of 'more representative' data.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">8 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Tracking: continuous data on movements, territory size, migration routes and habitat use without disturbance, over large areas and long periods; acoustic tags in seawater; geolocators for small birds; more representative than occasional sightings</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>DNA: identifies individuals, gene pool size / inbreeding and regional populations (tracing ivory, timber, fish); eDNA detects rare or hidden aquatic species (great crested newt, invasive carp) from shed cells without capture</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Image recognition: software identifies individuals from unique markings (tiger stripes, whale shark spots, fin damage) in camera-trap / drone / satellite images, giving population size, lifespan, social structure from large image sets quickly and objectively</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Acoustic: bat detectors, hydrophones and sonograms record species that are hard to see (bats, cetaceans, insects), giving presence, abundance and activity continuously, day and night</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Data collected continuously / over large areas / at night rather than at a few times and places a researcher can visit</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Less disturbance so animals behave naturally; larger sample sizes; objective rather than subjective identification</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 402-406; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.6.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MON-03", "sub": "eco-monitoring", "subname": "Ecological monitoring & new technologies", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.1.2.3.5-3.1.2.3.6", "pages": "393-394, 402-407", "url": "subtopic/eco-monitoring.html#MON-03", "snippet": "Which of the following would be most suitable for monitoring the movements of a whale over several months? Tick one box.", "text": " which of the following would be most suitable for monitoring the movements of a whale over several months? tick one box. explain why post-release monitoring is important in a captive breeding and release programme and describe two suitable methods. b - radio waves do not travel through seawater; acoustic or satellite tags transmit position shows survival, dispersal and breeding of released individuals so success can be assessed and future releases improved radio / gps tags or collars; leg rings / wing tags on birds; camera traps; dna from droppings to confirm identity and diet", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MON-03\" data-qid=\"MON-03\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/eco-monitoring.html\">Ecological monitoring &amp; new technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.5-3.1.2.3.6 &middot; Genn pp. 393-394, 402-407 &middot; MON-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Which of the following would be most suitable for monitoring the movements of a whale over several months? Tick one box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; Radio collar</li><li>B&nbsp; Acoustic / satellite tag</li><li>C&nbsp; Pitfall trap</li><li>D&nbsp; Geolocator tag recovered by recapture</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain why post-release monitoring is important in a captive breeding and release programme and describe <b>two</b> suitable methods.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b></b>&nbsp; B - radio waves do not travel through seawater; acoustic or satellite tags transmit position</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Shows survival, dispersal and breeding of released individuals so success can be assessed and future releases improved</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Radio / GPS tags or collars; leg rings / wing tags on birds; camera traps; DNA from droppings to confirm identity and diet</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 393-394, 402-407; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.5-3.1.2.3.6.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MON-04", "sub": "eco-monitoring", "subname": "Ecological monitoring & new technologies", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.1.2.3.6", "pages": "404-405", "url": "subtopic/eco-monitoring.html#MON-04", "snippet": "Which technique would be most suitable for detecting whether great crested newts are present in a pond without catching them? Tick one box.", "text": " which technique would be most suitable for detecting whether great crested newts are present in a pond without catching them? tick one box. explain why geolocator tags, rather than radio or gps transmitters, are used to track the migrations of small birds. explain how dna databases could help to control the illegal trade in elephant ivory. environmental dna (edna) analysis transmitters are too heavy for small birds / their batteries would not last for a long-term study geolocator tags are small and only record time and light levels; latitude and longitude for each day are worked out when the bird is recaptured and the data downloaded each separate (regional) population has its own unique gene pool dna from seized ivory can be compared with the database to identify where the elephant came from, so poaching hotspots / trade routes can be targeted", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MON-04\" data-qid=\"MON-04\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/eco-monitoring.html\">Ecological monitoring &amp; new technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.6 &middot; Genn pp. 404-405 &middot; MON-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Which technique would be most suitable for detecting whether great crested newts are present in a pond without catching them? Tick <b>one</b> box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; Environmental DNA (eDNA) analysis</li><li>B&nbsp; Radio tracking</li><li>C&nbsp; Lincoln index (mark-release-recapture)</li><li>D&nbsp; Leg rings</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain why geolocator tags, rather than radio or GPS transmitters, are used to track the migrations of small birds.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain how DNA databases could help to control the illegal trade in elephant ivory.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b>A</b>&nbsp; Environmental DNA (eDNA) analysis</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Transmitters are too heavy for small birds / their batteries would not last for a long-term study</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Geolocator tags are small and only record time and light levels; latitude and longitude for each day are worked out when the bird is recaptured and the data downloaded</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Each separate (regional) population has its own unique gene pool</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>DNA from seized ivory can be compared with the database to identify where the elephant came from, so poaching hotspots / trade routes can be targeted</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 404-405; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.6.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MON-05", "sub": "eco-monitoring", "subname": "Ecological monitoring & new technologies", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.1.2.3.6", "pages": "402-403, 406-407", "url": "subtopic/eco-monitoring.html#MON-05", "snippet": "Complete Table 1 to show the environmental information that can be collected by satellite sensors detecting each type of radiation.", "text": " complete table 1 to show the environmental information that can be collected by satellite sensors detecting each type of radiation. explain why radar surveys are not interrupted by cloudy weather. explain two advantages of satellite surveys over ground surveys for monitoring deforestation. describe how motion-sensitive cameras and image databases could be used together to estimate the size of a tiger population. images / maps of large areas; land-use change, deforestation, flooding vegetation density and surface temperature wind velocity, sea-surface altitude, wave height, oil pollution microwaves pass through clouds (unlike visible light) very large / remote areas can be surveyed quickly and repeatedly so changes over time are detected no need for researchers to visit inaccessible areas, reducing cost and disturbance; data are standardised across the whole area motion-sensitive cameras placed along trails record animals passing, using visible light by day and infrared at night each tiger has a unique pattern of facial / body stripes images are compared with the database so individuals are recognised rather than counted twice the number of different individuals recorded gives a minimum population; repeated captures give territory size, movements, lifespans and social groupings limitation: only works for species with unique visible features", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MON-05\" data-qid=\"MON-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/eco-monitoring.html\">Ecological monitoring &amp; new technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.6 &middot; Genn pp. 402-403, 406-407 &middot; MON-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Complete Table 1 to show the environmental information that can be collected by satellite sensors detecting each type of radiation.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Radiation detected</th><th>Environmental information collected</th></tr><tr><td>Reflected visible light</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Infrared emitted by the Earth's surface</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Reflected radar microwaves emitted by the satellite</td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain why radar surveys are not interrupted by cloudy weather.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain <b>two</b> advantages of satellite surveys over ground surveys for monitoring deforestation.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Describe how motion-sensitive cameras and image databases could be used together to estimate the size of a tiger population.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Images / maps of large areas; land-use change, deforestation, flooding</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Vegetation density and surface temperature</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wind velocity, sea-surface altitude, wave height, oil pollution</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Microwaves pass through clouds (unlike visible light)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Very large / remote areas can be surveyed quickly and repeatedly so changes over time are detected</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No need for researchers to visit inaccessible areas, reducing cost and disturbance; data are standardised across the whole area</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Motion-sensitive cameras placed along trails record animals passing, using visible light by day and infrared at night</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Each tiger has a unique pattern of facial / body stripes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Images are compared with the database so individuals are recognised rather than counted twice</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The number of different individuals recorded gives a minimum population; repeated captures give territory size, movements, lifespans and social groupings</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Limitation: only works for species with unique visible features</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 402-403, 406-407; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.6.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MON-06", "sub": "eco-monitoring", "subname": "Ecological monitoring & new technologies", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.5-3.1.2.3.6", "pages": "402, 405, 407", "url": "subtopic/eco-monitoring.html#MON-06", "snippet": "Explain why an electronic meter used to measure dissolved oxygen must be calibrated, and what should be done if the reading cannot be adjusted.", "text": " explain why an electronic meter used to measure dissolved oxygen must be calibrated, and what should be done if the reading cannot be adjusted. give three types of indirect evidence of the presence of an animal and the information each can provide. explain how auditory monitoring can be used to study bats. explain three assumptions of the mark-release-recapture method that may not be met. its reading is adjusted to match a meter known to be accurate so that the results are accurate / comparable if it cannot be adjusted the percentage error should be recorded so the results can be corrected droppings: diet, gender, territory size owl pellets: diet feeding marks (eg chewed hazel nuts): presence of species such as dormice nests / burrows: presence and breeding tracks / footprints: presence, movements territorial marks (scratching posts): presence and territory bat detectors / sonograms record the ultrasonic sounds bats produce, which differ between species the recordings provide evidence of presence, abundance and activity of species that cannot easily be seen marked individuals mix freely with the rest of the population marks do not fall off or wear away, and marking does not affect survival the population does not change during the study (no births, deaths or migration) all individuals have an equal chance of being caught / animals are mobile and can be caught", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MON-06\" data-qid=\"MON-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/eco-monitoring.html\">Ecological monitoring &amp; new technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.5-3.1.2.3.6 &middot; Genn pp. 402, 405, 407 &middot; MON-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Explain why an electronic meter used to measure dissolved oxygen must be calibrated, and what should be done if the reading cannot be adjusted.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Give <b>three</b> types of indirect evidence of the presence of an animal and the information each can provide.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain how auditory monitoring can be used to study bats.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Explain <b>three</b> assumptions of the mark-release-recapture method that may not be met.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Its reading is adjusted to match a meter known to be accurate so that the results are accurate / comparable</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>If it cannot be adjusted the percentage error should be recorded so the results can be corrected</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Droppings: diet, gender, territory size</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Owl pellets: diet</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Feeding marks (eg chewed hazel nuts): presence of species such as dormice</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nests / burrows: presence and breeding</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Tracks / footprints: presence, movements</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Territorial marks (scratching posts): presence and territory</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bat detectors / sonograms record the ultrasonic sounds bats produce, which differ between species</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The recordings provide evidence of presence, abundance and activity of species that cannot easily be seen</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Marked individuals mix freely with the rest of the population</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Marks do not fall off or wear away, and marking does not affect survival</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The population does not change during the study (no births, deaths or migration)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>All individuals have an equal chance of being caught / animals are mobile and can be caught</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 402, 405, 407; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.5-3.1.2.3.6.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MON-07", "sub": "eco-monitoring", "subname": "Ecological monitoring & new technologies", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 15, "flags": ["calc", "data", "ext"], "spec": "3.1.2.3.5-3.1.2.3.6", "pages": "402-407", "url": "subtopic/eco-monitoring.html#MON-07", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the number of adult great crested newts caught in bottle traps in a pond over five years, and the number of ponds in the area in which the species was detected using ", "text": "table 1 shows the number of adult great crested newts caught in bottle traps in a pond over five years, and the number of ponds in the area in which the species was detected using environmental dna (edna). calculate the percentage increase in the number of ponds where newts were detected between 2019 and 2023. explain how edna detects the presence of newts without catching them. suggest why trap catches are a less reliable measure of population change than the edna survey. evaluate the contribution of new technologies to ecological monitoring and wildlife conservation. (15 - 6) / 6 x 100 = 150% cells shed by the newts into the water contain their dna water samples are analysed for dna sequences unique to the species catches depend on trapping effort, weather and newt activity on the night, so they vary from year to year trapping only samples one pond and one part of the population, whereas edna surveys all 20 ponds with a standard method image databases of unique markings (tiger stripes, whale shark spots, dolphin fins) giving territory size, movements, lifespan and social groups; limited to species with unique features motion-sensitive and infrared cameras, cctv of nests with less disturbance; marking (rings, wing tags) dna: edna to detect presence (great crested newts, invasive carp); dna databases to identify regional populations and trace timber, ivory and fish auditory monitoring: bat detectors and sonograms for presence, abundance and activity position monitoring: radio, gps and acoustic transmitters; geolocator tags for small birds; data recorders; carrier systems - rovs / drones, auvs, balloons, aircraft, satellites, animals satellites: visible light for land use and deforestation, infrared for vegetation and temperature, radar for wind, waves, sea level and oil; gps for tracking evaluation: greater coverage, less disturbance and continuous data vs cost, battery / weight limits, calibration, need for ground-truthing, ethical issues of tagging; indirect evidence and traditional methods still needed justified conclusion", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MON-07\" data-qid=\"MON-07\" data-marks=\"15\" data-flags=\"calc data ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">15 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/eco-monitoring.html\">Ecological monitoring &amp; new technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.5-3.1.2.3.6 &middot; Genn pp. 402-407 &middot; MON-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the number of adult great crested newts caught in bottle traps in a pond over five years, and the number of ponds in the area in which the species was detected using environmental DNA (eDNA).</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Year</th><th>Adults caught in traps</th><th>Ponds with newts detected by eDNA (out of 20)</th></tr><tr><td>2019</td><td>14</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>2020</td><td>22</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>18</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>2022</td><td>31</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td>2023</td><td>27</td><td>15</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Calculate the percentage increase in the number of ponds where newts were detected between 2019 and 2023.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Explain how eDNA detects the presence of newts without catching them.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Suggest why trap catches are a less reliable measure of population change than the eDNA survey.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>Evaluate the contribution of new technologies to ecological monitoring and wildlife conservation.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(15 - 6) / 6 x 100</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 150%</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cells shed by the newts into the water contain their DNA</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Water samples are analysed for DNA sequences unique to the species</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Catches depend on trapping effort, weather and newt activity on the night, so they vary from year to year</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Trapping only samples one pond and one part of the population, whereas eDNA surveys all 20 ponds with a standard method</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Image databases of unique markings (tiger stripes, whale shark spots, dolphin fins) giving territory size, movements, lifespan and social groups; limited to species with unique features</li><li>Motion-sensitive and infrared cameras, CCTV of nests with less disturbance; marking (rings, wing tags)</li><li>DNA: eDNA to detect presence (great crested newts, invasive carp); DNA databases to identify regional populations and trace timber, ivory and fish</li><li>Auditory monitoring: bat detectors and sonograms for presence, abundance and activity</li><li>Position monitoring: radio, GPS and acoustic transmitters; geolocator tags for small birds; data recorders; carrier systems - ROVs / drones, AUVs, balloons, aircraft, satellites, animals</li><li>Satellites: visible light for land use and deforestation, infrared for vegetation and temperature, radar for wind, waves, sea level and oil; GPS for tracking</li><li>Evaluation: greater coverage, less disturbance and continuous data vs cost, battery / weight limits, calibration, need for ground-truthing, ethical issues of tagging; indirect evidence and traditional methods still needed</li><li>Justified conclusion</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 402-407; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.5-3.1.2.3.6.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AIR-01", "sub": "air-pollution", "subname": "Atmospheric pollution: smoke, acid rain, NOx, hydrocarbons, CO, smogs", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.1", "pages": "245-247", "url": "subtopic/air-pollution.html#AIR-01", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the daily deaths in London and the mean concentration of smoke in the air during the smog of December 1952.", "text": "figure 1 shows the daily deaths in london and the mean concentration of smoke in the air during the smog of december 1952. describe the relationship between smoke concentration and daily deaths shown in figure 1 and table 1. calculate the percentage increase in daily deaths between 1 december and 8 december. explain how the weather conditions in december 1952 caused the smog to form. explain why most of the deaths were among the elderly, the very young and people with existing respiratory disease. state the legislation introduced in the uk in response to this smog. deaths rise as smoke concentration rises, peaking at about 930 per day on 8 dec when smoke was highest (4.5 mg m-3) deaths fall as smoke falls but remain above the starting level for several days (time lag / delayed effect) (930 - 250) / 250 x 100 = 272% anticyclonic conditions: clear skies allowed rapid radiation of heat from the ground at night so the ground and air near it cooled low wind velocity so air layers did not mix; london lies in a valley / basin where cold dense air collected: a temperature inversion formed smoke from coal fires was trapped below the inversion; moist air cooled to its dew point forming fog; smoke + fog = smog; the smog's high albedo prolonged the inversion smoke particles (pm10) are inhaled deeply and irritate the lungs / kill cilia so particles and bacteria are not cleared, causing bronchitis / infections these groups have weaker lungs / immune systems / less reserve so the extra stress (with sulfur dioxide acting synergistically) was fatal clean air act (1956)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AIR-01\" data-qid=\"AIR-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/air-pollution.html\">Atmospheric pollution: smoke, acid rain, NOx, hydrocarbons, CO, smogs</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 245-247 &middot; AIR-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the daily deaths in London and the mean concentration of smoke in the air during the smog of December 1952.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,207.4 96.9,204.9 133.8,202.4 170.8,194.8 207.7,164.8 244.6,114.7 281.5,49.6 318.5,37.0 355.4,64.6 392.3,119.7 429.2,149.8 466.2,169.8 503.1,179.8 540.0,187.3\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"207.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"96.9\" cy=\"204.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"133.8\" cy=\"202.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"170.8\" cy=\"194.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"207.7\" cy=\"164.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"244.6\" cy=\"114.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"281.5\" cy=\"49.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"318.5\" cy=\"37.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"355.4\" cy=\"64.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"392.3\" cy=\"119.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"429.2\" cy=\"149.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"466.2\" cy=\"169.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"503.1\" cy=\"179.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"187.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">200</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">6.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">399</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">8.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">599</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">11.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">798</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">14</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">998</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Date (December 1952)</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Deaths per day</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Date</th><th>3 Dec</th><th>5 Dec</th><th>7 Dec</th><th>8 Dec</th><th>10 Dec</th><th>12 Dec</th></tr><tr><td>Smoke / mg m<sup>-3</sup></td><td>0.4</td><td>1.5</td><td>4.4</td><td>4.5</td><td>1.2</td><td>0.5</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Describe the relationship between smoke concentration and daily deaths shown in Figure 1 and Table 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Calculate the percentage increase in daily deaths between 1 December and 8 December.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain how the weather conditions in December 1952 caused the smog to form.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Explain why most of the deaths were among the elderly, the very young and people with existing respiratory disease.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.5</span>State the legislation introduced in the UK in response to this smog.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Deaths rise as smoke concentration rises, peaking at about 930 per day on 8 Dec when smoke was highest (4.5 mg m<sup>-3</sup>)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Deaths fall as smoke falls but remain above the starting level for several days (time lag / delayed effect)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(930 - 250) / 250 x 100</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 272%</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Anticyclonic conditions: clear skies allowed rapid radiation of heat from the ground at night so the ground and air near it cooled</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Low wind velocity so air layers did not mix; London lies in a valley / basin where cold dense air collected: a temperature inversion formed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Smoke from coal fires was trapped below the inversion; moist air cooled to its dew point forming fog; smoke + fog = smog; the smog's high albedo prolonged the inversion</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Smoke particles (PM10) are inhaled deeply and irritate the lungs / kill cilia so particles and bacteria are not cleared, causing bronchitis / infections</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>These groups have weaker lungs / immune systems / less reserve so the extra stress (with sulfur dioxide acting synergistically) was fatal</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Clean Air Act (1956)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 245-247; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AIR-02", "sub": "air-pollution", "subname": "Atmospheric pollution: smoke, acid rain, NOx, hydrocarbons, CO, smogs", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.2.1", "pages": "245-247", "url": "subtopic/air-pollution.html#AIR-02", "snippet": "Describe how an electrostatic precipitator removes smoke particles from the effluent gases of a coal-fired power station.", "text": " describe how an electrostatic precipitator removes smoke particles from the effluent gases of a coal-fired power station. explain how a cyclone separator works. explain why heating coal to remove tar reduces smoke emissions. explain why turbochargers reduce the smoke produced by diesel engines. explain why smaller smoke particles (pm1) are a greater health risk than larger ones. effluent gases pass through a chamber containing electrically charged wires / plates smoke particles become (negatively) charged and are attracted to the oppositely charged plates where they collect / stick plates are shaken and the particles fall as fly ash which is collected (and may be used, eg in building materials) effluent gases are forced to rotate / spin in a cylindrical chamber the (denser) particles are thrown outwards to the chamber walls where they fall and collect; cleaned gases leave through a central pipe the tar / volatile organic compounds that produce smoke on incomplete combustion are driven off, producing smokeless coal / coke turbochargers deliver more air / oxygen to the combustion chamber so combustion is more complete and fewer unburnt carbon particles / soot are produced (especially during acceleration) smaller particles remain suspended in the atmosphere for longer so they are more likely to be inhaled they penetrate deeper into the lungs / alveoli and are not cleared; may carry toxic / carcinogenic chemicals", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AIR-02\" data-qid=\"AIR-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/air-pollution.html\">Atmospheric pollution: smoke, acid rain, NOx, hydrocarbons, CO, smogs</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 245-247 &middot; AIR-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Describe how an <b>electrostatic precipitator</b> removes smoke particles from the effluent gases of a coal-fired power station.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain how a <b>cyclone separator</b> works.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain why heating coal to remove tar reduces smoke emissions.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Explain why turbochargers reduce the smoke produced by diesel engines.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.5</span>Explain why smaller smoke particles (PM1) are a greater health risk than larger ones.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Effluent gases pass through a chamber containing electrically charged wires / plates</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Smoke particles become (negatively) charged and are attracted to the oppositely charged plates where they collect / stick</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Plates are shaken and the particles fall as fly ash which is collected (and may be used, eg in building materials)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Effluent gases are forced to rotate / spin in a cylindrical chamber</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The (denser) particles are thrown outwards to the chamber walls where they fall and collect; cleaned gases leave through a central pipe</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The tar / volatile organic compounds that produce smoke on incomplete combustion are driven off, producing smokeless coal / coke</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Turbochargers deliver more air / oxygen to the combustion chamber</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So combustion is more complete and fewer unburnt carbon particles / soot are produced (especially during acceleration)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Smaller particles remain suspended in the atmosphere for longer so they are more likely to be inhaled</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They penetrate deeper into the lungs / alveoli and are not cleared; may carry toxic / carcinogenic chemicals</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 245-247; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AIR-03", "sub": "air-pollution", "subname": "Atmospheric pollution: smoke, acid rain, NOx, hydrocarbons, CO, smogs", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.2", "pages": "250-252", "url": "subtopic/air-pollution.html#AIR-03", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the pH of rainwater and the number of fish species in lakes in four areas of Scandinavia with different bedrock geology.", "text": "table 1 shows the ph of rainwater and the number of fish species in lakes in four areas of scandinavia with different bedrock geology. state the ph below which rain is described as acid rain and explain why unpolluted rain is already slightly acidic. use table 1 to explain why lakes in area c contain many more fish species than lakes in area a, even though the rainfall has the same ph. describe two effects of acid rain on non-living objects. explain how sulfur dioxide released from a power station is converted to sulfuric acid in the atmosphere. ph 5.6 atmospheric carbon dioxide dissolves in rainwater to form (weak) carbonic acid limestone bedrock (calcium carbonate) is alkaline / basic and neutralises the acids as water percolates through it, so the lake ph stays high (7.2) granite does not neutralise acids so lake water in a is acidic (4.6) low ph mobilises / dissolves aluminium ions from the soil and rock (0.42 mg dm-3 in a) which are toxic to fish (damage gills) acids also damage fish eggs / gills (exposed living cells) and dissolve the exoskeletons of invertebrate food; lichens and other food organisms lost corrodes metals: railway lines, railings, pipes, pylons, overhead power lines dissolves / weakens limestone and marble buildings and statues (eg york minster) sulfur dioxide is oxidised (by ozone / other oxidants) to sulfur trioxide sulfur trioxide dissolves in water droplets to form sulfuric acid (so2 alone gives the weaker sulfurous acid)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AIR-03\" data-qid=\"AIR-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/air-pollution.html\">Atmospheric pollution: smoke, acid rain, NOx, hydrocarbons, CO, smogs</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 250-252 &middot; AIR-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the pH of rainwater and the number of fish species in lakes in four areas of Scandinavia with different bedrock geology.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Lake area</th><th>Bedrock</th><th>Mean rainfall pH</th><th>Mean lake pH</th><th>Number of fish species</th><th>Dissolved aluminium / mg dm<sup>-3</sup></th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>Granite</td><td>4.3</td><td>4.6</td><td>0</td><td>0.42</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>Granite</td><td>4.8</td><td>5.1</td><td>3</td><td>0.20</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>Limestone</td><td>4.3</td><td>7.2</td><td>8</td><td>0.01</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>Limestone</td><td>5.5</td><td>7.6</td><td>9</td><td>0.01</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>State the pH below which rain is described as acid rain and explain why unpolluted rain is already slightly acidic.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Use Table 1 to explain why lakes in area C contain many more fish species than lakes in area A, even though the rainfall has the same pH.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Describe <b>two</b> effects of acid rain on non-living objects.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Explain how sulfur dioxide released from a power station is converted to sulfuric acid in the atmosphere.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>pH 5.6</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Atmospheric carbon dioxide dissolves in rainwater to form (weak) carbonic acid</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Limestone bedrock (calcium carbonate) is alkaline / basic and neutralises the acids as water percolates through it, so the lake pH stays high (7.2)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Granite does not neutralise acids so lake water in A is acidic (4.6)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Low pH mobilises / dissolves aluminium ions from the soil and rock (0.42 mg dm<sup>-3</sup> in A) which are toxic to fish (damage gills)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Acids also damage fish eggs / gills (exposed living cells) and dissolve the exoskeletons of invertebrate food; lichens and other food organisms lost</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Corrodes metals: railway lines, railings, pipes, pylons, overhead power lines</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Dissolves / weakens limestone and marble buildings and statues (eg York Minster)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sulfur dioxide is oxidised (by ozone / other oxidants) to sulfur trioxide</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sulfur trioxide dissolves in water droplets to form sulfuric acid (SO2 alone gives the weaker sulfurous acid)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 250-252; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AIR-04", "sub": "air-pollution", "subname": "Atmospheric pollution: smoke, acid rain, NOx, hydrocarbons, CO, smogs", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.2.2-3.4.3.2.3", "pages": "252-253", "url": "subtopic/air-pollution.html#AIR-04", "snippet": "Describe how sulfur can be removed from coal before combustion.", "text": " describe how sulfur can be removed from coal before combustion. describe the process of dry flue gas desulfurisation and explain how the product can be used. in wet flue gas desulfurisation the flue gases are bubbled through sodium sulfite solution. explain one advantage of this method over dry fgd. explain how each of the following reduces nox emissions. most sulfur in coal is present as (solid) iron pyrites (fes2) coal is crushed and put in a stream of flowing water: the coal is carried away while the denser pyrites are left behind (washing / streaming) flue gases are passed through a bed of crushed calcium carbonate (limestone) sulfur dioxide reacts (with oxygen and calcium carbonate) to form solid calcium sulfate (and carbon dioxide) if the gases have already been cleaned of smoke, the calcium sulfate is pure enough to be sold as gypsum for building plaster the sodium hydrogen sulfite formed can be heated to regenerate sodium sulfite (re-used) and release concentrated sulfur dioxide the pure sulfur dioxide is a valuable raw material for making sulfur / sulfuric acid, so a waste becomes a resource fluidised bed: increased surface area allows rapid combustion at lower temperatures so less nitrogen and oxygen react to form nox (production prevented) catalytic converter: platinum / palladium catalyst chemically reduces nox back to nitrogen and oxygen gases in the exhaust urea sprayed into the hot flue / exhaust gases reacts with nox, converting it to nitrogen, carbon dioxide and water", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AIR-04\" data-qid=\"AIR-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/air-pollution.html\">Atmospheric pollution: smoke, acid rain, NOx, hydrocarbons, CO, smogs</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.2-3.4.3.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 252-253 &middot; AIR-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Describe how sulfur can be removed from coal <b>before</b> combustion.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Describe the process of dry flue gas desulfurisation and explain how the product can be used.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>In wet flue gas desulfurisation the flue gases are bubbled through sodium sulfite solution. Explain <b>one</b> advantage of this method over dry FGD.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Explain how each of the following reduces NOx emissions.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Most sulfur in coal is present as (solid) iron pyrites (FeS2)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Coal is crushed and put in a stream of flowing water: the coal is carried away while the denser pyrites are left behind (washing / streaming)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Flue gases are passed through a bed of crushed calcium carbonate (limestone)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sulfur dioxide reacts (with oxygen and calcium carbonate) to form solid calcium sulfate (and carbon dioxide)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>If the gases have already been cleaned of smoke, the calcium sulfate is pure enough to be sold as gypsum for building plaster</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The sodium hydrogen sulfite formed can be heated to regenerate sodium sulfite (re-used) and release concentrated sulfur dioxide</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The pure sulfur dioxide is a valuable raw material for making sulfur / sulfuric acid, so a waste becomes a resource</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fluidised bed: increased surface area allows rapid combustion at lower temperatures so less nitrogen and oxygen react to form NOx (production prevented)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Catalytic converter: platinum / palladium catalyst chemically reduces NOx back to nitrogen and oxygen gases in the exhaust</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Urea sprayed into the hot flue / exhaust gases reacts with NOx, converting it to nitrogen, carbon dioxide and water</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 252-253; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.2-3.4.3.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AIR-05", "sub": "air-pollution", "subname": "Atmospheric pollution: smoke, acid rain, NOx, hydrocarbons, CO, smogs", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.3-3.4.3.2.4", "pages": "248-249, 253-254", "url": "subtopic/air-pollution.html#AIR-05", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the concentrations of three pollutants in the air of a large city in a valley during one sunny day.", "text": "figure 1 shows the concentrations of three pollutants in the air of a large city in a valley during one sunny day. use figure 1 to describe how the ozone concentration changes during the day and explain the pattern. explain why the early-morning peak in no is followed by a peak in no2. name one other toxic secondary pollutant produced in photochemical smogs and give one of its effects on humans. explain why cities such as los angeles and mexico city are particularly prone to photochemical smogs. ozone is very low overnight / early morning, rises from about 08:00, peaks at about 80 units at 14:00 and falls in the evening ozone is a secondary pollutant formed by photochemical reactions requiring sunlight (uv), so it peaks in the afternoon after several hours of sunlight no2 (from morning traffic) is broken down by uv to no and monatomic oxygen; monatomic oxygen reacts with o2 to form ozone ozone lags behind the no / no2 peaks (which are produced by the morning rush-hour traffic) because the reactions take time; falls at night when there is no sunlight no is the primary pollutant produced in engines when nitrogen and oxygen react at high temperature / pressure no is oxidised in the atmosphere (by oxygen / ozone) to no2, a secondary pollutant pans (peroxyacetyl nitrates) eye irritation / breathing difficulty / asthma / emphysema / increased risk of heart attacks (toxic at much lower concentrations than nox) heavy vehicle use giving high nox and hydrocarbon emissions sunny climate providing the uv for photochemical reactions valley / basin topography and frequent temperature inversions trapping pollutants", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AIR-05\" data-qid=\"AIR-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/air-pollution.html\">Atmospheric pollution: smoke, acid rain, NOx, hydrocarbons, CO, smogs</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.3-3.4.3.2.4 &middot; Genn pp. 248-249, 253-254 &middot; AIR-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the concentrations of three pollutants in the air of a large city in a valley during one sunny day.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,255.8 120.0,213.1 180.0,99.2 240.0,170.3 300.0,227.3 360.0,247.2 420.0,235.8 480.0,198.8 540.0,227.3\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"255.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"120.0\" cy=\"213.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"180.0\" cy=\"99.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"240.0\" cy=\"170.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"227.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"360.0\" cy=\"247.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"420.0\" cy=\"235.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"480.0\" cy=\"198.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"227.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<polyline points=\"60.0,255.8 120.0,241.5 180.0,170.3 240.0,113.4 300.0,156.1 360.0,198.8 420.0,213.1 480.0,184.6 540.0,213.1\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2f5f73\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"255.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"120.0\" cy=\"241.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"180.0\" cy=\"170.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"240.0\" cy=\"113.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"156.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"360.0\" cy=\"198.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"420.0\" cy=\"213.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"480.0\" cy=\"184.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"213.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<polyline points=\"60.0,264.3 120.0,264.3 180.0,255.8 240.0,198.8 300.0,99.2 360.0,42.2 420.0,70.7 480.0,156.1 540.0,227.3\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#8b5e3c\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"264.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"120.0\" cy=\"264.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"180.0\" cy=\"255.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"240.0\" cy=\"198.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"99.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"360.0\" cy=\"42.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"420.0\" cy=\"70.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"480.0\" cy=\"156.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"227.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">7.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">17.6</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">10.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">35.1</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">13.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">52.7</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">16.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">70.2</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">20</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">87.8</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Time of day / hours</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Concentration / arbitrary units</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"24\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#22302a\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"33\">NO</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"38\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"47\">NO₂</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"52\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"61\">Ozone</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Use Figure 1 to describe how the ozone concentration changes during the day and explain the pattern.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain why the early-morning peak in NO is followed by a peak in NO<sub>2</sub>.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Name <b>one</b> other toxic secondary pollutant produced in photochemical smogs and give one of its effects on humans.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Explain why cities such as Los Angeles and Mexico City are particularly prone to photochemical smogs.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ozone is very low overnight / early morning, rises from about 08:00, peaks at about 80 units at 14:00 and falls in the evening</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ozone is a secondary pollutant formed by photochemical reactions requiring sunlight (UV), so it peaks in the afternoon after several hours of sunlight</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>NO2 (from morning traffic) is broken down by UV to NO and monatomic oxygen; monatomic oxygen reacts with O2 to form ozone</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ozone lags behind the NO / NO2 peaks (which are produced by the morning rush-hour traffic) because the reactions take time; falls at night when there is no sunlight</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>NO is the primary pollutant produced in engines when nitrogen and oxygen react at high temperature / pressure</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>NO is oxidised in the atmosphere (by oxygen / ozone) to NO2, a secondary pollutant</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>PANs (peroxyacetyl nitrates)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Eye irritation / breathing difficulty / asthma / emphysema / increased risk of heart attacks (toxic at much lower concentrations than NOx)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Heavy vehicle use giving high NOx and hydrocarbon emissions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sunny climate providing the UV for photochemical reactions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Valley / basin topography and frequent temperature inversions trapping pollutants</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 248-249, 253-254; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.3-3.4.3.2.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AIR-06", "sub": "air-pollution", "subname": "Atmospheric pollution: smoke, acid rain, NOx, hydrocarbons, CO, smogs", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.2.4-3.4.3.2.5", "pages": "249, 254", "url": "subtopic/air-pollution.html#AIR-06", "snippet": "Give three sources of unburnt hydrocarbons in urban air.", "text": " give three sources of unburnt hydrocarbons in urban air. describe two methods used to control hydrocarbon emissions at petrol filling stations. explain how a catalytic converter controls the emissions of carbon monoxide, hydrocarbons and nox. explain why carbon monoxide is toxic to humans. unburnt fuel in vehicle exhaust gases evaporation from fuel tanks / spillage at filling stations solvents (paints, cleaning products) aerosol propellants gas leaks / fossil fuel exploitation (methane) vapours displaced from the vehicle tank are collected, condensed and returned to the main storage tank vapours are passed through an activated carbon filter where the hydrocarbons adsorb onto the carbon a platinum / palladium catalyst on a high surface-area honeycomb in the exhaust pipe carbon monoxide and hydrocarbons are oxidised to carbon dioxide (and water) nox are chemically reduced to nitrogen and oxygen binds to haemoglobin (more strongly than oxygen) so haemoglobin cannot carry oxygen from the lungs to tissues brain damage / death if enough haemoglobin is inactivated; low doses worsen chronic heart disease", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AIR-06\" data-qid=\"AIR-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/air-pollution.html\">Atmospheric pollution: smoke, acid rain, NOx, hydrocarbons, CO, smogs</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.4-3.4.3.2.5 &middot; Genn pp. 249, 254 &middot; AIR-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Give <b>three</b> sources of unburnt hydrocarbons in urban air.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Describe <b>two</b> methods used to control hydrocarbon emissions at petrol filling stations.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain how a catalytic converter controls the emissions of carbon monoxide, hydrocarbons and NOx.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Explain why carbon monoxide is toxic to humans.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Unburnt fuel in vehicle exhaust gases</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Evaporation from fuel tanks / spillage at filling stations</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Solvents (paints, cleaning products)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Aerosol propellants</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Gas leaks / fossil fuel exploitation (methane)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Vapours displaced from the vehicle tank are collected, condensed and returned to the main storage tank</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Vapours are passed through an activated carbon filter where the hydrocarbons adsorb onto the carbon</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A platinum / palladium catalyst on a high surface-area honeycomb in the exhaust pipe</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Carbon monoxide and hydrocarbons are oxidised to carbon dioxide (and water)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>NOx are chemically reduced to nitrogen and oxygen</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Binds to haemoglobin (more strongly than oxygen) so haemoglobin cannot carry oxygen from the lungs to tissues</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Brain damage / death if enough haemoglobin is inactivated; low doses worsen chronic heart disease</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 249, 254; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.4-3.4.3.2.5.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AIR-07", "sub": "air-pollution", "subname": "Atmospheric pollution: smoke, acid rain, NOx, hydrocarbons, CO, smogs", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.1-3.4.3.2.5", "pages": "245-254", "url": "subtopic/air-pollution.html#AIR-07", "snippet": "Explain how the properties of smoke and of the gases involved in acid rain lead to their environmental effects, and evaluate the effectiveness of the methods used to control them.", "text": " explain how the properties of smoke and of the gases involved in acid rain lead to their environmental effects, and evaluate the effectiveness of the methods used to control them. state one effect of tropospheric ozone on plants. smoke: solid particulates (pm10, pm5, pm1) from incomplete combustion; small particles stay suspended, penetrate lungs; toxic chemicals (acids, phenols, metals) carried on particles; high albedo cools climate; blocks light reducing photosynthesis; damages buildings smoke smogs form under temperature inversions in valleys (london 1952, 12 000 deaths); synergism with so2 controls: clean air act 1956 (smokeless zones), smokeless fuels / gas / electricity, coal treatment, electrostatic precipitators, cyclones, bag filters, scrubbers, dpfs, better combustion; smoke smogs have largely been eliminated in uk cities acid rain gases: so2 (coal combustion, smelting sulfide ores) oxidised by ozone to so3 - sulfuric acid; nox (high temperature combustion) - nitric acid; hcl from pvc incineration; reactive gases travel long distances (transboundary pollution) effects: ph denatures proteins / enzymes in exposed cells (gills, eggs, root hairs, stomata); dissolves exoskeletons; leaches ca and mg then mobilises toxic al and pb; corrodes metals, dissolves limestone; severity depends on bedrock (limestone neutralises) controls: fuel desulfurisation (coal washing, hydrodesulfurisation, low-sulfur ship fuels), dry and wet fgd, fluidised bed combustion, catalytic converters, urea sprays; switching from coal to gas / renewables evaluation: so2 emissions in europe have fallen dramatically and lake ph is recovering; nox harder to control because of growing vehicle numbers; catalytic converters need lead-free fuel and warm engines; fgd is costly and produces wastes / gypsum; controlling primary pollutants also prevents secondary pollutants (ozone, pans) judgement on relative success of controls for particulates vs so2 vs nox reduces growth rates of trees / crops damages leaf cuticles (allowing so2 to cause more damage)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AIR-07\" data-qid=\"AIR-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/air-pollution.html\">Atmospheric pollution: smoke, acid rain, NOx, hydrocarbons, CO, smogs</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.1-3.4.3.2.5 &middot; Genn pp. 245-254 &middot; AIR-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Explain how the properties of smoke and of the gases involved in acid rain lead to their environmental effects, and evaluate the effectiveness of the methods used to control them.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>State <b>one</b> effect of tropospheric ozone on plants.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Smoke: solid particulates (PM10, PM5, PM1) from incomplete combustion; small particles stay suspended, penetrate lungs; toxic chemicals (acids, phenols, metals) carried on particles; high albedo cools climate; blocks light reducing photosynthesis; damages buildings</li><li>Smoke smogs form under temperature inversions in valleys (London 1952, 12 000 deaths); synergism with SO2</li><li>Controls: Clean Air Act 1956 (smokeless zones), smokeless fuels / gas / electricity, coal treatment, electrostatic precipitators, cyclones, bag filters, scrubbers, DPFs, better combustion; smoke smogs have largely been eliminated in UK cities</li><li>Acid rain gases: SO2 (coal combustion, smelting sulfide ores) oxidised by ozone to SO3 - sulfuric acid; NOx (high temperature combustion) - nitric acid; HCl from PVC incineration; reactive gases travel long distances (transboundary pollution)</li><li>Effects: pH denatures proteins / enzymes in exposed cells (gills, eggs, root hairs, stomata); dissolves exoskeletons; leaches Ca and Mg then mobilises toxic Al and Pb; corrodes metals, dissolves limestone; severity depends on bedrock (limestone neutralises)</li><li>Controls: fuel desulfurisation (coal washing, hydrodesulfurisation, low-sulfur ship fuels), dry and wet FGD, fluidised bed combustion, catalytic converters, urea sprays; switching from coal to gas / renewables</li><li>Evaluation: SO2 emissions in Europe have fallen dramatically and lake pH is recovering; NOx harder to control because of growing vehicle numbers; catalytic converters need lead-free fuel and warm engines; FGD is costly and produces wastes / gypsum; controlling primary pollutants also prevents secondary pollutants (ozone, PANs)</li><li>Judgement on relative success of controls for particulates vs SO2 vs NOx</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 1 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 1.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduces growth rates of trees / crops</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Damages leaf cuticles (allowing SO2 to cause more damage)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 245-254; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.1-3.4.3.2.5.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AIR-08", "sub": "air-pollution", "subname": "Atmospheric pollution: smoke, acid rain, NOx, hydrocarbons, CO, smogs", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.1-3.4.3.2.3", "pages": "245-253", "url": "subtopic/air-pollution.html#AIR-08", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the annual emissions of two pollutants in the UK in 1970 and 2015.", "text": "table 1 shows the annual emissions of two pollutants in the uk in 1970 and 2015. calculate the percentage reduction in sulfur dioxide emissions between 1970 and 2015. suggest three reasons why sulfur dioxide emissions fell by a much larger percentage than nitrogen oxide emissions. explain why a reduction in nox emissions would also reduce concentrations of tropospheric ozone and pans. explain why lichens are used to monitor sulfur dioxide pollution. (6400 - 220) / 6400 x 100 = 96.6% (accept 97%) switch from coal to natural gas (which is desulfurised) / closure of coal-fired power stations removed the main so2 source flue gas desulfurisation fitted to remaining coal plants; coal / oil desulfurisation nox comes mainly from road transport and vehicle numbers / distance travelled increased greatly nox is produced from air (nitrogen + oxygen) in any high-temperature combustion, so cannot be removed from the fuel like sulfur catalytic converters only work when warm / diesel engines produce more nox / older vehicles remained in use ozone and pans are secondary pollutants formed by photochemical reactions involving no2 (broken down by uv to monatomic oxygen which forms ozone) if the primary pollutant is reduced the reactants are not present so the secondary pollutants cannot form lichens are very sensitive to acidic conditions / so2 and different species have different tolerances the species present, their abundance, size / health can be used as a biotic index of the pollution level they give a long-term integrated measure of pollution rather than a single instantaneous reading; cheap, no equipment needed", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AIR-08\" data-qid=\"AIR-08\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 8</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/air-pollution.html\">Atmospheric pollution: smoke, acid rain, NOx, hydrocarbons, CO, smogs</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.1-3.4.3.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 245-253 &middot; AIR-08</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the annual emissions of two pollutants in the UK in 1970 and 2015.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Pollutant</th><th>Emissions in 1970 / thousand tonnes</th><th>Emissions in 2015 / thousand tonnes</th><th>Main source in 2015</th></tr><tr><td>Sulfur dioxide</td><td>6 400</td><td>220</td><td>Energy industries</td></tr><tr><td>Nitrogen oxides</td><td>2 700</td><td>930</td><td>Road transport</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.1</span>Calculate the percentage reduction in sulfur dioxide emissions between 1970 and 2015.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.2</span>Suggest <b>three</b> reasons why sulfur dioxide emissions fell by a much larger percentage than nitrogen oxide emissions.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.3</span>Explain why a reduction in NOx emissions would also reduce concentrations of tropospheric ozone and PANs.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.4</span>Explain why lichens are used to monitor sulfur dioxide pollution.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(6400 - 220) / 6400 x 100</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 96.6% (accept 97%)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Switch from coal to natural gas (which is desulfurised) / closure of coal-fired power stations removed the main SO2 source</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Flue gas desulfurisation fitted to remaining coal plants; coal / oil desulfurisation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>NOx comes mainly from road transport and vehicle numbers / distance travelled increased greatly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>NOx is produced from air (nitrogen + oxygen) in any high-temperature combustion, so cannot be removed from the fuel like sulfur</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Catalytic converters only work when warm / diesel engines produce more NOx / older vehicles remained in use</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ozone and PANs are secondary pollutants formed by photochemical reactions involving NO2 (broken down by UV to monatomic oxygen which forms ozone)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>If the primary pollutant is reduced the reactants are not present so the secondary pollutants cannot form</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lichens are very sensitive to acidic conditions / SO2 and different species have different tolerances</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The species present, their abundance, size / health can be used as a biotic index of the pollution level</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They give a long-term integrated measure of pollution rather than a single instantaneous reading; cheap, no equipment needed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 245-253; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.1-3.4.3.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ATM-01", "sub": "atm-energy", "subname": "The atmosphere: energy processes (UV, IR, visible light)", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.2.1.1", "pages": "86-89", "url": "subtopic/atm-energy.html#ATM-01", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the composition of the dry atmosphere. Figure 1 shows the changes in carbon dioxide concentration measured at a site in the northern hemisphere over one year.", "text": "table 1 shows the composition of the dry atmosphere. figure 1 shows the changes in carbon dioxide concentration measured at a site in the northern hemisphere over one year. describe and explain the pattern shown in figure 1. explain why the atmosphere is described as being in a state of dynamic equilibrium. give two ways in which the atmosphere supports life other than by providing gases for biological molecules. name the two layers of the atmosphere most affected by human activities. co2 rises from about 412 ppm in january to a peak of 416 ppm in may, falls to a minimum of 407 ppm in september, then rises again during spring / summer, photosynthesis by plants (growing season) removes co2 faster than respiration releases it, so the concentration falls in autumn / winter photosynthesis is reduced (leaf fall) but respiration and decomposition continue, so co2 rises the atmosphere is in dynamic equilibrium: the concentration fluctuates around a mean; more land / vegetation in the northern hemisphere makes the cycle large natural processes that add gases (respiration, combustion, volcanoes, denitrification) are balanced by processes that remove them (photosynthesis, dissolving, nitrogen fixation) so the average composition stays the same over long timescales although the rates vary and concentrations fluctuate around a mean absorbs harmful uv / solar wind radiation in the stratosphere (ozone layer) greenhouse gases delay the escape of infrared, keeping the surface warm (33 °c warmer than without) atmospheric pressure allows liquid water to exist distributes heat from the tropics to higher latitudes by winds transports water vapour to areas that would otherwise be dry troposphere stratosphere", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ATM-01\" data-qid=\"ATM-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/atm-energy.html\">The atmosphere: energy processes (UV, IR, visible light)</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.1 &middot; Genn pp. 86-89 &middot; ATM-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the composition of the dry atmosphere. Figure 1 shows the changes in carbon dioxide concentration measured at a site in the northern hemisphere over one year.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Gas</th><th>Percentage by volume / %</th></tr><tr><td>Nitrogen</td><td>78</td></tr><tr><td>Oxygen</td><td>21</td></tr><tr><td>Argon and other rare gases</td><td>0.9</td></tr><tr><td>Carbon dioxide</td><td>0.04</td></tr><tr><td>Ozone</td><td>0.000007</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,120.0 103.6,107.5 147.3,95.0 190.9,82.5 234.5,70.0 278.2,95.0 321.8,132.5 365.5,170.0 409.1,182.5 452.7,170.0 496.4,145.0 540.0,120.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"120.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"103.6\" cy=\"107.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"147.3\" cy=\"95.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"190.9\" cy=\"82.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"234.5\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"278.2\" cy=\"95.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"321.8\" cy=\"132.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"365.5\" cy=\"170.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"409.1\" cy=\"182.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"452.7\" cy=\"170.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"496.4\" cy=\"145.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"120.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">400</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">404</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">5.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">408</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">7.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">412</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">9.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">416</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">12</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">420</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Month</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">CO₂ concentration / ppm</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Describe and explain the pattern shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain why the atmosphere is described as being in a state of <b>dynamic equilibrium</b>.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Give <b>two</b> ways in which the atmosphere supports life other than by providing gases for biological molecules.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Name the two layers of the atmosphere most affected by human activities.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>CO2 rises from about 412 ppm in January to a peak of 416 ppm in May, falls to a minimum of 407 ppm in September, then rises again</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>During spring / summer, photosynthesis by plants (growing season) removes CO2 faster than respiration releases it, so the concentration falls</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In autumn / winter photosynthesis is reduced (leaf fall) but respiration and decomposition continue, so CO2 rises</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The atmosphere is in dynamic equilibrium: the concentration fluctuates around a mean; more land / vegetation in the northern hemisphere makes the cycle large</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Natural processes that add gases (respiration, combustion, volcanoes, denitrification) are balanced by processes that remove them (photosynthesis, dissolving, nitrogen fixation)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So the average composition stays the same over long timescales although the rates vary and concentrations fluctuate around a mean</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Absorbs harmful UV / solar wind radiation in the stratosphere (ozone layer)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Greenhouse gases delay the escape of infrared, keeping the surface warm (33 &deg;C warmer than without)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Atmospheric pressure allows liquid water to exist</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Distributes heat from the tropics to higher latitudes by winds</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Transports water vapour to areas that would otherwise be dry</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Troposphere</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stratosphere</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 86-89; AQA spec 3.2.1.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ATM-02", "sub": "atm-energy", "subname": "The atmosphere: energy processes (UV, IR, visible light)", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.2.1.1", "pages": "87-89", "url": "subtopic/atm-energy.html#ATM-02", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows a simplified energy budget for the Earth and its atmosphere. Values are percentages of incoming solar radiation.", "text": "figure 1 shows a simplified energy budget for the earth and its atmosphere. values are percentages of incoming solar radiation. use figure 1 to calculate the planetary albedo of the earth. describe how each of the following wavelengths of electromagnetic radiation behaves in the atmosphere. explain why the amount of infrared re-emitted to the surface (85%) is larger than the amount emitted to space (70%). explain the term thermal stratification of the atmosphere. 25 + 5 = 30% uv: short wavelength; mostly absorbed in the stratosphere by monatomic, diatomic and triatomic oxygen (ozone layer) which are formed and destroyed in a dynamic equilibrium; converted to chemical energy in these reactions; little reaches the surface visible light: transmitted through the atmosphere easily (some reflected by clouds / dust); absorbed by the earth's surface and converted to heat; some absorbed by chlorophyll and converted to chemical energy in photosynthesis ir: emitted by the warm surface (long-wave); absorbed by greenhouse gases (co2, water vapour, methane) and converted to heat; the warm atmosphere re-emits ir to the surface and reduces conduction losses; some escapes to space greenhouse gases absorb most of the ir emitted by the surface and re-radiate it in all directions, including back down the surface is repeatedly warmed and re-emits, so the total ir exchanged between surface and atmosphere exceeds the incoming solar energy; only the net loss (70%) balances the absorbed solar energy at equilibrium the atmosphere forms layers (troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere) with different temperature gradients because energy is absorbed at different altitudes (eg uv heating the stratosphere, surface heating the troposphere)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ATM-02\" data-qid=\"ATM-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/atm-energy.html\">The atmosphere: energy processes (UV, IR, visible light)</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.1 &middot; Genn pp. 87-89 &middot; ATM-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows a simplified energy budget for the Earth and its atmosphere. Values are percentages of incoming solar radiation.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Energy flow</th><th>Percentage of incoming solar radiation / %</th></tr><tr><td>Reflected by clouds, dust and atmosphere</td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>Reflected by the Earth's surface</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Absorbed by the atmosphere</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>Absorbed by the Earth's surface</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>Infrared emitted by the surface and absorbed by greenhouse gases</td><td>95</td></tr><tr><td>Infrared re-emitted by the atmosphere back to the surface</td><td>85</td></tr><tr><td>Infrared emitted to space</td><td>70</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Use Figure 1 to calculate the planetary albedo of the Earth.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Describe how each of the following wavelengths of electromagnetic radiation behaves in the atmosphere.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain why the amount of infrared re-emitted to the surface (85%) is larger than the amount emitted to space (70%).\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Explain the term <b>thermal stratification</b> of the atmosphere.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>25 + 5 = 30%</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>UV: short wavelength; mostly absorbed in the stratosphere by monatomic, diatomic and triatomic oxygen (ozone layer) which are formed and destroyed in a dynamic equilibrium; converted to chemical energy in these reactions; little reaches the surface</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Visible light: transmitted through the atmosphere easily (some reflected by clouds / dust); absorbed by the Earth's surface and converted to heat; some absorbed by chlorophyll and converted to chemical energy in photosynthesis</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>IR: emitted by the warm surface (long-wave); absorbed by greenhouse gases (CO2, water vapour, methane) and converted to heat; the warm atmosphere re-emits IR to the surface and reduces conduction losses; some escapes to space</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Greenhouse gases absorb most of the IR emitted by the surface and re-radiate it in all directions, including back down</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The surface is repeatedly warmed and re-emits, so the total IR exchanged between surface and atmosphere exceeds the incoming solar energy; only the net loss (70%) balances the absorbed solar energy at equilibrium</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The atmosphere forms layers (troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere) with different temperature gradients because energy is absorbed at different altitudes (eg UV heating the stratosphere, surface heating the troposphere)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 87-89; AQA spec 3.2.1.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ATM-03", "sub": "atm-energy", "subname": "The atmosphere: energy processes (UV, IR, visible light)", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.1.1, 3.2.2.3", "pages": "87, 96-97", "url": "subtopic/atm-energy.html#ATM-03", "snippet": "Explain how energy absorbed in tropical regions is distributed to higher latitudes such as the UK.", "text": " explain how energy absorbed in tropical regions is distributed to higher latitudes such as the uk. explain why the uk has milder winters than moscow or churchill, canada, which are at similar latitudes. explain why the atmosphere would not retain liquid water if atmospheric pressure were much lower. give two industrially important gases extracted from the atmosphere. most solar energy is absorbed at the surface in the tropics where insolation is most intense / the sun is overhead the warm surface heats the air above; warm winds (eg south-westerlies) carry heat energy to higher latitudes winds blowing over the oceans create currents (north atlantic conveyor / gulf stream) carrying warm water from the tropics to nw europe evaporation in the tropics stores latent heat in water vapour which is released when it condenses at higher latitudes; also transports water to areas that would be dry the north atlantic conveyor brings warm water from the tropics to the coast of nw europe prevailing south-westerly winds blowing over the warm sea carry the heat onto the land atmospheric pressure controls how easily water molecules evaporate / escape from the surface at low pressure water would boil / evaporate at low temperatures so all surface water would become vapour nitrogen oxygen carbon dioxide argon / neon / krypton / xenon", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ATM-03\" data-qid=\"ATM-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/atm-energy.html\">The atmosphere: energy processes (UV, IR, visible light)</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.1, 3.2.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 87, 96-97 &middot; ATM-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Explain how energy absorbed in tropical regions is distributed to higher latitudes such as the UK.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain why the UK has milder winters than Moscow or Churchill, Canada, which are at similar latitudes.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain why the atmosphere would not retain liquid water if atmospheric pressure were much lower.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Give <b>two</b> industrially important gases extracted from the atmosphere.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Most solar energy is absorbed at the surface in the tropics where insolation is most intense / the Sun is overhead</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The warm surface heats the air above; warm winds (eg south-westerlies) carry heat energy to higher latitudes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Winds blowing over the oceans create currents (North Atlantic Conveyor / Gulf Stream) carrying warm water from the tropics to NW Europe</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Evaporation in the tropics stores latent heat in water vapour which is released when it condenses at higher latitudes; also transports water to areas that would be dry</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The North Atlantic Conveyor brings warm water from the tropics to the coast of NW Europe</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Prevailing south-westerly winds blowing over the warm sea carry the heat onto the land</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Atmospheric pressure controls how easily water molecules evaporate / escape from the surface</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>At low pressure water would boil / evaporate at low temperatures so all surface water would become vapour</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nitrogen</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Oxygen</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Carbon dioxide</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Argon / neon / krypton / xenon</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 87, 96-97; AQA spec 3.2.1.1, 3.2.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ATM-04", "sub": "atm-energy", "subname": "The atmosphere: energy processes (UV, IR, visible light)", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.2.1.1", "pages": "86-88", "url": "subtopic/atm-energy.html#ATM-04", "snippet": "Which gas makes up about 21% of the dry atmosphere by volume? Tick one box.", "text": " which gas makes up about 21% of the dry atmosphere by volume? tick one box. explain why it is difficult to predict the effect of a human activity on atmospheric processes. explain how winds transport both energy and water to areas such as the uk. oxygen a change to one process causes other processes to alter as a direct result (chains of linked processes / feedback) there is still a lot that is not understood about how atmospheric processes work the surface warmed in the tropics heats the air above; warm winds (south-westerlies from the caribbean) carry heat energy to higher latitudes winds carry water vapour (evaporated from warm oceans) to areas that would otherwise receive little precipitation", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ATM-04\" data-qid=\"ATM-04\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/atm-energy.html\">The atmosphere: energy processes (UV, IR, visible light)</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.1 &middot; Genn pp. 86-88 &middot; ATM-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Which gas makes up about 21% of the dry atmosphere by volume? Tick <b>one</b> box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; Nitrogen</li><li>B&nbsp; Oxygen</li><li>C&nbsp; Argon</li><li>D&nbsp; Carbon dioxide</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain why it is difficult to predict the effect of a human activity on atmospheric processes.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain how winds transport both energy and water to areas such as the UK.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b>B</b>&nbsp; Oxygen</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A change to one process causes other processes to alter as a direct result (chains of linked processes / feedback)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>There is still a lot that is not understood about how atmospheric processes work</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The surface warmed in the tropics heats the air above; warm winds (south-westerlies from the Caribbean) carry heat energy to higher latitudes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Winds carry water vapour (evaporated from warm oceans) to areas that would otherwise receive little precipitation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 86-88; AQA spec 3.2.1.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ATM-05", "sub": "atm-energy", "subname": "The atmosphere: energy processes (UV, IR, visible light)", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.2.1.1", "pages": "87-89", "url": "subtopic/atm-energy.html#ATM-05", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the mean surface temperature of the Earth with and without an atmosphere containing greenhouse gases.", "text": "table 1 shows the mean surface temperature of the earth with and without an atmosphere containing greenhouse gases. use table 1 to calculate the warming produced by the natural greenhouse effect. explain how the natural greenhouse effect warms the earth's surface. explain what is meant by a greenhouse gas. the wavelengths of radiation arriving at the earth are different from those leaving. describe this difference and explain why it matters to the energy budget. explain why a change in the movement of energy through the atmosphere could affect the distribution of species. 15 - (-18) = 33 °c visible light passes easily through the atmosphere and is absorbed by the surface, which warms the warm surface emits (long-wave / far) infrared radiation greenhouse gases (water vapour, co2, methane) absorb infrared better than the average for the atmosphere and convert it to heat the warm atmosphere emits infrared which is absorbed by the surface, and it reduces heat loss from land and oceans by conduction any gas that is better at absorbing infrared radiation than the average for the atmosphere arriving: mainly ultraviolet, visible light and near infrared (short wavelength); leaving: mainly long-wavelength (far) infrared gases that are transparent to the incoming short wavelengths can absorb the outgoing long wavelengths, so changing their concentration changes how much energy is retained the energy and the processes it drives control climate, ocean currents and the hydrological cycle these determine the abiotic conditions (temperature, rainfall) that each species is adapted to, so a change alters where species can survive", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ATM-05\" data-qid=\"ATM-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/atm-energy.html\">The atmosphere: energy processes (UV, IR, visible light)</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.1 &middot; Genn pp. 87-89 &middot; ATM-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the mean surface temperature of the Earth with and without an atmosphere containing greenhouse gases.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Mean surface temperature / &deg;C</th></tr><tr><td>No greenhouse effect</td><td>-18</td></tr><tr><td>Natural greenhouse effect</td><td>15</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Use Table 1 to calculate the warming produced by the natural greenhouse effect.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain how the natural greenhouse effect warms the Earth's surface.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain what is meant by a <b>greenhouse gas</b>.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>The wavelengths of radiation arriving at the Earth are different from those leaving. Describe this difference and explain why it matters to the energy budget.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.5</span>Explain why a change in the movement of energy through the atmosphere could affect the distribution of species.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>15 - (-18) = 33 &deg;C</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Visible light passes easily through the atmosphere and is absorbed by the surface, which warms</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The warm surface emits (long-wave / far) infrared radiation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Greenhouse gases (water vapour, CO<sub>2</sub>, methane) absorb infrared better than the average for the atmosphere and convert it to heat</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The warm atmosphere emits infrared which is absorbed by the surface, and it reduces heat loss from land and oceans by conduction</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Any gas that is better at absorbing infrared radiation than the average for the atmosphere</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Arriving: mainly ultraviolet, visible light and near infrared (short wavelength); leaving: mainly long-wavelength (far) infrared</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Gases that are transparent to the incoming short wavelengths can absorb the outgoing long wavelengths, so changing their concentration changes how much energy is retained</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The energy and the processes it drives control climate, ocean currents and the hydrological cycle</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>These determine the abiotic conditions (temperature, rainfall) that each species is adapted to, so a change alters where species can survive</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 87-89; AQA spec 3.2.1.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ATM-06", "sub": "atm-energy", "subname": "The atmosphere: energy processes (UV, IR, visible light)", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 15, "flags": ["data", "ext"], "spec": "3.2.1.1, 3.2.1.3", "pages": "86-89, 108-113", "url": "subtopic/atm-energy.html#ATM-06", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the three forms of oxygen found in the stratosphere.", "text": "table 1 shows the three forms of oxygen found in the stratosphere. explain how the three forms of oxygen protect life at the surface from ultraviolet radiation. give two other ways in which the atmosphere supports life. explain why the atmosphere is thermally stratified into layers. the troposphere and stratosphere are the layers most affected by human activities. discuss how human activities alter the energy processes in these two layers and evaluate the difficulties of predicting the consequences. together they form a dispersed layer in the stratosphere (the ozone layer / ozonosphere) they absorb ultraviolet light, which drives a dynamic equilibrium of reactions that form and destroy ozone the uv energy is converted to chemical / heat energy so most uv that passed the upper atmosphere never reaches the surface provides carbon, oxygen, hydrogen and nitrogen (as n2, o2, co2, h2o) for biological molecules delays the escape of infrared energy, keeping the surface warm distributes heat and water vapour by winds / ocean currents provides atmospheric pressure so liquid water can exist deflects damaging radiation of the solar wind in the upper atmosphere energy is absorbed at different altitudes (eg uv absorbed in the stratosphere, surface heating warms the troposphere) so temperature gradients change with altitude, producing layers troposphere: enhanced greenhouse effect - anthropogenic co2 (fossil fuels, deforestation), methane (rice, livestock, landfill), n2o, cfcs, tropospheric ozone; more infrared absorbed and re-emitted; aerosols and smoke increase albedo / scatter light; land-use change alters surface albedo and evapotranspiration stratosphere: cfcs / halons / hcfcs release chlorine and bromine which catalyse ozone destruction, shifting the o / o2 / o3 equilibrium; less uv absorbed in the stratosphere, more reaches the surface; stratospheric cooling consequences: changes to climate, ocean currents (thermohaline), hydrological cycle, sea level, species distributions; increased uv damage to organisms difficulties of prediction: linked processes where one change alters others; feedback (positive and negative) and tipping points; time lags (ocean heat capacity, long residence times of cfcs and co2); natural variability (solar output, volcanoes, milankovitch cycles); incomplete understanding of atmospheric processes; limited historical data so proxies needed; models with different assumptions evaluation: some effects well understood (greenhouse physics, ozone chemistry proven, montreal protocol success) but scale and timing of regional impacts uncertain; precautionary principle justified conclusion", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ATM-06\" data-qid=\"ATM-06\" data-marks=\"15\" data-flags=\"data ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">15 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/atm-energy.html\">The atmosphere: energy processes (UV, IR, visible light)</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.1, 3.2.1.3 &middot; Genn pp. 86-89, 108-113 &middot; ATM-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the three forms of oxygen found in the stratosphere.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Form of oxygen</th><th>Number of atoms per molecule</th></tr><tr><td>Monatomic oxygen (O)</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Diatomic oxygen (O<sub>2</sub>)</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Triatomic oxygen / ozone (O<sub>3</sub>)</td><td>3</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Explain how the three forms of oxygen protect life at the surface from ultraviolet radiation.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Give <b>two</b> other ways in which the atmosphere supports life.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain why the atmosphere is thermally stratified into layers.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>The troposphere and stratosphere are the layers most affected by human activities. Discuss how human activities alter the energy processes in these two layers and evaluate the difficulties of predicting the consequences.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Together they form a dispersed layer in the stratosphere (the ozone layer / ozonosphere)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They absorb ultraviolet light, which drives a dynamic equilibrium of reactions that form and destroy ozone</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The UV energy is converted to chemical / heat energy so most UV that passed the upper atmosphere never reaches the surface</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Provides carbon, oxygen, hydrogen and nitrogen (as N<sub>2</sub>, O<sub>2</sub>, CO<sub>2</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>O) for biological molecules</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Delays the escape of infrared energy, keeping the surface warm</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Distributes heat and water vapour by winds / ocean currents</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Provides atmospheric pressure so liquid water can exist</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Deflects damaging radiation of the solar wind in the upper atmosphere</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Energy is absorbed at different altitudes (eg UV absorbed in the stratosphere, surface heating warms the troposphere) so temperature gradients change with altitude, producing layers</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Troposphere: enhanced greenhouse effect - anthropogenic CO<sub>2</sub> (fossil fuels, deforestation), methane (rice, livestock, landfill), N<sub>2</sub>O, CFCs, tropospheric ozone; more infrared absorbed and re-emitted; aerosols and smoke increase albedo / scatter light; land-use change alters surface albedo and evapotranspiration</li><li>Stratosphere: CFCs / halons / HCFCs release chlorine and bromine which catalyse ozone destruction, shifting the O / O<sub>2</sub> / O<sub>3</sub> equilibrium; less UV absorbed in the stratosphere, more reaches the surface; stratospheric cooling</li><li>Consequences: changes to climate, ocean currents (thermohaline), hydrological cycle, sea level, species distributions; increased UV damage to organisms</li><li>Difficulties of prediction: linked processes where one change alters others; feedback (positive and negative) and tipping points; time lags (ocean heat capacity, long residence times of CFCs and CO<sub>2</sub>); natural variability (solar output, volcanoes, Milankovitch cycles); incomplete understanding of atmospheric processes; limited historical data so proxies needed; models with different assumptions</li><li>Evaluation: some effects well understood (greenhouse physics, ozone chemistry proven, Montreal Protocol success) but scale and timing of regional impacts uncertain; precautionary principle</li><li>Justified conclusion</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 86-89, 108-113; AQA spec 3.2.1.1, 3.2.1.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "OZ-01", "sub": "ozone", "subname": "Ozone depletion", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 15, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.1.3", "pages": "108-109", "url": "subtopic/ozone.html#OZ-01", "snippet": "Describe how ozone is formed and destroyed naturally in the stratosphere.", "text": " describe how ozone is formed and destroyed naturally in the stratosphere. explain the properties of cfcs that made them useful, and the properties that cause ozone depletion (the rowland-molina hypothesis). explain why increased uvb reaching the earth's surface is harmful to living organisms. give three effects. state what is meant by an ozone hole. give one reason why concentration in ppm is not used to measure ozone depletion. give one reason why the first evidence for ozone depletion came from ground-based measurements. uv light splits diatomic oxygen (o2) into two monatomic oxygen atoms (o) monatomic oxygen reacts with diatomic oxygen to form ozone (o3) uv light also splits ozone into o2 and o; the reactions form a dynamic equilibrium which absorbs uv (converting it to chemical energy / heat) useful: boiling points near ambient temperature so easily liquefied under low pressure (aerosol propellants, fridge / air-conditioning coolants); dissolve grease but do not damage electrical components (solvents); non-flammable; non-toxic persistence: chemically stable / unreactive in the troposphere so they remain long enough to be carried up to the stratosphere dissociation by uv: in the stratosphere higher uv levels break the carbon-chlorine bonds releasing chlorine free radicals chlorine reacts with monatomic oxygen (and ozone), preventing ozone formation; chlorine is regenerated so one atom destroys many ozone molecules; bromine / iodine act similarly uvb energy is absorbed by cells and breaks up biological molecules / dna causing mutations, skin cancer, cataracts, sunburn (human health) damage to leaf tissue and reduced photosynthesis / crop yields (plants) damage to marine algae, phytoplankton, corals and planktonic larvae (marine organisms), reducing productivity and food webs an area where the total ozone column falls below 220 dobson units (normal is about 300 du) - a level never recorded before cfc pollution concentration varies with altitude and the stratosphere is not normally sampled; dobson units measure the total column thickness instead the british antarctic survey at halley detected higher uv at ground level, showing ozone above had decreased (satellites above the stratosphere cannot measure downward uv directly)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"OZ-01\" data-qid=\"OZ-01\" data-marks=\"15\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">15 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/ozone.html\">Ozone depletion</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.3 &middot; Genn pp. 108-109 &middot; OZ-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Describe how ozone is formed and destroyed naturally in the stratosphere.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain the properties of CFCs that made them useful, and the properties that cause ozone depletion (the Rowland-Molina hypothesis).\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain why increased UVB reaching the Earth's surface is harmful to living organisms. Give <b>three</b> effects.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>State what is meant by an <b>ozone hole</b>.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.5</span>Give <b>one</b> reason why concentration in ppm is not used to measure ozone depletion.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.6</span>Give <b>one</b> reason why the first evidence for ozone depletion came from ground-based measurements.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>UV light splits diatomic oxygen (O2) into two monatomic oxygen atoms (O)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Monatomic oxygen reacts with diatomic oxygen to form ozone (O3)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>UV light also splits ozone into O2 and O; the reactions form a dynamic equilibrium which absorbs UV (converting it to chemical energy / heat)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 6 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Useful: boiling points near ambient temperature so easily liquefied under low pressure (aerosol propellants, fridge / air-conditioning coolants); dissolve grease but do not damage electrical components (solvents); non-flammable; non-toxic</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Persistence: chemically stable / unreactive in the troposphere so they remain long enough to be carried up to the stratosphere</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Dissociation by UV: in the stratosphere higher UV levels break the carbon-chlorine bonds releasing chlorine free radicals</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Chlorine reacts with monatomic oxygen (and ozone), preventing ozone formation; chlorine is regenerated so one atom destroys many ozone molecules; bromine / iodine act similarly</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>UVB energy is absorbed by cells and breaks up biological molecules / DNA causing mutations, skin cancer, cataracts, sunburn (human health)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Damage to leaf tissue and reduced photosynthesis / crop yields (plants)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Damage to marine algae, phytoplankton, corals and planktonic larvae (marine organisms), reducing productivity and food webs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>An area where the total ozone column falls below 220 Dobson Units (normal is about 300 DU) - a level never recorded before CFC pollution</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Concentration varies with altitude and the stratosphere is not normally sampled; Dobson Units measure the total column thickness instead</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.6</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The British Antarctic Survey at Halley detected higher UV at ground level, showing ozone above had decreased (satellites above the stratosphere cannot measure downward UV directly)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 108-109; AQA spec 3.2.1.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "OZ-02", "sub": "ozone", "subname": "Ozone depletion", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.2.1.3", "pages": "110-111", "url": "subtopic/ozone.html#OZ-02", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the minimum ozone concentration recorded over Antarctica each spring between 1979 and 2015.", "text": "figure 1 shows the minimum ozone concentration recorded over antarctica each spring between 1979 and 2015. describe the trend shown in figure 1. calculate the percentage decrease in minimum ozone between 1979 and 1995. explain why ozone depletion is most severe over antarctica in spring. explain why the data in figure 1 alone could not confirm that cfcs were the cause of the depletion, and describe how the cause was confirmed. minimum ozone fell steeply from about 225 du in 1979 to about 95 du in the mid 1990s since then it has fluctuated around 100-115 du with a slight recovery / no further decline (225 - 95) / 225 x 100 = 57.8% (accept 58%) stratospheric temperatures over antarctica are the lowest on earth so stratospheric clouds / ice crystals form ice crystal surfaces allow reactions that convert the chlorine released from cfcs into chlorine molecules (cl2) during the dark winter the polar vortex (rotating winds) prevents mixing with the rest of the atmosphere, keeping temperatures low and chlorine concentrated when sunlight returns in spring (september) it splits the chlorine molecules into free radicals that destroy ozone; levels recover by december as the vortex breaks down ground and satellite uv / ozone measurements show depletion but not the chemistry / correlation is not causation air samples collected from the stratosphere by helium balloons and high-flying research aircraft detected chlorine and chlorine monoxide, confirming the rowland-molina hypothesis", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"OZ-02\" data-qid=\"OZ-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/ozone.html\">Ozone depletion</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.3 &middot; Genn pp. 110-111 &middot; OZ-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the minimum ozone concentration recorded over Antarctica each spring between 1979 and 2015.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,82.5 113.3,107.5 166.7,165.8 220.0,178.3 273.3,190.8 326.7,186.7 380.0,182.5 433.3,178.3 486.7,186.7 540.0,174.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"82.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"113.3\" cy=\"107.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"166.7\" cy=\"165.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"220.0\" cy=\"178.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"273.3\" cy=\"190.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"326.7\" cy=\"186.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"380.0\" cy=\"182.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"433.3\" cy=\"178.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"486.7\" cy=\"186.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"174.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1979</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1986.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">60</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1993.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">120</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2000.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">180</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2007.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">240</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2015</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">300</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Year</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Minimum ozone / Dobson Units</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Describe the trend shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Calculate the percentage decrease in minimum ozone between 1979 and 1995.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain why ozone depletion is most severe over Antarctica in spring.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Explain why the data in Figure 1 alone could not confirm that CFCs were the cause of the depletion, and describe how the cause was confirmed.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Minimum ozone fell steeply from about 225 DU in 1979 to about 95 DU in the mid 1990s</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Since then it has fluctuated around 100-115 DU with a slight recovery / no further decline</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(225 - 95) / 225 x 100</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 57.8% (accept 58%)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stratospheric temperatures over Antarctica are the lowest on Earth so stratospheric clouds / ice crystals form</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ice crystal surfaces allow reactions that convert the chlorine released from CFCs into chlorine molecules (Cl2) during the dark winter</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The polar vortex (rotating winds) prevents mixing with the rest of the atmosphere, keeping temperatures low and chlorine concentrated</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>When sunlight returns in spring (September) it splits the chlorine molecules into free radicals that destroy ozone; levels recover by December as the vortex breaks down</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ground and satellite UV / ozone measurements show depletion but not the chemistry / correlation is not causation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Air samples collected from the stratosphere by helium balloons and high-flying research aircraft detected chlorine and chlorine monoxide, confirming the Rowland-Molina hypothesis</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 110-111; AQA spec 3.2.1.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "OZ-03", "sub": "ozone", "subname": "Ozone depletion", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.1.3", "pages": "112-113", "url": "subtopic/ozone.html#OZ-03", "snippet": "Describe the main features of the Montreal Protocol (1987).", "text": " describe the main features of the montreal protocol (1987). for each of the following cfc uses, give the alternative process or material now used and one disadvantage of the alternative. describe how waste cfcs from old refrigerators are disposed of safely. state one reason why the ozone layer is not expected to recover fully for many decades. manufacture and use of cfcs and other ozone-depleting substances (odss) phased out then banned hcfcs to be phased out (by 2030); some essential uses (eg halon fire extinguishers in aircraft) still permitted a fund to help (developing) countries implement the protocol; legally binding targets (unlike the 1985 vienna convention) aerosols: pump-action sprays / roll-on deodorants (alternative process), or hydrocarbon propellants (propane, butane) - flammable / fire risk refrigerants: hcfcs (less stable so break down in the troposphere, but still some ozone depletion) then hfcs (no chlorine) - more expensive, less effective, powerful greenhouse gases cfcs are drained from the appliance and incinerated / broken down at high temperature products are co2 and acidic gases (hcl, hf) which are neutralised with crushed lime cfcs are persistent, so those already released continue to reach the stratosphere and destroy ozone for many years; some odss (eg dichloromethane) are not banned", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"OZ-03\" data-qid=\"OZ-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/ozone.html\">Ozone depletion</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.3 &middot; Genn pp. 112-113 &middot; OZ-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Describe the main features of the Montreal Protocol (1987).\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>For each of the following CFC uses, give the alternative process or material now used and one disadvantage of the alternative.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Describe how waste CFCs from old refrigerators are disposed of safely.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>State <b>one</b> reason why the ozone layer is not expected to recover fully for many decades.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Manufacture and use of CFCs and other ozone-depleting substances (ODSs) phased out then banned</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>HCFCs to be phased out (by 2030); some essential uses (eg halon fire extinguishers in aircraft) still permitted</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A fund to help (developing) countries implement the Protocol; legally binding targets (unlike the 1985 Vienna Convention)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Aerosols: pump-action sprays / roll-on deodorants (alternative process), or hydrocarbon propellants (propane, butane) - flammable / fire risk</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Refrigerants: HCFCs (less stable so break down in the troposphere, but still some ozone depletion) then HFCs (no chlorine) - more expensive, less effective, powerful greenhouse gases</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>CFCs are drained from the appliance and incinerated / broken down at high temperature</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Products are CO2 and acidic gases (HCl, HF) which are neutralised with crushed lime</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>CFCs are persistent, so those already released continue to reach the stratosphere and destroy ozone for many years; some ODSs (eg dichloromethane) are not banned</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 112-113; AQA spec 3.2.1.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "OZ-04", "sub": "ozone", "subname": "Ozone depletion", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.2.1.3, 3.2.1.2", "pages": "108-113, 105-106", "url": "subtopic/ozone.html#OZ-04", "snippet": "Ozone depletion is often described as an environmental problem that has been tackled successfully. Compare the effectiveness of the methods used to restore the ozone layer with the", "text": " ozone depletion is often described as an environmental problem that has been tackled successfully. compare the effectiveness of the methods used to restore the ozone layer with the effectiveness of attempts to tackle global climate change, and explain the reasons for the difference. name the international agreement of 1985 that preceded the montreal protocol and state its main weakness. stages of scientific method illustrated by ozone: identification (antarctic uv data), hypothesis (rowland-molina 1974), data collection / analysis (dobson units, satellites, balloons confirming clo), proposed solutions, enactment (vienna 1985, montreal 1987) evidence of success: ozone hole area and depth stabilised and beginning to shrink; cfc concentrations in the atmosphere falling; uv levels not rising further; global ozone loss limited to about 4% reasons for success: international recognition of serious consequences (skin cancer); agreement of nearly every country; legally binding targets with a fund for developing countries; relatively few producers / products; alternatives (hcfcs, hfcs, pump sprays, hydrocarbons) already available and cheap; no lifestyle change needed limits: persistence of cfcs means recovery takes decades; some odss still used; hfc replacements are greenhouse gases climate change contrast: greenhouse gases come from almost every human activity (energy, transport, agriculture, industry) in every country; fossil fuels underpin economies so control requires major lifestyle and economic change agreements (kyoto, paris) have weaker / non-binding targets, disputes between developed and developing nations; emissions still rising; natural fluctuations, time delays and tipping points make cause-effect less clear to the public judgement: ozone control was a technical substitution problem; climate change is a systemic one, so the ozone success is only partly transferable vienna convention for the protection of the ozone layer - no legally binding goals", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"OZ-04\" data-qid=\"OZ-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/ozone.html\">Ozone depletion</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.3, 3.2.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 108-113, 105-106 &middot; OZ-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Ozone depletion is often described as an environmental problem that has been tackled successfully. Compare the effectiveness of the methods used to restore the ozone layer with the effectiveness of attempts to tackle global climate change, and explain the reasons for the difference.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Name the international agreement of 1985 that preceded the Montreal Protocol and state its main weakness.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Stages of scientific method illustrated by ozone: identification (Antarctic UV data), hypothesis (Rowland-Molina 1974), data collection / analysis (Dobson units, satellites, balloons confirming ClO), proposed solutions, enactment (Vienna 1985, Montreal 1987)</li><li>Evidence of success: ozone hole area and depth stabilised and beginning to shrink; CFC concentrations in the atmosphere falling; UV levels not rising further; global ozone loss limited to about 4%</li><li>Reasons for success: international recognition of serious consequences (skin cancer); agreement of nearly every country; legally binding targets with a fund for developing countries; relatively few producers / products; alternatives (HCFCs, HFCs, pump sprays, hydrocarbons) already available and cheap; no lifestyle change needed</li><li>Limits: persistence of CFCs means recovery takes decades; some ODSs still used; HFC replacements are greenhouse gases</li><li>Climate change contrast: greenhouse gases come from almost every human activity (energy, transport, agriculture, industry) in every country; fossil fuels underpin economies so control requires major lifestyle and economic change</li><li>Agreements (Kyoto, Paris) have weaker / non-binding targets, disputes between developed and developing nations; emissions still rising; natural fluctuations, time delays and tipping points make cause-effect less clear to the public</li><li>Judgement: ozone control was a technical substitution problem; climate change is a systemic one, so the ozone success is only partly transferable</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Vienna Convention for the Protection of the Ozone Layer - no legally binding goals</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 108-113, 105-106; AQA spec 3.2.1.3, 3.2.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "OZ-05", "sub": "ozone", "subname": "Ozone depletion", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.2.1.3", "pages": "108-112", "url": "subtopic/ozone.html#OZ-05", "snippet": "Which of the following statements about stratospheric ozone is correct? Tick one box.", "text": " which of the following statements about stratospheric ozone is correct? tick one box. explain the difference between the roles of ozone in the stratosphere and in the troposphere. explain why hcfcs cause less ozone depletion than cfcs. c stratospheric ozone is natural and beneficial: absorbs uv, protecting living organisms tropospheric ozone is a secondary pollutant (from nox photochemistry) that is toxic to plants and humans and is a greenhouse gas hcfcs are less chemically stable / contain hydrogen so they are broken down in the troposphere so most do not persist long enough to reach the stratosphere where uv would release their chlorine", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"OZ-05\" data-qid=\"OZ-05\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/ozone.html\">Ozone depletion</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.3 &middot; Genn pp. 108-112 &middot; OZ-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Which of the following statements about stratospheric ozone is correct? Tick one box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; Ozone is destroyed only by CFCs</li><li>B&nbsp; Ozone absorbs mainly infrared radiation</li><li>C&nbsp; Ozone is formed when monatomic oxygen reacts with diatomic oxygen</li><li>D&nbsp; Ozone concentrations are highest at ground level</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain the difference between the roles of ozone in the stratosphere and in the troposphere.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain why HCFCs cause less ozone depletion than CFCs.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b></b>&nbsp; C</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stratospheric ozone is natural and beneficial: absorbs UV, protecting living organisms</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Tropospheric ozone is a secondary pollutant (from NOx photochemistry) that is toxic to plants and humans and is a greenhouse gas</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>HCFCs are less chemically stable / contain hydrogen so they are broken down in the troposphere</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So most do not persist long enough to reach the stratosphere where UV would release their chlorine</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 108-112; AQA spec 3.2.1.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "BGC-01", "sub": "biogeochem", "subname": "Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.2.4.2", "pages": "148-149", "url": "subtopic/biogeochem.html#BGC-01", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the mass of carbon in the main reservoirs of the carbon cycle.", "text": "table 1 shows the mass of carbon in the main reservoirs of the carbon cycle. express the mass of carbon in the atmosphere in standard form, in tonnes. calculate the percentage increase in atmospheric carbon that would result from burning 10% of the fossil fuel reservoir, assuming all the carbon remained in the atmosphere. explain why the sedimentary carbonate rocks, although by far the largest reservoir, have little effect on atmospheric co2 concentrations. describe how carbon moves from the atmosphere into sedimentary carbonate rocks. explain why aerobic respiration returns carbon to the atmosphere as co2 but anaerobic respiration returns it mainly as methane. 7 x 1011 tonnes 10% of 10 000 = 1000 billion tonnes 1000 / 700 x 100 = 143% (accept 142.9) carbon is transferred into and out of rocks extremely slowly (fossilisation / sedimentation, weathering, volcanic release) residence time is millions of years so the flux to the atmosphere is tiny compared with respiration / photosynthesis / combustion co2 dissolves in the sea (forming carbonic acid / hydrogen carbonate ions) and is absorbed by phytoplankton through photosynthesis organisms such as molluscs, corals and planktonic organisms build calcium carbonate shells / exoskeletons when they die the shells sink and sediment on the seabed, becoming limestone / chalk over long periods aerobic respiration uses oxygen to break down organic compounds completely to co2 (and water), releasing much more energy without oxygen, anaerobic organisms only partly break down organic compounds, releasing carbon as methane (ch4), which is later oxidised to co2 in the atmosphere", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"BGC-01\" data-qid=\"BGC-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/biogeochem.html\">Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus</a> &middot; spec 3.2.4.2 &middot; Genn pp. 148-149 &middot; BGC-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the mass of carbon in the main reservoirs of the carbon cycle.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Reservoir</th><th>Mass of carbon / billion tonnes</th></tr><tr><td>Atmosphere (CO<sub>2</sub>)</td><td>700</td></tr><tr><td>Living organisms</td><td>470</td></tr><tr><td>Dead organic matter (soil)</td><td>3 700</td></tr><tr><td>Surface ocean waters</td><td>500</td></tr><tr><td>Deep ocean (dissolved)</td><td>35 000</td></tr><tr><td>Fossil fuels</td><td>10 000</td></tr><tr><td>Sedimentary carbonate rocks</td><td>20 000 000</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Express the mass of carbon in the atmosphere in standard form, in tonnes.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Calculate the percentage increase in atmospheric carbon that would result from burning 10% of the fossil fuel reservoir, assuming all the carbon remained in the atmosphere.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain why the sedimentary carbonate rocks, although by far the largest reservoir, have little effect on atmospheric CO<sub>2</sub> concentrations.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Describe how carbon moves from the atmosphere into sedimentary carbonate rocks.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.5</span>Explain why aerobic respiration returns carbon to the atmosphere as CO<sub>2</sub> but anaerobic respiration returns it mainly as methane.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>7 x 10<sup>11</sup> tonnes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>10% of 10 000 = 1000 billion tonnes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>1000 / 700 x 100 = 143% (accept 142.9)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Carbon is transferred into and out of rocks extremely slowly (fossilisation / sedimentation, weathering, volcanic release)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Residence time is millions of years so the flux to the atmosphere is tiny compared with respiration / photosynthesis / combustion</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>CO2 dissolves in the sea (forming carbonic acid / hydrogen carbonate ions) and is absorbed by phytoplankton through photosynthesis</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Organisms such as molluscs, corals and planktonic organisms build calcium carbonate shells / exoskeletons</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>When they die the shells sink and sediment on the seabed, becoming limestone / chalk over long periods</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Aerobic respiration uses oxygen to break down organic compounds completely to CO2 (and water), releasing much more energy</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Without oxygen, anaerobic organisms only partly break down organic compounds, releasing carbon as methane (CH4), which is later oxidised to CO2 in the atmosphere</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 148-149; AQA spec 3.2.4.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "BGC-02", "sub": "biogeochem", "subname": "Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.4.2", "pages": "150-151", "url": "subtopic/biogeochem.html#BGC-02", "snippet": "Explain how each of the following human activities changes the amount of carbon in the atmosphere.", "text": " explain how each of the following human activities changes the amount of carbon in the atmosphere. give three anaerobic environments created by human activities that release methane. explain how rising atmospheric co2 causes ocean acidification. ploughing: increases the oxygen supply to soil decomposers so aerobic decomposition of dead organic matter is faster; carbon in the soil dom store falls and atmospheric co2 rises draining: waterlogged anaerobic conditions preserved dead organic matter (peat) and produced methane slowly; drainage makes the soil aerobic so stored carbon is rapidly decomposed to co2 (though methane release stops) marine pollution: kills phytoplankton so less dissolved co2 is absorbed by photosynthesis and less carbon sinks to sediments in dead plankton; less co2 then dissolves from the atmosphere to replace it, so atmospheric co2 rises rice paddy fields landfill sites anaerobic sediments in reservoirs livestock intestines (cattle) manure / slurry stores more co2 dissolves in the sea, forming carbonic acid which dissociates into hydrogen carbonate and hydrogen ions; more h+ lowers the ph", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"BGC-02\" data-qid=\"BGC-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/biogeochem.html\">Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus</a> &middot; spec 3.2.4.2 &middot; Genn pp. 150-151 &middot; BGC-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain how each of the following human activities changes the amount of carbon in the atmosphere.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Give <b>three</b> anaerobic environments created by human activities that release methane.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain how rising atmospheric CO<sub>2</sub> causes ocean acidification.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Ploughing: increases the oxygen supply to soil decomposers so aerobic decomposition of dead organic matter is faster; carbon in the soil DOM store falls and atmospheric CO2 rises</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Draining: waterlogged anaerobic conditions preserved dead organic matter (peat) and produced methane slowly; drainage makes the soil aerobic so stored carbon is rapidly decomposed to CO2 (though methane release stops)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Marine pollution: kills phytoplankton so less dissolved CO2 is absorbed by photosynthesis and less carbon sinks to sediments in dead plankton; less CO2 then dissolves from the atmosphere to replace it, so atmospheric CO2 rises</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Rice paddy fields</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Landfill sites</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Anaerobic sediments in reservoirs</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Livestock intestines (cattle)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Manure / slurry stores</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>More CO2 dissolves in the sea, forming carbonic acid which dissociates into hydrogen carbonate and hydrogen ions; more H+ lowers the pH</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 150-151; AQA spec 3.2.4.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "BGC-03", "sub": "biogeochem", "subname": "Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.4.2", "pages": "151-152", "url": "subtopic/biogeochem.html#BGC-03", "snippet": "Explain why planting a new forest only sequesters carbon for a limited period, and how the storage can be extended.", "text": " explain why planting a new forest only sequesters carbon for a limited period, and how the storage can be extended. compare pre-combustion and post-combustion methods of carbon capture. describe two ways captured co2 can be stored and give one concern about long-term storage. trees absorb co2 by photosynthesis and store carbon in wood as they grow once mature there is no further net storage (photosynthesis balanced by respiration / decomposition), though the standing forest remains a reservoir harvesting the timber and keeping it as wood (eg house construction) then replanting produces additional storage as the new trees grow pre-combustion: the fuel is modified before use so no co2 is released when burnt - eg gasification of coal to hydrogen and co2 (co2 stored, hydrogen distributed); oxy-fuel combustion in pure oxygen giving only co2 and water vapour which is condensed out advantage: capture at a large central facility; suits small dispersed sources (vehicles) that cannot capture co2 post-combustion: co2 removed from exhaust gases by solvents, membrane filtration, adsorption / desorption, cryogenic separation, graphene disadvantage: only about 20% of flue gas is co2 so separation is costly / energy-intensive; none fully developed injected into depleted oil / gas fields or saline aquifers injected into oil reservoirs to provide pressure for secondary oil recovery reaction with fractured basalt to form solid carbonate minerals concern: leakage back to the atmosphere / monitoring over centuries; limited suitable geology; cost", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"BGC-03\" data-qid=\"BGC-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/biogeochem.html\">Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus</a> &middot; spec 3.2.4.2 &middot; Genn pp. 151-152 &middot; BGC-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Explain why planting a new forest only sequesters carbon for a limited period, and how the storage can be extended.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Compare pre-combustion and post-combustion methods of carbon capture.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Describe <b>two</b> ways captured CO<sub>2</sub> can be stored and give <b>one</b> concern about long-term storage.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Trees absorb CO2 by photosynthesis and store carbon in wood as they grow</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Once mature there is no further net storage (photosynthesis balanced by respiration / decomposition), though the standing forest remains a reservoir</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Harvesting the timber and keeping it as wood (eg house construction) then replanting produces additional storage as the new trees grow</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pre-combustion: the fuel is modified before use so no CO2 is released when burnt - eg gasification of coal to hydrogen and CO2 (CO2 stored, hydrogen distributed); oxy-fuel combustion in pure oxygen giving only CO2 and water vapour which is condensed out</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Advantage: capture at a large central facility; suits small dispersed sources (vehicles) that cannot capture CO2</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Post-combustion: CO2 removed from exhaust gases by solvents, membrane filtration, adsorption / desorption, cryogenic separation, graphene</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Disadvantage: only about 20% of flue gas is CO2 so separation is costly / energy-intensive; none fully developed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Injected into depleted oil / gas fields or saline aquifers</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Injected into oil reservoirs to provide pressure for secondary oil recovery</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reaction with fractured basalt to form solid carbonate minerals</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Concern: leakage back to the atmosphere / monitoring over centuries; limited suitable geology; cost</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 151-152; AQA spec 3.2.4.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "BGC-04", "sub": "biogeochem", "subname": "Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.2.4.3", "pages": "152-154", "url": "subtopic/biogeochem.html#BGC-04", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows a simplified nitrogen cycle with some processes labelled 1 to 6.", "text": "figure 1 shows a simplified nitrogen cycle with some processes labelled 1 to 6. name processes 1 to 6. explain why draining a waterlogged field increases the nitrate available to crops. explain why nitrogen is described as a resource that is 'not very abundant' for living organisms even though the atmosphere is 78% nitrogen. explain why growing legumes such as clover in a crop rotation reduces the need for artificial fertiliser. 1 nitrogen fixation; 2 nitrification 3 denitrification; 4 ammonification 5 ionisation; 6 leaching (3 marks for all six, 2 for four or five, 1 for two or three) drainage makes the soil aerobic aerobic nitrifying bacteria (and nitrogen fixers) increase so more ammonium is converted to nitrate anaerobic denitrifying bacteria decline so less nitrate is lost as nitrogen gas nitrogen gas is very unreactive so most organisms cannot use it directly only nitrogen fixation (bacteria, lightning, haber process) converts it to usable ammonium / nitrate; the cycle recycles a small usable amount rhizobium bacteria in their root nodules fix atmospheric nitrogen into ammonium / proteins when the plants die / are ploughed in, decomposition and nitrification release nitrates for the following crop", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"BGC-04\" data-qid=\"BGC-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/biogeochem.html\">Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus</a> &middot; spec 3.2.4.3 &middot; Genn pp. 152-154 &middot; BGC-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows a simplified nitrogen cycle with some processes labelled 1 to 6.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Number</th><th>Process description</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Nitrogen gas converted to ammonium ions by Rhizobium in root nodules</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Ammonium ions oxidised to nitrite then nitrate by Nitrosomonas and Nitrobacter</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Nitrates reduced to nitrogen gas by Pseudomonas in anaerobic soil</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Amino groups in dead organic matter released as ammonium ions by decomposers</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Nitrogen and oxygen react during lightning to form NOx</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Nitrates washed out of soil into rivers</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Name processes 1 to 6.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain why draining a waterlogged field increases the nitrate available to crops.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain why nitrogen is described as a resource that is 'not very abundant' for living organisms even though the atmosphere is 78% nitrogen.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Explain why growing legumes such as clover in a crop rotation reduces the need for artificial fertiliser.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>1 nitrogen fixation; 2 nitrification</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>3 denitrification; 4 ammonification</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>5 ionisation; 6 leaching (3 marks for all six, 2 for four or five, 1 for two or three)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Drainage makes the soil aerobic</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Aerobic nitrifying bacteria (and nitrogen fixers) increase so more ammonium is converted to nitrate</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Anaerobic denitrifying bacteria decline so less nitrate is lost as nitrogen gas</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nitrogen gas is very unreactive so most organisms cannot use it directly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Only nitrogen fixation (bacteria, lightning, Haber process) converts it to usable ammonium / nitrate; the cycle recycles a small usable amount</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Rhizobium bacteria in their root nodules fix atmospheric nitrogen into ammonium / proteins</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>When the plants die / are ploughed in, decomposition and nitrification release nitrates for the following crop</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 152-154; AQA spec 3.2.4.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "BGC-05", "sub": "biogeochem", "subname": "Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.2.4.3", "pages": "154-155", "url": "subtopic/biogeochem.html#BGC-05", "snippet": "Table 1 shows global reactive nitrogen fixed each year by natural processes and by human activities.", "text": "table 1 shows global reactive nitrogen fixed each year by natural processes and by human activities. use table 1 to calculate the percentage of total nitrogen fixation that is caused by human activities. describe the haber process and explain why it is unsustainable compared with biological nitrogen fixation. describe three consequences of the increased movement of nitrogen compounds caused by human activities. explain how composting or anaerobic digestion of biological wastes produces a better fertiliser than raw waste. human = 120 + 60 + 30 = 210; total = 325 210 / 325 x 100 = 64.6% (accept 65%) nitrogen and hydrogen react to form ammonia over an iron catalyst at high temperature and pressure; ammonia then converted to nitrates for fertiliser uses large amounts of energy from fossil fuels (also the hydrogen source), releasing co2 and nox biological fixation by bacteria occurs at low temperature using solar energy captured by plants (enzymes lower activation energy) eutrophication of water bodies by leached nitrates / sewage photochemical smogs and tropospheric ozone from nox acid rain (nitric acid) nitrous oxide as a greenhouse gas nox toxicity / respiratory disease nitrate in drinking water (methaemoglobinaemia) carbon is lost as co2 (composting) or ch4 (digestion) so the c:n ratio improves / more nitrogen-rich bulk and odour reduced, pathogens killed, easier to apply; digestion also yields biogas", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"BGC-05\" data-qid=\"BGC-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/biogeochem.html\">Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus</a> &middot; spec 3.2.4.3 &middot; Genn pp. 154-155 &middot; BGC-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows global reactive nitrogen fixed each year by natural processes and by human activities.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Source</th><th>Nitrogen fixed / million tonnes per year</th></tr><tr><td>Biological fixation (natural ecosystems)</td><td>110</td></tr><tr><td>Lightning</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Haber process (fertiliser manufacture)</td><td>120</td></tr><tr><td>Cultivation of legume crops</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>Combustion (NOx)</td><td>30</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Use Table 1 to calculate the percentage of total nitrogen fixation that is caused by human activities.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Describe the Haber process and explain why it is unsustainable compared with biological nitrogen fixation.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Describe <b>three</b> consequences of the increased movement of nitrogen compounds caused by human activities.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Explain how composting or anaerobic digestion of biological wastes produces a better fertiliser than raw waste.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Human = 120 + 60 + 30 = 210; total = 325</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>210 / 325 x 100 = 64.6% (accept 65%)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nitrogen and hydrogen react to form ammonia over an iron catalyst at high temperature and pressure; ammonia then converted to nitrates for fertiliser</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Uses large amounts of energy from fossil fuels (also the hydrogen source), releasing CO2 and NOx</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Biological fixation by bacteria occurs at low temperature using solar energy captured by plants (enzymes lower activation energy)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Eutrophication of water bodies by leached nitrates / sewage</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Photochemical smogs and tropospheric ozone from NOx</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Acid rain (nitric acid)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nitrous oxide as a greenhouse gas</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>NOx toxicity / respiratory disease</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nitrate in drinking water (methaemoglobinaemia)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Carbon is lost as CO2 (composting) or CH4 (digestion) so the C:N ratio improves / more nitrogen-rich</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bulk and odour reduced, pathogens killed, easier to apply; digestion also yields biogas</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 154-155; AQA spec 3.2.4.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "BGC-06", "sub": "biogeochem", "subname": "Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.4.4", "pages": "156-157", "url": "subtopic/biogeochem.html#BGC-06", "snippet": "Explain why phosphorus is more likely than nitrogen to be a limiting factor on biological productivity.", "text": " explain why phosphorus is more likely than nitrogen to be a limiting factor on biological productivity. explain how mycorrhizal fungi increase phosphate uptake by plants. describe how human activities have altered the phosphorus cycle. explain two strategies for the sustainable management of phosphorus supplies. there is no gaseous / atmospheric reservoir of phosphorus (no equivalent of nitrogen fixation) most phosphorus compounds have low solubility so little is available in soil water phosphorus lost to marine sediments only returns through very slow processes: mountain building (uplift) and weathering of rocks fungal hyphae form a symbiotic relationship with plant roots and spread through a large volume of soil greatly increasing the surface area for absorbing (poorly mobile) phosphate ions, passed to the plant in exchange for carbohydrates mining of calcium phosphate rock (and past mining of guano deposits, eg peru, nauru, now largely exhausted) treated to make more soluble ammonium phosphate fertilisers, mobilising phosphorus into the environment fertiliser runoff / eroded soil / sewage effluent carry phosphates into water bodies causing eutrophication (with nitrates); harvesting removes phosphorus from farmland use biological wastes (manure, sewage sludge, compost) as fertiliser to return phosphates rather than mining rock breed crops that absorb phosphate more efficiently maintain conditions for mycorrhizal fungi (less tillage, fewer fungicides, organic matter) recover phosphate from sewage effluent by iron(iii) sulfate precipitation; reduce soil erosion", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"BGC-06\" data-qid=\"BGC-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/biogeochem.html\">Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus</a> &middot; spec 3.2.4.4 &middot; Genn pp. 156-157 &middot; BGC-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Explain why phosphorus is more likely than nitrogen to be a limiting factor on biological productivity.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain how mycorrhizal fungi increase phosphate uptake by plants.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Describe how human activities have altered the phosphorus cycle.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Explain <b>two</b> strategies for the sustainable management of phosphorus supplies.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>There is no gaseous / atmospheric reservoir of phosphorus (no equivalent of nitrogen fixation)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Most phosphorus compounds have low solubility so little is available in soil water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Phosphorus lost to marine sediments only returns through very slow processes: mountain building (uplift) and weathering of rocks</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fungal hyphae form a symbiotic relationship with plant roots and spread through a large volume of soil</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Greatly increasing the surface area for absorbing (poorly mobile) phosphate ions, passed to the plant in exchange for carbohydrates</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mining of calcium phosphate rock (and past mining of guano deposits, eg Peru, Nauru, now largely exhausted)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Treated to make more soluble ammonium phosphate fertilisers, mobilising phosphorus into the environment</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fertiliser runoff / eroded soil / sewage effluent carry phosphates into water bodies causing eutrophication (with nitrates); harvesting removes phosphorus from farmland</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Use biological wastes (manure, sewage sludge, compost) as fertiliser to return phosphates rather than mining rock</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Breed crops that absorb phosphate more efficiently</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Maintain conditions for mycorrhizal fungi (less tillage, fewer fungicides, organic matter)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Recover phosphate from sewage effluent by iron(III) sulfate precipitation; reduce soil erosion</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 156-157; AQA spec 3.2.4.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "BGC-07", "sub": "biogeochem", "subname": "Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.2.4", "pages": "148-157", "url": "subtopic/biogeochem.html#BGC-07", "snippet": "Describe how human activities have altered the natural equilibria of the carbon and nitrogen cycles, and evaluate the strategies that could counteract these changes.", "text": " describe how human activities have altered the natural equilibria of the carbon and nitrogen cycles, and evaluate the strategies that could counteract these changes. state what is meant by the residence time of a substance in a reservoir. carbon: combustion of fossil fuels and biomass moving carbon from long-term stores to the atmosphere; deforestation reducing photosynthesis; ploughing / drainage increasing aerobic decomposition; new anaerobic environments (paddies, landfill, reservoirs, livestock) releasing methane; marine pollution reducing phytoplankton; consequences: rising co2 and ch4, climate change, ocean acidification, reduced soil organic matter nitrogen: haber process doubling fixation; legume cultivation; drainage increasing nitrification and reducing denitrification; combustion nox; sewage and fertiliser leaching; consequences: eutrophication, smogs, acid rain, n2o warming, nox toxicity counter-strategies carbon: renewables / nuclear replacing fossil fuels, energy conservation, afforestation matched to deforestation and timber use, conserving peat bogs and forests, increasing soil organic matter (low tillage, mulching), ccs (pre / post combustion, storage) counter-strategies nitrogen: reduced combustion, catalytic converters / urea sprays, biological fixation instead of haber, legumes and crop rotation, management of biological wastes as fertiliser, low-solubility fertilisers, timing, buffer strips, low tillage, nitrate vulnerable zones evaluation: some strategies well established and cheap (rotation, buffer strips), others expensive or unproven (ccs); scale of fossil fuel dependence; feedback and time lags; link to circular economy and low-temperature natural processes; judgement the average time an atom / molecule remains in the reservoir before being transferred out (volume / transfer rate)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"BGC-07\" data-qid=\"BGC-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/biogeochem.html\">Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus</a> &middot; spec 3.2.4 &middot; Genn pp. 148-157 &middot; BGC-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Describe how human activities have altered the natural equilibria of the carbon and nitrogen cycles, and evaluate the strategies that could counteract these changes.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>State what is meant by the <b>residence time</b> of a substance in a reservoir.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Carbon: combustion of fossil fuels and biomass moving carbon from long-term stores to the atmosphere; deforestation reducing photosynthesis; ploughing / drainage increasing aerobic decomposition; new anaerobic environments (paddies, landfill, reservoirs, livestock) releasing methane; marine pollution reducing phytoplankton; consequences: rising CO2 and CH4, climate change, ocean acidification, reduced soil organic matter</li><li>Nitrogen: Haber process doubling fixation; legume cultivation; drainage increasing nitrification and reducing denitrification; combustion NOx; sewage and fertiliser leaching; consequences: eutrophication, smogs, acid rain, N2O warming, NOx toxicity</li><li>Counter-strategies carbon: renewables / nuclear replacing fossil fuels, energy conservation, afforestation matched to deforestation and timber use, conserving peat bogs and forests, increasing soil organic matter (low tillage, mulching), CCS (pre / post combustion, storage)</li><li>Counter-strategies nitrogen: reduced combustion, catalytic converters / urea sprays, biological fixation instead of Haber, legumes and crop rotation, management of biological wastes as fertiliser, low-solubility fertilisers, timing, buffer strips, low tillage, nitrate vulnerable zones</li><li>Evaluation: some strategies well established and cheap (rotation, buffer strips), others expensive or unproven (CCS); scale of fossil fuel dependence; feedback and time lags; link to circular economy and low-temperature natural processes; judgement</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The average time an atom / molecule remains in the reservoir before being transferred out (volume / transfer rate)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 148-157; AQA spec 3.2.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "BGC-08", "sub": "biogeochem", "subname": "Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 5, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.2.4.2", "pages": "148-151", "url": "subtopic/biogeochem.html#BGC-08", "snippet": "A student measured the mass of carbon stored per hectare in three land uses in the same region.", "text": "a student measured the mass of carbon stored per hectare in three land uses in the same region. calculate the total carbon lost per hectare when mature woodland is converted to arable farmland. explain why the soil carbon in arable land is so much lower than in pasture, even though the vegetation carbon is similar. suggest two ways the arable farmer could increase soil carbon storage. (120 + 95) - (2 + 45) = 168 t ha-1 annual ploughing aerates the soil so aerobic decomposers break down dead organic matter faster, releasing co2 harvest removes biomass so less dead organic matter is returned; pasture roots and litter constantly add organic matter low / zero tillage adding manure, compost or mulch growing cover crops / leaving crop residues converting to long-term grass or tree crops in rotation", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"BGC-08\" data-qid=\"BGC-08\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 8</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/biogeochem.html\">Biogeochemical cycles: carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus</a> &middot; spec 3.2.4.2 &middot; Genn pp. 148-151 &middot; BGC-08</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A student measured the mass of carbon stored per hectare in three land uses in the same region.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Land use</th><th>Carbon in vegetation / t ha<sup>-1</sup></th><th>Carbon in soil dead organic matter / t ha<sup>-1</sup></th></tr><tr><td>Mature woodland</td><td>120</td><td>95</td></tr><tr><td>Permanent pasture</td><td>3</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>Arable (ploughed annually)</td><td>2</td><td>45</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.1</span>Calculate the total carbon lost per hectare when mature woodland is converted to arable farmland.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.2</span>Explain why the soil carbon in arable land is so much lower than in pasture, even though the vegetation carbon is similar.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.3</span>Suggest <b>two</b> ways the arable farmer could increase soil carbon storage.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(120 + 95) - (2 + 45) = 168 t ha<sup>-1</sup></span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Annual ploughing aerates the soil so aerobic decomposers break down dead organic matter faster, releasing CO2</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Harvest removes biomass so less dead organic matter is returned; pasture roots and litter constantly add organic matter</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Low / zero tillage</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Adding manure, compost or mulch</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Growing cover crops / leaving crop residues</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Converting to long-term grass or tree crops in rotation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 148-151; AQA spec 3.2.4.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CC-01", "sub": "climate-change", "subname": "Global climate change", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.2.1.2", "pages": "89-90", "url": "subtopic/climate-change.html#CC-01", "snippet": "Table 1 shows information about some greenhouse gases.", "text": "table 1 shows information about some greenhouse gases. explain the natural greenhouse effect. use table 1 to explain why carbon dioxide is considered the most important anthropogenic greenhouse gas even though methane and cfcs absorb far more infrared per molecule. explain why reducing methane emissions would produce a faster reduction in warming than reducing co2 emissions by the same mass. give two methods of reducing methane emissions from agriculture or waste. visible light passes through the atmosphere easily and is absorbed by the earth's surface, which warms and emits infrared greenhouse gases (naturally water vapour and co2) absorb infrared better than the average for the atmosphere, converting it to heat and re-emitting some back to the surface this keeps the surface about 33 °c warmer than it would otherwise be co2 is emitted in far larger quantities (every combustion process, deforestation) so its total effect is greatest co2 has a long residence time so it accumulates; methane has a short residence time (12 years) so it is removed faster cfc emissions are now very small following the montreal protocol methane has a much higher global warming potential (28x) so each tonne removed has a bigger effect its short residence time (about 12 years) means atmospheric concentrations fall quickly once emissions are cut collect landfill / anaerobic digester gas and use as fuel changes in cattle diet / fewer cattle / less meat consumption draining rice paddies periodically anaerobic digestion of manure with gas capture", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CC-01\" data-qid=\"CC-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/climate-change.html\">Global climate change</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 89-90 &middot; CC-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows information about some greenhouse gases.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Gas</th><th>Main anthropogenic sources</th><th>Approximate residence time</th><th>Global warming potential (per molecule, relative to CO<sub>2</sub> over 100 years)</th></tr><tr><td>Carbon dioxide</td><td>Fossil fuel combustion, deforestation, cement</td><td>100+ years (variable)</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Methane</td><td>Rice paddies, cattle, landfill, gas leaks, coal mines</td><td>About 12 years</td><td>28</td></tr><tr><td>Nitrous oxide (NOx)</td><td>Fertilisers, combustion</td><td>About 120 years</td><td>265</td></tr><tr><td>CFCs</td><td>Refrigerants, aerosols (now banned)</td><td>50-100+ years</td><td>5 000-10 000</td></tr><tr><td>Tropospheric ozone</td><td>Secondary pollutant from NOx and hydrocarbons</td><td>Days-weeks</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Explain the natural greenhouse effect.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Use Table 1 to explain why carbon dioxide is considered the most important anthropogenic greenhouse gas even though methane and CFCs absorb far more infrared per molecule.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain why reducing methane emissions would produce a faster reduction in warming than reducing CO<sub>2</sub> emissions by the same mass.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Give <b>two</b> methods of reducing methane emissions from agriculture or waste.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Visible light passes through the atmosphere easily and is absorbed by the Earth's surface, which warms and emits infrared</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Greenhouse gases (naturally water vapour and CO2) absorb infrared better than the average for the atmosphere, converting it to heat and re-emitting some back to the surface</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>This keeps the surface about 33 &deg;C warmer than it would otherwise be</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>CO2 is emitted in far larger quantities (every combustion process, deforestation) so its total effect is greatest</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>CO2 has a long residence time so it accumulates; methane has a short residence time (12 years) so it is removed faster</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>CFC emissions are now very small following the Montreal Protocol</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Methane has a much higher global warming potential (28x) so each tonne removed has a bigger effect</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Its short residence time (about 12 years) means atmospheric concentrations fall quickly once emissions are cut</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Collect landfill / anaerobic digester gas and use as fuel</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Changes in cattle diet / fewer cattle / less meat consumption</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Draining rice paddies periodically</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Anaerobic digestion of manure with gas capture</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 89-90; AQA spec 3.2.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CC-02", "sub": "climate-change", "subname": "Global climate change", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.2.1.2", "pages": "94-96", "url": "subtopic/climate-change.html#CC-02", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the area of Arctic sea ice in September (the annual minimum) in selected years. Figure 1 shows the mean thickness of Arctic sea ice in the same years.", "text": "table 1 shows the area of arctic sea ice in september (the annual minimum) in selected years. figure 1 shows the mean thickness of arctic sea ice in the same years. calculate the percentage decrease in september sea ice area between 1980 and 2020. explain why the loss of sea ice does not directly raise sea level, but the loss of land ice does. explain how the changes shown in table 1 and figure 1 could lead to further warming. explain two ways in which rising temperatures cause sea level to rise. explain why the break-up of an ice shelf can increase the rate at which land ice flows into the sea. (7.7 - 3.9) / 7.7 x 100 = 49.4% (accept 49%) floating sea ice has already displaced its own mass of water; when it melts it contracts and occupies the same volume as the ice below the water line melting land ice (glaciers, ice sheets) adds new water to the sea, increasing its volume ice and snow have a high albedo and reflect most sunlight; open sea water has a low albedo as ice area shrinks more sunlight is absorbed by the sea and converted to heat, raising temperatures this melts more ice: a positive feedback mechanism; thinner ice melts more easily each summer thermal expansion: warmer sea water expands (slow because of the huge volume and high heat capacity; only surface water warms directly) melting of land ice (glaciers, greenland and antarctic ice sheets) adds water to the oceans ice shelves grounded on the seabed hold back / block the forward movement of the glaciers or ice sheet behind them; once removed the land ice flows faster (eg west antarctica)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CC-02\" data-qid=\"CC-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/climate-change.html\">Global climate change</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 94-96 &middot; CC-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the area of Arctic sea ice in September (the annual minimum) in selected years. Figure 1 shows the mean thickness of Arctic sea ice in the same years.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Year</th><th>1980</th><th>1990</th><th>2000</th><th>2010</th><th>2020</th></tr><tr><td>September sea ice area / million km<sup>2</sup></td><td>7.7</td><td>6.2</td><td>6.3</td><td>4.9</td><td>3.9</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<rect x=\"74.4\" y=\"42.7\" width=\"67.2\" height=\"227.3\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"108.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1980</text>\n<rect x=\"170.4\" y=\"74.3\" width=\"67.2\" height=\"195.7\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"204.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1990</text>\n<rect x=\"266.4\" y=\"105.9\" width=\"67.2\" height=\"164.1\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2000</text>\n<rect x=\"362.4\" y=\"143.7\" width=\"67.2\" height=\"126.3\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"396.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2010</text>\n<rect x=\"458.4\" y=\"169.0\" width=\"67.2\" height=\"101.0\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"492.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2020</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0.792</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">1.584</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">2.376</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">3.168</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">3.96</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Year</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Mean ice thickness / m</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Calculate the percentage decrease in September sea ice area between 1980 and 2020.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain why the loss of sea ice does <b>not</b> directly raise sea level, but the loss of land ice does.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain how the changes shown in Table 1 and Figure 1 could lead to further warming.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Explain <b>two</b> ways in which rising temperatures cause sea level to rise.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.5</span>Explain why the break-up of an ice shelf can increase the rate at which land ice flows into the sea.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(7.7 - 3.9) / 7.7 x 100</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 49.4% (accept 49%)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Floating sea ice has already displaced its own mass of water; when it melts it contracts and occupies the same volume as the ice below the water line</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Melting land ice (glaciers, ice sheets) adds new water to the sea, increasing its volume</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ice and snow have a high albedo and reflect most sunlight; open sea water has a low albedo</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>As ice area shrinks more sunlight is absorbed by the sea and converted to heat, raising temperatures</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>This melts more ice: a positive feedback mechanism; thinner ice melts more easily each summer</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Thermal expansion: warmer sea water expands (slow because of the huge volume and high heat capacity; only surface water warms directly)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Melting of land ice (glaciers, Greenland and Antarctic ice sheets) adds water to the oceans</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ice shelves grounded on the seabed hold back / block the forward movement of the glaciers or ice sheet behind them; once removed the land ice flows faster (eg West Antarctica)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 94-96; AQA spec 3.2.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CC-03", "sub": "climate-change", "subname": "Global climate change", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.2.1.2, 3.2.2.3", "pages": "96-99", "url": "subtopic/climate-change.html#CC-03", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the sea surface temperature anomaly in the eastern Pacific off Peru and the annual Peruvian anchovy catch for a series of years including a strong El Niño event in 1", "text": "figure 1 shows the sea surface temperature anomaly in the eastern pacific off peru and the annual peruvian anchovy catch for a series of years including a strong el niño event in 1997-98. describe the normal (non-el niño) pattern of winds and ocean currents in the tropical pacific and explain why the peruvian coast supports a rich fishery. explain how an el niño event caused the collapse of the anchovy catch in 1998 shown in figure 1. describe two other effects of el niño events. suggest why some scientists link global climate change to changes in the frequency of el niño events, and why this link is uncertain. trade winds blow westwards across the pacific near the equator, driving a surface current in the same direction surface water moving away from south america is replaced by deep, cold water drawn upwards (upwelling) near the coast the upwelling water is rich in nutrients (nitrates, phosphates) so large algal / phytoplankton blooms occur these support a rich food web including the anchovy fishery; the water warms as it travels west the trade winds weaken or reverse so the surface current slows / reverses and warm water spreads east upwelling of nutrient-rich cold water off south america stops phytoplankton productivity collapses so the food web / anchovy population collapses; catch fell from about 7.7 to 1.7 million tonnes heavy rain and floods in the normally dry coastal deserts of peru / south america drought and reduced rainfall in eastern australia / indonesia droughts in ne africa, southern africa, china fewer hurricanes in the north atlantic / fewer cyclones in japan events appear to be occurring more frequently and are driven by wind patterns that warming may alter, but the causes of enso are not fully understood and natural fluctuations make trends hard to separate", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CC-03\" data-qid=\"CC-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/climate-change.html\">Global climate change</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.2, 3.2.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 96-99 &middot; CC-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the sea surface temperature anomaly in the eastern Pacific off Peru and the annual Peruvian anchovy catch for a series of years including a strong El Ni&ntilde;o event in 1997-98.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,39.7 128.6,103.6 197.1,97.8 265.7,121.0 334.3,237.1 402.9,101.7 471.4,51.3 540.0,130.7\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"39.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"128.6\" cy=\"103.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"197.1\" cy=\"97.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"265.7\" cy=\"121.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"334.3\" cy=\"237.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"402.9\" cy=\"101.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"471.4\" cy=\"51.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"130.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1994</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1995.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">2.58</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1996.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">5.17</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1998.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">7.75</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1999.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">10.3</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2001</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">12.9</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Year</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Anchovy catch / million tonnes</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Describe the normal (non-El Ni&ntilde;o) pattern of winds and ocean currents in the tropical Pacific and explain why the Peruvian coast supports a rich fishery.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain how an El Ni&ntilde;o event caused the collapse of the anchovy catch in 1998 shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Describe <b>two</b> other effects of El Ni&ntilde;o events.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Suggest why some scientists link global climate change to changes in the frequency of El Ni&ntilde;o events, and why this link is uncertain.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Trade winds blow westwards across the Pacific near the equator, driving a surface current in the same direction</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Surface water moving away from South America is replaced by deep, cold water drawn upwards (upwelling) near the coast</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The upwelling water is rich in nutrients (nitrates, phosphates) so large algal / phytoplankton blooms occur</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>These support a rich food web including the anchovy fishery; the water warms as it travels west</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The trade winds weaken or reverse so the surface current slows / reverses and warm water spreads east</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Upwelling of nutrient-rich cold water off South America stops</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Phytoplankton productivity collapses so the food web / anchovy population collapses; catch fell from about 7.7 to 1.7 million tonnes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Heavy rain and floods in the normally dry coastal deserts of Peru / South America</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Drought and reduced rainfall in eastern Australia / Indonesia</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Droughts in NE Africa, southern Africa, China</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fewer hurricanes in the North Atlantic / fewer cyclones in Japan</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Events appear to be occurring more frequently and are driven by wind patterns that warming may alter, but the causes of ENSO are not fully understood and natural fluctuations make trends hard to separate</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 96-99; AQA spec 3.2.1.2, 3.2.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CC-04", "sub": "climate-change", "subname": "Global climate change", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.1.2, 3.2.2.3", "pages": "96-97", "url": "subtopic/climate-change.html#CC-04", "snippet": "Describe the two processes that drive the North Atlantic Conveyor.", "text": " describe the two processes that drive the north atlantic conveyor. explain how global climate change could weaken the north atlantic conveyor and describe the possible consequence for the climate of the uk. explain why the effects of climate change on uk temperatures are difficult to predict. describe how deep ocean currents are monitored. friction with the prevailing south-westerly winds drags surface water north-eastwards from the tropical atlantic water in the ne atlantic cools, becomes denser (and more saline as ice forms) and sinks, drawing warm surface water in to replace it; the cold water returns southwards at depth (thermohaline circulation) higher temperatures melt land ice on greenland which flows into the north atlantic the fresh water dilutes the seawater, reducing its salinity and density less dense water sinks less easily so the flow rate of the current is reduced less heat is transported to nw europe so the uk could become colder (like moscow / churchill at the same latitude) even while global temperatures rise several interconnected systems act at once over different timescales: jet stream changes may raise temperatures, a slowing conveyor may lower them, greenhouse gases raise them natural fluctuations (solar output, orbit) can hide or exaggerate anthropogenic change; individual events do not show trends time delays between cause and effect (oceans take a long time to warm because of volume and heat capacity); incomplete understanding of the processes argo floats sink to a set depth for about 10 days, then surface and transmit temperature, salinity and position data; the sequence of positions shows current direction and speed (surface currents also by satellites and buoys)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CC-04\" data-qid=\"CC-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/climate-change.html\">Global climate change</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.2, 3.2.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 96-97 &middot; CC-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Describe the two processes that drive the North Atlantic Conveyor.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain how global climate change could weaken the North Atlantic Conveyor and describe the possible consequence for the climate of the UK.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain why the effects of climate change on UK temperatures are difficult to predict.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Describe how deep ocean currents are monitored.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Friction with the prevailing south-westerly winds drags surface water north-eastwards from the tropical Atlantic</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Water in the NE Atlantic cools, becomes denser (and more saline as ice forms) and sinks, drawing warm surface water in to replace it; the cold water returns southwards at depth (thermohaline circulation)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Higher temperatures melt land ice on Greenland which flows into the North Atlantic</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The fresh water dilutes the seawater, reducing its salinity and density</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Less dense water sinks less easily so the flow rate of the current is reduced</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Less heat is transported to NW Europe so the UK could become colder (like Moscow / Churchill at the same latitude) even while global temperatures rise</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Several interconnected systems act at once over different timescales: jet stream changes may raise temperatures, a slowing Conveyor may lower them, greenhouse gases raise them</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Natural fluctuations (solar output, orbit) can hide or exaggerate anthropogenic change; individual events do not show trends</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Time delays between cause and effect (oceans take a long time to warm because of volume and heat capacity); incomplete understanding of the processes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Argo floats sink to a set depth for about 10 days, then surface and transmit temperature, salinity and position data; the sequence of positions shows current direction and speed (surface currents also by satellites and buoys)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 96-97; AQA spec 3.2.1.2, 3.2.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CC-05", "sub": "climate-change", "subname": "Global climate change", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.1.2", "pages": "100-102", "url": "subtopic/climate-change.html#CC-05", "snippet": "Explain why it is difficult to be certain that a particular flood or heatwave is caused by anthropogenic climate change.", "text": " explain why it is difficult to be certain that a particular flood or heatwave is caused by anthropogenic climate change. explain why historic temperature records collected in towns may overestimate global warming. describe how each of the following sources of proxy data is used to estimate past climates and give one limitation. the climate naturally fluctuates (solar output, earth's orbit) so extreme events can occur without human influence an individual event, or even several, does not show a trend; the change may be in the frequency of events which can only be assessed years later changes occur at different time and spatial scales (local storm vs global trend); interconnected systems make attribution complex towns have a heat-island effect (heat emissions, dark surfaces absorbing light) as the town grows around the weather station the warming recorded is partly local, not global; older equipment / sparse global coverage also reduce reliability tree rings: width of each annual ring shows the growth rate, indicating temperature / rainfall in that year; rings easily dated by counting; limitation: growth also affected by other factors (water, pests, shading), only covers the life of the tree / limited regions ice cores: annual snow layers compact into ice; air bubbles trapped in the ice give the co2 (and methane) concentration when trapped; oxygen isotope ratios indicate temperature; up to 800 000 years (dome c); limitation: deep layers compressed / less distinct, dating needs radio-isotope analysis and volcanic ash layers; only polar regions others accepted: coral growth rings; pollen in lake sediments dated by radiocarbon", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CC-05\" data-qid=\"CC-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/climate-change.html\">Global climate change</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 100-102 &middot; CC-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Explain why it is difficult to be certain that a particular flood or heatwave is caused by anthropogenic climate change.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain why historic temperature records collected in towns may overestimate global warming.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Describe how each of the following sources of proxy data is used to estimate past climates and give one limitation.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The climate naturally fluctuates (solar output, Earth's orbit) so extreme events can occur without human influence</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>An individual event, or even several, does not show a trend; the change may be in the frequency of events which can only be assessed years later</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Changes occur at different time and spatial scales (local storm vs global trend); interconnected systems make attribution complex</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Towns have a heat-island effect (heat emissions, dark surfaces absorbing light)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>As the town grows around the weather station the warming recorded is partly local, not global; older equipment / sparse global coverage also reduce reliability</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 5 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Tree rings: width of each annual ring shows the growth rate, indicating temperature / rainfall in that year; rings easily dated by counting; limitation: growth also affected by other factors (water, pests, shading), only covers the life of the tree / limited regions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Ice cores: annual snow layers compact into ice; air bubbles trapped in the ice give the CO2 (and methane) concentration when trapped; oxygen isotope ratios indicate temperature; up to 800 000 years (Dome C); limitation: deep layers compressed / less distinct, dating needs radio-isotope analysis and volcanic ash layers; only polar regions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Others accepted: coral growth rings; pollen in lake sediments dated by radiocarbon</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 100-102; AQA spec 3.2.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CC-06", "sub": "climate-change", "subname": "Global climate change", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.2.1.2", "pages": "102", "url": "subtopic/climate-change.html#CC-06", "snippet": "Table 1 compares two types of satellite used to monitor the Earth.", "text": "table 1 compares two types of satellite used to monitor the earth. use table 1 to explain why leo satellites are used to monitor changes in ice cover and vegetation, whereas geostationary satellites are used to monitor weather. give three climate-related factors, other than ice cover, that are measured by satellite sensors. explain how a computer climate model can be tested for reliability before its predictions are used. explain why representative data are essential for climate modelling. leo: high resolution and coverage of the whole earth (including the poles) allows detailed mapping of ice area / thickness and forest cover but each area is only revisited every 15 days so they cannot follow rapidly changing weather geostationary: constant view of the same region allows continuous monitoring of cloud / storm movements, though the poles are not visible and detail is lower wind velocity ocean currents / sea surface temperature wave height ice thickness vegetation cover sea level atmospheric temperature / composition data for a past year (eg 1900) are fed in and the model's prediction for a later year (eg 2000) is compared with the real outcome if the prediction matches, the model can be trusted (with caution); it is continually modified as more data are collected and differences analysed models need data from all regions / oceans / altitudes; gaps (eg oceans, polar regions, past periods) introduce uncertainty into predictions unrepresentative data (eg urban heat islands, short time series) would bias the model; remote sensing helps fill gaps", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CC-06\" data-qid=\"CC-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/climate-change.html\">Global climate change</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 102 &middot; CC-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 compares two types of satellite used to monitor the Earth.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Low Earth Orbit (polar) satellite</th><th>Geostationary satellite</th></tr><tr><td>Altitude</td><td>About 800 km</td><td>36 000 km above the equator</td></tr><tr><td>Orbit period</td><td>About 1.5 hours</td><td>24 hours (fixed position)</td></tr><tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Whole Earth surveyed in about 15 days</td><td>Same third of the Earth continuously</td></tr><tr><td>Detail</td><td>High resolution</td><td>Lower resolution</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Use Table 1 to explain why LEO satellites are used to monitor changes in ice cover and vegetation, whereas geostationary satellites are used to monitor weather.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Give <b>three</b> climate-related factors, other than ice cover, that are measured by satellite sensors.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain how a computer climate model can be tested for reliability before its predictions are used.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Explain why representative data are essential for climate modelling.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>LEO: high resolution and coverage of the whole Earth (including the poles) allows detailed mapping of ice area / thickness and forest cover</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>But each area is only revisited every 15 days so they cannot follow rapidly changing weather</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Geostationary: constant view of the same region allows continuous monitoring of cloud / storm movements, though the poles are not visible and detail is lower</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wind velocity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ocean currents / sea surface temperature</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wave height</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ice thickness</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Vegetation cover</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sea level</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Atmospheric temperature / composition</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Data for a past year (eg 1900) are fed in and the model's prediction for a later year (eg 2000) is compared with the real outcome</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>If the prediction matches, the model can be trusted (with caution); it is continually modified as more data are collected and differences analysed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Models need data from all regions / oceans / altitudes; gaps (eg oceans, polar regions, past periods) introduce uncertainty into predictions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Unrepresentative data (eg urban heat islands, short time series) would bias the model; remote sensing helps fill gaps</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 102; AQA spec 3.2.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CC-07", "sub": "climate-change", "subname": "Global climate change", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 15, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.1.2, 3.6.1", "pages": "103-104, 371-372", "url": "subtopic/climate-change.html#CC-07", "snippet": "Explain the difference between a negative and a positive feedback mechanism.", "text": " explain the difference between a negative and a positive feedback mechanism. describe two negative feedback mechanisms that may reduce global warming. explain how melting permafrost and melting methane hydrate act as positive feedback mechanisms. give three other positive feedback mechanisms. explain what is meant by a climate tipping point. negative feedback: a change causes other changes that reduce the rate or size of the original change, re-establishing equilibrium positive feedback: a change causes other changes that increase the original change increased low-level cloud: higher temperatures increase evaporation and condensation, producing more cloud; clouds have a high albedo so more sunlight is reflected and warming is reduced increased photosynthesis: higher temperatures and co2 increase photosynthesis, removing co2; if stored in woody tissue atmospheric co2 rises less permafrost: frozen waterlogged arctic soils contain dead organic matter that decomposed anaerobically producing methane trapped in the frozen ground warming thaws the permafrost, releasing methane (a powerful greenhouse gas) and allowing further decomposition, causing more warming methane hydrate: methane from decomposed organic matter in deep sea sediments is held as a solid under high pressure and low temperature if deep water warms the hydrate melts, releasing methane into the atmosphere and causing further warming reduced albedo as snow / ice melts ocean acidification reducing coral survival and carbon sequestration increased forest and peat fires releasing co2 faster decomposition of soil organic matter releasing co2 increased water vapour (a greenhouse gas) in warmer air increased cirrus (high) cloud which traps infrared a point at which natural processes triggered by human-caused warming become self-sustaining / unstoppable so that stopping the original human activity would no longer stop climate change (eg runaway permafrost melt, methane hydrate release, forest fires)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CC-07\" data-qid=\"CC-07\" data-marks=\"15\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">15 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/climate-change.html\">Global climate change</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.2, 3.6.1 &middot; Genn pp. 103-104, 371-372 &middot; CC-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Explain the difference between a negative and a positive feedback mechanism.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Describe <b>two</b> negative feedback mechanisms that may reduce global warming.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Explain how melting permafrost and melting methane hydrate act as positive feedback mechanisms.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>Give <b>three</b> other positive feedback mechanisms.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.5</span>Explain what is meant by a climate <b>tipping point</b>.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Negative feedback: a change causes other changes that reduce the rate or size of the original change, re-establishing equilibrium</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Positive feedback: a change causes other changes that increase the original change</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Increased low-level cloud: higher temperatures increase evaporation and condensation, producing more cloud; clouds have a high albedo so more sunlight is reflected and warming is reduced</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Increased photosynthesis: higher temperatures and CO2 increase photosynthesis, removing CO2; if stored in woody tissue atmospheric CO2 rises less</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Permafrost: frozen waterlogged Arctic soils contain dead organic matter that decomposed anaerobically producing methane trapped in the frozen ground</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Warming thaws the permafrost, releasing methane (a powerful greenhouse gas) and allowing further decomposition, causing more warming</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Methane hydrate: methane from decomposed organic matter in deep sea sediments is held as a solid under high pressure and low temperature</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>If deep water warms the hydrate melts, releasing methane into the atmosphere and causing further warming</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduced albedo as snow / ice melts</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ocean acidification reducing coral survival and carbon sequestration</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Increased forest and peat fires releasing CO2</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Faster decomposition of soil organic matter releasing CO2</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Increased water vapour (a greenhouse gas) in warmer air</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Increased cirrus (high) cloud which traps infrared</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A point at which natural processes triggered by human-caused warming become self-sustaining / unstoppable</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So that stopping the original human activity would no longer stop climate change (eg runaway permafrost melt, methane hydrate release, forest fires)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 103-104, 371-372; AQA spec 3.2.1.2, 3.6.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CC-08", "sub": "climate-change", "subname": "Global climate change", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.1.2", "pages": "90-92", "url": "subtopic/climate-change.html#CC-08", "snippet": "Explain how climate change may affect the survival of species. Use named examples.", "text": " explain how climate change may affect the survival of species. use named examples. explain why a species living in an interdependent community may be unable to move to a new area even if suitable conditions exist there. explain why polar regions warming faster than the equator affects the weather in the uk. direct effect of abiotic change: species near the edge of their range of tolerance (temperature, water) may die out locally, eg sundews if marshes dry, frogs if climate drier; beech (shallow roots) suffers in drought more than oak changes in timing: earlier flowering / emergence may separate flowers from pollinators; plant toxins building up earlier may kill caterpillars; warmer winters disturb dormouse hibernation so they use fat reserves and starve biotic changes: food supply, predators, disease vectors (mosquitoes / malaria spreading), pollinators and seed dispersers may change distribution changes: species may colonise newly suitable areas (triton molluscs around the uk) but slow-growing species (trees) may disappear from old ranges faster than they colonise; suitable areas or biological corridors may not exist; human land use blocks movement population fragmentation: rising seas or upward retreat to mountains isolate populations; divided gene pools increase inbreeding; small populations may not be viable; no repopulation from elsewhere some species benefit (bats: warmer winters, more insects) but wetter stormier weather reduces feeding time all the species it depends on (food plants, pollinators, prey, mycorrhizae) will not move at the same speed less mobile species (plants, non-flying animals) cannot cross unsuitable areas without a biological corridor the temperature difference between mid-latitude and polar air drives the jet streams; a smaller difference makes the jet stream slower and more meandering (rossby waves) weather systems stall over an area, giving prolonged drought or rainfall / floods; unusually cold or warm air carried north or south", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CC-08\" data-qid=\"CC-08\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 8</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/climate-change.html\">Global climate change</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 90-92 &middot; CC-08</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.1</span>Explain how climate change may affect the survival of species. Use named examples.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.2</span>Explain why a species living in an interdependent community may be unable to move to a new area even if suitable conditions exist there.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.3</span>Explain why polar regions warming faster than the equator affects the weather in the UK.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Direct effect of abiotic change: species near the edge of their range of tolerance (temperature, water) may die out locally, eg sundews if marshes dry, frogs if climate drier; beech (shallow roots) suffers in drought more than oak</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Changes in timing: earlier flowering / emergence may separate flowers from pollinators; plant toxins building up earlier may kill caterpillars; warmer winters disturb dormouse hibernation so they use fat reserves and starve</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Biotic changes: food supply, predators, disease vectors (mosquitoes / malaria spreading), pollinators and seed dispersers may change</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Distribution changes: species may colonise newly suitable areas (triton molluscs around the UK) but slow-growing species (trees) may disappear from old ranges faster than they colonise; suitable areas or biological corridors may not exist; human land use blocks movement</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Population fragmentation: rising seas or upward retreat to mountains isolate populations; divided gene pools increase inbreeding; small populations may not be viable; no repopulation from elsewhere</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Some species benefit (bats: warmer winters, more insects) but wetter stormier weather reduces feeding time</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>All the species it depends on (food plants, pollinators, prey, mycorrhizae) will not move at the same speed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Less mobile species (plants, non-flying animals) cannot cross unsuitable areas without a biological corridor</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The temperature difference between mid-latitude and polar air drives the jet streams; a smaller difference makes the jet stream slower and more meandering (Rossby waves)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Weather systems stall over an area, giving prolonged drought or rainfall / floods; unusually cold or warm air carried north or south</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 90-92; AQA spec 3.2.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CC-09", "sub": "climate-change", "subname": "Global climate change", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.1.2", "pages": "99-100, 105-108", "url": "subtopic/climate-change.html#CC-09", "snippet": "A low-lying coastal town in eastern England is at risk of flooding from rising sea level and more intense rainfall.", "text": "a low-lying coastal town in eastern england is at risk of flooding from rising sea level and more intense rainfall. describe three ways the town could adapt to the increased flood risk. explain the circumstances in which managed retreat may be chosen instead of coastal defences. describe two impacts of higher temperatures on transport infrastructure. explain how carbon capture and storage (ccs) could reduce co2 emissions from a fossil fuel power station, and give one limitation. raise river banks / build sea walls, flood barriers or wave screens permeable urban surfaces (gravel, soil) instead of concrete / tarmac to slow runoff river flow management: upstream dams / low soil dams / afforestation / maintained flood plains to delay runoff raised buildings on stilts or floating houses pumping rainwater from land below river level managed retreat - abandoning land where protection costs exceed value (eg happisburgh) where the cost of flood prevention is greater than the value of what is protected, or defences would be ineffective land is abandoned as water levels rise / coast erodes; defences used only where land is very important road surfaces deform as tar melts - need tarmac with a higher melting point rail track expands and buckles above the temperature it was pre-stretched for (27 °c in uk) - relay with higher pre-stress bridge damage from high river flows / debris; landslides on deforested slopes co2 is captured from the flue gases (or carbon removed from the fuel before combustion) transported by pipeline / ship / tanker and stored in depleted oil or gas fields, aquifers, or used for secondary oil recovery limitation: developmental / expensive, uses energy (reducing efficiency), risk of leakage, limited storage sites", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CC-09\" data-qid=\"CC-09\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 9</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/climate-change.html\">Global climate change</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 99-100, 105-108 &middot; CC-09</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A low-lying coastal town in eastern England is at risk of flooding from rising sea level and more intense rainfall.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.1</span>Describe <b>three</b> ways the town could adapt to the increased flood risk.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.2</span>Explain the circumstances in which managed retreat may be chosen instead of coastal defences.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.3</span>Describe <b>two</b> impacts of higher temperatures on transport infrastructure.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.4</span>Explain how carbon capture and storage (CCS) could reduce CO<sub>2</sub> emissions from a fossil fuel power station, and give <b>one</b> limitation.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Raise river banks / build sea walls, flood barriers or wave screens</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Permeable urban surfaces (gravel, soil) instead of concrete / tarmac to slow runoff</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>River flow management: upstream dams / low soil dams / afforestation / maintained flood plains to delay runoff</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Raised buildings on stilts or floating houses</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pumping rainwater from land below river level</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Managed retreat - abandoning land where protection costs exceed value (eg Happisburgh)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Where the cost of flood prevention is greater than the value of what is protected, or defences would be ineffective</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Land is abandoned as water levels rise / coast erodes; defences used only where land is very important</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Road surfaces deform as tar melts - need tarmac with a higher melting point</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Rail track expands and buckles above the temperature it was pre-stretched for (27 &deg;C in UK) - relay with higher pre-stress</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bridge damage from high river flows / debris; landslides on deforested slopes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>CO2 is captured from the flue gases (or carbon removed from the fuel before combustion)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Transported by pipeline / ship / tanker and stored in depleted oil or gas fields, aquifers, or used for secondary oil recovery</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Limitation: developmental / expensive, uses energy (reducing efficiency), risk of leakage, limited storage sites</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 99-100, 105-108; AQA spec 3.2.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CC-10", "sub": "climate-change", "subname": "Global climate change", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.2.1.2", "pages": "89-107", "url": "subtopic/climate-change.html#CC-10", "snippet": "Evaluate the success of the methods and strategies used to reduce global climate change.", "text": " evaluate the success of the methods and strategies used to reduce global climate change. state what is meant by a carbon footprint. control of greenhouse gases: energy conservation (transport, buildings, industry), renewable and nuclear energy replacing fossil fuels, ccs, reduced deforestation and afforestation (carbon sequestration), methane capture from landfill / digesters, hfc controls, changes in agriculture geoengineering: white roofs, ocean fertilisation, solar shades - largely untried, possible unpredictable consequences international agreements: kyoto protocol, paris agreement - targets, but non-binding / uneven participation; carbon trading and taxes; comparison of per capita carbon footprints between countries evidence of limited success: global co2 concentrations still rising (about 280 ppm pre-industrial to over 410 ppm); temperatures up about 1 °c; emissions in some developed countries falling but rising in developing economies reasons for difficulty: fossil fuels underpin economies, need for major lifestyle change, cost, time delays and natural fluctuations masking effects, political disagreement, developing countries' right to develop adaptation strategies (flood defences, drainage, managed retreat, infrastructure changes) reduce impacts but do not address the cause comparison with the montreal protocol's success for ozone judgement: partial success - technology exists and some regional reductions achieved, but global strategies have not yet stopped the rise; tipping points make urgency greater the total greenhouse gas / co2 emissions caused by an individual, organisation, product or country (often expressed per capita per year)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CC-10\" data-qid=\"CC-10\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 10</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/climate-change.html\">Global climate change</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 89-107 &middot; CC-10</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">10.1</span>Evaluate the success of the methods and strategies used to reduce global climate change.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">10.2</span>State what is meant by a <b>carbon footprint</b>.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>10.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Control of greenhouse gases: energy conservation (transport, buildings, industry), renewable and nuclear energy replacing fossil fuels, CCS, reduced deforestation and afforestation (carbon sequestration), methane capture from landfill / digesters, HFC controls, changes in agriculture</li><li>Geoengineering: white roofs, ocean fertilisation, solar shades - largely untried, possible unpredictable consequences</li><li>International agreements: Kyoto Protocol, Paris Agreement - targets, but non-binding / uneven participation; carbon trading and taxes; comparison of per capita carbon footprints between countries</li><li>Evidence of limited success: global CO2 concentrations still rising (about 280 ppm pre-industrial to over 410 ppm); temperatures up about 1 &deg;C; emissions in some developed countries falling but rising in developing economies</li><li>Reasons for difficulty: fossil fuels underpin economies, need for major lifestyle change, cost, time delays and natural fluctuations masking effects, political disagreement, developing countries' right to develop</li><li>Adaptation strategies (flood defences, drainage, managed retreat, infrastructure changes) reduce impacts but do not address the cause</li><li>Comparison with the Montreal Protocol's success for ozone</li><li>Judgement: partial success - technology exists and some regional reductions achieved, but global strategies have not yet stopped the rise; tipping points make urgency greater</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>10.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The total greenhouse gas / CO2 emissions caused by an individual, organisation, product or country (often expressed per capita per year)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 89-107; AQA spec 3.2.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CC-11", "sub": "climate-change", "subname": "Global climate change", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.2.1.2", "pages": "94-96", "url": "subtopic/climate-change.html#CC-11", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the monthly flow of a river in South America that is fed partly by glacier meltwater, in 1990 and 2020. The glacier feeding the river shrank considerably between the", "text": "figure 1 shows the monthly flow of a river in south america that is fed partly by glacier meltwater, in 1990 and 2020. the glacier feeding the river shrank considerably between the two dates. compare the river flow in 1990 and 2020. explain how the shrinking of the glacier explains the differences. suggest two problems the change in flow causes for people living downstream. explain why a glacier can be extending down its valley yet still be losing ice volume. 2020 has higher peak flow in the wet season (32 vs 22 m3 s-1 in march) 2020 has much lower dry-season flow (2 vs 9 m3 s-1 in july / august) flow is much more variable / seasonal in 2020; the river almost dries up in 1990 snow / ice stored wet-season precipitation and released it gradually as meltwater during the dry season, evening out the flow with less ice, precipitation now falls as rain / melts immediately and runs off quickly, increasing wet-season flow and flood risk little stored ice remains to melt in the dry season so flow falls very low water shortages for irrigation / drinking / hep in the dry season flooding in the wet season sudden floods from ice-lake outbursts (glacial lake outburst floods) surface meltwater flows down cracks to the base, lubricating the ice so it slides faster; if this exceeds the faster melting at the front, the front advances but if snowfall at the source has not increased, total volume falls; both retreating and extending glaciers can indicate warming", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CC-11\" data-qid=\"CC-11\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 11</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/climate-change.html\">Global climate change</a> &middot; spec 3.2.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 94-96 &middot; CC-11</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the monthly flow of a river in South America that is fed partly by glacier meltwater, in 1990 and 2020. The glacier feeding the river shrank considerably between the two dates.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,141.4 103.6,127.1 147.3,112.9 190.9,155.7 234.5,184.3 278.2,198.6 321.8,205.7 365.5,205.7 409.1,198.6 452.7,184.3 496.4,170.0 540.0,155.7\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"141.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"103.6\" cy=\"127.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"147.3\" cy=\"112.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"190.9\" cy=\"155.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"234.5\" cy=\"184.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"278.2\" cy=\"198.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"321.8\" cy=\"205.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"365.5\" cy=\"205.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"409.1\" cy=\"198.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"452.7\" cy=\"184.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"496.4\" cy=\"170.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"155.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<polyline points=\"60.0,84.3 103.6,55.7 147.3,41.4 190.9,170.0 234.5,227.1 278.2,248.6 321.8,255.7 365.5,255.7 409.1,248.6 452.7,227.1 496.4,184.3 540.0,127.1\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2f5f73\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"84.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"103.6\" cy=\"55.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"147.3\" cy=\"41.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"190.9\" cy=\"170.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"234.5\" cy=\"227.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"278.2\" cy=\"248.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"321.8\" cy=\"255.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"365.5\" cy=\"255.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"409.1\" cy=\"248.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"452.7\" cy=\"227.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"496.4\" cy=\"184.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"127.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">7</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">5.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">14</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">7.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">21</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">9.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">28</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">12</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">35</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Month</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Mean river flow / m³/s</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"24\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#22302a\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"33\">1990</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"38\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"47\">2020</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">11.1</span>Compare the river flow in 1990 and 2020.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">11.2</span>Explain how the shrinking of the glacier explains the differences.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">11.3</span>Suggest <b>two</b> problems the change in flow causes for people living downstream.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">11.4</span>Explain why a glacier can be extending down its valley yet still be losing ice volume.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>11.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>2020 has higher peak flow in the wet season (32 vs 22 m<sup>3</sup> s<sup>-1</sup> in March)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>2020 has much lower dry-season flow (2 vs 9 m<sup>3</sup> s<sup>-1</sup> in July / August)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Flow is much more variable / seasonal in 2020; the river almost dries up</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>11.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In 1990 snow / ice stored wet-season precipitation and released it gradually as meltwater during the dry season, evening out the flow</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>With less ice, precipitation now falls as rain / melts immediately and runs off quickly, increasing wet-season flow and flood risk</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Little stored ice remains to melt in the dry season so flow falls very low</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>11.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Water shortages for irrigation / drinking / HEP in the dry season</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Flooding in the wet season</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sudden floods from ice-lake outbursts (glacial lake outburst floods)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>11.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Surface meltwater flows down cracks to the base, lubricating the ice so it slides faster; if this exceeds the faster melting at the front, the front advances</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>But if snowfall at the source has not increased, total volume falls; both retreating and extending glaciers can indicate warming</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 94-96; AQA spec 3.2.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ECON-01", "sub": "energy-conservation", "subname": "Energy conservation: transport, buildings, industry", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.3.4.2.1", "pages": "225-228", "url": "subtopic/energy-conservation.html#ECON-01", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the energy used per passenger-kilometre by different forms of transport when carrying a typical load.", "text": "table 1 shows the energy used per passenger-kilometre by different forms of transport when carrying a typical load. calculate the percentage reduction in energy per passenger-km achieved by carrying four people in a car instead of one. explain why bulk transport (buses, trains) uses less energy per passenger-km than cars. suggest three reasons why people continue to use cars despite the energy advantages of public transport. explain how integrated transport systems and active traffic management reduce energy use. (2.9 - 0.7) / 2.9 x 100 = 75.9% (accept 76%) although the larger vehicle uses more energy in total, it carries many more people / a larger load so the energy per passenger (or per tonne of goods) is lower; steel wheels on rails also have low rolling resistance flexibility of routes / destinations and door-to-door convenience time / speed and reliability where public transport is infrequent low fuel tax / cheap fuel carrying goods; comfort; status integrated systems (park and ride, bicycles on trains, linked timetables) make it convenient to use the most efficient mode for each part of a journey so more journeys shift from cars to bulk transport / cycling atm / smart motorways use variable speed limits to prevent congestion and keep traffic flowing freely vehicles avoid stop-start driving and run nearer their optimum speed (about 56 mph), reducing fuel wasted in acceleration and idling", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ECON-01\" data-qid=\"ECON-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-conservation.html\">Energy conservation: transport, buildings, industry</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 225-228 &middot; ECON-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the energy used per passenger-kilometre by different forms of transport when carrying a typical load.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Mode of transport</th><th>Typical occupancy</th><th>Energy use / MJ per passenger-km</th></tr><tr><td>Car (petrol, 1 occupant)</td><td>1</td><td>2.9</td></tr><tr><td>Car (petrol, 4 occupants)</td><td>4</td><td>0.7</td></tr><tr><td>Bus (diesel)</td><td>25</td><td>0.6</td></tr><tr><td>Electric train</td><td>200</td><td>0.3</td></tr><tr><td>Bicycle</td><td>1</td><td>0.06</td></tr><tr><td>Short-haul aircraft</td><td>120</td><td>2.4</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Calculate the percentage reduction in energy per passenger-km achieved by carrying four people in a car instead of one.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain why bulk transport (buses, trains) uses less energy per passenger-km than cars.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Suggest <b>three</b> reasons why people continue to use cars despite the energy advantages of public transport.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Explain how integrated transport systems and Active Traffic Management reduce energy use.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(2.9 - 0.7) / 2.9 x 100 = 75.9% (accept 76%)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Although the larger vehicle uses more energy in total, it carries many more people / a larger load</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So the energy per passenger (or per tonne of goods) is lower; steel wheels on rails also have low rolling resistance</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Flexibility of routes / destinations and door-to-door convenience</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Time / speed and reliability where public transport is infrequent</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Low fuel tax / cheap fuel</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Carrying goods; comfort; status</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Integrated systems (park and ride, bicycles on trains, linked timetables) make it convenient to use the most efficient mode for each part of a journey</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So more journeys shift from cars to bulk transport / cycling</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>ATM / smart motorways use variable speed limits to prevent congestion and keep traffic flowing freely</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Vehicles avoid stop-start driving and run nearer their optimum speed (about 56 mph), reducing fuel wasted in acceleration and idling</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 225-228; AQA spec 3.3.4.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ECON-02", "sub": "energy-conservation", "subname": "Energy conservation: transport, buildings, industry", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.3.4.2.1", "pages": "225-227", "url": "subtopic/energy-conservation.html#ECON-02", "snippet": "Explain how each of the following features of vehicle design reduces energy use.", "text": " explain how each of the following features of vehicle design reduces energy use. explain why solid steel wheels on rails lose less energy than pneumatic tyres on roads. describe two ways combustion efficiency in an internal combustion engine can be improved. aerodynamic shapes / fairings reduce friction with the air (air resistance) so less energy is needed to propel the vehicle, especially at speed; hydrodynamics for ships reduced mass (plastics, carbon-fibre composites, high-strength steel alloys, aluminium engine blocks, lighter batteries and magnets, rounded shapes) means less energy to accelerate and less rolling resistance, so lower fuel consumption conventional friction brakes convert kinetic energy to waste heat; regenerative braking uses a generator to convert it to electricity stored in a battery (or returned to the rail grid) which then propels the vehicle, so less fuel is needed to accelerate again pneumatic tyres deform as they rotate; the continual change in shape and air movement inside generates frictional heat (more if under-inflated) steel wheels do not deform so rolling resistance is very low more valves per cylinder to remove exhaust gases fully so they do not mix with fresh fuel better temperature control (thermostatic fan) electronic ignition control / regular servicing to keep spark timing correct turbochargers supplying more oxygen", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ECON-02\" data-qid=\"ECON-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-conservation.html\">Energy conservation: transport, buildings, industry</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 225-227 &middot; ECON-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain how each of the following features of vehicle design reduces energy use.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain why solid steel wheels on rails lose less energy than pneumatic tyres on roads.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Describe <b>two</b> ways combustion efficiency in an internal combustion engine can be improved.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Aerodynamic shapes / fairings reduce friction with the air (air resistance) so less energy is needed to propel the vehicle, especially at speed; hydrodynamics for ships</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Reduced mass (plastics, carbon-fibre composites, high-strength steel alloys, aluminium engine blocks, lighter batteries and magnets, rounded shapes) means less energy to accelerate and less rolling resistance, so lower fuel consumption</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Conventional friction brakes convert kinetic energy to waste heat; regenerative braking uses a generator to convert it to electricity stored in a battery (or returned to the rail grid) which then propels the vehicle, so less fuel is needed to accelerate again</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pneumatic tyres deform as they rotate; the continual change in shape and air movement inside generates frictional heat (more if under-inflated)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Steel wheels do not deform so rolling resistance is very low</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>More valves per cylinder to remove exhaust gases fully so they do not mix with fresh fuel</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Better temperature control (thermostatic fan)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Electronic ignition control / regular servicing to keep spark timing correct</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Turbochargers supplying more oxygen</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 225-227; AQA spec 3.3.4.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ECON-03", "sub": "energy-conservation", "subname": "Energy conservation: transport, buildings, industry", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.3.4.2.1", "pages": "227-228", "url": "subtopic/energy-conservation.html#ECON-03", "snippet": "Explain what is meant by the embodied energy of a vehicle and how it can be reduced.", "text": " explain what is meant by the embodied energy of a vehicle and how it can be reduced. describe four principles of designing vehicles for end of life. explain why there is an optimum speed for fuel efficiency in a car. give one way driver behaviour or vehicle systems can reduce fuel use in congested traffic. the energy used to extract raw materials, manufacture the components and assemble the vehicle (for a typical car about 1.5 years of its fuel use) reduced by using recycled materials (eg recycled aluminium needs 1/20 of the energy of new) lighter designs using less material; longer vehicle life spreading the embodied energy use of recyclable materials wherever possible easy identification of components and their composition (code stamps) easy dismantling and separation of components / materials reusable components for new vehicles compostable materials for parts that cannot be recycled at low speed a large proportion of the energy just runs the engine rather than moving the car at high speed energy is wasted overcoming air resistance (which rises with the square of speed); optimum for many cars about 56 mph automatic stop / start systems cutting the engine when stationary; smooth driving in higher gears avoiding sudden braking / acceleration", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ECON-03\" data-qid=\"ECON-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-conservation.html\">Energy conservation: transport, buildings, industry</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 227-228 &middot; ECON-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Explain what is meant by the <b>embodied energy</b> of a vehicle and how it can be reduced.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Describe <b>four</b> principles of designing vehicles for end of life.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain why there is an optimum speed for fuel efficiency in a car.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Give <b>one</b> way driver behaviour or vehicle systems can reduce fuel use in congested traffic.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The energy used to extract raw materials, manufacture the components and assemble the vehicle (for a typical car about 1.5 years of its fuel use)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduced by using recycled materials (eg recycled aluminium needs 1/20 of the energy of new)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lighter designs using less material; longer vehicle life spreading the embodied energy</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Use of recyclable materials wherever possible</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Easy identification of components and their composition (code stamps)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Easy dismantling and separation of components / materials</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reusable components for new vehicles</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Compostable materials for parts that cannot be recycled</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>At low speed a large proportion of the energy just runs the engine rather than moving the car</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>At high speed energy is wasted overcoming air resistance (which rises with the square of speed); optimum for many cars about 56 mph</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Automatic stop / start systems cutting the engine when stationary; smooth driving in higher gears avoiding sudden braking / acceleration</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 227-228; AQA spec 3.3.4.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ECON-04", "sub": "energy-conservation", "subname": "Energy conservation: transport, buildings, industry", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.3.4.2.2", "pages": "228-231", "url": "subtopic/energy-conservation.html#ECON-04", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the rate of heat loss through 1 m2 of different glazing systems for a 1 °C temperature difference (U-value).", "text": "table 1 shows the rate of heat loss through 1 m2 of different glazing systems for a 1 °c temperature difference (u-value). a house has 20 m2 of single glazing. the average temperature difference between inside and outside over the heating season is 12 °c. calculate the reduction in the rate of heat loss if the windows are replaced with argon-filled low-e double glazing. explain why the gap between the panes should be as large as possible but not too large. explain how low-emissivity glass and inert gas filling each reduce heat loss. explain why vacuum double glazing has a very narrow gap and where it is used. state one reason why insulation thickness has an optimum value. single: 5.8 x 20 x 12 = 1392 w argon: 1.6 x 20 x 12 = 384 w reduction = 1008 w (accept 1010) a wider gap of trapped gas reduces conduction because gas is a poor conductor if the gap is too wide convection currents start in the gas, carrying heat across; the optimum depends on the gas and window size low-e coating reflects long-wavelength infrared back into the room, reducing radiation losses argon / krypton / xenon have lower thermal conductivity than air so less conduction across the gap panes must be held apart by small spacers which cannot be long, so the gap is only about 0.2 mm used where there is not room for thick gas-filled multiple glazing (eg historic buildings) each extra layer saves less energy (diminishing returns) while cost rises, so beyond the optimum the cost of insulation exceeds the value of energy saved", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ECON-04\" data-qid=\"ECON-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-conservation.html\">Energy conservation: transport, buildings, industry</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 228-231 &middot; ECON-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the rate of heat loss through 1 m<sup>2</sup> of different glazing systems for a 1 &deg;C temperature difference (U-value).</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Glazing</th><th>Heat loss / W m<sup>-2</sup> K<sup>-1</sup></th></tr><tr><td>Single glazing</td><td>5.8</td></tr><tr><td>Double glazing, air filled</td><td>2.8</td></tr><tr><td>Double glazing, argon filled, low-e glass</td><td>1.6</td></tr><tr><td>Triple glazing, krypton filled, low-e glass</td><td>0.8</td></tr><tr><td>Double glazing, vacuum (0.2 mm gap)</td><td>0.74</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>A house has 20 m<sup>2</sup> of single glazing. The average temperature difference between inside and outside over the heating season is 12 &deg;C. Calculate the reduction in the rate of heat loss if the windows are replaced with argon-filled low-e double glazing.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain why the gap between the panes should be as large as possible but not too large.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain how low-emissivity glass and inert gas filling each reduce heat loss.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Explain why vacuum double glazing has a very narrow gap and where it is used.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.5</span>State <b>one</b> reason why insulation thickness has an optimum value.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Single: 5.8 x 20 x 12 = 1392 W</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Argon: 1.6 x 20 x 12 = 384 W</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduction = 1008 W (accept 1010)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A wider gap of trapped gas reduces conduction because gas is a poor conductor</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>If the gap is too wide convection currents start in the gas, carrying heat across; the optimum depends on the gas and window size</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Low-e coating reflects long-wavelength infrared back into the room, reducing radiation losses</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Argon / krypton / xenon have lower thermal conductivity than air so less conduction across the gap</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Panes must be held apart by small spacers which cannot be long, so the gap is only about 0.2 mm</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Used where there is not room for thick gas-filled multiple glazing (eg historic buildings)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Each extra layer saves less energy (diminishing returns) while cost rises, so beyond the optimum the cost of insulation exceeds the value of energy saved</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 228-231; AQA spec 3.3.4.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ECON-05", "sub": "energy-conservation", "subname": "Energy conservation: transport, buildings, industry", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.3.4.2.2", "pages": "228-232", "url": "subtopic/energy-conservation.html#ECON-05", "snippet": "Explain how each of the following building design features reduces energy use.", "text": " explain how each of the following building design features reduces energy use. compare the embodied energy of concrete with alternative building materials. describe how a heat exchanger allows a building to be ventilated with minimal heat loss. orientation: south-facing windows (northern hemisphere) maximise passive solar gains; rooms needing warmth on the sunny side; fewer / smaller windows on the north side where losses are greatest; solar screens / brise soleil prevent summer overheating low sa:v (compact shapes, terraced / joined buildings, fewer storeys of small footprint) means less external surface per unit volume through which heat is lost earth sheltering: the ground is warmer than winter air and shields the building from wind, so conduction and wind-chill losses are reduced cement manufacture (heating limestone) is very energy intensive so concrete has high embodied energy limecrete has about half the embodied energy of concrete; rammed earth, straw bales and lime mortar are lower still warm stale air leaving and cold fresh air entering pass through the exchanger in opposite directions (counter-current) separated by thin conducting pipes / plates with a large surface area heat transfers from the outgoing to the incoming air so fresh air enters already warmed", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ECON-05\" data-qid=\"ECON-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-conservation.html\">Energy conservation: transport, buildings, industry</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 228-232 &middot; ECON-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Explain how each of the following building design features reduces energy use.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Compare the embodied energy of concrete with alternative building materials.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Describe how a heat exchanger allows a building to be ventilated with minimal heat loss.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Orientation: south-facing windows (northern hemisphere) maximise passive solar gains; rooms needing warmth on the sunny side; fewer / smaller windows on the north side where losses are greatest; solar screens / brise soleil prevent summer overheating</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Low SA:V (compact shapes, terraced / joined buildings, fewer storeys of small footprint) means less external surface per unit volume through which heat is lost</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Earth sheltering: the ground is warmer than winter air and shields the building from wind, so conduction and wind-chill losses are reduced</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cement manufacture (heating limestone) is very energy intensive so concrete has high embodied energy</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Limecrete has about half the embodied energy of concrete; rammed earth, straw bales and lime mortar are lower still</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Warm stale air leaving and cold fresh air entering pass through the exchanger in opposite directions (counter-current) separated by thin conducting pipes / plates with a large surface area</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Heat transfers from the outgoing to the incoming air so fresh air enters already warmed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 228-232; AQA spec 3.3.4.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ECON-06", "sub": "energy-conservation", "subname": "Energy conservation: transport, buildings, industry", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.3.4.2.2", "pages": "232-233", "url": "subtopic/energy-conservation.html#ECON-06", "snippet": "Explain how occupancy sensors and programmable thermostats reduce energy waste.", "text": " explain how occupancy sensors and programmable thermostats reduce energy waste. explain why led lights use much less electricity than the filament and cfl lamps they replaced, and give two other examples of low-energy appliance design. describe three changes in human behaviour that reduce domestic energy use. explain why heating water on demand saves energy compared with storing hot water. occupancy sensors detect infrared / movement and switch lights or appliances off when no one is in the room programmable thermostats set different temperatures for different rooms and times so unused rooms / periods are not heated leds convert a much larger proportion of electricity to light rather than heat washing machines with cold / low-temperature cycles and fast spins (less drying); dishwashers using less water so less heating; cookers with double / triple glazed doors; fridges with linear compressors; led screens using half the power of plasma; germanium transistors turning off lights / appliances rather than standby turning thermostats and radiator valves down / off in unused rooms only heating the water needed (kettle) showering instead of bathing; eco-cycles choosing smaller vehicles / less travel stored hot water loses heat continuously through the tank walls and pipes heating as needed (or just before) avoids these standing losses; if storage is needed the tank should be well insulated", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ECON-06\" data-qid=\"ECON-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-conservation.html\">Energy conservation: transport, buildings, industry</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 232-233 &middot; ECON-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Explain how occupancy sensors and programmable thermostats reduce energy waste.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain why LED lights use much less electricity than the filament and CFL lamps they replaced, and give <b>two</b> other examples of low-energy appliance design.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Describe <b>three</b> changes in human behaviour that reduce domestic energy use.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Explain why heating water on demand saves energy compared with storing hot water.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Occupancy sensors detect infrared / movement and switch lights or appliances off when no one is in the room</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Programmable thermostats set different temperatures for different rooms and times so unused rooms / periods are not heated</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 3 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>LEDs convert a much larger proportion of electricity to light rather than heat</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Washing machines with cold / low-temperature cycles and fast spins (less drying); dishwashers using less water so less heating; cookers with double / triple glazed doors; fridges with linear compressors; LED screens using half the power of plasma; germanium transistors</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Turning off lights / appliances rather than standby</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Turning thermostats and radiator valves down / off in unused rooms</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Only heating the water needed (kettle)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Showering instead of bathing; eco-cycles</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Choosing smaller vehicles / less travel</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stored hot water loses heat continuously through the tank walls and pipes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Heating as needed (or just before) avoids these standing losses; if storage is needed the tank should be well insulated</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 232-233; AQA spec 3.3.4.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ECON-07", "sub": "energy-conservation", "subname": "Energy conservation: transport, buildings, industry", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.3.4.2.3", "pages": "233-235", "url": "subtopic/energy-conservation.html#ECON-07", "snippet": "Figure 1 compares the energy flows in a conventional gas-fired power station and a combined heat and power (CHP) station, each supplied with 100 units of fuel energy.", "text": "figure 1 compares the energy flows in a conventional gas-fired power station and a combined heat and power (chp) station, each supplied with 100 units of fuel energy. calculate the overall efficiency of each station. explain why the electrical efficiency of a chp station is often deliberately kept below 40%. explain why chp stations must be located close to the users of the heat. explain how each of the following features of an industrial heat exchanger increases its efficiency. explain why a single large spherical tank loses less heat than several small cubic tanks holding the same total volume of hot fluid. conventional: 40 / 100 = 40% chp: (35 + 45) / 100 = 80% extracting less energy as electricity leaves the cooling / condenser water at a higher temperature hotter water is more useful for district heating of homes, fish farms, greenhouses hot water loses heat and needs pumping energy over distance; heat cannot be transported as far as electricity long narrow pipes give a large surface area and copper is a good thermal conductor so heat transfers quickly hot and cold fluids flowing in opposite directions maintain a temperature gradient along the whole length so more heat is transferred slower flow allows more time for heat to transfer one large tank has a lower surface area : volume ratio than many small ones a sphere has the smallest surface area for any volume; less surface means less heat loss (with insulation of low thermal conductivity)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ECON-07\" data-qid=\"ECON-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-conservation.html\">Energy conservation: transport, buildings, industry</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 233-235 &middot; ECON-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 compares the energy flows in a conventional gas-fired power station and a combined heat and power (CHP) station, each supplied with 100 units of fuel energy.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th></th><th>Conventional power station</th><th>CHP station</th></tr><tr><td>Electricity output / units</td><td>40</td><td>35</td></tr><tr><td>Useful heat supplied to buildings / units</td><td>0</td><td>45</td></tr><tr><td>Waste heat lost / units</td><td>60</td><td>20</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Calculate the overall efficiency of each station.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Explain why the electrical efficiency of a CHP station is often deliberately kept below 40%.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Explain why CHP stations must be located close to the users of the heat.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>Explain how each of the following features of an industrial heat exchanger increases its efficiency.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.5</span>Explain why a single large spherical tank loses less heat than several small cubic tanks holding the same total volume of hot fluid.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Conventional: 40 / 100 = 40%</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>CHP: (35 + 45) / 100 = 80%</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Extracting less energy as electricity leaves the cooling / condenser water at a higher temperature</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hotter water is more useful for district heating of homes, fish farms, greenhouses</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hot water loses heat and needs pumping energy over distance; heat cannot be transported as far as electricity</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Long narrow pipes give a large surface area and copper is a good thermal conductor so heat transfers quickly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hot and cold fluids flowing in opposite directions maintain a temperature gradient along the whole length so more heat is transferred</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Slower flow allows more time for heat to transfer</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>One large tank has a lower surface area : volume ratio than many small ones</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A sphere has the smallest surface area for any volume; less surface means less heat loss (with insulation of low thermal conductivity)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 233-235; AQA spec 3.3.4.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ECON-08", "sub": "energy-conservation", "subname": "Energy conservation: transport, buildings, industry", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.3.4.2.3", "pages": "234-235", "url": "subtopic/energy-conservation.html#ECON-08", "snippet": "Explain how integrated manufacture saves energy in the iron and steel industry.", "text": " explain how integrated manufacture saves energy in the iron and steel industry. explain why recycling is not always energy efficient. explain how each of the following manages electricity infrastructure to conserve energy. give two locational factors affecting where new generating infrastructure is built. molten iron from the blast furnace is converted directly to steel on the same site so it does not cool and need re-melting; transport energy between sites is also avoided; waste heat from one process used by another (eg kalundborg) collecting and processing small dispersed quantities can use more energy in transport than is saved mass production and large-scale mining have economies of scale; some materials (glass) take more energy to make than plastic but can be refilled many times transmitting at high voltage means low current so less energy is lost as heat in the cables (loss proportional to current squared); overhead cables cheaper than underground which need cooling ict / smart grids co-ordinate real-time data on demand and supply from many sources, switching generation, storage (pumped storage, batteries) and flexible loads so surplus is not wasted and inefficient peaking plants are avoided proximity to the grid / demand to reduce transmission losses and cable costs access to cooling water for thermal stations resource availability (wind, tidal range, fuel deliveries) land availability and planning / public acceptance; stable geology", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ECON-08\" data-qid=\"ECON-08\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 8</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-conservation.html\">Energy conservation: transport, buildings, industry</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 234-235 &middot; ECON-08</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.1</span>Explain how integrated manufacture saves energy in the iron and steel industry.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.2</span>Explain why recycling is not always energy efficient.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.3</span>Explain how each of the following manages electricity infrastructure to conserve energy.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.4</span>Give <b>two</b> locational factors affecting where new generating infrastructure is built.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Molten iron from the blast furnace is converted directly to steel on the same site</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So it does not cool and need re-melting; transport energy between sites is also avoided; waste heat from one process used by another (eg Kalundborg)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Collecting and processing small dispersed quantities can use more energy in transport than is saved</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mass production and large-scale mining have economies of scale; some materials (glass) take more energy to make than plastic but can be refilled many times</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Transmitting at high voltage means low current so less energy is lost as heat in the cables (loss proportional to current squared); overhead cables cheaper than underground which need cooling</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>ICT / smart grids co-ordinate real-time data on demand and supply from many sources, switching generation, storage (pumped storage, batteries) and flexible loads so surplus is not wasted and inefficient peaking plants are avoided</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Proximity to the grid / demand to reduce transmission losses and cable costs</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Access to cooling water for thermal stations</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Resource availability (wind, tidal range, fuel deliveries)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Land availability and planning / public acceptance; stable geology</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 234-235; AQA spec 3.3.4.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ENF-01", "sub": "energy-features", "subname": "Importance & features of energy resources", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.3.1", "pages": "170-174", "url": "subtopic/energy-features.html#ENF-01", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the per capita energy consumption and GDP per capita of five countries.", "text": "table 1 shows the per capita energy consumption and gdp per capita of five countries. describe the relationship between gdp per capita and energy use per capita shown in table 1. explain why affluence increases per capita energy consumption. suggest two reasons why country b uses much less energy per capita than country a despite a similar level of affluence. explain why the per capita energy consumption of the uk has fallen in recent years even though consumption of goods has risen. give one way energy is used in each of the following: water supplies; agriculture. in general, higher gdp per capita is associated with higher energy use per capita (positive correlation) but not proportional: b has a high gdp but much lower energy use than a or e; c uses relatively high energy for its gdp affluent people buy more consumer goods, appliances and travel more / drive larger, less efficient vehicles and heat larger homes manufacture and transport of the extra products bought uses energy (indirect use); larger houses use more energy to build affluent countries can afford energy for non-essential uses; cheap local energy (eg petrol in the usa) encourages wasteful use higher energy prices / taxes encouraging conservation culture of energy conservation (eg scandinavia after past shortages) / environmental awareness milder climate needing less heating or air conditioning service-based economy: heavy industry (steel, aluminium) has moved to other countries so energy embodied in imports is not counted smaller / more efficient vehicles, better public transport, better insulated buildings decline of heavy industries such as steel and aluminium smelting in the uk the energy-intensive goods are now manufactured abroad (eg china) so the energy is counted in the exporting country; also efficiency improvements water: pumping, treatment / sterilisation, sewage treatment; agriculture: machinery, fertiliser manufacture (haber process), irrigation pumps, processing / refrigeration", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ENF-01\" data-qid=\"ENF-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-features.html\">Importance &amp; features of energy resources</a> &middot; spec 3.3.1 &middot; Genn pp. 170-174 &middot; ENF-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the per capita energy consumption and GDP per capita of five countries.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Country</th><th>GDP per capita / US$ thousand</th><th>Energy use per capita / GJ per year</th><th>Main energy resource</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>62</td><td>290</td><td>Oil, gas</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>44</td><td>125</td><td>Gas, nuclear, renewables</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>10</td><td>95</td><td>Coal</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>2</td><td>25</td><td>Biomass (wood), oil</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>55</td><td>220</td><td>Hydroelectricity, oil</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Describe the relationship between GDP per capita and energy use per capita shown in Table 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain why affluence increases per capita energy consumption.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Suggest <b>two</b> reasons why country B uses much less energy per capita than country A despite a similar level of affluence.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Explain why the per capita energy consumption of the UK has fallen in recent years even though consumption of goods has risen.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.5</span>Give <b>one</b> way energy is used in each of the following: water supplies; agriculture.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In general, higher GDP per capita is associated with higher energy use per capita (positive correlation)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>But not proportional: B has a high GDP but much lower energy use than A or E; C uses relatively high energy for its GDP</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Affluent people buy more consumer goods, appliances and travel more / drive larger, less efficient vehicles and heat larger homes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Manufacture and transport of the extra products bought uses energy (indirect use); larger houses use more energy to build</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Affluent countries can afford energy for non-essential uses; cheap local energy (eg petrol in the USA) encourages wasteful use</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Higher energy prices / taxes encouraging conservation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Culture of energy conservation (eg Scandinavia after past shortages) / environmental awareness</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Milder climate needing less heating or air conditioning</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Service-based economy: heavy industry (steel, aluminium) has moved to other countries so energy embodied in imports is not counted</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Smaller / more efficient vehicles, better public transport, better insulated buildings</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Decline of heavy industries such as steel and aluminium smelting in the UK</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The energy-intensive goods are now manufactured abroad (eg China) so the energy is counted in the exporting country; also efficiency improvements</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Water: pumping, treatment / sterilisation, sewage treatment; Agriculture: machinery, fertiliser manufacture (Haber process), irrigation pumps, processing / refrigeration</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 170-174; AQA spec 3.3.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ENF-02", "sub": "energy-features", "subname": "Importance & features of energy resources", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.3.2", "pages": "175-178", "url": "subtopic/energy-features.html#ENF-02", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the approximate energy density of some energy resources.", "text": "table 1 shows the approximate energy density of some energy resources. explain what is meant by energy density and why it is an important feature of an energy resource. use table 1 to calculate how many tonnes of coal contain the same energy as 1 kg of uranium. explain why low energy density does not prevent solar and wind power from being useful. explain the difference between an intermittent resource and an unpredictable resource, using named examples. explain why natural gas must be liquefied for transport by ship. the amount of energy contained in a given mass of the resource (or harnessed per kg of equipment for renewables) high energy density means smaller quantities are needed so storage and transport are easier and cheaper (eg 75 t of aviation fuel carries a 400 t aircraft 5600 km) high energy density fuels reach high temperatures needed for smelting / steam turbines 500 000 / 28 = 17 857 kg = 17.9 tonnes (accept 18; book quotes 13 t for 0.7% enriched fuel) the electricity they generate can power high-energy-requirement technologies electricity can be converted to high energy density secondary fuels, eg hydrogen by electrolysis, or stored (batteries, pumped storage) intermittent: not available all the time, eg tidal (but the times are predictable so alternatives can be planned) unpredictable: intermittent and the timing / amount cannot be forecast accurately, eg wind, solar gaseous natural gas has a very low energy density by volume; lng at -160 °c has 160 times the energy density so a ship can carry a worthwhile amount", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ENF-02\" data-qid=\"ENF-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-features.html\">Importance &amp; features of energy resources</a> &middot; spec 3.3.2 &middot; Genn pp. 175-178 &middot; ENF-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the approximate energy density of some energy resources.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Energy resource</th><th>Energy density / MJ kg<sup>-1</sup></th></tr><tr><td>Uranium (natural, in fission reactor)</td><td>500 000</td></tr><tr><td>Natural gas</td><td>55</td></tr><tr><td>Petrol</td><td>46</td></tr><tr><td>Coal</td><td>28</td></tr><tr><td>Dry wood</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td>Lithium-ion battery (stored electricity)</td><td>0.7</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain what is meant by <b>energy density</b> and why it is an important feature of an energy resource.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Use Table 1 to calculate how many tonnes of coal contain the same energy as 1 kg of uranium.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain why low energy density does not prevent solar and wind power from being useful.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Explain the difference between an <b>intermittent</b> resource and an <b>unpredictable</b> resource, using named examples.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.5</span>Explain why natural gas must be liquefied for transport by ship.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The amount of energy contained in a given mass of the resource (or harnessed per kg of equipment for renewables)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>High energy density means smaller quantities are needed so storage and transport are easier and cheaper (eg 75 t of aviation fuel carries a 400 t aircraft 5600 km)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>High energy density fuels reach high temperatures needed for smelting / steam turbines</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>500 000 / 28 = 17 857 kg</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 17.9 tonnes (accept 18; book quotes 13 t for 0.7% enriched fuel)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The electricity they generate can power high-energy-requirement technologies</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Electricity can be converted to high energy density secondary fuels, eg hydrogen by electrolysis, or stored (batteries, pumped storage)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Intermittent: not available all the time, eg tidal (but the times are predictable so alternatives can be planned)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Unpredictable: intermittent and the timing / amount cannot be forecast accurately, eg wind, solar</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Gaseous natural gas has a very low energy density by volume; LNG at -160 &deg;C has 160 times the energy density so a ship can carry a worthwhile amount</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 175-178; AQA spec 3.3.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ENF-03", "sub": "energy-features", "subname": "Importance & features of energy resources", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.3.2", "pages": "175-177", "url": "subtopic/energy-features.html#ENF-03", "snippet": "Distinguish between renewable and depletable energy resources, giving an example of a resource that is both renewable and depletable.", "text": " distinguish between renewable and depletable energy resources, giving an example of a resource that is both renewable and depletable. explain why the abundance of an energy resource is not the same as the amount available for use. give two examples. describe the locational constraints of two named renewable energy resources. renewable: re-forms naturally relatively quickly so use does not necessarily reduce future availability (solar, wind, tidal, geothermal, biofuel) depletable: use can reduce future availability - all non-renewables (fossil fuels, uranium) plus renewables exploited unsustainably wood / biofuels are renewable but depletable if forests are felled faster than they regrow other factors (technology, location, cost) restrict how much can be harnessed examples: coal too deep to mine; winds high above the ground / far offshore where turbines cannot be placed; waves in deep water where equipment cannot be anchored; low-intensity sunlight that cannot produce high temperatures new technologies or higher prices may make these usable in future wind: strong reliable winds - shallow seas, open plains, uplands; low land-use conflict (not near urban areas, ecologically sensitive or scenic areas); near the grid hep: high reliable rainfall, large catchment, narrow valley for a small dam with a large basin, impermeable stable bedrock solar: high light intensity, low cloud cover wave: coasts with strong reliable prevailing winds and a long fetch tidal: large tidal range, coastal features (estuaries, channels) that focus the flow geothermal: hot rocks near the surface / recent volcanic activity biofuels: nearby farmland / forest / urban waste to avoid transporting bulky low-density fuel", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ENF-03\" data-qid=\"ENF-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-features.html\">Importance &amp; features of energy resources</a> &middot; spec 3.3.2 &middot; Genn pp. 175-177 &middot; ENF-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Distinguish between <b>renewable</b> and <b>depletable</b> energy resources, giving an example of a resource that is both renewable and depletable.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain why the abundance of an energy resource is not the same as the amount available for use. Give <b>two</b> examples.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Describe the locational constraints of <b>two</b> named renewable energy resources.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Renewable: re-forms naturally relatively quickly so use does not necessarily reduce future availability (solar, wind, tidal, geothermal, biofuel)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Depletable: use can reduce future availability - all non-renewables (fossil fuels, uranium) plus renewables exploited unsustainably</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wood / biofuels are renewable but depletable if forests are felled faster than they regrow</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Other factors (technology, location, cost) restrict how much can be harnessed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Examples: coal too deep to mine; winds high above the ground / far offshore where turbines cannot be placed; waves in deep water where equipment cannot be anchored; low-intensity sunlight that cannot produce high temperatures</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>New technologies or higher prices may make these usable in future</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wind: strong reliable winds - shallow seas, open plains, uplands; low land-use conflict (not near urban areas, ecologically sensitive or scenic areas); near the grid</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>HEP: high reliable rainfall, large catchment, narrow valley for a small dam with a large basin, impermeable stable bedrock</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Solar: high light intensity, low cloud cover</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wave: coasts with strong reliable prevailing winds and a long fetch</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Tidal: large tidal range, coastal features (estuaries, channels) that focus the flow</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Geothermal: hot rocks near the surface / recent volcanic activity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Biofuels: nearby farmland / forest / urban waste to avoid transporting bulky low-density fuel</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 175-177; AQA spec 3.3.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ENF-04", "sub": "energy-features", "subname": "Importance & features of energy resources", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.3.2-3.3.3", "pages": "179-181", "url": "subtopic/energy-features.html#ENF-04", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the estimated cost of generating electricity from three sources between 1990 and 2020, and projections to 2030.", "text": "figure 1 shows the estimated cost of generating electricity from three sources between 1990 and 2020, and projections to 2030. describe the trends shown in figure 1. explain why new energy technologies are expensive when first introduced and why their costs then fall. explain why a renewable energy project may still be at a financial disadvantage compared with a gas-fired power station of the same total cost. explain why the price paid for fossil fuel energy does not reflect its full cost. solar pv cost fell dramatically from about 480 to 50 $/mwh (about 90%), the steepest fall wind fell steadily from 200 to 45 gas stayed roughly constant at 60-70; solar and wind became cheaper than gas around 2015-2020 and are projected to fall further development / research costs are still being paid for and there are no economies of scale in manufacture they compete with established technologies whose development costs were paid long ago and which benefit from mass production and existing infrastructure as output grows, mass production, improved designs / efficiency and experience reduce costs most of the renewable cost is the initial equipment, paid for with loans on which interest must be paid; running costs are very low most of the gas project's cost is running costs (fuel) paid from income without borrowing, so it has lower interest payments environmental damage (acid rain damage to buildings, crops, forests; health effects of air pollution; climate change) is paid for by others (health service, agriculture) not the energy user some impacts (climate change) cannot yet be costed; decommissioning and waste costs are also uncertain", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ENF-04\" data-qid=\"ENF-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-features.html\">Importance &amp; features of energy resources</a> &middot; spec 3.3.2-3.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 179-181 &middot; ENF-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the estimated cost of generating electricity from three sources between 1990 and 2020, and projections to 2030.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,41.4 180.0,103.3 300.0,174.8 420.0,246.2 540.0,255.7\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"41.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"180.0\" cy=\"103.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"174.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"420.0\" cy=\"246.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"255.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<polyline points=\"60.0,236.7 180.0,239.0 300.0,236.7 420.0,241.4 540.0,239.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2f5f73\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"236.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"180.0\" cy=\"239.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"236.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"420.0\" cy=\"241.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"239.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<polyline points=\"60.0,174.8 180.0,212.9 300.0,231.9 420.0,248.6 540.0,253.3\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#8b5e3c\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"174.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"180.0\" cy=\"212.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"231.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"420.0\" cy=\"248.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"253.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1990</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1998</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">105</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2006</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">210</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2014</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">315</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2022</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">420</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2030</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">525</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Year</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Cost / US$ per MWh</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"24\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#22302a\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"33\">Solar PV</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"38\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"47\">Natural gas</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"52\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"61\">Onshore wind</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Describe the trends shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain why new energy technologies are expensive when first introduced and why their costs then fall.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain why a renewable energy project may still be at a financial disadvantage compared with a gas-fired power station of the same total cost.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Explain why the price paid for fossil fuel energy does not reflect its full cost.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Solar PV cost fell dramatically from about 480 to 50 $/MWh (about 90%), the steepest fall</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wind fell steadily from 200 to 45</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Gas stayed roughly constant at 60-70; solar and wind became cheaper than gas around 2015-2020 and are projected to fall further</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Development / research costs are still being paid for and there are no economies of scale in manufacture</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They compete with established technologies whose development costs were paid long ago and which benefit from mass production and existing infrastructure</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>As output grows, mass production, improved designs / efficiency and experience reduce costs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Most of the renewable cost is the initial equipment, paid for with loans on which interest must be paid; running costs are very low</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Most of the gas project's cost is running costs (fuel) paid from income without borrowing, so it has lower interest payments</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Environmental damage (acid rain damage to buildings, crops, forests; health effects of air pollution; climate change) is paid for by others (health service, agriculture) not the energy user</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Some impacts (climate change) cannot yet be costed; decommissioning and waste costs are also uncertain</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 179-181; AQA spec 3.3.2-3.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ENF-05", "sub": "energy-features", "subname": "Importance & features of energy resources", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.3.3", "pages": "180-186", "url": "subtopic/energy-features.html#ENF-05", "snippet": "Explain why current global energy use is described as unsustainable.", "text": " explain why current global energy use is described as unsustainable. describe the environmental impacts of exploiting fossil fuels that occur before the fuel is used. explain why an 'energy gap' could occur if society waits until fossil fuels become expensive before developing alternatives. most energy comes from non-renewable fossil fuels whose reserves are depleting; the rate of new discoveries has fallen sharply since the 1980s use causes unacceptable environmental damage: atmospheric pollution, climate change, oil pollution, habitat loss economic / social: depleting resources become more expensive, reducing future affluence; risk of an energy gap if new technologies are not developed in time coal mining: habitat loss (especially open-cast), spoil heaps, subsidence from deep mines, acid drainage, dust, noise oil and gas extraction: spills from rigs, oil-based drilling mud polluting groundwater and sea, gas flaring releasing so2 / co2 / smoke pipelines causing habitat damage during construction; tanker transport risks fuel processing (refineries, coal washing) pollution; embodied energy of equipment and site development for all resources new technologies take many years to develop, prove and scale up, and need new infrastructure (grids, storage, vehicles) existing cheap fossil fuel technologies make it hard for alternatives to compete until fuel prices rise by then depletion would reduce supply faster than replacements could be built, causing shortages", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ENF-05\" data-qid=\"ENF-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-features.html\">Importance &amp; features of energy resources</a> &middot; spec 3.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 180-186 &middot; ENF-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Explain why current global energy use is described as unsustainable.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Describe the environmental impacts of exploiting fossil fuels that occur <b>before</b> the fuel is used.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain why an 'energy gap' could occur if society waits until fossil fuels become expensive before developing alternatives.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Most energy comes from non-renewable fossil fuels whose reserves are depleting; the rate of new discoveries has fallen sharply since the 1980s</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Use causes unacceptable environmental damage: atmospheric pollution, climate change, oil pollution, habitat loss</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Economic / social: depleting resources become more expensive, reducing future affluence; risk of an energy gap if new technologies are not developed in time</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Coal mining: habitat loss (especially open-cast), spoil heaps, subsidence from deep mines, acid drainage, dust, noise</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Oil and gas extraction: spills from rigs, oil-based drilling mud polluting groundwater and sea, gas flaring releasing SO2 / CO2 / smoke</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pipelines causing habitat damage during construction; tanker transport risks</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fuel processing (refineries, coal washing) pollution; embodied energy of equipment and site development for all resources</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>New technologies take many years to develop, prove and scale up, and need new infrastructure (grids, storage, vehicles)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Existing cheap fossil fuel technologies make it hard for alternatives to compete until fuel prices rise</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>By then depletion would reduce supply faster than replacements could be built, causing shortages</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 180-186; AQA spec 3.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "ENF-06", "sub": "energy-features", "subname": "Importance & features of energy resources", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.3.2-3.3.3", "pages": "175-182", "url": "subtopic/energy-features.html#ENF-06", "snippet": "Discuss how the features of energy resources determine their suitability for different uses and their environmental sustainability.", "text": " discuss how the features of energy resources determine their suitability for different uses and their environmental sustainability. state one reason why nuclear power stations can be located where transport infrastructure is poor. features: renewable / non-renewable, depletable, abundance, energy density, locational constraints, intermittency and predictability, need for conversions, applicability, ease of storage and transport, technological development, political and economic influences, environmental impacts fossil fuels: high energy density, easy storage and transport, reach high temperatures, well-developed technologies and infrastructure - but finite, polluting (acid rain, smoke, co2), politically concentrated (middle east oil) nuclear: very high energy density, base load, few fuel deliveries - but complex, expensive, waste, public opposition renewables: low energy density, intermittent (wind, solar unpredictable; tidal predictable), locationally constrained, cannot be stored without conversion, need storage / grid, cannot reach fossil-fuel temperatures or replace liquid vehicle fuels yet - but renewable, low pollution, falling costs sustainability concepts: impacts before use (extraction, processing, equipment embodied energy) and as a consequence of use (pollution, habitat damage, depletion) economic and political factors: subsidies, guaranteed prices, planning, external costs not included in price, energy security judgement: a diverse mix matched to uses (electricity vs heat vs transport) with storage is most sustainable; transition must start before depletion very high energy density fuel means only a few tens of tonnes are needed each year, compared with 10 000 t of coal per day for a coal-fired station", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"ENF-06\" data-qid=\"ENF-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-features.html\">Importance &amp; features of energy resources</a> &middot; spec 3.3.2-3.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 175-182 &middot; ENF-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Discuss how the features of energy resources determine their suitability for different uses and their environmental sustainability.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>State <b>one</b> reason why nuclear power stations can be located where transport infrastructure is poor.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Features: renewable / non-renewable, depletable, abundance, energy density, locational constraints, intermittency and predictability, need for conversions, applicability, ease of storage and transport, technological development, political and economic influences, environmental impacts</li><li>Fossil fuels: high energy density, easy storage and transport, reach high temperatures, well-developed technologies and infrastructure - but finite, polluting (acid rain, smoke, CO2), politically concentrated (Middle East oil)</li><li>Nuclear: very high energy density, base load, few fuel deliveries - but complex, expensive, waste, public opposition</li><li>Renewables: low energy density, intermittent (wind, solar unpredictable; tidal predictable), locationally constrained, cannot be stored without conversion, need storage / grid, cannot reach fossil-fuel temperatures or replace liquid vehicle fuels yet - but renewable, low pollution, falling costs</li><li>Sustainability concepts: impacts before use (extraction, processing, equipment embodied energy) and as a consequence of use (pollution, habitat damage, depletion)</li><li>Economic and political factors: subsidies, guaranteed prices, planning, external costs not included in price, energy security</li><li>Judgement: a diverse mix matched to uses (electricity vs heat vs transport) with storage is most sustainable; transition must start before depletion</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Very high energy density fuel means only a few tens of tonnes are needed each year, compared with 10 000 t of coal per day for a coal-fired station</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 175-182; AQA spec 3.3.2-3.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "EST-01", "sub": "energy-storage", "subname": "Fluctuations in supply/demand & energy storage", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "221-222", "url": "subtopic/energy-storage.html#EST-01", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the demand for electricity in the UK over a 24-hour period on a weekday in winter.", "text": "figure 1 shows the demand for electricity in the uk over a 24-hour period on a weekday in winter. describe the pattern of demand shown in figure 1. explain the causes of the fluctuations shown in figure 1 and give two other causes of fluctuations in electricity demand. explain why base-load power stations (nuclear, large coal) produce a surplus of electricity at night. give two causes of fluctuations in energy supply. lowest overnight (about 25 gw at 04:00), rising steeply from 06:00 to about 40 gw by 08:00 fairly steady through the day then a peak of about 50 gw at 18:00 (evening) before falling overnight 24-hour day / night cycle: people asleep overnight; morning rise as heating, lighting, cooking, industry and offices start evening peak: lighting, cooking, heating as people return home; short-term 'tv pickup' at programme breaks / mealtimes other causes: seasonal (winter heating / lighting, summer air conditioning); weather-related (cold snaps); weekday / weekend differences in industrial use they generate 24 hours a day because it is uneconomic / slow to turn them off and on, and output changes slowly demand falls faster at night than output can be reduced, so the surplus must be stored or is lost as heat from cables intermittent renewable resources: wind, solar, tidal bulk delivery of transported fuels (oil tankers, coal trains, biofuel harvests) breakdowns / maintenance of power stations", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"EST-01\" data-qid=\"EST-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-storage.html\">Fluctuations in supply/demand &amp; energy storage</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 221-222 &middot; EST-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the demand for electricity in the UK over a 24-hour period on a weekday in winter.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,127.1 100.0,146.2 140.0,151.0 180.0,127.1 220.0,79.5 260.0,70.0 300.0,74.8 340.0,79.5 380.0,60.5 420.0,31.9 460.0,51.0 500.0,89.0 540.0,122.4\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"127.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"100.0\" cy=\"146.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"140.0\" cy=\"151.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"180.0\" cy=\"127.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"220.0\" cy=\"79.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"260.0\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"74.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"340.0\" cy=\"79.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"380.0\" cy=\"60.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"420.0\" cy=\"31.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"460.0\" cy=\"51.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"500.0\" cy=\"89.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"122.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">10.5</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">9.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">21</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">14.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">31.5</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">19.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">42</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">24</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">52.5</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Time / hours</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Demand / GW</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Describe the pattern of demand shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain the causes of the fluctuations shown in Figure 1 and give <b>two</b> other causes of fluctuations in electricity demand.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain why base-load power stations (nuclear, large coal) produce a surplus of electricity at night.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Give <b>two</b> causes of fluctuations in energy <b>supply</b>.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lowest overnight (about 25 GW at 04:00), rising steeply from 06:00 to about 40 GW by 08:00</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fairly steady through the day then a peak of about 50 GW at 18:00 (evening) before falling overnight</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 4 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>24-hour day / night cycle: people asleep overnight; morning rise as heating, lighting, cooking, industry and offices start</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Evening peak: lighting, cooking, heating as people return home; short-term 'TV pickup' at programme breaks / mealtimes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Other causes: seasonal (winter heating / lighting, summer air conditioning); weather-related (cold snaps); weekday / weekend differences in industrial use</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They generate 24 hours a day because it is uneconomic / slow to turn them off and on, and output changes slowly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Demand falls faster at night than output can be reduced, so the surplus must be stored or is lost as heat from cables</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Intermittent renewable resources: wind, solar, tidal</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bulk delivery of transported fuels (oil tankers, coal trains, biofuel harvests)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Breakdowns / maintenance of power stations</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 221-222; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "EST-02", "sub": "energy-storage", "subname": "Fluctuations in supply/demand & energy storage", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc"], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "222", "url": "subtopic/energy-storage.html#EST-02", "snippet": "A pumped-storage HEP station has an upper reservoir 500 m above its turbines. It can hold 7 million m3 of water. The density of water is 1000 kg m-3 and g = 9.8 N kg-1.", "text": "a pumped-storage hep station has an upper reservoir 500 m above its turbines. it can hold 7 million m3 of water. the density of water is 1000 kg m-3 and g = 9.8 n kg-1. describe how pumped-storage hep is used for peak shaving. calculate the maximum gravitational potential energy stored when the upper reservoir is full. give your answer in standard form. the station returns 75% of the electricity used for pumping. explain why the process is still worthwhile despite this loss. give two locational requirements of a pumped-storage station. at times of low demand / surplus supply (night, windy periods) surplus electricity pumps water from the lower to the upper reservoir, storing it as gravitational potential energy at peaks in demand water flows down through the turbines generating electricity can go from standby to full power in under 15 seconds so it meets sudden peaks (tv pickup) that fossil / nuclear stations cannot follow mass = 7 x 106 x 1000 = 7 x 109 kg pe = m g h = 7 x 109 x 9.8 x 500 = 3.43 x 1013 j (accept 3.4 x 1013) the electricity used for pumping is surplus that would otherwise be wasted / lost as heat, or cheap off-peak electricity it avoids building rapid-response low-efficiency peaking plants (open-cycle gas turbines) and allows intermittent renewables to be used two water bodies / sites with a large height difference close together (mountainous) impermeable, stable rock close to the grid / demand adequate water supply", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"EST-02\" data-qid=\"EST-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-storage.html\">Fluctuations in supply/demand &amp; energy storage</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 222 &middot; EST-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A pumped-storage HEP station has an upper reservoir 500 m above its turbines. It can hold 7 million m<sup>3</sup> of water. The density of water is 1000 kg m<sup>-3</sup> and g = 9.8 N kg<sup>-1</sup>.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Describe how pumped-storage HEP is used for peak shaving.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Calculate the maximum gravitational potential energy stored when the upper reservoir is full. Give your answer in standard form.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>The station returns 75% of the electricity used for pumping. Explain why the process is still worthwhile despite this loss.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Give <b>two</b> locational requirements of a pumped-storage station.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>At times of low demand / surplus supply (night, windy periods) surplus electricity pumps water from the lower to the upper reservoir, storing it as gravitational potential energy</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>At peaks in demand water flows down through the turbines generating electricity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Can go from standby to full power in under 15 seconds so it meets sudden peaks (TV pickup) that fossil / nuclear stations cannot follow</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mass = 7 x 10<sup>6</sup> x 1000 = 7 x 10<sup>9</sup> kg</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>PE = m g h = 7 x 10<sup>9</sup> x 9.8 x 500</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 3.43 x 10<sup>13</sup> J (accept 3.4 x 10<sup>13</sup>)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The electricity used for pumping is surplus that would otherwise be wasted / lost as heat, or cheap off-peak electricity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It avoids building rapid-response low-efficiency peaking plants (open-cycle gas turbines) and allows intermittent renewables to be used</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Two water bodies / sites with a large height difference close together (mountainous)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Impermeable, stable rock</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Close to the grid / demand</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Adequate water supply</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 222; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "EST-03", "sub": "energy-storage", "subname": "Fluctuations in supply/demand & energy storage", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "219-221", "url": "subtopic/energy-storage.html#EST-03", "snippet": "Explain how hydrogen can be used to store energy from intermittent renewable resources, and describe two ways the stored energy can be released.", "text": " explain how hydrogen can be used to store energy from intermittent renewable resources, and describe two ways the stored energy can be released. compare three methods of storing hydrogen, giving a disadvantage of each. explain how a fuel cell differs from a rechargeable battery. explain what is meant by the 'hydrogen economy'. surplus electricity (eg windy nights) electrolyses water to produce hydrogen which is stored hydrogen has a high energy density so it can be transported / stored for long periods released by combustion for heat, industrial processes or steam turbines; or by fuel cells combining hydrogen and oxygen electrochemically to produce electricity and water can also be used as a vehicle fuel or converted to methane / ammonia compressed gas (up to 700 times normal volume) - energy to run the compressor, strong tanks liquefied at very low temperature - energy for refrigeration and compression adsorbed on a metal matrix (metal hydride) at low pressure - tanks larger and heavier than a petrol tank storing the same energy conversion to ammonia - some energy needed but less than the alternatives both convert chemical energy to electricity electrochemically, but a battery stores a fixed amount of chemicals which are reformed by recharging a fuel cell keeps producing electricity as long as fresh fuel (hydrogen / alcohol) and oxidant are supplied using hydrogen made from surplus renewable electricity as the main store and carrier of energy, released when demand exceeds supply or intermittent sources fail, for heating, vehicles and electricity", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"EST-03\" data-qid=\"EST-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-storage.html\">Fluctuations in supply/demand &amp; energy storage</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 219-221 &middot; EST-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Explain how hydrogen can be used to store energy from intermittent renewable resources, and describe <b>two</b> ways the stored energy can be released.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Compare <b>three</b> methods of storing hydrogen, giving a disadvantage of each.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain how a fuel cell differs from a rechargeable battery.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Explain what is meant by the 'hydrogen economy'.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Surplus electricity (eg windy nights) electrolyses water to produce hydrogen which is stored</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hydrogen has a high energy density so it can be transported / stored for long periods</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Released by combustion for heat, industrial processes or steam turbines; or by fuel cells combining hydrogen and oxygen electrochemically to produce electricity and water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Can also be used as a vehicle fuel or converted to methane / ammonia</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Compressed gas (up to 700 times normal volume) - energy to run the compressor, strong tanks</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Liquefied at very low temperature - energy for refrigeration and compression</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Adsorbed on a metal matrix (metal hydride) at low pressure - tanks larger and heavier than a petrol tank storing the same energy</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Conversion to ammonia - some energy needed but less than the alternatives</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Both convert chemical energy to electricity electrochemically, but a battery stores a fixed amount of chemicals which are reformed by recharging</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A fuel cell keeps producing electricity as long as fresh fuel (hydrogen / alcohol) and oxidant are supplied</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Using hydrogen made from surplus renewable electricity as the main store and carrier of energy, released when demand exceeds supply or intermittent sources fail, for heating, vehicles and electricity</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 219-221; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "EST-04", "sub": "energy-storage", "subname": "Fluctuations in supply/demand & energy storage", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "223-224", "url": "subtopic/energy-storage.html#EST-04", "snippet": "Table 1 shows features of three rechargeable battery types.", "text": "table 1 shows features of three rechargeable battery types. use table 1 to explain why lithium-ion batteries are used in electric vehicles but lead-acid batteries are still used for stationary storage in some off-grid solar systems. give two other factors that affect the viability of a rechargeable battery. describe how vehicle-to-grid (v2g) systems could reduce the need for peaking power stations. explain how power-to-gas (p2g) systems use existing infrastructure. vehicles: high energy density (200 wh kg-1) minimises battery mass so range is greater; many cycles and high efficiency stationary storage: mass is unimportant so the low energy density of lead-acid does not matter; lowest cost per kwh; well-established, recyclable lead-acid drawbacks: fewer cycles and lower efficiency; lithium fire risk needs management nickel-cadmium largely replaced because of cadmium toxicity and cost recharging speed self-discharge during non-use availability of raw materials (lithium, cobalt) recyclability / disposal parked electric vehicles plugged into the grid form a very large distributed battery at peaks a small proportion of their stored energy is fed back to the grid; surplus grid energy recharges them - avoiding costly, low-efficiency open-cycle gas turbines surplus electricity electrolyses water to hydrogen, which can be converted to methane methane is fed into the existing natural gas pipe network and storage, so no new distribution system is needed", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"EST-04\" data-qid=\"EST-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-storage.html\">Fluctuations in supply/demand &amp; energy storage</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 223-224 &middot; EST-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows features of three rechargeable battery types.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Battery type</th><th>Energy density / Wh kg<sup>-1</sup></th><th>Charge-discharge cycles</th><th>Storage efficiency / %</th><th>Cost per kWh / &pound;</th><th>Safety issues</th></tr><tr><td>Lead-acid</td><td>35</td><td>500</td><td>80</td><td>100</td><td>Toxic lead, acid</td></tr><tr><td>Nickel-cadmium</td><td>50</td><td>1500</td><td>75</td><td>250</td><td>Toxic cadmium</td></tr><tr><td>Lithium-ion</td><td>200</td><td>2000</td><td>92</td><td>150</td><td>Fire risk</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Use Table 1 to explain why lithium-ion batteries are used in electric vehicles but lead-acid batteries are still used for stationary storage in some off-grid solar systems.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Give <b>two</b> other factors that affect the viability of a rechargeable battery.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Describe how Vehicle-to-Grid (V2G) systems could reduce the need for peaking power stations.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Explain how Power-to-Gas (P2G) systems use existing infrastructure.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Vehicles: high energy density (200 Wh kg<sup>-1</sup>) minimises battery mass so range is greater; many cycles and high efficiency</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stationary storage: mass is unimportant so the low energy density of lead-acid does not matter; lowest cost per kWh; well-established, recyclable</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lead-acid drawbacks: fewer cycles and lower efficiency; lithium fire risk needs management</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nickel-cadmium largely replaced because of cadmium toxicity and cost</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Recharging speed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Self-discharge during non-use</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Availability of raw materials (lithium, cobalt)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Recyclability / disposal</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Parked electric vehicles plugged into the grid form a very large distributed battery</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>At peaks a small proportion of their stored energy is fed back to the grid; surplus grid energy recharges them - avoiding costly, low-efficiency open-cycle gas turbines</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Surplus electricity electrolyses water to hydrogen, which can be converted to methane</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Methane is fed into the existing natural gas pipe network and storage, so no new distribution system is needed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 223-224; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "EST-05", "sub": "energy-storage", "subname": "Fluctuations in supply/demand & energy storage", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "222-224", "url": "subtopic/energy-storage.html#EST-05", "snippet": "Explain why compressed air energy storage with heat recovery can achieve over 90% efficiency.", "text": " explain why compressed air energy storage with heat recovery can achieve over 90% efficiency. explain why large-volume hot water stores lose heat more slowly per unit of stored energy than small ones. explain why molten salt rather than water is used to store heat from csp plants. explain how high thermal mass building materials reduce energy use. describe one other energy storage technology. surplus energy drives a pump compressing air stored in underground caverns (salt mines); releasing it drives machinery / turbines compression produces a lot of heat; storing this heat (hot oil / molten salt) and returning it when the air expands recovers energy that would otherwise be lost as volume increases the surface area : volume ratio decreases heat is lost through the surface so there is less surface per unit of stored heat; very large stores can carry summer solar heat into winter (inter-seasonal storage) potassium nitrate has a much higher boiling point so it stores heat at up to 550 °c without pressurisation water could only reach such temperatures under high pressure, increasing costs; high temperature needed to raise steam for turbines materials with high specific heat capacity (concrete, water) warm and cool slowly they absorb heat in hot weather reducing overheating / air conditioning, and release it as the weather cools, delaying the need for heating flywheels: rotating mass stores kinetic energy for short-term smoothing / generation supercapacitors: electrochemical capacitors with 10-100 times the energy density of conventional capacitors; possible future large-scale electricity storage thermal storage in molten salt / high-volume tanks; hydrogen / p2g; batteries", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"EST-05\" data-qid=\"EST-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-storage.html\">Fluctuations in supply/demand &amp; energy storage</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 222-224 &middot; EST-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Explain why compressed air energy storage with heat recovery can achieve over 90% efficiency.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain why large-volume hot water stores lose heat more slowly per unit of stored energy than small ones.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain why molten salt rather than water is used to store heat from CSP plants.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Explain how high thermal mass building materials reduce energy use.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.5</span>Describe <b>one</b> other energy storage technology.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Surplus energy drives a pump compressing air stored in underground caverns (salt mines); releasing it drives machinery / turbines</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Compression produces a lot of heat; storing this heat (hot oil / molten salt) and returning it when the air expands recovers energy that would otherwise be lost</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>As volume increases the surface area : volume ratio decreases</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Heat is lost through the surface so there is less surface per unit of stored heat; very large stores can carry summer solar heat into winter (inter-seasonal storage)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Potassium nitrate has a much higher boiling point so it stores heat at up to 550 &deg;C without pressurisation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Water could only reach such temperatures under high pressure, increasing costs; high temperature needed to raise steam for turbines</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Materials with high specific heat capacity (concrete, water) warm and cool slowly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They absorb heat in hot weather reducing overheating / air conditioning, and release it as the weather cools, delaying the need for heating</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Flywheels: rotating mass stores kinetic energy for short-term smoothing / generation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Supercapacitors: electrochemical capacitors with 10-100 times the energy density of conventional capacitors; possible future large-scale electricity storage</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Thermal storage in molten salt / high-volume tanks; hydrogen / P2G; batteries</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 222-224; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "EST-06", "sub": "energy-storage", "subname": "Fluctuations in supply/demand & energy storage", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "217-224", "url": "subtopic/energy-storage.html#EST-06", "snippet": "Explain why the development of energy storage technologies is essential if renewable energy resources are to provide most of a country's electricity.", "text": " explain why the development of energy storage technologies is essential if renewable energy resources are to provide most of a country's electricity. state what is meant by peak shaving. renewables are intermittent (solar at night, calm days, slack tides) and some unpredictable; demand fluctuates daily, weekly, seasonally and short-term; supply and demand rarely match without storage, surplus is wasted and shortages must be met by fossil-fuel peaking plants; large-scale electricity storage not yet possible directly so energy must be converted pumped-storage hep: proven, rapid response, peak shaving, but limited sites batteries: lithium-ion for grid and vehicles; factors affecting viability; v2g hydrogen: electrolysis, storage methods, fuel cells, hydrogen economy, p2g into the gas grid; high energy density enables vehicle use compressed air with heat recovery; thermal storage (molten salt for csp, high-volume stores, thermal mass); flywheels; supercapacitors grid management / ict and diversity of resources (wind + solar + tidal + biofuel) reduce but do not remove the need for storage; energy conservation reduces peaks evaluation of efficiency losses, costs, resource limits (lithium), and which technologies suit short-term vs inter-seasonal storage; judgement storing surplus energy at times of low demand / high supply to meet later peaks in demand, so peak generating capacity can be reduced", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"EST-06\" data-qid=\"EST-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/energy-storage.html\">Fluctuations in supply/demand &amp; energy storage</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 217-224 &middot; EST-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Explain why the development of energy storage technologies is essential if renewable energy resources are to provide most of a country's electricity.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>State what is meant by <b>peak shaving</b>.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Renewables are intermittent (solar at night, calm days, slack tides) and some unpredictable; demand fluctuates daily, weekly, seasonally and short-term; supply and demand rarely match</li><li>Without storage, surplus is wasted and shortages must be met by fossil-fuel peaking plants; large-scale electricity storage not yet possible directly so energy must be converted</li><li>Pumped-storage HEP: proven, rapid response, peak shaving, but limited sites</li><li>Batteries: lithium-ion for grid and vehicles; factors affecting viability; V2G</li><li>Hydrogen: electrolysis, storage methods, fuel cells, hydrogen economy, P2G into the gas grid; high energy density enables vehicle use</li><li>Compressed air with heat recovery; thermal storage (molten salt for CSP, high-volume stores, thermal mass); flywheels; supercapacitors</li><li>Grid management / ICT and diversity of resources (wind + solar + tidal + biofuel) reduce but do not remove the need for storage; energy conservation reduces peaks</li><li>Evaluation of efficiency losses, costs, resource limits (lithium), and which technologies suit short-term vs inter-seasonal storage; judgement</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Storing surplus energy at times of low demand / high supply to meet later peaks in demand, so peak generating capacity can be reduced</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 217-224; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HYD-07", "sub": "hydro-management", "subname": "Water treatment, conservation & new sources", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.2.5", "pages": "122-124", "url": "subtopic/hydro-management.html#HYD-07", "snippet": "Describe four factors that should be considered when choosing a site for a dam and reservoir.", "text": " describe four factors that should be considered when choosing a site for a dam and reservoir. give one advantage and two disadvantages of an estuary barrage compared with a reservoir on land. explain why desalination of seawater is only used in some countries. explain how artificial recharge can make aquifer supplies sustainable. topography: a narrow exit from a large deep basin so a small dam holds a large volume geology: impermeable rock beneath (no seepage), strong enough to support the dam, no faults / seismic activity catchment area: large enough with regular, reliable rainfall / river inflow; climate not too hot (evaporation losses) catchment land use: low pollution risk (industry, pesticides); low soil erosion (sedimentation reduces capacity) infrastructure / proximity to demand: access for construction, cost of transporting water existing land use: value of what is lost (urban areas, conservation sites, farmland) - loss:benefit analysis advantage: fewer land-use conflicts as no land is flooded; large volume near coastal cities disadvantages: destroys / changes important intertidal habitats (mudflats for wading birds); obstacle to shipping; collects pollution from the whole river catchment; sedimentation very energy-intensive and expensive (reverse osmosis / distillation) only worthwhile where seawater is available and freshwater supplies are inadequate (eg malta, middle east) surplus water in wet periods (when infiltration capacity is exceeded and runoff would go to the sea) is pumped underground or diverted into lagoons to infiltrate, so abstraction in dry periods is balanced by recharge", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HYD-07\" data-qid=\"HYD-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/hydro-management.html\">Water treatment, conservation &amp; new sources</a> &middot; spec 3.2.2.5 &middot; Genn pp. 122-124 &middot; HYD-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Describe <b>four</b> factors that should be considered when choosing a site for a dam and reservoir.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Give <b>one</b> advantage and <b>two</b> disadvantages of an estuary barrage compared with a reservoir on land.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain why desalination of seawater is only used in some countries.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Explain how artificial recharge can make aquifer supplies sustainable.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Topography: a narrow exit from a large deep basin so a small dam holds a large volume</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Geology: impermeable rock beneath (no seepage), strong enough to support the dam, no faults / seismic activity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Catchment area: large enough with regular, reliable rainfall / river inflow; climate not too hot (evaporation losses)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Catchment land use: low pollution risk (industry, pesticides); low soil erosion (sedimentation reduces capacity)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Infrastructure / proximity to demand: access for construction, cost of transporting water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Existing land use: value of what is lost (urban areas, conservation sites, farmland) - loss:benefit analysis</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 3 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Advantage: fewer land-use conflicts as no land is flooded; large volume near coastal cities</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Disadvantages: destroys / changes important intertidal habitats (mudflats for wading birds); obstacle to shipping; collects pollution from the whole river catchment; sedimentation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Very energy-intensive and expensive (reverse osmosis / distillation)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Only worthwhile where seawater is available and freshwater supplies are inadequate (eg Malta, Middle East)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Surplus water in wet periods (when infiltration capacity is exceeded and runoff would go to the sea) is pumped underground or diverted into lagoons to infiltrate, so abstraction in dry periods is balanced by recharge</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 122-124; AQA spec 3.2.2.5.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HYD-08", "sub": "hydro-management", "subname": "Water treatment, conservation & new sources", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.2.2.5", "pages": "124-126", "url": "subtopic/hydro-management.html#HYD-08", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the effect of installing water meters on the mean daily water use of households in a UK town.", "text": "table 1 shows the effect of installing water meters on the mean daily water use of households in a uk town. calculate the percentage reduction in water use per person in metered households. explain why metering reduces water use. describe three ways in which domestic water use can be reduced other than metering. explain why about 20% of the water intended for public supply in the uk never reaches consumers, and how this could be reduced. explain how drip irrigation reduces the water needed to grow a crop compared with spray irrigation. (150 - 125) / 150 x 100 = 16.7% (accept 17%) a fixed fee gives no incentive to save water because the bill is the same however much is used metering matches the charge to the volume used so households save money by using less (fewer hosepipes, low-flow appliances) low-water appliances: dual-flush toilets, low-flow shower heads, efficient washing machines / dishwashers, tap timers greywater re-use: bath / shower water used to flush toilets or water gardens rainwater collection / water butts for garden use xeriscaping: planting drought-adapted species that need no watering repairing leaks / dripping taps old infrastructure / pipes in poor condition leak repair and good maintenance / pipe replacement / leak detection reduce wastage water is delivered directly to the plant roots / soil around each plant less evaporation loss from spray droplets and from wetting the whole soil surface; less runoff", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HYD-08\" data-qid=\"HYD-08\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/hydro-management.html\">Water treatment, conservation &amp; new sources</a> &middot; spec 3.2.2.5 &middot; Genn pp. 124-126 &middot; HYD-08</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the effect of installing water meters on the mean daily water use of households in a UK town.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th></th><th>Unmetered households (fixed charge)</th><th>Metered households</th></tr><tr><td>Mean water use / dm<sup>3</sup> per person per day</td><td>150</td><td>125</td></tr><tr><td>Percentage with low-flush toilets</td><td>35</td><td>58</td></tr><tr><td>Percentage using a garden hosepipe weekly in summer</td><td>44</td><td>20</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Calculate the percentage reduction in water use per person in metered households.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain why metering reduces water use.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Describe <b>three</b> ways in which domestic water use can be reduced other than metering.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Explain why about 20% of the water intended for public supply in the UK never reaches consumers, and how this could be reduced.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.5</span>Explain how drip irrigation reduces the water needed to grow a crop compared with spray irrigation.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(150 - 125) / 150 x 100 = 16.7% (accept 17%)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A fixed fee gives no incentive to save water because the bill is the same however much is used</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Metering matches the charge to the volume used so households save money by using less (fewer hosepipes, low-flow appliances)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Low-water appliances: dual-flush toilets, low-flow shower heads, efficient washing machines / dishwashers, tap timers</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Greywater re-use: bath / shower water used to flush toilets or water gardens</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Rainwater collection / water butts for garden use</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Xeriscaping: planting drought-adapted species that need no watering</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Repairing leaks / dripping taps</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Old infrastructure / pipes in poor condition leak</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Repair and good maintenance / pipe replacement / leak detection reduce wastage</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Water is delivered directly to the plant roots / soil around each plant</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Less evaporation loss from spray droplets and from wetting the whole soil surface; less runoff</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 124-126; AQA spec 3.2.2.5.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HYD-09", "sub": "hydro-management", "subname": "Water treatment, conservation & new sources", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.2.2.4", "pages": "126-128", "url": "subtopic/hydro-management.html#HYD-09", "snippet": "Figure 1 lists the stages in a water treatment works supplying drinking water from a lowland river.", "text": "figure 1 lists the stages in a water treatment works supplying drinking water from a lowland river. explain why clay particles do not settle out in the sedimentation stage, and describe how they are removed in stage 4. explain the purpose of aeration (stage 3). explain why ozone or uv light may be used for sterilisation instead of chlorine when water comes from peaty upland sources. describe how each of the following contaminants is removed from water. clay particles are very small and carry electrostatic charges on their surfaces so they repel each other and stay suspended flocculants (aluminium sulfate / alum or polyelectrolytes) are added and mixed to neutralise the charges the particles clump together (flocs) and settle in the clarifier tank increases dissolved oxygen and removes hydrogen sulfide (bad-egg smell) from anaerobic sources oxidises some dissolved metals (eg iron) so they become insoluble and can be removed; improves taste peaty water contains organic matter which reacts with chlorine to produce toxic substances (eg trihalomethanes) ozone / uv kill pathogens without forming these by-products (though chlorine / chloramine keeps water sterile during distribution) ion exchange: toxic ions adsorb onto polymer resin beads or zeolite (also used to soften water by removing calcium / magnesium) reverse osmosis / distillation (desalination) - expensive, energy-intensive activated carbon: organic molecules causing odour / taste and pesticides adsorb onto the high-surface-area carbon particles", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HYD-09\" data-qid=\"HYD-09\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/hydro-management.html\">Water treatment, conservation &amp; new sources</a> &middot; spec 3.2.2.4 &middot; Genn pp. 126-128 &middot; HYD-09</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 lists the stages in a water treatment works supplying drinking water from a lowland river.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Process</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Screens</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Sedimentation</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Aeration</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Flocculation and clarification</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Filtration through sand and gravel</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Activated carbon</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Sterilisation</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>pH adjustment</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Explain why clay particles do not settle out in the sedimentation stage, and describe how they are removed in stage 4.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain the purpose of aeration (stage 3).\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain why ozone or UV light may be used for sterilisation instead of chlorine when water comes from peaty upland sources.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Describe how each of the following contaminants is removed from water.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Clay particles are very small and carry electrostatic charges on their surfaces so they repel each other and stay suspended</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Flocculants (aluminium sulfate / alum or polyelectrolytes) are added and mixed to neutralise the charges</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The particles clump together (flocs) and settle in the clarifier tank</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Increases dissolved oxygen and removes hydrogen sulfide (bad-egg smell) from anaerobic sources</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Oxidises some dissolved metals (eg iron) so they become insoluble and can be removed; improves taste</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Peaty water contains organic matter which reacts with chlorine to produce toxic substances (eg trihalomethanes)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ozone / UV kill pathogens without forming these by-products (though chlorine / chloramine keeps water sterile during distribution)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ion exchange: toxic ions adsorb onto polymer resin beads or zeolite (also used to soften water by removing calcium / magnesium)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reverse osmosis / distillation (desalination) - expensive, energy-intensive</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Activated carbon: organic molecules causing odour / taste and pesticides adsorb onto the high-surface-area carbon particles</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 126-128; AQA spec 3.2.2.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HYD-10", "sub": "hydro-management", "subname": "Water treatment, conservation & new sources", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.2.2.4-3.2.2.5", "pages": "122-128", "url": "subtopic/hydro-management.html#HYD-10", "snippet": "Which of the following water sources is usually the most convenient for large communities? Tick one box.", "text": " which of the following water sources is usually the most convenient for large communities? tick one box. explain why the quality requirements for water differ between uses, using two named uses. explain why inter-basin transfers may be used and give one disadvantage. b - rivers are accessible over a large area with few natural contaminants potable / public supply: no pathogens, low toxins, good appearance / taste / smell (public health) industrial boiler / cooling water: soft water with low calcium so mineral deposits do not block pipes or reduce heat exchange; spray irrigation: low turbidity to avoid blocked pipes and low heavy metals canals / pipes move water from areas with a surplus to areas with a shortage (eg wales to england, china's yangtze transfers) disadvantage: high cost / energy for pumping; ecological impacts on the donor river; transfer of pollutants or invasive species; political conflict", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HYD-10\" data-qid=\"HYD-10\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/hydro-management.html\">Water treatment, conservation &amp; new sources</a> &middot; spec 3.2.2.4-3.2.2.5 &middot; Genn pp. 122-128 &middot; HYD-10</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Which of the following water sources is usually the most convenient for large communities? Tick one box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; Desalinated seawater</li><li>B&nbsp; River water</li><li>C&nbsp; Rainwater catchment</li><li>D&nbsp; Newly discovered aquifers</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain why the quality requirements for water differ between uses, using <b>two</b> named uses.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain why inter-basin transfers may be used and give <b>one</b> disadvantage.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b></b>&nbsp; B - rivers are accessible over a large area with few natural contaminants</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Potable / public supply: no pathogens, low toxins, good appearance / taste / smell (public health)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Industrial boiler / cooling water: soft water with low calcium so mineral deposits do not block pipes or reduce heat exchange; spray irrigation: low turbidity to avoid blocked pipes and low heavy metals</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Canals / pipes move water from areas with a surplus to areas with a shortage (eg Wales to England, China's Yangtze transfers)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Disadvantage: high cost / energy for pumping; ecological impacts on the donor river; transfer of pollutants or invasive species; political conflict</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 122-128; AQA spec 3.2.2.4-3.2.2.5.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HYD-11", "sub": "hydro-management", "subname": "Water treatment, conservation & new sources", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.2.2.5", "pages": "122-123", "url": "subtopic/hydro-management.html#HYD-11", "snippet": "Which of the following is a feature of a river that affects its usefulness as a water source? Tick one box.", "text": " which of the following is a feature of a river that affects its usefulness as a water source? tick one box. explain why rainwater collection is increasingly important in cities such as those in india. explain why an estuary barrage has fewer land-use conflicts than a reservoir on land but still causes environmental problems. its total annual discharge public supplies cannot match consumer demand / the water is often cleaner than rivers it also reduces urban flooding after heavy rain; surplus can be used for aquifer recharge the reservoir is created in the estuary so no farmland / settlements need to be flooded but it changes important inter-tidal habitats, obstructs shipping and can be polluted by activities anywhere in the river's catchment", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HYD-11\" data-qid=\"HYD-11\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/hydro-management.html\">Water treatment, conservation &amp; new sources</a> &middot; spec 3.2.2.5 &middot; Genn pp. 122-123 &middot; HYD-11</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Which of the following is a feature of a river that affects its usefulness as a water source? Tick <b>one</b> box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; Its total annual discharge</li><li>B&nbsp; The albedo of its surface</li><li>C&nbsp; Its salinity</li><li>D&nbsp; The age of its bedrock</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain why rainwater collection is increasingly important in cities such as those in India.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain why an estuary barrage has fewer land-use conflicts than a reservoir on land but still causes environmental problems.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b>A</b>&nbsp; Its total annual discharge</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Public supplies cannot match consumer demand / the water is often cleaner than rivers</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It also reduces urban flooding after heavy rain; surplus can be used for aquifer recharge</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The reservoir is created in the estuary so no farmland / settlements need to be flooded</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>But it changes important inter-tidal habitats, obstructs shipping and can be polluted by activities anywhere in the river's catchment</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 122-123; AQA spec 3.2.2.5.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HYD-12", "sub": "hydro-management", "subname": "Water treatment, conservation & new sources", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.2.2.4", "pages": "126-127", "url": "subtopic/hydro-management.html#HYD-12", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the water quality required for four different uses.", "text": "table 1 shows the water quality required for four different uses. complete table 1 by giving the quality requirement for each water use and the reason for it. explain why aeration is used in the treatment of water from anaerobic sources. explain how flocculation removes clay particles that sedimentation cannot. give two methods used to sterilise treated water. potable: no pathogens, toxins at acceptable levels, looks / smells / tastes good - public health, consumer complaints spray irrigation: low turbidity, low toxins such as heavy metals - blockage of pipes, contamination of food condenser cooling: no gross solids - pipe blockage boiler water: no dissolved minerals / soft water - mineral deposits reduce heat exchange and block pipes raises dissolved oxygen and removes hydrogen sulfide (bad-egg smell) from decay of organic matter some dissolved toxic / bad-tasting metals become insoluble when oxidised and can be removed clay particles carry electrostatic charges that make them repel each other so they stay suspended flocculants such as aluminium sulfate (alum) or polyelectrolytes neutralise the charges so the particles clump and settle in the clarifier chlorine ozone ultraviolet light", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HYD-12\" data-qid=\"HYD-12\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/hydro-management.html\">Water treatment, conservation &amp; new sources</a> &middot; spec 3.2.2.4 &middot; Genn pp. 126-127 &middot; HYD-12</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the water quality required for four different uses.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Complete Table 1 by giving the quality requirement for each water use and the reason for it.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Water use</th><th>Quality requirement</th><th>Reason</th></tr><tr><td>Potable (public) supply</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Spray irrigation</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Power station condenser cooling</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Industrial boiler water</td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain why aeration is used in the treatment of water from anaerobic sources.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain how flocculation removes clay particles that sedimentation cannot.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Give <b>two</b> methods used to sterilise treated water.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Potable: no pathogens, toxins at acceptable levels, looks / smells / tastes good - public health, consumer complaints</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Spray irrigation: low turbidity, low toxins such as heavy metals - blockage of pipes, contamination of food</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Condenser cooling: no gross solids - pipe blockage</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Boiler water: no dissolved minerals / soft water - mineral deposits reduce heat exchange and block pipes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Raises dissolved oxygen and removes hydrogen sulfide (bad-egg smell) from decay of organic matter</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Some dissolved toxic / bad-tasting metals become insoluble when oxidised and can be removed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Clay particles carry electrostatic charges that make them repel each other so they stay suspended</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Flocculants such as aluminium sulfate (alum) or polyelectrolytes neutralise the charges so the particles clump and settle in the clarifier</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Chlorine</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ozone</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ultraviolet light</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 126-127; AQA spec 3.2.2.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HYD-13", "sub": "hydro-management", "subname": "Water treatment, conservation & new sources", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 15, "flags": ["calc", "data", "ext"], "spec": "3.2.2.4-3.2.2.5", "pages": "122-127", "url": "subtopic/hydro-management.html#HYD-13", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the river level below a reservoir site during one year, before and after a river-regulation reservoir was built.", "text": "figure 1 shows the river level below a reservoir site during one year, before and after a river-regulation reservoir was built. use figure 1 to calculate the reduction in the annual range of river level produced by the reservoir. explain how the reservoir produces the changes shown in figure 1. explain why human activities in the catchment may have increased the extremes in river flow before the reservoir was built. discuss the factors that should be considered when selecting a site for a new reservoir, and evaluate the alternative strategies for increasing the sustainability of water supplies. no reservoir range = 3.9 - 0.7 = 3.2 m; with reservoir = 2.7 - 1.7 = 1.0 m reduction = 2.2 m after heavy rainfall surplus water is stored, reducing peak flows / flooding downstream during dry periods the dam is opened to release water and maintain river levels deforestation / urbanisation reduce infiltration and interception so runoff reaches the river faster after rain and less water is stored to maintain flow in dry periods site factors: topography - narrow exit from a large deep basin so a small dam holds a large volume; impermeable, strong bedrock without faults or seismic activity; large catchment with regular high rainfall and low evaporation; catchment land use with low pollution risk (pesticides, industry) and low soil erosion to avoid sedimentation; forest / vegetation in the flooded area decaying anaerobically releasing methane; access for workers and materials and distance to demand; value of the land lost (urban, conservation, agricultural) - loss-benefit analysis differs between countries alternatives for supply: rainwater collection, rivers, estuary barrages, desalination (energy-intensive so only where freshwater is inadequate), unexploited aquifers (n africa, kenya), inter-basin transfers (china, australia, wales-england), artificial aquifer recharge using surplus wet-season water, river-regulation reservoirs, afforestation to even out flow conservation strategies: low-water appliances, dual-flush toilets, xeriscaping, drip irrigation, grey-water re-use, leak repair (about 20% lost in the uk), metering treatment / pollution control allowing re-use of contaminated sources: sewage treatment, buffer strips, mine drainage control evaluation: reservoirs give large reliable supplies and flood control but flood land, cause habitat loss and methane; conservation is cheap and quick but limited by behaviour; transfers create conflicts between regions; desalination has high energy / carbon cost; combination needed as demand grows justified conclusion", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HYD-13\" data-qid=\"HYD-13\" data-marks=\"15\" data-flags=\"calc data ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">15 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/hydro-management.html\">Water treatment, conservation &amp; new sources</a> &middot; spec 3.2.2.4-3.2.2.5 &middot; Genn pp. 122-127 &middot; HYD-13</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the river level below a reservoir site during one year, before and after a river-regulation reservoir was built.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,70.0 103.6,53.3 147.3,92.2 190.9,136.7 234.5,181.1 278.2,214.4 321.8,231.1 365.5,225.6 409.1,192.2 452.7,125.6 496.4,75.6 540.0,58.9\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"103.6\" cy=\"53.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"147.3\" cy=\"92.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"190.9\" cy=\"136.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"234.5\" cy=\"181.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"278.2\" cy=\"214.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"321.8\" cy=\"231.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"365.5\" cy=\"225.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"409.1\" cy=\"192.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"452.7\" cy=\"125.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"496.4\" cy=\"75.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"58.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<polyline points=\"60.0,125.6 103.6,120.0 147.3,131.1 190.9,142.2 234.5,158.9 278.2,170.0 321.8,175.6 365.5,175.6 409.1,164.4 452.7,147.8 496.4,131.1 540.0,125.6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2f5f73\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"125.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"103.6\" cy=\"120.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"147.3\" cy=\"131.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"190.9\" cy=\"142.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"234.5\" cy=\"158.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"278.2\" cy=\"170.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"321.8\" cy=\"175.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"365.5\" cy=\"175.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"409.1\" cy=\"164.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"452.7\" cy=\"147.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"496.4\" cy=\"131.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"125.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0.9</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">5.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">1.8</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">7.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">2.7</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">9.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">3.6</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">12</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">4.5</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Month</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">River level / m</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"24\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#22302a\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"33\">No reservoir</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"38\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"47\">With reservoir</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Use Figure 1 to calculate the reduction in the annual range of river level produced by the reservoir.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Explain how the reservoir produces the changes shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Explain why human activities in the catchment may have increased the extremes in river flow before the reservoir was built.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>Discuss the factors that should be considered when selecting a site for a new reservoir, and evaluate the alternative strategies for increasing the sustainability of water supplies.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No reservoir range = 3.9 - 0.7 = 3.2 m; with reservoir = 2.7 - 1.7 = 1.0 m</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduction = 2.2 m</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>After heavy rainfall surplus water is stored, reducing peak flows / flooding downstream</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>During dry periods the dam is opened to release water and maintain river levels</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Deforestation / urbanisation reduce infiltration and interception</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So runoff reaches the river faster after rain and less water is stored to maintain flow in dry periods</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Site factors: topography - narrow exit from a large deep basin so a small dam holds a large volume; impermeable, strong bedrock without faults or seismic activity; large catchment with regular high rainfall and low evaporation; catchment land use with low pollution risk (pesticides, industry) and low soil erosion to avoid sedimentation; forest / vegetation in the flooded area decaying anaerobically releasing methane; access for workers and materials and distance to demand; value of the land lost (urban, conservation, agricultural) - loss-benefit analysis differs between countries</li><li>Alternatives for supply: rainwater collection, rivers, estuary barrages, desalination (energy-intensive so only where freshwater is inadequate), unexploited aquifers (N Africa, Kenya), inter-basin transfers (China, Australia, Wales-England), artificial aquifer recharge using surplus wet-season water, river-regulation reservoirs, afforestation to even out flow</li><li>Conservation strategies: low-water appliances, dual-flush toilets, xeriscaping, drip irrigation, grey-water re-use, leak repair (about 20% lost in the UK), metering</li><li>Treatment / pollution control allowing re-use of contaminated sources: sewage treatment, buffer strips, mine drainage control</li><li>Evaluation: reservoirs give large reliable supplies and flood control but flood land, cause habitat loss and methane; conservation is cheap and quick but limited by behaviour; transfers create conflicts between regions; desalination has high energy / carbon cost; combination needed as demand grows</li><li>Justified conclusion</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 122-127; AQA spec 3.2.2.4-3.2.2.5.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "REN-01", "sub": "renewables", "subname": "Renewable energy technologies", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "199-203", "url": "subtopic/renewables.html#REN-01", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the efficiency and cost of four types of solar photovoltaic (PV) cell.", "text": "table 1 shows the efficiency and cost of four types of solar photovoltaic (pv) cell. describe how a photovoltaic cell generates electricity. explain how anti-reflective surfaces and multi-junction cells increase efficiency. use table 1 to suggest which cell type would be chosen for a large solar farm on cheap desert land and which for a satellite. justify your choices. explain why solar pv output is intermittent and unpredictable. photons of light absorbed by the (n-type silicon) upper layer dislodge electrons, making it negatively charged electrons flow through an external conductor / circuit to the relatively positive lower layer; the current powers appliances smooth cells reflect about 30% of the light; grooved / textured surfaces (mimicking moth eyes) reflect light into the cell rather than away multi-junction cells have several layers of different materials, each absorbing different wavelengths so a greater proportion of the available light is absorbed and converted to electricity solar farm: polycrystalline / thin film - lowest cost per unit electricity where land area is not limiting (efficiency per m2 matters less) satellite: multi-junction - highest efficiency where mass / area is critical and cost is less important cost per watt = cost / efficiency: thin film 0.125, poly 0.125, mono 0.136, multi-junction 0.625 per unit intermittent: no output at night and low in winter (daily and seasonal cycles) - predictable unpredictable: cloud cover, dust and smoke reduce intensity and scatter light and cannot be forecast accurately", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"REN-01\" data-qid=\"REN-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/renewables.html\">Renewable energy technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 199-203 &middot; REN-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the efficiency and cost of four types of solar photovoltaic (PV) cell.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>PV cell type</th><th>Efficiency / %</th><th>Relative cost per m<sup>2</sup></th></tr><tr><td>Amorphous silicon (thin film)</td><td>8</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Polycrystalline silicon</td><td>16</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Monocrystalline silicon with anti-reflective surface</td><td>22</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Multi-junction cell</td><td>40</td><td>25</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Describe how a photovoltaic cell generates electricity.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain how anti-reflective surfaces and multi-junction cells increase efficiency.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Use Table 1 to suggest which cell type would be chosen for a large solar farm on cheap desert land and which for a satellite. Justify your choices.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Explain why solar PV output is intermittent <b>and</b> unpredictable.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Photons of light absorbed by the (n-type silicon) upper layer dislodge electrons, making it negatively charged</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Electrons flow through an external conductor / circuit to the relatively positive lower layer; the current powers appliances</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Smooth cells reflect about 30% of the light; grooved / textured surfaces (mimicking moth eyes) reflect light into the cell rather than away</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Multi-junction cells have several layers of different materials, each absorbing different wavelengths</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So a greater proportion of the available light is absorbed and converted to electricity</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Solar farm: polycrystalline / thin film - lowest cost per unit electricity where land area is not limiting (efficiency per m<sup>2</sup> matters less)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Satellite: multi-junction - highest efficiency where mass / area is critical and cost is less important</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cost per watt = cost / efficiency: thin film 0.125, poly 0.125, mono 0.136, multi-junction 0.625 per unit</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Intermittent: no output at night and low in winter (daily and seasonal cycles) - predictable</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Unpredictable: cloud cover, dust and smoke reduce intensity and scatter light and cannot be forecast accurately</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 199-203; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "REN-02", "sub": "renewables", "subname": "Renewable energy technologies", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "200-202", "url": "subtopic/renewables.html#REN-02", "snippet": "Explain how a ground-source heat pump can deliver more heat energy to a building than the electrical energy used to run it.", "text": " explain how a ground-source heat pump can deliver more heat energy to a building than the electrical energy used to run it. explain how concentrating solar power (csp) with thermal storage overcomes the intermittency of solar power. explain why csp can only be used in areas with little cloud, whereas pv can be used anywhere. describe two environmental impacts of solar pv. a fluid in a buried ground loop absorbs low-temperature heat from the ground; at the expansion valve the fluid boils at low pressure absorbing this heat a compressor raises the pressure so the gas condenses, releasing heat at a higher temperature inside the building the electricity only moves heat rather than creating it, so up to four times as much heat is delivered as electricity used (less if the source is colder) parabolic reflectors focus sunlight onto tubes of oil, increasing the energy density and reaching high temperatures the heat is transferred to molten salt (potassium nitrate) stored in large insulated tanks at up to 550 °c the stored heat boils water to drive steam turbines whenever electricity is required, including at night reflectors can only focus parallel rays of direct sunlight; cloud, dust and smoke scatter light so it cannot be concentrated pv cells convert diffuse as well as direct light, though output is lower manufacture: extraction / processing of silicon, metals and plastics; toxic wastes (silicon tetrachloride, cadmium) land take by large solar farms competing with other uses / affecting desert flora and fauna water for cleaning panels in arid areas disposal of old panels", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"REN-02\" data-qid=\"REN-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/renewables.html\">Renewable energy technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 200-202 &middot; REN-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain how a ground-source heat pump can deliver more heat energy to a building than the electrical energy used to run it.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain how Concentrating Solar Power (CSP) with thermal storage overcomes the intermittency of solar power.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain why CSP can only be used in areas with little cloud, whereas PV can be used anywhere.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Describe <b>two</b> environmental impacts of solar PV.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A fluid in a buried ground loop absorbs low-temperature heat from the ground; at the expansion valve the fluid boils at low pressure absorbing this heat</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A compressor raises the pressure so the gas condenses, releasing heat at a higher temperature inside the building</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The electricity only moves heat rather than creating it, so up to four times as much heat is delivered as electricity used (less if the source is colder)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Parabolic reflectors focus sunlight onto tubes of oil, increasing the energy density and reaching high temperatures</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The heat is transferred to molten salt (potassium nitrate) stored in large insulated tanks at up to 550 &deg;C</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The stored heat boils water to drive steam turbines whenever electricity is required, including at night</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reflectors can only focus parallel rays of direct sunlight; cloud, dust and smoke scatter light so it cannot be concentrated</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>PV cells convert diffuse as well as direct light, though output is lower</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Manufacture: extraction / processing of silicon, metals and plastics; toxic wastes (silicon tetrachloride, cadmium)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Land take by large solar farms competing with other uses / affecting desert flora and fauna</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Water for cleaning panels in arid areas</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Disposal of old panels</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 200-202; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "REN-03", "sub": "renewables", "subname": "Renewable energy technologies", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc"], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "203-205", "url": "subtopic/renewables.html#REN-03", "snippet": "A rural community in Pakistan is considering a low-head helical (Archimedes screw) turbine on a river rather than joining a high-head dam scheme.", "text": "a rural community in pakistan is considering a low-head helical (archimedes screw) turbine on a river rather than joining a high-head dam scheme. explain why low-head turbines are more suitable than high-head hep schemes for many rural areas. suggest one reason why such schemes may not be installed even at suitable sites. the maximum flow rate at the site is 6.5 m3 s-1 giving an output of 100 kw at 90% turbine efficiency. at 10% of maximum flow the efficiency falls to 40%. calculate the output at 10% of maximum flow. outline two ways a high-head reservoir can affect the river downstream. no large dam / reservoir needed so no flooding of farmland, homes or habitats and no barrier across the whole river (run-of-river / micro-hydro diverts part of the flow) suitable for rivers with a small drop in height and lower flow rates that are common in lowland rural areas small scale matches local demand; electricity used locally without long transmission lines / grid connection low construction cost and simple technology maintained by the community helical turbines tolerate high turbidity / debris without damage and fish pass through unharmed; kaplan turbines with adjustable blades cope with variable flow lack of capital / finance, technical knowledge or grid access; seasonal drying of rivers; conflicts with irrigation water; planning / land ownership output is proportional to flow x efficiency: 100 x 0.10 x (40 / 90) = 4.4 kw (accept 4.4-4.5) constant regulated flow removes seasonal extremes: dry-season sandbanks used by nesting birds / turtles lost; high flows that clear gravel spawning beds lost; floodplain no longer flooded sudden flow increases for peak generation raise turbidity, wash away species, reduce light for photosynthesis sediment trapped so less nutrient replenishment downstream; barrier to migrating fish (salmon, sturgeon) and isolation of populations", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"REN-03\" data-qid=\"REN-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/renewables.html\">Renewable energy technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 203-205 &middot; REN-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A rural community in Pakistan is considering a low-head helical (Archimedes screw) turbine on a river rather than joining a high-head dam scheme.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Explain why low-head turbines are more suitable than high-head HEP schemes for many rural areas.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Suggest <b>one</b> reason why such schemes may not be installed even at suitable sites.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>The maximum flow rate at the site is 6.5 m<sup>3</sup> s<sup>-1</sup> giving an output of 100 kW at 90% turbine efficiency. At 10% of maximum flow the efficiency falls to 40%. Calculate the output at 10% of maximum flow.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Outline <b>two</b> ways a high-head reservoir can affect the river downstream.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No large dam / reservoir needed so no flooding of farmland, homes or habitats and no barrier across the whole river (run-of-river / micro-hydro diverts part of the flow)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Suitable for rivers with a small drop in height and lower flow rates that are common in lowland rural areas</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Small scale matches local demand; electricity used locally without long transmission lines / grid connection</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Low construction cost and simple technology maintained by the community</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Helical turbines tolerate high turbidity / debris without damage and fish pass through unharmed; Kaplan turbines with adjustable blades cope with variable flow</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lack of capital / finance, technical knowledge or grid access; seasonal drying of rivers; conflicts with irrigation water; planning / land ownership</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Output is proportional to flow x efficiency: 100 x 0.10 x (40 / 90)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 4.4 kW (accept 4.4-4.5)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Constant regulated flow removes seasonal extremes: dry-season sandbanks used by nesting birds / turtles lost; high flows that clear gravel spawning beds lost; floodplain no longer flooded</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sudden flow increases for peak generation raise turbidity, wash away species, reduce light for photosynthesis</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sediment trapped so less nutrient replenishment downstream; barrier to migrating fish (salmon, sturgeon) and isolation of populations</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 203-205; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "REN-04", "sub": "renewables", "subname": "Renewable energy technologies", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "206-209", "url": "subtopic/renewables.html#REN-04", "snippet": "The kinetic energy available to a wind turbine is proportional to the cube of the wind velocity. Table 1 shows mean wind velocities at four possible wind farm sites.", "text": "the kinetic energy available to a wind turbine is proportional to the cube of the wind velocity. table 1 shows mean wind velocities at four possible wind farm sites. calculate how many times more kinetic energy is available at site c than at site d. use table 1 to evaluate the four sites for a large wind farm. give two advantages of vertical axis wind turbines (vawts) over horizontal axis turbines (hawts). explain how direct-drive turbines improve on turbines with a gearbox. 103 / 63 = 1000 / 216 = 4.6 times (accept 4.63) c has the highest velocity (most energy, and energy rises with the cube of velocity), strong reliable winds over water, few land-use conflicts, but long expensive cable and offshore installation / maintenance costs a is windy but on a migration route (bird strikes) and peat bog (foundations / tracks damage hydrology; carbon release); far from grid; scenic objections b is close to the grid with good wind but near a village (noise, visual objection, shadow flicker) - possible with careful siting d is cheap to connect and land can still be farmed between turbines, but low velocity gives little energy judgement, eg c or b; turbines spaced 3-5 blade diameters apart to avoid wind shadow driven by wind from any direction so no motor to turn them; suitable for turbulent urban sites generator can be at ground level so a lighter tower quieter operate at lower wind velocities blades more visible so fewer bird deaths no gearbox: fewer breakdowns, less friction so start generating at lower wind speeds, quieter, lighter nacelle (dc converted to ac by an inverter)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"REN-04\" data-qid=\"REN-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/renewables.html\">Renewable energy technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 206-209 &middot; REN-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>The kinetic energy available to a wind turbine is proportional to the cube of the wind velocity. Table 1 shows mean wind velocities at four possible wind farm sites.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Site</th><th>Mean wind velocity / m s<sup>-1</sup></th><th>Distance to grid / km</th><th>Other features</th></tr><tr><td>A: upland ridge</td><td>9</td><td>12</td><td>Migrating bird route; peat bog</td></tr><tr><td>B: coastal farmland</td><td>8</td><td>3</td><td>Near village</td></tr><tr><td>C: offshore shallow sea</td><td>10</td><td>15</td><td>Sandbanks</td></tr><tr><td>D: inland plain</td><td>6</td><td>2</td><td>Arable farmland</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Calculate how many times more kinetic energy is available at site C than at site D.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Use Table 1 to evaluate the four sites for a large wind farm.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Give <b>two</b> advantages of vertical axis wind turbines (VAWTs) over horizontal axis turbines (HAWTs).\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Explain how direct-drive turbines improve on turbines with a gearbox.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>10<sup>3</sup> / 6<sup>3</sup> = 1000 / 216</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 4.6 times (accept 4.63)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>C has the highest velocity (most energy, and energy rises with the cube of velocity), strong reliable winds over water, few land-use conflicts, but long expensive cable and offshore installation / maintenance costs</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A is windy but on a migration route (bird strikes) and peat bog (foundations / tracks damage hydrology; carbon release); far from grid; scenic objections</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>B is close to the grid with good wind but near a village (noise, visual objection, shadow flicker) - possible with careful siting</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>D is cheap to connect and land can still be farmed between turbines, but low velocity gives little energy</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Judgement, eg C or B; turbines spaced 3-5 blade diameters apart to avoid wind shadow</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Driven by wind from any direction so no motor to turn them; suitable for turbulent urban sites</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Generator can be at ground level so a lighter tower</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Quieter</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Operate at lower wind velocities</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Blades more visible so fewer bird deaths</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No gearbox: fewer breakdowns, less friction so start generating at lower wind speeds, quieter, lighter nacelle (DC converted to AC by an inverter)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 206-209; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "REN-05", "sub": "renewables", "subname": "Renewable energy technologies", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "210-211, 214-217", "url": "subtopic/renewables.html#REN-05", "snippet": "Explain the locational requirements for harnessing wave power.", "text": " explain the locational requirements for harnessing wave power. describe how one named wave power device generates electricity. give two design problems that have delayed the development of wave power. compare the environmental impacts of tidal barrages, tidal lagoons and in-stream tidal turbines. high mean wind velocities that are consistent in strength and direction a long fetch (distance of open water) over which waves build up, eg atlantic coasts of the uk oscillating water column (eg islay limpet): waves force water up and down in a submerged chamber; the air forced in and out drives a turbine or point absorber (floating buoy moving relative to a fixed base turns a generator); surface attenuator (pelamis - hinged sections push pistons pumping fluid over a turbine); overtopping device (wave dragon - waves fill a reservoir above sea level which drains through turbines); oscillating wave surge converter (oyster) equipment must withstand storms and corrosion difficult to anchor off stormy coasts / in deep water expensive to transmit electricity from isolated coasts to consumers competition with established technologies barrage: huge quantities of construction materials; reduces tidal range so intertidal mudflats used by wading birds are lost; changes sedimentation (erosion near turbines, deposition elsewhere), lower turbidity; pollutants concentrate in the lagoon; blocks migratory fish and shipping lagoon: similar but smaller scale; only part of the estuary enclosed so the most sensitive areas and main channel can be avoided in-stream turbines: do not block the tidal flow so little impact on range or sediment; but noise affects cetaceans and many dispersed turbines could have a larger total impact; harness less energy", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"REN-05\" data-qid=\"REN-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/renewables.html\">Renewable energy technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 210-211, 214-217 &middot; REN-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Explain the locational requirements for harnessing wave power.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Describe how <b>one</b> named wave power device generates electricity.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Give <b>two</b> design problems that have delayed the development of wave power.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Compare the environmental impacts of tidal barrages, tidal lagoons and in-stream tidal turbines.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>High mean wind velocities that are consistent in strength and direction</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A long fetch (distance of open water) over which waves build up, eg Atlantic coasts of the UK</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Oscillating water column (eg Islay Limpet): waves force water up and down in a submerged chamber; the air forced in and out drives a turbine</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Or point absorber (floating buoy moving relative to a fixed base turns a generator); surface attenuator (Pelamis - hinged sections push pistons pumping fluid over a turbine); overtopping device (Wave Dragon - waves fill a reservoir above sea level which drains through turbines); oscillating wave surge converter (Oyster)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Equipment must withstand storms and corrosion</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Difficult to anchor off stormy coasts / in deep water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Expensive to transmit electricity from isolated coasts to consumers</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Competition with established technologies</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 4 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Barrage: huge quantities of construction materials; reduces tidal range so intertidal mudflats used by wading birds are lost; changes sedimentation (erosion near turbines, deposition elsewhere), lower turbidity; pollutants concentrate in the lagoon; blocks migratory fish and shipping</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Lagoon: similar but smaller scale; only part of the estuary enclosed so the most sensitive areas and main channel can be avoided</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>In-stream turbines: do not block the tidal flow so little impact on range or sediment; but noise affects cetaceans and many dispersed turbines could have a larger total impact; harness less energy</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 210-211, 214-217; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "REN-06", "sub": "renewables", "subname": "Renewable energy technologies", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "214-215", "url": "subtopic/renewables.html#REN-06", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the water level inside and outside a tidal barrage over one 12.5-hour tidal cycle and the periods when electricity is generated.", "text": "figure 1 shows the water level inside and outside a tidal barrage over one 12.5-hour tidal cycle and the periods when electricity is generated. use figure 1 to explain when electricity is generated by the barrage. explain why tidal power is intermittent but predictable. explain the difference between spring and neap tides and why this affects output. explain how a tidal lagoon can be used to generate electricity when there is no tidal flow. give two coastal features that make a site suitable for tidal power. when there is a difference in level between the sea and the basin: on the ebb (about 6-8 hours) water flows out through the turbines also possible on the flood when the sea is higher than the basin; no generation when levels are equal (slack water) two periods of slack water in each 12.5-hour cycle (twice in about 25 hours) give no flow; the timing does not match demand tides depend on the positions of the moon and sun which are known, so output can be forecast years ahead and alternative supplies planned spring tides: sun and moon aligned so their gravitational effects combine giving a large tidal range; neap: effects oppose giving a small range energy depends on the mass of water and height difference, so output is much greater at springs when demand is low, surplus electricity pumps water into the lagoon above sea level the stored water is released through the turbines later when demand is high (pumped storage) large tidal range (eg severn estuary) estuaries, bays or headlands / channels that focus the flow and increase velocity", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"REN-06\" data-qid=\"REN-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/renewables.html\">Renewable energy technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 214-215 &middot; REN-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the water level inside and outside a tidal barrage over one 12.5-hour tidal cycle and the periods when electricity is generated.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,270.0 117.6,145.0 175.2,70.0 232.8,145.0 300.0,270.0 367.2,145.0 424.8,70.0 482.4,145.0 540.0,270.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"117.6\" cy=\"145.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"175.2\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"232.8\" cy=\"145.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"367.2\" cy=\"145.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"424.8\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"482.4\" cy=\"145.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<polyline points=\"60.0,120.0 117.6,120.0 175.2,70.0 232.8,70.0 300.0,70.0 367.2,195.0 424.8,70.0 482.4,70.0 540.0,70.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2f5f73\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"120.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"117.6\" cy=\"120.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"175.2\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"232.8\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"367.2\" cy=\"195.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"424.8\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"482.4\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2.5</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">1</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">5</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">2</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">7.5</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">3</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">10</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">4</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">12.5</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">5</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Time / hours</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Water level / m</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"24\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#22302a\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"33\">Sea level</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"38\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"47\">Level behind barrage</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Use Figure 1 to explain when electricity is generated by the barrage.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain why tidal power is intermittent but predictable.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain the difference between spring and neap tides and why this affects output.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Explain how a tidal lagoon can be used to generate electricity when there is no tidal flow.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.5</span>Give <b>two</b> coastal features that make a site suitable for tidal power.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>When there is a difference in level between the sea and the basin: on the ebb (about 6-8 hours) water flows out through the turbines</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Also possible on the flood when the sea is higher than the basin; no generation when levels are equal (slack water)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Two periods of slack water in each 12.5-hour cycle (twice in about 25 hours) give no flow; the timing does not match demand</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Tides depend on the positions of the Moon and Sun which are known, so output can be forecast years ahead and alternative supplies planned</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Spring tides: Sun and Moon aligned so their gravitational effects combine giving a large tidal range; neap: effects oppose giving a small range</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Energy depends on the mass of water and height difference, so output is much greater at springs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>When demand is low, surplus electricity pumps water into the lagoon above sea level</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The stored water is released through the turbines later when demand is high (pumped storage)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Large tidal range (eg Severn Estuary)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Estuaries, bays or headlands / channels that focus the flow and increase velocity</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 214-215; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "REN-07", "sub": "renewables", "subname": "Renewable energy technologies", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "212-214", "url": "subtopic/renewables.html#REN-07", "snippet": "Explain three advantages of biofuels over other renewable energy resources.", "text": " explain three advantages of biofuels over other renewable energy resources. explain three disadvantages of biofuel crops. describe two new biofuel technologies. explain how new low-temperature turbine technology could allow geothermal electricity generation in the uk. supply rate of energy crops can be controlled and fuels stored so supply matches demand (not intermittent) liquid biofuels (alcohol, biodiesel) can replace petrol / diesel in vehicles carbon neutral in principle: co2 released equals co2 absorbed in photosynthesis energy density of alcohol / biodiesel nearly as high as fossil fuels; wastes (straw, sewage, landfill gas) are cheap large areas of farmland compete with food production or cause clearance of wildlife habitats (eg palm oil) intensive farming inputs (fertiliser, pesticide, machinery fuel) may release nearly as much co2 as using fossil fuels directly low energy density of straw, wood, miscanthus makes transport uneconomic - must be used locally supply from wastes limited to the amount produced hydrogen from algae: some algae deprived of sulfur produce hydrogen during photosynthesis anaerobic digestion extended to crop wastes and marine algae; small-scale digesters (eg one cow's manure supplying a family's cooking gas) biofuels from microorganisms (eg oil-producing microalgae) conventional geothermal steam turbines need rocks above 150 °c (recent volcanic activity), too deep to reach economically in the uk new turbines use fluids such as butane / pentane that boil at low temperatures, so water as cool as 60 °c from deep boreholes / hot dry rock systems can drive them", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"REN-07\" data-qid=\"REN-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/renewables.html\">Renewable energy technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 212-214 &middot; REN-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Explain <b>three</b> advantages of biofuels over other renewable energy resources.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Explain <b>three</b> disadvantages of biofuel crops.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Describe <b>two</b> new biofuel technologies.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>Explain how new low-temperature turbine technology could allow geothermal electricity generation in the UK.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Supply rate of energy crops can be controlled and fuels stored so supply matches demand (not intermittent)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Liquid biofuels (alcohol, biodiesel) can replace petrol / diesel in vehicles</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Carbon neutral in principle: CO2 released equals CO2 absorbed in photosynthesis</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Energy density of alcohol / biodiesel nearly as high as fossil fuels; wastes (straw, sewage, landfill gas) are cheap</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Large areas of farmland compete with food production or cause clearance of wildlife habitats (eg palm oil)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Intensive farming inputs (fertiliser, pesticide, machinery fuel) may release nearly as much CO2 as using fossil fuels directly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Low energy density of straw, wood, Miscanthus makes transport uneconomic - must be used locally</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Supply from wastes limited to the amount produced</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hydrogen from algae: some algae deprived of sulfur produce hydrogen during photosynthesis</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Anaerobic digestion extended to crop wastes and marine algae; small-scale digesters (eg one cow's manure supplying a family's cooking gas)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Biofuels from microorganisms (eg oil-producing microalgae)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Conventional geothermal steam turbines need rocks above 150 &deg;C (recent volcanic activity), too deep to reach economically in the UK</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>New turbines use fluids such as butane / pentane that boil at low temperatures, so water as cool as 60 &deg;C from deep boreholes / hot dry rock systems can drive them</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 212-214; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "REN-08", "sub": "renewables", "subname": "Renewable energy technologies", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "196-217", "url": "subtopic/renewables.html#REN-08", "snippet": "Evaluate the potential contribution of renewable energy resources to future energy supplies in a country such as the UK.", "text": " evaluate the potential contribution of renewable energy resources to future energy supplies in a country such as the uk. state the factor by which the kinetic energy of wind increases when wind velocity doubles. properties of renewables: intermittency, predictability, low energy density, storage, applicability, locational constraints, impacts, technology level, economics wind: large offshore resource in shallow seas, falling costs, hawt / vawt / direct drive developments; intermittent, needs storage and grid; land-use conflicts solar: pv costs falling rapidly, roof space, but low winter insolation in the uk; csp unsuitable; heat pumps and photothermal for heating hep: most large sites used; low-head, helical and micro-hydro extend use; pumped storage vital tidal: large range (severn, mersey), predictable; barrages costly with high impact, lagoons and in-stream turbines lower impact wave: long atlantic fetch, but technology immature biofuels: wastes, anaerobic digestion, energy crops vs food / habitat geothermal: limited to low-temperature schemes / heat pumps systems issues: storage (pumped hep, batteries, hydrogen, p2g, v2g), grid management, diversity of sources, energy conservation reducing demand, electrification of transport judgement: renewables could supply most electricity with storage and a diverse mix, but heat and transport are harder; transition needs investment and lifestyle change eight times (energy proportional to velocity cubed)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"REN-08\" data-qid=\"REN-08\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 8</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/renewables.html\">Renewable energy technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 196-217 &middot; REN-08</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.1</span>Evaluate the potential contribution of renewable energy resources to future energy supplies in a country such as the UK.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.2</span>State the factor by which the kinetic energy of wind increases when wind velocity doubles.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Properties of renewables: intermittency, predictability, low energy density, storage, applicability, locational constraints, impacts, technology level, economics</li><li>Wind: large offshore resource in shallow seas, falling costs, HAWT / VAWT / direct drive developments; intermittent, needs storage and grid; land-use conflicts</li><li>Solar: PV costs falling rapidly, roof space, but low winter insolation in the UK; CSP unsuitable; heat pumps and photothermal for heating</li><li>HEP: most large sites used; low-head, helical and micro-hydro extend use; pumped storage vital</li><li>Tidal: large range (Severn, Mersey), predictable; barrages costly with high impact, lagoons and in-stream turbines lower impact</li><li>Wave: long Atlantic fetch, but technology immature</li><li>Biofuels: wastes, anaerobic digestion, energy crops vs food / habitat</li><li>Geothermal: limited to low-temperature schemes / heat pumps</li><li>Systems issues: storage (pumped HEP, batteries, hydrogen, P2G, V2G), grid management, diversity of sources, energy conservation reducing demand, electrification of transport</li><li>Judgement: renewables could supply most electricity with storage and a diverse mix, but heat and transport are harder; transition needs investment and lifestyle change</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Eight times (energy proportional to velocity cubed)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 196-217; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "REN-09", "sub": "renewables", "subname": "Renewable energy technologies", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 15, "flags": ["calc", "data", "ext"], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "199-203", "url": "subtopic/renewables.html#REN-09", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the mean daily solar energy received on a horizontal surface at three locations.", "text": "table 1 shows the mean daily solar energy received on a horizontal surface at three locations. use table 1 to calculate how many times more solar energy is received in december in the sahara than in southern england. explain why northern norway receives more solar energy in june than in december. explain why concentrating solar power systems using parabolic reflectors only work where there is no cloud. explain why the low energy density of solar power is a problem for large-scale generation. evaluate the extent to which new technologies could overcome the problems of using solar power as a large-scale energy resource. 4.2 / 0.7 = 6 long summer days (almost 24 hours of daylight) so the total energy received is high, even though the sun is low in winter days are very short / the sun does not rise, so light levels are extremely low reflectors can only focus parallel rays of direct sunlight onto the absorber clouds, smoke and dust scatter the light so it cannot be focused and intensified very large areas of collectors are needed to harness significant amounts of energy (and the changing angle of incidence reduces it further) problems: intermittency (daily and seasonal cycles), unpredictable cloud cover, low energy density, changing angle of incidence, land requirements, water for cleaning in deserts, toxic wastes in manufacture (silicon tetrachloride, cadmium), efficiency loss at high temperature photothermal: evacuated tubes reduce conduction / convection losses; thermal stores (water, sand, concrete, molten salt) allow later use; passive solar architecture, brise soleil, light tubes; heat pumps deliver up to four times the energy used photovoltaic developments: multijunction cells absorbing more wavelengths, anti-reflective / moth-eye textured surfaces (smooth cells reflect 30%), pv-thermal hybrids cooling the cell, transparent pv in windows, heliostats keeping the optimum angle, self-cleaning nanohydrophobic surfaces concentrating solar power with molten-salt storage to 550 °c so steam turbines run when needed - overcomes intermittency integration: grid-connected solar farms, rooftop space avoiding land conflicts, batteries, pumped storage, links to energy storage and demand management evaluation: strong in sunny deserts / low latitudes, weaker in high latitudes in winter; cost falling; manufacturing impacts; no single technology solves all problems; justified conclusion", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"REN-09\" data-qid=\"REN-09\" data-marks=\"15\" data-flags=\"calc data ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 9</span><span class=\"meta\">15 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/renewables.html\">Renewable energy technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 199-203 &middot; REN-09</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the mean daily solar energy received on a horizontal surface at three locations.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Location</th><th>Latitude</th><th>Mean solar energy in June / kWh m<sup>-2</sup> day<sup>-1</sup></th><th>Mean solar energy in December / kWh m<sup>-2</sup> day<sup>-1</sup></th></tr><tr><td>Sahara desert</td><td>25&deg;N</td><td>7.8</td><td>4.2</td></tr><tr><td>Southern England</td><td>51&deg;N</td><td>5.0</td><td>0.7</td></tr><tr><td>Northern Norway</td><td>69&deg;N</td><td>4.6</td><td>0.0</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.1</span>Use Table 1 to calculate how many times more solar energy is received in December in the Sahara than in southern England.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.2</span>Explain why northern Norway receives more solar energy in June than in December.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.3</span>Explain why concentrating solar power systems using parabolic reflectors only work where there is no cloud.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.4</span>Explain why the low energy density of solar power is a problem for large-scale generation.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.5</span>Evaluate the extent to which new technologies could overcome the problems of using solar power as a large-scale energy resource.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>4.2 / 0.7 = 6</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Long summer days (almost 24 hours of daylight) so the total energy received is high, even though the Sun is low</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In winter days are very short / the Sun does not rise, so light levels are extremely low</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reflectors can only focus parallel rays of direct sunlight onto the absorber</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Clouds, smoke and dust scatter the light so it cannot be focused and intensified</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Very large areas of collectors are needed to harness significant amounts of energy (and the changing angle of incidence reduces it further)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Problems: intermittency (daily and seasonal cycles), unpredictable cloud cover, low energy density, changing angle of incidence, land requirements, water for cleaning in deserts, toxic wastes in manufacture (silicon tetrachloride, cadmium), efficiency loss at high temperature</li><li>Photothermal: evacuated tubes reduce conduction / convection losses; thermal stores (water, sand, concrete, molten salt) allow later use; passive solar architecture, brise soleil, light tubes; heat pumps deliver up to four times the energy used</li><li>Photovoltaic developments: multijunction cells absorbing more wavelengths, anti-reflective / moth-eye textured surfaces (smooth cells reflect 30%), PV-thermal hybrids cooling the cell, transparent PV in windows, heliostats keeping the optimum angle, self-cleaning nanohydrophobic surfaces</li><li>Concentrating solar power with molten-salt storage to 550 &deg;C so steam turbines run when needed - overcomes intermittency</li><li>Integration: grid-connected solar farms, rooftop space avoiding land conflicts, batteries, pumped storage, links to energy storage and demand management</li><li>Evaluation: strong in sunny deserts / low latitudes, weaker in high latitudes in winter; cost falling; manufacturing impacts; no single technology solves all problems; justified conclusion</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 199-203; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HM-01", "sub": "heavy-metals", "subname": "Heavy metals: lead, mercury", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.11", "pages": "275-276", "url": "subtopic/heavy-metals.html#HM-01", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the mean concentration of lead in the blood of children in a large city between 1976 and 2000, and the year in which lead additives in petrol began to be phased out.", "text": "figure 1 shows the mean concentration of lead in the blood of children in a large city between 1976 and 2000, and the year in which lead additives in petrol began to be phased out. calculate the percentage decrease in mean blood lead between 1976 and 2000. explain why tetraethyl lead was added to petrol and why this caused a public health threat. suggest two reasons, other than petrol additives, why blood lead concentrations continued to fall after 1990. explain how adding small amounts of phosphoric acid to a water supply reduces lead in drinking water. explain why discarded lead fishing weights and shotgun pellets can poison waterfowl. (15.0 - 2.2) / 15.0 x 100 = 85.3% (accept 85%) added as an anti-knock agent to smooth combustion and reduce engine wear lead particulates were released in exhaust gases into the air of cities and inhaled / deposited on soil and food lead is a neurotoxin that bioaccumulates; chronic exposure damages the nervous system / brain development in children replacement of lead water pipes / lead solder with copper / tin-based solder; phosphoric acid dosing of water supplies forming insoluble lead phosphate phasing out of lead-based paint / safer paint removal lead-free electrical solder restrictions on lead fishing weights / shotgun pellets industrial emission controls reacts with the lead in old pipes to form an insoluble layer of lead phosphate on the inside of the pipe this prevents lead dissolving into the water swans / ducks swallow them (mistaking them for snails / grit) and the lead is absorbed, causing lead poisoning", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HM-01\" data-qid=\"HM-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/heavy-metals.html\">Heavy metals: lead, mercury</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.11 &middot; Genn pp. 275-276 &middot; HM-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the mean concentration of lead in the blood of children in a large city between 1976 and 2000, and the year in which lead additives in petrol began to be phased out.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,39.7 140.0,78.0 220.0,131.8 300.0,177.9 380.0,208.6 460.0,223.9 540.0,236.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"39.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"140.0\" cy=\"78.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"220.0\" cy=\"131.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"177.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"380.0\" cy=\"208.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"460.0\" cy=\"223.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"236.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1976</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1980.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">3.26</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1985.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">6.51</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1990.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">9.77</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1995.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">13</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2000</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">16.3</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Year</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Blood lead / &amp;micro;g/dl</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Calculate the percentage decrease in mean blood lead between 1976 and 2000.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain why tetraethyl lead was added to petrol and why this caused a public health threat.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Suggest <b>two</b> reasons, other than petrol additives, why blood lead concentrations continued to fall after 1990.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Explain how adding small amounts of phosphoric acid to a water supply reduces lead in drinking water.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.5</span>Explain why discarded lead fishing weights and shotgun pellets can poison waterfowl.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(15.0 - 2.2) / 15.0 x 100</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 85.3% (accept 85%)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Added as an anti-knock agent to smooth combustion and reduce engine wear</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lead particulates were released in exhaust gases into the air of cities and inhaled / deposited on soil and food</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lead is a neurotoxin that bioaccumulates; chronic exposure damages the nervous system / brain development in children</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Replacement of lead water pipes / lead solder with copper / tin-based solder; phosphoric acid dosing of water supplies forming insoluble lead phosphate</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Phasing out of lead-based paint / safer paint removal</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lead-free electrical solder</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Restrictions on lead fishing weights / shotgun pellets</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Industrial emission controls</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reacts with the lead in old pipes to form an insoluble layer of lead phosphate on the inside of the pipe</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>This prevents lead dissolving into the water</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Swans / ducks swallow them (mistaking them for snails / grit) and the lead is absorbed, causing lead poisoning</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 275-276; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.11.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HM-02", "sub": "heavy-metals", "subname": "Heavy metals: lead, mercury", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.2.11", "pages": "276-277", "url": "subtopic/heavy-metals.html#HM-02", "snippet": "In the 1950s a chemical factory at Minamata, Japan, discharged inorganic mercury compounds into a shallow bay. Thousands of local people who ate fish and shellfish from the bay suf", "text": "in the 1950s a chemical factory at minamata, japan, discharged inorganic mercury compounds into a shallow bay. thousands of local people who ate fish and shellfish from the bay suffered severe neurological damage and many babies were born with birth abnormalities. explain how the low-toxicity inorganic mercury discharged into the bay came to cause such severe poisoning. explain why methyl mercury caused both neurological damage in adults and birth abnormalities. compare the toxicity of the three chemical forms of mercury. inorganic mercury settled into anaerobic sediments where anaerobic microbes converted it to organic (methyl) mercury methyl mercury is liposoluble so it is absorbed easily and stored in fat / passes through cell membranes it bioaccumulated in fish and shellfish and biomagnified along the food chain to reach much higher concentrations than in the water local people ate large amounts of fish (critical group) so received toxic doses liposoluble so it crosses the blood-brain barrier into the brain, inhibiting enzymes / nerve function (neurotoxin) it crosses the placenta and harms the developing unborn baby mercury is a teratogen, preventing normal gene expression / development elemental (liquid) mercury: not easily absorbed through skin or gut, though vapour is absorbed in the lungs inorganic compounds (eg mercury oxide): moderately well absorbed in the gut organic compounds (eg methyl mercury): easily absorbed through skin, gut and lungs; liposoluble so most toxic and bioaccumulates", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HM-02\" data-qid=\"HM-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/heavy-metals.html\">Heavy metals: lead, mercury</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.11 &middot; Genn pp. 276-277 &middot; HM-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>In the 1950s a chemical factory at Minamata, Japan, discharged inorganic mercury compounds into a shallow bay. Thousands of local people who ate fish and shellfish from the bay suffered severe neurological damage and many babies were born with birth abnormalities.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain how the low-toxicity inorganic mercury discharged into the bay came to cause such severe poisoning.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain why methyl mercury caused both neurological damage in adults and birth abnormalities.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Compare the toxicity of the three chemical forms of mercury.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Inorganic mercury settled into anaerobic sediments where anaerobic microbes converted it to organic (methyl) mercury</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Methyl mercury is liposoluble so it is absorbed easily and stored in fat / passes through cell membranes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It bioaccumulated in fish and shellfish and biomagnified along the food chain to reach much higher concentrations than in the water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Local people ate large amounts of fish (critical group) so received toxic doses</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Liposoluble so it crosses the blood-brain barrier into the brain, inhibiting enzymes / nerve function (neurotoxin)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It crosses the placenta and harms the developing unborn baby</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mercury is a teratogen, preventing normal gene expression / development</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Elemental (liquid) mercury: not easily absorbed through skin or gut, though vapour is absorbed in the lungs</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Inorganic compounds (eg mercury oxide): moderately well absorbed in the gut</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Organic compounds (eg methyl mercury): easily absorbed through skin, gut and lungs; liposoluble so most toxic and bioaccumulates</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 276-277; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.11.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HM-03", "sub": "heavy-metals", "subname": "Heavy metals: lead, mercury", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.2.11", "pages": "275-278", "url": "subtopic/heavy-metals.html#HM-03", "snippet": "Give three sources of mercury pollution.", "text": " give three sources of mercury pollution. describe three methods used to control mercury pollution. explain why heavy metal wastes are stored under alkaline conditions. state what is meant by synergism and give an example involving a heavy metal. disposal of items containing mercury: batteries, thermometers, fluorescent lights chemical plants producing chlorine using mercury electrodes combustion of coal gold mining (amalgamation) replacement of mercury thermometers with alcohol / electronic thermometers (discontinued uses) removal from effluents by reverse osmosis / activated carbon filters / ion exchange filters disposal / storage of wastes at high ph to reduce solubility encapsulation of mercury-containing hazardous waste in cement most heavy metals are more soluble at low ph / less soluble at high ph so at high ph they remain insoluble / immobile and cannot be leached into water or absorbed by organisms the effects of two pollutants interact to produce a different (greater) effect than either alone cadmium and zinc; ozone and sulfur dioxide", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HM-03\" data-qid=\"HM-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/heavy-metals.html\">Heavy metals: lead, mercury</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.11 &middot; Genn pp. 275-278 &middot; HM-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Give <b>three</b> sources of mercury pollution.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Describe <b>three</b> methods used to control mercury pollution.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain why heavy metal wastes are stored under alkaline conditions.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>State what is meant by synergism and give an example involving a heavy metal.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Disposal of items containing mercury: batteries, thermometers, fluorescent lights</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Chemical plants producing chlorine using mercury electrodes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Combustion of coal</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Gold mining (amalgamation)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Replacement of mercury thermometers with alcohol / electronic thermometers (discontinued uses)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Removal from effluents by reverse osmosis / activated carbon filters / ion exchange filters</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Disposal / storage of wastes at high pH to reduce solubility</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Encapsulation of mercury-containing hazardous waste in cement</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Most heavy metals are more soluble at low pH / less soluble at high pH</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So at high pH they remain insoluble / immobile and cannot be leached into water or absorbed by organisms</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The effects of two pollutants interact to produce a different (greater) effect than either alone</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cadmium and zinc; ozone and sulfur dioxide</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 275-278; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.11.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HM-04", "sub": "heavy-metals", "subname": "Heavy metals: lead, mercury", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.11", "pages": "275-279", "url": "subtopic/heavy-metals.html#HM-04", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the concentration of cadmium in rice and in the kidneys of people living in two villages in Japan. Village A is downstream of a zinc mine.", "text": "table 1 shows the concentration of cadmium in rice and in the kidneys of people living in two villages in japan. village a is downstream of a zinc mine. calculate how many times more concentrated cadmium is in the kidneys of village a residents than in their rice. explain why cadmium and zinc pollution often occur together. use table 1 and your knowledge of the properties of cadmium to explain the high rate of bone disease in village a. suggest two ways cadmium pollution from the mine could be controlled. cadmium is used in cadmium-telluride photovoltaic panels. suggest why this new use may cause pollution in the future. 180 / 1.2 = 150 cadmium and zinc occur together in the same ores so mine drainage / smelting releases both mine drainage water contaminated irrigation water / paddy soil so rice absorbed cadmium (1.2 vs 0.05 mg kg-1) cadmium is liposoluble / not excreted so it bioaccumulates in the body (kidneys) with chronic exposure to small doses over years cadmium causes kidney failure and bone decalcification / skeletal collapse (itai-itai disease) rice is the staple food so residents receive a large dose; village b has no exposure and no disease (correlation supports causation) collect mine drainage water and neutralise / raise ph with lime so cadmium precipitates and stays insoluble cover / cap spoil heaps to prevent leaching and dust recycle nickel-cadmium batteries / hazardous waste landfill phytoremediation of contaminated paddy soils manufacture may release cadmium in effluents / dust panels have a limited life; disposal of old panels (landfill, incineration of pigments) could release cadmium unless recycled", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HM-04\" data-qid=\"HM-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/heavy-metals.html\">Heavy metals: lead, mercury</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.11 &middot; Genn pp. 275-279 &middot; HM-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the concentration of cadmium in rice and in the kidneys of people living in two villages in Japan. Village A is downstream of a zinc mine.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Village</th><th>Cadmium in rice / mg kg<sup>-1</sup></th><th>Cadmium in kidney of residents / mg kg<sup>-1</sup></th><th>Cases of bone disease (itai-itai) per 1000 people</th></tr><tr><td>A (downstream of mine)</td><td>1.2</td><td>180</td><td>34</td></tr><tr><td>B (control)</td><td>0.05</td><td>22</td><td>0</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Calculate how many times more concentrated cadmium is in the kidneys of village A residents than in their rice.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain why cadmium and zinc pollution often occur together.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Use Table 1 and your knowledge of the properties of cadmium to explain the high rate of bone disease in village A.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Suggest <b>two</b> ways cadmium pollution from the mine could be controlled.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.5</span>Cadmium is used in cadmium-telluride photovoltaic panels. Suggest why this new use may cause pollution in the future.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>180 / 1.2 = 150</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cadmium and zinc occur together in the same ores so mine drainage / smelting releases both</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mine drainage water contaminated irrigation water / paddy soil so rice absorbed cadmium (1.2 vs 0.05 mg kg<sup>-1</sup>)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cadmium is liposoluble / not excreted so it bioaccumulates in the body (kidneys) with chronic exposure to small doses over years</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cadmium causes kidney failure and bone decalcification / skeletal collapse (itai-itai disease)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Rice is the staple food so residents receive a large dose; village B has no exposure and no disease (correlation supports causation)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Collect mine drainage water and neutralise / raise pH with lime so cadmium precipitates and stays insoluble</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cover / cap spoil heaps to prevent leaching and dust</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Recycle nickel-cadmium batteries / hazardous waste landfill</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Phytoremediation of contaminated paddy soils</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Manufacture may release cadmium in effluents / dust</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Panels have a limited life; disposal of old panels (landfill, incineration of pigments) could release cadmium unless recycled</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 275-279; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.11.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HM-05", "sub": "heavy-metals", "subname": "Heavy metals: lead, mercury", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.11", "pages": "275-279", "url": "subtopic/heavy-metals.html#HM-05", "snippet": "Which of the following is the main reason that lead-based paint is a greater risk to children than to adults? Tick one box.", "text": " which of the following is the main reason that lead-based paint is a greater risk to children than to adults? tick one box. explain how tri-butyl tin (tbt) in anti-fouling paint caused pollution even though only small amounts dissolved into the sea. iron is not toxic, yet iron-rich drainage from mine spoil heaps kills fish. explain why, and describe how it is controlled. give two uses of lead that continue because they do not cause significant lead release into the environment. explain why heavy metals are described as neurotoxins. b tbt is highly toxic to marine molluscs / crustaceans and an endocrine disruptor altering growth and reproduction of oysters / whelks even at very low concentrations it bioaccumulated in shellfish and could pass to humans eating them it was assumed dilution would make it harmless (illustrates the need for the precautionary principle); now prohibited and replaced by copper in the low-oxygen conditions of the spoil heap iron is in its soluble reduced form and is leached into the river in the river it is oxidised to insoluble iron oxide (orange sediment); the oxidation uses dissolved oxygen so the river is deoxygenated, killing aerobic organisms control: drainage water is passed over mesh screens where the iron oxidises and is deposited before the water reaches the river; the solid iron is removed periodically lead-acid car batteries (recycled) lead flashing on roofs / windows radiation shielding they inhibit enzyme function, especially the enzymes of the nervous system / nerve cells", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HM-05\" data-qid=\"HM-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/heavy-metals.html\">Heavy metals: lead, mercury</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.11 &middot; Genn pp. 275-279 &middot; HM-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Which of the following is the main reason that lead-based paint is a greater risk to children than to adults? Tick one box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; Children inhale more lead vapour</li><li>B&nbsp; Children pick up dust / flakes and suck their fingers</li><li>C&nbsp; Lead is more soluble in children's blood</li><li>D&nbsp; Children spend more time near roads</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain how tri-butyl tin (TBT) in anti-fouling paint caused pollution even though only small amounts dissolved into the sea.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Iron is not toxic, yet iron-rich drainage from mine spoil heaps kills fish. Explain why, and describe how it is controlled.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Give <b>two</b> uses of lead that continue because they do not cause significant lead release into the environment.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.5</span>Explain why heavy metals are described as neurotoxins.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b></b>&nbsp; B</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>TBT is highly toxic to marine molluscs / crustaceans and an endocrine disruptor altering growth and reproduction of oysters / whelks even at very low concentrations</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It bioaccumulated in shellfish and could pass to humans eating them</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It was assumed dilution would make it harmless (illustrates the need for the precautionary principle); now prohibited and replaced by copper</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In the low-oxygen conditions of the spoil heap iron is in its soluble reduced form and is leached into the river</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In the river it is oxidised to insoluble iron oxide (orange sediment); the oxidation uses dissolved oxygen so the river is deoxygenated, killing aerobic organisms</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Control: drainage water is passed over mesh screens where the iron oxidises and is deposited before the water reaches the river; the solid iron is removed periodically</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lead-acid car batteries (recycled)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lead flashing on roofs / windows</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Radiation shielding</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They inhibit enzyme function, especially the enzymes of the nervous system / nerve cells</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 275-279; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.11.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HM-06", "sub": "heavy-metals", "subname": "Heavy metals: lead, mercury", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.11", "pages": "275-279", "url": "subtopic/heavy-metals.html#HM-06", "snippet": "Which chemical form of mercury is most easily absorbed and biomagnified in food chains? Tick one box.", "text": " which chemical form of mercury is most easily absorbed and biomagnified in food chains? tick one box. explain why anaerobic sediments increased the hazard of the inorganic mercury discharged at minamata. explain why heavy metal wastes are stored under alkaline conditions. organic (methyl) mercury anaerobic microbes converted the inorganic mercury into organic methyl mercury methyl mercury is liposoluble so it is absorbed, crosses into the brain and biomagnifies along the food chain to fish eaten by people most heavy metals are less soluble at high ph so they remain immobile and are not leached into water or absorbed by organisms", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HM-06\" data-qid=\"HM-06\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/heavy-metals.html\">Heavy metals: lead, mercury</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.11 &middot; Genn pp. 275-279 &middot; HM-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Which chemical form of mercury is most easily absorbed and biomagnified in food chains? Tick <b>one</b> box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; Elemental (liquid) mercury</li><li>B&nbsp; Inorganic mercury compounds</li><li>C&nbsp; Organic (methyl) mercury</li><li>D&nbsp; Mercury vapour</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain why anaerobic sediments increased the hazard of the inorganic mercury discharged at Minamata.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain why heavy metal wastes are stored under alkaline conditions.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b>C</b>&nbsp; Organic (methyl) mercury</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Anaerobic microbes converted the inorganic mercury into organic methyl mercury</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Methyl mercury is liposoluble so it is absorbed, crosses into the brain and biomagnifies along the food chain to fish eaten by people</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Most heavy metals are less soluble at high pH</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So they remain immobile and are not leached into water or absorbed by organisms</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 275-279; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.11.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SW-01", "sub": "solid-waste", "subname": "Solid wastes", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.12", "pages": "280-282", "url": "subtopic/solid-waste.html#SW-01", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows how municipal solid waste was treated in the UK in 2000 and 2019.", "text": "figure 1 shows how municipal solid waste was treated in the uk in 2000 and 2019. describe the changes shown in figure 1. explain three environmental disadvantages of landfill that led to the change. describe four features of a well-managed landfill site. suggest why incineration has increased more than recycling for some waste types. landfill fell from 79% to 14% (large decrease) incineration rose from 9% to 42% and recycling / composting rose from 11% to 43% (now the largest) organic matter decays anaerobically releasing methane (and co2) - greenhouse gases toxic leachate may leak into groundwater / rivers from poorly managed sites uses large areas of land; loss of habitats / farmland; contamination prevents later use resource value of metals, glass, plastics is lost transport noise / congestion / litter / pests / odour polymer / clay liner to prevent escape of leachate collection and treatment of leachate collection of methane and use as a fuel impermeable cap (clay and polymer) when complete to stop water entering regular covering with soil (pests, odour); deodorising sprays perimeter fence to trap wind-blown litter separation and recording of waste types; reactive / flammable wastes not dumped together mixed / contaminated / dirty wastes (plastics, dirty paper) are difficult and costly to separate and recycle but can be burnt with energy recovery", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SW-01\" data-qid=\"SW-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/solid-waste.html\">Solid wastes</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.12 &middot; Genn pp. 280-282 &middot; SW-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows how municipal solid waste was treated in the UK in 2000 and 2019.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<rect x=\"78.0\" y=\"72.5\" width=\"42.0\" height=\"197.5\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<rect x=\"120.0\" y=\"235.0\" width=\"42.0\" height=\"35.0\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<text x=\"120.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Landfill</text>\n<rect x=\"198.0\" y=\"247.5\" width=\"42.0\" height=\"22.5\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<rect x=\"240.0\" y=\"165.0\" width=\"42.0\" height=\"105.0\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<text x=\"240.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Incineration</text>\n<rect x=\"318.0\" y=\"242.5\" width=\"42.0\" height=\"27.5\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<rect x=\"360.0\" y=\"162.5\" width=\"42.0\" height=\"107.5\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<text x=\"360.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Recycling / composting</text>\n<rect x=\"438.0\" y=\"267.5\" width=\"42.0\" height=\"2.5\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<rect x=\"480.0\" y=\"267.5\" width=\"42.0\" height=\"2.5\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<text x=\"480.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Other</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">20</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">40</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">60</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">80</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">100</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Treatment method</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Percentage of waste / %</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"24\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"33\">2000</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"38\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"47\">2019</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Describe the changes shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain <b>three</b> environmental disadvantages of landfill that led to the change.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Describe <b>four</b> features of a well-managed landfill site.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Suggest why incineration has increased more than recycling for some waste types.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Landfill fell from 79% to 14% (large decrease)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Incineration rose from 9% to 42% and recycling / composting rose from 11% to 43% (now the largest)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Organic matter decays anaerobically releasing methane (and CO2) - greenhouse gases</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Toxic leachate may leak into groundwater / rivers from poorly managed sites</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Uses large areas of land; loss of habitats / farmland; contamination prevents later use</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Resource value of metals, glass, plastics is lost</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Transport noise / congestion / litter / pests / odour</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Polymer / clay liner to prevent escape of leachate</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Collection and treatment of leachate</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Collection of methane and use as a fuel</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Impermeable cap (clay and polymer) when complete to stop water entering</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Regular covering with soil (pests, odour); deodorising sprays</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Perimeter fence to trap wind-blown litter</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Separation and recording of waste types; reactive / flammable wastes not dumped together</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mixed / contaminated / dirty wastes (plastics, dirty paper) are difficult and costly to separate and recycle but can be burnt with energy recovery</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 280-282; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.12.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SW-02", "sub": "solid-waste", "subname": "Solid wastes", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.2.12", "pages": "283-284", "url": "subtopic/solid-waste.html#SW-02", "snippet": "Give three advantages of incinerating domestic waste.", "text": " give three advantages of incinerating domestic waste. explain how each of the following features of a modern incinerator reduces pollution. explain why the ash from waste incinerators needs careful disposal. state one reason why incineration is expensive. volume of ash is much less than the original waste (less landfill) heat used for district heating / electricity generation (energy recovery) no sorting / complicated management needed destroys pathogens / flammable organic wastes converted to co2 and water refuse-derived fuel can replace fossil fuels (eg cement kilns) high temperature and rapid cooling reduce the formation of toxic dioxins (from organic wastes reacting with chlorine) scrubber dissolves hydrogen chloride (from chlorine in pvc) which is then neutralised with alkali / limestone; also removes heavy metals for storage at high ph wet waste needs extra fuel to burn and lowers combustion temperature; composting recovers its nutrient value recycling conserves the resource value of metals, glass and plastics that would otherwise be lost ash may contain concentrated heavy metals / toxic residues must be stored in solid form at high ph / hazardous waste landfill so the metals remain insoluble fuel (natural gas) needed to burn wet / non-flammable wastes cost of treating effluent gases (so2, nox, hcl, smoke)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SW-02\" data-qid=\"SW-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/solid-waste.html\">Solid wastes</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.12 &middot; Genn pp. 283-284 &middot; SW-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Give <b>three</b> advantages of incinerating domestic waste.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain how each of the following features of a modern incinerator reduces pollution.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain why the ash from waste incinerators needs careful disposal.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>State <b>one</b> reason why incineration is expensive.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Volume of ash is much less than the original waste (less landfill)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Heat used for district heating / electricity generation (energy recovery)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No sorting / complicated management needed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Destroys pathogens / flammable organic wastes converted to CO2 and water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Refuse-derived fuel can replace fossil fuels (eg cement kilns)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>High temperature and rapid cooling reduce the formation of toxic dioxins (from organic wastes reacting with chlorine)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Scrubber dissolves hydrogen chloride (from chlorine in PVC) which is then neutralised with alkali / limestone; also removes heavy metals for storage at high pH</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wet waste needs extra fuel to burn and lowers combustion temperature; composting recovers its nutrient value</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Recycling conserves the resource value of metals, glass and plastics that would otherwise be lost</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ash may contain concentrated heavy metals / toxic residues</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Must be stored in solid form at high pH / hazardous waste landfill so the metals remain insoluble</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 1 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 1.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fuel (natural gas) needed to burn wet / non-flammable wastes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cost of treating effluent gases (SO2, NOx, HCl, smoke)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 283-284; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.12.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SW-03", "sub": "solid-waste", "subname": "Solid wastes", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.12", "pages": "284-285, 303", "url": "subtopic/solid-waste.html#SW-03", "snippet": "Table 1 shows three specialist solid wastes.", "text": "table 1 shows three specialist solid wastes. complete table 1 to show the main hazard of each waste and the disposal method used. explain why encapsulated asbestos in cement roof panels is not considered dangerous while it is intact. describe the process of encapsulation used for intermediate-level radioactive waste and wastes containing heavy metals. explain why vitrification is preferred to encapsulation in cement for high-level waste. asbestos: fibres become airborne when it disintegrates and are inhaled, causing asbestosis (scar tissue thickens alveoli) and mesothelioma; disposal: double-wrapped in heavy-duty polythene in a specialised, recorded landfill (secure, permanent, sealed storage) cyanide: very toxic enzyme inhibitor (aerobic respiration) if inhaled / ingested; disposal: incineration so carbon and nitrogen are separated and oxidised to co2 and nox (much smaller problem) high-level radioactive waste: intense ionising radiation, heat generation, long half-lives; disposal: vitrification - mixed with molten glass in sealed stainless steel containers in ventilated concrete stores the fibres are bound / cannot become airborne so cannot be inhaled waste is mixed with a cement slurry and poured into containers of impermeable, unreactive material the solid cement immobilises the waste so it cannot leak / leach out even if the glass shatters the waste stays encapsulated in the fragments; glass is more stable / resistant to heat and leaching over very long timescales", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SW-03\" data-qid=\"SW-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/solid-waste.html\">Solid wastes</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.12 &middot; Genn pp. 284-285, 303 &middot; SW-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows three specialist solid wastes.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Complete Table 1 to show the main hazard of each waste and the disposal method used.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Waste</th><th>Main hazard</th><th>Disposal method</th></tr><tr><td>Asbestos</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Cyanide compounds</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>High-level radioactive waste</td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain why encapsulated asbestos in cement roof panels is not considered dangerous while it is intact.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Describe the process of encapsulation used for intermediate-level radioactive waste and wastes containing heavy metals.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Explain why vitrification is preferred to encapsulation in cement for high-level waste.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Asbestos: fibres become airborne when it disintegrates and are inhaled, causing asbestosis (scar tissue thickens alveoli) and mesothelioma; disposal: double-wrapped in heavy-duty polythene in a specialised, recorded landfill (secure, permanent, sealed storage)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Cyanide: very toxic enzyme inhibitor (aerobic respiration) if inhaled / ingested; disposal: incineration so carbon and nitrogen are separated and oxidised to CO2 and NOx (much smaller problem)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>High-level radioactive waste: intense ionising radiation, heat generation, long half-lives; disposal: vitrification - mixed with molten glass in sealed stainless steel containers in ventilated concrete stores</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The fibres are bound / cannot become airborne so cannot be inhaled</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Waste is mixed with a cement slurry and poured into containers of impermeable, unreactive material</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The solid cement immobilises the waste so it cannot leak / leach out</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Even if the glass shatters the waste stays encapsulated in the fragments; glass is more stable / resistant to heat and leaching over very long timescales</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 284-285, 303; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.12.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SW-04", "sub": "solid-waste", "subname": "Solid wastes", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.2.12", "pages": "282-283", "url": "subtopic/solid-waste.html#SW-04", "snippet": "A coal-mine spoil heap on a hillside has been abandoned. The spoil contains shale with some iron pyrites and residual coal.", "text": "a coal-mine spoil heap on a hillside has been abandoned. the spoil contains shale with some iron pyrites and residual coal. explain three problems this spoil heap could cause and describe how each could be managed. explain why deep layers of soil are applied when a spoil heap containing toxic materials is restored. explain why an old steel-works spoil heap may be valuable for wildlife conservation. instability / landslips on the hillside, especially after heavy rain - compaction, drainage, establishing vegetation whose roots bind the spoil, regrading slopes acid drainage from oxidation of pyrites, with toxic metals - collect and treat leachate, add lime to raise ph spontaneous combustion of residual coal / hydrocarbons - layers of fine-grained material to reduce air flow lack of nutrients / poor plant growth - add topsoil, sewage sludge, fertiliser unattractive topography - landscaping / reshaping dust / heavy metals - cover and keep at high ph so that plant roots cannot reach the toxic layer otherwise toxins could be taken up and brought to the surface / dispersed via leaf litter and food chains steel slag is alkaline, producing unusual calcareous / high-ph soil conditions supports uncommon plants such as cowslips / orchids that cannot compete on fertile neutral soils", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SW-04\" data-qid=\"SW-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/solid-waste.html\">Solid wastes</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.12 &middot; Genn pp. 282-283 &middot; SW-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A coal-mine spoil heap on a hillside has been abandoned. The spoil contains shale with some iron pyrites and residual coal.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Explain <b>three</b> problems this spoil heap could cause and describe how each could be managed.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain why deep layers of soil are applied when a spoil heap containing toxic materials is restored.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain why an old steel-works spoil heap may be valuable for wildlife conservation.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Instability / landslips on the hillside, especially after heavy rain - compaction, drainage, establishing vegetation whose roots bind the spoil, regrading slopes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Acid drainage from oxidation of pyrites, with toxic metals - collect and treat leachate, add lime to raise pH</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Spontaneous combustion of residual coal / hydrocarbons - layers of fine-grained material to reduce air flow</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lack of nutrients / poor plant growth - add topsoil, sewage sludge, fertiliser</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Unattractive topography - landscaping / reshaping</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Dust / heavy metals - cover and keep at high pH</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So that plant roots cannot reach the toxic layer</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Otherwise toxins could be taken up and brought to the surface / dispersed via leaf litter and food chains</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Steel slag is alkaline, producing unusual calcareous / high-pH soil conditions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Supports uncommon plants such as cowslips / orchids that cannot compete on fertile neutral soils</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 282-283; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.12.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SW-05", "sub": "solid-waste", "subname": "Solid wastes", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.2.12", "pages": "279-282", "url": "subtopic/solid-waste.html#SW-05", "snippet": "Explain how affluence and the strategies of manufacturers increase the amount of solid waste produced.", "text": " explain how affluence and the strategies of manufacturers increase the amount of solid waste produced. give three factors that affect the choice of disposal method for a solid waste. explain one environmental advantage and one disadvantage of composting garden and food waste rather than sending it to landfill. explain why mixed municipal waste is difficult to recycle. affluent people buy more consumer goods / trivia / disposable items and throw items away rather than repairing them built-in obsolescence: products designed to have short lives / wear out / go out of fashion so customers upgrade disposable products (razors, lighters, pens) that must be replaced over-packaging to make products attractive; also increases wastes from manufacture and mineral extraction properties of the waste: toxicity, flammability, degradability, radioactivity population density (collection costs) land availability for landfill availability of recycling technology / markets legislation / regulatory framework (landfill tax) public willingness to recycle / environmental awareness processing costs / household income advantage: aerobic decomposition produces little methane; nutrients / organic matter returned to soil (circular economy); reduces landfill volume disadvantage: needs separation / collection of clean organic waste; odour / pests / pathogens if poorly managed; low-value product; not suitable for mixed waste many different materials are mixed together / contaminated so identification and separation are costly and the quality of recovered material is reduced", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SW-05\" data-qid=\"SW-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/solid-waste.html\">Solid wastes</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.12 &middot; Genn pp. 279-282 &middot; SW-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Explain how affluence and the strategies of manufacturers increase the amount of solid waste produced.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Give <b>three</b> factors that affect the choice of disposal method for a solid waste.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain <b>one</b> environmental advantage and <b>one</b> disadvantage of composting garden and food waste rather than sending it to landfill.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Explain why mixed municipal waste is difficult to recycle.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Affluent people buy more consumer goods / trivia / disposable items and throw items away rather than repairing them</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Built-in obsolescence: products designed to have short lives / wear out / go out of fashion so customers upgrade</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Disposable products (razors, lighters, pens) that must be replaced</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Over-packaging to make products attractive; also increases wastes from manufacture and mineral extraction</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Properties of the waste: toxicity, flammability, degradability, radioactivity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Population density (collection costs)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Land availability for landfill</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Availability of recycling technology / markets</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Legislation / regulatory framework (landfill tax)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Public willingness to recycle / environmental awareness</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Processing costs / household income</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Advantage: aerobic decomposition produces little methane; nutrients / organic matter returned to soil (circular economy); reduces landfill volume</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Disadvantage: needs separation / collection of clean organic waste; odour / pests / pathogens if poorly managed; low-value product; not suitable for mixed waste</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Many different materials are mixed together / contaminated so identification and separation are costly and the quality of recovered material is reduced</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 279-282; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.12.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SW-06", "sub": "solid-waste", "subname": "Solid wastes", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.12", "pages": "279-285", "url": "subtopic/solid-waste.html#SW-06", "snippet": "Which of the following is the main advantage of composting organic waste rather than landfilling it? Tick one box.", "text": " which of the following is the main advantage of composting organic waste rather than landfilling it? tick one box. explain why the ash from waste incinerators must be disposed of carefully. give two features of a well-managed landfill site that reduce pollution. aerobic decomposition produces little methane and returns nutrients to the soil it may contain concentrated heavy metals / toxic residues it is stored in solid form at high ph / in hazardous-waste landfill so the metals stay insoluble impermeable polymer / clay liner to stop leachate escaping collection and treatment of leachate collection of methane and use as fuel daily covering of waste / final capping and restoration", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SW-06\" data-qid=\"SW-06\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/solid-waste.html\">Solid wastes</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.12 &middot; Genn pp. 279-285 &middot; SW-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Which of the following is the main advantage of composting organic waste rather than landfilling it? Tick <b>one</b> box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; Aerobic decomposition produces little methane and returns nutrients to the soil</li><li>B&nbsp; It produces more electricity</li><li>C&nbsp; It removes heavy metals</li><li>D&nbsp; It destroys asbestos fibres</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain why the ash from waste incinerators must be disposed of carefully.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Give <b>two</b> features of a well-managed landfill site that reduce pollution.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b>A</b>&nbsp; Aerobic decomposition produces little methane and returns nutrients to the soil</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It may contain concentrated heavy metals / toxic residues</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It is stored in solid form at high pH / in hazardous-waste landfill so the metals stay insoluble</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Impermeable polymer / clay liner to stop leachate escaping</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Collection and treatment of leachate</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Collection of methane and use as fuel</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Daily covering of waste / final capping and restoration</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 279-285; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.12.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FN-01", "sub": "fossil-nuclear", "subname": "Fossil fuels & nuclear power: sustainability and new technologies", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "187-188", "url": "subtopic/fossil-nuclear.html#FN-01", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the percentage of the oil in a reservoir that can be recovered using different recovery techniques.", "text": "figure 1 shows the percentage of the oil in a reservoir that can be recovered using different recovery techniques. describe how primary oil recovery works and explain why it recovers only about 20% of the oil. describe secondary recovery and explain how it can be linked to carbon capture and storage. describe three tertiary (enhanced) recovery techniques. explain three advantages of directional drilling. natural pressure of gas above the oil (or dissolved in it) or water beneath forces oil up the production well; pump-jacks ('nodding donkeys') increase flow pressure falls as oil is removed so the remaining oil no longer flows to the well water or natural gas is pumped down an injection well to maintain the pressure and flow of oil (recovery to about 40%) captured co2 can be pumped down instead, increasing oil recovery while storing the co2 underground steam injection (from combustion or solar parabolic concentrators) to heat the oil and reduce its viscosity controlled underground combustion to heat the oil detergents / solvents to reduce surface tension so oil flows more easily bacteria that partially digest heavy oil into lighter oils and produce co2 to maintain pressure many wells can be drilled from a single platform, reducing land take / number of rigs deposits under locations where rigs cannot be placed (urban areas, sensitive habitats) can be reached drilling can follow softer strata (quicker) and target multiple small reservoirs up to 10 km away, increasing total recovery", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FN-01\" data-qid=\"FN-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/fossil-nuclear.html\">Fossil fuels &amp; nuclear power: sustainability and new technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 187-188 &middot; FN-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the percentage of the oil in a reservoir that can be recovered using different recovery techniques.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<rect x=\"84.0\" y=\"220.0\" width=\"112.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"140.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Primary</text>\n<rect x=\"244.0\" y=\"170.0\" width=\"112.0\" height=\"100.0\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Primary + secondary</text>\n<rect x=\"404.0\" y=\"120.0\" width=\"112.0\" height=\"150.0\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"460.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Primary + secondary + tertiary</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">20</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">40</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">60</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">80</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">100</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Recovery technique</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Cumulative oil recovered / %</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Describe how primary oil recovery works and explain why it recovers only about 20% of the oil.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Describe secondary recovery and explain how it can be linked to carbon capture and storage.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Describe <b>three</b> tertiary (enhanced) recovery techniques.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Explain <b>three</b> advantages of directional drilling.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Natural pressure of gas above the oil (or dissolved in it) or water beneath forces oil up the production well; pump-jacks ('nodding donkeys') increase flow</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pressure falls as oil is removed so the remaining oil no longer flows to the well</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Water or natural gas is pumped down an injection well to maintain the pressure and flow of oil (recovery to about 40%)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Captured CO2 can be pumped down instead, increasing oil recovery while storing the CO2 underground</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Steam injection (from combustion or solar parabolic concentrators) to heat the oil and reduce its viscosity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Controlled underground combustion to heat the oil</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Detergents / solvents to reduce surface tension so oil flows more easily</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bacteria that partially digest heavy oil into lighter oils and produce CO2 to maintain pressure</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Many wells can be drilled from a single platform, reducing land take / number of rigs</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Deposits under locations where rigs cannot be placed (urban areas, sensitive habitats) can be reached</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Drilling can follow softer strata (quicker) and target multiple small reservoirs up to 10 km away, increasing total recovery</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 187-188; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FN-02", "sub": "fossil-nuclear", "subname": "Fossil fuels & nuclear power: sustainability and new technologies", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "184, 189-190", "url": "subtopic/fossil-nuclear.html#FN-02", "snippet": "Explain why oil and gas trapped in shale cannot be extracted by conventional drilling and describe how hydraulic fracturing (fracking) allows extraction.", "text": " explain why oil and gas trapped in shale cannot be extracted by conventional drilling and describe how hydraulic fracturing (fracking) allows extraction. describe four environmental concerns about fracking and suggest how they may be reduced. explain why tar sands and oil shales are not included in most estimates of oil reserves despite containing more oil than conventional crude oil. shale has very low permeability so the 'tight' oil / gas cannot flow through the rock to a well fluid is pumped down at high pressure to open fissures in the shale along which oil / gas can flow to the recovery well sand grains (to hold fissures open) and solvents are pumped in to increase the recovery rate natural gas may enter aquifer water injected chemicals may reach aquifers or the surface; toxic metals in the rock may be mobilised large volumes of water needed earth tremors as fracking releases natural tensions in the crust (though not tremors that could not occur naturally) reduced by: collection, treatment and re-use of waste water; well casing standards; restricting sites away from aquifers / sensitive areas; monitoring extraction is expensive and energy-intensive (quarrying and hot water treatment, steam / solvent / combustion in-situ; heating shale to release oil) so it is not economically viable at current prices reserves only include what is economically exploitable now; high environmental impacts (land take, water, co2) also limit exploitation", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FN-02\" data-qid=\"FN-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/fossil-nuclear.html\">Fossil fuels &amp; nuclear power: sustainability and new technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 184, 189-190 &middot; FN-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain why oil and gas trapped in shale cannot be extracted by conventional drilling and describe how hydraulic fracturing (fracking) allows extraction.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Describe <b>four</b> environmental concerns about fracking and suggest how they may be reduced.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain why tar sands and oil shales are not included in most estimates of oil reserves despite containing more oil than conventional crude oil.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Shale has very low permeability so the 'tight' oil / gas cannot flow through the rock to a well</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fluid is pumped down at high pressure to open fissures in the shale along which oil / gas can flow to the recovery well</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sand grains (to hold fissures open) and solvents are pumped in to increase the recovery rate</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Natural gas may enter aquifer water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Injected chemicals may reach aquifers or the surface; toxic metals in the rock may be mobilised</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Large volumes of water needed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Earth tremors as fracking releases natural tensions in the crust (though not tremors that could not occur naturally)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduced by: collection, treatment and re-use of waste water; well casing standards; restricting sites away from aquifers / sensitive areas; monitoring</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Extraction is expensive and energy-intensive (quarrying and hot water treatment, steam / solvent / combustion in-situ; heating shale to release oil) so it is not economically viable at current prices</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reserves only include what is economically exploitable now; high environmental impacts (land take, water, CO2) also limit exploitation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 184, 189-190; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FN-03", "sub": "fossil-nuclear", "subname": "Fossil fuels & nuclear power: sustainability and new technologies", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "187, 190", "url": "subtopic/fossil-nuclear.html#FN-03", "snippet": "Describe coal gasification and coal liquefaction and explain how each increases the usefulness of coal.", "text": " describe coal gasification and coal liquefaction and explain how each increases the usefulness of coal. describe what methane hydrate is and where it is found. describe two proposed methods of extracting methane from hydrate deposits and explain two environmental risks. gasification: coal (including deposits too deep to mine) is burnt underground under controlled conditions to produce fuel gases (hydrogen, carbon monoxide, methane) allows deep coal to be exploited and gas can be piped / used in gas turbines or for hydrogen with ccs liquefaction: coal is converted to liquid hydrocarbons directly with solvents or via gasification and chemical conversion provides liquid fuels for vehicles / aircraft that solid coal cannot supply a solid, ice-like crystalline structure in which methane is trapped within water ice found at low temperatures (polar regions / permafrost) or high pressure in ocean sediments around continental margins water heating: hot water pumped into sediments melts the hydrate releasing methane depressurisation: drilling reduces pressure so methane dissociates from the crystals co2 injection: co2 bonds more strongly to the ice and displaces methane (could double as ccs) risks: methane not collected escapes to the atmosphere - powerful greenhouse gas / positive feedback; seabed ecology poorly understood; sediment instability / landslides", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FN-03\" data-qid=\"FN-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/fossil-nuclear.html\">Fossil fuels &amp; nuclear power: sustainability and new technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 187, 190 &middot; FN-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Describe coal gasification and coal liquefaction and explain how each increases the usefulness of coal.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Describe what methane hydrate is and where it is found.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Describe <b>two</b> proposed methods of extracting methane from hydrate deposits and explain <b>two</b> environmental risks.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Gasification: coal (including deposits too deep to mine) is burnt underground under controlled conditions to produce fuel gases (hydrogen, carbon monoxide, methane)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Allows deep coal to be exploited and gas can be piped / used in gas turbines or for hydrogen with CCS</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Liquefaction: coal is converted to liquid hydrocarbons directly with solvents or via gasification and chemical conversion</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Provides liquid fuels for vehicles / aircraft that solid coal cannot supply</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A solid, ice-like crystalline structure in which methane is trapped within water ice</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Found at low temperatures (polar regions / permafrost) or high pressure in ocean sediments around continental margins</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Water heating: hot water pumped into sediments melts the hydrate releasing methane</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Depressurisation: drilling reduces pressure so methane dissociates from the crystals</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>CO2 injection: CO2 bonds more strongly to the ice and displaces methane (could double as CCS)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Risks: methane not collected escapes to the atmosphere - powerful greenhouse gas / positive feedback; seabed ecology poorly understood; sediment instability / landslides</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 187, 190; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FN-04", "sub": "fossil-nuclear", "subname": "Fossil fuels & nuclear power: sustainability and new technologies", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "191-193", "url": "subtopic/fossil-nuclear.html#FN-04", "snippet": "Table 1 compares a 1000 MW coal-fired power station with a 1000 MW nuclear power station.", "text": "table 1 compares a 1000 mw coal-fired power station with a 1000 mw nuclear power station. explain how nuclear fission releases energy in a reactor. use table 1 to explain why nuclear power is used for base-load electricity but not for meeting peaks in demand. use table 1 to explain why nuclear power stations can be built where coal-fired stations could not. explain why the co2 emissions of nuclear power are not zero. give two reasons why the true cost of nuclear electricity is uncertain. nuclei of large atoms (uranium-235, plutonium-239) split when hit by a neutron a small amount of mass is converted to a large amount of energy (e = mc2) and more neutrons are released, causing a chain reaction (controlled by control rods / moderator) the heat boils water to steam which drives turbines and generators output changes slowly (days) so it cannot follow rapid fluctuations in demand very high construction cost but low fuel cost means it is most economic running continuously at full output only 30 t of fuel a year (very high energy density) so no need for railways / ports to deliver millions of tonnes of coal still need condenser cooling water (coast / large lake) and a stable site uranium must be mined, purified, concentrated and processed into fuel using (fossil) energy; reactor construction and decommissioning have embodied energy no commercial reactor has been fully decommissioned so costs are unknown and often exceed estimates large projects often overrun original cost estimates long-term waste disposal costs uncertain state subsidies may hide costs", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FN-04\" data-qid=\"FN-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/fossil-nuclear.html\">Fossil fuels &amp; nuclear power: sustainability and new technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 191-193 &middot; FN-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 compares a 1000 MW coal-fired power station with a 1000 MW nuclear power station.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Coal-fired</th><th>Nuclear (uranium)</th></tr><tr><td>Fuel used per year</td><td>3.5 million tonnes</td><td>30 tonnes</td></tr><tr><td>CO<sub>2</sub> emitted per kWh / g</td><td>900</td><td>12 (mostly fuel processing and construction)</td></tr><tr><td>Construction cost / &pound; billion</td><td>2</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel cost per kWh / pence</td><td>3.0</td><td>0.5</td></tr><tr><td>Typical response to changes in demand</td><td>Hours</td><td>Slow (days)</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Explain how nuclear fission releases energy in a reactor.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Use Table 1 to explain why nuclear power is used for base-load electricity but not for meeting peaks in demand.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Use Table 1 to explain why nuclear power stations can be built where coal-fired stations could not.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Explain why the CO<sub>2</sub> emissions of nuclear power are not zero.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.5</span>Give <b>two</b> reasons why the true cost of nuclear electricity is uncertain.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nuclei of large atoms (uranium-235, plutonium-239) split when hit by a neutron</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A small amount of mass is converted to a large amount of energy (E = mc<sup>2</sup>) and more neutrons are released, causing a chain reaction (controlled by control rods / moderator)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The heat boils water to steam which drives turbines and generators</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Output changes slowly (days) so it cannot follow rapid fluctuations in demand</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Very high construction cost but low fuel cost means it is most economic running continuously at full output</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Only 30 t of fuel a year (very high energy density) so no need for railways / ports to deliver millions of tonnes of coal</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Still need condenser cooling water (coast / large lake) and a stable site</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Uranium must be mined, purified, concentrated and processed into fuel using (fossil) energy; reactor construction and decommissioning have embodied energy</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No commercial reactor has been fully decommissioned so costs are unknown and often exceed estimates</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Large projects often overrun original cost estimates</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Long-term waste disposal costs uncertain</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>State subsidies may hide costs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 191-193; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FN-05", "sub": "fossil-nuclear", "subname": "Fossil fuels & nuclear power: sustainability and new technologies", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "192-194", "url": "subtopic/fossil-nuclear.html#FN-05", "snippet": "Describe three factors that have restricted the growth of nuclear power.", "text": " describe three factors that have restricted the growth of nuclear power. explain how a plutonium 'breeder' reactor can produce more fissile fuel than it uses. give two advantages and two disadvantages of thorium reactors compared with uranium reactors. public opposition following accidents (three mile island 1979, chernobyl 1986, fukushima 2011) very complex, expensive technology needing an advanced industrial infrastructure concerns about links between civil nuclear materials and weapons / terrorism uncertainty over permanent disposal of radioactive waste uncertain decommissioning costs only 0.7% of natural uranium is fissile u-235; the remaining 99.3% is non-fissile u-238 (a 'fertile' fuel) neutron bombardment in the reactor converts u-238 to fissile plutonium-239 (via beta decays) so much more energy is harnessed from the mined uranium; fast reactors need no moderator but are complex and costly advantages: thorium is three times more abundant; much less radioactive waste with shorter half-lives; harder to make weapons material; no fuel enrichment needed disadvantages: thorium-232 is not fissile so must be bred into uranium-233 (slow breeding rate); u-233 is an alpha emitter so very hazardous to handle; less developed technology with high development costs", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FN-05\" data-qid=\"FN-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/fossil-nuclear.html\">Fossil fuels &amp; nuclear power: sustainability and new technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 192-194 &middot; FN-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Describe <b>three</b> factors that have restricted the growth of nuclear power.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain how a plutonium 'breeder' reactor can produce more fissile fuel than it uses.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Give <b>two</b> advantages and <b>two</b> disadvantages of thorium reactors compared with uranium reactors.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Public opposition following accidents (Three Mile Island 1979, Chernobyl 1986, Fukushima 2011)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Very complex, expensive technology needing an advanced industrial infrastructure</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Concerns about links between civil nuclear materials and weapons / terrorism</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Uncertainty over permanent disposal of radioactive waste</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Uncertain decommissioning costs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Only 0.7% of natural uranium is fissile U-235; the remaining 99.3% is non-fissile U-238 (a 'fertile' fuel)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Neutron bombardment in the reactor converts U-238 to fissile plutonium-239 (via beta decays)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So much more energy is harnessed from the mined uranium; fast reactors need no moderator but are complex and costly</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Advantages: thorium is three times more abundant; much less radioactive waste with shorter half-lives; harder to make weapons material; no fuel enrichment needed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Disadvantages: thorium-232 is not fissile so must be bred into uranium-233 (slow breeding rate); U-233 is an alpha emitter so very hazardous to handle; less developed technology with high development costs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 192-194; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FN-06", "sub": "fossil-nuclear", "subname": "Fossil fuels & nuclear power: sustainability and new technologies", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.3.4.1", "pages": "195-196", "url": "subtopic/fossil-nuclear.html#FN-06", "snippet": "Explain the difference between nuclear fission and nuclear fusion.", "text": " explain the difference between nuclear fission and nuclear fusion. state the sources of the two fuels used in fusion research. explain why very high temperatures, a vacuum and a magnetic field are needed in a toroidal (torus) fusion reactor. give two potential advantages of fusion over fission and one reason why it is not yet a commercial energy source. fission: splitting the nuclei of large atoms (u-235, pu-239) when hit by neutrons fusion: joining the nuclei of small atoms (deuterium and tritium - isotopes of hydrogen) to form helium, releasing a neutron and energy - the energy source of stars deuterium (hydrogen-2) extracted from (sea)water tritium (hydrogen-3) produced by neutron bombardment of lithium (a lithium blanket around the reactor) very high temperature turns hydrogen into a plasma (electrons stripped away) and gives nuclei enough kinetic energy to overcome the repulsion between positive nuclei and collide vacuum so the plasma is not cooled by air magnetic field holds the plasma centrally so it does not touch the container walls and cool fuel (deuterium / lithium) is abundant / effectively unlimited much less radioactive waste (no long-lived fission products); no chain reaction / meltdown risk; no weapons material not yet achieved sustained net energy output: reactors (jet) use more energy than they release; iter aims for 500 mw out from 50 mw in; laser fusion also experimental; commercial viability decades away", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FN-06\" data-qid=\"FN-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/fossil-nuclear.html\">Fossil fuels &amp; nuclear power: sustainability and new technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 195-196 &middot; FN-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Explain the difference between nuclear fission and nuclear fusion.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>State the sources of the two fuels used in fusion research.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain why very high temperatures, a vacuum and a magnetic field are needed in a toroidal (torus) fusion reactor.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Give <b>two</b> potential advantages of fusion over fission and one reason why it is not yet a commercial energy source.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fission: splitting the nuclei of large atoms (U-235, Pu-239) when hit by neutrons</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fusion: joining the nuclei of small atoms (deuterium and tritium - isotopes of hydrogen) to form helium, releasing a neutron and energy - the energy source of stars</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Deuterium (hydrogen-2) extracted from (sea)water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Tritium (hydrogen-3) produced by neutron bombardment of lithium (a lithium blanket around the reactor)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Very high temperature turns hydrogen into a plasma (electrons stripped away) and gives nuclei enough kinetic energy to overcome the repulsion between positive nuclei and collide</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Vacuum so the plasma is not cooled by air</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Magnetic field holds the plasma centrally so it does not touch the container walls and cool</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fuel (deuterium / lithium) is abundant / effectively unlimited</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Much less radioactive waste (no long-lived fission products); no chain reaction / meltdown risk; no weapons material</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Not yet achieved sustained net energy output: reactors (JET) use more energy than they release; ITER aims for 500 MW out from 50 MW in; laser fusion also experimental; commercial viability decades away</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 195-196; AQA spec 3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FN-07", "sub": "fossil-nuclear", "subname": "Fossil fuels & nuclear power: sustainability and new technologies", "topic": "Energy resources", "tslug": "energy", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.3.3-3.3.4.1", "pages": "183-196", "url": "subtopic/fossil-nuclear.html#FN-07", "snippet": "Evaluate the extent to which new technologies could allow fossil fuels and nuclear fission to continue to supply a large proportion of the world's energy sustainably.", "text": " evaluate the extent to which new technologies could allow fossil fuels and nuclear fission to continue to supply a large proportion of the world's energy sustainably. state the approximate percentage of natural gas in a reservoir that can be recovered using its own pressure. fossil fuel technologies: secondary / tertiary recovery raising recovery from 20% to 60%; directional drilling and subsea wells; rovs; fracking for tight oil and gas; tar sands / oil shales; coal gasification / liquefaction; enhanced gas recovery; methane hydrates; ccs (pre / post combustion, storage) limits: still finite (discoveries declining since the 1980s); rising energy and financial cost of unconventional sources; environmental impacts (aquifers, water use, tremors, land take, habitat damage); co2 and other pollution; ccs unproven at scale and impractical for vehicles nuclear: new uranium extraction (polymer adsorption from seawater, phosphate mining, coal ash); molten salt reactors; plutonium breeder and thorium reactors increasing fuel use many-fold; fusion as a long-term prospect limits: cost overruns, decommissioning, waste, public opposition, proliferation, embodied energy of low-grade uranium; fusion not commercial sustainability criteria: maintaining supply without unacceptable environmental, economic or social impact; comparison with renewables and energy conservation; energy gap argument judgement: technologies can extend supplies and reduce some impacts but cannot make non-renewables sustainable indefinitely; role as transition / base-load alongside renewables 80-90%", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FN-07\" data-qid=\"FN-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/fossil-nuclear.html\">Fossil fuels &amp; nuclear power: sustainability and new technologies</a> &middot; spec 3.3.3-3.3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 183-196 &middot; FN-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Evaluate the extent to which new technologies could allow fossil fuels and nuclear fission to continue to supply a large proportion of the world's energy sustainably.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>State the approximate percentage of natural gas in a reservoir that can be recovered using its own pressure.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Fossil fuel technologies: secondary / tertiary recovery raising recovery from 20% to 60%; directional drilling and subsea wells; ROVs; fracking for tight oil and gas; tar sands / oil shales; coal gasification / liquefaction; enhanced gas recovery; methane hydrates; CCS (pre / post combustion, storage)</li><li>Limits: still finite (discoveries declining since the 1980s); rising energy and financial cost of unconventional sources; environmental impacts (aquifers, water use, tremors, land take, habitat damage); CO2 and other pollution; CCS unproven at scale and impractical for vehicles</li><li>Nuclear: new uranium extraction (polymer adsorption from seawater, phosphate mining, coal ash); molten salt reactors; plutonium breeder and thorium reactors increasing fuel use many-fold; fusion as a long-term prospect</li><li>Limits: cost overruns, decommissioning, waste, public opposition, proliferation, embodied energy of low-grade uranium; fusion not commercial</li><li>Sustainability criteria: maintaining supply without unacceptable environmental, economic or social impact; comparison with renewables and energy conservation; energy gap argument</li><li>Judgement: technologies can extend supplies and reduce some impacts but cannot make non-renewables sustainable indefinitely; role as transition / base-load alongside renewables</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>80-90%</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 183-196; AQA spec 3.3.3-3.3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HYD-01", "sub": "hydro-cycle", "subname": "The hydrosphere: hydrological cycle & unsustainable exploitation", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.2.2.1", "pages": "115-116", "url": "subtopic/hydro-cycle.html#HYD-01", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the volume of water in some reservoirs of the hydrological cycle and the annual transfer rates through them.", "text": "table 1 shows the volume of water in some reservoirs of the hydrological cycle and the annual transfer rates through them. the residence time of water in a reservoir is given by: residence time = volume in reservoir / annual transfer rate. use table 1 to calculate the residence time of water in the atmosphere. give your answer to the nearest whole day. calculate the residence time of water in the oceans. explain why the atmosphere is an important reservoir even though it holds only 0.001% of global water. explain why human changes to a small reservoir such as soil moisture are more obvious than changes to the oceans. explain why the hydrological cycle is described as being in dynamic equilibrium. 13 / 480 = 0.0271 years 0.0271 x 365 = 10 days (accept 9.9-10) 1 340 000 / 425 = 3153 years (accept 3100-3200) it has a very short residence time / very rapid transfer rate (480 x 1015 dm3 per year) so it moves large volumes of water each year, delivering precipitation to land; small reservoirs with fast transfers respond quickly to change the ocean is so large that an added or removed volume is a tiny proportion of the total the same volume is a large proportion of a small reservoir so its level / residence time changes noticeably water is constantly moving between reservoirs (evaporation, precipitation, runoff, infiltration, transpiration) but inflows and outflows for each reservoir balance so the volume in each reservoir stays constant over long periods", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HYD-01\" data-qid=\"HYD-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/hydro-cycle.html\">The hydrosphere: hydrological cycle &amp; unsustainable exploitation</a> &middot; spec 3.2.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 115-116 &middot; HYD-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the volume of water in some reservoirs of the hydrological cycle and the annual transfer rates through them.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Reservoir</th><th>Volume / x 10<sup>15</sup> dm<sup>3</sup></th><th>Annual transfer (inflow = outflow) / x 10<sup>15</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> yr<sup>-1</sup></th><th>Percentage of global water / %</th></tr><tr><td>Oceans</td><td>1 340 000</td><td>425</td><td>97</td></tr><tr><td>Ice caps and glaciers</td><td>24 000</td><td>3</td><td>1.7</td></tr><tr><td>Groundwater</td><td>23 000</td><td>12</td><td>1.7</td></tr><tr><td>Lakes and rivers</td><td>180</td><td>40</td><td>0.013</td></tr><tr><td>Soil moisture</td><td>17</td><td>80</td><td>0.001</td></tr><tr><td>Atmosphere</td><td>13</td><td>480</td><td>0.001</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>The residence time of water in a reservoir is given by: residence time = volume in reservoir / annual transfer rate. Use Table 1 to calculate the residence time of water in the atmosphere. Give your answer to the nearest whole day.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Calculate the residence time of water in the oceans.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain why the atmosphere is an important reservoir even though it holds only 0.001% of global water.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Explain why human changes to a small reservoir such as soil moisture are more obvious than changes to the oceans.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.5</span>Explain why the hydrological cycle is described as being in dynamic equilibrium.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>13 / 480 = 0.0271 years</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>0.0271 x 365</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 10 days (accept 9.9-10)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>1 340 000 / 425 = 3153 years (accept 3100-3200)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It has a very short residence time / very rapid transfer rate (480 x 10<sup>15</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> per year)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So it moves large volumes of water each year, delivering precipitation to land; small reservoirs with fast transfers respond quickly to change</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The ocean is so large that an added or removed volume is a tiny proportion of the total</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The same volume is a large proportion of a small reservoir so its level / residence time changes noticeably</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Water is constantly moving between reservoirs (evaporation, precipitation, runoff, infiltration, transpiration)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>But inflows and outflows for each reservoir balance so the volume in each reservoir stays constant over long periods</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 115-116; AQA spec 3.2.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HYD-02", "sub": "hydro-cycle", "subname": "The hydrosphere: hydrological cycle & unsustainable exploitation", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.2.1", "pages": "116-117", "url": "subtopic/hydro-cycle.html#HYD-02", "snippet": "Explain how each of the following human activities alters processes in the hydrological cycle.", "text": " explain how each of the following human activities alters processes in the hydrological cycle. describe two ways in which global climate change alters the hydrological cycle. explain why the impact of industrial and domestic water use on the hydrological cycle is usually local. deforestation: less interception so less evaporation from leaves; less transpiration so less water returned to the atmosphere and less precipitation downwind; more infiltration and surface runoff, higher river flows / flooding urban development: impermeable concrete / tarmac reduce infiltration and aquifer recharge; runoff is faster and greater, increasing river flooding downstream; drains carry water quickly to rivers agriculture: soil compaction by machinery / trampling reduces infiltration and soil moisture, increases runoff; irrigation increases evaporation; loss of soil biota (worms) reduces infiltration and water retention; drainage lowers water tables higher temperatures increase evaporation and change the amount, timing, type (rain vs snow) and location of precipitation more rapid melting of snow and ice changes river regimes: more flooding after rain, lower flows in dry periods changed wind patterns move humid air to different areas sea level rise / salt incursion into coastal aquifers most water abstracted is cleaned and returned to the river / sea so the main impact is on quality (pollution) not quantity effects are noticeable only where large urban centres draw on restricted local supplies (aquifers, small rivers)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HYD-02\" data-qid=\"HYD-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/hydro-cycle.html\">The hydrosphere: hydrological cycle &amp; unsustainable exploitation</a> &middot; spec 3.2.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 116-117 &middot; HYD-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain how each of the following human activities alters processes in the hydrological cycle.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Describe <b>two</b> ways in which global climate change alters the hydrological cycle.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain why the impact of industrial and domestic water use on the hydrological cycle is usually local.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Deforestation: less interception so less evaporation from leaves; less transpiration so less water returned to the atmosphere and less precipitation downwind; more infiltration and surface runoff, higher river flows / flooding</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Urban development: impermeable concrete / tarmac reduce infiltration and aquifer recharge; runoff is faster and greater, increasing river flooding downstream; drains carry water quickly to rivers</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Agriculture: soil compaction by machinery / trampling reduces infiltration and soil moisture, increases runoff; irrigation increases evaporation; loss of soil biota (worms) reduces infiltration and water retention; drainage lowers water tables</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Higher temperatures increase evaporation and change the amount, timing, type (rain vs snow) and location of precipitation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>More rapid melting of snow and ice changes river regimes: more flooding after rain, lower flows in dry periods</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Changed wind patterns move humid air to different areas</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sea level rise / salt incursion into coastal aquifers</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Most water abstracted is cleaned and returned to the river / sea so the main impact is on quality (pollution) not quantity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Effects are noticeable only where large urban centres draw on restricted local supplies (aquifers, small rivers)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 116-117; AQA spec 3.2.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HYD-03", "sub": "hydro-cycle", "subname": "The hydrosphere: hydrological cycle & unsustainable exploitation", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.2.2.1-3.2.2.2", "pages": "117-118", "url": "subtopic/hydro-cycle.html#HYD-03", "snippet": "The Aral Sea in central Asia was once the fourth largest lake in the world. From the 1960s water from the two rivers feeding it (the Amu Darya and Syr Darya) was diverted to irriga", "text": "the aral sea in central asia was once the fourth largest lake in the world. from the 1960s water from the two rivers feeding it (the amu darya and syr darya) was diverted to irrigate cotton and rice. table 1 shows changes in the lake. calculate the percentage reduction in the area of the aral sea between 1960 and 2015. explain why the salinity of the lake increased as the area fell. explain why the fish catch fell to zero, and describe two other environmental consequences of the shrinking of the lake. suggest why disagreements over the rivers continue between the countries of the region. (68 000 - 7 000) / 68 000 x 100 = 89.7% (accept 90%) inflow from the rivers was reduced (sometimes to zero) so less water entered while evaporation continued evaporation removes pure water leaving the dissolved salts behind, so they became concentrated in a smaller volume salinity rose above the range of tolerance of the freshwater fish species / osmotic stress; pollution by pesticides, fertilisers and industrial waste concentrated in the lake exposed lake bed of salt and pesticide-contaminated dust blown by wind onto farmland and settlements (health problems, soil salinisation) loss of wetland habitats and wildlife; local climate more extreme (loss of the moderating effect of the water body) loss of livelihoods; conflict between the countries sharing the rivers upstream abstraction for irrigation reduces the flow available downstream upstream hep dams hold water in summer for winter generation, reducing summer irrigation water downstream; growing populations increase demand", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HYD-03\" data-qid=\"HYD-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/hydro-cycle.html\">The hydrosphere: hydrological cycle &amp; unsustainable exploitation</a> &middot; spec 3.2.2.1-3.2.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 117-118 &middot; HYD-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>The Aral Sea in central Asia was once the fourth largest lake in the world. From the 1960s water from the two rivers feeding it (the Amu Darya and Syr Darya) was diverted to irrigate cotton and rice. Table 1 shows changes in the lake.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Year</th><th>1960</th><th>1980</th><th>2000</th><th>2015</th></tr><tr><td>Lake area / km<sup>2</sup></td><td>68 000</td><td>52 000</td><td>24 000</td><td>7 000</td></tr><tr><td>Salinity / g dm<sup>-3</sup></td><td>10</td><td>18</td><td>45</td><td>over 100</td></tr><tr><td>Commercial fish catch / thousand tonnes</td><td>40</td><td>10</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Calculate the percentage reduction in the area of the Aral Sea between 1960 and 2015.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain why the salinity of the lake increased as the area fell.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain why the fish catch fell to zero, and describe <b>two</b> other environmental consequences of the shrinking of the lake.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Suggest why disagreements over the rivers continue between the countries of the region.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(68 000 - 7 000) / 68 000 x 100</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 89.7% (accept 90%)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Inflow from the rivers was reduced (sometimes to zero) so less water entered while evaporation continued</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Evaporation removes pure water leaving the dissolved salts behind, so they became concentrated in a smaller volume</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Salinity rose above the range of tolerance of the freshwater fish species / osmotic stress; pollution by pesticides, fertilisers and industrial waste concentrated in the lake</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Exposed lake bed of salt and pesticide-contaminated dust blown by wind onto farmland and settlements (health problems, soil salinisation)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Loss of wetland habitats and wildlife; local climate more extreme (loss of the moderating effect of the water body)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Loss of livelihoods; conflict between the countries sharing the rivers</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Upstream abstraction for irrigation reduces the flow available downstream</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Upstream HEP dams hold water in summer for winter generation, reducing summer irrigation water downstream; growing populations increase demand</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 117-118; AQA spec 3.2.2.1-3.2.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HYD-04", "sub": "hydro-cycle", "subname": "The hydrosphere: hydrological cycle & unsustainable exploitation", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.2.2.1-3.2.2.2", "pages": "119-121", "url": "subtopic/hydro-cycle.html#HYD-04", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the depth of the water table below the ground surface in an aquifer beneath a coastal farming region between 1970 and 2020.", "text": "figure 1 shows the depth of the water table below the ground surface in an aquifer beneath a coastal farming region between 1970 and 2020. calculate the mean rate at which the water table fell between 1970 and 2020. explain why the water table is falling. explain the features a rock must have to form an aquifer. describe four consequences of over-exploiting this coastal aquifer. (44 - 8) / 50 = 0.72 m yr-1 abstraction (for irrigation) exceeds the rate of natural recharge by infiltration / percolation of precipitation the dynamic equilibrium between inflow and outflow has been upset so the stored volume declines porosity: a high proportion of the rock volume is space that can hold water (chalk, limestone, sandstone) permeability: the spaces are interconnected and large enough for water to flow through (clay is porous but not permeable) impermeable rock (granite, clay) beneath to stop water escaping, and permeable rock / soil above to allow recharge salt water incursion: seawater flows into the aquifer to replace the fresh water, making it unusable for irrigation (osmotic dehydration of crops) subsidence: emptied pore spaces no longer support the rock so it compacts, damaging buildings and pipes (eg mexico city) drying up of groundwater-fed rivers, lakes and wetlands as the water table falls below their beds ecological impacts: plants needing high water availability die; wetland animals lost; food chain effects reduced supplies: abstraction may suddenly have to stop after years of hidden decline", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HYD-04\" data-qid=\"HYD-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/hydro-cycle.html\">The hydrosphere: hydrological cycle &amp; unsustainable exploitation</a> &middot; spec 3.2.2.1-3.2.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 119-121 &middot; HYD-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the depth of the water table below the ground surface in an aquifer beneath a coastal farming region between 1970 and 2020.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,228.0 156.0,207.0 252.0,170.2 348.0,128.2 444.0,80.9 540.0,38.9\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"228.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"156.0\" cy=\"207.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"252.0\" cy=\"170.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"348.0\" cy=\"128.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"444.0\" cy=\"80.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"38.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1970</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1980</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">9.52</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1990</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">19</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2000</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">28.6</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2010</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">38.1</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2020</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">47.6</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Year</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Depth to water table / m</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Calculate the mean rate at which the water table fell between 1970 and 2020.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain why the water table is falling.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain the features a rock must have to form an aquifer.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Describe <b>four</b> consequences of over-exploiting this coastal aquifer.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(44 - 8) / 50 = 0.72 m yr<sup>-1</sup></span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Abstraction (for irrigation) exceeds the rate of natural recharge by infiltration / percolation of precipitation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The dynamic equilibrium between inflow and outflow has been upset so the stored volume declines</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Porosity: a high proportion of the rock volume is space that can hold water (chalk, limestone, sandstone)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Permeability: the spaces are interconnected and large enough for water to flow through (clay is porous but not permeable)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Impermeable rock (granite, clay) beneath to stop water escaping, and permeable rock / soil above to allow recharge</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Salt water incursion: seawater flows into the aquifer to replace the fresh water, making it unusable for irrigation (osmotic dehydration of crops)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Subsidence: emptied pore spaces no longer support the rock so it compacts, damaging buildings and pipes (eg Mexico City)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Drying up of groundwater-fed rivers, lakes and wetlands as the water table falls below their beds</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ecological impacts: plants needing high water availability die; wetland animals lost; food chain effects</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduced supplies: abstraction may suddenly have to stop after years of hidden decline</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 119-121; AQA spec 3.2.2.1-3.2.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HYD-05", "sub": "hydro-cycle", "subname": "The hydrosphere: hydrological cycle & unsustainable exploitation", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.2.1-3.2.2.2", "pages": "119-121", "url": "subtopic/hydro-cycle.html#HYD-05", "snippet": "Explain why abstraction from an aquifer containing 'fossil' (palaeo) water, such as the High Plains / Ogallala Aquifer in the USA, cannot be sustainable.", "text": " explain why abstraction from an aquifer containing 'fossil' (palaeo) water, such as the high plains / ogallala aquifer in the usa, cannot be sustainable. describe how the grace satellites are used to monitor aquifer depletion and give one advantage over monitoring boreholes. describe three measures being introduced on the north china plain to reduce aquifer depletion. suggest why large-scale afforestation on the north china plain may reduce aquifer recharge even though trees reduce soil erosion. the water was recharged during or before the last ice age (10 000-20 000 years ago) when the climate was wetter current recharge is little or none so any abstraction reduces the stored volume the large volume means shortages take a long time to appear, but eventually the supply runs out; ancient water is often saline, causing soil salinisation two satellites whose orbits are affected by the force of gravity the mass of water in aquifers below changes gravity, so changes in the orbit / distance between the satellites indicate changes in groundwater mass advantage: the whole earth is surveyed every 30 days, whereas borehole water-table measurements are slow and hard to coordinate over large areas low-pressure irrigation pipes to reduce leaks growing crops that need less water than wheat / maize charging consumers by volume used (metering) inter-basin transfer of water 450 km from the yangtze trees intercept rainfall and transpire large volumes, reducing soil moisture and the water percolating down to the aquifer", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HYD-05\" data-qid=\"HYD-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/hydro-cycle.html\">The hydrosphere: hydrological cycle &amp; unsustainable exploitation</a> &middot; spec 3.2.2.1-3.2.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 119-121 &middot; HYD-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Explain why abstraction from an aquifer containing 'fossil' (palaeo) water, such as the High Plains / Ogallala Aquifer in the USA, cannot be sustainable.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Describe how the GRACE satellites are used to monitor aquifer depletion and give <b>one</b> advantage over monitoring boreholes.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Describe <b>three</b> measures being introduced on the North China Plain to reduce aquifer depletion.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Suggest why large-scale afforestation on the North China Plain may reduce aquifer recharge even though trees reduce soil erosion.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The water was recharged during or before the last ice age (10 000-20 000 years ago) when the climate was wetter</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Current recharge is little or none so any abstraction reduces the stored volume</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The large volume means shortages take a long time to appear, but eventually the supply runs out; ancient water is often saline, causing soil salinisation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Two satellites whose orbits are affected by the force of gravity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The mass of water in aquifers below changes gravity, so changes in the orbit / distance between the satellites indicate changes in groundwater mass</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Advantage: the whole Earth is surveyed every 30 days, whereas borehole water-table measurements are slow and hard to coordinate over large areas</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Low-pressure irrigation pipes to reduce leaks</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Growing crops that need less water than wheat / maize</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Charging consumers by volume used (metering)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Inter-basin transfer of water 450 km from the Yangtze</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Trees intercept rainfall and transpire large volumes, reducing soil moisture and the water percolating down to the aquifer</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 119-121; AQA spec 3.2.2.1-3.2.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HYD-06", "sub": "hydro-cycle", "subname": "The hydrosphere: hydrological cycle & unsustainable exploitation", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.2.1", "pages": "117", "url": "subtopic/hydro-cycle.html#HYD-06", "snippet": "A dam and reservoir are to be built on a river to supply water and regulate flow.", "text": "a dam and reservoir are to be built on a river to supply water and regulate flow. explain three effects the reservoir may have on wildlife in and around the river. explain why decomposition of flooded vegetation in a new reservoir contributes to climate change. explain how a river-regulation reservoir reduces both flooding and water shortages downstream. habitat change: flooding destroys the previous terrestrial / river habitats but creates a wetland which may be valuable (wetlands are uncommon) wildlife barrier: the dam blocks migrating fish (salmon, sturgeon) and prevents recolonisation along the river changed river regime downstream: regulated flow removes low-flow periods needed by species such as river turtles nesting on sandbanks and high flows that wash sediment from gravel spawning beds sedimentation: sediment settles in the reservoir instead of fertilising the floodplain and building river banks / coastlines downstream, increasing erosion microclimate: water's high heat capacity reduces temperature fluctuations; higher wind speeds; more evaporation, humidity, cloud and precipitation downwind vegetation / dead organic matter under water decays anaerobically releasing methane, a powerful greenhouse gas after heavy rain surplus water is stored, reducing peak flow / flooding downstream in dry periods the dam releases water to maintain river levels / supplies", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HYD-06\" data-qid=\"HYD-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/hydro-cycle.html\">The hydrosphere: hydrological cycle &amp; unsustainable exploitation</a> &middot; spec 3.2.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 117 &middot; HYD-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A dam and reservoir are to be built on a river to supply water and regulate flow.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Explain <b>three</b> effects the reservoir may have on wildlife in and around the river.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain why decomposition of flooded vegetation in a new reservoir contributes to climate change.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain how a river-regulation reservoir reduces both flooding and water shortages downstream.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 6 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Habitat change: flooding destroys the previous terrestrial / river habitats but creates a wetland which may be valuable (wetlands are uncommon)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Wildlife barrier: the dam blocks migrating fish (salmon, sturgeon) and prevents recolonisation along the river</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Changed river regime downstream: regulated flow removes low-flow periods needed by species such as river turtles nesting on sandbanks and high flows that wash sediment from gravel spawning beds</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Sedimentation: sediment settles in the reservoir instead of fertilising the floodplain and building river banks / coastlines downstream, increasing erosion</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Microclimate: water's high heat capacity reduces temperature fluctuations; higher wind speeds; more evaporation, humidity, cloud and precipitation downwind</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Vegetation / dead organic matter under water decays anaerobically</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Releasing methane, a powerful greenhouse gas</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>After heavy rain surplus water is stored, reducing peak flow / flooding downstream</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In dry periods the dam releases water to maintain river levels / supplies</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 117; AQA spec 3.2.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MIN-01", "sub": "minerals", "subname": "Mineral resources (the lithosphere)", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.3.2", "pages": "132-134", "url": "subtopic/minerals.html#MIN-01", "snippet": "Describe how hydrothermal deposition produces exploitable concentrations of metal ores.", "text": " describe how hydrothermal deposition produces exploitable concentrations of metal ores. explain how proterozoic marine sediments produced the world's major iron ore deposits. explain how alluvial (physical sediment) processes separate and concentrate minerals such as gold and tin ore. give two examples of mineral deposits formed by biological sedimentation and state their origin. igneous intrusions (batholiths) of magma cool within the crust, heating groundwater to produce superheated, pressurised water the hot water dissolves minerals from the surrounding rock and the mineral-rich solutions travel along fissures / cracks away from the batholith as the solutions cool the dissolved minerals crystallise in order of solubility (least soluble first) - fractional crystallisation so different metals are separated and concentrated in veins, eg tin, copper, lead, silver, gold, arsenic (sw england batholith) dissolved iron compounds in ancient seas were oxidised by the oxygen released by early photosynthetic organisms (2.5-1.8 billion years ago) iron oxides are insoluble so they were deposited as sediments (haematite, magnetite) flowing water carries solid particles; the ability to carry them depends on the water velocity and the density of the solids as velocity falls, dense minerals (gold, tin ore) are deposited first while lighter material is carried further, concentrating the heavy minerals in particular places limestone / chalk from the shells of marine organisms coal from terrestrial vegetation crude oil and natural gas from marine organisms (plankton)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MIN-01\" data-qid=\"MIN-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/minerals.html\">Mineral resources (the lithosphere)</a> &middot; spec 3.2.3.2 &middot; Genn pp. 132-134 &middot; MIN-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Describe how hydrothermal deposition produces exploitable concentrations of metal ores.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain how Proterozoic marine sediments produced the world's major iron ore deposits.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain how alluvial (physical sediment) processes separate and concentrate minerals such as gold and tin ore.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Give <b>two</b> examples of mineral deposits formed by biological sedimentation and state their origin.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Igneous intrusions (batholiths) of magma cool within the crust, heating groundwater to produce superheated, pressurised water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The hot water dissolves minerals from the surrounding rock and the mineral-rich solutions travel along fissures / cracks away from the batholith</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>As the solutions cool the dissolved minerals crystallise in order of solubility (least soluble first) - fractional crystallisation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So different metals are separated and concentrated in veins, eg tin, copper, lead, silver, gold, arsenic (SW England batholith)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Dissolved iron compounds in ancient seas were oxidised by the oxygen released by early photosynthetic organisms (2.5-1.8 billion years ago)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Iron oxides are insoluble so they were deposited as sediments (haematite, magnetite)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Flowing water carries solid particles; the ability to carry them depends on the water velocity and the density of the solids</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>As velocity falls, dense minerals (gold, tin ore) are deposited first while lighter material is carried further, concentrating the heavy minerals in particular places</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Limestone / chalk from the shells of marine organisms</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Coal from terrestrial vegetation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Crude oil and natural gas from marine organisms (plankton)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 132-134; AQA spec 3.2.3.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MIN-02", "sub": "minerals", "subname": "Mineral resources (the lithosphere)", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.2.3.3", "pages": "135-136", "url": "subtopic/minerals.html#MIN-02", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the mass of copper contained in the Earth's crust at different ore purities, illustrating Lasky's principle.", "text": "figure 1 shows the mass of copper contained in the earth's crust at different ore purities, illustrating lasky's principle. state lasky's principle. use figure 1 to calculate how many times more copper is contained in deposits of 0.25-0.5% purity than in deposits above 2.0%. explain the difference between the reserves and the resource of a mineral. explain, using lasky's principle, why the ability to exploit slightly lower grade ores greatly increases reserves. explain why the main problem for future mineral supplies is not the quantity that exists in the crust. as the (linear) purity / grade of a mineral deposit decreases, the amount of the mineral present increases exponentially / logarithmically 2400 / 40 = 60 times reserves: the amount that can be exploited now, economically, with existing technology (deposits above the cut-off ore grade) resource: all the material that could be exploited technically and economically now or in the future (with realistic price rises or new technology), including deep, low-grade or protected deposits the resource is finite but the reserves change: they rise if prices rise or technology improves, and fall if prices fall lowering the cut-off ore grade includes deposits of lower purity in the reserves because the amount of material rises exponentially as purity falls, a small drop in coog adds a very large mass (eg 0.5-1.0% has 4x the copper of 1.0-2.0%) the total quantity (stock / resource base) is far greater than could ever be used but most is at purities too low or in locations too inaccessible for current economic exploitation the challenge is developing technologies (low-energy extraction such as bioleaching, phytomining; better exploration) that make low-grade deposits exploitable; energy needs rise rapidly as ore grade falls", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MIN-02\" data-qid=\"MIN-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/minerals.html\">Mineral resources (the lithosphere)</a> &middot; spec 3.2.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 135-136 &middot; MIN-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the mass of copper contained in the Earth's crust at different ore purities, illustrating Lasky's principle.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<rect x=\"74.4\" y=\"269.0\" width=\"67.2\" height=\"1.0\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"108.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">above 2.0</text>\n<rect x=\"170.4\" y=\"266.4\" width=\"67.2\" height=\"3.6\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"204.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1.0-2.0</text>\n<rect x=\"266.4\" y=\"255.6\" width=\"67.2\" height=\"14.4\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">0.5-1.0</text>\n<rect x=\"362.4\" y=\"212.6\" width=\"67.2\" height=\"57.4\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"396.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">0.25-0.5</text>\n<rect x=\"458.4\" y=\"42.7\" width=\"67.2\" height=\"227.3\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"492.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">0.1-0.25</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">2090</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">4180</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">6270</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">8360</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">10450</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Ore purity / % copper</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Mass of copper in deposits / million tonnes</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>State Lasky's principle.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Use Figure 1 to calculate how many times more copper is contained in deposits of 0.25-0.5% purity than in deposits above 2.0%.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain the difference between the <b>reserves</b> and the <b>resource</b> of a mineral.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Explain, using Lasky's principle, why the ability to exploit slightly lower grade ores greatly increases reserves.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.5</span>Explain why the main problem for future mineral supplies is not the quantity that exists in the crust.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>As the (linear) purity / grade of a mineral deposit decreases, the amount of the mineral present increases exponentially / logarithmically</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>2400 / 40 = 60 times</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reserves: the amount that can be exploited now, economically, with existing technology (deposits above the cut-off ore grade)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Resource: all the material that could be exploited technically and economically now or in the future (with realistic price rises or new technology), including deep, low-grade or protected deposits</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The resource is finite but the reserves change: they rise if prices rise or technology improves, and fall if prices fall</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lowering the cut-off ore grade includes deposits of lower purity in the reserves</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Because the amount of material rises exponentially as purity falls, a small drop in COOG adds a very large mass (eg 0.5-1.0% has 4x the copper of 1.0-2.0%)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The total quantity (stock / resource base) is far greater than could ever be used</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>But most is at purities too low or in locations too inaccessible for current economic exploitation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The challenge is developing technologies (low-energy extraction such as bioleaching, phytomining; better exploration) that make low-grade deposits exploitable; energy needs rise rapidly as ore grade falls</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 135-136; AQA spec 3.2.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MIN-03", "sub": "minerals", "subname": "Mineral resources (the lithosphere)", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.2.3.4", "pages": "137", "url": "subtopic/minerals.html#MIN-03", "snippet": "Table 1 shows some techniques used to locate mineral deposits.", "text": "table 1 shows some techniques used to locate mineral deposits. complete table 1 by adding the name of the technique or a description of how it works. explain why trial drilling is usually carried out only after remote sensing surveys. explain how infrared spectroscopy from satellites can help identify mineral deposits. give one advantage of portable field analysis equipment such as x-ray fluorescence. gravimetry: gravimeters detect variations in gravity caused by differences in rock density / mass (metal ores usually denser than sedimentary rock) magnetometry seismic survey: sound / shock waves from controlled explosions or a seismic vibrator; echoes recorded by geophones give the depth, density and shape of rock strata resistivity trial drilling is the most expensive technique per site remote sensing (satellite, aircraft, ground surveys) covers large areas at low cost per unit area and identifies promising sites drilling is then targeted where deposits are likely; it is the only method that produces actual rock samples for chemical analysis to confirm composition and purity (proven reserves) different minerals emit / reflect infrared at different wavelengths multispectral sensors detect these wavelengths over the whole surface (eg landsat 8 every 16 days) giving information on surface mineral composition results are available immediately in the field, saving time waiting for laboratory analysis", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MIN-03\" data-qid=\"MIN-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/minerals.html\">Mineral resources (the lithosphere)</a> &middot; spec 3.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 137 &middot; MIN-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows some techniques used to locate mineral deposits.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Complete Table 1 by adding the name of the technique or a description of how it works.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Technique</th><th>How it works</th></tr><tr><td>Gravimetry</td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Detects rocks that are more magnetic, such as magnetite iron ore or ores of tungsten and cobalt</td></tr><tr><td>Seismic survey</td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Measures how difficult it is for electricity to pass through rocks; sedimentary rocks with high water content have lower values than igneous rocks</td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain why trial drilling is usually carried out only after remote sensing surveys.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain how infrared spectroscopy from satellites can help identify mineral deposits.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Give <b>one</b> advantage of portable field analysis equipment such as X-ray fluorescence.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Gravimetry: gravimeters detect variations in gravity caused by differences in rock density / mass (metal ores usually denser than sedimentary rock)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Magnetometry</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Seismic survey: sound / shock waves from controlled explosions or a seismic vibrator; echoes recorded by geophones give the depth, density and shape of rock strata</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Resistivity</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Trial drilling is the most expensive technique per site</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Remote sensing (satellite, aircraft, ground surveys) covers large areas at low cost per unit area and identifies promising sites</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Drilling is then targeted where deposits are likely; it is the only method that produces actual rock samples for chemical analysis to confirm composition and purity (proven reserves)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Different minerals emit / reflect infrared at different wavelengths</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Multispectral sensors detect these wavelengths over the whole surface (eg Landsat 8 every 16 days) giving information on surface mineral composition</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Results are available immediately in the field, saving time waiting for laboratory analysis</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 137; AQA spec 3.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MIN-04", "sub": "minerals", "subname": "Mineral resources (the lithosphere)", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.2.3.5", "pages": "138-139", "url": "subtopic/minerals.html#MIN-04", "snippet": "Table 1 shows information about two copper deposits, X and Y, being assessed for mining. The current cut-off ore grade for copper is 0.4%.", "text": "table 1 shows information about two copper deposits, x and y, being assessed for mining. the current cut-off ore grade for copper is 0.4%. explain what is meant by the cut-off ore grade and state which deposit is currently included in the copper reserves. use table 1 to discuss the factors that affect the viability of mining each deposit. state two changes that would decrease the cut-off ore grade. the lowest ore purity that can be mined economically with existing technology deposit x (0.9% is above 0.4%; y at 0.35% is part of the resource, not the reserves) x has higher grade so less rock mined, less spoil, less energy and pollution per tonne of copper but x is deep: costs rise more than proportionally with depth (sides cannot be vertical so much more rock removed; more water inflow and pumping) x has hard overburden requiring blasting (cost, noise, dust) and high rainfall increasing drainage costs x is far from transport infrastructure so transport costs are high y is shallow with loose overburden (cheap to remove but landslide risk so gentler slopes / larger land take), near a railway, low rainfall y's grade is below the coog so it is not viable now but would become viable if copper prices rose or extraction technology improved (eg bioleaching); both sulfides risk acid mine drainage higher market value / price of the metal improved extraction technology lower energy costs", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MIN-04\" data-qid=\"MIN-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/minerals.html\">Mineral resources (the lithosphere)</a> &middot; spec 3.2.3.5 &middot; Genn pp. 138-139 &middot; MIN-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows information about two copper deposits, X and Y, being assessed for mining. The current cut-off ore grade for copper is 0.4%.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Deposit X</th><th>Deposit Y</th></tr><tr><td>Ore grade / % copper</td><td>0.9</td><td>0.35</td></tr><tr><td>Depth of deposit / m</td><td>350</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td>Overburden</td><td>Hard igneous rock</td><td>Loose glacial sediment</td></tr><tr><td>Distance to nearest railway / km</td><td>120</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>Annual rainfall / mm</td><td>2 100</td><td>500</td></tr><tr><td>Chemical form</td><td>Copper sulfide</td><td>Copper sulfide</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Explain what is meant by the <b>cut-off ore grade</b> and state which deposit is currently included in the copper reserves.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Use Table 1 to discuss the factors that affect the viability of mining each deposit.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>State <b>two</b> changes that would decrease the cut-off ore grade.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The lowest ore purity that can be mined economically with existing technology</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Deposit X (0.9% is above 0.4%; Y at 0.35% is part of the resource, not the reserves)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>X has higher grade so less rock mined, less spoil, less energy and pollution per tonne of copper</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>But X is deep: costs rise more than proportionally with depth (sides cannot be vertical so much more rock removed; more water inflow and pumping)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>X has hard overburden requiring blasting (cost, noise, dust) and high rainfall increasing drainage costs</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>X is far from transport infrastructure so transport costs are high</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Y is shallow with loose overburden (cheap to remove but landslide risk so gentler slopes / larger land take), near a railway, low rainfall</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Y's grade is below the COOG so it is not viable now but would become viable if copper prices rose or extraction technology improved (eg bioleaching); both sulfides risk acid mine drainage</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Higher market value / price of the metal</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Improved extraction technology</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lower energy costs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 138-139; AQA spec 3.2.3.5.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MIN-05", "sub": "minerals", "subname": "Mineral resources (the lithosphere)", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.3.6", "pages": "139-142", "url": "subtopic/minerals.html#MIN-05", "snippet": "Explain why mineral extraction causes more land-use conflict than most other human activities.", "text": " explain why mineral extraction causes more land-use conflict than most other human activities. describe how each of the following environmental impacts of an operating mine can be reduced. explain how spoil heaps can be made stable and describe what happened at aberfan in 1966. minerals can only be exploited where they were deposited (no choice of location), unlike housing, roads or ports land take is larger than the mine void: buildings, access roads, overburden and spoil heaps, buffer zones; open-cast mines take far more land than deep mines dust: water sprays on roads / blasting areas make particles heavier and clump so they settle noise: baffle mounds / embankments absorb and deflect; blasting at set, predictable daytime hours; quieter machinery turbid water: sedimentation lagoons where water stands until solids settle before discharge leachate: collect drainage water and pass through crushed limestone to neutralise acid and immobilise toxic metals amenity: landscaping and tree planting to screen the mine; restoration plans agreed as a condition of planning permission landscaping to reduce gradients, compaction, drainage to prevent waterlogging, adding soil / nutrients and planting vegetation whose roots bind the surface at aberfan a waterlogged coal-mine spoil heap became unstable and slid into the village killing 144 people, mainly children in the primary school", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MIN-05\" data-qid=\"MIN-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/minerals.html\">Mineral resources (the lithosphere)</a> &middot; spec 3.2.3.6 &middot; Genn pp. 139-142 &middot; MIN-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Explain why mineral extraction causes more land-use conflict than most other human activities.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Describe how each of the following environmental impacts of an operating mine can be reduced.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain how spoil heaps can be made stable and describe what happened at Aberfan in 1966.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Minerals can only be exploited where they were deposited (no choice of location), unlike housing, roads or ports</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Land take is larger than the mine void: buildings, access roads, overburden and spoil heaps, buffer zones; open-cast mines take far more land than deep mines</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Dust: water sprays on roads / blasting areas make particles heavier and clump so they settle</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Noise: baffle mounds / embankments absorb and deflect; blasting at set, predictable daytime hours; quieter machinery</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Turbid water: sedimentation lagoons where water stands until solids settle before discharge</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Leachate: collect drainage water and pass through crushed limestone to neutralise acid and immobilise toxic metals</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Amenity: landscaping and tree planting to screen the mine; restoration plans agreed as a condition of planning permission</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Landscaping to reduce gradients, compaction, drainage to prevent waterlogging, adding soil / nutrients and planting vegetation whose roots bind the surface</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>At Aberfan a waterlogged coal-mine spoil heap became unstable and slid into the village</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Killing 144 people, mainly children in the primary school</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 139-142; AQA spec 3.2.3.6.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MIN-06", "sub": "minerals", "subname": "Mineral resources (the lithosphere)", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 5, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.3.6", "pages": "140, 142", "url": "subtopic/minerals.html#MIN-06", "snippet": "A sand and gravel pit near a town is due to close. Three options for the site are proposed: a wetland nature reserve, housing, or return to farmland.", "text": "a sand and gravel pit near a town is due to close. three options for the site are proposed: a wetland nature reserve, housing, or return to farmland. explain why flooded sand and gravel pits are often developed as wetland nature reserves. explain the conditions that would need to be met for the site to be used for housing or farmland. explain why moving the wildlife from a site before mining begins is rarely completely successful. the void fills with water naturally; wetlands are uncommon / valuable habitats in most regions the new habitat may have greater wildlife value than the farmland lost; low cost compared with filling and levelling housing: ground must be stable (no subsidence), no toxic metal wastes (otherwise industrial use is safer) farmland: landscape not too steep or uneven, no toxic materials, sufficiently fertile soil (topsoil replaced) species that cannot be caught / found, or whose habitat requirements are unknown or hard to recreate, are lost; transplanted plants often fail", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MIN-06\" data-qid=\"MIN-06\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/minerals.html\">Mineral resources (the lithosphere)</a> &middot; spec 3.2.3.6 &middot; Genn pp. 140, 142 &middot; MIN-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A sand and gravel pit near a town is due to close. Three options for the site are proposed: a wetland nature reserve, housing, or return to farmland.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Explain why flooded sand and gravel pits are often developed as wetland nature reserves.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain the conditions that would need to be met for the site to be used for housing or farmland.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain why moving the wildlife from a site before mining begins is rarely completely successful.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The void fills with water naturally; wetlands are uncommon / valuable habitats in most regions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The new habitat may have greater wildlife value than the farmland lost; low cost compared with filling and levelling</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Housing: ground must be stable (no subsidence), no toxic metal wastes (otherwise industrial use is safer)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Farmland: landscape not too steep or uneven, no toxic materials, sufficiently fertile soil (topsoil replaced)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Species that cannot be caught / found, or whose habitat requirements are unknown or hard to recreate, are lost; transplanted plants often fail</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 140, 142; AQA spec 3.2.3.6.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MIN-07", "sub": "minerals", "subname": "Mineral resources (the lithosphere)", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.3.7", "pages": "143-145", "url": "subtopic/minerals.html#MIN-07", "snippet": "Describe how bioleaching is used to extract copper from low-grade ores or spoil heaps, and explain why it may be more sustainable than smelting.", "text": " describe how bioleaching is used to extract copper from low-grade ores or spoil heaps, and explain why it may be more sustainable than smelting. describe the process of phytomining. explain how polymer adsorption may provide a low-energy source of uranium in the future. acidophilic bacteria (eg acidithiobacillus) oxidise sulfide ores, producing sulfuric acid which dissolves the metal ions leachate (rainwater / recirculated solution) percolating through the spoil heap is collected; recirculation increases the metal concentration copper is recovered from solution by electrolysis or by displacement with scrap iron (iron is more reactive so copper is deposited as solid metal) smelting needs chemical reduction at high temperatures; energy needed rises rapidly as ore grade falls, so low-grade ores are uneconomic bioleaching works at low temperatures with low energy inputs, so it can exploit low-grade deposits and wastes (increasing reserves) - mimics natural low-temperature processes plants (hyperaccumulators) absorb metal ions from soil / water and concentrate them in their leaves the vegetation is harvested and incinerated the concentrated metal in the ash is dissolved in acid and separated by electrolysis; also decontaminates polluted sites uranium ions dissolved in seawater adsorb onto the surface of synthetic or natural polymers (lignin from wood, chitin from shrimp shells) the polymers are collected and the uranium recovered, avoiding mining / high-energy extraction", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MIN-07\" data-qid=\"MIN-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/minerals.html\">Mineral resources (the lithosphere)</a> &middot; spec 3.2.3.7 &middot; Genn pp. 143-145 &middot; MIN-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Describe how bioleaching is used to extract copper from low-grade ores or spoil heaps, and explain why it may be more sustainable than smelting.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Describe the process of phytomining.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Explain how polymer adsorption may provide a low-energy source of uranium in the future.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Acidophilic bacteria (eg Acidithiobacillus) oxidise sulfide ores, producing sulfuric acid which dissolves the metal ions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Leachate (rainwater / recirculated solution) percolating through the spoil heap is collected; recirculation increases the metal concentration</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Copper is recovered from solution by electrolysis or by displacement with scrap iron (iron is more reactive so copper is deposited as solid metal)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Smelting needs chemical reduction at high temperatures; energy needed rises rapidly as ore grade falls, so low-grade ores are uneconomic</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bioleaching works at low temperatures with low energy inputs, so it can exploit low-grade deposits and wastes (increasing reserves) - mimics natural low-temperature processes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Plants (hyperaccumulators) absorb metal ions from soil / water and concentrate them in their leaves</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The vegetation is harvested and incinerated</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The concentrated metal in the ash is dissolved in acid and separated by electrolysis; also decontaminates polluted sites</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Uranium ions dissolved in seawater adsorb onto the surface of synthetic or natural polymers (lignin from wood, chitin from shrimp shells)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The polymers are collected and the uranium recovered, avoiding mining / high-energy extraction</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 143-145; AQA spec 3.2.3.7.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MIN-08", "sub": "minerals", "subname": "Mineral resources (the lithosphere)", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.3.7", "pages": "145-146", "url": "subtopic/minerals.html#MIN-08", "snippet": "Describe polymetallic (manganese) nodules and explain why they are not yet exploited on a large scale.", "text": " describe polymetallic (manganese) nodules and explain why they are not yet exploited on a large scale. explain the environmental impacts of mining polymetallic nodules. explain why rare earth metals are a supply concern and describe one new extraction technique. metal-rich lumps 5-10 cm across on the deep seabed (4000-5000 m), containing about 30% manganese plus nickel, copper, cobalt, iron, titanium exploitation would be very expensive (remotely operated collectors, pumping to the surface) / no viable technology yet international agreement on ownership of the seabed and its resources is needed collectors disturb the seabed and kill benthic organisms living on it separating nodules from sediment creates plumes that increase turbidity with unknown consequences re-deposition of sediment covers and kills filter feeders and other benthic organisms; slow recovery in the deep sea used in small amounts in many essential technologies (smartphones, hybrid vehicles, wind turbines, fibre optics, leds) exploitable land deposits are limited and mainly in china; chemical separation is complex and produces large amounts of toxic waste bacterial adsorption: dissolved rare earth ions adsorb onto the cell surfaces of bacteria (bacillus subtilis) and are washed off at specific phs to separate them deep sea sediments contain relatively high concentrations but no viable extraction method yet", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MIN-08\" data-qid=\"MIN-08\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 8</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/minerals.html\">Mineral resources (the lithosphere)</a> &middot; spec 3.2.3.7 &middot; Genn pp. 145-146 &middot; MIN-08</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.1</span>Describe polymetallic (manganese) nodules and explain why they are not yet exploited on a large scale.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.2</span>Explain the environmental impacts of mining polymetallic nodules.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.3</span>Explain why rare earth metals are a supply concern and describe one new extraction technique.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Metal-rich lumps 5-10 cm across on the deep seabed (4000-5000 m), containing about 30% manganese plus nickel, copper, cobalt, iron, titanium</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Exploitation would be very expensive (remotely operated collectors, pumping to the surface) / no viable technology yet</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>International agreement on ownership of the seabed and its resources is needed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Collectors disturb the seabed and kill benthic organisms living on it</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Separating nodules from sediment creates plumes that increase turbidity with unknown consequences</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Re-deposition of sediment covers and kills filter feeders and other benthic organisms; slow recovery in the deep sea</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Used in small amounts in many essential technologies (smartphones, hybrid vehicles, wind turbines, fibre optics, LEDs)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Exploitable land deposits are limited and mainly in China; chemical separation is complex and produces large amounts of toxic waste</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bacterial adsorption: dissolved rare earth ions adsorb onto the cell surfaces of bacteria (Bacillus subtilis) and are washed off at specific pHs to separate them</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Deep sea sediments contain relatively high concentrations but no viable extraction method yet</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 145-146; AQA spec 3.2.3.7.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MIN-09", "sub": "minerals", "subname": "Mineral resources (the lithosphere)", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.2.3.7", "pages": "146-147", "url": "subtopic/minerals.html#MIN-09", "snippet": "Table 1 compares the energy used to produce 1 tonne of aluminium from bauxite ore with the energy used to produce 1 tonne from recycled aluminium cans.", "text": "table 1 compares the energy used to produce 1 tonne of aluminium from bauxite ore with the energy used to produce 1 tonne from recycled aluminium cans. calculate the percentage energy saving from recycling aluminium rather than extracting it from ore. give three advantages of recycling metals other than energy saving. explain four difficulties that reduce the effectiveness of recycling schemes. explain what is meant by cradle to cradle design. (210 - 12) / 210 x 100 = 94.3% (accept 94%) conserves mineral reserves / extends their lifetime reduces the environmental impacts of mining (land take, habitat loss, spoil, dust, noise, turbid water) reduces mineral processing pollution (eg red mud from bauxite refining) reduces waste disposal impacts / landfill volume less co2 / climate change identification of materials: slow, labour-intensive to determine composition where it matters (eg alloys, plastic types) separation of mixed materials: alloys cannot easily be separated so recycled aluminium cannot be used where pure metal is needed; mixed wastes reduce quality reduction in quality / contamination (eg paper mixed with food waste) transport / collection: small dispersed quantities cannot use bulk transport so costs and energy per tonne are higher (may exceed savings) lack of consumer cooperation in separating wastes; waste losses to litter and general waste; labour costs designing products so that the materials can be re-used at the end of their lives - easy separation of components and identification of materials - extending the lifetime of mineral use", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MIN-09\" data-qid=\"MIN-09\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 9</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/minerals.html\">Mineral resources (the lithosphere)</a> &middot; spec 3.2.3.7 &middot; Genn pp. 146-147 &middot; MIN-09</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 compares the energy used to produce 1 tonne of aluminium from bauxite ore with the energy used to produce 1 tonne from recycled aluminium cans.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Source</th><th>Energy used / GJ per tonne</th><th>CO<sub>2</sub> emitted / tonnes per tonne of aluminium</th></tr><tr><td>Bauxite ore (extraction, refining, smelting)</td><td>210</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>Recycled cans (collection, sorting, remelting)</td><td>12</td><td>0.7</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.1</span>Calculate the percentage energy saving from recycling aluminium rather than extracting it from ore.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.2</span>Give <b>three</b> advantages of recycling metals other than energy saving.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.3</span>Explain <b>four</b> difficulties that reduce the effectiveness of recycling schemes.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.4</span>Explain what is meant by <b>Cradle to Cradle</b> design.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(210 - 12) / 210 x 100</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 94.3% (accept 94%)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Conserves mineral reserves / extends their lifetime</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduces the environmental impacts of mining (land take, habitat loss, spoil, dust, noise, turbid water)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduces mineral processing pollution (eg red mud from bauxite refining)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduces waste disposal impacts / landfill volume</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Less CO2 / climate change</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Identification of materials: slow, labour-intensive to determine composition where it matters (eg alloys, plastic types)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Separation of mixed materials: alloys cannot easily be separated so recycled aluminium cannot be used where pure metal is needed; mixed wastes reduce quality</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduction in quality / contamination (eg paper mixed with food waste)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Transport / collection: small dispersed quantities cannot use bulk transport so costs and energy per tonne are higher (may exceed savings)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lack of consumer cooperation in separating wastes; waste losses to litter and general waste; labour costs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Designing products so that the materials can be re-used at the end of their lives - easy separation of components and identification of materials - extending the lifetime of mineral use</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 146-147; AQA spec 3.2.3.7.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "MIN-10", "sub": "minerals", "subname": "Mineral resources (the lithosphere)", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.2.3", "pages": "130-147", "url": "subtopic/minerals.html#MIN-10", "snippet": "As high-grade mineral deposits become depleted, discuss the strategies that could be used to secure future supplies of metals.", "text": " as high-grade mineral deposits become depleted, discuss the strategies that could be used to secure future supplies of metals. state what is meant by the stock (resource base) of a mineral. context: lasky's principle - vast quantities exist at low grade; reserves vs resource; coog affected by price, technology, energy costs improved exploration: higher resolution multispectral satellite sensors, ir spectroscopy, gravimetry, magnetometry, seismic, resistivity, portable field analysis, targeted trial drilling mechanisation allowing deeper underground mines and larger open-cast mines low-energy extraction of low-grade ores: bioleaching with acidophilic bacteria and fungi, phytomining, iron displacement, bacterial adsorption of rare earths, polymer adsorption from seawater previously inaccessible deposits: polymetallic nodules, deep sea sediments, antarctica (currently protected) - environmental and legal issues extending use: recycling (advantages, difficulties), cradle to cradle design, product lifetime extension, substitution of scarce materials, circular economy evaluation: environmental impacts vs benefits; energy costs; economics; which strategies are most sustainable; conclusion all of the material that exists in the lithosphere, including that which will never be technologically or economically exploitable", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"MIN-10\" data-qid=\"MIN-10\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 10</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/minerals.html\">Mineral resources (the lithosphere)</a> &middot; spec 3.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 130-147 &middot; MIN-10</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">10.1</span>As high-grade mineral deposits become depleted, discuss the strategies that could be used to secure future supplies of metals.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">10.2</span>State what is meant by the <b>stock</b> (resource base) of a mineral.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>10.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Context: Lasky's principle - vast quantities exist at low grade; reserves vs resource; COOG affected by price, technology, energy costs</li><li>Improved exploration: higher resolution multispectral satellite sensors, IR spectroscopy, gravimetry, magnetometry, seismic, resistivity, portable field analysis, targeted trial drilling</li><li>Mechanisation allowing deeper underground mines and larger open-cast mines</li><li>Low-energy extraction of low-grade ores: bioleaching with acidophilic bacteria and fungi, phytomining, iron displacement, bacterial adsorption of rare earths, polymer adsorption from seawater</li><li>Previously inaccessible deposits: polymetallic nodules, deep sea sediments, Antarctica (currently protected) - environmental and legal issues</li><li>Extending use: recycling (advantages, difficulties), Cradle to Cradle design, product lifetime extension, substitution of scarce materials, circular economy</li><li>Evaluation: environmental impacts vs benefits; energy costs; economics; which strategies are most sustainable; conclusion</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>10.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>All of the material that exists in the lithosphere, including that which will never be technologically or economically exploitable</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 130-147; AQA spec 3.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "NOISE-01", "sub": "noise", "subname": "Noise pollution", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.13", "pages": "286-287", "url": "subtopic/noise.html#NOISE-01", "snippet": "Table 1 shows typical noise levels of some sounds.", "text": "table 1 shows typical noise levels of some sounds. explain why a logarithmic scale is used to measure sound. use table 1 to calculate how many times greater the sound intensity of a chain saw is than that of a busy road. a machine produces 85 db. a second identical machine is switched on next to it. state the new noise level and explain your answer. explain what is meant by the statement that 0 db does not mean there is no sound. state the frequency range of hearing for a typical young person and explain why the db(a) scale is used for human hearing. the human ear is sensitive to a very wide range of sound pressures / powers (about 1012 range) a logarithmic scale compresses this into a convenient range of numbers: each 10 db increase represents a ten-fold increase in sound intensity difference = 30 db = 3 x 10 db = 103 = 1000 times 88 db doubling the sound intensity increases the level by 3 db (logarithmic scale) 0 db is set as the quietest sound that can be heard (at 1000 hz) - the threshold of human hearing; quieter sounds exist but cannot be heard 20 to 20 000 hz db(a) weights sounds between about 1000 and 4000 hz more heavily because the nerve cells / hair cells in the cochlea are more sensitive to these frequencies so they are heard more easily / cause more annoyance; range narrows with age or noise damage", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"NOISE-01\" data-qid=\"NOISE-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/noise.html\">Noise pollution</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.13 &middot; Genn pp. 286-287 &middot; NOISE-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows typical noise levels of some sounds.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Sound</th><th>Noise level / dB</th></tr><tr><td>Threshold of human hearing (at 1000 Hz)</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Leaves rustling</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>Library</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td>Normal conversation</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>Busy road</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>Chain saw</td><td>110</td></tr><tr><td>Jet engine at 30 m</td><td>140</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Explain why a logarithmic scale is used to measure sound.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Use Table 1 to calculate how many times greater the sound intensity of a chain saw is than that of a busy road.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>A machine produces 85 dB. A second identical machine is switched on next to it. State the new noise level and explain your answer.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Explain what is meant by the statement that 0 dB does not mean there is no sound.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.5</span>State the frequency range of hearing for a typical young person and explain why the dB(A) scale is used for human hearing.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The human ear is sensitive to a very wide range of sound pressures / powers (about 10<sup>12</sup> range)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A logarithmic scale compresses this into a convenient range of numbers: each 10 dB increase represents a ten-fold increase in sound intensity</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Difference = 30 dB = 3 x 10 dB</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 10<sup>3</sup> = 1000 times</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>88 dB</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Doubling the sound intensity increases the level by 3 dB (logarithmic scale)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>0 dB is set as the quietest sound that can be heard (at 1000 Hz) - the threshold of human hearing; quieter sounds exist but cannot be heard</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>20 to 20 000 Hz</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>dB(A) weights sounds between about 1000 and 4000 Hz more heavily</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Because the nerve cells / hair cells in the cochlea are more sensitive to these frequencies so they are heard more easily / cause more annoyance; range narrows with age or noise damage</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 286-287; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.13.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "NOISE-02", "sub": "noise", "subname": "Noise pollution", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.2.13", "pages": "287", "url": "subtopic/noise.html#NOISE-02", "snippet": "Describe three effects of noise on human health other than hearing damage.", "text": " describe three effects of noise on human health other than hearing damage. explain how noise can damage non-living objects. explain how noise can reduce the breeding success of birds. explain why military sonar is a threat to cetaceans. stress-related illness: ulcers, high blood pressure, heart disease behavioural changes: irritability, aggression, lack of concentration leading to accidents communication problems / educational underperformance (eg schools under flight paths) sleep disturbance acoustic fatigue: objects exposed to sound at their natural resonant frequency vibrate, causing stress cracking / structural failure (eg early jet engine cowlings) vibration damage: repetitive vibration from heavy vehicles damages buildings, bridges, underground pipes shock impacts: the force of a sudden very loud noise (sonic boom) breaks windows / dislodges masonry birds that sing to establish territories / attract mates cannot be heard against background noise birds scared by sudden noise leave nests so predators take eggs / chicks; cliff-nesting seabirds knock eggs or chicks into the sea whales / dolphins use hearing / echolocation to find food and communicate; loud sonar causes hearing damage if close to the source behavioural changes / disorientation possibly linked to mass strandings; reduced feeding success", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"NOISE-02\" data-qid=\"NOISE-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/noise.html\">Noise pollution</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.13 &middot; Genn pp. 287 &middot; NOISE-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Describe <b>three</b> effects of noise on human health other than hearing damage.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain how noise can damage non-living objects.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain how noise can reduce the breeding success of birds.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Explain why military sonar is a threat to cetaceans.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stress-related illness: ulcers, high blood pressure, heart disease</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Behavioural changes: irritability, aggression, lack of concentration leading to accidents</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Communication problems / educational underperformance (eg schools under flight paths)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sleep disturbance</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Acoustic fatigue: objects exposed to sound at their natural resonant frequency vibrate, causing stress cracking / structural failure (eg early jet engine cowlings)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Vibration damage: repetitive vibration from heavy vehicles damages buildings, bridges, underground pipes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Shock impacts: the force of a sudden very loud noise (sonic boom) breaks windows / dislodges masonry</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Birds that sing to establish territories / attract mates cannot be heard against background noise</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Birds scared by sudden noise leave nests so predators take eggs / chicks; cliff-nesting seabirds knock eggs or chicks into the sea</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Whales / dolphins use hearing / echolocation to find food and communicate; loud sonar causes hearing damage if close to the source</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Behavioural changes / disorientation possibly linked to mass strandings; reduced feeding success</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 287; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.13.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "NOISE-03", "sub": "noise", "subname": "Noise pollution", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.13", "pages": "288-292", "url": "subtopic/noise.html#NOISE-03", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the bypass ratio and first year of use of jet engines used on four airliners.", "text": "table 1 shows the bypass ratio and first year of use of jet engines used on four airliners. explain how a high bypass ratio reduces engine noise. describe two other features of aircraft design that reduce noise. explain how each of the following airport operating procedures reduces noise nuisance. explain why aircraft are noisier on take-off than on landing. a turbofan at the front forces bypass air around the inner cowling / core jet the bypass air smooths the flow of the turbulent hot exhaust gases, reducing the turbulence that creates noise the higher the ratio of bypass air to jet exhaust the quieter the engine (trent 1000 quietest, olympus noisiest) chevron (serrated) nozzles mix bypass air with surrounding air more smoothly acoustic liners in the engine cowlings absorb noise hush kits fitted to older low-bypass engines fairings on undercarriage and wing-flap hinges reduce turbulence; welded rather than riveted panels lighter composite aircraft need less thrust blended wing with engines on top so the body shields the ground constant (3°) descent avoids periods of high thrust in a stepped descent and keeps aircraft higher for longer night flights (especially take-offs, which are noisier) are restricted / banned when people are sleeping and background noise is low noisier aircraft pay higher charges / use up more quota points and fines for exceeding limits, so airlines are encouraged to buy quieter aircraft more time to slow down after landing so wheel brakes can be used instead of noisy reverse thrusters engines run at higher power / maximum thrust and the steep climb angle needs full power (although the aircraft climbs quickly out of earshot)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"NOISE-03\" data-qid=\"NOISE-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/noise.html\">Noise pollution</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.13 &middot; Genn pp. 288-292 &middot; NOISE-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the bypass ratio and first year of use of jet engines used on four airliners.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Engine</th><th>Aircraft</th><th>Bypass ratio</th><th>First use</th></tr><tr><td>RR Olympus</td><td>Concorde</td><td>0 : 1</td><td>Early 1950s</td></tr><tr><td>RR Conway</td><td>Boeing 707</td><td>0.25 : 1</td><td>Late 1950s</td></tr><tr><td>P&amp;W JT9D</td><td>Boeing 747</td><td>5 : 1</td><td>1969</td></tr><tr><td>RR Trent 1000</td><td>Boeing 787</td><td>10 : 1</td><td>2011</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Explain how a high bypass ratio reduces engine noise.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Describe <b>two</b> other features of aircraft design that reduce noise.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain how each of the following airport operating procedures reduces noise nuisance.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Explain why aircraft are noisier on take-off than on landing.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A turbofan at the front forces bypass air around the inner cowling / core jet</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The bypass air smooths the flow of the turbulent hot exhaust gases, reducing the turbulence that creates noise</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The higher the ratio of bypass air to jet exhaust the quieter the engine (Trent 1000 quietest, Olympus noisiest)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Chevron (serrated) nozzles mix bypass air with surrounding air more smoothly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Acoustic liners in the engine cowlings absorb noise</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hush kits fitted to older low-bypass engines</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fairings on undercarriage and wing-flap hinges reduce turbulence; welded rather than riveted panels</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lighter composite aircraft need less thrust</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Blended wing with engines on top so the body shields the ground</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Constant (3&deg;) descent avoids periods of high thrust in a stepped descent and keeps aircraft higher for longer</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Night flights (especially take-offs, which are noisier) are restricted / banned when people are sleeping and background noise is low</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Noisier aircraft pay higher charges / use up more quota points and fines for exceeding limits, so airlines are encouraged to buy quieter aircraft</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>More time to slow down after landing so wheel brakes can be used instead of noisy reverse thrusters</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Engines run at higher power / maximum thrust and the steep climb angle needs full power (although the aircraft climbs quickly out of earshot)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 288-292; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.13.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "NOISE-04", "sub": "noise", "subname": "Noise pollution", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.13", "pages": "292-294", "url": "subtopic/noise.html#NOISE-04", "snippet": "Complete Table 1 to show one method of controlling each source of railway noise.", "text": " complete table 1 to show one method of controlling each source of railway noise. explain why road traffic noise affects more people than any other form of noise pollution. describe three ways of reducing road traffic noise reaching residents. suggest how noise from pile-driving at sea for wind turbine foundations can be reduced to protect cetaceans. wheel vibration: track polishing / grinding, sound-absorbing ballast, sound-absorbing suspension pantograph: aerodynamic fairing wheel squeal: lubrication of wheels / track on corners braking: composite-material brakes instead of cast iron roads pass through / near almost all residential areas and traffic is continuous through the day, unlike airports or railways which affect limited areas sound-absorbing road surfaces (porous asphalt / rubber-crumb asphalt) acoustic fences, embankments / baffle mounds, walls or dense tree planting traffic management for free flow (less acceleration noise); re-routing traffic away from residential areas double glazing / acoustic insulation of buildings vehicle design: better aerodynamics, engine insulation, quieter exhausts, quieter tyres air bubble curtains around the pile driver absorb noise acoustic survey to detect cetaceans first and delay work if present; increase volume gradually (soft start) so animals can leave drilling instead of pile-driving", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"NOISE-04\" data-qid=\"NOISE-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/noise.html\">Noise pollution</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.13 &middot; Genn pp. 292-294 &middot; NOISE-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Complete Table 1 to show one method of controlling each source of railway noise.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Source of railway noise</th><th>Control method</th></tr><tr><td>Wheel vibration on the track</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Pantograph turbulence</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Wheel squeal on corners</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Braking squeal</td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain why road traffic noise affects more people than any other form of noise pollution.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Describe <b>three</b> ways of reducing road traffic noise reaching residents.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Suggest how noise from pile-driving at sea for wind turbine foundations can be reduced to protect cetaceans.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wheel vibration: track polishing / grinding, sound-absorbing ballast, sound-absorbing suspension</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pantograph: aerodynamic fairing</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wheel squeal: lubrication of wheels / track on corners</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Braking: composite-material brakes instead of cast iron</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Roads pass through / near almost all residential areas and traffic is continuous through the day, unlike airports or railways which affect limited areas</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sound-absorbing road surfaces (porous asphalt / rubber-crumb asphalt)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Acoustic fences, embankments / baffle mounds, walls or dense tree planting</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Traffic management for free flow (less acceleration noise); re-routing traffic away from residential areas</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Double glazing / acoustic insulation of buildings</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Vehicle design: better aerodynamics, engine insulation, quieter exhausts, quieter tyres</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Air bubble curtains around the pile driver absorb noise</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Acoustic survey to detect cetaceans first and delay work if present; increase volume gradually (soft start) so animals can leave</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Drilling instead of pile-driving</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 292-294; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.13.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "NOISE-05", "sub": "noise", "subname": "Noise pollution", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.2.13", "pages": "293-295, 418", "url": "subtopic/noise.html#NOISE-05", "snippet": "A motor-racing circuit has residents living 500 m away. The circuit operator wants to compare two designs of acoustic barrier: an earth baffle mound and a timber acoustic fence.", "text": "a motor-racing circuit has residents living 500 m away. the circuit operator wants to compare two designs of acoustic barrier: an earth baffle mound and a timber acoustic fence. describe how you would carry out an investigation to compare the effectiveness of the two barriers. explain why readings should be taken with the same wind direction. explain the difference between the la10 and la90 measures of traffic noise. outline two ways in which the residents' houses could be designed to reduce the noise reaching occupants. use a calibrated sound meter (db(a)) at fixed distances (eg 10, 50, 100, 250, 500 m) from the track behind each barrier and at a control site with no barrier standardise the noise source: same cars / same race or a standard test noise; same time of day, wind speed / direction, weather; same height of meter above ground take repeated readings at each distance and calculate means (and standard deviation) to assess variability use a measure such as la10 or leq over a set period rather than a single peak reading compare reduction in db relative to the control at each distance; plot noise level against distance for each barrier; statistical test (t-test) to assess significance wind carries sound / affects how far it travels so different wind directions would change readings independently of the barrier la10 is the noise level exceeded for 10% of the period - a measure of the noisiest periods la90 is the level exceeded for 90% of the period - a measure of the quietest / background level double / triple glazing acoustic insulation in walls / roof bedrooms on the side facing away from the track dense hedges / trees and earth mounds in gardens", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"NOISE-05\" data-qid=\"NOISE-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/noise.html\">Noise pollution</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.13 &middot; Genn pp. 293-295, 418 &middot; NOISE-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A motor-racing circuit has residents living 500 m away. The circuit operator wants to compare two designs of acoustic barrier: an earth baffle mound and a timber acoustic fence.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Describe how you would carry out an investigation to compare the effectiveness of the two barriers.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain why readings should be taken with the same wind direction.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain the difference between the LA10 and LA90 measures of traffic noise.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Outline <b>two</b> ways in which the residents' houses could be designed to reduce the noise reaching occupants.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Use a calibrated sound meter (dB(A)) at fixed distances (eg 10, 50, 100, 250, 500 m) from the track behind each barrier and at a control site with no barrier</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Standardise the noise source: same cars / same race or a standard test noise; same time of day, wind speed / direction, weather; same height of meter above ground</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Take repeated readings at each distance and calculate means (and standard deviation) to assess variability</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Use a measure such as LA10 or Leq over a set period rather than a single peak reading</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Compare reduction in dB relative to the control at each distance; plot noise level against distance for each barrier; statistical test (t-test) to assess significance</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wind carries sound / affects how far it travels so different wind directions would change readings independently of the barrier</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>LA10 is the noise level exceeded for 10% of the period - a measure of the noisiest periods</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>LA90 is the level exceeded for 90% of the period - a measure of the quietest / background level</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Double / triple glazing</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Acoustic insulation in walls / roof</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bedrooms on the side facing away from the track</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Dense hedges / trees and earth mounds in gardens</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 293-295, 418; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.13.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "NOISE-06", "sub": "noise", "subname": "Noise pollution", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.13", "pages": "286-295", "url": "subtopic/noise.html#NOISE-06", "snippet": "Using named examples, explain how the sources of noise pollution can be controlled by changes to design, operation and location.", "text": " using named examples, explain how the sources of noise pollution can be controlled by changes to design, operation and location. state what is meant by the leq 57 db contour used around uk airports. principles: db is logarithmic (10 db = 10x; 3 db = 2x) so small reductions in db are large reductions in intensity; effects on humans (hearing, stress, behaviour) and wildlife (breeding birds, bats, cetaceans) aircraft design: high bypass ratio engines, chevron nozzles, hush kits, acoustic liners, fairings, composites, blended wing aircraft operation: take-off angle, flight paths avoiding urban areas, constant descent, reduced reverse thrust, night restrictions, chapter noise limits, fines and noise charges, quota count airport design / location: away from population centres, taxi and engine-test areas away from housing, acoustic walls, baffle mounds, land-use restrictions, free acoustic insulation railway: track polishing, sound-absorbing ballast and suspension, pantograph fairings, wheel lubrication, composite brakes road: porous / rubber asphalt, acoustic fences, embankments, tree planting, aerodynamics, engine insulation, traffic management and routing, double glazing industrial: silencers on compressors, pressing instead of stamping, nylon rollers, drilling instead of pile-driving, bubble curtains, timing of blasting, acoustic mats / curtains, remote operation, ear protection domestic: acoustic absorbers in appliances, volume limiters, selection of quieter appliances, ear defenders evaluation: which approaches are most effective / cost-effective; conflicts (airports near cities for convenience); legislation and monitoring (leq 57 db contours) needed to enforce the area within which the average (equivalent continuous) sound level between 07:00 and 23:00 in summer exceeds 57 db - taken as the onset of significant community annoyance", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"NOISE-06\" data-qid=\"NOISE-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/noise.html\">Noise pollution</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.13 &middot; Genn pp. 286-295 &middot; NOISE-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Using named examples, explain how the sources of noise pollution can be controlled by changes to design, operation and location.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>State what is meant by the <b>Leq 57 dB</b> contour used around UK airports.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Principles: dB is logarithmic (10 dB = 10x; 3 dB = 2x) so small reductions in dB are large reductions in intensity; effects on humans (hearing, stress, behaviour) and wildlife (breeding birds, bats, cetaceans)</li><li>Aircraft design: high bypass ratio engines, chevron nozzles, hush kits, acoustic liners, fairings, composites, blended wing</li><li>Aircraft operation: take-off angle, flight paths avoiding urban areas, constant descent, reduced reverse thrust, night restrictions, Chapter noise limits, fines and noise charges, quota count</li><li>Airport design / location: away from population centres, taxi and engine-test areas away from housing, acoustic walls, baffle mounds, land-use restrictions, free acoustic insulation</li><li>Railway: track polishing, sound-absorbing ballast and suspension, pantograph fairings, wheel lubrication, composite brakes</li><li>Road: porous / rubber asphalt, acoustic fences, embankments, tree planting, aerodynamics, engine insulation, traffic management and routing, double glazing</li><li>Industrial: silencers on compressors, pressing instead of stamping, nylon rollers, drilling instead of pile-driving, bubble curtains, timing of blasting, acoustic mats / curtains, remote operation, ear protection</li><li>Domestic: acoustic absorbers in appliances, volume limiters, selection of quieter appliances, ear defenders</li><li>Evaluation: which approaches are most effective / cost-effective; conflicts (airports near cities for convenience); legislation and monitoring (Leq 57 dB contours) needed to enforce</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The area within which the average (equivalent continuous) sound level between 07:00 and 23:00 in summer exceeds 57 dB - taken as the onset of significant community annoyance</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 286-295; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.13.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "NUT-01", "sub": "nutrient-pollution", "subname": "Nutrient pollution, sewage treatment & acid mine drainage", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.9", "pages": "266-268", "url": "subtopic/nutrient-pollution.html#NUT-01", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the dissolved oxygen concentration in a shallow lowland lake over 48 hours in late summer. The lake receives runoff from arable farmland and effluent from a sewage w", "text": "figure 1 shows the dissolved oxygen concentration in a shallow lowland lake over 48 hours in late summer. the lake receives runoff from arable farmland and effluent from a sewage works. describe the pattern shown in figure 1. explain the causes of the pattern shown in figure 1. explain why fish kills in eutrophic lakes usually happen at night or at the end of summer. give two other harmful effects of algal blooms. dissolved oxygen fluctuates daily / 24-hour cycle: highest in the afternoon (about 13-13.5 mg dm-3 at 16 and 40 h) and lowest at night / early morning (2.5-3.5 mg dm-3 at 4 and 28 h) the night-time minimum is lower on the second night (2.5 vs 3.5) nitrates (from fertiliser runoff) and phosphates (from sewage effluent) cause eutrophication: rapid growth of algae / cyanobacteria (algal bloom) during the day photosynthesis by the dense algae releases oxygen so concentrations rise (above the normal saturation level) at night there is no photosynthesis but respiration by algae / animals / bacteria continues, using oxygen aerobic bacterial decomposition of dead algae / shaded macrophytes uses oxygen; warm summer water holds less oxygen and decay is rapid at night no photosynthesis replaces the oxygen used in respiration / decomposition so concentrations fall below the minimum fish can tolerate at the end of summer large amounts of dead vegetation decay rapidly in warm water causing severe deoxygenation cyanobacteria release toxins harmful to livestock, pets and people floating algae shade submerged / rooted macrophytes which die, disrupting food webs algae are an unreliable food source (die off rapidly) so food webs are poor blocked filters / unpleasant taste and smell in water supplies", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"NUT-01\" data-qid=\"NUT-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/nutrient-pollution.html\">Nutrient pollution, sewage treatment &amp; acid mine drainage</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.9 &middot; Genn pp. 266-268 &middot; NUT-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the dissolved oxygen concentration in a shallow lowland lake over 48 hours in late summer. The lake receives runoff from arable farmland and effluent from a sewage works.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,167.3 100.0,210.1 140.0,192.9 180.0,81.6 220.0,38.8 260.0,115.9 300.0,175.8 340.0,227.2 380.0,204.9 420.0,90.2 460.0,47.4 500.0,124.5 540.0,184.4\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"167.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"100.0\" cy=\"210.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"140.0\" cy=\"192.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"180.0\" cy=\"81.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"220.0\" cy=\"38.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"260.0\" cy=\"115.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"175.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"340.0\" cy=\"227.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"380.0\" cy=\"204.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"420.0\" cy=\"90.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"460.0\" cy=\"47.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"500.0\" cy=\"124.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"184.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">9.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">2.92</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">19.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">5.84</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">28.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">8.76</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">38.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">11.7</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">48</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">14.6</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Time / hours</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Dissolved oxygen / mg/dm³</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Describe the pattern shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain the causes of the pattern shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain why fish kills in eutrophic lakes usually happen at night or at the end of summer.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Give <b>two</b> other harmful effects of algal blooms.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Dissolved oxygen fluctuates daily / 24-hour cycle: highest in the afternoon (about 13-13.5 mg dm<sup>-3</sup> at 16 and 40 h) and lowest at night / early morning (2.5-3.5 mg dm<sup>-3</sup> at 4 and 28 h)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The night-time minimum is lower on the second night (2.5 vs 3.5)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nitrates (from fertiliser runoff) and phosphates (from sewage effluent) cause eutrophication: rapid growth of algae / cyanobacteria (algal bloom)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>During the day photosynthesis by the dense algae releases oxygen so concentrations rise (above the normal saturation level)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>At night there is no photosynthesis but respiration by algae / animals / bacteria continues, using oxygen</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Aerobic bacterial decomposition of dead algae / shaded macrophytes uses oxygen; warm summer water holds less oxygen and decay is rapid</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>At night no photosynthesis replaces the oxygen used in respiration / decomposition so concentrations fall below the minimum fish can tolerate</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>At the end of summer large amounts of dead vegetation decay rapidly in warm water causing severe deoxygenation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cyanobacteria release toxins harmful to livestock, pets and people</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Floating algae shade submerged / rooted macrophytes which die, disrupting food webs</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Algae are an unreliable food source (die off rapidly) so food webs are poor</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Blocked filters / unpleasant taste and smell in water supplies</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 266-268; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.9.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "NUT-02", "sub": "nutrient-pollution", "subname": "Nutrient pollution, sewage treatment & acid mine drainage", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.2.9", "pages": "267-268", "url": "subtopic/nutrient-pollution.html#NUT-02", "snippet": "Explain why nitrates in drinking water can cause blue baby syndrome (methaemoglobinaemia).", "text": " explain why nitrates in drinking water can cause blue baby syndrome (methaemoglobinaemia). explain why the link between nitrates in drinking water and stomach cancer is uncertain. describe three ways farmers in a nitrate vulnerable zone can reduce nitrate leaching into aquifers. explain how phosphates are removed from sewage effluent and from lakes polluted in the past. bacteria in the gut convert nitrates to nitrites nitrites react with haemoglobin so it cannot carry oxygen infants are most at risk because they have more of the gut bacteria and their haemoglobin reacts faster; usually from shallow wells receiving arable drainage nitrites may be converted in the gut to nitrosamines which are carcinogenic but most research has been on rats / the evidence in humans is not conclusive; nitrites are also ingested in processed food so the source cannot be isolated reduce use of nitrate fertilisers / match application to crop need avoid ploughing / applying fertiliser at times of heavy rain (autumn / winter) use low-solubility / slow-release fertilisers rather than soluble rapid-release ones store manure / slurry where it cannot leach or wash into rivers grow crops with lower nitrogen requirements (eg triticale instead of wheat) grow legumes with nitrogen-fixing bacteria instead of relying on artificial fertiliser iron(iii) sulfate is added to effluent: phosphates are precipitated as insoluble iron phosphate (a fine sediment that can be used as fertiliser) lakes: dredging phosphate-rich sediments / adding iron(iii) sulfate to reduce phosphate solubility (eg the norfolk broads)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"NUT-02\" data-qid=\"NUT-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/nutrient-pollution.html\">Nutrient pollution, sewage treatment &amp; acid mine drainage</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.9 &middot; Genn pp. 267-268 &middot; NUT-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain why nitrates in drinking water can cause <b>blue baby syndrome</b> (methaemoglobinaemia).\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain why the link between nitrates in drinking water and stomach cancer is uncertain.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Describe <b>three</b> ways farmers in a Nitrate Vulnerable Zone can reduce nitrate leaching into aquifers.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Explain how phosphates are removed from sewage effluent and from lakes polluted in the past.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bacteria in the gut convert nitrates to nitrites</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nitrites react with haemoglobin so it cannot carry oxygen</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Infants are most at risk because they have more of the gut bacteria and their haemoglobin reacts faster; usually from shallow wells receiving arable drainage</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nitrites may be converted in the gut to nitrosamines which are carcinogenic</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>But most research has been on rats / the evidence in humans is not conclusive; nitrites are also ingested in processed food so the source cannot be isolated</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduce use of nitrate fertilisers / match application to crop need</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Avoid ploughing / applying fertiliser at times of heavy rain (autumn / winter)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Use low-solubility / slow-release fertilisers rather than soluble rapid-release ones</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Store manure / slurry where it cannot leach or wash into rivers</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Grow crops with lower nitrogen requirements (eg triticale instead of wheat)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Grow legumes with nitrogen-fixing bacteria instead of relying on artificial fertiliser</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Iron(III) sulfate is added to effluent: phosphates are precipitated as insoluble iron phosphate (a fine sediment that can be used as fertiliser)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lakes: dredging phosphate-rich sediments / adding iron(III) sulfate to reduce phosphate solubility (eg the Norfolk Broads)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 267-268; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.9.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "NUT-03", "sub": "nutrient-pollution", "subname": "Nutrient pollution, sewage treatment & acid mine drainage", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.9", "pages": "269-272", "url": "subtopic/nutrient-pollution.html#NUT-03", "snippet": "Figure 1 is a flow diagram of the main stages in a sewage treatment works using activated sludge treatment.", "text": "figure 1 is a flow diagram of the main stages in a sewage treatment works using activated sludge treatment. explain the purpose of screens and grit traps. explain why primary sedimentation is described as the most important process. describe what happens in the aeration tank and explain why the sludge from the secondary sedimentation tank is returned to it. explain why tertiary treatment may be needed before effluent is discharged, and describe one tertiary process. screens (metal grills) trap floating / suspended non-biodegradable items (paper, plastic) which are landfilled or incinerated grit traps: the channel widens so flow slows and stones / road grit sink out because there is not enough kinetic energy to carry them the effluent stands in large tanks so the faecal / organic solids sink and are removed as sludge this removes over 95% of the organic matter from the fluid so the risk of deoxygenation of the river is greatly reduced aerobic bacteria digest / break down the remaining dissolved and suspended organic matter air (or pure oxygen) is mixed in by paddle wheels or air stones so oxygen shortage does not limit the aerobic process the suspended bacteria collected in the secondary sedimentation tank are the 'activated sludge'; returning them maintains a large bacterial population so digestion is rapid effluent from secondary treatment may still contain dissolved inorganic nutrients (phosphates, nitrates) that cause eutrophication, and some bacteria needed where the discharge site is ecologically sensitive or used by many people (bathing water, drinking water abstraction) phosphate stripping with iron(iii) sulfate; micro-strainers (very fine sieves) then uv light or chlorine to kill remaining bacteria", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"NUT-03\" data-qid=\"NUT-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/nutrient-pollution.html\">Nutrient pollution, sewage treatment &amp; acid mine drainage</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.9 &middot; Genn pp. 269-272 &middot; NUT-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 is a flow diagram of the main stages in a sewage treatment works using activated sludge treatment.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Process</th></tr><tr><td>Pre-treatment</td><td>Screens and grit traps</td></tr><tr><td>Primary treatment</td><td>Primary sedimentation</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary treatment</td><td>Aeration tank then secondary sedimentation</td></tr><tr><td>Tertiary treatment</td><td>Phosphate stripping / micro-strainers / UV</td></tr><tr><td>Sludge treatment</td><td>Anaerobic digestion</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Explain the purpose of screens and grit traps.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain why primary sedimentation is described as the most important process.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Describe what happens in the aeration tank and explain why the sludge from the secondary sedimentation tank is returned to it.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Explain why tertiary treatment may be needed before effluent is discharged, and describe <b>one</b> tertiary process.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Screens (metal grills) trap floating / suspended non-biodegradable items (paper, plastic) which are landfilled or incinerated</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Grit traps: the channel widens so flow slows and stones / road grit sink out because there is not enough kinetic energy to carry them</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The effluent stands in large tanks so the faecal / organic solids sink and are removed as sludge</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>This removes over 95% of the organic matter from the fluid so the risk of deoxygenation of the river is greatly reduced</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Aerobic bacteria digest / break down the remaining dissolved and suspended organic matter</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Air (or pure oxygen) is mixed in by paddle wheels or air stones so oxygen shortage does not limit the aerobic process</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The suspended bacteria collected in the secondary sedimentation tank are the 'activated sludge'; returning them maintains a large bacterial population so digestion is rapid</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Effluent from secondary treatment may still contain dissolved inorganic nutrients (phosphates, nitrates) that cause eutrophication, and some bacteria</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Needed where the discharge site is ecologically sensitive or used by many people (bathing water, drinking water abstraction)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Phosphate stripping with iron(III) sulfate; micro-strainers (very fine sieves) then UV light or chlorine to kill remaining bacteria</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 269-272; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.9.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "NUT-04", "sub": "nutrient-pollution", "subname": "Nutrient pollution, sewage treatment & acid mine drainage", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.2.9", "pages": "270-273", "url": "subtopic/nutrient-pollution.html#NUT-04", "snippet": "Describe the process of anaerobic sludge digestion and give two benefits of the process.", "text": " describe the process of anaerobic sludge digestion and give two benefits of the process. explain two problems of using sewage sludge as an agricultural fertiliser and how each can be reduced. explain why it is more efficient to have separate collection systems for foul sewage and for surface drainage water. sludge from primary sedimentation is digested by anaerobic microbes in a warm tank for about four weeks kills most pathogens reduces odour and volume of the sludge produces biogas (mainly methane with some co2) used as fuel to heat the digester / generate electricity digested sludge has a better c:n ratio and can be used as agricultural fertiliser heavy metals from urban wastes / road runoff may be present and absorbed by crops - reduced by adding lime to lower metal solubility remaining pathogens - destroyed by heat treatment of the sludge before use timing: spreading in the growing season covers crops; in winter vehicles compact wet soils - apply at suitable times / when soils are dry odour / public objection - digestion reduces odour foul waste is a small volume needing full treatment so smaller, cheaper treatment works can be built; surface water needs little treatment the treatment works is not affected by weather-related fluctuations in flow (storms) so storm overflows of untreated sewage are avoided", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"NUT-04\" data-qid=\"NUT-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/nutrient-pollution.html\">Nutrient pollution, sewage treatment &amp; acid mine drainage</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.9 &middot; Genn pp. 270-273 &middot; NUT-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Describe the process of anaerobic sludge digestion and give <b>two</b> benefits of the process.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain <b>two</b> problems of using sewage sludge as an agricultural fertiliser and how each can be reduced.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain why it is more efficient to have separate collection systems for foul sewage and for surface drainage water.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sludge from primary sedimentation is digested by anaerobic microbes in a warm tank for about four weeks</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Kills most pathogens</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduces odour and volume of the sludge</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Produces biogas (mainly methane with some CO2) used as fuel to heat the digester / generate electricity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Digested sludge has a better C:N ratio and can be used as agricultural fertiliser</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Heavy metals from urban wastes / road runoff may be present and absorbed by crops - reduced by adding lime to lower metal solubility</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Remaining pathogens - destroyed by heat treatment of the sludge before use</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Timing: spreading in the growing season covers crops; in winter vehicles compact wet soils - apply at suitable times / when soils are dry</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Odour / public objection - digestion reduces odour</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Foul waste is a small volume needing full treatment so smaller, cheaper treatment works can be built; surface water needs little treatment</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The treatment works is not affected by weather-related fluctuations in flow (storms) so storm overflows of untreated sewage are avoided</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 270-273; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.9.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "NUT-05", "sub": "nutrient-pollution", "subname": "Nutrient pollution, sewage treatment & acid mine drainage", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.9-3.4.3.2.10", "pages": "273-275", "url": "subtopic/nutrient-pollution.html#NUT-05", "snippet": "Table 1 shows measurements taken from a stream above and below the point where drainage water from an old metal-mine spoil heap enters it.", "text": "table 1 shows measurements taken from a stream above and below the point where drainage water from an old metal-mine spoil heap enters it. explain how the acidic drainage water is produced in the spoil heap. use table 1 to explain why the number of invertebrate taxa falls so sharply 50 m downstream, even though dissolved oxygen is unchanged. suggest why the ph and zinc concentration have partly recovered 2 km downstream. describe how acid mine drainage can be controlled. spoil contains residual sulfide ores (eg iron pyrites, zinc sulfide) these react with oxygen in the air spaces between the waste particles (oxidation) producing metal oxides and oxides of sulfur which dissolve in drainage water to form sulfuric acid the low ph (3.4) directly damages exposed cells / denatures proteins and dissolves calcium-based exoskeletons low ph increases the solubility of zinc (0.02 to 6.5 mg dm-3) which is toxic (inhibits enzymes) only a few tolerant taxa survive; the sensitive mayfly larvae (indicator of clean water) are eliminated; dissolved oxygen is not the limiting factor here dilution by tributaries / more water neutralisation by alkaline rocks (limestone) in the stream bed; zinc precipitates / adsorbs onto sediments as ph rises collect the drainage water and pass it through crushed limestone / add lime to neutralise the acid higher ph also reduces the solubility of toxic metals so they precipitate and can be removed", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"NUT-05\" data-qid=\"NUT-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/nutrient-pollution.html\">Nutrient pollution, sewage treatment &amp; acid mine drainage</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.9-3.4.3.2.10 &middot; Genn pp. 273-275 &middot; NUT-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows measurements taken from a stream above and below the point where drainage water from an old metal-mine spoil heap enters it.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Site</th><th>pH</th><th>Dissolved zinc / mg dm<sup>-3</sup></th><th>Dissolved oxygen / % saturation</th><th>Number of invertebrate taxa</th><th>Mayfly larvae present?</th></tr><tr><td>Upstream</td><td>7.1</td><td>0.02</td><td>96</td><td>18</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>50 m downstream</td><td>3.4</td><td>6.5</td><td>94</td><td>3</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>2 km downstream</td><td>5.9</td><td>1.1</td><td>95</td><td>9</td><td>No</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Explain how the acidic drainage water is produced in the spoil heap.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Use Table 1 to explain why the number of invertebrate taxa falls so sharply 50 m downstream, even though dissolved oxygen is unchanged.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Suggest why the pH and zinc concentration have partly recovered 2 km downstream.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Describe how acid mine drainage can be controlled.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Spoil contains residual sulfide ores (eg iron pyrites, zinc sulfide)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>These react with oxygen in the air spaces between the waste particles (oxidation)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Producing metal oxides and oxides of sulfur which dissolve in drainage water to form sulfuric acid</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The low pH (3.4) directly damages exposed cells / denatures proteins and dissolves calcium-based exoskeletons</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Low pH increases the solubility of zinc (0.02 to 6.5 mg dm<sup>-3</sup>) which is toxic (inhibits enzymes)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Only a few tolerant taxa survive; the sensitive mayfly larvae (indicator of clean water) are eliminated; dissolved oxygen is not the limiting factor here</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Dilution by tributaries / more water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Neutralisation by alkaline rocks (limestone) in the stream bed; zinc precipitates / adsorbs onto sediments as pH rises</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Collect the drainage water and pass it through crushed limestone / add lime to neutralise the acid</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Higher pH also reduces the solubility of toxic metals so they precipitate and can be removed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 273-275; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.9-3.4.3.2.10.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "NUT-06", "sub": "nutrient-pollution", "subname": "Nutrient pollution, sewage treatment & acid mine drainage", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.2.9", "pages": "273-275", "url": "subtopic/nutrient-pollution.html#NUT-06", "snippet": "Explain why organic pollution of rivers is usually monitored by measuring Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD) rather than the concentrations of the pollutants themselves.", "text": " explain why organic pollution of rivers is usually monitored by measuring biochemical oxygen demand (bod) rather than the concentrations of the pollutants themselves. explain why a coliform count is used to assess the health risk from sewage contamination rather than testing for pathogens such as typhoid. give three characteristics of species that make them useful in a biotic index. give one advantage and one disadvantage of using a biotic index rather than chemical monitoring. many different organic substances may be present, each with a different potential to cause deoxygenation, so it is difficult to measure or forecast them individually bod measures the effect (amount of oxygen used by microbes decomposing the organic matter) under standardised conditions (eg 5 days, 20 °c, dark) it gives a single measure directly related to the risk of deoxygenation e. coli is a common gut bacterium so its presence confirms faecal contamination and its number indicates how serious the contamination is pathogens are not always present so their absence would not prove the water is uncontaminated different species have different sensitivities to pollution easy to identify easy to find / normally present / usually common widely distributed advantage: assesses the effect of pollution over a period of time (recent pollution events are detected even if the pollutant has gone); no expensive equipment needed disadvantage: identification needs skill; does not identify which pollutant is present so further chemical tests are required; species may be absent for other reasons", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"NUT-06\" data-qid=\"NUT-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/nutrient-pollution.html\">Nutrient pollution, sewage treatment &amp; acid mine drainage</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.9 &middot; Genn pp. 273-275 &middot; NUT-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Explain why organic pollution of rivers is usually monitored by measuring Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD) rather than the concentrations of the pollutants themselves.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain why a coliform count is used to assess the health risk from sewage contamination rather than testing for pathogens such as typhoid.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Give <b>three</b> characteristics of species that make them useful in a biotic index.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Give <b>one</b> advantage and <b>one</b> disadvantage of using a biotic index rather than chemical monitoring.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Many different organic substances may be present, each with a different potential to cause deoxygenation, so it is difficult to measure or forecast them individually</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>BOD measures the effect (amount of oxygen used by microbes decomposing the organic matter) under standardised conditions (eg 5 days, 20 &deg;C, dark)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It gives a single measure directly related to the risk of deoxygenation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>E. coli is a common gut bacterium so its presence confirms faecal contamination and its number indicates how serious the contamination is</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pathogens are not always present so their absence would not prove the water is uncontaminated</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Different species have different sensitivities to pollution</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Easy to identify</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Easy to find / normally present / usually common</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Widely distributed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Advantage: assesses the effect of pollution over a period of time (recent pollution events are detected even if the pollutant has gone); no expensive equipment needed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Disadvantage: identification needs skill; does not identify which pollutant is present so further chemical tests are required; species may be absent for other reasons</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 273-275; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.9.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "NUT-07", "sub": "nutrient-pollution", "subname": "Nutrient pollution, sewage treatment & acid mine drainage", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.9", "pages": "266-272", "url": "subtopic/nutrient-pollution.html#NUT-07", "snippet": "Explain how organic wastes and inorganic nutrients cause water pollution and evaluate the effectiveness of the methods used to control them.", "text": " explain how organic wastes and inorganic nutrients cause water pollution and evaluate the effectiveness of the methods used to control them. give one reason why algae cause eutrophication problems more quickly than rooted plants when nutrients are added to a lake. organic nutrients (sewage, manure, silage effluent, paper mill / tannery / food processing effluent) are food for aerobic microbes whose respiration deoxygenates the water, killing fish and invertebrates; bacterial growths cover the bed, blocking light pathogens in sewage (cholera, typhoid, dysentery) spread via contaminated water; decay of organic matter releases inorganic nutrients inorganic nutrients: nitrates (fertiliser / manure runoff, leaching) and phosphates (sewage effluent, detergents, eroded soil) cause cultural eutrophication: algal blooms, shading of macrophytes, cyanobacterial toxins, night-time and end-of-summer deoxygenation human health: nitrites and methaemoglobinaemia; possible carcinogen via nitrosamines control of organic wastes: sewage treatment (pre-treatment, primary sedimentation removing >95% organic matter, aeration / activated sludge or trickling filters, anaerobic digestion of sludge with biogas) control of nutrients: tertiary phosphate stripping with iron(iii) sulfate, denitrification in filter beds, micro-strainers / uv; nitrate vulnerable zones, fertiliser timing / amount, low-solubility fertilisers, legumes, manure management; dredging sediments evaluation: sewage treatment has virtually eliminated deoxygenation and waterborne disease in developed countries but storm overflows and combined sewers still discharge untreated sewage; tertiary treatment is expensive so only used at sensitive sites; diffuse agricultural nitrate is hard to control and aquifers respond slowly; phosphate legacy in sediments judgement on point-source (sewage) control being more successful than diffuse-source (agriculture) control algae / cyanobacteria absorb nutrients directly from the water (not the sediments) so they use dissolved nutrients more easily and grow rapidly", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"NUT-07\" data-qid=\"NUT-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/nutrient-pollution.html\">Nutrient pollution, sewage treatment &amp; acid mine drainage</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.9 &middot; Genn pp. 266-272 &middot; NUT-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Explain how organic wastes and inorganic nutrients cause water pollution and evaluate the effectiveness of the methods used to control them.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Give <b>one</b> reason why algae cause eutrophication problems more quickly than rooted plants when nutrients are added to a lake.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Organic nutrients (sewage, manure, silage effluent, paper mill / tannery / food processing effluent) are food for aerobic microbes whose respiration deoxygenates the water, killing fish and invertebrates; bacterial growths cover the bed, blocking light</li><li>Pathogens in sewage (cholera, typhoid, dysentery) spread via contaminated water; decay of organic matter releases inorganic nutrients</li><li>Inorganic nutrients: nitrates (fertiliser / manure runoff, leaching) and phosphates (sewage effluent, detergents, eroded soil) cause cultural eutrophication: algal blooms, shading of macrophytes, cyanobacterial toxins, night-time and end-of-summer deoxygenation</li><li>Human health: nitrites and methaemoglobinaemia; possible carcinogen via nitrosamines</li><li>Control of organic wastes: sewage treatment (pre-treatment, primary sedimentation removing &gt;95% organic matter, aeration / activated sludge or trickling filters, anaerobic digestion of sludge with biogas)</li><li>Control of nutrients: tertiary phosphate stripping with iron(III) sulfate, denitrification in filter beds, micro-strainers / UV; Nitrate Vulnerable Zones, fertiliser timing / amount, low-solubility fertilisers, legumes, manure management; dredging sediments</li><li>Evaluation: sewage treatment has virtually eliminated deoxygenation and waterborne disease in developed countries but storm overflows and combined sewers still discharge untreated sewage; tertiary treatment is expensive so only used at sensitive sites; diffuse agricultural nitrate is hard to control and aquifers respond slowly; phosphate legacy in sediments</li><li>Judgement on point-source (sewage) control being more successful than diffuse-source (agriculture) control</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Algae / cyanobacteria absorb nutrients directly from the water (not the sediments) so they use dissolved nutrients more easily and grow rapidly</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 266-272; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.9.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PEST-01", "sub": "pesticides", "subname": "Pesticides", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.8", "pages": "262-263", "url": "subtopic/pesticides.html#PEST-01", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows how the percentage mortality of a pest species and of a non-target bee species change with the dose of an insecticide applied to a crop.", "text": "figure 1 shows how the percentage mortality of a pest species and of a non-target bee species change with the dose of an insecticide applied to a crop. use figure 1 to estimate the ld50 of the insecticide for the pest species. define the term mdnf (maximum dose never fatal). use figure 1 to explain why it may not be possible to kill all of the pest species without harming bees. suggest how the farmer could reduce the harm to bees without reducing the dose applied. explain why a pesticide with high specificity is less likely to cause environmental damage. 25 g ha-1 (accept 24-27) the highest dose that can be applied without killing any members of the population / any individuals of the (non-target) species the mdaf (minimum dose always fatal) of the pest is about 50 g ha-1 this is higher than the mdnf of the bee (about 20 g ha-1) so a dose that kills all pests kills some bees / the two curves overlap between about 20 and 50 g ha-1 a dose of 30-40 g ha-1 would kill most pests with limited bee deaths (compromise) spray at night / when bees are not flying spray when the crop is not in flower spray on still days to reduce spray drift use a systemic pesticide / seed coating rather than spraying use smaller droplets for more even coverage specificity is the extent to which the pesticide is toxic only to the target species / range of species affected a highly specific pesticide has low toxicity to non-target species (eg bees, fish, mammals) so fewer non-target deaths / less loss of predators, pollinators fewer indirect (food chain) effects / fewer inter-species relationships lost example: pyrethroids have high insect toxicity but low mammal toxicity", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PEST-01\" data-qid=\"PEST-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pesticides.html\">Pesticides</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.8 &middot; Genn pp. 262-263 &middot; PEST-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows how the percentage mortality of a pest species and of a non-target bee species change with the dose of an insecticide applied to a crop.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,270.0 140.0,257.5 220.0,195.0 300.0,95.0 380.0,32.5 460.0,20.0 540.0,20.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"140.0\" cy=\"257.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"220.0\" cy=\"195.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"95.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"380.0\" cy=\"32.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"460.0\" cy=\"20.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"20.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<polyline points=\"60.0,270.0 140.0,270.0 220.0,270.0 300.0,260.0 380.0,220.0 460.0,132.5 540.0,45.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2f5f73\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"140.0\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"220.0\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"260.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"380.0\" cy=\"220.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"460.0\" cy=\"132.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"45.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">12</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">20</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">24</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">40</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">36</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">60</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">48</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">80</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">60</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">100</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Dose / g/ha</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Mortality / %</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"24\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#22302a\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"33\">Pest species</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"38\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"47\">Bee (non-target)</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Use Figure 1 to estimate the LD<sub>50</sub> of the insecticide for the pest species.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Define the term <b>MDNF</b> (maximum dose never fatal).\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Use Figure 1 to explain why it may not be possible to kill all of the pest species without harming bees.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Suggest how the farmer could reduce the harm to bees without reducing the dose applied.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.5</span>Explain why a pesticide with high specificity is less likely to cause environmental damage.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>25 g ha<sup>-1</sup> (accept 24-27)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The highest dose that can be applied without killing any members of the population / any individuals of the (non-target) species</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The MDAF (minimum dose always fatal) of the pest is about 50 g ha<sup>-1</sup></span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>This is higher than the MDNF of the bee (about 20 g ha<sup>-1</sup>)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So a dose that kills all pests kills some bees / the two curves overlap between about 20 and 50 g ha<sup>-1</sup></span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A dose of 30-40 g ha<sup>-1</sup> would kill most pests with limited bee deaths (compromise)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Spray at night / when bees are not flying</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Spray when the crop is not in flower</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Spray on still days to reduce spray drift</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Use a systemic pesticide / seed coating rather than spraying</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Use smaller droplets for more even coverage</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Specificity is the extent to which the pesticide is toxic only to the target species / range of species affected</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A highly specific pesticide has low toxicity to non-target species (eg bees, fish, mammals)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So fewer non-target deaths / less loss of predators, pollinators</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fewer indirect (food chain) effects / fewer inter-species relationships lost</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Example: pyrethroids have high insect toxicity but low mammal toxicity</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 262-263; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.8.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PEST-02", "sub": "pesticides", "subname": "Pesticides", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.8", "pages": "263-265", "url": "subtopic/pesticides.html#PEST-02", "snippet": "Table 1 shows some properties of four groups of insecticide.", "text": "table 1 shows some properties of four groups of insecticide. use table 1 to explain why organochlorine insecticides caused the decline of birds of prey such as the peregrine falcon in the 1950s and 1960s. organophosphates replaced organochlorines for many uses. use table 1 to give one advantage and one disadvantage of this change. explain why pyrethroids should not be used near rivers or fish farms. describe how the properties of neonicotinoids allow them to be used as a seed coating, and explain why this method of application reduces harm to non-target species. high persistence means ddt remains in the environment for years / decades, so it has time to disperse and be taken up high liposolubility means it dissolves in fat and is stored in body tissues rather than excreted (bioaccumulation) concentration increases at each trophic level as predators eat many contaminated prey (biomagnification) birds of prey are at the top of the food chain so receive the highest concentrations sub-lethal doses caused thin eggshells that broke / reduced breeding success; higher doses caused sterility or death advantage: low persistence / low liposolubility so they do not bioaccumulate or biomagnify / break down quickly disadvantage: high mammalian toxicity so farm workers / humans are at risk of acute poisoning (neurotoxin inhibiting acetylcholinesterase); chronic effects (memory loss, depression); suspected carcinogens pyrethroids are highly toxic to fish (even though mammal toxicity is low) neonicotinoids are water soluble so they are absorbed by the roots (from the soil) and translocated through the plant (systemic) they are moderately persistent so remain in the soil long enough to be absorbed seed coating avoids spraying so no spray drift / no dust onto flowers or surrounding habitats so bees / pollinators are less exposed than when the crop is sprayed", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PEST-02\" data-qid=\"PEST-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pesticides.html\">Pesticides</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.8 &middot; Genn pp. 263-265 &middot; PEST-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows some properties of four groups of insecticide.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Insecticide group</th><th>Toxicity to insects</th><th>Toxicity to mammals</th><th>Persistence</th><th>Liposolubility</th></tr><tr><td>Organochlorines (eg DDT)</td><td>High</td><td>Low</td><td>High</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Organophosphates (eg malathion)</td><td>High</td><td>High</td><td>Low</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>Pyrethroids</td><td>High</td><td>Low</td><td>Low</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>Neonicotinoids</td><td>High</td><td>Low</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Low (water soluble)</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Use Table 1 to explain why organochlorine insecticides caused the decline of birds of prey such as the peregrine falcon in the 1950s and 1960s.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Organophosphates replaced organochlorines for many uses. Use Table 1 to give <b>one</b> advantage and <b>one</b> disadvantage of this change.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain why pyrethroids should not be used near rivers or fish farms.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Describe how the properties of neonicotinoids allow them to be used as a seed coating, and explain why this method of application reduces harm to non-target species.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>High persistence means DDT remains in the environment for years / decades, so it has time to disperse and be taken up</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>High liposolubility means it dissolves in fat and is stored in body tissues rather than excreted (bioaccumulation)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Concentration increases at each trophic level as predators eat many contaminated prey (biomagnification)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Birds of prey are at the top of the food chain so receive the highest concentrations</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sub-lethal doses caused thin eggshells that broke / reduced breeding success; higher doses caused sterility or death</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Advantage: low persistence / low liposolubility so they do not bioaccumulate or biomagnify / break down quickly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Disadvantage: high mammalian toxicity so farm workers / humans are at risk of acute poisoning (neurotoxin inhibiting acetylcholinesterase); chronic effects (memory loss, depression); suspected carcinogens</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pyrethroids are highly toxic to fish (even though mammal toxicity is low)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Neonicotinoids are water soluble so they are absorbed by the roots (from the soil) and translocated through the plant (systemic)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They are moderately persistent so remain in the soil long enough to be absorbed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Seed coating avoids spraying so no spray drift / no dust onto flowers or surrounding habitats</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So bees / pollinators are less exposed than when the crop is sprayed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 263-265; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.8.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PEST-03", "sub": "pesticides", "subname": "Pesticides", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.8", "pages": "263-264, 266", "url": "subtopic/pesticides.html#PEST-03", "snippet": "A pesticide was applied to a field of oilseed rape. Table 1 shows the concentration of the pesticide in organisms sampled from a pond next to the field.", "text": "a pesticide was applied to a field of oilseed rape. table 1 shows the concentration of the pesticide in organisms sampled from a pond next to the field. calculate how many times more concentrated the pesticide is in the heron than in the pond water. give your answer to two significant figures. name the process shown by the data in table 1. explain the two properties of the pesticide that cause the pattern shown in table 1. the pesticide was probably an organochlorine. suggest why the use of organochlorines is now banned or heavily restricted in most countries, even though ddt is still used to control malaria mosquitoes in some houses. 38.0 / 0.002 = 19 000 (19 000 times / 1.9 x 104) biomagnification (accept bioaccumulation as part of the process only if biomagnification is also implied by increase up the food chain) persistence: the pesticide is not broken down (by light, microbes, enzymes) so it remains in the environment / organisms long enough to pass along the food chain liposolubility: the pesticide dissolves in lipids / fat so it is stored in body tissue and not excreted in (water-based) urine each organism eats many organisms from the level below and retains the pesticide so concentration rises at each trophic level herons have long lives so accumulate over time organochlorines killed non-target insects (bees, butterflies) and top predators (birds of prey, otters) through biomagnification they persist for decades so the damage continued long after use use in houses (on walls / nets) does not enter food chains / no runoff into water, so does not biomagnify benefit of preventing malaria deaths outweighs the risk (risk:benefit)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PEST-03\" data-qid=\"PEST-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pesticides.html\">Pesticides</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.8 &middot; Genn pp. 263-264, 266 &middot; PEST-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A pesticide was applied to a field of oilseed rape. Table 1 shows the concentration of the pesticide in organisms sampled from a pond next to the field.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Organism</th><th>Trophic level</th><th>Pesticide concentration / mg kg<sup>-1</sup></th></tr><tr><td>Pond water</td><td>-</td><td>0.002</td></tr><tr><td>Phytoplankton</td><td>1</td><td>0.04</td></tr><tr><td>Water fleas (Daphnia)</td><td>2</td><td>0.5</td></tr><tr><td>Small fish</td><td>3</td><td>4.2</td></tr><tr><td>Heron</td><td>4</td><td>38.0</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Calculate how many times more concentrated the pesticide is in the heron than in the pond water. Give your answer to two significant figures.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Name the process shown by the data in Table 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain the <b>two</b> properties of the pesticide that cause the pattern shown in Table 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>The pesticide was probably an organochlorine. Suggest why the use of organochlorines is now banned or heavily restricted in most countries, even though DDT is still used to control malaria mosquitoes in some houses.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>38.0 / 0.002</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 19 000 (19 000 times / 1.9 x 10<sup>4</sup>)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Biomagnification (accept bioaccumulation as part of the process only if biomagnification is also implied by increase up the food chain)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Persistence: the pesticide is not broken down (by light, microbes, enzymes) so it remains in the environment / organisms long enough to pass along the food chain</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Liposolubility: the pesticide dissolves in lipids / fat so it is stored in body tissue and not excreted in (water-based) urine</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Each organism eats many organisms from the level below and retains the pesticide so concentration rises at each trophic level</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Herons have long lives so accumulate over time</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Organochlorines killed non-target insects (bees, butterflies) and top predators (birds of prey, otters) through biomagnification</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They persist for decades so the damage continued long after use</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Use in houses (on walls / nets) does not enter food chains / no runoff into water, so does not biomagnify</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Benefit of preventing malaria deaths outweighs the risk (risk:benefit)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 263-264, 266; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.8.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PEST-04", "sub": "pesticides", "subname": "Pesticides", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.8", "pages": "265-266", "url": "subtopic/pesticides.html#PEST-04", "snippet": "Neonicotinoids are the most widely used group of insecticides. Explain how the properties of neonicotinoids affect their impact on bee populations and describe the methods used to ", "text": " neonicotinoids are the most widely used group of insecticides. explain how the properties of neonicotinoids affect their impact on bee populations and describe the methods used to reduce this impact. state one way in which pesticide use in agriculture may be reduced by using natural processes. neonicotinoids are neurotoxins similar to nicotine; they inhibit the neurotransmitter acetylcholine high toxicity to insects but low toxicity to vertebrates because they cannot cross the vertebrate blood-brain barrier very toxic to bees: sub-lethal doses reduce navigation ability, foraging and immunity to disease; linked to colony decline water soluble and moderately persistent: remain in soil, absorbed by roots and translocated (systemic), so present in pollen and nectar persistence may allow build-up in aquifers / soil; broken down fastest in sunlight and by soil microbes synergism with some fungicides increases toxicity to bees exposure greatest when sprayed onto crops or when seed-coating dust is blown by wind control: seed coating rather than spraying; spraying when crops are not in flower / at night; still days to reduce drift restrictions on use (eu bans on outdoor use on flowering crops); use of alternative pest control (cultural, biological, integrated control) evaluation: reduced pesticide use but neonicotinoid alternatives (pyrethroids) also harm pollinators; trade-off with crop yields biological control (introduced predators / pathogens) predator habitats (hedgerows, beetle banks) crop rotation companion / barrier crops sterile male technique / pheromone traps genetic resistance", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PEST-04\" data-qid=\"PEST-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pesticides.html\">Pesticides</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.8 &middot; Genn pp. 265-266 &middot; PEST-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Neonicotinoids are the most widely used group of insecticides. Explain how the properties of neonicotinoids affect their impact on bee populations and describe the methods used to reduce this impact.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>State <b>one</b> way in which pesticide use in agriculture may be reduced by using natural processes.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Neonicotinoids are neurotoxins similar to nicotine; they inhibit the neurotransmitter acetylcholine</li><li>High toxicity to insects but low toxicity to vertebrates because they cannot cross the vertebrate blood-brain barrier</li><li>Very toxic to bees: sub-lethal doses reduce navigation ability, foraging and immunity to disease; linked to colony decline</li><li>Water soluble and moderately persistent: remain in soil, absorbed by roots and translocated (systemic), so present in pollen and nectar</li><li>Persistence may allow build-up in aquifers / soil; broken down fastest in sunlight and by soil microbes</li><li>Synergism with some fungicides increases toxicity to bees</li><li>Exposure greatest when sprayed onto crops or when seed-coating dust is blown by wind</li><li>Control: seed coating rather than spraying; spraying when crops are not in flower / at night; still days to reduce drift</li><li>Restrictions on use (EU bans on outdoor use on flowering crops); use of alternative pest control (cultural, biological, integrated control)</li><li>Evaluation: reduced pesticide use but neonicotinoid alternatives (pyrethroids) also harm pollinators; trade-off with crop yields</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 1 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 1.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Biological control (introduced predators / pathogens)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Predator habitats (hedgerows, beetle banks)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Crop rotation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Companion / barrier crops</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sterile male technique / pheromone traps</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Genetic resistance</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 265-266; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.8.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PEST-05", "sub": "pesticides", "subname": "Pesticides", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.8", "pages": "262-264", "url": "subtopic/pesticides.html#PEST-05", "snippet": "Two insecticides, X and Y, were tested on a pest insect and on a predatory ground beetle that feeds on the pest. Table 1 shows the results.", "text": "two insecticides, x and y, were tested on a pest insect and on a predatory ground beetle that feeds on the pest. table 1 shows the results. explain what is meant by ld50. using table 1, explain which insecticide is more likely to cause indirect effects on the pest population through the food chain. suggest two environmental problems that could be caused by insecticide y that are less likely with insecticide x. explain why the half-life of insecticide x makes it more likely to bioaccumulate. give two reasons why a systemic insecticide may be preferred to a contact insecticide. the dose that kills 50% of the (test) population insecticide x: ld50 for the beetle (6) is very similar to that of the pest (5) so it has low specificity / kills the predator as well loss of the predator removes natural control so the pest population may recover faster / increase (resurgence) y has a much higher ld50 for the beetle (150) so few predators killed / more specific high water solubility so it is leached into rivers / groundwater / runoff toxic to aquatic organisms downstream mobile so it can affect areas away from the field washed off the crop quickly so more frequent application needed long half-life = high persistence so it is not broken down quickly so it remains in the organism / environment long enough to build up in tissues (especially if liposoluble) absorbed and translocated within the plant so protects new growth not washed off by rain does not need to cover all leaf surfaces / less spray needed less spray drift so less harm to non-target species", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PEST-05\" data-qid=\"PEST-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pesticides.html\">Pesticides</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.8 &middot; Genn pp. 262-264 &middot; PEST-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Two insecticides, X and Y, were tested on a pest insect and on a predatory ground beetle that feeds on the pest. Table 1 shows the results.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Insecticide</th><th>LD<sub>50</sub> for pest / mg kg<sup>-1</sup></th><th>LD<sub>50</sub> for ground beetle / mg kg<sup>-1</sup></th><th>Half-life in soil / days</th><th>Solubility in water</th></tr><tr><td>X</td><td>5</td><td>6</td><td>180</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>Y</td><td>8</td><td>150</td><td>4</td><td>High</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Explain what is meant by LD<sub>50</sub>.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Using Table 1, explain which insecticide is more likely to cause indirect effects on the pest population through the food chain.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Suggest <b>two</b> environmental problems that could be caused by insecticide Y that are less likely with insecticide X.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Explain why the half-life of insecticide X makes it more likely to bioaccumulate.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.5</span>Give <b>two</b> reasons why a systemic insecticide may be preferred to a contact insecticide.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The dose that kills 50% of the (test) population</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Insecticide X: LD50 for the beetle (6) is very similar to that of the pest (5) so it has low specificity / kills the predator as well</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Loss of the predator removes natural control so the pest population may recover faster / increase (resurgence)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Y has a much higher LD50 for the beetle (150) so few predators killed / more specific</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>High water solubility so it is leached into rivers / groundwater / runoff</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Toxic to aquatic organisms downstream</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mobile so it can affect areas away from the field</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Washed off the crop quickly so more frequent application needed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Long half-life = high persistence so it is not broken down quickly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So it remains in the organism / environment long enough to build up in tissues (especially if liposoluble)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Absorbed and translocated within the plant so protects new growth</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Not washed off by rain</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Does not need to cover all leaf surfaces / less spray needed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Less spray drift so less harm to non-target species</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 262-264; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.8.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PEST-06", "sub": "pesticides", "subname": "Pesticides", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 5, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.2.8", "pages": "263-266", "url": "subtopic/pesticides.html#PEST-06", "snippet": "Define the term persistence as applied to a pollutant.", "text": " define the term persistence as applied to a pollutant. explain how the persistence of a pesticide is affected by two named environmental factors. the length of time a pollutant remains in the environment before it is broken down / degraded (chemically or biologically) light: uv light degrades many pesticides (eg neonicotinoids break down fastest in sunlight) so persistence is shorter in bright conditions temperature: higher temperatures increase the rate of chemical breakdown / microbial decomposition soil microbes / oxygen: aerobic microbial activity breaks pesticides down so persistence is shorter in well-aerated, biologically active soils ph / presence of other chemicals may increase or decrease the rate of degradation adsorption onto clay / organic matter may protect from degradation and increase persistence", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PEST-06\" data-qid=\"PEST-06\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pesticides.html\">Pesticides</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.8 &middot; Genn pp. 263-266 &middot; PEST-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Define the term <b>persistence</b> as applied to a pollutant.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain how the persistence of a pesticide is affected by <b>two</b> named environmental factors.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The length of time a pollutant remains in the environment before it is broken down / degraded (chemically or biologically)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Light: UV light degrades many pesticides (eg neonicotinoids break down fastest in sunlight) so persistence is shorter in bright conditions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Temperature: higher temperatures increase the rate of chemical breakdown / microbial decomposition</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Soil microbes / oxygen: aerobic microbial activity breaks pesticides down so persistence is shorter in well-aerated, biologically active soils</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>pH / presence of other chemicals may increase or decrease the rate of degradation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Adsorption onto clay / organic matter may protect from degradation and increase persistence</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 263-266; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.8.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PEST-07", "sub": "pesticides", "subname": "Pesticides", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.8", "pages": "262-266", "url": "subtopic/pesticides.html#PEST-07", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the number of breeding pairs of sparrowhawks in a study area in the UK between 1945 and 1985, and the years in which DDT use began and was banned.", "text": "figure 1 shows the number of breeding pairs of sparrowhawks in a study area in the uk between 1945 and 1985, and the years in which ddt use began and was banned. describe the trend shown in figure 1. calculate the percentage decrease in breeding pairs between 1950 and 1965. show your working. ddt use in the uk began in the late 1940s and was restricted from the mid 1960s. explain how the properties of ddt could account for the pattern in figure 1. suggest why the recovery after 1970 was slow. numbers fell from about 210 pairs in 1945 to a minimum of about 25 in 1965 (steepest fall 1955-1960) numbers then recovered / rose steadily after 1970, reaching about 190 by 1985 (not yet back to 1945 level) (200 - 25) / 200 x 100 = 87.5% ddt is persistent (remains for decades) and liposoluble so it bioaccumulates in prey (small birds) and biomagnifies in sparrowhawks (top predator) sub-lethal doses caused thin eggshells / reduced breeding success so the population declined even without adult deaths higher doses caused sterility / death after restrictions, environmental concentrations slowly fell (persistence explains the delay before recovery) breeding success recovered so the population rose ddt persists in soil / sediments / food chains for many years so exposure continued birds of prey are k-selected: few young per year, slow to mature so populations grow slowly other pressures (habitat loss, persecution)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PEST-07\" data-qid=\"PEST-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pesticides.html\">Pesticides</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.8 &middot; Genn pp. 262-266 &middot; PEST-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the number of breeding pairs of sparrowhawks in a study area in the UK between 1945 and 1985, and the years in which DDT use began and was banned.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,40.2 120.0,51.2 180.0,116.8 240.0,204.4 300.0,242.6 360.0,237.2 420.0,193.4 480.0,127.8 540.0,62.1\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"40.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"120.0\" cy=\"51.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"180.0\" cy=\"116.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"240.0\" cy=\"204.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"242.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"360.0\" cy=\"237.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"420.0\" cy=\"193.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"480.0\" cy=\"127.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"62.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1945</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1953</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">45.7</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1961</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">91.4</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1969</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">137</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1977</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">183</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1985</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">228</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Year</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Breeding pairs</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Describe the trend shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Calculate the percentage decrease in breeding pairs between 1950 and 1965. Show your working.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>DDT use in the UK began in the late 1940s and was restricted from the mid 1960s. Explain how the properties of DDT could account for the pattern in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>Suggest why the recovery after 1970 was slow.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Numbers fell from about 210 pairs in 1945 to a minimum of about 25 in 1965 (steepest fall 1955-1960)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Numbers then recovered / rose steadily after 1970, reaching about 190 by 1985 (not yet back to 1945 level)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(200 - 25) / 200 x 100</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 87.5%</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>DDT is persistent (remains for decades) and liposoluble so it bioaccumulates in prey (small birds) and biomagnifies in sparrowhawks (top predator)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sub-lethal doses caused thin eggshells / reduced breeding success so the population declined even without adult deaths</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Higher doses caused sterility / death</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>After restrictions, environmental concentrations slowly fell (persistence explains the delay before recovery)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Breeding success recovered so the population rose</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>DDT persists in soil / sediments / food chains for many years so exposure continued</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Birds of prey are k-selected: few young per year, slow to mature so populations grow slowly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Other pressures (habitat loss, persecution)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 262-266; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.8.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PEST-08", "sub": "pesticides", "subname": "Pesticides", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.8", "pages": "262-266", "url": "subtopic/pesticides.html#PEST-08", "snippet": "Which of the following properties would make a pesticide least likely to biomagnify? Tick one box.", "text": " which of the following properties would make a pesticide least likely to biomagnify? tick one box. explain the difference between bioaccumulation and biomagnification. explain why pesticides are described as an unusual type of pollutant. b - low liposolubility and low persistence bioaccumulation: build-up of a pollutant in the tissues of an individual organism over time because uptake is faster than excretion / breakdown biomagnification: increase in concentration of the pollutant at successive trophic levels of a food chain they are intended to be toxic (to the target species) they are deliberately released / spread into the environment (rather than being an accidental or waste release)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PEST-08\" data-qid=\"PEST-08\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 8</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pesticides.html\">Pesticides</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.8 &middot; Genn pp. 262-266 &middot; PEST-08</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.1</span>Which of the following properties would make a pesticide <b>least</b> likely to biomagnify? Tick one box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; High liposolubility and high persistence</li><li>B&nbsp; Low liposolubility and low persistence</li><li>C&nbsp; High liposolubility and low persistence</li><li>D&nbsp; Low liposolubility and high persistence</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.2</span>Explain the difference between <b>bioaccumulation</b> and <b>biomagnification</b>.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.3</span>Explain why pesticides are described as an unusual type of pollutant.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b></b>&nbsp; B - Low liposolubility and low persistence</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bioaccumulation: build-up of a pollutant in the tissues of an individual organism over time because uptake is faster than excretion / breakdown</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Biomagnification: increase in concentration of the pollutant at successive trophic levels of a food chain</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They are intended to be toxic (to the target species)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They are deliberately released / spread into the environment (rather than being an accidental or waste release)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 262-266; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.8.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PPRO-01", "sub": "pollutant-properties", "subname": "Properties of pollutants & environmental factors", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.4.1", "pages": "236-240", "url": "subtopic/pollutant-properties.html#PPRO-01", "snippet": "Table 1 shows some properties of three pollutants.", "text": "table 1 shows some properties of three pollutants. define the term environmental half-life. use table 1 to explain why ddt is more likely than nitrate ions to biomagnify in a food chain. suggest why the release of hydrogen cyanide at bhopal, india, in 1984 caused deaths very close to the source rather than over a wide area. explain how the high water solubility of nitrate ions affects the area over which they cause pollution and the concentration of the pollution. give two examples of pollutants that are forms of energy rather than substances. the time taken for half of a pollutant (released into the environment) to break down / degrade ddt is liposoluble so it is stored in fat / can pass through phospholipid cell membranes and is not excreted (nitrates are water soluble and excreted easily) ddt is persistent (long half-life) so it is not broken down before it is passed to the next trophic level so ddt bioaccumulates in each organism and becomes more concentrated at each successive trophic level the gas is denser than air so it settled close to the ground / did not rise and disperse low wind velocity (at night) meant it was not dispersed / diluted short half-life so it broke down before travelling far high solubility means nitrates are easily dispersed / leached / carried by water so they affect a larger area (eg rivers, aquifers, the sea) but dispersal dilutes the pollutant so the concentration at any one point may be lower noise / sound heat / thermal pollution ionising radiation light", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PPRO-01\" data-qid=\"PPRO-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pollutant-properties.html\">Properties of pollutants &amp; environmental factors</a> &middot; spec 3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 236-240 &middot; PPRO-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows some properties of three pollutants.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Pollutant</th><th>State at 20 &deg;C</th><th>Solubility in water</th><th>Solubility in lipids</th><th>Environmental half-life</th></tr><tr><td>DDT (insecticide)</td><td>Solid</td><td>Very low</td><td>High</td><td>About 15 years in soil</td></tr><tr><td>Nitrate ions</td><td>Dissolved ion</td><td>Very high</td><td>Very low</td><td>Not applicable (not degraded)</td></tr><tr><td>Hydrogen cyanide</td><td>Gas (denser than air)</td><td>High</td><td>Low</td><td>Hours</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Define the term <b>environmental half-life</b>.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Use Table 1 to explain why DDT is more likely than nitrate ions to biomagnify in a food chain.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Suggest why the release of hydrogen cyanide at Bhopal, India, in 1984 caused deaths very close to the source rather than over a wide area.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Explain how the high water solubility of nitrate ions affects the area over which they cause pollution and the concentration of the pollution.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.5</span>Give <b>two</b> examples of pollutants that are forms of energy rather than substances.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The time taken for half of a pollutant (released into the environment) to break down / degrade</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>DDT is liposoluble so it is stored in fat / can pass through phospholipid cell membranes and is not excreted (nitrates are water soluble and excreted easily)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>DDT is persistent (long half-life) so it is not broken down before it is passed to the next trophic level</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So DDT bioaccumulates in each organism and becomes more concentrated at each successive trophic level</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The gas is denser than air so it settled close to the ground / did not rise and disperse</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Low wind velocity (at night) meant it was not dispersed / diluted</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Short half-life so it broke down before travelling far</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>High solubility means nitrates are easily dispersed / leached / carried by water so they affect a larger area (eg rivers, aquifers, the sea)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>But dispersal dilutes the pollutant so the concentration at any one point may be lower</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Noise / sound</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Heat / thermal pollution</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ionising radiation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Light</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 236-240; AQA spec 3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PPRO-02", "sub": "pollutant-properties", "subname": "Properties of pollutants & environmental factors", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.1", "pages": "239-240", "url": "subtopic/pollutant-properties.html#PPRO-02", "snippet": "Explain the difference between a mutagen, a carcinogen and a teratogen.", "text": " explain the difference between a mutagen, a carcinogen and a teratogen. explain why a birth abnormality caused by a teratogen cannot be inherited by future generations, whereas one caused by a mutagen may be. distinguish between somatic and gonadic effects of a mutagen. explain what is meant by synergism and give an example. mutagen: an agent that changes the chemical structure / base sequence of dna (causes mutations), eg ionising radiation, uv, pcbs, cadmium, asbestos carcinogen: a mutagen that causes cancer / uncontrolled cell division producing a tumour teratogen: an agent that causes birth abnormalities by preventing normal gene expression (inhibiting proteins / enzymes) without changing dna structure, eg mercury, 2,4,5-t teratogens do not change the dna structure, so the abnormality is not in the genes and cannot be passed on mutagens change dna; if the mutation is in a gamete / egg / sperm (gonadic effect) it can be inherited somatic: mutation in a general body cell, affecting the individual only (eg cancer / tumour) gonadic: mutation in cells of the ovaries or testes / gametes / embryo, which may cause abnormalities in offspring two or more pollutants whose effects interact to produce a different (usually more serious) effect the pollutants themselves do not react to form a new pollutant (that would be a secondary pollutant) example: ozone damages leaf cuticles so sulfur dioxide can cause more damage to the exposed cells; smoke and sulfur dioxide in smogs; neonicotinoids with fungicides", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PPRO-02\" data-qid=\"PPRO-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pollutant-properties.html\">Properties of pollutants &amp; environmental factors</a> &middot; spec 3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 239-240 &middot; PPRO-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain the difference between a <b>mutagen</b>, a <b>carcinogen</b> and a <b>teratogen</b>.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain why a birth abnormality caused by a teratogen cannot be inherited by future generations, whereas one caused by a mutagen may be.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Distinguish between <b>somatic</b> and <b>gonadic</b> effects of a mutagen.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Explain what is meant by <b>synergism</b> and give an example.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mutagen: an agent that changes the chemical structure / base sequence of DNA (causes mutations), eg ionising radiation, UV, PCBs, cadmium, asbestos</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Carcinogen: a mutagen that causes cancer / uncontrolled cell division producing a tumour</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Teratogen: an agent that causes birth abnormalities by preventing normal gene expression (inhibiting proteins / enzymes) without changing DNA structure, eg mercury, 2,4,5-T</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Teratogens do not change the DNA structure, so the abnormality is not in the genes and cannot be passed on</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mutagens change DNA; if the mutation is in a gamete / egg / sperm (gonadic effect) it can be inherited</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Somatic: mutation in a general body cell, affecting the individual only (eg cancer / tumour)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Gonadic: mutation in cells of the ovaries or testes / gametes / embryo, which may cause abnormalities in offspring</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Two or more pollutants whose effects interact to produce a different (usually more serious) effect</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The pollutants themselves do not react to form a new pollutant (that would be a secondary pollutant)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Example: ozone damages leaf cuticles so sulfur dioxide can cause more damage to the exposed cells; smoke and sulfur dioxide in smogs; neonicotinoids with fungicides</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 239-240; AQA spec 3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PPRO-03", "sub": "pollutant-properties", "subname": "Properties of pollutants & environmental factors", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.4.2.2", "pages": "241-242", "url": "subtopic/pollutant-properties.html#PPRO-03", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows how air temperature changes with altitude above a city in a valley on two different nights. Line A shows normal conditions. Line B shows the conditions on a still, c", "text": "figure 1 shows how air temperature changes with altitude above a city in a valley on two different nights. line a shows normal conditions. line b shows the conditions on a still, cloudless night. name the atmospheric condition shown by line b in figure 1. use figure 1 to explain why pollutants released at ground level would disperse on night a but not on night b. explain why the conditions on night b (still, cloudless) allowed an inversion to form. give two types of pollution event that are made more severe by temperature inversions. temperature inversion on night a, temperature falls with altitude; warm effluent gases are less dense / more buoyant than the surrounding air so they rise as they rise they cool but the surrounding air is also cooler so they stay buoyant and continue to rise, dispersing and diluting on night b the air near the ground is colder / denser than the air above (inversion layer at about 400 m) pollutants cool as they rise and become denser than the warmer air above so they are trapped below the inversion layer and become concentrated cloudless sky allows infrared energy to be radiated from the ground so the ground cools rapidly the cold ground cools the air in contact with it, producing a cold dense layer near the ground low wind velocity means the air layers of different temperatures do not mix valley allows cold dense air to collect / drain into the low ground smoke smogs (eg london 1952) photochemical smogs (eg los angeles, mexico city) build-up of nox / carbon monoxide / pm10 in cities", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PPRO-03\" data-qid=\"PPRO-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pollutant-properties.html\">Properties of pollutants &amp; environmental factors</a> &middot; spec 3.4.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 241-242 &middot; PPRO-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows how air temperature changes with altitude above a city in a valley on two different nights. Line A shows normal conditions. Line B shows the conditions on a still, cloudless night.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"540.0,270.0 429.2,207.5 318.5,145.0 207.7,82.5 96.9,20.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"429.2\" cy=\"207.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"318.5\" cy=\"145.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"207.7\" cy=\"82.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"96.9\" cy=\"20.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<polyline points=\"96.9,270.0 244.6,228.3 355.4,186.7 281.5,145.0 170.8,82.5 60.0,20.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2f5f73\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"96.9\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"244.6\" cy=\"228.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"355.4\" cy=\"186.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"281.5\" cy=\"145.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"170.8\" cy=\"82.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"20.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">240</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">6.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">480</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">8.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">720</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">11.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">960</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">14</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">1.2e+03</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Temperature / &amp;deg;C</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Altitude / m</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"24\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#22302a\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"33\">A (normal)</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"38\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"47\">B (still, cloudless night)</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Name the atmospheric condition shown by line B in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Use Figure 1 to explain why pollutants released at ground level would disperse on night A but not on night B.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain why the conditions on night B (still, cloudless) allowed an inversion to form.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Give <b>two</b> types of pollution event that are made more severe by temperature inversions.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Temperature inversion</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>On night A, temperature falls with altitude; warm effluent gases are less dense / more buoyant than the surrounding air so they rise</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>As they rise they cool but the surrounding air is also cooler so they stay buoyant and continue to rise, dispersing and diluting</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>On night B the air near the ground is colder / denser than the air above (inversion layer at about 400 m)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pollutants cool as they rise and become denser than the warmer air above so they are trapped below the inversion layer and become concentrated</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cloudless sky allows infrared energy to be radiated from the ground so the ground cools rapidly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The cold ground cools the air in contact with it, producing a cold dense layer near the ground</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Low wind velocity means the air layers of different temperatures do not mix</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Valley allows cold dense air to collect / drain into the low ground</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Smoke smogs (eg London 1952)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Photochemical smogs (eg Los Angeles, Mexico City)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Build-up of NOx / carbon monoxide / PM10 in cities</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 241-242; AQA spec 3.4.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PPRO-04", "sub": "pollutant-properties", "subname": "Properties of pollutants & environmental factors", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.2.2", "pages": "240-242, 255", "url": "subtopic/pollutant-properties.html#PPRO-04", "snippet": "Explain how each of the following environmental factors affects the rate of degradation of pollutants. Give a named example of a pollutant for each factor.", "text": " explain how each of the following environmental factors affects the rate of degradation of pollutants. give a named example of a pollutant for each factor. explain how the presence of adsorbent materials such as clay can reduce the severity of pollution by heavy metals or radioactive caesium-137. explain why a fall in ph increases the harm caused by many heavy metals. temperature: most chemical / biological reactions are faster at higher temperature so degradable pollutants (eg sewage) break down faster; but faster decomposition may increase deoxygenation light: provides the activation energy for chemical reactions; photodegradation of pesticides (eg neonicotinoids break down fastest in sunlight); light drives photochemical smog reactions producing secondary pollutants oxygen: needed for aerobic bacterial decomposition of organic wastes / sewage; oxidation of sulfide ores produces sulfur dioxide / acid mine drainage; oxidation state affects the solubility of metals pollutant ions adsorb onto (the surface of) clay particles / organic matter in soil or sediment so they are immobilised and cannot be absorbed by organisms / leached into water; radiation is absorbed in the ground but they may be released later if conditions change (eg storms disturbing sediment, low ph) many heavy metals (eg lead, aluminium) are more soluble at low ph so they are mobilised / leached from soil or sediments and can be absorbed by organisms / enter water bodies", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PPRO-04\" data-qid=\"PPRO-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pollutant-properties.html\">Properties of pollutants &amp; environmental factors</a> &middot; spec 3.4.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 240-242, 255 &middot; PPRO-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Explain how each of the following environmental factors affects the rate of degradation of pollutants. Give a named example of a pollutant for each factor.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain how the presence of adsorbent materials such as clay can reduce the severity of pollution by heavy metals or radioactive caesium-137.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain why a fall in pH increases the harm caused by many heavy metals.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Temperature: most chemical / biological reactions are faster at higher temperature so degradable pollutants (eg sewage) break down faster; but faster decomposition may increase deoxygenation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Light: provides the activation energy for chemical reactions; photodegradation of pesticides (eg neonicotinoids break down fastest in sunlight); light drives photochemical smog reactions producing secondary pollutants</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Oxygen: needed for aerobic bacterial decomposition of organic wastes / sewage; oxidation of sulfide ores produces sulfur dioxide / acid mine drainage; oxidation state affects the solubility of metals</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pollutant ions adsorb onto (the surface of) clay particles / organic matter in soil or sediment</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So they are immobilised and cannot be absorbed by organisms / leached into water; radiation is absorbed in the ground</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>But they may be released later if conditions change (eg storms disturbing sediment, low pH)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Many heavy metals (eg lead, aluminium) are more soluble at low pH</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So they are mobilised / leached from soil or sediments and can be absorbed by organisms / enter water bodies</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 240-242, 255; AQA spec 3.4.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PPRO-05", "sub": "pollutant-properties", "subname": "Properties of pollutants & environmental factors", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.4.1, 3.4.2.1", "pages": "236-241, 254-255", "url": "subtopic/pollutant-properties.html#PPRO-05", "snippet": "A factory discharges a persistent, water-soluble pollutant into a river. Table 1 shows the concentration of the pollutant measured at sites along the river on a day with normal riv", "text": "a factory discharges a persistent, water-soluble pollutant into a river. table 1 shows the concentration of the pollutant measured at sites along the river on a day with normal river flow. calculate the percentage decrease in pollutant concentration between site b and site e. explain the large decrease in concentration between sites c and d. explain why the concentration changes very little between sites d and e. suggest why the pollution might be more serious if the same discharge entered a lake instead of a river. explain why the factory might be permitted to discharge the effluent into the sea near a strong current but not into the river. (4.8 - 1.4) / 4.8 x 100 = 70.8% (accept 71%) a tributary joins the river, more than doubling the flow (15 to 36 m3 s-1) so the pollutant is diluted by the extra water (dispersal), not broken down the pollutant is persistent so it is not degraded / broken down as it travels little extra water joins the river (flow 36 to 38) so little further dilution lake has a long residence time / water does not flow away so the pollutant accumulates rather than being carried away smaller volume of water / less dilution / less dispersal by currents persistent pollutant gradually becomes more concentrated (eg by evaporation) marine discharge: large volume of water and currents dilute and disperse the pollutant to acceptably low concentrations (control of emission location) river: small volume so concentrations stay high / drinking water abstraction / sensitive wildlife downstream", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PPRO-05\" data-qid=\"PPRO-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pollutant-properties.html\">Properties of pollutants &amp; environmental factors</a> &middot; spec 3.4.1, 3.4.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 236-241, 254-255 &middot; PPRO-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A factory discharges a persistent, water-soluble pollutant into a river. Table 1 shows the concentration of the pollutant measured at sites along the river on a day with normal river flow.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Site</th><th>Distance downstream of discharge / km</th><th>Pollutant concentration / mg dm<sup>-3</sup></th><th>River flow / m<sup>3</sup> s<sup>-1</sup></th></tr><tr><td>A (upstream)</td><td>-2</td><td>0.0</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>0.5</td><td>4.8</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>5</td><td>3.9</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>D (below tributary)</td><td>12</td><td>1.6</td><td>36</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>30</td><td>1.4</td><td>38</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Calculate the percentage decrease in pollutant concentration between site B and site E.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain the large decrease in concentration between sites C and D.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain why the concentration changes very little between sites D and E.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Suggest why the pollution might be more serious if the same discharge entered a lake instead of a river.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.5</span>Explain why the factory might be permitted to discharge the effluent into the sea near a strong current but not into the river.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(4.8 - 1.4) / 4.8 x 100</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 70.8% (accept 71%)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A tributary joins the river, more than doubling the flow (15 to 36 m<sup>3</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So the pollutant is diluted by the extra water (dispersal), not broken down</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The pollutant is persistent so it is not degraded / broken down as it travels</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Little extra water joins the river (flow 36 to 38) so little further dilution</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lake has a long residence time / water does not flow away so the pollutant accumulates rather than being carried away</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Smaller volume of water / less dilution / less dispersal by currents</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Persistent pollutant gradually becomes more concentrated (eg by evaporation)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Marine discharge: large volume of water and currents dilute and disperse the pollutant to acceptably low concentrations (control of emission location)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>River: small volume so concentrations stay high / drinking water abstraction / sensitive wildlife downstream</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 236-241, 254-255; AQA spec 3.4.1, 3.4.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PPRO-06", "sub": "pollutant-properties", "subname": "Properties of pollutants & environmental factors", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.4.1", "pages": "237-238", "url": "subtopic/pollutant-properties.html#PPRO-06", "snippet": "Which of the following is a secondary pollutant? Tick one box.", "text": " which of the following is a secondary pollutant? tick one box. explain the difference between a primary pollutant and a secondary pollutant, using acid rain as an example. b - tropospheric ozone (formed by reactions of nox in sunlight) primary pollutant: released directly by human activities, eg sulfur dioxide from burning coal / smelting sulfide ores; nox from high-temperature combustion secondary pollutant: produced by chemical reactions between primary pollutants, often with non-pollutants (water, oxygen, sunlight) sulfur dioxide is oxidised by ozone to sulfur trioxide which dissolves to form sulfuric acid; nox dissolve to form nitric acid the acids (sulfurous, sulfuric, nitric) are secondary pollutants; ozone itself is a secondary pollutant of nox photochemistry", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PPRO-06\" data-qid=\"PPRO-06\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pollutant-properties.html\">Properties of pollutants &amp; environmental factors</a> &middot; spec 3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 237-238 &middot; PPRO-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Which of the following is a <b>secondary</b> pollutant? Tick one box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; Sulfur dioxide from a coal-fired power station</li><li>B&nbsp; Tropospheric ozone</li><li>C&nbsp; Carbon monoxide from a car exhaust</li><li>D&nbsp; Nitrogen monoxide from a diesel engine</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain the difference between a primary pollutant and a secondary pollutant, using acid rain as an example.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b></b>&nbsp; B - Tropospheric ozone (formed by reactions of NOx in sunlight)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Primary pollutant: released directly by human activities, eg sulfur dioxide from burning coal / smelting sulfide ores; NOx from high-temperature combustion</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Secondary pollutant: produced by chemical reactions between primary pollutants, often with non-pollutants (water, oxygen, sunlight)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sulfur dioxide is oxidised by ozone to sulfur trioxide which dissolves to form sulfuric acid; NOx dissolve to form nitric acid</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The acids (sulfurous, sulfuric, nitric) are secondary pollutants; ozone itself is a secondary pollutant of NOx photochemistry</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 237-238; AQA spec 3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PPRO-07", "sub": "pollutant-properties", "subname": "Properties of pollutants & environmental factors", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.4.1", "pages": "236-240", "url": "subtopic/pollutant-properties.html#PPRO-07", "snippet": "Discuss how an understanding of the properties of pollutants can be used to predict their behaviour in the environment and to plan strategies to control them.", "text": " discuss how an understanding of the properties of pollutants can be used to predict their behaviour in the environment and to plan strategies to control them. state what is meant by bioaccumulation. state of matter: solids deposit near the source, gases disperse widely in the atmosphere; density (lead dust, hydrogen cyanide at bhopal) controls how far particles/gases travel persistence / half-life: persistent pollutants (organochlorines, cfcs, heavy metals, radioactive wastes with long half-lives) travel further, accumulate and need containment rather than dilution; non-persistent pollutants (sewage, organophosphates) can be allowed to degrade toxicity, specificity, mode of action (enzyme inhibition, neurotoxins) determine acceptable concentrations and which species are at risk; ld50 / mdaf / mdnf used to set doses solubility in water (nitrates disperse and leach - control by nitrate vulnerable zones) vs liposolubility (ddt, mercury bioaccumulate and biomagnify - control by bans / restrictions) reactivity: reactive pollutants may degrade quickly or form secondary pollutants (ozone, acid rain, pans) - controlling primary pollutants (catalytic converters, fgd) prevents secondary pollutants adsorption onto clay / sediments immobilises metals and caesium; can be used deliberately (activated carbon filters, polymer adsorption) mutagenic / carcinogenic / teratogenic action means no safe dose; exposure kept as low as reasonably achievable (alara) synergism (ozone + so2; smoke + so2) means pollutants must be considered together environmental features (temperature inversions, currents, ph, oxygen) interact with properties: critical pathway analysis uses properties + environment to predict where pollutants go and to focus monitoring evaluation: properties of new chemicals can be predicted from similar ones (precautionary principle); understanding allows prevention rather than clean-up the increase in the amount / concentration of a substance within an (individual) organism over time, because intake exceeds excretion or breakdown", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PPRO-07\" data-qid=\"PPRO-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pollutant-properties.html\">Properties of pollutants &amp; environmental factors</a> &middot; spec 3.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 236-240 &middot; PPRO-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Discuss how an understanding of the properties of pollutants can be used to predict their behaviour in the environment and to plan strategies to control them.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>State what is meant by <b>bioaccumulation</b>.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>State of matter: solids deposit near the source, gases disperse widely in the atmosphere; density (lead dust, hydrogen cyanide at Bhopal) controls how far particles/gases travel</li><li>Persistence / half-life: persistent pollutants (organochlorines, CFCs, heavy metals, radioactive wastes with long half-lives) travel further, accumulate and need containment rather than dilution; non-persistent pollutants (sewage, organophosphates) can be allowed to degrade</li><li>Toxicity, specificity, mode of action (enzyme inhibition, neurotoxins) determine acceptable concentrations and which species are at risk; LD50 / MDAF / MDNF used to set doses</li><li>Solubility in water (nitrates disperse and leach - control by nitrate vulnerable zones) vs liposolubility (DDT, mercury bioaccumulate and biomagnify - control by bans / restrictions)</li><li>Reactivity: reactive pollutants may degrade quickly or form secondary pollutants (ozone, acid rain, PANs) - controlling primary pollutants (catalytic converters, FGD) prevents secondary pollutants</li><li>Adsorption onto clay / sediments immobilises metals and caesium; can be used deliberately (activated carbon filters, polymer adsorption)</li><li>Mutagenic / carcinogenic / teratogenic action means no safe dose; exposure kept As Low As Reasonably Achievable (ALARA)</li><li>Synergism (ozone + SO2; smoke + SO2) means pollutants must be considered together</li><li>Environmental features (temperature inversions, currents, pH, oxygen) interact with properties: Critical Pathway Analysis uses properties + environment to predict where pollutants go and to focus monitoring</li><li>Evaluation: properties of new chemicals can be predicted from similar ones (precautionary principle); understanding allows prevention rather than clean-up</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The increase in the amount / concentration of a substance within an (individual) organism over time, because intake exceeds excretion or breakdown</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 236-240; AQA spec 3.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PPRO-08", "sub": "pollutant-properties", "subname": "Properties of pollutants & environmental factors", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.1, 3.4.2", "pages": "238-242", "url": "subtopic/pollutant-properties.html#PPRO-08", "snippet": "Mercury released into a lake from a factory was in the inorganic form. Fish in the lake were later found to contain high concentrations of methyl mercury, an organic form.", "text": "mercury released into a lake from a factory was in the inorganic form. fish in the lake were later found to contain high concentrations of methyl mercury, an organic form. suggest how the inorganic mercury was converted to methyl mercury. explain why methyl mercury is a more hazardous pollutant than inorganic mercury. explain why the health risk from eating fish from the lake may remain for many years after the discharges stop. suggest three factors that should be included in a critical pathway analysis of a proposed mercury discharge. inorganic mercury sank into the sediments / deep water where conditions are anaerobic / low oxygen it was chemically reduced / converted by (anaerobic) bacteria to methyl mercury methyl mercury is liposoluble so it passes through cell membranes and is stored in fat / not excreted so it bioaccumulates in organisms and biomagnifies along the food chain (eg in fish, then humans eating fish) it is a neurotoxin / teratogen causing brain damage and birth abnormalities (eg minamata) mercury is persistent / not degraded; it is an element so cannot be broken down it remains in the sediments and continues to be released / converted and taken up by the food chain; long-lived fish continue to accumulate it water current velocity and direction (dispersal) properties of the pollutant: solubility, persistence, liposolubility / potential to bioaccumulate food chain pathways to humans (fish, shellfish) / the critical group sediment type / adsorption onto clay volume of the water body / residence time / dilution sensitive locations downstream (fisheries, drinking water abstraction)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PPRO-08\" data-qid=\"PPRO-08\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 8</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/pollutant-properties.html\">Properties of pollutants &amp; environmental factors</a> &middot; spec 3.4.1, 3.4.2 &middot; Genn pp. 238-242 &middot; PPRO-08</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Mercury released into a lake from a factory was in the inorganic form. Fish in the lake were later found to contain high concentrations of methyl mercury, an organic form.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.1</span>Suggest how the inorganic mercury was converted to methyl mercury.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.2</span>Explain why methyl mercury is a more hazardous pollutant than inorganic mercury.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.3</span>Explain why the health risk from eating fish from the lake may remain for many years after the discharges stop.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.4</span>Suggest <b>three</b> factors that should be included in a Critical Pathway Analysis of a proposed mercury discharge.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Inorganic mercury sank into the sediments / deep water where conditions are anaerobic / low oxygen</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It was chemically reduced / converted by (anaerobic) bacteria to methyl mercury</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Methyl mercury is liposoluble so it passes through cell membranes and is stored in fat / not excreted</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So it bioaccumulates in organisms and biomagnifies along the food chain (eg in fish, then humans eating fish)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It is a neurotoxin / teratogen causing brain damage and birth abnormalities (eg Minamata)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mercury is persistent / not degraded; it is an element so cannot be broken down</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It remains in the sediments and continues to be released / converted and taken up by the food chain; long-lived fish continue to accumulate it</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Water current velocity and direction (dispersal)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Properties of the pollutant: solubility, persistence, liposolubility / potential to bioaccumulate</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Food chain pathways to humans (fish, shellfish) / the critical group</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sediment type / adsorption onto clay</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Volume of the water body / residence time / dilution</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sensitive locations downstream (fisheries, drinking water abstraction)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 238-242; AQA spec 3.4.1, 3.4.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RAD-01", "sub": "radiation", "subname": "Ionising radiation", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.14", "pages": "299-301", "url": "subtopic/radiation.html#RAD-01", "snippet": "Table 1 shows some radioactive isotopes, their half-lives and the type of radiation they emit.", "text": "table 1 shows some radioactive isotopes, their half-lives and the type of radiation they emit. define the term half-life. a sample of iodine-131 has an activity of 6400 bq. calculate its activity after 32 days. use table 1 to explain why isotopes with half-lives of several decades, such as caesium-137, cause the greatest concern after a nuclear accident. explain why plutonium-239 is relatively safe outside the body but very dangerous if inhaled or ingested. explain what is meant by an activation product. the time taken for half of the atoms of a radioactive isotope to decay (activity halves) 32 / 8 = 4 half-lives 6400 / 24 = 400 bq short half-life isotopes (i-131) release all their radiation quickly: dangerous but only briefly so short-term precautions (potassium iodide tablets, evacuation) are sufficient very long half-life isotopes (u-238) emit only small amounts of radiation in any period so the level of danger is low decades-long half-lives combine a high rate of radiation release with persistence for most or all of a human lifetime, so short-term protection is inadequate (land contaminated for generations) alpha particles have very low penetrating power and are absorbed by clothing / dead skin so external sources cause little exposure inside the body the alpha particles are absorbed in a very small mass of tissue so damage is concentrated (high rbe - weighting factor 20) long half-life means it continues to emit for the rest of the person's life / cannot be excreted quickly (contamination not just exposure) a previously stable nucleus that becomes radioactive after absorbing a neutron (eg in reactor structures), later decaying to emit ionising radiation", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RAD-01\" data-qid=\"RAD-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/radiation.html\">Ionising radiation</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.14 &middot; Genn pp. 299-301 &middot; RAD-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows some radioactive isotopes, their half-lives and the type of radiation they emit.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Isotope</th><th>Half-life</th><th>Main radiation</th><th>Source</th></tr><tr><td>Iodine-131</td><td>8 days</td><td>Beta / gamma</td><td>Fission in reactors</td></tr><tr><td>Strontium-90</td><td>29 years</td><td>Beta</td><td>Fission in reactors</td></tr><tr><td>Caesium-137</td><td>30 years</td><td>Beta / gamma</td><td>Fission in reactors</td></tr><tr><td>Plutonium-239</td><td>24 400 years</td><td>Alpha</td><td>Neutron bombardment of U-238</td></tr><tr><td>Uranium-238</td><td>4.5 x 10<sup>9</sup> years</td><td>Alpha</td><td>Occurs naturally in rocks</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Define the term <b>half-life</b>.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>A sample of iodine-131 has an activity of 6400 Bq. Calculate its activity after 32 days.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Use Table 1 to explain why isotopes with half-lives of several decades, such as caesium-137, cause the greatest concern after a nuclear accident.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Explain why plutonium-239 is relatively safe outside the body but very dangerous if inhaled or ingested.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.5</span>Explain what is meant by an <b>activation product</b>.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The time taken for half of the atoms of a radioactive isotope to decay (activity halves)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>32 / 8 = 4 half-lives</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>6400 / 2<sup>4</sup> = 400 Bq</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Short half-life isotopes (I-131) release all their radiation quickly: dangerous but only briefly so short-term precautions (potassium iodide tablets, evacuation) are sufficient</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Very long half-life isotopes (U-238) emit only small amounts of radiation in any period so the level of danger is low</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Decades-long half-lives combine a high rate of radiation release with persistence for most or all of a human lifetime, so short-term protection is inadequate (land contaminated for generations)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Alpha particles have very low penetrating power and are absorbed by clothing / dead skin so external sources cause little exposure</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Inside the body the alpha particles are absorbed in a very small mass of tissue so damage is concentrated (high RBE - weighting factor 20)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Long half-life means it continues to emit for the rest of the person's life / cannot be excreted quickly (contamination not just exposure)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A previously stable nucleus that becomes radioactive after absorbing a neutron (eg in reactor structures), later decaying to emit ionising radiation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 299-301; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.14.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RAD-02", "sub": "radiation", "subname": "Ionising radiation", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.14", "pages": "301, 303", "url": "subtopic/radiation.html#RAD-02", "snippet": "Explain how ionising radiation damages living cells.", "text": " explain how ionising radiation damages living cells. distinguish between acute and chronic effects of radiation exposure. distinguish between exposure and contamination. a worker receives an absorbed dose of 0.002 gy from alpha radiation. calculate the effective dose in sieverts. state what is measured by the becquerel. radiation absorbed by tissue produces free radicals (ions with unpaired electrons), mostly from water, which are highly reactive free radicals damage dna / chromosomes in the nucleus causing mutations (cancer, inherited effects) - rapidly dividing cells (skin, gut, bone marrow) most affected damage to other cell parts can stop the cell functioning or kill it; low damage may be repaired by the intact nucleus acute: effects appearing quickly after a large dose over a short time (radiation sickness, damage to bone marrow / immune system / gut, haemorrhage, rapid death) chronic: effects appearing slowly, in proportion to the dose received over a long period (cancers, cataracts) exposure: absorption of ionising radiation from a source the person is close enough to contamination: physically carrying radioactive material on or in the body, so exposure continues wherever the person goes; sealed sources can expose but not contaminate sieverts = grays x radiation weighting factor (20 for alpha) 0.002 x 20 = 0.04 sv (40 msv) the activity of a source (number of decays per second)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RAD-02\" data-qid=\"RAD-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/radiation.html\">Ionising radiation</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.14 &middot; Genn pp. 301, 303 &middot; RAD-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain how ionising radiation damages living cells.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Distinguish between acute and chronic effects of radiation exposure.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Distinguish between <b>exposure</b> and <b>contamination</b>.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>A worker receives an absorbed dose of 0.002 Gy from alpha radiation. Calculate the effective dose in sieverts.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.5</span>State what is measured by the becquerel.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Radiation absorbed by tissue produces free radicals (ions with unpaired electrons), mostly from water, which are highly reactive</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Free radicals damage DNA / chromosomes in the nucleus causing mutations (cancer, inherited effects) - rapidly dividing cells (skin, gut, bone marrow) most affected</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Damage to other cell parts can stop the cell functioning or kill it; low damage may be repaired by the intact nucleus</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Acute: effects appearing quickly after a large dose over a short time (radiation sickness, damage to bone marrow / immune system / gut, haemorrhage, rapid death)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Chronic: effects appearing slowly, in proportion to the dose received over a long period (cancers, cataracts)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Exposure: absorption of ionising radiation from a source the person is close enough to</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Contamination: physically carrying radioactive material on or in the body, so exposure continues wherever the person goes; sealed sources can expose but not contaminate</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sieverts = grays x radiation weighting factor (20 for alpha)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>0.002 x 20 = 0.04 Sv (40 mSv)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The activity of a source (number of decays per second)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 301, 303; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.14.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RAD-03", "sub": "radiation", "subname": "Ionising radiation", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.14", "pages": "302-303", "url": "subtopic/radiation.html#RAD-03", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the radiation dose rate measured at different distances from a small gamma source.", "text": "figure 1 shows the radiation dose rate measured at different distances from a small gamma source. use figure 1 to show that the dose rate follows the inverse square law. calculate the dose rate at 20 m from the source. explain how each of the following reduces the exposure of workers handling radioactive sources. explain why potassium iodide tablets are issued to people near a nuclear accident. state the meaning of alara. doubling the distance from 1 m to 2 m reduces the dose rate from 400 to 100 (one quarter) distance x 10 (1 to 10 m) reduces it by 100 times (400 to 4); dose is proportional to 1/distance2 400 / 202 = 1 µsv h-1 sealed container prevents contact / contamination so exposure stops when the worker moves away; container may absorb alpha completely; remote handling barriers (lead, concrete, thick steel flask walls) absorb radiation before it reaches the worker; thickness depends on radiation type dose received is proportional to time near the source so work is organised to minimise time close to it (job rotation) dosemeters / film badges record exposure; air monitors detect alpha-emitting particles; contamination monitors on leaving check that no material is carried out stable iodine saturates the thyroid so radioactive iodine-131 released is not taken up / concentrated in the thyroid gland exposure should be as low as reasonably achievable", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RAD-03\" data-qid=\"RAD-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/radiation.html\">Ionising radiation</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.14 &middot; Genn pp. 302-303 &middot; RAD-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the radiation dose rate measured at different distances from a small gamma source.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,42.5 113.3,213.1 166.7,245.0 220.0,255.8 273.3,260.9 433.3,266.4 540.0,267.7\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"42.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"113.3\" cy=\"213.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"166.7\" cy=\"245.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"220.0\" cy=\"255.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"273.3\" cy=\"260.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"433.3\" cy=\"266.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"267.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">87.9</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">176</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">6.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">264</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">8.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">352</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">10</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">440</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Distance from source / m</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Dose rate / &amp;micro;Sv/h</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Use Figure 1 to show that the dose rate follows the inverse square law.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Calculate the dose rate at 20 m from the source.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain how each of the following reduces the exposure of workers handling radioactive sources.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Explain why potassium iodide tablets are issued to people near a nuclear accident.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.5</span>State the meaning of ALARA.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Doubling the distance from 1 m to 2 m reduces the dose rate from 400 to 100 (one quarter)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Distance x 10 (1 to 10 m) reduces it by 100 times (400 to 4); dose is proportional to 1/distance<sup>2</sup></span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>400 / 20<sup>2</sup> = 1 &micro;Sv h<sup>-1</sup></span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sealed container prevents contact / contamination so exposure stops when the worker moves away; container may absorb alpha completely; remote handling</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Barriers (lead, concrete, thick steel flask walls) absorb radiation before it reaches the worker; thickness depends on radiation type</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Dose received is proportional to time near the source so work is organised to minimise time close to it (job rotation)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Dosemeters / film badges record exposure; air monitors detect alpha-emitting particles; contamination monitors on leaving check that no material is carried out</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stable iodine saturates the thyroid</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So radioactive iodine-131 released is not taken up / concentrated in the thyroid gland</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Exposure should be As Low As Reasonably Achievable</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 302-303; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.14.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RAD-04", "sub": "radiation", "subname": "Ionising radiation", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.14", "pages": "297-299", "url": "subtopic/radiation.html#RAD-04", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the average annual radiation dose received by a person in the UK from different sources.", "text": "table 1 shows the average annual radiation dose received by a person in the uk from different sources. explain why radon concentrations are high inside some houses in devon and cornwall. use table 1 to calculate the percentage of the average dose that comes from artificial (human-made) sources. suggest two reasons why the dose received by an individual may differ greatly from the average. explain why a risk:benefit analysis is needed for the use of x-rays in medicine, and give two reasons why the risks of ionising radiation are difficult to assess. the underlying granite contains uranium whose decay produces the radioactive gas radon (an alpha emitter) radon travels up cracks in the rock and through foundations into buildings it becomes concentrated inside enclosed / poorly ventilated buildings; control by ventilating foundations / sumps 16 + 1.5 = 17.5% where they live (geology / radon, altitude) occupation (radiographer, nuclear worker, aircrew, miner) medical history (number of x-rays / ct scans) lifestyle / diet (eg shellfish near discharges); smoking x-rays carry a small risk of mutation / cancer but the benefit (diagnosis, avoiding invasive surgery) may outweigh it; risks can be reduced (shielding, dose limits) symptoms of chronic low-dose exposure take a long time to develop the same symptoms (cancers) have other causes so effects are hard to attribute accurate data on human exposure / doses is often unavailable (especially after accidents) people who benefit and people who take the risk may be different (eg uranium miners vs electricity consumers)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RAD-04\" data-qid=\"RAD-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/radiation.html\">Ionising radiation</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.14 &middot; Genn pp. 297-299 &middot; RAD-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the average annual radiation dose received by a person in the UK from different sources.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Source</th><th>Percentage of average dose / %</th></tr><tr><td>Radon gas from the ground</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>Medical (X-rays, CT scans)</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td>Gamma rays from rocks / buildings</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td>Internal (food and drink)</td><td>9.5</td></tr><tr><td>Cosmic radiation</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>Occupational, fallout, nuclear discharges, consumer products</td><td>1.5</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Explain why radon concentrations are high inside some houses in Devon and Cornwall.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Use Table 1 to calculate the percentage of the average dose that comes from artificial (human-made) sources.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Suggest <b>two</b> reasons why the dose received by an individual may differ greatly from the average.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Explain why a risk:benefit analysis is needed for the use of X-rays in medicine, and give <b>two</b> reasons why the risks of ionising radiation are difficult to assess.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The underlying granite contains uranium whose decay produces the radioactive gas radon (an alpha emitter)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Radon travels up cracks in the rock and through foundations into buildings</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It becomes concentrated inside enclosed / poorly ventilated buildings; control by ventilating foundations / sumps</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>16 + 1.5 = 17.5%</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Where they live (geology / radon, altitude)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Occupation (radiographer, nuclear worker, aircrew, miner)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Medical history (number of X-rays / CT scans)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lifestyle / diet (eg shellfish near discharges); smoking</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>X-rays carry a small risk of mutation / cancer but the benefit (diagnosis, avoiding invasive surgery) may outweigh it; risks can be reduced (shielding, dose limits)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Symptoms of chronic low-dose exposure take a long time to develop</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The same symptoms (cancers) have other causes so effects are hard to attribute</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Accurate data on human exposure / doses is often unavailable (especially after accidents)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>People who benefit and people who take the risk may be different (eg uranium miners vs electricity consumers)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 297-299; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.14.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RAD-05", "sub": "radiation", "subname": "Ionising radiation", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.14", "pages": "303", "url": "subtopic/radiation.html#RAD-05", "snippet": "Complete Table 1 to show the source and disposal method for each category of radioactive waste from nuclear electricity generation.", "text": " complete table 1 to show the source and disposal method for each category of radioactive waste from nuclear electricity generation. explain why high-level waste must be cooled and why its storage must be monitored for very long periods. describe how low-level liquid and gaseous wastes are treated before discharge. high-level: used fuel rods / fission products separated in reprocessing; vitrification in molten glass in sealed stainless steel containers, surrounded by concrete, air-cooled to remove decay heat (sellafield) intermediate: fuel cladding / metal tubes around fuel rods, filters from reprocessing; mixed with cement (encapsulated) in stainless steel drums, stored in concrete vaults low-level solid: contaminated clothing, equipment; sealed in plastic bags inside steel drums / containers in a concrete-lined, monitored landfill (drigg) radioactive decay of the concentrated fission products generates heat which could damage containers isotopes with long half-lives (plutonium 24 400 years) remain hazardous for thousands of years liquids: filtered and passed through ion-exchange (sands / resins) to remove radionuclides, then discharged (monitored by cpa / cgm) gases: filtered (hepa / charcoal filters) to remove particles and iodine before release", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RAD-05\" data-qid=\"RAD-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/radiation.html\">Ionising radiation</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.14 &middot; Genn pp. 303 &middot; RAD-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Complete Table 1 to show the source and disposal method for each category of radioactive waste from nuclear electricity generation.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Waste category</th><th>Example / source</th><th>Storage or disposal method</th></tr><tr><td>High-level waste</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate-level waste</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Low-level solid waste</td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain why high-level waste must be cooled and why its storage must be monitored for very long periods.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Describe how low-level liquid and gaseous wastes are treated before discharge.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>High-level: used fuel rods / fission products separated in reprocessing; vitrification in molten glass in sealed stainless steel containers, surrounded by concrete, air-cooled to remove decay heat (Sellafield)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Intermediate: fuel cladding / metal tubes around fuel rods, filters from reprocessing; mixed with cement (encapsulated) in stainless steel drums, stored in concrete vaults</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Low-level solid: contaminated clothing, equipment; sealed in plastic bags inside steel drums / containers in a concrete-lined, monitored landfill (Drigg)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Radioactive decay of the concentrated fission products generates heat which could damage containers</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Isotopes with long half-lives (plutonium 24 400 years) remain hazardous for thousands of years</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Liquids: filtered and passed through ion-exchange (sands / resins) to remove radionuclides, then discharged (monitored by CPA / CGM)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Gases: filtered (HEPA / charcoal filters) to remove particles and iodine before release</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 303; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.14.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RAD-06", "sub": "radiation", "subname": "Ionising radiation", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.14", "pages": "295-304", "url": "subtopic/radiation.html#RAD-06", "snippet": "Evaluate the risks and benefits of the uses of ionising radiation, and explain the principles used to control exposure of workers and the public.", "text": " evaluate the risks and benefits of the uses of ionising radiation, and explain the principles used to control exposure of workers and the public. give one reason why the risks and benefits of nuclear electricity may not fall on the same people. uses / benefits: nuclear electricity (low carbon, high energy density), medicine (x-rays, ct, radiotherapy, tracers, sterilising equipment), industry (thickness gauges, polymer cross-linking, testing turbines, oil exploration), agriculture (food irradiation, sterile male technique, mutation breeding), research tracers, smoke detectors, ship / submarine propulsion risks: free radical production, dna damage, mutation, cancer; acute and chronic, somatic and gonadic effects; contamination vs exposure; activation products; long-lived wastes; accidents (chernobyl, fukushima); weapons fallout factors affecting risk: type of radiation (alpha, beta, gamma, neutrons - penetration, rbe / weighting factors), half-life, dose (gy, sv), route of exposure risk:benefit analysis and why it is difficult (latency, alternative causes, poor exposure data, risks and benefits to different people, offsets such as avoided surgery) principles: alara, batneec; closed sources, remote handling, absorbers / shielding, distance (inverse square law), time, protective clothing, decontamination, potassium iodide monitoring: dosemeters, film badges, air and contamination monitors; environmental monitoring via cpa and cgm; waste categories and disposal (vitrification, encapsulation, drigg) evaluation / judgement: benefits generally outweigh risks when controls are applied; public perception vs actual risk (background radon 50% of dose vs <1% from nuclear industry) consumers of the electricity may live in a different country / region from the uranium miners or the communities near waste stores who bear the risks", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RAD-06\" data-qid=\"RAD-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/radiation.html\">Ionising radiation</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.14 &middot; Genn pp. 295-304 &middot; RAD-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Evaluate the risks and benefits of the uses of ionising radiation, and explain the principles used to control exposure of workers and the public.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Give <b>one</b> reason why the risks and benefits of nuclear electricity may not fall on the same people.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Uses / benefits: nuclear electricity (low carbon, high energy density), medicine (X-rays, CT, radiotherapy, tracers, sterilising equipment), industry (thickness gauges, polymer cross-linking, testing turbines, oil exploration), agriculture (food irradiation, sterile male technique, mutation breeding), research tracers, smoke detectors, ship / submarine propulsion</li><li>Risks: free radical production, DNA damage, mutation, cancer; acute and chronic, somatic and gonadic effects; contamination vs exposure; activation products; long-lived wastes; accidents (Chernobyl, Fukushima); weapons fallout</li><li>Factors affecting risk: type of radiation (alpha, beta, gamma, neutrons - penetration, RBE / weighting factors), half-life, dose (Gy, Sv), route of exposure</li><li>Risk:benefit analysis and why it is difficult (latency, alternative causes, poor exposure data, risks and benefits to different people, offsets such as avoided surgery)</li><li>Principles: ALARA, BATNEEC; closed sources, remote handling, absorbers / shielding, distance (inverse square law), time, protective clothing, decontamination, potassium iodide</li><li>Monitoring: dosemeters, film badges, air and contamination monitors; environmental monitoring via CPA and CGM; waste categories and disposal (vitrification, encapsulation, Drigg)</li><li>Evaluation / judgement: benefits generally outweigh risks when controls are applied; public perception vs actual risk (background radon 50% of dose vs &lt;1% from nuclear industry)</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Consumers of the electricity may live in a different country / region from the uranium miners or the communities near waste stores who bear the risks</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 295-304; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.14.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SOIL-01", "sub": "soils", "subname": "Soils: fertility, erosion & management", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.2.5.1", "pages": "158-160, 412", "url": "subtopic/soils.html#SOIL-01", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the textural composition of four soil samples.", "text": "table 1 shows the textural composition of four soil samples. identify the sample that is a loam and explain why loams are ideal for cultivating most crops. explain why sample a is likely to have the highest permeability but the lowest nutrient retention. compare the thermal capacity and root penetration of soils a and c. describe how the texture of a soil sample could be determined by sedimentation. b (about 40:40:20 sand:silt:clay) balanced mix gives good drainage and aeration from the sand fraction water retention and nutrient (ion) retention from the clay fraction; easy root penetration and cultivation large sand particles leave large pore spaces so water drains rapidly (high permeability / aeration) nutrient ions adsorb onto the (charged) surfaces of clay particles but not onto sand with only 5% clay there is little surface to hold ions so nutrients are leached; low water retention too c (clay) holds much water so has a high thermal capacity: warms and cools slowly; a warms rapidly after cold weather clay particles held together by capillary water so roots find it hard to push between them; penetration is easy in sandy a shake a known mass of soil with water in a measuring cylinder and leave to settle; particles settle in order of size (sand first, then silt, then clay; organic matter floats) measure the depth / volume of each layer to calculate the percentage of each fraction, then use the soil triangle to name the textural class", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SOIL-01\" data-qid=\"SOIL-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/soils.html\">Soils: fertility, erosion &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.2.5.1 &middot; Genn pp. 158-160, 412 &middot; SOIL-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the textural composition of four soil samples.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Soil sample</th><th>Sand / %</th><th>Silt / %</th><th>Clay / %</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>85</td><td>10</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>40</td><td>40</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>20</td><td>25</td><td>55</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>10</td><td>80</td><td>10</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Identify the sample that is a loam and explain why loams are ideal for cultivating most crops.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain why sample A is likely to have the highest permeability but the lowest nutrient retention.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Compare the thermal capacity and root penetration of soils A and C.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Describe how the texture of a soil sample could be determined by sedimentation.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>B (about 40:40:20 sand:silt:clay)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Balanced mix gives good drainage and aeration from the sand fraction</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Water retention and nutrient (ion) retention from the clay fraction; easy root penetration and cultivation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Large sand particles leave large pore spaces so water drains rapidly (high permeability / aeration)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nutrient ions adsorb onto the (charged) surfaces of clay particles but not onto sand</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>With only 5% clay there is little surface to hold ions so nutrients are leached; low water retention too</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>C (clay) holds much water so has a high thermal capacity: warms and cools slowly; A warms rapidly after cold weather</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Clay particles held together by capillary water so roots find it hard to push between them; penetration is easy in sandy A</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Shake a known mass of soil with water in a measuring cylinder and leave to settle; particles settle in order of size (sand first, then silt, then clay; organic matter floats)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Measure the depth / volume of each layer to calculate the percentage of each fraction, then use the soil triangle to name the textural class</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 158-160, 412; AQA spec 3.2.5.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SOIL-02", "sub": "soils", "subname": "Soils: fertility, erosion & management", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.5.1", "pages": "158-161", "url": "subtopic/soils.html#SOIL-02", "snippet": "Explain the roles of three named groups of soil organisms in maintaining soil fertility.", "text": " explain the roles of three named groups of soil organisms in maintaining soil fertility. explain why a soil ph between 5.5 and 7.0 is best for fertility. explain how soil compaction by heavy machinery reduces fertility. detritivores (worms, beetle larvae, millipedes, woodlice) physically break up dead organic matter, increasing its surface area, and release nutrients; worms create tunnels improving drainage and aeration decomposers (bacteria, fungi) secrete enzymes that break down dead organic matter, releasing nutrient ions (ammonification) and forming humus which binds particles into crumb peds nitrogen-fixing bacteria convert nitrogen gas to ammonium ions; nitrifying bacteria oxidise ammonium to nitrite then nitrate for root absorption mycorrhizal fungi form symbiotic associations with roots increasing phosphate uptake this is the range of tolerance of most plants and soil biota; acidic soils leach nutrients and mobilise toxic aluminium, damaging root membranes in alkaline soils phosphates become insoluble / unavailable interstitial spaces are reduced so aeration falls (aerobic processes such as nitrification and decomposition slow) and roots cannot penetrate infiltration falls so waterlogging is more likely and runoff / erosion increase", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SOIL-02\" data-qid=\"SOIL-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/soils.html\">Soils: fertility, erosion &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.2.5.1 &middot; Genn pp. 158-161 &middot; SOIL-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain the roles of <b>three</b> named groups of soil organisms in maintaining soil fertility.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain why a soil pH between 5.5 and 7.0 is best for fertility.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain how soil compaction by heavy machinery reduces fertility.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 6 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Detritivores (worms, beetle larvae, millipedes, woodlice) physically break up dead organic matter, increasing its surface area, and release nutrients; worms create tunnels improving drainage and aeration</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Decomposers (bacteria, fungi) secrete enzymes that break down dead organic matter, releasing nutrient ions (ammonification) and forming humus which binds particles into crumb peds</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Nitrogen-fixing bacteria convert nitrogen gas to ammonium ions; nitrifying bacteria oxidise ammonium to nitrite then nitrate for root absorption</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Mycorrhizal fungi form symbiotic associations with roots increasing phosphate uptake</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>This is the range of tolerance of most plants and soil biota; acidic soils leach nutrients and mobilise toxic aluminium, damaging root membranes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In alkaline soils phosphates become insoluble / unavailable</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Interstitial spaces are reduced so aeration falls (aerobic processes such as nitrification and decomposition slow) and roots cannot penetrate</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Infiltration falls so waterlogging is more likely and runoff / erosion increase</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 158-161; AQA spec 3.2.5.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SOIL-03", "sub": "soils", "subname": "Soils: fertility, erosion & management", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.2.5.2", "pages": "162-164", "url": "subtopic/soils.html#SOIL-03", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the annual soil loss measured on experimental plots with different vegetation cover on a 10% slope in a region with heavy seasonal rain.", "text": "figure 1 shows the annual soil loss measured on experimental plots with different vegetation cover on a 10% slope in a region with heavy seasonal rain. describe the pattern shown in figure 1. explain four ways in which vegetation reduces the rate of soil erosion. explain why ploughing up and down a slope causes more erosion than contour ploughing. explain why the region's climate makes the soil vulnerable to both wind and water erosion. soil loss increases as vegetation cover decreases: forest lowest (0.5), bare soil highest (60 t ha-1 yr-1) - over 100 times more ploughing downslope loses nearly three times as much as contour ploughing for the same crop leaves / leaf litter intercept raindrops so soil particles are not dislodged by rain splash roots bind soil particles together vegetation acts as a windbreak reducing wind velocity and the kinetic energy available to carry particles plants increase infiltration (root channels, organic matter) so less surface runoff organic matter / humus from vegetation binds particles into stable peds furrows running downslope act as channels / gulleys that increase the velocity (kinetic energy) of runoff furrows along the contour trap the water so it loses energy, deposits particles and infiltrates in the dry season the soil (especially low-clay soils) is loose and dry with little cohesion so wind blows it away in the wet season heavy rain causes rain splash and surface runoff when infiltration capacity is exceeded", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SOIL-03\" data-qid=\"SOIL-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/soils.html\">Soils: fertility, erosion &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.2.5.2 &middot; Genn pp. 162-164 &middot; SOIL-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the annual soil loss measured on experimental plots with different vegetation cover on a 10% slope in a region with heavy seasonal rain.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<rect x=\"74.4\" y=\"268.1\" width=\"67.2\" height=\"1.9\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"108.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Forest</text>\n<rect x=\"170.4\" y=\"262.4\" width=\"67.2\" height=\"7.6\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"204.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Grassland</text>\n<rect x=\"266.4\" y=\"235.9\" width=\"67.2\" height=\"34.1\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Wheat, contour ploughed</text>\n<rect x=\"362.4\" y=\"175.3\" width=\"67.2\" height=\"94.7\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"396.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Wheat, ploughed downslope</text>\n<rect x=\"458.4\" y=\"42.7\" width=\"67.2\" height=\"227.3\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"492.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bare soil</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">13.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">26.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">39.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">52.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">66</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Land cover</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Soil loss / t/ha/yr</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Describe the pattern shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain <b>four</b> ways in which vegetation reduces the rate of soil erosion.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain why ploughing up and down a slope causes more erosion than contour ploughing.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Explain why the region's climate makes the soil vulnerable to <b>both</b> wind and water erosion.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Soil loss increases as vegetation cover decreases: forest lowest (0.5), bare soil highest (60 t ha<sup>-1</sup> yr<sup>-1</sup>) - over 100 times more</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ploughing downslope loses nearly three times as much as contour ploughing for the same crop</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Leaves / leaf litter intercept raindrops so soil particles are not dislodged by rain splash</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Roots bind soil particles together</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Vegetation acts as a windbreak reducing wind velocity and the kinetic energy available to carry particles</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Plants increase infiltration (root channels, organic matter) so less surface runoff</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Organic matter / humus from vegetation binds particles into stable peds</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Furrows running downslope act as channels / gulleys that increase the velocity (kinetic energy) of runoff</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Furrows along the contour trap the water so it loses energy, deposits particles and infiltrates</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In the dry season the soil (especially low-clay soils) is loose and dry with little cohesion so wind blows it away</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In the wet season heavy rain causes rain splash and surface runoff when infiltration capacity is exceeded</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 162-164; AQA spec 3.2.5.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SOIL-04", "sub": "soils", "subname": "Soils: fertility, erosion & management", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.2.5.2", "pages": "162-164", "url": "subtopic/soils.html#SOIL-04", "snippet": "Explain how each of the following human activities increases soil erosion.", "text": " explain how each of the following human activities increases soil erosion. explain why landslides become more likely on steep slopes some years after deforestation. describe four environmental impacts of accelerated soil erosion away from the field where it occurs. overgrazing: vegetation is eaten faster than it grows so soil is exposed; hooves disturb the soil, damage roots and compact it, reducing infiltration and increasing runoff reduced biota (ploughing, agrochemicals, less organic matter): less humus so less adhesion between particles; fewer worms so less aeration and lower infiltration capacity; fewer nutrients released so less vegetation cover tree roots that held the soil together decompose so the soil is bound less strongly heavy rain adds mass and lubricates particles; without interception more water enters the soil, making slumping more likely sedimentation in rivers reduces flow capacity so flooding downstream is more likely sedimentation in reservoirs reduces their storage volume coastal sedimentation covering reefs / seagrass; turbidity reducing light for aquatic plants increased atmospheric particulates from wind erosion worsening asthma; dust covering crops / urban areas desertification as vegetation cannot re-establish; reduced rainfall nutrients (phosphates) carried on soil particles causing eutrophication", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SOIL-04\" data-qid=\"SOIL-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/soils.html\">Soils: fertility, erosion &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.2.5.2 &middot; Genn pp. 162-164 &middot; SOIL-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Explain how each of the following human activities increases soil erosion.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain why landslides become more likely on steep slopes some years after deforestation.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Describe <b>four</b> environmental impacts of accelerated soil erosion away from the field where it occurs.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Overgrazing: vegetation is eaten faster than it grows so soil is exposed; hooves disturb the soil, damage roots and compact it, reducing infiltration and increasing runoff</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Reduced biota (ploughing, agrochemicals, less organic matter): less humus so less adhesion between particles; fewer worms so less aeration and lower infiltration capacity; fewer nutrients released so less vegetation cover</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Tree roots that held the soil together decompose so the soil is bound less strongly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Heavy rain adds mass and lubricates particles; without interception more water enters the soil, making slumping more likely</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sedimentation in rivers reduces flow capacity so flooding downstream is more likely</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sedimentation in reservoirs reduces their storage volume</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Coastal sedimentation covering reefs / seagrass; turbidity reducing light for aquatic plants</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Increased atmospheric particulates from wind erosion worsening asthma; dust covering crops / urban areas</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Desertification as vegetation cannot re-establish; reduced rainfall</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nutrients (phosphates) carried on soil particles causing eutrophication</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 162-164; AQA spec 3.2.5.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SOIL-05", "sub": "soils", "subname": "Soils: fertility, erosion & management", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.2.5.2", "pages": "167-169", "url": "subtopic/soils.html#SOIL-05", "snippet": "The Universal Soil Loss Equation (USLE) is A = R x K x LS x C x P. Table 1 gives factor values for a farm.", "text": "the universal soil loss equation (usle) is a = r x k x ls x c x p. table 1 gives factor values for a farm. calculate the annual soil loss for beans with spring ploughing, ploughed up and down the slope. give your answer to one decimal place. calculate the annual soil loss for wheat with mulch tillage and strip cropping with contour ploughing. the natural rate of soil formation on the farm is 1 t ha-1 yr-1. use your answers and table 2 to evaluate the sustainability of the two farming systems. explain why the usle gives only an estimate of soil loss. suggest which single factor in the usle a farmer can change most easily. 110 x 0.67 x 1.35 x (0.50 x 0.90) x 1.0 = 44.8 t ha-1 yr-1 (accept 44.7-44.8) 110 x 0.67 x 1.35 x (0.35 x 0.60) x 0.25 = 5.2 t ha-1 yr-1 beans / up-and-down ploughing: severe class (over 30) and about 45 times the formation rate - clearly unsustainable, soil depth will fall wheat / mulch / strip cropping: moderate class, reduced by about 88% but still 5 times the formation rate so not fully sustainable further measures (long-term crops, terracing, windbreaks, more organic matter) or grazing land (c = 0.02) needed to bring loss below 1 t ha-1 yr-1 factors are averages / arbitrary values for typical conditions; rainfall erosivity varies between years / storms does not include gully erosion, landslides, wind erosion or local variation in soil; based on data from particular regions c (crop / tillage) or p (erosion control practice) - r, k and ls are fixed by climate, soil and topography", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SOIL-05\" data-qid=\"SOIL-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/soils.html\">Soils: fertility, erosion &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.2.5.2 &middot; Genn pp. 167-169 &middot; SOIL-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>The Universal Soil Loss Equation (USLE) is A = R x K x LS x C x P. Table 1 gives factor values for a farm.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Description</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>R</td><td>Rainfall erosivity for the farm</td><td>110</td></tr><tr><td>K</td><td>Soil erodibility: loam</td><td>0.67</td></tr><tr><td>LS</td><td>Slope length and gradient: 120 m, 6%</td><td>1.35</td></tr><tr><td>C (crop type x tillage)</td><td>Beans (0.50) with spring ploughing (0.90); or wheat (0.35) with mulch tillage (0.60)</td><td>see question</td></tr><tr><td>P</td><td>Ploughing up and down slope 1.0; contour ploughing 0.50; strip cropping with contour ploughing 0.25</td><td>see question</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 2</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Soil erosion class</th><th>Rate of soil loss / t ha<sup>-1</sup> yr<sup>-1</sup></th></tr><tr><td>Low</td><td>under 5</td></tr><tr><td>Moderate</td><td>5-10</td></tr><tr><td>High</td><td>10-20</td></tr><tr><td>Very high</td><td>20-30</td></tr><tr><td>Severe</td><td>over 30</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Calculate the annual soil loss for beans with spring ploughing, ploughed up and down the slope. Give your answer to one decimal place.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Calculate the annual soil loss for wheat with mulch tillage and strip cropping with contour ploughing.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>The natural rate of soil formation on the farm is 1 t ha<sup>-1</sup> yr<sup>-1</sup>. Use your answers and Table 2 to evaluate the sustainability of the two farming systems.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Explain why the USLE gives only an estimate of soil loss.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.5</span>Suggest which single factor in the USLE a farmer can change most easily.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>110 x 0.67 x 1.35 x (0.50 x 0.90) x 1.0</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 44.8 t ha<sup>-1</sup> yr<sup>-1</sup> (accept 44.7-44.8)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>110 x 0.67 x 1.35 x (0.35 x 0.60) x 0.25</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 5.2 t ha<sup>-1</sup> yr<sup>-1</sup></span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Beans / up-and-down ploughing: severe class (over 30) and about 45 times the formation rate - clearly unsustainable, soil depth will fall</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wheat / mulch / strip cropping: moderate class, reduced by about 88% but still 5 times the formation rate so not fully sustainable</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Further measures (long-term crops, terracing, windbreaks, more organic matter) or grazing land (C = 0.02) needed to bring loss below 1 t ha<sup>-1</sup> yr<sup>-1</sup></span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Factors are averages / arbitrary values for typical conditions; rainfall erosivity varies between years / storms</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Does not include gully erosion, landslides, wind erosion or local variation in soil; based on data from particular regions</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>C (crop / tillage) or P (erosion control practice) - R, K and LS are fixed by climate, soil and topography</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 167-169; AQA spec 3.2.5.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SOIL-06", "sub": "soils", "subname": "Soils: fertility, erosion & management", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.2.5.3", "pages": "164-166", "url": "subtopic/soils.html#SOIL-06", "snippet": "Complete Table 1 to explain how each method reduces soil erosion.", "text": " complete table 1 to explain how each method reduces soil erosion. explain why long-term crops such as tea, coffee or orchards reduce erosion compared with annual arable crops. describe three ways livestock can be managed to reduce soil erosion. terracing: sloping land divided into narrow level fields held by retaining walls along contours; water slows as it crosses each terrace and infiltrates instead of running off tied ridging: on nearly flat land a criss-cross of intersecting ridges retains rainwater, increasing infiltration and preventing runoff windbreaks: hedgerows / tree rows reduce wind velocity (for up to 30 times their height) so the wind has less kinetic energy to carry particles; protect bare fields after harvest mulching: organic matter on the surface protects soil from raindrop impact and wind; decomposes to humus which binds particles; retains moisture strip cropping: strips of different crops harvested at different times (at 90° to the wind) so there is always a crop protecting part of the field; slows runoff no frequent replanting / soil disturbance (ploughing) that breaks up soil structure and exposes particles permanent root systems and canopy cover protect the soil all year control stocking density so vegetation regrows faster than it is eaten restrict access to vulnerable areas such as river banks (fencing) move / remove livestock at high-risk times, eg after heavy rain when soil is wet rotational grazing to rest pastures", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SOIL-06\" data-qid=\"SOIL-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/soils.html\">Soils: fertility, erosion &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.2.5.3 &middot; Genn pp. 164-166 &middot; SOIL-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Complete Table 1 to explain how each method reduces soil erosion.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Method</th><th>How it reduces erosion</th></tr><tr><td>Terracing</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Tied ridging</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Windbreaks / hedgerows</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Mulching</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Strip cropping</td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain why long-term crops such as tea, coffee or orchards reduce erosion compared with annual arable crops.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Describe <b>three</b> ways livestock can be managed to reduce soil erosion.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Terracing: sloping land divided into narrow level fields held by retaining walls along contours; water slows as it crosses each terrace and infiltrates instead of running off</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Tied ridging: on nearly flat land a criss-cross of intersecting ridges retains rainwater, increasing infiltration and preventing runoff</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Windbreaks: hedgerows / tree rows reduce wind velocity (for up to 30 times their height) so the wind has less kinetic energy to carry particles; protect bare fields after harvest</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mulching: organic matter on the surface protects soil from raindrop impact and wind; decomposes to humus which binds particles; retains moisture</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Strip cropping: strips of different crops harvested at different times (at 90&deg; to the wind) so there is always a crop protecting part of the field; slows runoff</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No frequent replanting / soil disturbance (ploughing) that breaks up soil structure and exposes particles</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Permanent root systems and canopy cover protect the soil all year</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Control stocking density so vegetation regrows faster than it is eaten</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Restrict access to vulnerable areas such as river banks (fencing)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Move / remove livestock at high-risk times, eg after heavy rain when soil is wet</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Rotational grazing to rest pastures</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 164-166; AQA spec 3.2.5.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SOIL-07", "sub": "soils", "subname": "Soils: fertility, erosion & management", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.2.5.1, 3.7.2.1", "pages": "158-159, 412", "url": "subtopic/soils.html#SOIL-07", "snippet": "A student determined the water and organic matter content of a soil sample. Table 1 shows the results.", "text": "a student determined the water and organic matter content of a soil sample. table 1 shows the results. calculate the percentage water content of the fresh soil. calculate the percentage organic matter content of the dry soil. explain why the soil is dried to constant mass before the organic matter is burnt off. explain how the organic matter content affects the fertility and erosion risk of this soil. suggest one reason why two students measuring the same field might get different organic matter results. water = 65.0 - 55.0 = 10.0 g; fresh soil = 40.0 g 10.0 / 40.0 x 100 = 25.0% organic matter = 55.0 - 52.6 = 2.4 g; dry soil = 30.0 g 2.4 / 30.0 x 100 = 8.0% so all water is removed and the mass loss on burning is due to organic matter only heating until mass no longer changes shows all water has evaporated organic matter releases nutrients as it decomposes and provides food for soil biota increases water retention and, as humus, binds particles into crumb peds improving structure / aeration surface organic matter / humus adhesion reduce rain splash and wind erosion samples taken from different depths / locations (organic matter is concentrated near the surface); incomplete burning; different heating times", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SOIL-07\" data-qid=\"SOIL-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/soils.html\">Soils: fertility, erosion &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.2.5.1, 3.7.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 158-159, 412 &middot; SOIL-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A student determined the water and organic matter content of a soil sample. Table 1 shows the results.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Mass / g</th></tr><tr><td>Empty crucible</td><td>25.0</td></tr><tr><td>Crucible + fresh soil</td><td>65.0</td></tr><tr><td>Crucible + soil after drying at 105 &deg;C to constant mass</td><td>55.0</td></tr><tr><td>Crucible + soil after heating in a furnace at 550 &deg;C</td><td>52.6</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Calculate the percentage water content of the fresh soil.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Calculate the percentage organic matter content of the dry soil.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Explain why the soil is dried to constant mass before the organic matter is burnt off.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>Explain how the organic matter content affects the fertility and erosion risk of this soil.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.5</span>Suggest <b>one</b> reason why two students measuring the same field might get different organic matter results.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Water = 65.0 - 55.0 = 10.0 g; fresh soil = 40.0 g</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>10.0 / 40.0 x 100 = 25.0%</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Organic matter = 55.0 - 52.6 = 2.4 g; dry soil = 30.0 g</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>2.4 / 30.0 x 100 = 8.0%</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So all water is removed and the mass loss on burning is due to organic matter only</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Heating until mass no longer changes shows all water has evaporated</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Organic matter releases nutrients as it decomposes and provides food for soil biota</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Increases water retention and, as humus, binds particles into crumb peds improving structure / aeration</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Surface organic matter / humus adhesion reduce rain splash and wind erosion</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Samples taken from different depths / locations (organic matter is concentrated near the surface); incomplete burning; different heating times</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 158-159, 412; AQA spec 3.2.5.1, 3.7.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SOIL-08", "sub": "soils", "subname": "Soils: fertility, erosion & management", "topic": "The physical environment", "tslug": "physical", "papers": [1], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.2.5", "pages": "158-169", "url": "subtopic/soils.html#SOIL-08", "snippet": "Which soil property is most directly responsible for capillary rise of water from below? Tick one box.", "text": " which soil property is most directly responsible for capillary rise of water from below? tick one box. distinguish between hygroscopic, capillary and gravitational water in soil and state which are available to plants. explain why crumb peds produce more fertile soil than platy peds. b - tiny pores between clay particles allow capillary rise; none in sand hygroscopic: held tightly by adhesive forces on particle surfaces; cannot drain and cannot be absorbed by plants capillary: held in pores by surface tension; does not drain but can be absorbed by plants gravitational: drains freely through large pores; available to plants only briefly as it passes small round crumbs leave spaces giving good drainage, aeration and easy root penetration; large flat platy peds pack together reducing all three", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SOIL-08\" data-qid=\"SOIL-08\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 8</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/soils.html\">Soils: fertility, erosion &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.2.5 &middot; Genn pp. 158-169 &middot; SOIL-08</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.1</span>Which soil property is most directly responsible for capillary rise of water from below? Tick one box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; High sand content</li><li>B&nbsp; High clay content</li><li>C&nbsp; Crumb peds</li><li>D&nbsp; High pH</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.2</span>Distinguish between hygroscopic, capillary and gravitational water in soil and state which are available to plants.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.3</span>Explain why crumb peds produce more fertile soil than platy peds.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b></b>&nbsp; B - tiny pores between clay particles allow capillary rise; none in sand</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hygroscopic: held tightly by adhesive forces on particle surfaces; cannot drain and cannot be absorbed by plants</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Capillary: held in pores by surface tension; does not drain but can be absorbed by plants</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Gravitational: drains freely through large pores; available to plants only briefly as it passes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Small round crumbs leave spaces giving good drainage, aeration and easy root penetration; large flat platy peds pack together reducing all three</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 158-169; AQA spec 3.2.5.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "WPOL-01", "sub": "water-pollution", "subname": "Thermal & oil pollution", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.6", "pages": "256-257", "url": "subtopic/water-pollution.html#WPOL-01", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the maximum concentration of dissolved oxygen in fresh water at different temperatures. A power station discharges warm cooling water into a river. The lowest dissol", "text": "figure 1 shows the maximum concentration of dissolved oxygen in fresh water at different temperatures. a power station discharges warm cooling water into a river. the lowest dissolved oxygen concentration that trout can tolerate is 6.5 mg dm-3. describe the relationship shown in figure 1. use figure 1 to determine the highest water temperature at which trout could survive if the water were fully saturated with oxygen. in summer the river upstream of the power station is at 20 °c and the effluent raises the temperature by 7 °c. use figure 1 to calculate the reduction in maximum dissolved oxygen concentration caused by the effluent. explain why the actual dissolved oxygen concentration downstream may be much lower than the values in figure 1. describe three effects of warmer water on aquatic organisms other than reduced dissolved oxygen. describe how a cooling tower reduces the temperature of power station effluent water. as temperature increases the maximum dissolved oxygen concentration decreases (non-linear: falls faster at low temperatures) about 39 °c (accept 38-40) 9.1 - 7.9 (value at 27 °c, accept 7.8-8.0) = 1.2 mg dm-3 (accept 1.1-1.3) figure 1 shows the maximum / saturation values; processes that remove oxygen (aerobic decomposition of organic matter / respiration) can give values below the line higher temperature increases the metabolic rate of organisms and the rate of bacterial decomposition so oxygen is used faster; values above the line are impossible increased metabolic rate so more energy used / less surplus for growth eggs develop faster / hatch earlier (out of step with food supply) non-indigenous warm-water species may thrive and out-compete indigenous species toxicity of other pollutants is greater as toxins are metabolised faster reduced resistance to disease proteins / enzymes denatured if temperature is very high (above about 40 °c) hot water is sprayed from a shower floor in a tall hollow tower; air absorbs the heat and rises, drawing in more air (updraught), so the water returned is cooler (and saturated with oxygen)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"WPOL-01\" data-qid=\"WPOL-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/water-pollution.html\">Thermal &amp; oil pollution</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.6 &middot; Genn pp. 256-257 &middot; WPOL-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the maximum concentration of dissolved oxygen in fresh water at different temperatures. A power station discharges warm cooling water into a river. The lowest dissolved oxygen concentration that trout can tolerate is 6.5 mg dm<sup>-3</sup>.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,41.9 120.0,70.0 180.0,93.4 240.0,112.2 300.0,127.8 360.0,140.3 420.0,151.2 480.0,160.6 540.0,170.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"41.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"120.0\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"180.0\" cy=\"93.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"240.0\" cy=\"112.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"127.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"360.0\" cy=\"140.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"420.0\" cy=\"151.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"480.0\" cy=\"160.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"170.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">3.2</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">16</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">6.4</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">24</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">9.6</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">32</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">12.8</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">40</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">16</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Water temperature / &amp;deg;C</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Maximum dissolved oxygen / mg/dm³</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Describe the relationship shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Use Figure 1 to determine the highest water temperature at which trout could survive if the water were fully saturated with oxygen.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>In summer the river upstream of the power station is at 20 &deg;C and the effluent raises the temperature by 7 &deg;C. Use Figure 1 to calculate the reduction in maximum dissolved oxygen concentration caused by the effluent.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Explain why the actual dissolved oxygen concentration downstream may be much lower than the values in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.5</span>Describe <b>three</b> effects of warmer water on aquatic organisms other than reduced dissolved oxygen.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.6</span>Describe how a cooling tower reduces the temperature of power station effluent water.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>As temperature increases the maximum dissolved oxygen concentration decreases (non-linear: falls faster at low temperatures)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>About 39 &deg;C (accept 38-40)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>9.1 - 7.9 (value at 27 &deg;C, accept 7.8-8.0)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 1.2 mg dm<sup>-3</sup> (accept 1.1-1.3)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Figure 1 shows the maximum / saturation values; processes that remove oxygen (aerobic decomposition of organic matter / respiration) can give values below the line</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Higher temperature increases the metabolic rate of organisms and the rate of bacterial decomposition so oxygen is used faster; values above the line are impossible</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Increased metabolic rate so more energy used / less surplus for growth</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Eggs develop faster / hatch earlier (out of step with food supply)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Non-indigenous warm-water species may thrive and out-compete indigenous species</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Toxicity of other pollutants is greater as toxins are metabolised faster</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduced resistance to disease</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Proteins / enzymes denatured if temperature is very high (above about 40 &deg;C)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.6</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hot water is sprayed from a shower floor in a tall hollow tower; air absorbs the heat and rises, drawing in more air (updraught), so the water returned is cooler (and saturated with oxygen)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 256-257; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.6.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "WPOL-02", "sub": "water-pollution", "subname": "Thermal & oil pollution", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.2.7", "pages": "258-259", "url": "subtopic/water-pollution.html#WPOL-02", "snippet": "Give three sources of oil pollution of the sea other than tanker accidents.", "text": " give three sources of oil pollution of the sea other than tanker accidents. explain how an oil spill at sea can kill seabirds. explain two ways in which an oil slick can harm organisms that live in the water beneath it. explain why oil pollution is most serious when it occurs inshore or in an enclosed body of water. discharge of tank-washing water from tankers oil rig / drilling accidents / leakage during drilling pipeline leaks leakage from storage tanks / refinery spills waste lubricating oil from vehicles / industry washed into drains other ship accidents (fuel oil) oil sticks feathers together so they lose their insulating properties and birds die of hypothermia feathers lose buoyancy / waterproofing so heavily oiled birds drown birds swallow toxic oil when preening; time spent cleaning feathers reduces time for feeding themselves / their young the surface film reduces the diffusion of oxygen from the atmosphere into the water, so aerobic organisms suffer from low dissolved oxygen toxic components (benzene, xylene) dissolve and poison plankton near the surface oil blocks light so algae / phytoplankton cannot photosynthesise oil masks odours / narcotises so animals cannot find mates or food by smell shore organisms (molluscs, crustaceans) are covered and asphyxiated dispersal / dilution by currents and waves is prevented so oil stays concentrated; in open water most spills disperse in days or weeks inshore areas have more sensitive ecosystems (shores, reefs, mangroves, breeding sites) and more human activities (fishing, tourism) to be affected", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"WPOL-02\" data-qid=\"WPOL-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/water-pollution.html\">Thermal &amp; oil pollution</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.7 &middot; Genn pp. 258-259 &middot; WPOL-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Give <b>three</b> sources of oil pollution of the sea other than tanker accidents.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain how an oil spill at sea can kill seabirds.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain <b>two</b> ways in which an oil slick can harm organisms that live in the water beneath it.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Explain why oil pollution is most serious when it occurs inshore or in an enclosed body of water.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Discharge of tank-washing water from tankers</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Oil rig / drilling accidents / leakage during drilling</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pipeline leaks</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Leakage from storage tanks / refinery spills</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Waste lubricating oil from vehicles / industry washed into drains</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Other ship accidents (fuel oil)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Oil sticks feathers together so they lose their insulating properties and birds die of hypothermia</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Feathers lose buoyancy / waterproofing so heavily oiled birds drown</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Birds swallow toxic oil when preening; time spent cleaning feathers reduces time for feeding themselves / their young</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The surface film reduces the diffusion of oxygen from the atmosphere into the water, so aerobic organisms suffer from low dissolved oxygen</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Toxic components (benzene, xylene) dissolve and poison plankton near the surface</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Oil blocks light so algae / phytoplankton cannot photosynthesise</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Oil masks odours / narcotises so animals cannot find mates or food by smell</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Shore organisms (molluscs, crustaceans) are covered and asphyxiated</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Dispersal / dilution by currents and waves is prevented so oil stays concentrated; in open water most spills disperse in days or weeks</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Inshore areas have more sensitive ecosystems (shores, reefs, mangroves, breeding sites) and more human activities (fishing, tourism) to be affected</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 258-259; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.7.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "WPOL-03", "sub": "water-pollution", "subname": "Thermal & oil pollution", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.7", "pages": "259-260", "url": "subtopic/water-pollution.html#WPOL-03", "snippet": "Table 1 shows features of oil tanker design and operation that reduce the risk of oil pollution.", "text": "table 1 shows features of oil tanker design and operation that reduce the risk of oil pollution. complete table 1 by explaining how each feature reduces oil pollution. explain how each of the following reduces the risk of tanker accidents. describe how a bund wall prevents oil pollution from a storage tank. explain the purpose of an oil interceptor on the drainage system of a car park. double hull: a gap of up to 2 m between the hulls so damage to the outer hull in a collision / grounding does not release oil inert gas system: cooled engine exhaust gas (no oxygen) fills the space above the oil so an explosive oil vapour / air mixture cannot form separate ballast tanks: ballast water is not carried in oil tanks so no residual oil is discharged when ballast is pumped out twin engines / rudders: if one fails the ship can still be steered / propelled and is not carried onto rocks by currents or wind crude oil washing: sludges are mixed and removed with the cargo so tanks do not need washing with seawater that was then discharged; oily water is unloaded at the terminal offshore routes keep tankers further from the coast so if there is engine failure there is more time to deal with it before the ship is carried ashore gps gives accurate navigation; ais lets ship positions be monitored and coordinated to avoid collisions and groundings the tank stands on an impermeable base surrounded by a wall the volume enclosed by the wall is greater than the tank volume so all the oil is contained if the tank splits / cannot overflow separates floating oil from drainage water so the oil is retained (for later treatment) and only water flows to the drains / river", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"WPOL-03\" data-qid=\"WPOL-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/water-pollution.html\">Thermal &amp; oil pollution</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.7 &middot; Genn pp. 259-260 &middot; WPOL-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows features of oil tanker design and operation that reduce the risk of oil pollution.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Complete Table 1 by explaining how each feature reduces oil pollution.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>How it reduces oil pollution</th></tr><tr><td>Double hull</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Inert gas system</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Separate oil and ballast tanks</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Twin engines / rudders</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Recirculation of oil during unloading (crude oil washing)</td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain how each of the following reduces the risk of tanker accidents.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Describe how a <b>bund wall</b> prevents oil pollution from a storage tank.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Explain the purpose of an oil interceptor on the drainage system of a car park.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Double hull: a gap of up to 2 m between the hulls so damage to the outer hull in a collision / grounding does not release oil</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Inert gas system: cooled engine exhaust gas (no oxygen) fills the space above the oil so an explosive oil vapour / air mixture cannot form</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Separate ballast tanks: ballast water is not carried in oil tanks so no residual oil is discharged when ballast is pumped out</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Twin engines / rudders: if one fails the ship can still be steered / propelled and is not carried onto rocks by currents or wind</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Crude oil washing: sludges are mixed and removed with the cargo so tanks do not need washing with seawater that was then discharged; oily water is unloaded at the terminal</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Offshore routes keep tankers further from the coast so if there is engine failure there is more time to deal with it before the ship is carried ashore</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>GPS gives accurate navigation; AIS lets ship positions be monitored and coordinated to avoid collisions and groundings</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The tank stands on an impermeable base surrounded by a wall</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The volume enclosed by the wall is greater than the tank volume so all the oil is contained if the tank splits / cannot overflow</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Separates floating oil from drainage water so the oil is retained (for later treatment) and only water flows to the drains / river</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 259-260; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.7.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "WPOL-04", "sub": "water-pollution", "subname": "Thermal & oil pollution", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.4.3.2.7", "pages": "261-262", "url": "subtopic/water-pollution.html#WPOL-04", "snippet": "In 2020 the tanker MV Wakashio ran aground on a coral reef off Mauritius and leaked about 1000 tonnes of fuel oil into a shallow lagoon near mangroves and a marine park.", "text": "in 2020 the tanker mv wakashio ran aground on a coral reef off mauritius and leaked about 1000 tonnes of fuel oil into a shallow lagoon near mangroves and a marine park. explain why inflatable booms were deployed immediately, and give one limitation of booms. describe how skimmers and absorbent materials remove oil, and state how the collected oil is disposed of. explain why dispersants were not used in the lagoon. explain how bioremediation could help remove the oil that remained on the mangrove sediments, and suggest how it could be accelerated. booms are floating tubes with a skirt that restrict the movement of floating oil, keeping it near the source / away from sensitive sites (mangroves, reef, marine park) they work best in sheltered / calm water such as the lagoon limitation: they do not work in open water where currents or waves are strong; oil can flow under the skirt in a strong current skimmers: rotating (metal) discs / belts pick up floating oil which is scraped off and stored absorbent materials (high surface area, eg textile mops, straw, human hair) soak up oil and are then removed collected oil / absorbents are incinerated or sent to landfill (or oil recycled) dispersants break the oil into an emulsion of droplets, making it more mobile so it spreads through the water column (to the reef / seabed organisms) rather than staying on the surface they do not reduce the amount of oil; the dispersant / oil mixture may be toxic to corals and plankton in shallow, enclosed water bacteria that break down hydrocarbons digest the residual oil (most rapid where temperature, oxygen and nutrients are high) accelerated by adding nutrients (nitrates / phosphates) and aerating (ploughing) / irrigating the sediment", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"WPOL-04\" data-qid=\"WPOL-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/water-pollution.html\">Thermal &amp; oil pollution</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.7 &middot; Genn pp. 261-262 &middot; WPOL-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>In 2020 the tanker MV Wakashio ran aground on a coral reef off Mauritius and leaked about 1000 tonnes of fuel oil into a shallow lagoon near mangroves and a marine park.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Explain why inflatable booms were deployed immediately, and give <b>one</b> limitation of booms.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Describe how skimmers and absorbent materials remove oil, and state how the collected oil is disposed of.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain why dispersants were <b>not</b> used in the lagoon.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Explain how bioremediation could help remove the oil that remained on the mangrove sediments, and suggest how it could be accelerated.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Booms are floating tubes with a skirt that restrict the movement of floating oil, keeping it near the source / away from sensitive sites (mangroves, reef, marine park)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They work best in sheltered / calm water such as the lagoon</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Limitation: they do not work in open water where currents or waves are strong; oil can flow under the skirt in a strong current</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Skimmers: rotating (metal) discs / belts pick up floating oil which is scraped off and stored</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Absorbent materials (high surface area, eg textile mops, straw, human hair) soak up oil and are then removed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Collected oil / absorbents are incinerated or sent to landfill (or oil recycled)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Dispersants break the oil into an emulsion of droplets, making it more mobile so it spreads through the water column (to the reef / seabed organisms) rather than staying on the surface</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They do not reduce the amount of oil; the dispersant / oil mixture may be toxic to corals and plankton in shallow, enclosed water</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bacteria that break down hydrocarbons digest the residual oil (most rapid where temperature, oxygen and nutrients are high)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Accelerated by adding nutrients (nitrates / phosphates) and aerating (ploughing) / irrigating the sediment</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 261-262; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.7.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "WPOL-05", "sub": "water-pollution", "subname": "Thermal & oil pollution", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.7", "pages": "258-262", "url": "subtopic/water-pollution.html#WPOL-05", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the number of large oil spills (over 700 tonnes) from tankers worldwide and the amount of oil transported by sea between 1975 and 2015.", "text": "figure 1 shows the number of large oil spills (over 700 tonnes) from tankers worldwide and the amount of oil transported by sea between 1975 and 2015. calculate the percentage increase in oil transported by sea between 1975 and 2015. describe the trend in the number of large oil spills shown in figure 1. suggest three reasons for the decline in the number of large spills despite the increase in oil transported. explain why the total amount of oil pollution entering the sea may not have fallen as much as the number of large tanker spills. (2900 - 1400) / 1400 x 100 = 107% (accept 107.1) large decrease from 24 per year (1975-79) to 2 per year (2010-14) steepest fall between the late 1970s and 1980s; slower decline / levelling off after 1995 double-hulled tankers required for all large tankers improved navigation (gps, ais) and offshore shipping routes inert gas systems reducing explosions twin engines / rudders reducing groundings after mechanical failure stricter regulation, inspection and polluter-pays liability (eg after exxon valdez) separate ballast tanks / crude oil washing most oil pollution comes from dispersed / diffuse sources not tanker accidents: waste lubricating oil, industrial oil in drains, road runoff routine operations: small spills, pipeline leaks, storage tank leakage, oil rig accidents (eg deepwater horizon) growth in oil use and transport means more small releases even if large accidents are rarer", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"WPOL-05\" data-qid=\"WPOL-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/water-pollution.html\">Thermal &amp; oil pollution</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.7 &middot; Genn pp. 258-262 &middot; WPOL-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the number of large oil spills (over 700 tonnes) from tankers worldwide and the amount of oil transported by sea between 1975 and 2015.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<rect x=\"69.0\" y=\"42.7\" width=\"42.0\" height=\"227.3\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"90.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1975</text>\n<rect x=\"129.0\" y=\"184.8\" width=\"42.0\" height=\"85.2\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"150.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1980</text>\n<rect x=\"189.0\" y=\"194.2\" width=\"42.0\" height=\"75.8\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"210.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1985</text>\n<rect x=\"249.0\" y=\"194.2\" width=\"42.0\" height=\"75.8\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"270.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1990</text>\n<rect x=\"309.0\" y=\"232.1\" width=\"42.0\" height=\"37.9\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"330.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1995</text>\n<rect x=\"369.0\" y=\"241.6\" width=\"42.0\" height=\"28.4\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"390.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2000</text>\n<rect x=\"429.0\" y=\"232.1\" width=\"42.0\" height=\"37.9\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"450.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2005</text>\n<rect x=\"489.0\" y=\"251.1\" width=\"42.0\" height=\"18.9\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"510.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2010</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">5.28</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">10.56</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">15.84</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">21.12</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">26.4</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Five-year period beginning</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Number of large spills</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Year</th><th>1975</th><th>1985</th><th>1995</th><th>2005</th><th>2015</th></tr><tr><td>Oil transported by sea / million tonnes</td><td>1 400</td><td>1 200</td><td>1 800</td><td>2 400</td><td>2 900</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Calculate the percentage increase in oil transported by sea between 1975 and 2015.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Describe the trend in the number of large oil spills shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Suggest <b>three</b> reasons for the decline in the number of large spills despite the increase in oil transported.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Explain why the total amount of oil pollution entering the sea may not have fallen as much as the number of large tanker spills.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(2900 - 1400) / 1400 x 100</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 107% (accept 107.1)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Large decrease from 24 per year (1975-79) to 2 per year (2010-14)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Steepest fall between the late 1970s and 1980s; slower decline / levelling off after 1995</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Double-hulled tankers required for all large tankers</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Improved navigation (GPS, AIS) and offshore shipping routes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Inert gas systems reducing explosions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Twin engines / rudders reducing groundings after mechanical failure</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stricter regulation, inspection and polluter-pays liability (eg after Exxon Valdez)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Separate ballast tanks / crude oil washing</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Most oil pollution comes from dispersed / diffuse sources not tanker accidents: waste lubricating oil, industrial oil in drains, road runoff</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Routine operations: small spills, pipeline leaks, storage tank leakage, oil rig accidents (eg Deepwater Horizon)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Growth in oil use and transport means more small releases even if large accidents are rarer</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 258-262; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.7.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "WPOL-06", "sub": "water-pollution", "subname": "Thermal & oil pollution", "topic": "Pollution", "tslug": "pollution", "papers": [1], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.4.3.2.6-3.4.3.2.7", "pages": "254-262", "url": "subtopic/water-pollution.html#WPOL-06", "snippet": "Which of the following oil spill treatments is most likely to kill organisms living deep in beach sediments that were not affected by the oil? Tick one box.", "text": " which of the following oil spill treatments is most likely to kill organisms living deep in beach sediments that were not affected by the oil? tick one box. explain how polymerising agents make an oil spill easier to clean up. explain why deoxygenation is a more common problem in water than in air. b - steam washing (hot water / steam jets kill organisms in the sediment) chemicals cause the oil molecules to join together / solidify producing more solid material that can be collected / lifted more easily than liquid oil air contains about 210 cm3 of oxygen per litre whereas water at 20 °c holds a maximum of about 6 cm3 per litre (less than 3% of the amount in air) so a small amount of oxygen-consuming activity (decomposition, respiration) or warming can remove most of the oxygen; many aquatic organisms are near the lower end of their range of tolerance", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"WPOL-06\" data-qid=\"WPOL-06\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/water-pollution.html\">Thermal &amp; oil pollution</a> &middot; spec 3.4.3.2.6-3.4.3.2.7 &middot; Genn pp. 254-262 &middot; WPOL-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Which of the following oil spill treatments is most likely to kill organisms living deep in beach sediments that were not affected by the oil? Tick one box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; Absorbent mops</li><li>B&nbsp; Steam washing</li><li>C&nbsp; Inflatable booms</li><li>D&nbsp; Polymerising agents</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain how polymerising agents make an oil spill easier to clean up.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain why deoxygenation is a more common problem in water than in air.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b></b>&nbsp; B - Steam washing (hot water / steam jets kill organisms in the sediment)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Chemicals cause the oil molecules to join together / solidify</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Producing more solid material that can be collected / lifted more easily than liquid oil</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Air contains about 210 cm<sup>3</sup> of oxygen per litre whereas water at 20 &deg;C holds a maximum of about 6 cm<sup>3</sup> per litre (less than 3% of the amount in air)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So a small amount of oxygen-consuming activity (decomposition, respiration) or warming can remove most of the oxygen; many aquatic organisms are near the lower end of their range of tolerance</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 254-262; AQA spec 3.4.3.2.6-3.4.3.2.7.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AGR-01", "sub": "agroecosystems", "subname": "Agroecosystems: abiotic & biotic factors", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.5.1.1", "pages": "306-309", "url": "subtopic/agroecosystems.html#AGR-01", "snippet": "Table 1 shows climate data for two sites where a farmer is considering growing maize, a frost-sensitive crop that needs a thermal growing season of at least 120 days.", "text": "table 1 shows climate data for two sites where a farmer is considering growing maize, a frost-sensitive crop that needs a thermal growing season of at least 120 days. use table 1 to explain which site is more suitable for maize. describe three ways in which temperature can be controlled to increase crop productivity. explain why keeping livestock warm increases the amount of food converted to growth. explain how day length affects livestock production and how it can be manipulated. b: frost-free period (150 days) exceeds the 120 needed whereas a (110) is too short - late frosts would kill seedlings b is warmer / longer thermal growing season because south-facing slopes receive more insolation; valley floors are frost pockets where cold dense air collects but b has lower rainfall and a steeper slope (erosion / machinery difficulties) so irrigation / contour cultivation may be needed greenhouses / polytunnels heated by solar gain or burning gas / paraffin (ventilated to prevent overheating) woven plastic cloches / fleece over rows to retain warm air near the ground burning oil / gas or using large fans in orchards to prevent frost damage to blossom site selection (aspect, avoiding frost pockets); heated buildings and shelter for livestock mammals and birds use food energy to maintain a constant body temperature (thermoregulation) reducing heat loss (shelter, heated buildings) means less food energy is used for heat and more is available for growth long days increase milk production and egg laying; poultry grow best with short days; sheep mate as days shorten in autumn artificial lighting extends the growing season for greenhouse crops or simulates autumn in spring to give sheep a second mating / lambing season", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AGR-01\" data-qid=\"AGR-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/agroecosystems.html\">Agroecosystems: abiotic &amp; biotic factors</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.1 &middot; Genn pp. 306-309 &middot; AGR-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows climate data for two sites where a farmer is considering growing maize, a frost-sensitive crop that needs a thermal growing season of at least 120 days.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Site A: valley floor</th><th>Site B: south-facing slope</th></tr><tr><td>Frost-free period / days</td><td>110</td><td>150</td></tr><tr><td>Days with mean temperature above 5 &deg;C</td><td>160</td><td>175</td></tr><tr><td>Mean summer rainfall / mm</td><td>180</td><td>150</td></tr><tr><td>Gradient / %</td><td>1</td><td>8</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Use Table 1 to explain which site is more suitable for maize.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Describe <b>three</b> ways in which temperature can be controlled to increase crop productivity.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain why keeping livestock warm increases the amount of food converted to growth.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Explain how day length affects livestock production and how it can be manipulated.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>B: frost-free period (150 days) exceeds the 120 needed whereas A (110) is too short - late frosts would kill seedlings</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>B is warmer / longer thermal growing season because south-facing slopes receive more insolation; valley floors are frost pockets where cold dense air collects</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>But B has lower rainfall and a steeper slope (erosion / machinery difficulties) so irrigation / contour cultivation may be needed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Greenhouses / polytunnels heated by solar gain or burning gas / paraffin (ventilated to prevent overheating)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Woven plastic cloches / fleece over rows to retain warm air near the ground</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Burning oil / gas or using large fans in orchards to prevent frost damage to blossom</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Site selection (aspect, avoiding frost pockets); heated buildings and shelter for livestock</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mammals and birds use food energy to maintain a constant body temperature (thermoregulation)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reducing heat loss (shelter, heated buildings) means less food energy is used for heat and more is available for growth</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Long days increase milk production and egg laying; poultry grow best with short days; sheep mate as days shorten in autumn</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Artificial lighting extends the growing season for greenhouse crops or simulates autumn in spring to give sheep a second mating / lambing season</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 306-309; AQA spec 3.5.1.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AGR-02", "sub": "agroecosystems", "subname": "Agroecosystems: abiotic & biotic factors", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.1.1", "pages": "308-310, 313", "url": "subtopic/agroecosystems.html#AGR-02", "snippet": "Explain why a moderate water shortage reduces crop productivity even though the plants survive.", "text": " explain why a moderate water shortage reduces crop productivity even though the plants survive. explain the problems caused by waterlogged soils and describe two ways farmers reduce soil water levels. explain how irrigating with groundwater can cause soil salinisation and how this harms crops. state how a farmer could raise the ph of an acidic soil and explain why a low ph reduces fertility. plants close their stomata to reduce transpiration losses closed stomata stop the absorption of carbon dioxide so photosynthesis and growth stop less water for nutrient absorption / transport and cell turgidity; severe shortage dehydrates cells and inhibits biochemical reactions anaerobic soil favours denitrifying bacteria and inhibits nitrifying bacteria so nitrate is lost faster than it forms; higher risk of fungal disease; root respiration inhibited drainage ditches or pipes deep ploughing; avoiding compaction by machinery / livestock; adding organic matter to encourage worms groundwater often contains dissolved salts; when the water evaporates from the soil the salts are left behind and accumulate high soil salinity causes osmotic dehydration of roots / crop death; washing out with extra water uses more water and salinises rivers add crushed lime (calcium carbonate / hydroxide); low ph increases leaching, inhibits nutrient uptake and mobilises toxic aluminium and lead", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AGR-02\" data-qid=\"AGR-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/agroecosystems.html\">Agroecosystems: abiotic &amp; biotic factors</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.1 &middot; Genn pp. 308-310, 313 &middot; AGR-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain why a moderate water shortage reduces crop productivity even though the plants survive.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain the problems caused by waterlogged soils and describe <b>two</b> ways farmers reduce soil water levels.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain how irrigating with groundwater can cause soil salinisation and how this harms crops.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>State how a farmer could raise the pH of an acidic soil and explain why a low pH reduces fertility.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Plants close their stomata to reduce transpiration losses</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Closed stomata stop the absorption of carbon dioxide so photosynthesis and growth stop</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Less water for nutrient absorption / transport and cell turgidity; severe shortage dehydrates cells and inhibits biochemical reactions</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 4 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Anaerobic soil favours denitrifying bacteria and inhibits nitrifying bacteria so nitrate is lost faster than it forms; higher risk of fungal disease; root respiration inhibited</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Drainage ditches or pipes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Deep ploughing; avoiding compaction by machinery / livestock; adding organic matter to encourage worms</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Groundwater often contains dissolved salts; when the water evaporates from the soil the salts are left behind and accumulate</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>High soil salinity causes osmotic dehydration of roots / crop death; washing out with extra water uses more water and salinises rivers</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Add crushed lime (calcium carbonate / hydroxide); low pH increases leaching, inhibits nutrient uptake and mobilises toxic aluminium and lead</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 308-310, 313; AQA spec 3.5.1.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AGR-03", "sub": "agroecosystems", "subname": "Agroecosystems: abiotic & biotic factors", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.5.1.1", "pages": "310-312", "url": "subtopic/agroecosystems.html#AGR-03", "snippet": "Table 1 compares organic and inorganic fertilisers.", "text": "table 1 compares organic and inorganic fertilisers. complete table 1 by giving two advantages and two disadvantages of each type of fertiliser. explain how hydroponic cultivation maximises productivity and give two disadvantages. organic advantages: often waste products, locally available and cheap; add humus improving structure and water retention; increase soil biota; slow release reduces leaching organic disadvantages: nutrient composition cannot be controlled; released slowly so needs long-term planning; bulky with high water content so expensive to transport; cannot be applied to a growing crop; odour / pathogens inorganic advantages: composition controlled to match crop needs; nutrients released rapidly; concentrated and easy to apply inorganic disadvantages: high energy (fossil fuel) manufacture (haber process); no organic matter so humus and biota decline; some toxic to worms; high solubility so leached causing eutrophication; raw materials (phosphate rock) finite crops grown in a circulating nutrient solution (inert medium) in greenhouses; nutrient concentrations monitored and adjusted so nutrients are never limiting; abiotic factors controlled roots are small (all in contact with nutrients) so more growth goes into the harvested crop; no soil-borne pathogens or weeds; crop stays fresh with roots disadvantages: high inputs of nutrients and energy (heating, lighting, pumping); high level of technical knowledge; only economic for high-value crops", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AGR-03\" data-qid=\"AGR-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/agroecosystems.html\">Agroecosystems: abiotic &amp; biotic factors</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.1 &middot; Genn pp. 310-312 &middot; AGR-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 compares organic and inorganic fertilisers.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Complete Table 1 by giving two advantages and two disadvantages of each type of fertiliser.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Fertiliser</th><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr><tr><td>Organic (manure, sewage sludge, compost)</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Inorganic (eg ammonium nitrate, NPK)</td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain how hydroponic cultivation maximises productivity and give <b>two</b> disadvantages.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 6 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Organic advantages: often waste products, locally available and cheap; add humus improving structure and water retention; increase soil biota; slow release reduces leaching</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Organic disadvantages: nutrient composition cannot be controlled; released slowly so needs long-term planning; bulky with high water content so expensive to transport; cannot be applied to a growing crop; odour / pathogens</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Inorganic advantages: composition controlled to match crop needs; nutrients released rapidly; concentrated and easy to apply</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Inorganic disadvantages: high energy (fossil fuel) manufacture (Haber process); no organic matter so humus and biota decline; some toxic to worms; high solubility so leached causing eutrophication; raw materials (phosphate rock) finite</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 4 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Crops grown in a circulating nutrient solution (inert medium) in greenhouses; nutrient concentrations monitored and adjusted so nutrients are never limiting; abiotic factors controlled</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Roots are small (all in contact with nutrients) so more growth goes into the harvested crop; no soil-borne pathogens or weeds; crop stays fresh with roots</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Disadvantages: high inputs of nutrients and energy (heating, lighting, pumping); high level of technical knowledge; only economic for high-value crops</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 310-312; AQA spec 3.5.1.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AGR-04", "sub": "agroecosystems", "subname": "Agroecosystems: abiotic & biotic factors", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.5.1.1", "pages": "314-318", "url": "subtopic/agroecosystems.html#AGR-04", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the yield of a vegetable crop under different pest control methods over three years on one farm.", "text": "table 1 shows the yield of a vegetable crop under different pest control methods over three years on one farm. explain how crop rotation and beetle banks reduce pest damage. use table 1 to evaluate integrated control compared with the other two methods. describe how the sterile male technique controls insect pests and explain why it is species-specific. rotation: each crop has its own pests; growing a different crop each year (4-5 year cycle) means pests left at the end of a season die before that crop returns, so populations cannot build up early beetle banks / hedgerows provide habitat and overwintering sites for natural predators (ground beetles, ladybirds) that eat aphids rotation also evens out nutrient demands and includes legumes integrated control gives almost the same yield as chemical control (18.1 vs 18.5) with far fewer applications (1-2 vs 6) it retains most of the biodiversity of cultural control (26 vs 31 species) whereas chemical control alone leaves only 8 cultural control alone loses about 12% yield; integrated control combines cultural methods, resistant varieties and carefully timed specific non-persistent pesticides only when essential - best balance of yield, cost and impact large numbers of males are sterilised with gamma radiation and released females of many insects mate only once and store sperm, so mating with a sterile male produces no offspring; enough sterile males reduce fertile matings below replacement level (screw worm fly, mediterranean fruit fly) only the target species' females mate with them so non-target species are unaffected; fails if sterilised males behave abnormally or re-colonisation occurs", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AGR-04\" data-qid=\"AGR-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/agroecosystems.html\">Agroecosystems: abiotic &amp; biotic factors</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.1 &middot; Genn pp. 314-318 &middot; AGR-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the yield of a vegetable crop under different pest control methods over three years on one farm.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Method</th><th>Mean yield / t ha<sup>-1</sup></th><th>Pesticide applications per year</th><th>Non-target insect species recorded in field margins</th></tr><tr><td>Chemical insecticide only</td><td>18.5</td><td>6</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>Crop rotation + beetle banks + hedgerows</td><td>16.2</td><td>0</td><td>31</td></tr><tr><td>Integrated control</td><td>18.1</td><td>1-2</td><td>26</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Explain how crop rotation and beetle banks reduce pest damage.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Use Table 1 to evaluate integrated control compared with the other two methods.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Describe how the sterile male technique controls insect pests and explain why it is species-specific.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 4 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Rotation: each crop has its own pests; growing a different crop each year (4-5 year cycle) means pests left at the end of a season die before that crop returns, so populations cannot build up early</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Beetle banks / hedgerows provide habitat and overwintering sites for natural predators (ground beetles, ladybirds) that eat aphids</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Rotation also evens out nutrient demands and includes legumes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Integrated control gives almost the same yield as chemical control (18.1 vs 18.5) with far fewer applications (1-2 vs 6)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It retains most of the biodiversity of cultural control (26 vs 31 species) whereas chemical control alone leaves only 8</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cultural control alone loses about 12% yield; integrated control combines cultural methods, resistant varieties and carefully timed specific non-persistent pesticides only when essential - best balance of yield, cost and impact</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Large numbers of males are sterilised with gamma radiation and released</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Females of many insects mate only once and store sperm, so mating with a sterile male produces no offspring; enough sterile males reduce fertile matings below replacement level (screw worm fly, Mediterranean fruit fly)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Only the target species' females mate with them so non-target species are unaffected; fails if sterilised males behave abnormally or re-colonisation occurs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 314-318; AQA spec 3.5.1.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AGR-05", "sub": "agroecosystems", "subname": "Agroecosystems: abiotic & biotic factors", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.1.1", "pages": "316-320", "url": "subtopic/agroecosystems.html#AGR-05", "snippet": "Describe two case studies of biological control, one successful and one that caused problems.", "text": " describe two case studies of biological control, one successful and one that caused problems. explain how pheromone traps can be used in pest control. explain why the use of antibiotics as growth promoters in livestock is a concern for human health. give one way farmers can support crop pollinators. success: cactoblastis moth introduced from south america controlled prickly pear cactus in australia; encarsia parasitic wasps control greenhouse whitefly problems: cane toads introduced to australia to control sugar cane beetles did not control them but ate many other species and are toxic to predators; rosy wolfsnail introduced to polynesia to control african land snails ate endemic partulid snails (48 species extinct); harlequin ladybirds introduced to europe eat native ladybirds lesson: control agents should be specialist feeders on the pest release an artificial sex scent attracting males (or females) of the pest species used to monitor when the pest arrives so pesticide is timed correctly, or (in enclosed greenhouses) to trap enough of one sex to prevent breeding regular low doses kill only the most sensitive bacteria so resistant strains survive and multiply agricultural use exceeds medical use, so resistance spreads resistant zoonotic bacteria (e. coli, salmonella, campylobacter) can transfer to humans and are harder to treat; hence reduced use of antibiotics is a sustainability goal grow nectar-rich flowering plants alongside crops; restrict pesticides harmful to bees / spray when not in flower; introduce bee hives", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AGR-05\" data-qid=\"AGR-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/agroecosystems.html\">Agroecosystems: abiotic &amp; biotic factors</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.1 &middot; Genn pp. 316-320 &middot; AGR-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Describe <b>two</b> case studies of biological control, one successful and one that caused problems.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain how pheromone traps can be used in pest control.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain why the use of antibiotics as growth promoters in livestock is a concern for human health.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Give <b>one</b> way farmers can support crop pollinators.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 4 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Success: Cactoblastis moth introduced from South America controlled prickly pear cactus in Australia; Encarsia parasitic wasps control greenhouse whitefly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Problems: cane toads introduced to Australia to control sugar cane beetles did not control them but ate many other species and are toxic to predators; rosy wolfsnail introduced to Polynesia to control African land snails ate endemic Partulid snails (48 species extinct); harlequin ladybirds introduced to Europe eat native ladybirds</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Lesson: control agents should be specialist feeders on the pest</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Release an artificial sex scent attracting males (or females) of the pest species</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Used to monitor when the pest arrives so pesticide is timed correctly, or (in enclosed greenhouses) to trap enough of one sex to prevent breeding</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Regular low doses kill only the most sensitive bacteria so resistant strains survive and multiply</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Agricultural use exceeds medical use, so resistance spreads</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Resistant zoonotic bacteria (E. coli, Salmonella, Campylobacter) can transfer to humans and are harder to treat; hence reduced use of antibiotics is a sustainability goal</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Grow nectar-rich flowering plants alongside crops; restrict pesticides harmful to bees / spray when not in flower; introduce bee hives</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 316-320; AQA spec 3.5.1.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FS-01", "sub": "food-species", "subname": "Manipulation of food species & agricultural energetics", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.1.2", "pages": "321-323", "url": "subtopic/food-species.html#FS-01", "snippet": "Compare selective breeding, asexual reproduction and cross-breeding as methods of improving livestock or crops.", "text": " compare selective breeding, asexual reproduction and cross-breeding as methods of improving livestock or crops. explain how artificial insemination and embryo transfer increase the rate at which desirable characteristics spread through a livestock population. selective breeding: parents chosen for desirable genetic characteristics (limousin muscle growth, merino wool, cheviot hardiness); combines genes but characteristics of offspring not fully predictable; many generations needed; risk of inbreeding asexual reproduction (cuttings, runners, tissue culture, cloning): offspring genetically identical to the parent so characteristics are predictable and survival high; but no variation so no improvement and vulnerability to the same disease; cloning of animals (nucleus transfer into an enucleated egg, surrogate) still developing cross-breeding: two breeds combined to give a mix of characteristics with hybrid vigour (heterosis) and lower inbreeding risk, eg zebu (heat tolerant) x ayrshire (high milk yield) ai: semen from a male with desirable characteristics can fertilise many more females than natural mating; frozen semen stored and transported easily and used after the male dies embryo transfer: fsh stimulates a superior female to release many eggs, which are fertilised (ivf) with sperm from a superior male each embryo is implanted in a different surrogate female so the donor produces many offspring per year rather than one per pregnancy", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FS-01\" data-qid=\"FS-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/food-species.html\">Manipulation of food species &amp; agricultural energetics</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 321-323 &middot; FS-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Compare selective breeding, asexual reproduction and cross-breeding as methods of improving livestock or crops.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain how artificial insemination and embryo transfer increase the rate at which desirable characteristics spread through a livestock population.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Selective breeding: parents chosen for desirable genetic characteristics (Limousin muscle growth, Merino wool, Cheviot hardiness); combines genes but characteristics of offspring not fully predictable; many generations needed; risk of inbreeding</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Asexual reproduction (cuttings, runners, tissue culture, cloning): offspring genetically identical to the parent so characteristics are predictable and survival high; but no variation so no improvement and vulnerability to the same disease; cloning of animals (nucleus transfer into an enucleated egg, surrogate) still developing</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Cross-breeding: two breeds combined to give a mix of characteristics with hybrid vigour (heterosis) and lower inbreeding risk, eg Zebu (heat tolerant) x Ayrshire (high milk yield)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>AI: semen from a male with desirable characteristics can fertilise many more females than natural mating; frozen semen stored and transported easily and used after the male dies</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Embryo transfer: FSH stimulates a superior female to release many eggs, which are fertilised (IVF) with sperm from a superior male</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Each embryo is implanted in a different surrogate female</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So the donor produces many offspring per year rather than one per pregnancy</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 321-323; AQA spec 3.5.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FS-02", "sub": "food-species", "subname": "Manipulation of food species & agricultural energetics", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.5.1.2", "pages": "323-324", "url": "subtopic/food-species.html#FS-02", "snippet": "Table 1 describes three genetically modified (GM) crops.", "text": "table 1 describes three genetically modified (gm) crops. explain the main advantage of gm over conventional selective breeding. for each crop in table 1, explain one potential benefit. explain four concerns about gm crops. give one reason why gm crops have been slow to enter cultivation in europe. single genes can be transferred from any species, not just closely related ones that produce fertile hybrids desired characteristics introduced without linked unwanted ones; faster bt: reduces insecticide spraying so fewer non-target deaths, lower cost / energy roundup ready: weeds controlled by spraying glyphosate without harming the crop, so higher yields with less cultivation / tillage golden rice: provides vitamin a in the staple diet, reducing about 500 000 cases of blindness a year in india / sub-saharan africa gene transfer by pollen to conventional / organic crops or wild relatives (eg bt gene killing insects feeding on wild plants) antibiotic-resistance marker genes could transfer to pathogenic bacteria; possible allergies; gene transfer to gut bacteria detection in the human food chain from crops approved only for animal feed economic: seeds must be bought each year from patent-holding companies, raising costs and reducing local crop diversity and seed saving in ledcs; herbicide-resistant crops may increase herbicide use public / political concern that risks are not fully assessed - more evidence needed (precautionary principle)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FS-02\" data-qid=\"FS-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/food-species.html\">Manipulation of food species &amp; agricultural energetics</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 323-324 &middot; FS-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 describes three genetically modified (GM) crops.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>GM crop</th><th>Gene source</th><th>Characteristic</th></tr><tr><td>Bt maize / cotton</td><td>Bacillus thuringiensis bacterium</td><td>Produces a toxin that kills insect pests</td></tr><tr><td>Roundup Ready soya</td><td>Agrobacterium</td><td>Resistant to the herbicide glyphosate</td></tr><tr><td>Golden rice</td><td>Daffodil and Erwinia bacterium</td><td>Produces vitamin A in the grain</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain the main advantage of GM over conventional selective breeding.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>For each crop in Table 1, explain one potential benefit.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain <b>four</b> concerns about GM crops.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Give <b>one</b> reason why GM crops have been slow to enter cultivation in Europe.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Single genes can be transferred from any species, not just closely related ones that produce fertile hybrids</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Desired characteristics introduced without linked unwanted ones; faster</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bt: reduces insecticide spraying so fewer non-target deaths, lower cost / energy</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Roundup Ready: weeds controlled by spraying glyphosate without harming the crop, so higher yields with less cultivation / tillage</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Golden rice: provides vitamin A in the staple diet, reducing about 500 000 cases of blindness a year in India / sub-Saharan Africa</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Gene transfer by pollen to conventional / organic crops or wild relatives (eg Bt gene killing insects feeding on wild plants)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Antibiotic-resistance marker genes could transfer to pathogenic bacteria; possible allergies; gene transfer to gut bacteria</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Detection in the human food chain from crops approved only for animal feed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Economic: seeds must be bought each year from patent-holding companies, raising costs and reducing local crop diversity and seed saving in LEDCs; herbicide-resistant crops may increase herbicide use</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Public / political concern that risks are not fully assessed - more evidence needed (precautionary principle)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 323-324; AQA spec 3.5.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FS-03", "sub": "food-species", "subname": "Manipulation of food species & agricultural energetics", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.5.1.2", "pages": "324-327", "url": "subtopic/food-species.html#FS-03", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the energy inputs and food energy outputs of three farming systems.", "text": "table 1 shows the energy inputs and food energy outputs of three farming systems. calculate the energy ratio (output / input) for each system. use your answers to explain the difference between productivity and efficiency. explain what is meant by an energy subsidy and give three examples. explain why spreading the same total inputs over a larger area would increase total food production. extensive cereal 5.0; intensive cereal 3.0 intensive beef 0.1 intensive cereal is most productive per hectare (60 gj) but less efficient (3.0) than extensive (5.0) each extra unit of input gives a smaller extra yield (law of diminishing returns) beef has very low productivity and efficiency because energy is lost at an extra trophic level any input that increases productivity but requires the use of energy (usually fossil fuel) manufacture of nitrate fertiliser (haber process) and pesticides; fuel for machinery; pumping irrigation water; grain drying; food processing and transport; embodied energy of machinery because of diminishing returns, the first units of input on unfertilised land give the largest yield increases moving inputs from intensively farmed land (where extra input adds little) to extensively farmed land gives a greater total yield - though land, cost and distribution limit this", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FS-03\" data-qid=\"FS-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/food-species.html\">Manipulation of food species &amp; agricultural energetics</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 324-327 &middot; FS-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the energy inputs and food energy outputs of three farming systems.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>System</th><th>Energy input / GJ ha<sup>-1</sup> yr<sup>-1</sup></th><th>Food energy output / GJ ha<sup>-1</sup> yr<sup>-1</sup></th></tr><tr><td>Extensive cereal farming (low inputs)</td><td>4</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>Intensive cereal farming (fertiliser, pesticide, machinery)</td><td>20</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>Intensive beef (grain-fed)</td><td>40</td><td>4</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Calculate the energy ratio (output / input) for each system.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Use your answers to explain the difference between productivity and efficiency.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain what is meant by an <b>energy subsidy</b> and give <b>three</b> examples.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Explain why spreading the same total inputs over a larger area would increase total food production.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Extensive cereal 5.0; intensive cereal 3.0</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Intensive beef 0.1</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Intensive cereal is most productive per hectare (60 GJ) but less efficient (3.0) than extensive (5.0)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Each extra unit of input gives a smaller extra yield (law of diminishing returns)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Beef has very low productivity and efficiency because energy is lost at an extra trophic level</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 3 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Any input that increases productivity but requires the use of energy (usually fossil fuel)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Manufacture of nitrate fertiliser (Haber process) and pesticides; fuel for machinery; pumping irrigation water; grain drying; food processing and transport; embodied energy of machinery</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Because of diminishing returns, the first units of input on unfertilised land give the largest yield increases</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Moving inputs from intensively farmed land (where extra input adds little) to extensively farmed land gives a greater total yield - though land, cost and distribution limit this</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 324-327; AQA spec 3.5.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FS-04", "sub": "food-species", "subname": "Manipulation of food species & agricultural energetics", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.5.1.2", "pages": "326-328", "url": "subtopic/food-species.html#FS-04", "snippet": "Table 1 shows food conversion ratios (FCR) for livestock: the mass of feed needed to produce 1 kg of live mass gain.", "text": "table 1 shows food conversion ratios (fcr) for livestock: the mass of feed needed to produce 1 kg of live mass gain. use table 1 to explain why intensive poultry and fish production convert feed to food more efficiently than beef. explain three ways farmers reduce food chain energy losses in livestock production. explain why growing crops for direct human consumption usually produces more food per hectare than rearing livestock, and describe one situation where livestock is the better option. chicken and salmon need far less feed per kg gained (1.2-1.8 vs 7.0) fish are ectotherms so no energy is used for thermoregulation and water supports their mass; poultry grow fast and are kept warm indoors with movement restricted cattle are large endotherms, use energy in movement, and lose energy in methane from cellulose digestion control of movement (housing / restricted grazing) so less energy is used in muscle activity temperature control (heated / insulated buildings) so less energy is used in maintaining body temperature species selection: choose species with low fcrs (poultry, fish); high-energy easily digested diets; slaughter at the fastest growth stage only about 10% of the energy at one trophic level is passed to the next; most is lost as heat in respiration / metabolism or in undigested material humans eating plants (primary consumers) receive more of the energy captured by photosynthesis than eating animals fed on those plants (secondary consumers) livestock better where land cannot grow crops (upland, semi-arid, steep, thin soils): grazing animals with symbiotic gut bacteria digest cellulose in grass that humans cannot omnivores (pigs) convert food wastes (dairy by-products, catering waste) into meat", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FS-04\" data-qid=\"FS-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/food-species.html\">Manipulation of food species &amp; agricultural energetics</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 326-328 &middot; FS-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows food conversion ratios (FCR) for livestock: the mass of feed needed to produce 1 kg of live mass gain.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Livestock</th><th>FCR / kg feed per kg gain</th></tr><tr><td>Farmed salmon</td><td>1.2</td></tr><tr><td>Chicken</td><td>1.8</td></tr><tr><td>Pork</td><td>3.0</td></tr><tr><td>Beef (feedlot)</td><td>7.0</td></tr><tr><td>Sheep</td><td>8.0</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Use Table 1 to explain why intensive poultry and fish production convert feed to food more efficiently than beef.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain <b>three</b> ways farmers reduce food chain energy losses in livestock production.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain why growing crops for direct human consumption usually produces more food per hectare than rearing livestock, and describe <b>one</b> situation where livestock is the better option.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Chicken and salmon need far less feed per kg gained (1.2-1.8 vs 7.0)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fish are ectotherms so no energy is used for thermoregulation and water supports their mass; poultry grow fast and are kept warm indoors with movement restricted</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cattle are large endotherms, use energy in movement, and lose energy in methane from cellulose digestion</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Control of movement (housing / restricted grazing) so less energy is used in muscle activity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Temperature control (heated / insulated buildings) so less energy is used in maintaining body temperature</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Species selection: choose species with low FCRs (poultry, fish); high-energy easily digested diets; slaughter at the fastest growth stage</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Only about 10% of the energy at one trophic level is passed to the next; most is lost as heat in respiration / metabolism or in undigested material</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Humans eating plants (primary consumers) receive more of the energy captured by photosynthesis than eating animals fed on those plants (secondary consumers)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Livestock better where land cannot grow crops (upland, semi-arid, steep, thin soils): grazing animals with symbiotic gut bacteria digest cellulose in grass that humans cannot</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Omnivores (pigs) convert food wastes (dairy by-products, catering waste) into meat</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 326-328; AQA spec 3.5.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FS-05", "sub": "food-species", "subname": "Manipulation of food species & agricultural energetics", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 5, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.1.2", "pages": "321-322", "url": "subtopic/food-species.html#FS-05", "snippet": "State what is meant by hybrid vigour.", "text": " state what is meant by hybrid vigour. explain how cross-breeding zebu and ayrshire cattle produced a breed suited to hot climates. give two potential applications of cloning to livestock farming. cross-bred offspring are healthier / more productive because the problems caused by homozygous recessive genes in inbred varieties are reduced (heterosis) zebu tolerate heat but have a low milk yield; ayrshire have a high milk yield but are not heat tolerant cross-breeding combined the desirable characteristics: heat tolerance with a high milk yield replacement of valuable animals that die with genetically identical individuals replacement of herds culled during a disease outbreak production of large numbers of individuals with desirable characteristics", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FS-05\" data-qid=\"FS-05\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/food-species.html\">Manipulation of food species &amp; agricultural energetics</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 321-322 &middot; FS-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>State what is meant by <b>hybrid vigour</b>.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain how cross-breeding Zebu and Ayrshire cattle produced a breed suited to hot climates.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Give <b>two</b> potential applications of cloning to livestock farming.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cross-bred offspring are healthier / more productive because the problems caused by homozygous recessive genes in inbred varieties are reduced (heterosis)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Zebu tolerate heat but have a low milk yield; Ayrshire have a high milk yield but are not heat tolerant</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cross-breeding combined the desirable characteristics: heat tolerance with a high milk yield</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Replacement of valuable animals that die with genetically identical individuals</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Replacement of herds culled during a disease outbreak</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Production of large numbers of individuals with desirable characteristics</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 321-322; AQA spec 3.5.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FS-06", "sub": "food-species", "subname": "Manipulation of food species & agricultural energetics", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.5.1.2", "pages": "326-328", "url": "subtopic/food-species.html#FS-06", "snippet": "Table 1 shows typical energy values for an agroecosystem. Values are units of energy remaining from 100 000 units of solar energy.", "text": "table 1 shows typical energy values for an agroecosystem. values are units of energy remaining from 100 000 units of solar energy. calculate the percentage of the solar energy reaching the crop that is stored in crop biomass. explain why so little of the solar energy is stored in the crop. explain why few food chains have more than four trophic levels. explain why rearing sheep or cattle is the best option for food production in upland and semi-arid areas. explain how pigs can increase the efficiency of a food production system. 1000 / 100 000 x 100 = 1% some light is reflected by the leaves some light is transmitted (passes through the leaves without being absorbed) or is of wavelengths not absorbed much of the energy captured is used in the plant's metabolic processes and released as low energy-density heat (respiration) less energy is passed to each trophic level than was received from the previous one (heat losses in respiration) after four levels too little energy remains to support a further population permanent grassland is a more sustainable land use than arable crops in these areas (soil / rainfall unsuitable for crops) grazing animals can digest grass cellulose using symbiotic bacteria in their stomachs, producing food that humans can eat pigs are omnivores that can eat food wastes not wanted by people (eg cheese-making wastes, catering leftovers) so edible meat is produced from material that would otherwise be wasted, without using land / crops that could feed people", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FS-06\" data-qid=\"FS-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/food-species.html\">Manipulation of food species &amp; agricultural energetics</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 326-328 &middot; FS-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows typical energy values for an agroecosystem. Values are units of energy remaining from 100 000 units of solar energy.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Energy / arbitrary units</th></tr><tr><td>Solar energy reaching the crop</td><td>100 000</td></tr><tr><td>Energy captured during photosynthesis</td><td>4 000</td></tr><tr><td>Energy stored in crop biomass</td><td>1 000</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Calculate the percentage of the solar energy reaching the crop that is stored in crop biomass.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain why so little of the solar energy is stored in the crop.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain why few food chains have more than four trophic levels.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Explain why rearing sheep or cattle is the best option for food production in upland and semi-arid areas.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.5</span>Explain how pigs can increase the efficiency of a food production system.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>1000 / 100 000 x 100 = 1%</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Some light is reflected by the leaves</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Some light is transmitted (passes through the leaves without being absorbed) or is of wavelengths not absorbed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Much of the energy captured is used in the plant's metabolic processes and released as low energy-density heat (respiration)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Less energy is passed to each trophic level than was received from the previous one (heat losses in respiration)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>After four levels too little energy remains to support a further population</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Permanent grassland is a more sustainable land use than arable crops in these areas (soil / rainfall unsuitable for crops)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Grazing animals can digest grass cellulose using symbiotic bacteria in their stomachs, producing food that humans can eat</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pigs are omnivores that can eat food wastes not wanted by people (eg cheese-making wastes, catering leftovers)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So edible meat is produced from material that would otherwise be wasted, without using land / crops that could feed people</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 326-328; AQA spec 3.5.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FS-07", "sub": "food-species", "subname": "Manipulation of food species & agricultural energetics", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.5.1.2", "pages": "324-325", "url": "subtopic/food-species.html#FS-07", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the effect of increasing inputs (fertiliser, water and pesticides) on the yield of a crop.", "text": "figure 1 shows the effect of increasing inputs (fertiliser, water and pesticides) on the yield of a crop. describe the relationship shown in figure 1. calculate the extra yield produced by the second unit of input and by the fifth unit of input. explain why, if enough land is available, spreading inputs evenly over a large area gives a greater total yield than concentrating them on a small area. suggest why inputs are nevertheless often used unevenly across the world. explain why intensive agriculture may be very productive but not very efficient. yield increases as inputs increase but each extra unit of input produces a smaller increase in yield than the previous one (the curve flattens / law of diminishing returns) second unit: 70 - 50 = 20 units fifth unit: 94 - 90 = 4 units the first units of input on each area give the largest yield increases concentrating inputs means later units add very little, whereas the same inputs spread thinly all work at the steep part of the curve some areas have much greater potential productivity so investment there is sensible affluent societies can afford to buy more inputs while poorer communities that would benefit most cannot afford them (economic reasons) yield per unit area is very high because of large inputs but the extra yield per unit of input is small, so efficiency (yield per unit input / energy ratio) is low", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FS-07\" data-qid=\"FS-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/food-species.html\">Manipulation of food species &amp; agricultural energetics</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 324-325 &middot; FS-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the effect of increasing inputs (fertiliser, water and pesticides) on the yield of a crop.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,220.0 156.0,145.0 252.0,95.0 348.0,65.0 444.0,45.0 540.0,35.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"220.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"156.0\" cy=\"145.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"252.0\" cy=\"95.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"348.0\" cy=\"65.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"444.0\" cy=\"45.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"35.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">20</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">40</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">60</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">80</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">5</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">100</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Inputs / arbitrary units</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Yield / arbitrary units</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Describe the relationship shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Calculate the extra yield produced by the second unit of input and by the fifth unit of input.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Explain why, if enough land is available, spreading inputs evenly over a large area gives a greater total yield than concentrating them on a small area.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>Suggest why inputs are nevertheless often used unevenly across the world.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.5</span>Explain why intensive agriculture may be very productive but not very efficient.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Yield increases as inputs increase</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>But each extra unit of input produces a smaller increase in yield than the previous one (the curve flattens / law of diminishing returns)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Second unit: 70 - 50 = 20 units</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fifth unit: 94 - 90 = 4 units</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The first units of input on each area give the largest yield increases</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Concentrating inputs means later units add very little, whereas the same inputs spread thinly all work at the steep part of the curve</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Some areas have much greater potential productivity so investment there is sensible</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Affluent societies can afford to buy more inputs while poorer communities that would benefit most cannot afford them (economic reasons)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Yield per unit area is very high because of large inputs</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>But the extra yield per unit of input is small, so efficiency (yield per unit input / energy ratio) is low</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 324-325; AQA spec 3.5.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AGI-01", "sub": "agri-impacts", "subname": "Impacts of agriculture, social/political factors & sustainable agriculture", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.1.3", "pages": "328-330", "url": "subtopic/agri-impacts.html#AGI-01", "snippet": "Explain how each of the following agricultural changes affects wildlife habitats.", "text": " explain how each of the following agricultural changes affects wildlife habitats. explain why some farmland habitats are valuable for wildlife conservation. describe how agriculture contributes to climate change. drainage makes soils aerobic and drier, increasing grass growth but wetland plants and the animals they support (waders, amphibians, invertebrates) cannot survive fertiliser increases nutrient availability so vigorous species (grasses, nettles) grow taller and out-compete smaller wildflowers for light; diversity falls hedgerow removal destroys habitat / corridors and nesting sites; loss of natural pest predator habitats increases pest damage; larger fields increase wind erosion; genetic contamination and introduced species also accepted long-continued traditional farming has created plagioclimax habitats colonised by many species: hay meadows, chalk grassland, grazed heath / moorland, hedgerows these communities depend on the farming continuing co2 from fossil fuel use in machinery and embodied energy of fertiliser (haber process) and machinery; ploughing increases soil respiration methane from livestock intestines and rice paddies; nitrous oxide from manure and nitrogen fertilisers", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AGI-01\" data-qid=\"AGI-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/agri-impacts.html\">Impacts of agriculture, social/political factors &amp; sustainable agriculture</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.3 &middot; Genn pp. 328-330 &middot; AGI-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Explain how each of the following agricultural changes affects wildlife habitats.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain why some farmland habitats are valuable for wildlife conservation.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Describe how agriculture contributes to climate change.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Drainage makes soils aerobic and drier, increasing grass growth but wetland plants and the animals they support (waders, amphibians, invertebrates) cannot survive</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Fertiliser increases nutrient availability so vigorous species (grasses, nettles) grow taller and out-compete smaller wildflowers for light; diversity falls</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Hedgerow removal destroys habitat / corridors and nesting sites; loss of natural pest predator habitats increases pest damage; larger fields increase wind erosion; genetic contamination and introduced species also accepted</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Long-continued traditional farming has created plagioclimax habitats colonised by many species: hay meadows, chalk grassland, grazed heath / moorland, hedgerows</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>These communities depend on the farming continuing</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>CO2 from fossil fuel use in machinery and embodied energy of fertiliser (Haber process) and machinery; ploughing increases soil respiration</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Methane from livestock intestines and rice paddies; nitrous oxide from manure and nitrogen fertilisers</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 328-330; AQA spec 3.5.1.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AGI-02", "sub": "agri-impacts", "subname": "Impacts of agriculture, social/political factors & sustainable agriculture", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.1.3-3.5.1.4", "pages": "329-333", "url": "subtopic/agri-impacts.html#AGI-02", "snippet": "Describe how agriculture alters the hydrological cycle.", "text": " describe how agriculture alters the hydrological cycle. explain how each of the following consumer choices can reduce the environmental impact of food production. explain why post-war european governments introduced grants and guaranteed prices, and describe one environmental consequence. irrigation depletes rivers and aquifers (eg aral sea, ogallala); increases evaporation soil erosion and compaction reduce water retention and infiltration so runoff and river flow fluctuations increase evapotranspiration changes: increased in irrigated arid areas, decreased where forests were cleared seasonal / local: avoids energy for heating, lighting greenhouses out of season and long-distance transport (food miles) and the pollution this causes organic: uses natural processes (rotation, legumes, manure, biological control, predator habitats) instead of artificial fertilisers and pesticides, so less pollution, energy use and harm to non-target species; free-range and fairtrade also accepted for welfare / social reasons food shortages after 1945 (dependence on us food aid); farmers could not afford loans to invest so grants funded machinery, drainage, hedgerow removal, liming, improved livestock guaranteed market / price support: government bought surpluses to hold prices up (and sold stores in poor years) giving farmers security to increase output consequences: loss of hedgerows and wetlands, intensification, pesticide and fertiliser pollution; later food surpluses (butter mountains) that could not be sold without harming ledc producers", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AGI-02\" data-qid=\"AGI-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/agri-impacts.html\">Impacts of agriculture, social/political factors &amp; sustainable agriculture</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.3-3.5.1.4 &middot; Genn pp. 329-333 &middot; AGI-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Describe how agriculture alters the hydrological cycle.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain how each of the following consumer choices can reduce the environmental impact of food production.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain why post-war European governments introduced grants and guaranteed prices, and describe <b>one</b> environmental consequence.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Irrigation depletes rivers and aquifers (eg Aral Sea, Ogallala); increases evaporation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Soil erosion and compaction reduce water retention and infiltration so runoff and river flow fluctuations increase</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Evapotranspiration changes: increased in irrigated arid areas, decreased where forests were cleared</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Seasonal / local: avoids energy for heating, lighting greenhouses out of season and long-distance transport (food miles) and the pollution this causes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Organic: uses natural processes (rotation, legumes, manure, biological control, predator habitats) instead of artificial fertilisers and pesticides, so less pollution, energy use and harm to non-target species; free-range and Fairtrade also accepted for welfare / social reasons</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Food shortages after 1945 (dependence on US food aid); farmers could not afford loans to invest so grants funded machinery, drainage, hedgerow removal, liming, improved livestock</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Guaranteed market / price support: government bought surpluses to hold prices up (and sold stores in poor years) giving farmers security to increase output</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Consequences: loss of hedgerows and wetlands, intensification, pesticide and fertiliser pollution; later food surpluses (butter mountains) that could not be sold without harming LEDC producers</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 329-333; AQA spec 3.5.1.3-3.5.1.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AGI-03", "sub": "agri-impacts", "subname": "Impacts of agriculture, social/political factors & sustainable agriculture", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.1.4", "pages": "332-334", "url": "subtopic/agri-impacts.html#AGI-03", "snippet": "Explain why food surpluses produced in MEDCs in the 1970s could not easily be sold.", "text": " explain why food surpluses produced in medcs in the 1970s could not easily be sold. describe four methods used to reduce over-production while maintaining farm incomes. describe three features that farmers may be paid for under an agri-environmental scheme and the conservation purpose of each. other medcs (usa, canada, australia) also had surpluses; japan already had suppliers eastern europe / ussr needed food but could not pay selling cheaply in ledcs would undercut local farmers, put them out of business and reduce their long-term food production quotas limiting production (eg milk) set-aside payments for taking land out of production farm diversification (recreation, farm shops, specialised products) and alternative crops / livestock (biofuels, pharmaceutical poppies, llamas, deer, bees) agri-environmental schemes (esa, css, ess, cs) paying for environmentally beneficial farming hedgerows at least 1.5 m tall cut at 2-year intervals outside the breeding season - bird / insect habitat and landscape buffer strips / field margins (no cultivation, fertiliser or pesticide within 2 m) - protect ditches and rivers, habitat beetle banks of tussocky grass - natural pest predators skylark nesting plots; wild bird seed / nectar plants - winter food and pollinators in-field tree protection; controlled stocking density to reduce compaction; ditch management; dry-stone walls; woodland management", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AGI-03\" data-qid=\"AGI-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/agri-impacts.html\">Impacts of agriculture, social/political factors &amp; sustainable agriculture</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.4 &middot; Genn pp. 332-334 &middot; AGI-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Explain why food surpluses produced in MEDCs in the 1970s could not easily be sold.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Describe <b>four</b> methods used to reduce over-production while maintaining farm incomes.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Describe <b>three</b> features that farmers may be paid for under an agri-environmental scheme and the conservation purpose of each.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Other MEDCs (USA, Canada, Australia) also had surpluses; Japan already had suppliers</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Eastern Europe / USSR needed food but could not pay</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Selling cheaply in LEDCs would undercut local farmers, put them out of business and reduce their long-term food production</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Quotas limiting production (eg milk)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Set-aside payments for taking land out of production</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Farm diversification (recreation, farm shops, specialised products) and alternative crops / livestock (biofuels, pharmaceutical poppies, llamas, deer, bees)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Agri-environmental schemes (ESA, CSS, ESS, CS) paying for environmentally beneficial farming</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hedgerows at least 1.5 m tall cut at 2-year intervals outside the breeding season - bird / insect habitat and landscape</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Buffer strips / field margins (no cultivation, fertiliser or pesticide within 2 m) - protect ditches and rivers, habitat</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Beetle banks of tussocky grass - natural pest predators</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Skylark nesting plots; wild bird seed / nectar plants - winter food and pollinators</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In-field tree protection; controlled stocking density to reduce compaction; ditch management; dry-stone walls; woodland management</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 332-334; AQA spec 3.5.1.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AGI-04", "sub": "agri-impacts", "subname": "Impacts of agriculture, social/political factors & sustainable agriculture", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.5.1.5", "pages": "335-337", "url": "subtopic/agri-impacts.html#AGI-04", "snippet": "Discuss the strategies that can be used to increase the sustainability of agriculture.", "text": " discuss the strategies that can be used to increase the sustainability of agriculture. state what is meant by permaculture. why needed: growing population (to 12 billion), land degradation since the fertile crescent, reliance on artificial fertilisers, pesticides and unsustainable irrigation; limits to new land and imports pest control: cultural methods (weeding, mulching, rotation, barrier / companion crops, predator habitats, biological control, sterile males, pheromones), resistant varieties, integrated control, reduced antibiotics nutrients: natural processes - nitrogen-fixing legumes, decomposition, recycling organic matter (manure, compost), crop rotation, permaculture, conservation of soil biota and mycorrhizae, reduced artificial fertiliser; phosphate recycling energy: natural processes instead of haber-process fertiliser, low tillage, less machinery, renewable energy, low food miles, reduced methane (cattle diets, rice varieties) water: low water-use crops, drip irrigation, reservoirs and aquifer recharge, avoiding saline water; soil: organic matter maintenance, erosion control biodiversity: retaining hedgerows, ditches, ponds, woodlands; cwr conservation and seed banks; agri-environment schemes social: control of impacts on rural communities, fairtrade, local food; consumer choices evaluation: yields vs sustainability, cost, need for policy support; judgement agricultural systems designed to mimic natural ecosystems - perennial crops, mixed species, recycling of nutrients, minimal external inputs", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AGI-04\" data-qid=\"AGI-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/agri-impacts.html\">Impacts of agriculture, social/political factors &amp; sustainable agriculture</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.5 &middot; Genn pp. 335-337 &middot; AGI-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Discuss the strategies that can be used to increase the sustainability of agriculture.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>State what is meant by <b>permaculture</b>.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Why needed: growing population (to 12 billion), land degradation since the fertile crescent, reliance on artificial fertilisers, pesticides and unsustainable irrigation; limits to new land and imports</li><li>Pest control: cultural methods (weeding, mulching, rotation, barrier / companion crops, predator habitats, biological control, sterile males, pheromones), resistant varieties, integrated control, reduced antibiotics</li><li>Nutrients: natural processes - nitrogen-fixing legumes, decomposition, recycling organic matter (manure, compost), crop rotation, permaculture, conservation of soil biota and mycorrhizae, reduced artificial fertiliser; phosphate recycling</li><li>Energy: natural processes instead of Haber-process fertiliser, low tillage, less machinery, renewable energy, low food miles, reduced methane (cattle diets, rice varieties)</li><li>Water: low water-use crops, drip irrigation, reservoirs and aquifer recharge, avoiding saline water; soil: organic matter maintenance, erosion control</li><li>Biodiversity: retaining hedgerows, ditches, ponds, woodlands; CWR conservation and seed banks; agri-environment schemes</li><li>Social: control of impacts on rural communities, Fairtrade, local food; consumer choices</li><li>Evaluation: yields vs sustainability, cost, need for policy support; judgement</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Agricultural systems designed to mimic natural ecosystems - perennial crops, mixed species, recycling of nutrients, minimal external inputs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 335-337; AQA spec 3.5.1.5.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AGI-05", "sub": "agri-impacts", "subname": "Impacts of agriculture, social/political factors & sustainable agriculture", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 5, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.1.3", "pages": "328-329", "url": "subtopic/agri-impacts.html#AGI-05", "snippet": "Give two habitats created by farming that support important wildlife communities.", "text": " give two habitats created by farming that support important wildlife communities. explain how drainage and nutrient enrichment of farmland reduce biodiversity. explain one problem that can be caused by introducing a biological control species. hedgerows hay meadows grazed moorland / heathland chalk grassland drainage produces more aerobic soils so wetland plants and the animals they support cannot survive fertilisers increase growth of tall species such as grasses which out-compete smaller plants such as wildflowers the introduced (non-indigenous) predator may eat other species as well as the pest", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AGI-05\" data-qid=\"AGI-05\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/agri-impacts.html\">Impacts of agriculture, social/political factors &amp; sustainable agriculture</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.3 &middot; Genn pp. 328-329 &middot; AGI-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Give <b>two</b> habitats created by farming that support important wildlife communities.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain how drainage and nutrient enrichment of farmland reduce biodiversity.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain <b>one</b> problem that can be caused by introducing a biological control species.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hedgerows</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hay meadows</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Grazed moorland / heathland</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Chalk grassland</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Drainage produces more aerobic soils so wetland plants and the animals they support cannot survive</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fertilisers increase growth of tall species such as grasses which out-compete smaller plants such as wildflowers</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The introduced (non-indigenous) predator may eat other species as well as the pest</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 328-329; AQA spec 3.5.1.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AGI-06", "sub": "agri-impacts", "subname": "Impacts of agriculture, social/political factors & sustainable agriculture", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.1.3-3.5.1.4", "pages": "329-332", "url": "subtopic/agri-impacts.html#AGI-06", "snippet": "Explain how GPS mapping, drone surveys and computer-based yield monitoring can reduce the environmental impacts of agriculture.", "text": " explain how gps mapping, drone surveys and computer-based yield monitoring can reduce the environmental impacts of agriculture. explain why productive agricultural technologies are less available in ledcs. explain how buying fairtrade food affects producers. explain how nitrate from farmland can threaten human health. give two agricultural sources of methane. surveys monitor photosynthesis rates, biomass, soil water content and the spread of pests / diseases so inputs are applied only where needed yield mapping of different parts of a field controls fertiliser application in each area for future crops this maximises yield while avoiding wastage / reducing leaching and nutrient pollution machinery, pesticides, fertilisers, improved breeds and infrastructure (transport, refrigeration) are expensive they are mainly available in affluent societies; poorer farmers cannot afford the inputs provides an income that allows producers to afford basic rights such as water, education, health care and food nitrates are leached into water bodies used as sources of potable water high nitrate levels cause blue baby syndrome (methaemoglobinaemia) and nitrates may be carcinogenic anaerobic digestion by microbes in rice padi fields livestock intestines (ruminant digestion) anaerobic decay of manure / slurry", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AGI-06\" data-qid=\"AGI-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/agri-impacts.html\">Impacts of agriculture, social/political factors &amp; sustainable agriculture</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.3-3.5.1.4 &middot; Genn pp. 329-332 &middot; AGI-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Explain how GPS mapping, drone surveys and computer-based yield monitoring can reduce the environmental impacts of agriculture.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain why productive agricultural technologies are less available in LEDCs.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain how buying fairtrade food affects producers.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Explain how nitrate from farmland can threaten human health.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.5</span>Give <b>two</b> agricultural sources of methane.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Surveys monitor photosynthesis rates, biomass, soil water content and the spread of pests / diseases so inputs are applied only where needed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Yield mapping of different parts of a field controls fertiliser application in each area for future crops</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>This maximises yield while avoiding wastage / reducing leaching and nutrient pollution</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Machinery, pesticides, fertilisers, improved breeds and infrastructure (transport, refrigeration) are expensive</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They are mainly available in affluent societies; poorer farmers cannot afford the inputs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Provides an income that allows producers to afford basic rights such as water, education, health care and food</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nitrates are leached into water bodies used as sources of potable water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>High nitrate levels cause blue baby syndrome (methaemoglobinaemia) and nitrates may be carcinogenic</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Anaerobic digestion by microbes in rice padi fields</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Livestock intestines (ruminant digestion)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Anaerobic decay of manure / slurry</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 329-332; AQA spec 3.5.1.3-3.5.1.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AGI-07", "sub": "agri-impacts", "subname": "Impacts of agriculture, social/political factors & sustainable agriculture", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 15, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.5.1.5", "pages": "335-337", "url": "subtopic/agri-impacts.html#AGI-07", "snippet": "Explain why the potential to increase food supplies by cultivating new land is limited.", "text": " explain why the potential to increase food supplies by cultivating new land is limited. explain why importing food may reduce the availability of food in the area where it is produced. explain why organic agriculture is more than simply 'not using pesticides and artificial fertilisers'. evaluate the strategies that could be used to make agriculture more sustainable. about 10% of land is already cultivated and 25% grazed most remaining land is too dry, wet, cold, hot or steep, is covered in ice, or has no soil land becomes too expensive for local people to rent / is used to grow export crops for more affluent societies so people with less money lose access to the land and food they used to produce it uses natural processes to supply nutrients (legumes, recycled organic matter, soil biota), control pests and provide ecological services so that pesticides and artificial fertilisers become unnecessary rather than just being banned unsustainable features: reliance on chemical pesticides (resistance, non-target impacts, bans), non-renewable rock phosphate, fossil-fuel-intensive nitrate manufacture, over-exploitation of rivers / groundwater, salinisation, loss of soil organic matter, greenhouse gases from fuel, rice and livestock, loss of species providing ecosystem services pest control: cultural methods (weeding, mulching, crop rotation, barrier crops, culling), biological control, predator habitats, polyculture / companion crops, integrated control, reduced antibiotic use nutrients: recycling organic matter, crop rotation, cultivation of legumes, conservation of soil biota, reduced artificial fertiliser especially nitrate, low tillage water: low water-use crops, reservoirs and aquifer recharge, drip irrigation rather than sprays soil and carbon: low-tillage farming to maintain organic matter, reduced machinery, renewable energy, drought-tolerant rice varieties so fields are drained earlier, high-carbohydrate ground feed for cattle reduces methane biodiversity: conserve habitats of crop wild relatives, seed banks, retain hedgerows, ditches, ponds, woodland; agri-environment schemes evaluation: growing population (perhaps 12 billion) needs more food; lower trophic level diets; obstacles - cost, need for support, gaining acceptance of new techniques; learning from past land degradation (fertile crescent, us mid-west) judgement: no single strategy sufficient; combination of ecological principles and new technology; justified conclusion", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AGI-07\" data-qid=\"AGI-07\" data-marks=\"15\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">15 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/agri-impacts.html\">Impacts of agriculture, social/political factors &amp; sustainable agriculture</a> &middot; spec 3.5.1.5 &middot; Genn pp. 335-337 &middot; AGI-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Explain why the potential to increase food supplies by cultivating new land is limited.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Explain why importing food may reduce the availability of food in the area where it is produced.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Explain why organic agriculture is more than simply 'not using pesticides and artificial fertilisers'.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>Evaluate the strategies that could be used to make agriculture more sustainable.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>About 10% of land is already cultivated and 25% grazed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Most remaining land is too dry, wet, cold, hot or steep, is covered in ice, or has no soil</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Land becomes too expensive for local people to rent / is used to grow export crops for more affluent societies</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So people with less money lose access to the land and food they used to produce</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>It uses natural processes to supply nutrients (legumes, recycled organic matter, soil biota), control pests and provide ecological services</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So that pesticides and artificial fertilisers become unnecessary rather than just being banned</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Unsustainable features: reliance on chemical pesticides (resistance, non-target impacts, bans), non-renewable rock phosphate, fossil-fuel-intensive nitrate manufacture, over-exploitation of rivers / groundwater, salinisation, loss of soil organic matter, greenhouse gases from fuel, rice and livestock, loss of species providing ecosystem services</li><li>Pest control: cultural methods (weeding, mulching, crop rotation, barrier crops, culling), biological control, predator habitats, polyculture / companion crops, integrated control, reduced antibiotic use</li><li>Nutrients: recycling organic matter, crop rotation, cultivation of legumes, conservation of soil biota, reduced artificial fertiliser especially nitrate, low tillage</li><li>Water: low water-use crops, reservoirs and aquifer recharge, drip irrigation rather than sprays</li><li>Soil and carbon: low-tillage farming to maintain organic matter, reduced machinery, renewable energy, drought-tolerant rice varieties so fields are drained earlier, high-carbohydrate ground feed for cattle reduces methane</li><li>Biodiversity: conserve habitats of crop wild relatives, seed banks, retain hedgerows, ditches, ponds, woodland; agri-environment schemes</li><li>Evaluation: growing population (perhaps 12 billion) needs more food; lower trophic level diets; obstacles - cost, need for support, gaining acceptance of new techniques; learning from past land degradation (fertile crescent, US mid-west)</li><li>Judgement: no single strategy sufficient; combination of ecological principles and new technology; justified conclusion</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 335-337; AQA spec 3.5.1.5.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FISH-01", "sub": "fishing", "subname": "Marine productivity & fishing", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.5.2.1", "pages": "338-339", "url": "subtopic/fishing.html#FISH-01", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the seasonal changes in light intensity, nutrient concentration and phytoplankton biomass in the surface water of a temperate ocean in the northern hemisphere.", "text": "figure 1 shows the seasonal changes in light intensity, nutrient concentration and phytoplankton biomass in the surface water of a temperate ocean in the northern hemisphere. use figure 1 to explain the spring peak and summer decline in phytoplankton biomass. explain why tropical open oceans have low productivity all year despite high light and temperature. explain why areas of upwelling and coastal waters are the most productive parts of the sea. explain why phosphate is often the limiting nutrient in the sea. in winter nutrients are high (storms disturb sediments, deep mixing) but light is too low for photosynthesis in spring light rises while nutrients are still high so growth is rapid - the spring bloom (peak about may) the bloom uses up nutrients (falling to 10 by july); dead plankton sink out of the photic zone carrying nutrients to the seabed so nutrients become limiting in summer despite high light; a smaller autumn bloom follows when storms return nutrients while light is still adequate warm surface water is stable (no seasonal mixing / storms) so nutrients lost to deep water in sinking dead organisms are not replaced nutrient (especially phosphate) concentrations in the photic zone remain very low - the limiting factor upwelling (offshore winds, seamounts, currents deflected upwards) brings cold nutrient-rich deep water into the photic zone coastal waters receive nutrients washed off the land in rivers and storms disturb shallow sediments nutrients plus light in the shallow photic zone allow high algal productivity supporting rich food webs and fisheries (eg peru anchovy) phosphates have low solubility and no atmospheric reservoir; they are only added by river runoff or upwelling and are lost to sediments", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FISH-01\" data-qid=\"FISH-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/fishing.html\">Marine productivity &amp; fishing</a> &middot; spec 3.5.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 338-339 &middot; FISH-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the seasonal changes in light intensity, nutrient concentration and phytoplankton biomass in the surface water of a temperate ocean in the northern hemisphere.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,245.0 147.3,170.0 234.5,70.0 321.8,20.0 409.1,120.0 496.4,232.5 540.0,245.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"245.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"147.3\" cy=\"170.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"234.5\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"321.8\" cy=\"20.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"409.1\" cy=\"120.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"496.4\" cy=\"232.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"245.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<polyline points=\"60.0,45.0 147.3,57.5 234.5,195.0 321.8,245.0 409.1,220.0 496.4,95.0 540.0,57.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2f5f73\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"45.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"147.3\" cy=\"57.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"234.5\" cy=\"195.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"321.8\" cy=\"245.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"409.1\" cy=\"220.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"496.4\" cy=\"95.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"57.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<polyline points=\"60.0,257.5 147.3,220.0 234.5,32.5 321.8,195.0 409.1,157.5 496.4,245.0 540.0,257.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#8b5e3c\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"257.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"147.3\" cy=\"220.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"234.5\" cy=\"32.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"321.8\" cy=\"195.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"409.1\" cy=\"157.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"496.4\" cy=\"245.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"257.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">20</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">5.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">40</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">7.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">60</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">9.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">80</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">12</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">100</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Month</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Relative value</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"24\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#22302a\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"33\">Light</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"38\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"47\">Nutrients</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"52\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"61\">Phytoplankton</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Use Figure 1 to explain the spring peak and summer decline in phytoplankton biomass.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain why tropical open oceans have low productivity all year despite high light and temperature.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain why areas of upwelling and coastal waters are the most productive parts of the sea.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Explain why phosphate is often the limiting nutrient in the sea.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In winter nutrients are high (storms disturb sediments, deep mixing) but light is too low for photosynthesis</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In spring light rises while nutrients are still high so growth is rapid - the spring bloom (peak about May)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The bloom uses up nutrients (falling to 10 by July); dead plankton sink out of the photic zone carrying nutrients to the seabed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So nutrients become limiting in summer despite high light; a smaller autumn bloom follows when storms return nutrients while light is still adequate</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Warm surface water is stable (no seasonal mixing / storms) so nutrients lost to deep water in sinking dead organisms are not replaced</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nutrient (especially phosphate) concentrations in the photic zone remain very low - the limiting factor</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Upwelling (offshore winds, seamounts, currents deflected upwards) brings cold nutrient-rich deep water into the photic zone</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Coastal waters receive nutrients washed off the land in rivers and storms disturb shallow sediments</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nutrients plus light in the shallow photic zone allow high algal productivity supporting rich food webs and fisheries (eg Peru anchovy)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Phosphates have low solubility and no atmospheric reservoir; they are only added by river runoff or upwelling and are lost to sediments</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 338-339; AQA spec 3.5.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FISH-02", "sub": "fishing", "subname": "Marine productivity & fishing", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.5.2.2", "pages": "340-343", "url": "subtopic/fishing.html#FISH-02", "snippet": "Table 1 shows features of some fishing methods.", "text": "table 1 shows features of some fishing methods. complete table 1. explain why by-catch is a particular problem with shrimp trawling and drift netting. explain what is meant by ghost fishing and describe two ways it can be reduced. demersal trawl: seabed species - cod, haddock, plaice, shrimps, scampi; chains / weights damage the seabed (mix aerobic and anaerobic layers, destroy sea fans, corals, seagrass); high by-catch in mixed fisheries; high fuel use purse seine: shoaling pelagic species - tuna, sardines, herring, anchovy; can remove whole shoals; dolphins associated with tuna shoals may be caught pelagic long-line: tuna, squid; albatrosses (long-lived, low breeding rate) drown on baited hooks; sharks and turtles caught shrimp trawls use very small mesh so crabs, molluscs, sea urchins, starfish, juvenile fish cannot escape - by-catch can be 99% of the catch drift nets are non-selective curtains near the surface catching whales, dolphins, turtles, sharks lost or discarded nets and traps continue to catch and kill organisms; dead animals act as bait attracting more biodegradable ropes / panels so lost traps fall apart; radio transmitters on traps so they can be recovered; net recovery schemes", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FISH-02\" data-qid=\"FISH-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/fishing.html\">Marine productivity &amp; fishing</a> &middot; spec 3.5.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 340-343 &middot; FISH-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows features of some fishing methods.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Complete Table 1.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Method</th><th>Target species</th><th>One environmental disadvantage</th></tr><tr><td>Demersal trawling</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Purse seining</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Pelagic long-lining</td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain why by-catch is a particular problem with shrimp trawling and drift netting.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain what is meant by <b>ghost fishing</b> and describe <b>two</b> ways it can be reduced.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Demersal trawl: seabed species - cod, haddock, plaice, shrimps, scampi; chains / weights damage the seabed (mix aerobic and anaerobic layers, destroy sea fans, corals, seagrass); high by-catch in mixed fisheries; high fuel use</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Purse seine: shoaling pelagic species - tuna, sardines, herring, anchovy; can remove whole shoals; dolphins associated with tuna shoals may be caught</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Pelagic long-line: tuna, squid; albatrosses (long-lived, low breeding rate) drown on baited hooks; sharks and turtles caught</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Shrimp trawls use very small mesh so crabs, molluscs, sea urchins, starfish, juvenile fish cannot escape - by-catch can be 99% of the catch</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Drift nets are non-selective curtains near the surface catching whales, dolphins, turtles, sharks</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lost or discarded nets and traps continue to catch and kill organisms; dead animals act as bait attracting more</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Biodegradable ropes / panels so lost traps fall apart; radio transmitters on traps so they can be recovered; net recovery schemes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 340-343; AQA spec 3.5.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FISH-03", "sub": "fishing", "subname": "Marine productivity & fishing", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.5.2.2", "pages": "341-342, 348-349", "url": "subtopic/fishing.html#FISH-03", "snippet": "Table 1 shows life-history features of three fish species and the age at which they reach a catchable size.", "text": "table 1 shows life-history features of three fish species and the age at which they reach a catchable size. use table 1 to explain why orange roughy populations collapsed within ten years of large-scale fishing beginning in the late 1970s. explain why herring populations recover from overfishing much faster than cod. explain why the total mass of fish landed can stay the same while a population is being overfished. describe how the greenland shark and blue (common) skate illustrate the threat of by-catch to k-selected species. k-selected: very slow growth, does not breed until 30, few eggs, so the population cannot replace losses fish reach catchable size (20) before they breed (30) so fishing can remove the entire future breeding population deep-water aggregations easily located and caught by trawling recovery takes decades so fisheries have had to be closed herring breed at 2 years - the same age they become catchable - so there is always a breeding population and many young cod are catchable at 2 but do not mature until 4, so heavy fishing removes most fish before they breed larger numbers of smaller / younger fish are caught, or bigger boats, nets and sonar increase catch effort masks the decline of the breeding population until a sudden collapse neither is a target species but both are caught in demersal trawls; skate now extinct in the baltic and critically endangered elsewhere very late maturity (skate 11 years, greenland shark 100-150 years, living over 400) and few young mean even low by-catch mortality exceeds replacement", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FISH-03\" data-qid=\"FISH-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/fishing.html\">Marine productivity &amp; fishing</a> &middot; spec 3.5.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 341-342, 348-349 &middot; FISH-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows life-history features of three fish species and the age at which they reach a catchable size.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Species</th><th>Age at catchable size / years</th><th>Age at first breeding / years</th><th>Eggs per female per year</th><th>Maximum lifespan / years</th></tr><tr><td>Atlantic cod</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>Millions</td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>Orange roughy</td><td>20</td><td>30</td><td>Thousands</td><td>150</td></tr><tr><td>Herring</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>Tens of thousands</td><td>20</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Use Table 1 to explain why orange roughy populations collapsed within ten years of large-scale fishing beginning in the late 1970s.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain why herring populations recover from overfishing much faster than cod.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain why the total mass of fish landed can stay the same while a population is being overfished.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Describe how the Greenland shark and blue (common) skate illustrate the threat of by-catch to k-selected species.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>k-selected: very slow growth, does not breed until 30, few eggs, so the population cannot replace losses</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fish reach catchable size (20) before they breed (30) so fishing can remove the entire future breeding population</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Deep-water aggregations easily located and caught by trawling</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Recovery takes decades so fisheries have had to be closed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Herring breed at 2 years - the same age they become catchable - so there is always a breeding population and many young</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cod are catchable at 2 but do not mature until 4, so heavy fishing removes most fish before they breed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Larger numbers of smaller / younger fish are caught, or bigger boats, nets and sonar increase catch effort</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Masks the decline of the breeding population until a sudden collapse</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Neither is a target species but both are caught in demersal trawls; skate now extinct in the Baltic and critically endangered elsewhere</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Very late maturity (skate 11 years, Greenland shark 100-150 years, living over 400) and few young mean even low by-catch mortality exceeds replacement</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 341-342, 348-349; AQA spec 3.5.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FISH-04", "sub": "fishing", "subname": "Marine productivity & fishing", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.5.2.2", "pages": "349-351", "url": "subtopic/fishing.html#FISH-04", "snippet": "The Russell formula describes changes in a fish stock: S2 = S1 + (A + G) - (C + M), where S1 and S2 are the stock biomass at the start and end of the year, A = recruitment of young", "text": "the russell formula describes changes in a fish stock: s2 = s1 + (a + g) - (c + m), where s1 and s2 are the stock biomass at the start and end of the year, a = recruitment of young fish, g = growth, c = catch and m = natural mortality. table 1 gives estimates for a cod stock. calculate s2 and state whether the stock is being fished sustainably. calculate the maximum catch that would have kept the stock constant. explain why the catch is the only term in the formula that is easy to measure, and why the others are difficult. describe how each of the following measures obtained from commercial catches can indicate overfishing. 600 + (90 + 150) - (220 + 80) = 540 stock fell by 60 so the catch exceeds the msy / unsustainable a + g - m = 90 + 150 - 80 = 160 catch is landed and weighed at ports so it is recorded other terms need data on fish in the sea: populations are mobile (crossing national boundaries), very unevenly distributed representative sampling of a large enough proportion of the area is impractical / expensive; survival of eggs and larvae varies greatly between years cpue: if more effort (boat-days, engine power, net size) is needed to land the same catch, the population is declining even though total catch is maintained; a better measure than catch size which technology can prop up falling mean size / age (from scale or otolith growth rings) shows older adults are being removed faster than replaced and the catch depends on small young fish", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FISH-04\" data-qid=\"FISH-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/fishing.html\">Marine productivity &amp; fishing</a> &middot; spec 3.5.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 349-351 &middot; FISH-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>The Russell formula describes changes in a fish stock: S<sub>2</sub> = S<sub>1</sub> + (A + G) - (C + M), where S<sub>1</sub> and S<sub>2</sub> are the stock biomass at the start and end of the year, A = recruitment of young fish, G = growth, C = catch and M = natural mortality. Table 1 gives estimates for a cod stock.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Term</th><th>Biomass / thousand tonnes</th></tr><tr><td>S<sub>1</sub></td><td>600</td></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>90</td></tr><tr><td>G</td><td>150</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>220</td></tr><tr><td>M</td><td>80</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Calculate S<sub>2</sub> and state whether the stock is being fished sustainably.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Calculate the maximum catch that would have kept the stock constant.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain why the catch is the only term in the formula that is easy to measure, and why the others are difficult.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Describe how each of the following measures obtained from commercial catches can indicate overfishing.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>600 + (90 + 150) - (220 + 80) = 540</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stock fell by 60 so the catch exceeds the MSY / unsustainable</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A + G - M = 90 + 150 - 80 = 160</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Catch is landed and weighed at ports so it is recorded</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Other terms need data on fish in the sea: populations are mobile (crossing national boundaries), very unevenly distributed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Representative sampling of a large enough proportion of the area is impractical / expensive; survival of eggs and larvae varies greatly between years</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>CPUE: if more effort (boat-days, engine power, net size) is needed to land the same catch, the population is declining even though total catch is maintained; a better measure than catch size which technology can prop up</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Falling mean size / age (from scale or otolith growth rings) shows older adults are being removed faster than replaced and the catch depends on small young fish</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 349-351; AQA spec 3.5.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FISH-05", "sub": "fishing", "subname": "Marine productivity & fishing", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.2.2", "pages": "344-348", "url": "subtopic/fishing.html#FISH-05", "snippet": "Explain how each of the following reduces the environmental impact of fishing.", "text": " explain how each of the following reduces the environmental impact of fishing. explain why catch quotas work well for pelagic species such as mackerel but poorly in mixed demersal fisheries. normal diagonal mesh closes as the net fills / stretches so small fish cannot escape; square-mesh panels do not close so juveniles escape to grow and breed; teds let turtles out; larger mesh sizes generally minimum size lets fish reach breeding age before capture (eg uk lobster minimum carapace above breeding size, cod 35 cm); maximum size / v-notching egg-carrying females protects large, highly fecund breeding adults (hebridean lobsters over 145 mm, nassau grouper) ntzs (lundy, lamlash bay, chagos, great barrier reef) protect breeding populations and habitats; surplus young spill over and increase surrounding catches; damaged communities recover closed seasons, ideally in the breeding season, let fish grow and breed; fishermen may oppose because shoaling breeders are easy to catch pelagic species form single-species shoals so fishing stops when the quota is reached in mixed fisheries many species are caught together; once one species' quota is reached it must be discarded (dead) while fishing for others continues", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FISH-05\" data-qid=\"FISH-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/fishing.html\">Marine productivity &amp; fishing</a> &middot; spec 3.5.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 344-348 &middot; FISH-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[8 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Explain how each of the following reduces the environmental impact of fishing.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain why catch quotas work well for pelagic species such as mackerel but poorly in mixed demersal fisheries.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">8 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Normal diagonal mesh closes as the net fills / stretches so small fish cannot escape; square-mesh panels do not close so juveniles escape to grow and breed; TEDs let turtles out; larger mesh sizes generally</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Minimum size lets fish reach breeding age before capture (eg UK lobster minimum carapace above breeding size, cod 35 cm); maximum size / V-notching egg-carrying females protects large, highly fecund breeding adults (Hebridean lobsters over 145 mm, Nassau grouper)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>NTZs (Lundy, Lamlash Bay, Chagos, Great Barrier Reef) protect breeding populations and habitats; surplus young spill over and increase surrounding catches; damaged communities recover</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Closed seasons, ideally in the breeding season, let fish grow and breed; fishermen may oppose because shoaling breeders are easy to catch</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pelagic species form single-species shoals so fishing stops when the quota is reached</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In mixed fisheries many species are caught together; once one species' quota is reached it must be discarded (dead) while fishing for others continues</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 344-348; AQA spec 3.5.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FISH-06", "sub": "fishing", "subname": "Marine productivity & fishing", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.2.2", "pages": "344-348", "url": "subtopic/fishing.html#FISH-06", "snippet": "Describe three methods of reducing seabird by-catch in long-line fisheries.", "text": " describe three methods of reducing seabird by-catch in long-line fisheries. explain how restricting fishing effort and restricting fishing methods reduce overfishing. describe how captive rearing and release ('population seeding') can increase wild populations of lobsters or oysters. explain why collecting data on planktonic fish eggs and larvae helps to prevent population collapse. sinkers / weighted lines so hooks sink quickly below the reach of albatrosses setting lines at night when birds are not feeding bird-scaring streamers / decoys curved hooks that catch tuna but not birds effort: limits on boat size, engine power, days at sea, number of licences; decommissioning payments to reduce fleet size where overfishing is unavoidable methods: bans or length limits on drift nets; bans on demersal trawling over sensitive seabed (rockall bank, hatton bank deep-water reefs) shorter trawl tows so turtles / by-catch survive (over 99% survive under 10 minutes) eggs / larvae are reared in captivity through the planktonic stage when most die (predation, failure to find habitat) juveniles are released when survival chances are good, increasing recruitment to the adult population egg / larval survival varies greatly between years so adult numbers do not predict recruitment several poor years would lower the msy; egg surveys allow catches to be reduced in advance rather than after a sudden collapse", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FISH-06\" data-qid=\"FISH-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/fishing.html\">Marine productivity &amp; fishing</a> &middot; spec 3.5.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 344-348 &middot; FISH-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Describe <b>three</b> methods of reducing seabird by-catch in long-line fisheries.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain how restricting fishing effort and restricting fishing methods reduce overfishing.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Describe how captive rearing and release ('population seeding') can increase wild populations of lobsters or oysters.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Explain why collecting data on planktonic fish eggs and larvae helps to prevent population collapse.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sinkers / weighted lines so hooks sink quickly below the reach of albatrosses</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Setting lines at night when birds are not feeding</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bird-scaring streamers / decoys</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Curved hooks that catch tuna but not birds</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Effort: limits on boat size, engine power, days at sea, number of licences; decommissioning payments to reduce fleet size where overfishing is unavoidable</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Methods: bans or length limits on drift nets; bans on demersal trawling over sensitive seabed (Rockall Bank, Hatton Bank deep-water reefs)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Shorter trawl tows so turtles / by-catch survive (over 99% survive under 10 minutes)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Eggs / larvae are reared in captivity through the planktonic stage when most die (predation, failure to find habitat)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Juveniles are released when survival chances are good, increasing recruitment to the adult population</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Egg / larval survival varies greatly between years so adult numbers do not predict recruitment</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Several poor years would lower the MSY; egg surveys allow catches to be reduced in advance rather than after a sudden collapse</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 344-348; AQA spec 3.5.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FISH-07", "sub": "fishing", "subname": "Marine productivity & fishing", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 15, "flags": ["calc", "data", "ext"], "spec": "3.5.2.2", "pages": "341-345", "url": "subtopic/fishing.html#FISH-07", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the catch made by a shrimp trawler during one fishing trip.", "text": "table 1 shows the catch made by a shrimp trawler during one fishing trip. calculate the percentage of the total catch that was by-catch. explain why shrimp trawling produces such a high proportion of by-catch. explain why k-selected species such as the common skate and the greenland shark are especially vulnerable to overfishing. evaluate the methods that can be used to reduce the environmental impacts of fishing. by-catch = 5.9 + 1.9 = 7.8 t; total = 8.4 t 7.8 / 8.4 x 100 = 92.9% (accept 93%) the nets have a very small mesh size so few non-target animals can escape they breed at an old age (skate about 11 years; greenland shark 100-150 years) and produce few young they may reach a catchable size before they are sexually mature, so fishing can remove the whole breeding population before it has bred populations recover slowly, unlike r-selected species (herring, mackerel) which breed young and produce many young; they are caught as by-catch in demersal trawls so fishing continues even though they have no commercial value impacts: population decline of k-selected species, by-catch (immature target fish, over-quota fish, non-commercial species; drift nets - whales, dolphins, turtles; longlines - albatrosses; pair trawls - porpoises), ghost fishing, seabed damage by demersal trawls, coral and seagrass damage, dynamite fishing, food-web effects catch quotas: limit weight landed; work well for single-species pelagic shoals but in mixed fisheries over-quota fish are discarded dead so the quota fails to protect them gear design: mesh size and square-mesh escape panels let small fish escape (diagonal mesh closes under drag); turtle exclusion devices; acoustic pingers for dolphins; curved hooks, decoys, sinkers and night setting to protect albatrosses; biodegradable / radio-tracked traps to reduce ghost fishing restricted effort: limits on boat size, engine power and days at sea; decommissioning; restricted methods - drift-net bans, demersal trawling bans on sensitive seabeds (rockall bank, hatton bank) evaluation: enforcement at sea is difficult; some methods reduce catch or raise costs; quotas need reliable stock data; effectiveness depends on species (single vs mixed shoals) and on international cooperation as fish migrate; as local stocks decline fishing moves to more distant waters justified conclusion weighing effectiveness of different methods", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FISH-07\" data-qid=\"FISH-07\" data-marks=\"15\" data-flags=\"calc data ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">15 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/fishing.html\">Marine productivity &amp; fishing</a> &middot; spec 3.5.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 341-345 &middot; FISH-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the catch made by a shrimp trawler during one fishing trip.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Component of catch</th><th>Mass / tonnes</th></tr><tr><td>Shrimps (target species)</td><td>0.6</td></tr><tr><td>Crabs, sea urchins, molluscs and starfish</td><td>5.9</td></tr><tr><td>Immature fish of commercial species</td><td>1.9</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Calculate the percentage of the total catch that was by-catch.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Explain why shrimp trawling produces such a high proportion of by-catch.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Explain why k-selected species such as the common skate and the Greenland shark are especially vulnerable to overfishing.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>Evaluate the methods that can be used to reduce the environmental impacts of fishing.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>By-catch = 5.9 + 1.9 = 7.8 t; total = 8.4 t</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>7.8 / 8.4 x 100 = 92.9% (accept 93%)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The nets have a very small mesh size so few non-target animals can escape</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They breed at an old age (skate about 11 years; Greenland shark 100-150 years) and produce few young</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They may reach a catchable size before they are sexually mature, so fishing can remove the whole breeding population before it has bred</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Populations recover slowly, unlike r-selected species (herring, mackerel) which breed young and produce many young; they are caught as by-catch in demersal trawls so fishing continues even though they have no commercial value</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Impacts: population decline of k-selected species, by-catch (immature target fish, over-quota fish, non-commercial species; drift nets - whales, dolphins, turtles; longlines - albatrosses; pair trawls - porpoises), ghost fishing, seabed damage by demersal trawls, coral and seagrass damage, dynamite fishing, food-web effects</li><li>Catch quotas: limit weight landed; work well for single-species pelagic shoals but in mixed fisheries over-quota fish are discarded dead so the quota fails to protect them</li><li>Gear design: mesh size and square-mesh escape panels let small fish escape (diagonal mesh closes under drag); turtle exclusion devices; acoustic pingers for dolphins; curved hooks, decoys, sinkers and night setting to protect albatrosses; biodegradable / radio-tracked traps to reduce ghost fishing</li><li>Restricted effort: limits on boat size, engine power and days at sea; decommissioning; restricted methods - drift-net bans, demersal trawling bans on sensitive seabeds (Rockall Bank, Hatton Bank)</li><li>Evaluation: enforcement at sea is difficult; some methods reduce catch or raise costs; quotas need reliable stock data; effectiveness depends on species (single vs mixed shoals) and on international cooperation as fish migrate; as local stocks decline fishing moves to more distant waters</li><li>Justified conclusion weighing effectiveness of different methods</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 341-345; AQA spec 3.5.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AQUA-01", "sub": "aquaculture", "subname": "Aquaculture", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.2.3", "pages": "352-355", "url": "subtopic/aquaculture.html#AQUA-01", "snippet": "Explain why carnivorous species such as salmon dominate aquaculture in MEDCs while herbivores such as carp and tilapia dominate in LEDCs.", "text": " explain why carnivorous species such as salmon dominate aquaculture in medcs while herbivores such as carp and tilapia dominate in ledcs. describe how all-female rainbow trout are produced and explain why this is done. explain how each of the following abiotic factors is controlled in an intensive salmon farm. medcs: market demand and higher prices for the flavour of carnivorous fish (salmon, trout) justify high inputs (fishmeal, cooler climates suit them) ledcs: herbivores / omnivores feed on natural vegetation, phytoplankton and wastes so need low inputs; warm climates suit tilapia herbivores are more energy-efficient (lower trophic level) so more food per unit area some genetically female (xx) fish are treated with testosterone so they become functional males producing only x-carrying sperm crossed with normal females (xx) all offspring are xx females female trout meat has a better flavour; in tilapia the reverse (all males) because males grow larger / more efficiently salmon need high oxygen: water sprays / weirs aerate; wastes removed to prevent deoxygenation constant circular flow keeps fish swimming the same way - fewer collisions / injuries, more muscular fish, higher stocking density artificial lighting delays maturation (salmon stop growing at maturity) so larger fish; controls smoltification cool sites (scotland, norway) because warm water holds less oxygen although warmth increases growth", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AQUA-01\" data-qid=\"AQUA-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/aquaculture.html\">Aquaculture</a> &middot; spec 3.5.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 352-355 &middot; AQUA-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Explain why carnivorous species such as salmon dominate aquaculture in MEDCs while herbivores such as carp and tilapia dominate in LEDCs.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Describe how all-female rainbow trout are produced and explain why this is done.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain how each of the following abiotic factors is controlled in an intensive salmon farm.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>MEDCs: market demand and higher prices for the flavour of carnivorous fish (salmon, trout) justify high inputs (fishmeal, cooler climates suit them)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>LEDCs: herbivores / omnivores feed on natural vegetation, phytoplankton and wastes so need low inputs; warm climates suit tilapia</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Herbivores are more energy-efficient (lower trophic level) so more food per unit area</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Some genetically female (XX) fish are treated with testosterone so they become functional males producing only X-carrying sperm</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Crossed with normal females (XX) all offspring are XX females</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Female trout meat has a better flavour; in tilapia the reverse (all males) because males grow larger / more efficiently</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Salmon need high oxygen: water sprays / weirs aerate; wastes removed to prevent deoxygenation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Constant circular flow keeps fish swimming the same way - fewer collisions / injuries, more muscular fish, higher stocking density</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Artificial lighting delays maturation (salmon stop growing at maturity) so larger fish; controls smoltification</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cool sites (Scotland, Norway) because warm water holds less oxygen although warmth increases growth</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 352-355; AQA spec 3.5.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AQUA-02", "sub": "aquaculture", "subname": "Aquaculture", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.5.2.3", "pages": "352-356", "url": "subtopic/aquaculture.html#AQUA-02", "snippet": "Table 1 compares extensive oyster farming with intensive salmon farming.", "text": "table 1 compares extensive oyster farming with intensive salmon farming. use table 1 to explain the relative merits of extensive and intensive aquaculture. describe the stages in breeding and rearing salmon in an intensive farm. explain how shrimp farming has affected mangroves and how disease is controlled in shrimp lagoons. extensive: low inputs of energy, food and chemicals so low cost and low environmental impact; sustainable; but low yield per area and dependent on natural productivity intensive: control of all limiting factors gives very high yield and profit per area; but high inputs (fishmeal from wild fish, energy), disease and lice at high density, pollution from wastes, antibiotic / pesticide use, escapes judgement depends on species, market and site adults with desirable characteristics (fast growth, shape, colour) stripped of eggs and milt which are mixed for fertilisation eggs raised in aerated freshwater tanks, dead / diseased eggs removed daily; fry fed fishmeal pellets and moved through tanks as they grow after 12-18 months smolts transferred to seawater cages, fed pellets until harvest at 3-5 kg about 10% of the world's mangroves have been cleared for coastal shrimp lagoons (up to 100 ha), removing nurseries and coastal protection lagoons need water exchange to remove wastes and aeration to prevent deoxygenation as wastes decompose eyestalk ablation stimulates breeding; high densities increase disease so antibiotics / pesticides used", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AQUA-02\" data-qid=\"AQUA-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/aquaculture.html\">Aquaculture</a> &middot; spec 3.5.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 352-356 &middot; AQUA-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 compares extensive oyster farming with intensive salmon farming.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Extensive oyster farm</th><th>Intensive salmon farm</th></tr><tr><td>Food input</td><td>None - filter natural plankton</td><td>Fishmeal / plant pellets</td></tr><tr><td>Stocking density</td><td>Low, spaced on seabed</td><td>High, in sea cages / tanks</td></tr><tr><td>Breeding</td><td>Wild spat settle on cleared seabed or hatchery</td><td>Selected adults stripped of eggs and milt</td></tr><tr><td>Disease / pest control</td><td>Removal of predatory crabs</td><td>Antibiotics, pesticides, wrasse, removal of diseased fish</td></tr><tr><td>Yield per hectare</td><td>Low</td><td>Very high</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Use Table 1 to explain the relative merits of extensive and intensive aquaculture.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Describe the stages in breeding and rearing salmon in an intensive farm.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain how shrimp farming has affected mangroves and how disease is controlled in shrimp lagoons.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 4 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Extensive: low inputs of energy, food and chemicals so low cost and low environmental impact; sustainable; but low yield per area and dependent on natural productivity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Intensive: control of all limiting factors gives very high yield and profit per area; but high inputs (fishmeal from wild fish, energy), disease and lice at high density, pollution from wastes, antibiotic / pesticide use, escapes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Judgement depends on species, market and site</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Adults with desirable characteristics (fast growth, shape, colour) stripped of eggs and milt which are mixed for fertilisation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Eggs raised in aerated freshwater tanks, dead / diseased eggs removed daily; fry fed fishmeal pellets and moved through tanks as they grow</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>After 12-18 months smolts transferred to seawater cages, fed pellets until harvest at 3-5 kg</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>About 10% of the world's mangroves have been cleared for coastal shrimp lagoons (up to 100 ha), removing nurseries and coastal protection</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lagoons need water exchange to remove wastes and aeration to prevent deoxygenation as wastes decompose</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Eyestalk ablation stimulates breeding; high densities increase disease so antibiotics / pesticides used</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 352-356; AQA spec 3.5.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AQUA-03", "sub": "aquaculture", "subname": "Aquaculture", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.2.3", "pages": "356-359", "url": "subtopic/aquaculture.html#AQUA-03", "snippet": "Explain how each of the following environmental impacts of salmon farming arises and how it can be reduced.", "text": " explain how each of the following environmental impacts of salmon farming arises and how it can be reduced. explain why culling seals and cormorants near fish farms is controversial and give an alternative. carnivores fed pellets of sandeels, herring, anchovy; overfishing sandeels reduces puffins and terns; reduced by more plant protein / vegetable oil in pellets or farming herbivores (tilapia) high density spreads lice (which spread to wild fish) and infections; pesticides (organophosphates, pyrethroids, hydrogen peroxide) kill wildlife; antibiotics breed resistant bacteria; reduced by lower densities, single-age tanks with flow from young to old, wrasse as biological control, warm-water washes, circulating flow escaped farmed fish breed with wild fish introducing disadvantageous genes (bright colour), or non-indigenous escapees colonise as predators / competitors; reduced by better cage design, sterile triploid fish (pressure-treated eggs), species that cannot breed locally (japanese oysters in uk) faeces and uneaten food decompose causing deoxygenation under cages; reduced by monitored feeding, siting cages in strong currents, effluent treatment / reed beds for freshwater farms, imta with seaweed and shellfish absorbing wastes predators are killed under licence to protect stock, reducing wild populations of protected / valued species better cage designs, netting, fencing, bird scarers exclude predators without killing", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AQUA-03\" data-qid=\"AQUA-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/aquaculture.html\">Aquaculture</a> &middot; spec 3.5.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 356-359 &middot; AQUA-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[8 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Explain how each of the following environmental impacts of salmon farming arises and how it can be reduced.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain why culling seals and cormorants near fish farms is controversial and give an alternative.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">8 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Carnivores fed pellets of sandeels, herring, anchovy; overfishing sandeels reduces puffins and terns; reduced by more plant protein / vegetable oil in pellets or farming herbivores (tilapia)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>High density spreads lice (which spread to wild fish) and infections; pesticides (organophosphates, pyrethroids, hydrogen peroxide) kill wildlife; antibiotics breed resistant bacteria; reduced by lower densities, single-age tanks with flow from young to old, wrasse as biological control, warm-water washes, circulating flow</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Escaped farmed fish breed with wild fish introducing disadvantageous genes (bright colour), or non-indigenous escapees colonise as predators / competitors; reduced by better cage design, sterile triploid fish (pressure-treated eggs), species that cannot breed locally (Japanese oysters in UK)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Faeces and uneaten food decompose causing deoxygenation under cages; reduced by monitored feeding, siting cages in strong currents, effluent treatment / reed beds for freshwater farms, IMTA with seaweed and shellfish absorbing wastes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Predators are killed under licence to protect stock, reducing wild populations of protected / valued species</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Better cage designs, netting, fencing, bird scarers exclude predators without killing</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 356-359; AQA spec 3.5.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AQUA-04", "sub": "aquaculture", "subname": "Aquaculture", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.2.3", "pages": "357-358", "url": "subtopic/aquaculture.html#AQUA-04", "snippet": "Describe a polyculture system using several species of carp and explain how it increases total productivity.", "text": " describe a polyculture system using several species of carp and explain how it increases total productivity. explain how integrated multi-trophic aquaculture (imta) and aquaponics reduce pollution while increasing food production. explain why tuna aquaculture cannot yet replace tuna fishing. silver carp eat phytoplankton, bighead carp zooplankton, grass carp and tilapia vegetation, common carp bottom-feed on molluscs / insects, catfish or perch eat small fish species occupy different niches / trophic levels so they do not compete and all food sources are used bottom feeders re-suspend nutrients boosting plankton / plant growth predators control over-breeding species (tilapia) so remaining fish grow larger; total yield exceeds any single species imta: fed species (salmon, shrimp) alongside inorganic extractive species (seaweeds absorbing ammonia and phosphate) and organic extractive species (filter-feeding mussels eating waste particles / plankton) wastes of one species become nutrients for another so less pollution and extra harvests aquaponics: aquaculture water rich in nutrients / organic matter feeds hydroponic salad crops (lettuce, herbs, watercress) plants remove the nutrients that would cause deoxygenation and cleaned water returns to the fish (tilapia, carp, catfish); rice-fish paddies similar bluefin tuna do not breed well in captivity so young fish are caught from the wild and fattened in sea cages production still depends on (and removes juveniles from) the wild population; carnivores need large fish inputs", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AQUA-04\" data-qid=\"AQUA-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/aquaculture.html\">Aquaculture</a> &middot; spec 3.5.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 357-358 &middot; AQUA-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Describe a polyculture system using several species of carp and explain how it increases total productivity.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain how integrated multi-trophic aquaculture (IMTA) and aquaponics reduce pollution while increasing food production.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain why tuna aquaculture cannot yet replace tuna fishing.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Silver carp eat phytoplankton, bighead carp zooplankton, grass carp and tilapia vegetation, common carp bottom-feed on molluscs / insects, catfish or perch eat small fish</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Species occupy different niches / trophic levels so they do not compete and all food sources are used</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bottom feeders re-suspend nutrients boosting plankton / plant growth</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Predators control over-breeding species (tilapia) so remaining fish grow larger; total yield exceeds any single species</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>IMTA: fed species (salmon, shrimp) alongside inorganic extractive species (seaweeds absorbing ammonia and phosphate) and organic extractive species (filter-feeding mussels eating waste particles / plankton)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wastes of one species become nutrients for another so less pollution and extra harvests</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Aquaponics: aquaculture water rich in nutrients / organic matter feeds hydroponic salad crops (lettuce, herbs, watercress)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Plants remove the nutrients that would cause deoxygenation and cleaned water returns to the fish (tilapia, carp, catfish); rice-fish paddies similar</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bluefin tuna do not breed well in captivity so young fish are caught from the wild and fattened in sea cages</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Production still depends on (and removes juveniles from) the wild population; carnivores need large fish inputs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 357-358; AQA spec 3.5.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "AQUA-05", "sub": "aquaculture", "subname": "Aquaculture", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.5.2", "pages": "338-359", "url": "subtopic/aquaculture.html#AQUA-05", "snippet": "Evaluate the extent to which aquaculture can replace fishing as a source of aquatic food.", "text": " evaluate the extent to which aquaculture can replace fishing as a source of aquatic food. state what is meant by a fed aquaculture species. context: wild catches have not increased since about 1990 as stocks are fully or over-exploited; aquaculture output growing rapidly and approaching the wild catch; fish are the last large-scale hunted food advantages of aquaculture: control of limiting factors, selective breeding, gender control, high yields per area; fish have low fcrs (no thermoregulation / support) compared with livestock; herbivores (tilapia, carp) and molluscs very efficient; polyculture, imta, aquaponics, rice-fish limits: carnivorous species (salmon, shrimp) need fishmeal from wild fish so still depend on fishing and deplete forage fish (sandeels - puffins); some species (bluefin tuna) will not breed in captivity; consumer preference for carnivores environmental impacts: habitat loss (mangroves), organic pollution, pesticides, antibiotics and resistance, lice, escapes and gene pool contamination, predator control; and how they can be reduced fishing can be sustainable with quotas, gear design, ntzs, size limits, effort limits; msy management needs good data judgement: aquaculture can supply much of the growth in demand, especially herbivores and shellfish, but cannot fully replace fishing; both need sustainable management a species that is given artificial food (pellets) rather than relying on natural food in the water, eg salmon, shrimp", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"AQUA-05\" data-qid=\"AQUA-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/aquaculture.html\">Aquaculture</a> &middot; spec 3.5.2 &middot; Genn pp. 338-359 &middot; AQUA-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Evaluate the extent to which aquaculture can replace fishing as a source of aquatic food.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>State what is meant by a <b>fed</b> aquaculture species.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Context: wild catches have not increased since about 1990 as stocks are fully or over-exploited; aquaculture output growing rapidly and approaching the wild catch; fish are the last large-scale hunted food</li><li>Advantages of aquaculture: control of limiting factors, selective breeding, gender control, high yields per area; fish have low FCRs (no thermoregulation / support) compared with livestock; herbivores (tilapia, carp) and molluscs very efficient; polyculture, IMTA, aquaponics, rice-fish</li><li>Limits: carnivorous species (salmon, shrimp) need fishmeal from wild fish so still depend on fishing and deplete forage fish (sandeels - puffins); some species (bluefin tuna) will not breed in captivity; consumer preference for carnivores</li><li>Environmental impacts: habitat loss (mangroves), organic pollution, pesticides, antibiotics and resistance, lice, escapes and gene pool contamination, predator control; and how they can be reduced</li><li>Fishing can be sustainable with quotas, gear design, NTZs, size limits, effort limits; MSY management needs good data</li><li>Judgement: aquaculture can supply much of the growth in demand, especially herbivores and shellfish, but cannot fully replace fishing; both need sustainable management</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A species that is given artificial food (pellets) rather than relying on natural food in the water, eg salmon, shrimp</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 338-359; AQA spec 3.5.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PRI-01", "sub": "conservation-priorities", "subname": "Conservation priorities, legislation & protocols", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.1.2.3.1", "pages": "26-27", "url": "subtopic/conservation-priorities.html#PRI-01", "snippet": "Table 1 shows some of the IUCN criteria used to place species in Red List categories.", "text": "table 1 shows some of the iucn criteria used to place species in red list categories. state three roles of the iucn. a species of lemur has declined from 12 000 to 2 100 mature adults in three generations and occupies 400 km2 of forest. use table 1 to determine its red list category. explain your answer. explain why only a small proportion of species have been assessed for the red list, and give two criteria used to decide which species to assess. give one example of a species whose red list category improved because of conservation and state the actions responsible. coordinating global data on biodiversity conservation (red list) increasing understanding of the importance of biodiversity deploying nature-based solutions to global challenges in climate, food and sustainable development decline = (12 000 - 2100) / 12 000 x 100 = 82.5% decline over 80% meets the critically endangered criterion even though habitat area (400 km2) and adults (2100) only meet endangered a species is placed in the highest category for which it meets any one criterion, so critically endangered categorising a species requires a great deal of research (population, range, trends) and resources; about 85 000 of millions of species assessed key species in habitats under particular threat (eg lemurs indicating the state of madagascar's forests) evolutionarily unique species / edge species; endemic species; keystone species; flagship species; species with fragmented populations iberian lynx: critically endangered to endangered after captive breeding and protection of two habitat areas (population trebled in 15 years); fregate island beetle after rat eradication", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PRI-01\" data-qid=\"PRI-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/conservation-priorities.html\">Conservation priorities, legislation &amp; protocols</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.1 &middot; Genn pp. 26-27 &middot; PRI-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows some of the IUCN criteria used to place species in Red List categories.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Category</th><th>Population decline over 10 years or 3 generations</th><th>Area of habitat</th><th>Number of mature adults</th><th>Probability of extinction in the wild</th></tr><tr><td>Vulnerable</td><td>over 40%</td><td>under 2000 km<sup>2</sup></td><td>under 10 000</td><td>over 10% in 100 years</td></tr><tr><td>Endangered</td><td>over 60%</td><td>under 500 km<sup>2</sup></td><td>under 2500</td><td>over 20% in 20 years or 5 generations</td></tr><tr><td>Critically endangered</td><td>over 80%</td><td>under 10 km<sup>2</sup></td><td>under 250</td><td>over 50% in 10 years or 3 generations</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>State <b>three</b> roles of the IUCN.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>A species of lemur has declined from 12 000 to 2 100 mature adults in three generations and occupies 400 km<sup>2</sup> of forest. Use Table 1 to determine its Red List category. Explain your answer.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain why only a small proportion of species have been assessed for the Red List, and give <b>two</b> criteria used to decide which species to assess.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Give <b>one</b> example of a species whose Red List category improved because of conservation and state the actions responsible.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Coordinating global data on biodiversity conservation (Red List)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Increasing understanding of the importance of biodiversity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Deploying nature-based solutions to global challenges in climate, food and sustainable development</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Decline = (12 000 - 2100) / 12 000 x 100 = 82.5%</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Decline over 80% meets the Critically Endangered criterion even though habitat area (400 km<sup>2</sup>) and adults (2100) only meet Endangered</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A species is placed in the highest category for which it meets any one criterion, so Critically Endangered</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Categorising a species requires a great deal of research (population, range, trends) and resources; about 85 000 of millions of species assessed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Key species in habitats under particular threat (eg lemurs indicating the state of Madagascar's forests)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Evolutionarily unique species / EDGE species; endemic species; keystone species; flagship species; species with fragmented populations</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Iberian lynx: Critically Endangered to Endangered after captive breeding and protection of two habitat areas (population trebled in 15 years); Fregate Island beetle after rat eradication</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 26-27; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PRI-02", "sub": "conservation-priorities", "subname": "Conservation priorities, legislation & protocols", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.1", "pages": "28-29", "url": "subtopic/conservation-priorities.html#PRI-02", "snippet": "Explain the meaning of each of the following terms and explain why such species are given conservation priority.", "text": " explain the meaning of each of the following terms and explain why such species are given conservation priority. explain why the degree of population dispersal is considered when setting priorities. edge: evolutionarily distinct and globally endangered - few close relatives, often the last species of their genus / family (bactrian camel, pygmy hippo, northern bald ibis); loss would remove a unique branch of evolution / genes endemic: found in only one area (often islands - galapagos, madagascar, seychelles: aldabra giant tortoise, red ruffed lemur); if lost locally there is no population elsewhere to recolonise keystone: disproportionately important for the structure of the community relative to its abundance - predation of dominant species, seed dispersal, habitat creation (jaguar, forest elephants keeping paths and water holes open, wolves, beavers); losing them causes loss of many other species flagship: high public profile (tiger, panda, elephant, orang-utan) raising funds and support that also protects the whole habitat and less charismatic species (fungi, bats, moths) a fragmented population may consist of isolated groups each too small to be viable (resources, inbreeding) even if total numbers are large a species confined to a single area (eg bonobos in one country) is vulnerable to any local change / conflict, unlike chimpanzees spread across many countries", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PRI-02\" data-qid=\"PRI-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/conservation-priorities.html\">Conservation priorities, legislation &amp; protocols</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.1 &middot; Genn pp. 28-29 &middot; PRI-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[8 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain the meaning of each of the following terms and explain why such species are given conservation priority.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain why the degree of population dispersal is considered when setting priorities.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">8 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>EDGE: Evolutionarily Distinct and Globally Endangered - few close relatives, often the last species of their genus / family (Bactrian camel, pygmy hippo, northern bald ibis); loss would remove a unique branch of evolution / genes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Endemic: found in only one area (often islands - Galapagos, Madagascar, Seychelles: Aldabra giant tortoise, red ruffed lemur); if lost locally there is no population elsewhere to recolonise</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Keystone: disproportionately important for the structure of the community relative to its abundance - predation of dominant species, seed dispersal, habitat creation (jaguar, forest elephants keeping paths and water holes open, wolves, beavers); losing them causes loss of many other species</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Flagship: high public profile (tiger, panda, elephant, orang-utan) raising funds and support that also protects the whole habitat and less charismatic species (fungi, bats, moths)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A fragmented population may consist of isolated groups each too small to be viable (resources, inbreeding) even if total numbers are large</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A species confined to a single area (eg bonobos in one country) is vulnerable to any local change / conflict, unlike chimpanzees spread across many countries</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 28-29; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PRI-03", "sub": "conservation-priorities", "subname": "Conservation priorities, legislation & protocols", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.2", "pages": "30-31", "url": "subtopic/conservation-priorities.html#PRI-03", "snippet": "Describe four ways in which the Wildlife and Countryside Act (1981) protects wildlife in the UK.", "text": " describe four ways in which the wildlife and countryside act (1981) protects wildlife in the uk. explain the difference between cites appendix i and appendix ii, giving an example species for each. explain why controlling international trade can be more effective than trying to stop collection of a species in its own country. designation of protected areas (sssis, mczs) protection of wild birds and their nests (except some pests / game birds) protection of named mammals such as otters, dormice, red squirrels, badgers and their setts uprooting wild plants generally illegal bats must not be disturbed; roof timber treatments must be non-toxic to bats appendix i: species threatened with extinction - all international (commercial) trade banned, except movement for conservation breeding examples: great apes, big cats, rhinos, blue whale appendix ii: species that may become threatened if trade is not closely controlled - trade permitted under licence from countries where the species is well protected examples: honduras mahogany, hippopotamus, green iguana, great white / basking shark, venus fly trap collection may be hard to police in remote areas / poor countries, but if the animals or products cannot be exported / imported there is no market removing the profit removes the incentive to collect", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PRI-03\" data-qid=\"PRI-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/conservation-priorities.html\">Conservation priorities, legislation &amp; protocols</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.2 &middot; Genn pp. 30-31 &middot; PRI-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Describe <b>four</b> ways in which the Wildlife and Countryside Act (1981) protects wildlife in the UK.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain the difference between CITES Appendix I and Appendix II, giving an example species for each.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain why controlling international trade can be more effective than trying to stop collection of a species in its own country.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Designation of protected areas (SSSIs, MCZs)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Protection of wild birds and their nests (except some pests / game birds)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Protection of named mammals such as otters, dormice, red squirrels, badgers and their setts</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Uprooting wild plants generally illegal</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bats must not be disturbed; roof timber treatments must be non-toxic to bats</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Appendix I: species threatened with extinction - all international (commercial) trade banned, except movement for conservation breeding</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Examples: great apes, big cats, rhinos, blue whale</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Appendix II: species that may become threatened if trade is not closely controlled - trade permitted under licence from countries where the species is well protected</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Examples: Honduras mahogany, hippopotamus, green iguana, great white / basking shark, Venus fly trap</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Collection may be hard to police in remote areas / poor countries, but if the animals or products cannot be exported / imported there is no market</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Removing the profit removes the incentive to collect</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 30-31; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PRI-04", "sub": "conservation-priorities", "subname": "Conservation priorities, legislation & protocols", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.1.2.3.2", "pages": "30, 38-39", "url": "subtopic/conservation-priorities.html#PRI-04", "snippet": "Table 1 shows some designated protected areas in the UK.", "text": "table 1 shows some designated protected areas in the uk. complete table 1 to show the legislation under which each area is designated and its key feature. explain how designating a protected area protects habitats and species. explain why voluntary agri-environmental schemes are needed in addition to legislation. sssi: uk legislation (wildlife and countryside act); best examples of uk flora, fauna, geological features; management plan agreed with owner who must notify operations likely to damage (ploughing, drainage, pesticides, tree planting / removal) spa: eu birds directive; places of international importance for birds (with sacs under the habitats directive form the natura 2000 network; may restrict activities in surrounding areas) ramsar: ramsar convention (intergovernmental agreement); internationally important wetlands, originally for waterfowl, now wetland conservation and wise use generally mcz: uk marine legislation (marine and coastal access act) under the w&ca framework; protects marine habitats / species by restricting activities such as fishing and dredging; nnr: best sssis with complete communities legal status: restrictions on damaging activities within the area (and sometimes in surrounding areas, eg drainage) management agreements between owners and statutory bodies to maintain suitable conditions but it does not protect against external influences such as air pollution or climate change; very few sites are state-owned so protection depends on owner cooperation much of the uk's wildlife lives in habitats created by farming (hay meadows, hedgerows) which only survive if the farming continues managing land for wildlife costs money / reduces income so farmers need payments (points for beetle banks, hedgerows, buffer strips, wild bird seed, low-input grassland) benefits everyone so society pays through central funding; legislation cannot compel positive management", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PRI-04\" data-qid=\"PRI-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/conservation-priorities.html\">Conservation priorities, legislation &amp; protocols</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.2 &middot; Genn pp. 30, 38-39 &middot; PRI-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows some designated protected areas in the UK.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Complete Table 1 to show the legislation under which each area is designated and its key feature.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Designation</th><th>Designated under</th><th>Key feature</th></tr><tr><td>Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Special Protection Area (SPA)</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Ramsar site</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ)</td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain how designating a protected area protects habitats and species.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain why voluntary agri-environmental schemes are needed in addition to legislation.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>SSSI: UK legislation (Wildlife and Countryside Act); best examples of UK flora, fauna, geological features; management plan agreed with owner who must notify Operations Likely to Damage (ploughing, drainage, pesticides, tree planting / removal)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>SPA: EU Birds Directive; places of international importance for birds (with SACs under the Habitats Directive form the Natura 2000 network; may restrict activities in surrounding areas)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ramsar: Ramsar Convention (intergovernmental agreement); internationally important wetlands, originally for waterfowl, now wetland conservation and wise use generally</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>MCZ: UK marine legislation (Marine and Coastal Access Act) under the W&amp;CA framework; protects marine habitats / species by restricting activities such as fishing and dredging; NNR: best SSSIs with complete communities</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Legal status: restrictions on damaging activities within the area (and sometimes in surrounding areas, eg drainage)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Management agreements between owners and statutory bodies to maintain suitable conditions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>But it does not protect against external influences such as air pollution or climate change; very few sites are state-owned so protection depends on owner cooperation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Much of the UK's wildlife lives in habitats created by farming (hay meadows, hedgerows) which only survive if the farming continues</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Managing land for wildlife costs money / reduces income so farmers need payments (points for beetle banks, hedgerows, buffer strips, wild bird seed, low-input grassland)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Benefits everyone so society pays through central funding; legislation cannot compel positive management</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 30, 38-39; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "PRI-05", "sub": "conservation-priorities", "subname": "Conservation priorities, legislation & protocols", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.1.2.3.2", "pages": "31-32", "url": "subtopic/conservation-priorities.html#PRI-05", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the estimated population of blue whales and the number killed by whaling in selected years.", "text": "table 1 shows the estimated population of blue whales and the number killed by whaling in selected years. calculate the percentage decline in the blue whale population between 1900 and 1986. explain why whale populations were so easily over-exploited. describe how the international whaling commission regulates whaling. explain why the itto has been less successful than the iwc in achieving sustainable exploitation. (240 000 - 2 000) / 240 000 x 100 = 99.2% whales are k-selected: slow growing, late maturing, one calf every few years, so populations recover very slowly technology (harpoon cannons, steam / factory ships) allowed catches far above the sustainable yield within decades no regulation / open access to the ocean; whales were valuable (oil, spermaceti) and easily found moratorium on commercial whaling since 1986 until populations recover total protection for certain species; whale sanctuaries limits on numbers and sizes taken; protection of suckling mothers and calves research into whale biology and threats (ship strikes, net entanglement); exceptions: aboriginal subsistence quotas (eg inuit bowheads), 'scientific' permits (japan - ruled not research in 2014), norway / iceland set their own quotas itto's 1990 target of trade only from sustainably managed forests by 2000 was not met and little progress since timber comes from many countries / private land with weak enforcement, high demand and alternative land uses; whaling involves few nations and a single treaty body", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"PRI-05\" data-qid=\"PRI-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/conservation-priorities.html\">Conservation priorities, legislation &amp; protocols</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.2 &middot; Genn pp. 31-32 &middot; PRI-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the estimated population of blue whales and the number killed by whaling in selected years.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Year</th><th>1900</th><th>1930</th><th>1960</th><th>1986</th><th>2020</th></tr><tr><td>Estimated population</td><td>240 000</td><td>100 000</td><td>5 000</td><td>2 000</td><td>15 000</td></tr><tr><td>Whales killed that year</td><td>200</td><td>30 000</td><td>1 200</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Calculate the percentage decline in the blue whale population between 1900 and 1986.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain why whale populations were so easily over-exploited.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Describe how the International Whaling Commission regulates whaling.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Explain why the ITTO has been less successful than the IWC in achieving sustainable exploitation.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(240 000 - 2 000) / 240 000 x 100 = 99.2%</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Whales are k-selected: slow growing, late maturing, one calf every few years, so populations recover very slowly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Technology (harpoon cannons, steam / factory ships) allowed catches far above the sustainable yield within decades</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No regulation / open access to the ocean; whales were valuable (oil, spermaceti) and easily found</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Moratorium on commercial whaling since 1986 until populations recover</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Total protection for certain species; whale sanctuaries</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Limits on numbers and sizes taken; protection of suckling mothers and calves</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Research into whale biology and threats (ship strikes, net entanglement); exceptions: aboriginal subsistence quotas (eg Inuit bowheads), 'scientific' permits (Japan - ruled not research in 2014), Norway / Iceland set their own quotas</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>ITTO's 1990 target of trade only from sustainably managed forests by 2000 was not met and little progress since</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Timber comes from many countries / private land with weak enforcement, high demand and alternative land uses; whaling involves few nations and a single treaty body</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 31-32; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CBR-01", "sub": "cbr-habitat", "subname": "Captive breeding, habitat creation & management", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.3", "pages": "32-34", "url": "subtopic/cbr-habitat.html#CBR-01", "snippet": "Explain the difference between in-situ and ex-situ conservation and state when ex-situ conservation is needed.", "text": " explain the difference between in-situ and ex-situ conservation and state when ex-situ conservation is needed. give four criteria used to select species for captive breeding and release programmes. explain why each of the following species would be difficult to keep or breed in captivity. in-situ: conserving species in their natural habitat with the whole community; ex-situ: away from the natural habitat, eg zoos, botanic gardens, seed banks, captive breeding needed when conservation in the wild is impossible or insufficient to prevent extinction (habitat gone, population too small, poaching) is the wild population threatened? is there a genetically diverse captive population? is in-situ conservation failing? is keeping a captive population realistic (habitat size, food, relationships, cost)? is release likely to be successful now or in future (suitable habitat, threats removed)? blue whale: habitat size far too large to provide; food (huge quantities of krill / plankton) cannot be supplied; cost large blue butterfly: complex inter-species relationship - larvae are taken into ant nests where they overwinter feeding on ant grubs; requires specific ant species and thyme food plant", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CBR-01\" data-qid=\"CBR-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/cbr-habitat.html\">Captive breeding, habitat creation &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 32-34 &middot; CBR-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Explain the difference between in-situ and ex-situ conservation and state when ex-situ conservation is needed.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Give <b>four</b> criteria used to select species for captive breeding and release programmes.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain why each of the following species would be difficult to keep or breed in captivity.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In-situ: conserving species in their natural habitat with the whole community; ex-situ: away from the natural habitat, eg zoos, botanic gardens, seed banks, captive breeding</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Needed when conservation in the wild is impossible or insufficient to prevent extinction (habitat gone, population too small, poaching)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Is the wild population threatened?</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Is there a genetically diverse captive population?</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Is in-situ conservation failing?</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Is keeping a captive population realistic (habitat size, food, relationships, cost)?</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Is release likely to be successful now or in future (suitable habitat, threats removed)?</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Blue whale: habitat size far too large to provide; food (huge quantities of krill / plankton) cannot be supplied; cost</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Large blue butterfly: complex inter-species relationship - larvae are taken into ant nests where they overwinter feeding on ant grubs; requires specific ant species and thyme food plant</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 32-34; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CBR-02", "sub": "cbr-habitat", "subname": "Captive breeding, habitat creation & management", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.3", "pages": "33-35", "url": "subtopic/cbr-habitat.html#CBR-02", "snippet": "The Hawaiian goose (nene) was reduced by hunting, habitat loss and introduced predators to about 30 birds. A captive breeding programme started with just seven individuals and has ", "text": "the hawaiian goose (nene) was reduced by hunting, habitat loss and introduced predators to about 30 birds. a captive breeding programme started with just seven individuals and has increased the wild population to over 2000. explain why starting the programme with seven individuals caused problems, and describe how these problems are managed. explain three reasons, other than gene pool size, why species kept in captivity may fail to breed. describe how artificial insemination and embryo transfer increase breeding success in captive mammals. very small gene pool so individuals are closely related and inbreeding is likely harmful recessive genes are expressed when both parents carry them: some goslings had thin hair-like feathers with poor insulation so survival in cold weather was low a stud book records family trees so breeding is arranged between the least related individuals (often coordinated by a specialist zoo) exchange of individuals / semen between zoos; avoid hybridisation with related species / subspecies breeding is triggered by stimuli (day length, light, temperature, food, body fat) which may be unknown / not provided population interactions: mixing breeding and non-breeding individuals causes conflict; some need a choice of partners, others pair for life breeding habitat needed: courtship display sites, nest sites, social groups (flamingos only breed in large groups - mirrors used) stress / disturbance ai: semen collected (and can be stored / cryopreserved for years) and inserted into the female, so animals need not be transported or meet; avoids dangerous mating (elephants, rhinos) and partner rejection embryo transfer: a female of the endangered species (eg bongo) is treated with hormones to release many eggs, which are fertilised and implanted into surrogate females of a common related species (eland) so many offspring are produced per cycle instead of one calf per year, overcoming the shortage of breeding females", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CBR-02\" data-qid=\"CBR-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/cbr-habitat.html\">Captive breeding, habitat creation &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 33-35 &middot; CBR-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>The Hawaiian goose (nene) was reduced by hunting, habitat loss and introduced predators to about 30 birds. A captive breeding programme started with just seven individuals and has increased the wild population to over 2000.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain why starting the programme with seven individuals caused problems, and describe how these problems are managed.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain <b>three</b> reasons, other than gene pool size, why species kept in captivity may fail to breed.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Describe how artificial insemination and embryo transfer increase breeding success in captive mammals.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Very small gene pool so individuals are closely related and inbreeding is likely</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Harmful recessive genes are expressed when both parents carry them: some goslings had thin hair-like feathers with poor insulation so survival in cold weather was low</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A stud book records family trees so breeding is arranged between the least related individuals (often coordinated by a specialist zoo)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Exchange of individuals / semen between zoos; avoid hybridisation with related species / subspecies</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Breeding is triggered by stimuli (day length, light, temperature, food, body fat) which may be unknown / not provided</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Population interactions: mixing breeding and non-breeding individuals causes conflict; some need a choice of partners, others pair for life</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Breeding habitat needed: courtship display sites, nest sites, social groups (flamingos only breed in large groups - mirrors used)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stress / disturbance</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>AI: semen collected (and can be stored / cryopreserved for years) and inserted into the female, so animals need not be transported or meet; avoids dangerous mating (elephants, rhinos) and partner rejection</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Embryo transfer: a female of the endangered species (eg bongo) is treated with hormones to release many eggs, which are fertilised and implanted into surrogate females of a common related species (eland)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So many offspring are produced per cycle instead of one calf per year, overcoming the shortage of breeding females</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 33-35; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CBR-03", "sub": "cbr-habitat", "subname": "Captive breeding, habitat creation & management", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.3", "pages": "36-37", "url": "subtopic/cbr-habitat.html#CBR-03", "snippet": "Explain the difference between hard and soft release and state the types of species for which each is suitable.", "text": " explain the difference between hard and soft release and state the types of species for which each is suitable. describe five features of a suitable release site. explain the importance of post-release monitoring. hard release: individuals released with no post-release support - suitable for species whose behaviour is instinctive and need no learned skills (insects, fish, amphibians, reptiles) soft release: gradual release into larger areas with food provided while they learn to find food, avoid predators - needed for mammals and birds problems faced: finding / recognising food and poisonous foods, hunting skills (captive-born predators never learned), avoiding predators, acceptance into wild social groups large enough suitable habitat for a viable population reliable food supply low predation risk / original threat removed suitable breeding sites water support of the local human population official / legal protection of habitat and species tracking (radio / gps tags, rings) shows survival, dispersal and breeding of released animals so the success of the project can be assessed identifies causes of failure so future releases can be improved / extra support given", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CBR-03\" data-qid=\"CBR-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/cbr-habitat.html\">Captive breeding, habitat creation &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 36-37 &middot; CBR-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Explain the difference between hard and soft release and state the types of species for which each is suitable.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Describe <b>five</b> features of a suitable release site.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain the importance of post-release monitoring.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hard release: individuals released with no post-release support - suitable for species whose behaviour is instinctive and need no learned skills (insects, fish, amphibians, reptiles)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Soft release: gradual release into larger areas with food provided while they learn to find food, avoid predators - needed for mammals and birds</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Problems faced: finding / recognising food and poisonous foods, hunting skills (captive-born predators never learned), avoiding predators, acceptance into wild social groups</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 5 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 5.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Large enough suitable habitat for a viable population</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reliable food supply</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Low predation risk / original threat removed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Suitable breeding sites</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Support of the local human population</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Official / legal protection of habitat and species</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Tracking (radio / GPS tags, rings) shows survival, dispersal and breeding of released animals so the success of the project can be assessed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Identifies causes of failure so future releases can be improved / extra support given</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 36-37; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CBR-04", "sub": "cbr-habitat", "subname": "Captive breeding, habitat creation & management", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.3", "pages": "35-37", "url": "subtopic/cbr-habitat.html#CBR-04", "snippet": "Describe how cloning could be used to conserve the northern white rhinoceros, and explain why success has been limited.", "text": " describe how cloning could be used to conserve the northern white rhinoceros, and explain why success has been limited. explain how micro-propagation and cloning are used to conserve rare plants such as the atlantic black poplar. describe the purpose and methods of the millennium seed bank. the nucleus of a stem cell from the endangered species is transferred into an empty egg of a closely related species (southern white rhino) the embryo is implanted into a surrogate female of the related species which gives birth to a northern white rhino very low success rates with wildlife so far; deep-frozen cells could allow clones even after the last individuals die micro-propagation: tissue culture producing many cell clusters from one plant, each grown into a genetically identical plant black poplar: cuttings / pollarded stems of pure native trees are rooted to produce many identical trees needed because the flowers are pollinated by introduced subspecies producing hybrids, so cloning maintains the pure gene pool (but no genetic diversity) stores seeds of wild plants (especially threatened species and crop wild relatives) so species are not lost if extinct in the wild seeds collected worldwide; target of 10 000 seeds per species to maintain a diverse gene pool stored dry and refrigerated / frozen in an underground reinforced vault to reduce risk of accident seeds germinated periodically to check viability and replenish stocks; can be used for reintroduction / breeding", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CBR-04\" data-qid=\"CBR-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/cbr-habitat.html\">Captive breeding, habitat creation &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 35-37 &middot; CBR-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Describe how cloning could be used to conserve the northern white rhinoceros, and explain why success has been limited.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain how micro-propagation and cloning are used to conserve rare plants such as the Atlantic black poplar.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Describe the purpose and methods of the Millennium Seed Bank.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The nucleus of a stem cell from the endangered species is transferred into an empty egg of a closely related species (southern white rhino)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The embryo is implanted into a surrogate female of the related species which gives birth to a northern white rhino</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Very low success rates with wildlife so far; deep-frozen cells could allow clones even after the last individuals die</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Micro-propagation: tissue culture producing many cell clusters from one plant, each grown into a genetically identical plant</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Black poplar: cuttings / pollarded stems of pure native trees are rooted to produce many identical trees</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Needed because the flowers are pollinated by introduced subspecies producing hybrids, so cloning maintains the pure gene pool (but no genetic diversity)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stores seeds of wild plants (especially threatened species and crop wild relatives) so species are not lost if extinct in the wild</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Seeds collected worldwide; target of 10 000 seeds per species to maintain a diverse gene pool</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stored dry and refrigerated / frozen in an underground reinforced vault to reduce risk of accident</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Seeds germinated periodically to check viability and replenish stocks; can be used for reintroduction / breeding</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 35-37; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CBR-05", "sub": "cbr-habitat", "subname": "Captive breeding, habitat creation & management", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.4", "pages": "40-42", "url": "subtopic/cbr-habitat.html#CBR-05", "snippet": "A conservation organisation is converting 700 ha of arable farmland on the coast into a wetland reserve for breeding and wintering birds (as at Wallasea Island).", "text": "a conservation organisation is converting 700 ha of arable farmland on the coast into a wetland reserve for breeding and wintering birds (as at wallasea island). explain why habitat area and shape are important in the design of the reserve. explain how the reserve could be designed to provide a range of niches. explain why some species may need to be deliberately introduced while others colonise naturally. explain the value of a biological corridor linking the reserve to other wetlands. area must be large enough to support viable populations (breeding numbers high enough, gene pool large enough to avoid inbreeding); species higher up food chains need larger areas (lower density) large areas contain a full range of resources (eg different fruiting trees / feeding areas through the year) shape: the perimeter has edge conditions (a mix of neighbouring habitats); a compact shape maximises core habitat for species needing core conditions some species benefit from edges (higher light) or small isolated habitats (ponds too small for predatory fish suit amphibians) varied topography: banks, lagoons, islands, ditches created with machinery give a range of water depths (emergent, floating-leaved, submerged plant zones) islands provide roosting / nesting sites safe from terrestrial predators (foxes) control of salinity (eg intertidal conditions for opossum shrimps feeding dunlin and avocet); scrapes for skylarks; different water depths for waders and ducks mobile species (birds, flying insects) colonise easily less mobile species (many plants, amphibians, invertebrates) may not reach an isolated site so must be introduced; keystone species especially allows dispersal of surplus young, mixing of gene pools (less inbreeding) and recolonisation if a population dies out; avoids hazards (eg rope bridges for howler monkeys over roads)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CBR-05\" data-qid=\"CBR-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/cbr-habitat.html\">Captive breeding, habitat creation &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 40-42 &middot; CBR-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A conservation organisation is converting 700 ha of arable farmland on the coast into a wetland reserve for breeding and wintering birds (as at Wallasea Island).</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Explain why habitat area and shape are important in the design of the reserve.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain how the reserve could be designed to provide a range of niches.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain why some species may need to be deliberately introduced while others colonise naturally.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Explain the value of a biological corridor linking the reserve to other wetlands.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Area must be large enough to support viable populations (breeding numbers high enough, gene pool large enough to avoid inbreeding); species higher up food chains need larger areas (lower density)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Large areas contain a full range of resources (eg different fruiting trees / feeding areas through the year)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Shape: the perimeter has edge conditions (a mix of neighbouring habitats); a compact shape maximises core habitat for species needing core conditions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Some species benefit from edges (higher light) or small isolated habitats (ponds too small for predatory fish suit amphibians)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Varied topography: banks, lagoons, islands, ditches created with machinery give a range of water depths (emergent, floating-leaved, submerged plant zones)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Islands provide roosting / nesting sites safe from terrestrial predators (foxes)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Control of salinity (eg intertidal conditions for opossum shrimps feeding dunlin and avocet); scrapes for skylarks; different water depths for waders and ducks</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mobile species (birds, flying insects) colonise easily</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Less mobile species (many plants, amphibians, invertebrates) may not reach an isolated site so must be introduced; keystone species especially</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Allows dispersal of surplus young, mixing of gene pools (less inbreeding) and recolonisation if a population dies out; avoids hazards (eg rope bridges for howler monkeys over roads)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 40-42; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CBR-06", "sub": "cbr-habitat", "subname": "Captive breeding, habitat creation & management", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.4, 3.1.3.1", "pages": "43-46", "url": "subtopic/cbr-habitat.html#CBR-06", "snippet": "Describe how each of the following abiotic factors can be managed to increase biodiversity in a nature reserve.", "text": " describe how each of the following abiotic factors can be managed to increase biodiversity in a nature reserve. explain how biotic factors can be managed to increase the survival of a species. give four examples. light: selective felling to create clearings for plants needing high light while retaining dense canopy for shade species; clearings also give warm sand / soil for lizard eggs; leave dead wood; woodland floor plants (bluebells) grow before the canopy leafs dissolved oxygen: narrowing a slow river increases flow and turbulence so more oxygen dissolves, suiting trout and mayfly larvae; shallow water warms quickly for egg development nutrients: removing fertile topsoil / not fertilising allows plants of nutrient-poor soils (eg sundew on acidic nitrate-deficient soils) that cannot compete on fertile ground; dung patches add local nutrients food: wildflower grasslands for seed-eating birds; providing food species control of predation: island nest sites unreachable by foxes; trapping / culling / eradicating introduced rats (scilly isles - manx shearwater and storm petrel breeding success increased) control of competitors: removing rhododendron so wildflowers get light pollination: a range of flowering plants with different flowering times so pollinators have food all season seed dispersal species (hippos, elephants, monkeys) conserved; control of introduced pathogens", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CBR-06\" data-qid=\"CBR-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/cbr-habitat.html\">Captive breeding, habitat creation &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.4, 3.1.3.1 &middot; Genn pp. 43-46 &middot; CBR-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Describe how each of the following abiotic factors can be managed to increase biodiversity in a nature reserve.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain how biotic factors can be managed to increase the survival of a species. Give <b>four</b> examples.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Light: selective felling to create clearings for plants needing high light while retaining dense canopy for shade species; clearings also give warm sand / soil for lizard eggs; leave dead wood; woodland floor plants (bluebells) grow before the canopy leafs</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Dissolved oxygen: narrowing a slow river increases flow and turbulence so more oxygen dissolves, suiting trout and mayfly larvae; shallow water warms quickly for egg development</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Nutrients: removing fertile topsoil / not fertilising allows plants of nutrient-poor soils (eg sundew on acidic nitrate-deficient soils) that cannot compete on fertile ground; dung patches add local nutrients</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Food: wildflower grasslands for seed-eating birds; providing food species</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Control of predation: island nest sites unreachable by foxes; trapping / culling / eradicating introduced rats (Scilly Isles - Manx shearwater and storm petrel breeding success increased)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Control of competitors: removing rhododendron so wildflowers get light</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pollination: a range of flowering plants with different flowering times so pollinators have food all season</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Seed dispersal species (hippos, elephants, monkeys) conserved; control of introduced pathogens</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 43-46; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.4, 3.1.3.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CBR-07", "sub": "cbr-habitat", "subname": "Captive breeding, habitat creation & management", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.1.3.3", "pages": "47-49", "url": "subtopic/cbr-habitat.html#CBR-07", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the number of plant species per m2 in a chalk grassland reserve under different management, ten years after management began.", "text": "table 1 shows the number of plant species per m2 in a chalk grassland reserve under different management, ten years after management began. explain what is meant by a plagioclimax community and why chalk grassland is an example. use table 1 to explain why grazing produced the highest plant diversity. explain why different grazing animals are chosen for different plagioclimax habitats. explain how burning maintains heathland. a community maintained at a stage before the climax by regular human activity (grazing, mowing, burning, coppicing) that deflects succession chalk grassland was produced by centuries of sheep grazing; without it succession to scrub and woodland resumes grazing removes taller / dominant grasses and prevents scrub establishing (5% cover) so small plants are not shaded out trampling creates bare ground for wildflower seed germination and warm patches for invertebrates / reptiles dung creates small nutrient-rich patches supporting different plants and invertebrates; sheep eat short grasses and some bushes mowing removes everything at once including plants grazers avoid, and removes flowers / seeds before setting; burning removes vegetation but also kills invertebrates and allows scrub regrowth; abandoned plot dominated by scrub (55%) so few species sheep eat short grass (some breeds eat scrub); ponies avoid flowers but eat bracken; cattle remove long rough grass choice depends on the vegetation to be controlled and the plants to be protected removes mature heather bushes and young trees that grazers will not eat, creating open unshaded ground heat stimulates dormant heather seeds to germinate so young heather re-establishes", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CBR-07\" data-qid=\"CBR-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/cbr-habitat.html\">Captive breeding, habitat creation &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.1.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 47-49 &middot; CBR-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the number of plant species per m<sup>2</sup> in a chalk grassland reserve under different management, ten years after management began.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Management</th><th>Mean plant species per m<sup>2</sup></th><th>Percentage of area covered by scrub / bushes</th></tr><tr><td>No management (abandoned)</td><td>8</td><td>55</td></tr><tr><td>Sheep grazing</td><td>24</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Annual mowing (August)</td><td>19</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Winter burning</td><td>14</td><td>10</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Explain what is meant by a <b>plagioclimax</b> community and why chalk grassland is an example.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Use Table 1 to explain why grazing produced the highest plant diversity.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Explain why different grazing animals are chosen for different plagioclimax habitats.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>Explain how burning maintains heathland.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A community maintained at a stage before the climax by regular human activity (grazing, mowing, burning, coppicing) that deflects succession</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Chalk grassland was produced by centuries of sheep grazing; without it succession to scrub and woodland resumes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Grazing removes taller / dominant grasses and prevents scrub establishing (5% cover) so small plants are not shaded out</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Trampling creates bare ground for wildflower seed germination and warm patches for invertebrates / reptiles</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Dung creates small nutrient-rich patches supporting different plants and invertebrates; sheep eat short grasses and some bushes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mowing removes everything at once including plants grazers avoid, and removes flowers / seeds before setting; burning removes vegetation but also kills invertebrates and allows scrub regrowth; abandoned plot dominated by scrub (55%) so few species</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sheep eat short grass (some breeds eat scrub); ponies avoid flowers but eat bracken; cattle remove long rough grass</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Choice depends on the vegetation to be controlled and the plants to be protected</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Removes mature heather bushes and young trees that grazers will not eat, creating open unshaded ground</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Heat stimulates dormant heather seeds to germinate so young heather re-establishes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 47-49; AQA spec 3.1.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CBR-08", "sub": "cbr-habitat", "subname": "Captive breeding, habitat creation & management", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.3.3-3.1.3.4", "pages": "47-49", "url": "subtopic/cbr-habitat.html#CBR-08", "snippet": "Describe coppicing and explain how it increases the biodiversity of a woodland.", "text": " describe coppicing and explain how it increases the biodiversity of a woodland. explain how the reintroduction of grey wolves to yellowstone national park increased biodiversity. explain why culling or removing species may be necessary in habitat management. trees (hazel, oak) are cut to ground level every 3-30 years and allowed to regrow from the stump about 0.5 ha cut each year on a rotation (eg 8 years) so the wood is a patchwork of areas of different ages each age has its own community: newly cut areas get high light for wildflowers and butterflies; older areas give dense cover for nesting birds / dormice pollarding cuts above browsing height so deer / livestock cannot eat the regrowth wolves (top predator, exterminated by 1926) reduced deer populations and changed their behaviour fewer young trees eaten so trees and riverside vegetation recovered more trees allowed beaver populations to increase; beaver dams increased wetland area wetland species (birds, fish, invertebrates) became more common; overall biodiversity rose, though wolves cause problems for livestock farmers undesirable species (introduced predators / competitors, or natural succession species such as scrub, conifers in the new forest heathland) reduce survival of the target species removal restores suitable conditions (eg heathland for sand lizards and smooth snakes) or reduces predation / competition", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CBR-08\" data-qid=\"CBR-08\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 8</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/cbr-habitat.html\">Captive breeding, habitat creation &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.1.3.3-3.1.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 47-49 &middot; CBR-08</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.1</span>Describe coppicing and explain how it increases the biodiversity of a woodland.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.2</span>Explain how the reintroduction of grey wolves to Yellowstone National Park increased biodiversity.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.3</span>Explain why culling or removing species may be necessary in habitat management.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Trees (hazel, oak) are cut to ground level every 3-30 years and allowed to regrow from the stump</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>About 0.5 ha cut each year on a rotation (eg 8 years) so the wood is a patchwork of areas of different ages</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Each age has its own community: newly cut areas get high light for wildflowers and butterflies; older areas give dense cover for nesting birds / dormice</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pollarding cuts above browsing height so deer / livestock cannot eat the regrowth</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wolves (top predator, exterminated by 1926) reduced deer populations and changed their behaviour</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fewer young trees eaten so trees and riverside vegetation recovered</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>More trees allowed beaver populations to increase; beaver dams increased wetland area</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wetland species (birds, fish, invertebrates) became more common; overall biodiversity rose, though wolves cause problems for livestock farmers</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Undesirable species (introduced predators / competitors, or natural succession species such as scrub, conifers in the New Forest heathland) reduce survival of the target species</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Removal restores suitable conditions (eg heathland for sand lizards and smooth snakes) or reduces predation / competition</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 47-49; AQA spec 3.1.3.3-3.1.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "CBR-09", "sub": "cbr-habitat", "subname": "Captive breeding, habitat creation & management", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.1.2.3.3-3.1.2.3.4", "pages": "32-49", "url": "subtopic/cbr-habitat.html#CBR-09", "snippet": "Evaluate the relative importance of ex-situ and in-situ methods in the conservation of endangered species.", "text": " evaluate the relative importance of ex-situ and in-situ methods in the conservation of endangered species. state what is meant by rewilding. in-situ: species remain in natural conditions with the whole community and inter-species relationships; protected areas (sssi, nnr, sac, spa, ramsar, mcz), land ownership, agri-environment schemes, habitat creation and design (area, shape, corridors, diversity), management of abiotic and biotic factors, plagioclimax maintenance, population control, reintroductions and rewilding (yellowstone wolves, scottish beavers) limits of in-situ: external threats (pollution, climate change), enforcement, cost, conflicts with land use, small fragmented populations ex-situ: captive breeding when in-situ is impossible or insufficient; selection criteria; difficulties keeping and breeding (habitat size, food, relationships, breeding triggers, group dynamics, gene pool / inbreeding, hybridisation); techniques (stud books, cryopreservation, ai, embryo transfer, micropropagation, cloning); seed banks; successes (hawaiian goose, iberian lynx, red kite, large blue) limits of ex-situ: expensive, few species, loss of wild behaviour, small gene pools, release requires suitable habitat and post-release support (hard vs soft release, monitoring) judgement: ex-situ is a temporary safeguard / support that only succeeds if in-situ habitat exists to return to; both needed, in-situ fundamental restoring natural processes / communities to a habitat changed by humans, often by reintroducing missing (keystone) species and removing human management such as grazing", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"CBR-09\" data-qid=\"CBR-09\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 9</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/cbr-habitat.html\">Captive breeding, habitat creation &amp; management</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.3-3.1.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 32-49 &middot; CBR-09</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.1</span>Evaluate the relative importance of ex-situ and in-situ methods in the conservation of endangered species.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.2</span>State what is meant by <b>rewilding</b>.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>In-situ: species remain in natural conditions with the whole community and inter-species relationships; protected areas (SSSI, NNR, SAC, SPA, Ramsar, MCZ), land ownership, agri-environment schemes, habitat creation and design (area, shape, corridors, diversity), management of abiotic and biotic factors, plagioclimax maintenance, population control, reintroductions and rewilding (Yellowstone wolves, Scottish beavers)</li><li>Limits of in-situ: external threats (pollution, climate change), enforcement, cost, conflicts with land use, small fragmented populations</li><li>Ex-situ: captive breeding when in-situ is impossible or insufficient; selection criteria; difficulties keeping and breeding (habitat size, food, relationships, breeding triggers, group dynamics, gene pool / inbreeding, hybridisation); techniques (stud books, cryopreservation, AI, embryo transfer, micropropagation, cloning); seed banks; successes (Hawaiian goose, Iberian lynx, red kite, large blue)</li><li>Limits of ex-situ: expensive, few species, loss of wild behaviour, small gene pools, release requires suitable habitat and post-release support (hard vs soft release, monitoring)</li><li>Judgement: ex-situ is a temporary safeguard / support that only succeeds if in-situ habitat exists to return to; both needed, in-situ fundamental</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Restoring natural processes / communities to a habitat changed by humans, often by reintroducing missing (keystone) species and removing human management such as grazing</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 32-49; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.3-3.1.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "LIFE-01", "sub": "life-conditions", "subname": "Conditions for life on Earth", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.1.1.1", "pages": "5-7", "url": "subtopic/life-conditions.html#LIFE-01", "snippet": "Table 1 shows some features of Earth, Mars and Venus.", "text": "table 1 shows some features of earth, mars and venus. use table 1 to explain why liquid water cannot exist on the surface of mars. explain how the earth's mass and its distance from the sun created suitable conditions for life. explain the importance of the earth's magnetic field and its speed of rotation for living organisms. explain how the tilt of the earth's axis affects living organisms. mars has low mass so weak gravity and could not retain a dense atmosphere very low atmospheric pressure means water would boil / evaporate away even at low temperatures greater distance from the sun gives low insolation and a mean temperature of -60 °c so water is frozen mass great enough for gravity to retain an atmosphere containing the elements needed (c, h, o, n as co2, methane, ammonia, water vapour) and to maintain pressure so liquid water does not boil distance from the sun (the 'goldilocks zone') gives insolation producing temperatures at which water is liquid (0-35 °c over most of the surface) and proteins are not denatured too close (venus) - too hot; too far (mars) - too cold the molten core produces a magnetosphere that deflects the solar wind / charged particles, preventing biologically damaging radiation reaching the surface the 24-hour rotation limits heating by day and cooling by night, reducing temperature extremes (venus' slow rotation gives extreme conditions) the tilted axis causes seasonal variation in insolation as the earth orbits the sun producing seasons (temperature, day length) to which species' life cycles (breeding, migration, dormancy) are adapted; the moon's gravity keeps the tilt stable and creates tides", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"LIFE-01\" data-qid=\"LIFE-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/life-conditions.html\">Conditions for life on Earth</a> &middot; spec 3.1.1.1 &middot; Genn pp. 5-7 &middot; LIFE-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows some features of Earth, Mars and Venus.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Venus</th><th>Earth</th><th>Mars</th></tr><tr><td>Mean distance from Sun / million km</td><td>108</td><td>150</td><td>228</td></tr><tr><td>Mass relative to Earth</td><td>0.82</td><td>1.0</td><td>0.11</td></tr><tr><td>Atmospheric pressure relative to Earth</td><td>92</td><td>1.0</td><td>0.006</td></tr><tr><td>Mean surface temperature / &deg;C</td><td>460</td><td>15</td><td>-60</td></tr><tr><td>Magnetic field</td><td>Very weak</td><td>Strong</td><td>Very weak</td></tr><tr><td>Rotation period / hours</td><td>5 800</td><td>24</td><td>25</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Use Table 1 to explain why liquid water cannot exist on the surface of Mars.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain how the Earth's mass and its distance from the Sun created suitable conditions for life.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain the importance of the Earth's magnetic field and its speed of rotation for living organisms.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Explain how the tilt of the Earth's axis affects living organisms.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mars has low mass so weak gravity and could not retain a dense atmosphere</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Very low atmospheric pressure means water would boil / evaporate away even at low temperatures</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Greater distance from the Sun gives low insolation and a mean temperature of -60 &deg;C so water is frozen</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mass great enough for gravity to retain an atmosphere containing the elements needed (C, H, O, N as CO2, methane, ammonia, water vapour) and to maintain pressure so liquid water does not boil</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Distance from the Sun (the 'Goldilocks zone') gives insolation producing temperatures at which water is liquid (0-35 &deg;C over most of the surface) and proteins are not denatured</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Too close (Venus) - too hot; too far (Mars) - too cold</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The molten core produces a magnetosphere that deflects the solar wind / charged particles, preventing biologically damaging radiation reaching the surface</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The 24-hour rotation limits heating by day and cooling by night, reducing temperature extremes (Venus' slow rotation gives extreme conditions)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The tilted axis causes seasonal variation in insolation as the Earth orbits the Sun</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Producing seasons (temperature, day length) to which species' life cycles (breeding, migration, dormancy) are adapted; the Moon's gravity keeps the tilt stable and creates tides</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 5-7; AQA spec 3.1.1.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "LIFE-02", "sub": "life-conditions", "subname": "Conditions for life on Earth", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.1.1-3.1.1.2", "pages": "7-8", "url": "subtopic/life-conditions.html#LIFE-02", "snippet": "Explain four properties of water that make it essential for life.", "text": " explain four properties of water that make it essential for life. describe how photosynthetic organisms changed the atmosphere and explain how this allowed life to colonise the land. explain how carbon sequestration by early photoautotrophs helped maintain a suitable temperature on earth. general physiological solvent: most biological reactions take place between substances dissolved in water transport medium in blood / sap carrying gases, sugars, amino acids, nutrients, wastes high specific heat capacity moderates temperature changes; evaporation absorbs heat (cooling) anomalous expansion: ice floats so water bodies do not freeze solid; absorbs uv protecting early marine life; provides aquatic habitats from about 2.7 billion years ago photosynthetic archaea / bacteria (then algae and plants) released oxygen; at first it reacted with dissolved iron (forming iron ore deposits) then built up in the oceans and atmosphere (from about 2.45 billion years ago) oxygen in the stratosphere absorbed uv, forming ozone (o3) in a dynamic equilibrium of o, o2 and o3 the ozone layer reduced uv reaching the surface so organisms could survive out of the water oxygen allowed aerobic respiration releasing more energy so complex multicellular organisms evolved; many anaerobic archaea died out photosynthesis removed co2 (a greenhouse gas) from the atmosphere, storing carbon in sediments, carbonate rocks and fossil fuels this reduced infrared retention, offsetting the increasing brightness of the sun (about 10% per billion years) so temperatures did not rise excessively", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"LIFE-02\" data-qid=\"LIFE-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/life-conditions.html\">Conditions for life on Earth</a> &middot; spec 3.1.1.1-3.1.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 7-8 &middot; LIFE-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain <b>four</b> properties of water that make it essential for life.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Describe how photosynthetic organisms changed the atmosphere and explain how this allowed life to colonise the land.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain how carbon sequestration by early photoautotrophs helped maintain a suitable temperature on Earth.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>General physiological solvent: most biological reactions take place between substances dissolved in water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Transport medium in blood / sap carrying gases, sugars, amino acids, nutrients, wastes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>High specific heat capacity moderates temperature changes; evaporation absorbs heat (cooling)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Anomalous expansion: ice floats so water bodies do not freeze solid; absorbs UV protecting early marine life; provides aquatic habitats</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>From about 2.7 billion years ago photosynthetic archaea / bacteria (then algae and plants) released oxygen; at first it reacted with dissolved iron (forming iron ore deposits) then built up in the oceans and atmosphere (from about 2.45 billion years ago)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Oxygen in the stratosphere absorbed UV, forming ozone (O3) in a dynamic equilibrium of O, O2 and O3</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The ozone layer reduced UV reaching the surface so organisms could survive out of the water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Oxygen allowed aerobic respiration releasing more energy so complex multicellular organisms evolved; many anaerobic archaea died out</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Photosynthesis removed CO2 (a greenhouse gas) from the atmosphere, storing carbon in sediments, carbonate rocks and fossil fuels</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>This reduced infrared retention, offsetting the increasing brightness of the Sun (about 10% per billion years) so temperatures did not rise excessively</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 7-8; AQA spec 3.1.1.1-3.1.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "LIFE-03", "sub": "life-conditions", "subname": "Conditions for life on Earth", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.1.2-3.1.1.3", "pages": "8-9", "url": "subtopic/life-conditions.html#LIFE-03", "snippet": "Explain how the development of biogeochemical cycles and of transpiration by land plants helped to maintain stable conditions for life.", "text": " explain how the development of biogeochemical cycles and of transpiration by land plants helped to maintain stable conditions for life. describe three limitations of early methods of studying past conditions on earth. describe how each of the following modern techniques provides information about past conditions. interconnected biological processes recycle nutrient elements so small amounts support life indefinitely without depletion; waste products of one process are raw materials of another so wastes do not build up transpiration returned water vapour to the atmosphere increasing rainfall elsewhere allowing more plant growth, further stabilising climate and soils lack of ancient historical / instrumental data reliance on proxy data (tree rings, pollen) of limited reliability for ancient conditions lack of sophisticated equipment / inability to measure many factors limited coordination between researchers; lack of data collection in many areas (oceans, poles) the ratio of oxygen-18 to oxygen-16 in the ice indicates the temperature when the snow fell air bubbles trapped in ice give the composition of the past atmosphere (co2, methane) ratio of carbon-14 to carbon-12 dates organic material (eg pollen in sediments) more magnesium is incorporated into calcite at higher temperatures, so the ratio estimates past sea temperature", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"LIFE-03\" data-qid=\"LIFE-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/life-conditions.html\">Conditions for life on Earth</a> &middot; spec 3.1.1.2-3.1.1.3 &middot; Genn pp. 8-9 &middot; LIFE-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Explain how the development of biogeochemical cycles and of transpiration by land plants helped to maintain stable conditions for life.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Describe <b>three</b> limitations of early methods of studying past conditions on Earth.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Describe how each of the following modern techniques provides information about past conditions.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Interconnected biological processes recycle nutrient elements so small amounts support life indefinitely without depletion; waste products of one process are raw materials of another so wastes do not build up</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Transpiration returned water vapour to the atmosphere increasing rainfall elsewhere</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Allowing more plant growth, further stabilising climate and soils</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lack of ancient historical / instrumental data</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reliance on proxy data (tree rings, pollen) of limited reliability for ancient conditions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lack of sophisticated equipment / inability to measure many factors</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Limited coordination between researchers; lack of data collection in many areas (oceans, poles)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The ratio of oxygen-18 to oxygen-16 in the ice indicates the temperature when the snow fell</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Air bubbles trapped in ice give the composition of the past atmosphere (CO2, methane)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ratio of carbon-14 to carbon-12 dates organic material (eg pollen in sediments)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>More magnesium is incorporated into calcite at higher temperatures, so the ratio estimates past sea temperature</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 8-9; AQA spec 3.1.1.2-3.1.1.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "LIFE-04", "sub": "life-conditions", "subname": "Conditions for life on Earth", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.1.1.1", "pages": "5-7", "url": "subtopic/life-conditions.html#LIFE-04", "snippet": "Which feature of the Earth prevents most atmospheric gases from escaping into space? Tick one box.", "text": " which feature of the earth prevents most atmospheric gases from escaping into space? tick one box. explain how the speed of rotation of the earth and the tilt of its axis affect the conditions for life. explain why the presence of liquid water on earth depends on both the distance from the sun and atmospheric pressure. its mass the 24-hour rotation period means the surface is not exposed to sunlight for long, so temperature extremes / excessive heating and cooling are reduced the axis is tilted relative to the orbit so the amount of insolation at each latitude changes through the year, producing seasonal variations in conditions the distance from the sun gives surface temperatures between 0 and 35 °c in most areas: too close and water would boil / evaporate, too far and it would freeze atmospheric pressure (produced by the mass of gases held by gravity) is high enough to stop the water boiling / evaporating away; at much lower pressure there would be no liquid water", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"LIFE-04\" data-qid=\"LIFE-04\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/life-conditions.html\">Conditions for life on Earth</a> &middot; spec 3.1.1.1 &middot; Genn pp. 5-7 &middot; LIFE-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Which feature of the Earth prevents most atmospheric gases from escaping into space? Tick <b>one</b> box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; Its mass</li><li>B&nbsp; Its magnetic field</li><li>C&nbsp; Its distance from the Sun</li><li>D&nbsp; Its speed of rotation</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain how the speed of rotation of the Earth and the tilt of its axis affect the conditions for life.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain why the presence of liquid water on Earth depends on <b>both</b> the distance from the Sun <b>and</b> atmospheric pressure.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b>A</b>&nbsp; Its mass</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The 24-hour rotation period means the surface is not exposed to sunlight for long, so temperature extremes / excessive heating and cooling are reduced</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The axis is tilted relative to the orbit so the amount of insolation at each latitude changes through the year, producing seasonal variations in conditions</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The distance from the Sun gives surface temperatures between 0 and 35 &deg;C in most areas: too close and water would boil / evaporate, too far and it would freeze</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Atmospheric pressure (produced by the mass of gases held by gravity) is high enough to stop the water boiling / evaporating away; at much lower pressure there would be no liquid water</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 5-7; AQA spec 3.1.1.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "LIFE-05", "sub": "life-conditions", "subname": "Conditions for life on Earth", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.1.1.2", "pages": "6-8", "url": "subtopic/life-conditions.html#LIFE-05", "snippet": "Table 1 shows some events in the history of life on Earth.", "text": "table 1 shows some events in the history of life on earth. use table 1 to calculate how many million years passed between the appearance of photosynthetic organisms and the start of the rise in atmospheric oxygen. explain why atmospheric oxygen did not start to rise as soon as photosynthesis began. explain how the rise in atmospheric oxygen made life on the land surface possible. explain how early photosynthetic organisms helped to prevent a long-term rise in the earth's temperature. give two other ways in which living organisms changed the conditions on earth. 2.7 - 2.45 = 0.25 billion = 250 million years for millions of years all the oxygen produced reacted with (dissolved) iron in the oceans only when all the iron had reacted did surplus dissolved oxygen build up in the oceans and then escape into the atmosphere oxygen in the atmosphere absorbed ultraviolet light a dynamic equilibrium of reactions between o, o2 and o3 (the ozone layer) developed less uv reached the surface so organisms were no longer restricted to water (which had absorbed the uv) aerobic respiration became possible so more complex / larger organisms (plants and animals) evolved; many anaerobic archaea and bacteria died out photoautotrophs absorbed carbon dioxide, a greenhouse gas, and some of the carbon was stored in geological sediments such as carbonate rocks and fossil fuels (carbon sequestration) the lower co2 concentration reduced the retention of heat, offsetting the increase in the brightness of the sun (about 10% per billion years) biogeochemical cycles developed so small amounts of nutrient elements could support life for long periods without depletion transpiration by land plants returned water vapour to the atmosphere and increased rainfall in other areas regulation of atmospheric composition (eg oxygen / co2 by photosynthesis and respiration)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"LIFE-05\" data-qid=\"LIFE-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/life-conditions.html\">Conditions for life on Earth</a> &middot; spec 3.1.1.2 &middot; Genn pp. 6-8 &middot; LIFE-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows some events in the history of life on Earth.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Event</th><th>Time before present / billion years</th></tr><tr><td>Formation of the Earth</td><td>4.6</td></tr><tr><td>First life (Archaea) on Earth</td><td>3.5</td></tr><tr><td>First photosynthetic organisms</td><td>2.7</td></tr><tr><td>Oxygen concentration in the atmosphere starts to rise</td><td>2.45</td></tr><tr><td>End of the period of oxygen build-up (Proterozoic)</td><td>0.54</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Use Table 1 to calculate how many <b>million</b> years passed between the appearance of photosynthetic organisms and the start of the rise in atmospheric oxygen.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain why atmospheric oxygen did not start to rise as soon as photosynthesis began.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain how the rise in atmospheric oxygen made life on the land surface possible.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Explain how early photosynthetic organisms helped to prevent a long-term rise in the Earth's temperature.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.5</span>Give <b>two</b> other ways in which living organisms changed the conditions on Earth.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>2.7 - 2.45 = 0.25 billion = 250 million years</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>For millions of years all the oxygen produced reacted with (dissolved) iron in the oceans</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Only when all the iron had reacted did surplus dissolved oxygen build up in the oceans and then escape into the atmosphere</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Oxygen in the atmosphere absorbed ultraviolet light</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A dynamic equilibrium of reactions between O, O<sub>2</sub> and O<sub>3</sub> (the ozone layer) developed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Less UV reached the surface so organisms were no longer restricted to water (which had absorbed the UV)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Aerobic respiration became possible so more complex / larger organisms (plants and animals) evolved; many anaerobic Archaea and bacteria died out</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Photoautotrophs absorbed carbon dioxide, a greenhouse gas, and some of the carbon was stored in geological sediments such as carbonate rocks and fossil fuels (carbon sequestration)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The lower CO<sub>2</sub> concentration reduced the retention of heat, offsetting the increase in the brightness of the Sun (about 10% per billion years)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Biogeochemical cycles developed so small amounts of nutrient elements could support life for long periods without depletion</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Transpiration by land plants returned water vapour to the atmosphere and increased rainfall in other areas</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Regulation of atmospheric composition (eg oxygen / CO<sub>2</sub> by photosynthesis and respiration)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 6-8; AQA spec 3.1.1.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "LIFE-06", "sub": "life-conditions", "subname": "Conditions for life on Earth", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.1.1.1-3.1.1.3", "pages": "6-9", "url": "subtopic/life-conditions.html#LIFE-06", "snippet": "Explain why proxy data are needed to study the early conditions on Earth.", "text": " explain why proxy data are needed to study the early conditions on earth. complete table 1 to show what each analytical technique can be used to estimate. explain why an understanding of continental drift and ocean currents has been important in explaining past changes in conditions. life probably first developed around volcanic vents on the seabed. explain why the conditions in the oceans were more suitable than those on land 3.5 billion years ago. direct measurements of past conditions cannot be taken, so indirect evidence that is related to the condition must be used age / date of the sample (radioisotope dating) past temperatures composition of the past atmosphere (eg co2 / methane concentrations) temperature: more magnesium is incorporated into calcite at higher temperatures movement of continents changed the position of land masses / latitude and therefore the climate and habitats present ocean currents redistribute heat and nutrients; changes in currents (eg as continents moved) altered climates, so past conditions cannot be explained without knowing how the systems worked there was no oxygen / ozone layer so high levels of uv radiation reached the land surface; water absorbed the uv and protected organisms in the oceans warm temperatures around the vents and a rich mix of chemicals / increasingly complex organic molecules in the sea made biological reactions more likely the atmosphere contained toxic gases such as ammonia; the early archaea were anaerobic so did not need oxygen water acted as the solvent for reactions / transport", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"LIFE-06\" data-qid=\"LIFE-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/life-conditions.html\">Conditions for life on Earth</a> &middot; spec 3.1.1.1-3.1.1.3 &middot; Genn pp. 6-9 &middot; LIFE-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Explain why <b>proxy data</b> are needed to study the early conditions on Earth.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Complete Table 1 to show what each analytical technique can be used to estimate.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Technique</th><th>What it estimates</th></tr><tr><td>Ratio of carbon-12 to carbon-14 in a sample</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Ratio of oxygen-18 to oxygen-16</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Analysis of air bubbles trapped in ice cores</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Ratio of magnesium to calcium in calcite deposits</td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain why an understanding of continental drift and ocean currents has been important in explaining past changes in conditions.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Life probably first developed around volcanic vents on the seabed. Explain why the conditions in the oceans were more suitable than those on land 3.5 billion years ago.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Direct measurements of past conditions cannot be taken, so indirect evidence that is related to the condition must be used</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Age / date of the sample (radioisotope dating)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Past temperatures</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Composition of the past atmosphere (eg CO<sub>2</sub> / methane concentrations)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Temperature: more magnesium is incorporated into calcite at higher temperatures</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Movement of continents changed the position of land masses / latitude and therefore the climate and habitats present</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ocean currents redistribute heat and nutrients; changes in currents (eg as continents moved) altered climates, so past conditions cannot be explained without knowing how the systems worked</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>There was no oxygen / ozone layer so high levels of UV radiation reached the land surface; water absorbed the UV and protected organisms in the oceans</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Warm temperatures around the vents and a rich mix of chemicals / increasingly complex organic molecules in the sea made biological reactions more likely</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The atmosphere contained toxic gases such as ammonia; the early Archaea were anaerobic so did not need oxygen</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Water acted as the solvent for reactions / transport</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 6-9; AQA spec 3.1.1.1-3.1.1.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SUS-03", "sub": "natural-systems", "subname": "Energy & material cycles: natural vs human systems", "topic": "Sustainability", "tslug": "sustainability", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.6.2", "pages": "373", "url": "subtopic/natural-systems.html#SUS-03", "snippet": "Table 1 compares nitrogen fixation by Rhizobium bacteria in legume root nodules with the Haber process.", "text": "table 1 compares nitrogen fixation by rhizobium bacteria in legume root nodules with the haber process. use table 1 to explain how natural processes differ from human industrial processes in their use of energy. give three other natural processes driven by solar energy that human society depends on. explain how the principle of low-temperature natural processes is being applied in human systems. natural processes are driven by renewable, low energy-density solar energy captured by photosynthesis; human processes usually use high energy-density non-renewable fossil fuels natural processes occur at low temperatures because enzymes lower the activation energy of reactions; human processes need high temperatures and pressures natural processes produce no toxic wastes / their products are raw materials for other processes; industrial processes emit co2 and nox so natural fixation is sustainable indefinitely with a low carbon footprint hydrological cycle (evaporation, precipitation) carbon cycle / photosynthesis atmospheric circulation / winds thermohaline circulation (density differences from heating / evaporation) decomposition bioleaching and phytomining extract metals at low temperature instead of high-temperature smelting anaerobic digestion and composting treat wastes; enzymes in industry (biological washing powders, biofuels from algae / microorganisms) heat pumps and solar thermal capture low-density heat; low-temperature manufacturing using biological polymers; fuel cells instead of combustion", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SUS-03\" data-qid=\"SUS-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/natural-systems.html\">Energy &amp; material cycles: natural vs human systems</a> &middot; spec 3.6.2 &middot; Genn pp. 373 &middot; SUS-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 compares nitrogen fixation by Rhizobium bacteria in legume root nodules with the Haber process.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Biological fixation</th><th>Haber process</th></tr><tr><td>Energy source</td><td>Sunlight captured by the plant (carbohydrates)</td><td>Fossil fuels (natural gas / coal)</td></tr><tr><td>Temperature</td><td>Soil temperature (10-30 &deg;C)</td><td>About 450 &deg;C</td></tr><tr><td>Pressure</td><td>Atmospheric</td><td>200 atmospheres</td></tr><tr><td>Catalyst</td><td>Nitrogenase enzyme</td><td>Iron</td></tr><tr><td>Energy density of input</td><td>Low</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Waste products</td><td>None - all products used</td><td>CO<sub>2</sub>, NOx</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Use Table 1 to explain how natural processes differ from human industrial processes in their use of energy.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Give <b>three</b> other natural processes driven by solar energy that human society depends on.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain how the principle of low-temperature natural processes is being applied in human systems.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Natural processes are driven by renewable, low energy-density solar energy captured by photosynthesis; human processes usually use high energy-density non-renewable fossil fuels</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Natural processes occur at low temperatures because enzymes lower the activation energy of reactions; human processes need high temperatures and pressures</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Natural processes produce no toxic wastes / their products are raw materials for other processes; industrial processes emit CO2 and NOx</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So natural fixation is sustainable indefinitely with a low carbon footprint</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hydrological cycle (evaporation, precipitation)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Carbon cycle / photosynthesis</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Atmospheric circulation / winds</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Thermohaline circulation (density differences from heating / evaporation)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Decomposition</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bioleaching and phytomining extract metals at low temperature instead of high-temperature smelting</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Anaerobic digestion and composting treat wastes; enzymes in industry (biological washing powders, biofuels from algae / microorganisms)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Heat pumps and solar thermal capture low-density heat; low-temperature manufacturing using biological polymers; fuel cells instead of combustion</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 373; AQA spec 3.6.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SUS-04", "sub": "natural-systems", "subname": "Energy & material cycles: natural vs human systems", "topic": "Sustainability", "tslug": "sustainability", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.6.3", "pages": "373-374", "url": "subtopic/natural-systems.html#SUS-04", "snippet": "Explain how natural material cycles differ from linear human systems of material use.", "text": " explain how natural material cycles differ from linear human systems of material use. explain why the use of toxic metals in electronic products is a particular problem for sustainability. describe how the design of a product can move it from a linear to a cyclical system. natural cycles use a small number of abundant elements (c, h, o, n, p) built into monomers and polymers (carbohydrates, proteins); wastes of one process are raw materials for another so nothing is depleted or accumulates natural wastes are biodegradable / non-toxic and are broken down by decomposers into raw materials human systems are linear: extract - process - manufacture - use - discard; minerals are dispersed after use or mixed so recovery is difficult human wastes are often persistent and toxic (organochlorines, pcbs, heavy metals in electronics) and build up; fossil fuel use is inherently linear and unsustainable metals are dispersed in small amounts within complex mixed products so they cannot easily be separated / recovered at end of life they leach from landfill or are released by incineration; persistent, bioaccumulating design for durability, repair and re-use (replaceable parts, no built-in batteries, spare parts and support, no planned obsolescence) easy identification of materials (coding) and separation of components so they can be recycled separation of technical materials (metals, plastics) from biological materials (which can be composted / digested) use of recycled and recyclable / biodegradable materials; reduced packaging and material use (cradle to cradle)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SUS-04\" data-qid=\"SUS-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/natural-systems.html\">Energy &amp; material cycles: natural vs human systems</a> &middot; spec 3.6.3 &middot; Genn pp. 373-374 &middot; SUS-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain how natural material cycles differ from linear human systems of material use.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain why the use of toxic metals in electronic products is a particular problem for sustainability.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Describe how the design of a product can move it from a linear to a cyclical system.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Natural cycles use a small number of abundant elements (C, H, O, N, P) built into monomers and polymers (carbohydrates, proteins); wastes of one process are raw materials for another so nothing is depleted or accumulates</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Natural wastes are biodegradable / non-toxic and are broken down by decomposers into raw materials</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Human systems are linear: extract - process - manufacture - use - discard; minerals are dispersed after use or mixed so recovery is difficult</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Human wastes are often persistent and toxic (organochlorines, PCBs, heavy metals in electronics) and build up; fossil fuel use is inherently linear and unsustainable</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Metals are dispersed in small amounts within complex mixed products so they cannot easily be separated / recovered at end of life</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They leach from landfill or are released by incineration; persistent, bioaccumulating</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Design for durability, repair and re-use (replaceable parts, no built-in batteries, spare parts and support, no planned obsolescence)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Easy identification of materials (coding) and separation of components so they can be recycled</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Separation of technical materials (metals, plastics) from biological materials (which can be composted / digested)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Use of recycled and recyclable / biodegradable materials; reduced packaging and material use (Cradle to Cradle)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 373-374; AQA spec 3.6.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SUS-13", "sub": "natural-systems", "subname": "Energy & material cycles: natural vs human systems", "topic": "Sustainability", "tslug": "sustainability", "papers": [2], "marks": 5, "flags": [], "spec": "3.6.2", "pages": "373", "url": "subtopic/natural-systems.html#SUS-13", "snippet": "Give two natural processes that are driven by solar energy.", "text": " give two natural processes that are driven by solar energy. explain why natural processes can take place at low temperatures. give one human activity that uses high temperatures to achieve a result that natural systems achieve at low temperature. hydrological cycle carbon cycle nitrogen cycle atmospheric circulation thermohaline circulation photosynthesis enzymes reduce the activation energy of reactions so reactions such as photosynthesis, decomposition and nitrogen fixation do not need high temperatures haber process (nitrogen fixation) incineration of wastes (decomposition) many manufacturing processes", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SUS-13\" data-qid=\"SUS-13\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/natural-systems.html\">Energy &amp; material cycles: natural vs human systems</a> &middot; spec 3.6.2 &middot; Genn pp. 373 &middot; SUS-13</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Give <b>two</b> natural processes that are driven by solar energy.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain why natural processes can take place at low temperatures.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Give <b>one</b> human activity that uses high temperatures to achieve a result that natural systems achieve at low temperature.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hydrological cycle</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Carbon cycle</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Nitrogen cycle</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Atmospheric circulation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Thermohaline circulation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Photosynthesis</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Enzymes reduce the activation energy of reactions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So reactions such as photosynthesis, decomposition and nitrogen fixation do not need high temperatures</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 1 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 1.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Haber process (nitrogen fixation)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Incineration of wastes (decomposition)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Many manufacturing processes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 373; AQA spec 3.6.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SUS-14", "sub": "natural-systems", "subname": "Energy & material cycles: natural vs human systems", "topic": "Sustainability", "tslug": "sustainability", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.6.2-3.6.3", "pages": "373-374", "url": "subtopic/natural-systems.html#SUS-14", "snippet": "Complete Table 1 to compare natural systems with typical linear human systems.", "text": " complete table 1 to compare natural systems with typical linear human systems. explain why natural systems can be sustained using a relatively small number of elements. explain why the human use of minerals makes them difficult to recover and re-use. explain why toxic metals in electronic products cause problems that the wastes of natural systems do not. energy: low energy-density renewable (solar) / non-renewable fossil fuels materials: cyclic - wastes of one process are raw materials of the next / linear - used once then discarded, depletion wastes: biodegradable, non-toxic or do not build up / toxic, persistent (eg pcbs, organochlorines, toxic metals) that bioaccumulate temperature: low / high (haber process, incineration) abundant simple raw materials build into monomers and then a wide range of polymers (carbohydrates, proteins) they are re-used in cycles so there is no depletion of raw materials or build-up of wastes use often disperses the minerals (eg in products spread over the world / corrosion) or produces mixtures of materials that are difficult to separate, so sustainable exploitation is reduced they cannot easily be removed from end-of-life appliances and are not biodegradable natural wastes are non-toxic / broken down to raw materials for other processes, whereas persistent toxins may bioaccumulate and biomagnify", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SUS-14\" data-qid=\"SUS-14\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/natural-systems.html\">Energy &amp; material cycles: natural vs human systems</a> &middot; spec 3.6.2-3.6.3 &middot; Genn pp. 373-374 &middot; SUS-14</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Complete Table 1 to compare natural systems with typical linear human systems.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Natural systems</th><th>Linear human systems</th></tr><tr><td>Energy source</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Use of materials</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Nature of wastes</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Temperature of processes</td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain why natural systems can be sustained using a relatively small number of elements.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain why the human use of minerals makes them difficult to recover and re-use.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Explain why toxic metals in electronic products cause problems that the wastes of natural systems do not.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Energy: low energy-density renewable (solar) / non-renewable fossil fuels</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Materials: cyclic - wastes of one process are raw materials of the next / linear - used once then discarded, depletion</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wastes: biodegradable, non-toxic or do not build up / toxic, persistent (eg PCBs, organochlorines, toxic metals) that bioaccumulate</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Temperature: low / high (Haber process, incineration)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Abundant simple raw materials build into monomers and then a wide range of polymers (carbohydrates, proteins)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They are re-used in cycles so there is no depletion of raw materials or build-up of wastes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Use often disperses the minerals (eg in products spread over the world / corrosion)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Or produces mixtures of materials that are difficult to separate, so sustainable exploitation is reduced</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They cannot easily be removed from end-of-life appliances and are not biodegradable</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Natural wastes are non-toxic / broken down to raw materials for other processes, whereas persistent toxins may bioaccumulate and biomagnify</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 373-374; AQA spec 3.6.2-3.6.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SUS-15", "sub": "natural-systems", "subname": "Energy & material cycles: natural vs human systems", "topic": "Sustainability", "tslug": "sustainability", "papers": [2], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.6.2-3.6.3", "pages": "373", "url": "subtopic/natural-systems.html#SUS-15", "snippet": "Which of the following is a feature of natural systems? Tick one box.", "text": " which of the following is a feature of natural systems? tick one box. explain why nitrogen fixation by bacteria is more sustainable than the haber process. explain why incineration of wastes is less sustainable than natural decomposition. waste products that are the raw materials for other processes bacteria fix nitrogen at low temperature using enzymes and energy ultimately from sunlight (renewable) the haber process needs high temperature and pressure powered by non-renewable fossil fuels, releasing co2 incineration uses high temperatures / fuel and releases pollutants; nutrients are not returned as usable raw materials decomposition at low temperature recycles nutrients as the raw materials for other natural processes (material cycle)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SUS-15\" data-qid=\"SUS-15\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/natural-systems.html\">Energy &amp; material cycles: natural vs human systems</a> &middot; spec 3.6.2-3.6.3 &middot; Genn pp. 373 &middot; SUS-15</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Which of the following is a feature of natural systems? Tick <b>one</b> box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; Reactions that require high temperatures</li><li>B&nbsp; Reliance on non-renewable energy</li><li>C&nbsp; Waste products that are the raw materials for other processes</li><li>D&nbsp; Dispersal of elements so they cannot be re-used</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain why nitrogen fixation by bacteria is more sustainable than the Haber process.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain why incineration of wastes is less sustainable than natural decomposition.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b>C</b>&nbsp; Waste products that are the raw materials for other processes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bacteria fix nitrogen at low temperature using enzymes and energy ultimately from sunlight (renewable)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The Haber process needs high temperature and pressure powered by non-renewable fossil fuels, releasing CO<sub>2</sub></span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Incineration uses high temperatures / fuel and releases pollutants; nutrients are not returned as usable raw materials</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Decomposition at low temperature recycles nutrients as the raw materials for other natural processes (material cycle)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 373; AQA spec 3.6.2-3.6.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HAB-01", "sub": "habitats", "subname": "Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.4", "pages": "50-52", "url": "subtopic/habitats.html#HAB-01", "snippet": "Describe the ecological features of temperate broadleaf woodland and explain how woodland floor plants such as bluebells are adapted to them.", "text": " describe the ecological features of temperate broadleaf woodland and explain how woodland floor plants such as bluebells are adapted to them. explain why an ancient woodland (existing before 1600) usually has higher biodiversity than a recently planted woodland. describe three threats to uk broadleaf woodland. no major temperature extremes; regular water supply with no pronounced dry season; deep fertile soils built by leaf litter and root binding; strong seasonality deciduous canopy present spring to autumn shades the floor during the best growing temperatures bluebells, snowdrops, lesser celandine grow and flower early in spring before the trees leaf winter food shortage causes animals to hibernate (dormice), store food or migrate long time for species to colonise; some woodland plants colonise very slowly so their presence (ancient woodland indicators) shows the wood is old natural woodland has trees of all ages with clearings where old trees died, giving varied light and dead wood habitats a new plantation has a single-age dense canopy with low light and few niches until trees start to die (over 200 years) clearance for farmland, plantations, urban development, roads, mineral extraction fragmentation isolating remaining woods so populations cannot move between them and are vulnerable management change: end of coppicing so woods become uniform and dark; replacement by conifer monocultures overgrazing by deer preventing regeneration; introduced pathogens (ash dieback)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HAB-01\" data-qid=\"HAB-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/habitats.html\">Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 50-52 &middot; HAB-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Describe the ecological features of temperate broadleaf woodland and explain how woodland floor plants such as bluebells are adapted to them.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain why an ancient woodland (existing before 1600) usually has higher biodiversity than a recently planted woodland.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Describe <b>three</b> threats to UK broadleaf woodland.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No major temperature extremes; regular water supply with no pronounced dry season; deep fertile soils built by leaf litter and root binding; strong seasonality</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Deciduous canopy present spring to autumn shades the floor during the best growing temperatures</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bluebells, snowdrops, lesser celandine grow and flower early in spring before the trees leaf</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Winter food shortage causes animals to hibernate (dormice), store food or migrate</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Long time for species to colonise; some woodland plants colonise very slowly so their presence (ancient woodland indicators) shows the wood is old</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Natural woodland has trees of all ages with clearings where old trees died, giving varied light and dead wood habitats</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A new plantation has a single-age dense canopy with low light and few niches until trees start to die (over 200 years)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Clearance for farmland, plantations, urban development, roads, mineral extraction</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fragmentation isolating remaining woods so populations cannot move between them and are vulnerable</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Management change: end of coppicing so woods become uniform and dark; replacement by conifer monocultures</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Overgrazing by deer preventing regeneration; introduced pathogens (ash dieback)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 50-52; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HAB-02", "sub": "habitats", "subname": "Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.1.2.3.4", "pages": "50-52", "url": "subtopic/habitats.html#HAB-02", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the number of bird species and butterfly species recorded in three woodlands in the same region.", "text": "table 1 shows the number of bird species and butterfly species recorded in three woodlands in the same region. use table 1 and your knowledge to explain the differences in biodiversity between the three woodlands. describe three conservation efforts used to protect uk woodlands. explain why a high biodiversity increases the ecological stability of a woodland. a: coppice rotation creates a patchwork of different ages / light levels - clearings with wildflowers for butterflies, dense regrowth for nesting birds; oak supports many invertebrates b: same tree species but the closed canopy shades the floor so few flowering plants, fewer invertebrates and butterflies; fewer niches c: non-indigenous single-species, single-age conifers support few native invertebrates; dense shade; no clearings or dead wood; needles acidify soil habitat diversity / structural variety increases the range of niches and hence species designated protected areas: sssis, nnrs (sherwood forest), sacs (epping forest), national parks legal protection of ancient woodland: planning permission refused for developments destroying irreplaceable habitats (2012 nppf) conservation management: coppicing, creating clearings, planting mixed native species, leaving dead wood, controlling deer planting new native woodlands linking fragments (corridors); woodland trust / national trust ownership no species is completely dominant and food webs have many alternative links so a change in the population of one species has a relatively small impact on the whole community", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HAB-02\" data-qid=\"HAB-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/habitats.html\">Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 50-52 &middot; HAB-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the number of bird species and butterfly species recorded in three woodlands in the same region.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Woodland</th><th>Management</th><th>Bird species</th><th>Butterfly species</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>Ancient oak wood, coppiced on rotation with clearings</td><td>42</td><td>23</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>Ancient oak wood, unmanaged for 60 years, dense canopy</td><td>28</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>40-year-old conifer plantation</td><td>14</td><td>3</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Use Table 1 and your knowledge to explain the differences in biodiversity between the three woodlands.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Describe <b>three</b> conservation efforts used to protect UK woodlands.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain why a high biodiversity increases the ecological stability of a woodland.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 5 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>A: coppice rotation creates a patchwork of different ages / light levels - clearings with wildflowers for butterflies, dense regrowth for nesting birds; oak supports many invertebrates</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>B: same tree species but the closed canopy shades the floor so few flowering plants, fewer invertebrates and butterflies; fewer niches</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>C: non-indigenous single-species, single-age conifers support few native invertebrates; dense shade; no clearings or dead wood; needles acidify soil</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Habitat diversity / structural variety increases the range of niches and hence species</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Designated protected areas: SSSIs, NNRs (Sherwood Forest), SACs (Epping Forest), National Parks</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Legal protection of ancient woodland: planning permission refused for developments destroying irreplaceable habitats (2012 NPPF)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Conservation management: coppicing, creating clearings, planting mixed native species, leaving dead wood, controlling deer</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Planting new native woodlands linking fragments (corridors); Woodland Trust / National Trust ownership</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No species is completely dominant and food webs have many alternative links</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So a change in the population of one species has a relatively small impact on the whole community</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 50-52; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HAB-03", "sub": "habitats", "subname": "Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.4", "pages": "52-55", "url": "subtopic/habitats.html#HAB-03", "snippet": "Explain why tropical rainforests have such high biodiversity.", "text": " explain why tropical rainforests have such high biodiversity. explain why rainforest species may be particularly vulnerable to environmental change. explain why rainforest plants and animals such as frogs are a rich source of medicines. explain the importance of tropical rainforest for the hydrological cycle and for soils. stable tropical climate over long periods gave time for species to evolve and adapt to local abiotic and biotic factors; many rare species with small ranges high light, warm temperatures and high rainfall all year allow continuous photosynthesis and growth, supporting rich food webs with no seasonal food shortage (no hibernation / migration) stratification (emergent, canopy, understorey, shrub, ground layers) and epiphytes create many niches; intense competition for light drives specialisation biotic pressures (predation, inter-species relationships) drive evolution of specialised pollination / seed dispersal relationships; plants flower and fruit at different times adapted to stable conditions so they have narrow ranges of tolerance; a small change can move conditions outside the range many species are rare with small ranges / small populations no cold winter kills herbivores so plants defend themselves with toxic chemicals (alkaloids) that are medicinally active in controlled doses (quinine, diosgenin, curare) animals without teeth / claws (frogs, reptiles) rely on chemical defences or venoms (fer-de-lance venom - captopril) over half the amazon's rainfall comes from transpiration within the forest; clearance reduces downwind rainfall rapid decay recycles nutrients so soils are shallow / poor; canopy and roots prevent erosion of the thin soil - clearance leads to rapid erosion and infertility", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HAB-03\" data-qid=\"HAB-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/habitats.html\">Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 52-55 &middot; HAB-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Explain why tropical rainforests have such high biodiversity.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain why rainforest species may be particularly vulnerable to environmental change.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain why rainforest plants and animals such as frogs are a rich source of medicines.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Explain the importance of tropical rainforest for the hydrological cycle and for soils.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stable tropical climate over long periods gave time for species to evolve and adapt to local abiotic and biotic factors; many rare species with small ranges</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>High light, warm temperatures and high rainfall all year allow continuous photosynthesis and growth, supporting rich food webs with no seasonal food shortage (no hibernation / migration)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stratification (emergent, canopy, understorey, shrub, ground layers) and epiphytes create many niches; intense competition for light drives specialisation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Biotic pressures (predation, inter-species relationships) drive evolution of specialised pollination / seed dispersal relationships; plants flower and fruit at different times</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Adapted to stable conditions so they have narrow ranges of tolerance; a small change can move conditions outside the range</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Many species are rare with small ranges / small populations</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No cold winter kills herbivores so plants defend themselves with toxic chemicals (alkaloids) that are medicinally active in controlled doses (quinine, diosgenin, curare)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Animals without teeth / claws (frogs, reptiles) rely on chemical defences or venoms (fer-de-lance venom - captopril)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Over half the Amazon's rainfall comes from transpiration within the forest; clearance reduces downwind rainfall</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Rapid decay recycles nutrients so soils are shallow / poor; canopy and roots prevent erosion of the thin soil - clearance leads to rapid erosion and infertility</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 52-55; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HAB-04", "sub": "habitats", "subname": "Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.1.2.3.4", "pages": "55-57", "url": "subtopic/habitats.html#HAB-04", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the main causes of tropical rainforest loss in a South American country between 2000 and 2020.", "text": "figure 1 shows the main causes of tropical rainforest loss in a south american country between 2000 and 2020. explain why traditional 'slash and burn' subsistence agriculture was sustainable but has become a threat. explain why clearance for cattle ranching or cash crops is usually unsustainable. describe how each of the following threatens rainforest. describe how a debt-for-nature swap works. small clearings were farmed for a few years then abandoned as nutrients declined; the forest recolonised and soil fertility recovered before the area was cleared again population growth shortens the gap between clearances so the forest and soil do not recover clearings enlarge and degrade; less regrowth rainforest soils are thin and nutrient-poor (nutrients held in the biomass); once cleared, fertility falls in a few years and yields drop, so more land is cleared abandoned land may not be recolonised if no forest survives nearby; erosion of exposed soil mining (aluminium, iron, gold, copper) destroys habitat directly and pollutes rivers with turbid drainage, cyanide and mercury from processing reservoirs (eg tucurui, 2850 km2) flood large areas, block migratory fish and river dolphins; decomposing vegetation produces acidic, deoxygenated water; fluctuating downstream flows droughts increase frequency, area and duration of fires (long fires boil sap and kill trees); cloud forests lose humidity as clouds form higher so frogs and epiphytes decline; some models predict replacement by savannah a creditor country or conservation charity cancels / buys part of a debtor country's foreign debt in return the country guarantees to protect an area of rainforest (eg usa - guatemala, brazil; wwf - costa rica, philippines)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HAB-04\" data-qid=\"HAB-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/habitats.html\">Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 55-57 &middot; HAB-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the main causes of tropical rainforest loss in a South American country between 2000 and 2020.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<rect x=\"70.3\" y=\"42.7\" width=\"48.0\" height=\"227.3\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"94.3\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Cattle ranching</text>\n<rect x=\"138.9\" y=\"158.9\" width=\"48.0\" height=\"111.1\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"162.9\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Soya / cash crops</text>\n<rect x=\"207.4\" y=\"209.4\" width=\"48.0\" height=\"60.6\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"231.4\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Subsistence farming</text>\n<rect x=\"276.0\" y=\"219.5\" width=\"48.0\" height=\"50.5\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Timber</text>\n<rect x=\"344.6\" y=\"244.7\" width=\"48.0\" height=\"25.3\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"368.6\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mining</text>\n<rect x=\"413.1\" y=\"249.8\" width=\"48.0\" height=\"20.2\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"437.1\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Reservoirs / roads</text>\n<rect x=\"481.7\" y=\"259.9\" width=\"48.0\" height=\"10.1\" fill=\"#3f6b4a\"/>\n<text x=\"505.7\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Fires</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">9.9</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">19.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">29.7</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">39.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">49.5</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Cause</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Percentage of forest loss / %</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Explain why traditional 'slash and burn' subsistence agriculture was sustainable but has become a threat.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain why clearance for cattle ranching or cash crops is usually unsustainable.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Describe how each of the following threatens rainforest.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Describe how a debt-for-nature swap works.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Small clearings were farmed for a few years then abandoned as nutrients declined; the forest recolonised and soil fertility recovered before the area was cleared again</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Population growth shortens the gap between clearances so the forest and soil do not recover</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Clearings enlarge and degrade; less regrowth</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Rainforest soils are thin and nutrient-poor (nutrients held in the biomass); once cleared, fertility falls in a few years and yields drop, so more land is cleared</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Abandoned land may not be recolonised if no forest survives nearby; erosion of exposed soil</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mining (aluminium, iron, gold, copper) destroys habitat directly and pollutes rivers with turbid drainage, cyanide and mercury from processing</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reservoirs (eg Tucurui, 2850 km<sup>2</sup>) flood large areas, block migratory fish and river dolphins; decomposing vegetation produces acidic, deoxygenated water; fluctuating downstream flows</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Droughts increase frequency, area and duration of fires (long fires boil sap and kill trees); cloud forests lose humidity as clouds form higher so frogs and epiphytes decline; some models predict replacement by savannah</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A creditor country or conservation charity cancels / buys part of a debtor country's foreign debt</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In return the country guarantees to protect an area of rainforest (eg USA - Guatemala, Brazil; WWF - Costa Rica, Philippines)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 55-57; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HAB-05", "sub": "habitats", "subname": "Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.1.2.3.4", "pages": "56-57", "url": "subtopic/habitats.html#HAB-05", "snippet": "Describe the conservation efforts used to protect tropical rainforests and evaluate their effectiveness.", "text": " describe the conservation efforts used to protect tropical rainforests and evaluate their effectiveness. state one reason why rainforest conservation is important to countries far from the tropics. protected areas: korup np cameroon (1260 km2, 600 tree species, endemic ancistrocladus korupensis with anti-hiv / antimalarial alkaloids); tumucumaque np brazil (39 000 km2, adjoining french guiana reserve); alto maues; rio bravo belize funded by charities, companies and ecotourism debt for nature swaps; sustainable exploitation: selective logging of high-value timber (mahogany) funding conservation; itto aims (largely unmet); cites listing of timber species (honduras mahogany appendix ii) ecotourism income (but visitor damage in costa rica, malaysia); community projects and employment; carbon sequestration payments effectiveness: protected areas work where enforced but face illegal logging, mining, poaching, roads, conflict; drivers (population growth, demand for beef, soya, palm oil, timber, minerals) originate outside the forest; climate change cannot be controlled locally judgement: combination of large protected areas, economic alternatives for local people and reduced international demand needed; overall loss continues carbon sequestration reducing climate change; genetic resources / medicines; rainfall regulation; global biodiversity", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HAB-05\" data-qid=\"HAB-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/habitats.html\">Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 56-57 &middot; HAB-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Describe the conservation efforts used to protect tropical rainforests and evaluate their effectiveness.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>State <b>one</b> reason why rainforest conservation is important to countries far from the tropics.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Protected areas: Korup NP Cameroon (1260 km<sup>2</sup>, 600 tree species, endemic Ancistrocladus korupensis with anti-HIV / antimalarial alkaloids); Tumucumaque NP Brazil (39 000 km<sup>2</sup>, adjoining French Guiana reserve); Alto Maues; Rio Bravo Belize funded by charities, companies and ecotourism</li><li>Debt for nature swaps; sustainable exploitation: selective logging of high-value timber (mahogany) funding conservation; ITTO aims (largely unmet); CITES listing of timber species (Honduras mahogany Appendix II)</li><li>Ecotourism income (but visitor damage in Costa Rica, Malaysia); community projects and employment; carbon sequestration payments</li><li>Effectiveness: protected areas work where enforced but face illegal logging, mining, poaching, roads, conflict; drivers (population growth, demand for beef, soya, palm oil, timber, minerals) originate outside the forest; climate change cannot be controlled locally</li><li>Judgement: combination of large protected areas, economic alternatives for local people and reduced international demand needed; overall loss continues</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Carbon sequestration reducing climate change; genetic resources / medicines; rainfall regulation; global biodiversity</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 56-57; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HAB-06", "sub": "habitats", "subname": "Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.4", "pages": "57-60", "url": "subtopic/habitats.html#HAB-06", "snippet": "Explain how the relationship between coral polyps and zooxanthellae allows reefs to support a high biomass in nutrient-poor tropical seas.", "text": " explain how the relationship between coral polyps and zooxanthellae allows reefs to support a high biomass in nutrient-poor tropical seas. describe the abiotic conditions needed by reef-building corals and explain why these make reefs vulnerable. explain three ways in which coral reefs are important to humans. zooxanthellae are photosynthetic algae living inside polyp cells (up to 30 000 per mm2) they use the polyp's co2 and excretory wastes (nitrogen, phosphorus) for photosynthesis and growth, so nutrients are recycled efficiently they supply the polyp with glucose and oxygen - over 95% of its food energy; the polyp provides a protected habitat and nutrients polyps also trap plankton with cilia and stinging nematocysts; surplus co2 is used to build the calcium carbonate skeleton high light (shallow, clear water) for the algae; warm stable temperatures (about 18-30 °c); low turbidity; constant salinity; well-oxygenated water found only where all these coincide (about 1% of ocean area) small changes (warming, sediment, freshwater runoff) move conditions outside the narrow range of tolerance fisheries: support over a quarter of marine species including fish, lobsters, conch erosion protection: complex structure dissipates wave energy protecting low coral islands; coral sand builds shores medicinal discoveries: azt and ara-c from sponges, dolastatin from sea hares, bryostatin from bugula, trabectedin from sea squirts, secosteroids, coral for bone grafts climate control: carbonate skeletons sequester co2 tourism (diving, snorkelling)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HAB-06\" data-qid=\"HAB-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/habitats.html\">Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 57-60 &middot; HAB-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Explain how the relationship between coral polyps and zooxanthellae allows reefs to support a high biomass in nutrient-poor tropical seas.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Describe the abiotic conditions needed by reef-building corals and explain why these make reefs vulnerable.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain <b>three</b> ways in which coral reefs are important to humans.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Zooxanthellae are photosynthetic algae living inside polyp cells (up to 30 000 per mm<sup>2</sup>)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They use the polyp's CO2 and excretory wastes (nitrogen, phosphorus) for photosynthesis and growth, so nutrients are recycled efficiently</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They supply the polyp with glucose and oxygen - over 95% of its food energy; the polyp provides a protected habitat and nutrients</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Polyps also trap plankton with cilia and stinging nematocysts; surplus CO2 is used to build the calcium carbonate skeleton</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>High light (shallow, clear water) for the algae; warm stable temperatures (about 18-30 &deg;C); low turbidity; constant salinity; well-oxygenated water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Found only where all these coincide (about 1% of ocean area)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Small changes (warming, sediment, freshwater runoff) move conditions outside the narrow range of tolerance</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fisheries: support over a quarter of marine species including fish, lobsters, conch</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Erosion protection: complex structure dissipates wave energy protecting low coral islands; coral sand builds shores</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Medicinal discoveries: AZT and Ara-C from sponges, dolastatin from sea hares, bryostatin from Bugula, trabectedin from sea squirts, secosteroids, coral for bone grafts</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Climate control: carbonate skeletons sequester CO2</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Tourism (diving, snorkelling)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 57-60; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HAB-07", "sub": "habitats", "subname": "Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.1.2.3.4", "pages": "60-63", "url": "subtopic/habitats.html#HAB-07", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the percentage of live coral cover on a reef near a growing coastal resort between 1990 and 2020, and the years of two major bleaching events.", "text": "figure 1 shows the percentage of live coral cover on a reef near a growing coastal resort between 1990 and 2020, and the years of two major bleaching events. describe the changes in coral cover shown in figure 1. explain what coral bleaching is and give three causes. explain how the growing resort could threaten the reef other than through bleaching. explain why a no-take zone on the reef could increase fish catches in surrounding areas. overall decline from 62% to 22% sharp falls in 1998 (to 35%) and 2016 (to 18%) coinciding with bleaching events, with partial recovery after each the polyps expel their symbiotic zooxanthellae so the coral loses colour; bleached corals are alive but grow very slowly and may die if algae do not return raised water temperature (climate change / el niño) pollution: sewage, pesticides, cyanide, sunscreen; low oxygen from zooplankton blooms after overfishing high light / solar activity; ocean acidification hinders skeleton formation coastal construction and dredging increase sedimentation / turbidity covering and killing polyps sewage and fertiliser nutrients cause algal growth that shades corals and prevents egg release physical damage from anchors, divers, souvenir collection; loss of mangroves / seagrass nurseries that trap sediment protected breeding populations grow larger and produce surplus young / adults that spill over into fished areas", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HAB-07\" data-qid=\"HAB-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/habitats.html\">Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 60-63 &middot; HAB-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the percentage of live coral cover on a reef near a growing coastal resort between 1990 and 2020, and the years of two major bleaching events.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,48.6 140.0,62.9 188.0,145.0 252.0,120.0 316.0,109.3 380.0,127.1 476.0,205.7 540.0,191.4\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"48.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"140.0\" cy=\"62.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"188.0\" cy=\"145.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"252.0\" cy=\"120.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"316.0\" cy=\"109.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"380.0\" cy=\"127.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"476.0\" cy=\"205.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"191.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1990</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1996</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">14</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2002</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">28</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2008</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">42</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2014</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">56</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2020</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">70</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Year</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Live coral cover / %</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Describe the changes in coral cover shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Explain what coral bleaching is and give <b>three</b> causes.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Explain how the growing resort could threaten the reef other than through bleaching.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>Explain why a no-take zone on the reef could increase fish catches in surrounding areas.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Overall decline from 62% to 22%</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sharp falls in 1998 (to 35%) and 2016 (to 18%) coinciding with bleaching events, with partial recovery after each</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The polyps expel their symbiotic zooxanthellae so the coral loses colour; bleached corals are alive but grow very slowly and may die if algae do not return</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Raised water temperature (climate change / El Ni&ntilde;o)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pollution: sewage, pesticides, cyanide, sunscreen; low oxygen from zooplankton blooms after overfishing</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>High light / solar activity; ocean acidification hinders skeleton formation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Coastal construction and dredging increase sedimentation / turbidity covering and killing polyps</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sewage and fertiliser nutrients cause algal growth that shades corals and prevents egg release</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Physical damage from anchors, divers, souvenir collection; loss of mangroves / seagrass nurseries that trap sediment</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Protected breeding populations grow larger and produce surplus young / adults that spill over into fished areas</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 60-63; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HAB-08", "sub": "habitats", "subname": "Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.4", "pages": "61-64", "url": "subtopic/habitats.html#HAB-08", "snippet": "Explain how overfishing of the giant triton mollusc damaged coral reefs in Australia.", "text": " explain how overfishing of the giant triton mollusc damaged coral reefs in australia. describe four management practices used in marine protected areas to protect coral reefs. explain why deep-water coral reefs recover much more slowly from trawling damage than tropical reefs. give two threats to deep-water reefs and one conservation measure. giant tritons are predators of the crown-of-thorns starfish which eats coral polyps removing tritons allowed starfish populations to explode, destroying large areas of reef (food web / trophic cascade) fixed mooring buoys so boats do not anchor on coral education of divers / snorkellers not to touch coral or kick up sediment; bans on spear fishing and souvenir collection fishing controls: no-take zones (chagos, great barrier reef, lundy, lamlash bay), seasons, minimum and maximum catch sizes, bans on dynamite / small-mesh nets / trawling protection of turtle nesting beaches; control of coastal development; protection of mangroves and seagrass; artificial reefs (reef balls, sunk ships / oil rigs) no sunlight so no symbiotic algae; polyps rely only on captured food so have far less energy and grow very slowly damage from trawls takes hundreds of years to repair; the slow-growing, low-fecundity fish (orange roughy) they support are easily over-exploited deep-water trawling (also by-catch); oil and gas exploration in deeper water; ocean acidification protected areas banning trawling, eg darwin mounds sac off nw scotland, oculina bank off florida", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HAB-08\" data-qid=\"HAB-08\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 8</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/habitats.html\">Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 61-64 &middot; HAB-08</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.1</span>Explain how overfishing of the giant triton mollusc damaged coral reefs in Australia.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.2</span>Describe <b>four</b> management practices used in marine protected areas to protect coral reefs.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.3</span>Explain why deep-water coral reefs recover much more slowly from trawling damage than tropical reefs.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">08.4</span>Give <b>two</b> threats to deep-water reefs and <b>one</b> conservation measure.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Giant tritons are predators of the crown-of-thorns starfish which eats coral polyps</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Removing tritons allowed starfish populations to explode, destroying large areas of reef (food web / trophic cascade)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fixed mooring buoys so boats do not anchor on coral</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Education of divers / snorkellers not to touch coral or kick up sediment; bans on spear fishing and souvenir collection</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fishing controls: no-take zones (Chagos, Great Barrier Reef, Lundy, Lamlash Bay), seasons, minimum and maximum catch sizes, bans on dynamite / small-mesh nets / trawling</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Protection of turtle nesting beaches; control of coastal development; protection of mangroves and seagrass; artificial reefs (reef balls, sunk ships / oil rigs)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No sunlight so no symbiotic algae; polyps rely only on captured food so have far less energy and grow very slowly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Damage from trawls takes hundreds of years to repair; the slow-growing, low-fecundity fish (orange roughy) they support are easily over-exploited</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>08.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Deep-water trawling (also by-catch); oil and gas exploration in deeper water; ocean acidification</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Protected areas banning trawling, eg Darwin Mounds SAC off NW Scotland, Oculina Bank off Florida</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 61-64; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HAB-09", "sub": "habitats", "subname": "Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.1.2.3.4", "pages": "64-65", "url": "subtopic/habitats.html#HAB-09", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the numbers of breeding pairs of two seabird species on an island before and after a rat eradication programme.", "text": "table 1 shows the numbers of breeding pairs of two seabird species on an island before and after a rat eradication programme. explain why oceanic islands have many endemic species but low overall biodiversity. use table 1 to explain why introduced rats had such a severe effect on these birds. explain how avian malaria threatens hawaiian birds and how climate change worsens the threat. give one other conservation measure for oceanic islands besides eradicating introduced species. isolation makes colonisation difficult: only marine species, seabirds, plants with floating seeds or chance arrivals (tortoises on logs, wind-blown birds) reach them whole taxa (terrestrial mammals, amphibians) may be absent so few original colonists colonists evolve in isolation under different selection pressures to fill the empty niches, producing unique species (galapagos, madagascar lemurs, hawaii) endemics have small ranges and populations so are vulnerable to extinction the birds evolved without mammal predators so nest on the ground / in burrows with no defence rats eat eggs and chicks; numbers fell to very low levels (45 and 12 pairs) after eradication breeding success rose about 7-12 fold, confirming predation was the main limiting factor introduced mosquitoes carry avian malaria to which endemic birds have no resistance; birds survive only above about 1500 m where it is too cold for malaria warming allows mosquitoes / malaria to spread to higher altitudes, shrinking the safe range control of developments and visitor numbers (biosecurity, quarantine of imports); protected areas; captive breeding of endemics", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HAB-09\" data-qid=\"HAB-09\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 9</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/habitats.html\">Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 64-65 &middot; HAB-09</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the numbers of breeding pairs of two seabird species on an island before and after a rat eradication programme.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Species</th><th>Breeding pairs before eradication</th><th>Breeding pairs 8 years after eradication</th></tr><tr><td>Manx shearwater</td><td>45</td><td>310</td></tr><tr><td>Storm petrel</td><td>12</td><td>140</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.1</span>Explain why oceanic islands have many endemic species but low overall biodiversity.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.2</span>Use Table 1 to explain why introduced rats had such a severe effect on these birds.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.3</span>Explain how avian malaria threatens Hawaiian birds and how climate change worsens the threat.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">09.4</span>Give <b>one</b> other conservation measure for oceanic islands besides eradicating introduced species.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Isolation makes colonisation difficult: only marine species, seabirds, plants with floating seeds or chance arrivals (tortoises on logs, wind-blown birds) reach them</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Whole taxa (terrestrial mammals, amphibians) may be absent so few original colonists</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Colonists evolve in isolation under different selection pressures to fill the empty niches, producing unique species (Galapagos, Madagascar lemurs, Hawaii)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Endemics have small ranges and populations so are vulnerable to extinction</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The birds evolved without mammal predators so nest on the ground / in burrows with no defence</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Rats eat eggs and chicks; numbers fell to very low levels (45 and 12 pairs)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>After eradication breeding success rose about 7-12 fold, confirming predation was the main limiting factor</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Introduced mosquitoes carry avian malaria to which endemic birds have no resistance; birds survive only above about 1500 m where it is too cold for malaria</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Warming allows mosquitoes / malaria to spread to higher altitudes, shrinking the safe range</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>09.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Control of developments and visitor numbers (biosecurity, quarantine of imports); protected areas; captive breeding of endemics</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 64-65; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HAB-10", "sub": "habitats", "subname": "Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.4", "pages": "66-67", "url": "subtopic/habitats.html#HAB-10", "snippet": "Describe the ecological features of mangroves and the adaptations of mangrove trees.", "text": " describe the ecological features of mangroves and the adaptations of mangrove trees. explain four reasons why mangroves are important. describe the main threats to mangroves and the conservation efforts used. tropical intertidal coastal forests of halophytic (salt-tolerant) trees in saline, oxygen-deficient (anaerobic) mud prop / aerial roots for support in soft sediment and for gas exchange (pneumatophores); salt excretion seeds germinate on the parent tree and float, rooting where they touch sand - rapid natural regeneration coastal erosion / storm protection: roots and vegetation absorb wave energy (least tsunami damage in 2004 where mangroves were intact) fisheries: nursery grounds where dense roots protect young fish that later live on reefs / open sea protection of coral reefs: slow river water so suspended solids settle and nutrients are absorbed before reaching reefs resources: timber, fuel; medicines (antimicrobials against mrsa, cyanobacterium for lung cancer); high biodiversity (crabs, shrimps, sponges, birds) clearance for aquaculture (shrimp ponds), urban development, ports, tourism; pollution; sea level rise / climate change; destruction of coral reefs that shelter the coast legal protection / designated areas (eg sri lanka protected all remaining mangroves in 2015) replanting and natural regeneration; control of damaging activities; ramsar sites", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HAB-10\" data-qid=\"HAB-10\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 10</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/habitats.html\">Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 66-67 &middot; HAB-10</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">10.1</span>Describe the ecological features of mangroves and the adaptations of mangrove trees.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">10.2</span>Explain <b>four</b> reasons why mangroves are important.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">10.3</span>Describe the main threats to mangroves and the conservation efforts used.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>10.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Tropical intertidal coastal forests of halophytic (salt-tolerant) trees in saline, oxygen-deficient (anaerobic) mud</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Prop / aerial roots for support in soft sediment and for gas exchange (pneumatophores); salt excretion</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Seeds germinate on the parent tree and float, rooting where they touch sand - rapid natural regeneration</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>10.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Coastal erosion / storm protection: roots and vegetation absorb wave energy (least tsunami damage in 2004 where mangroves were intact)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fisheries: nursery grounds where dense roots protect young fish that later live on reefs / open sea</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Protection of coral reefs: slow river water so suspended solids settle and nutrients are absorbed before reaching reefs</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Resources: timber, fuel; medicines (antimicrobials against MRSA, cyanobacterium for lung cancer); high biodiversity (crabs, shrimps, sponges, birds)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>10.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Clearance for aquaculture (shrimp ponds), urban development, ports, tourism; pollution; sea level rise / climate change; destruction of coral reefs that shelter the coast</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Legal protection / designated areas (eg Sri Lanka protected all remaining mangroves in 2015)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Replanting and natural regeneration; control of damaging activities; Ramsar sites</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 66-67; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HAB-11", "sub": "habitats", "subname": "Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.1.2.3.4", "pages": "67-71", "url": "subtopic/habitats.html#HAB-11", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows a simplified Antarctic food web.", "text": "figure 1 shows a simplified antarctic food web. explain why the sea around antarctica is so productive despite the cold. use figure 1 to explain why fishing for krill and for patagonian toothfish threatens other species. explain the importance of antarctica in controlling global natural cycles. explain why the area of winter sea ice around antarctica has increased in recent years even though air and sea are warming. upwelling currents bring nutrients from the seabed to surface waters 24-hour daylight in summer allows a huge phytoplankton bloom algae also grow under sea ice where krill shelter and feed; krill support most of the food web krill is the main food of baleen whales, penguins, seals and fish so over-harvesting removes food from most of the web toothfish overfishing removes the food of sperm whales and elephant seals long-lining for toothfish catches and drowns albatrosses on the hooks (by-catch) ice on land stores 70% of the earth's fresh water, keeping sea levels low high albedo of ice reflects sunlight, limiting warming (reduction in ice area causes further warming) carbon sequestration: carbon in algae eaten by krill sinks to the seabed in krill faeces stronger winds spreading ice; more snowfall from warmer, moister air freezing onto slush; fresh meltwater from land forming a surface layer that freezes more easily (long-term trend still expected to be a decline)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HAB-11\" data-qid=\"HAB-11\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 11</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/habitats.html\">Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 67-71 &middot; HAB-11</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows a simplified Antarctic food web.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Trophic level</th><th>Organisms</th></tr><tr><td>Producers</td><td>Phytoplankton, ice algae</td></tr><tr><td>Primary consumers</td><td>Zooplankton, krill</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary consumers</td><td>Small fish, squid, baleen whales, crabeater seals, penguins</td></tr><tr><td>Higher consumers</td><td>Toothfish, albatross, leopard seal, killer whale</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">11.1</span>Explain why the sea around Antarctica is so productive despite the cold.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">11.2</span>Use Figure 1 to explain why fishing for krill and for Patagonian toothfish threatens other species.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">11.3</span>Explain the importance of Antarctica in controlling global natural cycles.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">11.4</span>Explain why the area of winter sea ice around Antarctica has increased in recent years even though air and sea are warming.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>11.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Upwelling currents bring nutrients from the seabed to surface waters</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>24-hour daylight in summer allows a huge phytoplankton bloom</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Algae also grow under sea ice where krill shelter and feed; krill support most of the food web</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>11.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Krill is the main food of baleen whales, penguins, seals and fish so over-harvesting removes food from most of the web</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Toothfish overfishing removes the food of sperm whales and elephant seals</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Long-lining for toothfish catches and drowns albatrosses on the hooks (by-catch)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>11.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ice on land stores 70% of the Earth's fresh water, keeping sea levels low</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>High albedo of ice reflects sunlight, limiting warming (reduction in ice area causes further warming)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Carbon sequestration: carbon in algae eaten by krill sinks to the seabed in krill faeces</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>11.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stronger winds spreading ice; more snowfall from warmer, moister air freezing onto slush; fresh meltwater from land forming a surface layer that freezes more easily (long-term trend still expected to be a decline)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 67-71; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HAB-12", "sub": "habitats", "subname": "Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.3.4", "pages": "69-71", "url": "subtopic/habitats.html#HAB-12", "snippet": "Describe how each of the following threatens Antarctica.", "text": " describe how each of the following threatens antarctica. describe the main features of the antarctic treaty (1959) and the regulations that protect antarctica. warming melts ice shelves that hold back glaciers and lubricates ice flow so land ice reaches the sea faster (antarctic peninsula most affected); sea ice loss removes krill habitat and lowers albedo raised uv harms planktonic algae, krill and fish / sea urchin larvae in surface waters (land animals protected by fur / feathers) disturbance of wildlife, introduction of non-indigenous species and pathogens, fuel and sewage pollution; visitor numbers rising rapidly at a few landing sites reserves of coal, oil, gold, uranium, platinum etc; pressure to exploit will rise as reserves elsewhere deplete though currently banned and operations extremely difficult treaty signed by 52 countries including all active there; no military activity (except supporting research), no nuclear explosions or radioactive waste dumping no commercial mineral exploitation fisheries regulated by ccamlr: krill and some fish exploited, fisheries closed if over-exploited; no commercial exploitation of mammals tourism control: no accommodation on land (wastes stay on ships), one ship per site with maximum 100 ashore, trained guides, keep 5 m from wildlife, no litter, cleaned protective clothing to prevent introductions waste management: all wastes removed (fuel containers, batteries, plastics, oil); sewage only discharged where sea currents disperse it; old waste removed unless removal causes more damage only long-term residents are research scientists and support staff; strict regulation of research impacts", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HAB-12\" data-qid=\"HAB-12\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 12</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/habitats.html\">Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 69-71 &middot; HAB-12</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">12.1</span>Describe how each of the following threatens Antarctica.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">12.2</span>Describe the main features of the Antarctic Treaty (1959) and the regulations that protect Antarctica.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>12.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Warming melts ice shelves that hold back glaciers and lubricates ice flow so land ice reaches the sea faster (Antarctic Peninsula most affected); sea ice loss removes krill habitat and lowers albedo</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Raised UV harms planktonic algae, krill and fish / sea urchin larvae in surface waters (land animals protected by fur / feathers)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Disturbance of wildlife, introduction of non-indigenous species and pathogens, fuel and sewage pollution; visitor numbers rising rapidly at a few landing sites</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reserves of coal, oil, gold, uranium, platinum etc; pressure to exploit will rise as reserves elsewhere deplete though currently banned and operations extremely difficult</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>12.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Treaty signed by 52 countries including all active there; no military activity (except supporting research), no nuclear explosions or radioactive waste dumping</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No commercial mineral exploitation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fisheries regulated by CCAMLR: krill and some fish exploited, fisheries closed if over-exploited; no commercial exploitation of mammals</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Tourism control: no accommodation on land (wastes stay on ships), one ship per site with maximum 100 ashore, trained guides, keep 5 m from wildlife, no litter, cleaned protective clothing to prevent introductions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Waste management: all wastes removed (fuel containers, batteries, plastics, oil); sewage only discharged where sea currents disperse it; old waste removed unless removal causes more damage</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Only long-term residents are research scientists and support staff; strict regulation of research impacts</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 69-71; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HAB-13", "sub": "habitats", "subname": "Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.1.2.3.4", "pages": "50-71", "url": "subtopic/habitats.html#HAB-13", "snippet": "Which of the following habitats has low biodiversity but a high proportion of endemic species? Tick one box.", "text": " which of the following habitats has low biodiversity but a high proportion of endemic species? tick one box. compare the ecological features of tropical rainforest and antarctica that explain their very different biodiversity. b rainforest: high light, warm, wet all year, stable conditions for long periods, continuous productivity, many niches (stratification) antarctica: very low temperatures, low precipitation, extreme seasonal light, 98% ice cover so almost no terrestrial life antarctic biodiversity is marine, supported by upwelling nutrients and the summer bloom; rainforest supports over half of terrestrial species on 10% of land both are important carbon stores / climate regulators but rainforest species are specialists vulnerable to small changes; antarctic species adapted to extreme fluctuation", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HAB-13\" data-qid=\"HAB-13\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 13</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/habitats.html\">Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 50-71 &middot; HAB-13</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">13.1</span>Which of the following habitats has low biodiversity but a high proportion of endemic species? Tick one box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; Tropical rainforest</li><li>B&nbsp; Oceanic islands</li><li>C&nbsp; Temperate broadleaf woodland</li><li>D&nbsp; Mangroves</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">13.2</span>Compare the ecological features of tropical rainforest and Antarctica that explain their very different biodiversity.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>13.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b></b>&nbsp; B</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>13.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Rainforest: high light, warm, wet all year, stable conditions for long periods, continuous productivity, many niches (stratification)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Antarctica: very low temperatures, low precipitation, extreme seasonal light, 98% ice cover so almost no terrestrial life</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Antarctic biodiversity is marine, supported by upwelling nutrients and the summer bloom; rainforest supports over half of terrestrial species on 10% of land</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Both are important carbon stores / climate regulators but rainforest species are specialists vulnerable to small changes; Antarctic species adapted to extreme fluctuation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 50-71; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HAB-14", "sub": "habitats", "subname": "Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 15, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.1.2.3.4", "pages": "57-62", "url": "subtopic/habitats.html#HAB-14", "snippet": "Explain how the relationship between coral polyps and zooxanthellae allows a high biomass of life in nutrient-poor tropical seas.", "text": " explain how the relationship between coral polyps and zooxanthellae allows a high biomass of life in nutrient-poor tropical seas. explain why growing coral reefs help to resist climate change. discuss the importance of tropical coral reefs and evaluate the methods used to conserve them. zooxanthellae are photosynthetic algae living inside the polyp cells the algae absorb co2 and excretory wastes from the polyp for photosynthesis and growth (efficient nutrient recycling) they supply the polyp with oxygen and glucose - over 95% of its food energy in return the algae gain a protected habitat and nutrients they could not absorb from the nutrient-deficient water polyps also feed on plankton captured by cilia and nematocysts corals deposit a skeleton of calcium carbonate (calcite) each carbonate molecule requires the absorption of a molecule of carbon dioxide, so reefs are a carbon sink importance: cover 1% of ocean area but support over a quarter of marine species; fisheries (fish, lobsters, conch); medicines (azt, ara-c, dolastatin, secosteroids, bryostatin, trabectedin, bone grafts); climate control as carbon sink; erosion protection of low-lying islands; tourism income threats: physical damage (anchors, divers, litter, coral extraction), collection of ornaments (cites appendix ii), sedimentation from rivers and coastal development, pollution (oil, nutrients causing algal shading, sewage, pesticides, sunscreen), ocean acidification, bleaching from warming, overfishing (giant triton / crown of thorns starfish), introduced species (red lionfish), loss of mangroves and seagrass nurseries conservation: marine protected areas / national parks (great barrier reef, belize, chagos), mooring buoys, diver education, no take zones which can increase catches elsewhere, sustainable ecotourism and fishing, protection of mangroves and seagrass, erosion and pollution control, planning control of ports and resorts fishing controls: closed seasons, minimum and maximum catchable sizes, bans on dynamite, trawling and small-mesh nets evaluation: local measures cannot control global threats (warming, acidification) which need international action; enforcement in remote areas is difficult; conflicts with local livelihoods; large vs small ntzs justified conclusion", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HAB-14\" data-qid=\"HAB-14\" data-marks=\"15\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 14</span><span class=\"meta\">15 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/habitats.html\">Habitat case studies: woodland, rainforest, reefs, islands, mangroves, Antarctica</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 57-62 &middot; HAB-14</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">14.1</span>Explain how the relationship between coral polyps and zooxanthellae allows a high biomass of life in nutrient-poor tropical seas.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">14.2</span>Explain why growing coral reefs help to resist climate change.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">14.3</span>Discuss the importance of tropical coral reefs and evaluate the methods used to conserve them.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>14.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Zooxanthellae are photosynthetic algae living inside the polyp cells</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The algae absorb CO<sub>2</sub> and excretory wastes from the polyp for photosynthesis and growth (efficient nutrient recycling)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They supply the polyp with oxygen and glucose - over 95% of its food energy</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>In return the algae gain a protected habitat and nutrients they could not absorb from the nutrient-deficient water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Polyps also feed on plankton captured by cilia and nematocysts</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>14.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Corals deposit a skeleton of calcium carbonate (calcite)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Each carbonate molecule requires the absorption of a molecule of carbon dioxide, so reefs are a carbon sink</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>14.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Importance: cover 1% of ocean area but support over a quarter of marine species; fisheries (fish, lobsters, conch); medicines (AZT, Ara-C, dolastatin, secosteroids, bryostatin, trabectedin, bone grafts); climate control as carbon sink; erosion protection of low-lying islands; tourism income</li><li>Threats: physical damage (anchors, divers, litter, coral extraction), collection of ornaments (CITES Appendix II), sedimentation from rivers and coastal development, pollution (oil, nutrients causing algal shading, sewage, pesticides, sunscreen), ocean acidification, bleaching from warming, overfishing (giant triton / crown of thorns starfish), introduced species (red lionfish), loss of mangroves and seagrass nurseries</li><li>Conservation: marine protected areas / national parks (Great Barrier Reef, Belize, Chagos), mooring buoys, diver education, No Take Zones which can increase catches elsewhere, sustainable ecotourism and fishing, protection of mangroves and seagrass, erosion and pollution control, planning control of ports and resorts</li><li>Fishing controls: closed seasons, minimum and maximum catchable sizes, bans on dynamite, trawling and small-mesh nets</li><li>Evaluation: local measures cannot control global threats (warming, acidification) which need international action; enforcement in remote areas is difficult; conflicts with local livelihoods; large vs small NTZs</li><li>Justified conclusion</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 57-62; AQA spec 3.1.2.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FOR-01", "sub": "forests", "subname": "Forest resources & deforestation", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.3.1", "pages": "360-364", "url": "subtopic/forests.html#FOR-01", "snippet": "Describe the resources obtained from forests. Give one example for each category.", "text": " describe the resources obtained from forests. give one example for each category. explain why forests are an important carbon reservoir and how they can be used to counteract climate change. explain how forests regulate the hydrological cycle and local climate. timber: building construction, furniture, tools, telegraph poles, concrete shuttering (oak, beech, mahogany, teak, conifer softwood) fuel: fuelwood and charcoal - still the main energy source for most people in ledcs food: fruit, nuts, bushmeat, livestock fodder; crops of forest origin (coffee, cacao, bananas, brazil nuts); pigs and chickens were forest animals fibres: paper (wood pulp cellulose), cotton, viscose / rayon medicines: quinine (cinchona), taxol (yew), aspirin (willow); most species not yet researched wood is mainly cellulose which is hard to digest, so carbon stored in wood has a long residence time; total forest carbon (vegetation plus soil) is about double that in the atmosphere growing trees sequester co2 by photosynthesis and release oxygen; forest soils hold more carbon than the trees (especially boreal) planting / protecting forests removes co2 and reduces the enhanced greenhouse effect highest transpiration of any ecosystem increases downwind rainfall; interception increases evaporation; deep organic soils retain water and delay runoff so river flow is more even and flooding reduced low albedo absorbs sunlight; heat stored and released at night reduces day-night temperature extremes; canopy microclimate: lower light, wind, higher humidity", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FOR-01\" data-qid=\"FOR-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/forests.html\">Forest resources &amp; deforestation</a> &middot; spec 3.5.3.1 &middot; Genn pp. 360-364 &middot; FOR-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Describe the resources obtained from forests. Give <b>one</b> example for each category.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain why forests are an important carbon reservoir and how they can be used to counteract climate change.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain how forests regulate the hydrological cycle and local climate.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Timber: building construction, furniture, tools, telegraph poles, concrete shuttering (oak, beech, mahogany, teak, conifer softwood)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fuel: fuelwood and charcoal - still the main energy source for most people in LEDCs</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Food: fruit, nuts, bushmeat, livestock fodder; crops of forest origin (coffee, cacao, bananas, Brazil nuts); pigs and chickens were forest animals</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fibres: paper (wood pulp cellulose), cotton, viscose / rayon</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Medicines: quinine (cinchona), taxol (yew), aspirin (willow); most species not yet researched</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wood is mainly cellulose which is hard to digest, so carbon stored in wood has a long residence time; total forest carbon (vegetation plus soil) is about double that in the atmosphere</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Growing trees sequester CO2 by photosynthesis and release oxygen; forest soils hold more carbon than the trees (especially boreal)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Planting / protecting forests removes CO2 and reduces the enhanced greenhouse effect</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Highest transpiration of any ecosystem increases downwind rainfall; interception increases evaporation; deep organic soils retain water and delay runoff so river flow is more even and flooding reduced</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Low albedo absorbs sunlight; heat stored and released at night reduces day-night temperature extremes; canopy microclimate: lower light, wind, higher humidity</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 360-364; AQA spec 3.5.3.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FOR-02", "sub": "forests", "subname": "Forest resources & deforestation", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.5.3.2", "pages": "365-367", "url": "subtopic/forests.html#FOR-02", "snippet": "Table 1 compares an indigenous mixed broadleaf woodland with a Sitka spruce plantation of the same area in Scotland.", "text": "table 1 compares an indigenous mixed broadleaf woodland with a sitka spruce plantation of the same area in scotland. use table 1 to explain why the plantation has a much higher timber yield but far lower biodiversity. explain why commercial plantations are felled before the trees reach full size. explain why there is little demand today for coppiced or pollarded wood. non-indigenous species (sitka spruce from north america) chosen for fast growth and pest resistance; indigenous wildlife's needs for food, pollination, seed dispersal and habitat are not met single species: easy planting, thinning and felling over large areas; but each tree species supports its own community so few species overall close planting makes trees compete for light so they grow tall and straight (good timber) but shades out ground flora and the species that depend on them simple age structure: uniform size makes management easy but gives little variety of light, temperature, wind; no old trees or dead wood for invertebrates and woodpeckers mixed woodland: varied niches, stratification, dead wood, indigenous trees each with their own dependent species trees are felled when income will be greatest - approaching full size when the growth rate starts to decline continuing to grow them ties up land and capital for little extra timber; natural forests keep old and dead trees industrial society replaced species-specific uses (baskets, fencing, charcoal, tool handles) with large-scale uses: chipboard, plywood, pulp wood fuel replaced by fossil fuels / electricity; so traditional management declined and woods became uniform", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FOR-02\" data-qid=\"FOR-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/forests.html\">Forest resources &amp; deforestation</a> &middot; spec 3.5.3.2 &middot; Genn pp. 365-367 &middot; FOR-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 compares an indigenous mixed broadleaf woodland with a Sitka spruce plantation of the same area in Scotland.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Mixed indigenous woodland</th><th>Sitka spruce plantation</th></tr><tr><td>Tree species</td><td>12</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Age structure</td><td>All ages, dead wood present</td><td>All planted in the same year</td></tr><tr><td>Planting density</td><td>Natural, variable</td><td>Close, 2 m spacing</td></tr><tr><td>Timber yield / m<sup>3</sup> ha<sup>-1</sup> yr<sup>-1</sup></td><td>4</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td>Bird species</td><td>38</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>Ground flora species</td><td>60</td><td>4</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Use Table 1 to explain why the plantation has a much higher timber yield but far lower biodiversity.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain why commercial plantations are felled before the trees reach full size.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain why there is little demand today for coppiced or pollarded wood.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 6 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Non-indigenous species (Sitka spruce from North America) chosen for fast growth and pest resistance; indigenous wildlife's needs for food, pollination, seed dispersal and habitat are not met</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Single species: easy planting, thinning and felling over large areas; but each tree species supports its own community so few species overall</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Close planting makes trees compete for light so they grow tall and straight (good timber) but shades out ground flora and the species that depend on them</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Simple age structure: uniform size makes management easy but gives little variety of light, temperature, wind; no old trees or dead wood for invertebrates and woodpeckers</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Mixed woodland: varied niches, stratification, dead wood, indigenous trees each with their own dependent species</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Trees are felled when income will be greatest - approaching full size when the growth rate starts to decline</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Continuing to grow them ties up land and capital for little extra timber; natural forests keep old and dead trees</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Industrial society replaced species-specific uses (baskets, fencing, charcoal, tool handles) with large-scale uses: chipboard, plywood, pulp</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wood fuel replaced by fossil fuels / electricity; so traditional management declined and woods became uniform</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 365-367; AQA spec 3.5.3.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FOR-03", "sub": "forests", "subname": "Forest resources & deforestation", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.5.3.3", "pages": "367-368", "url": "subtopic/forests.html#FOR-03", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the area of forest lost each year in three regions between 1990 and 2020.", "text": "figure 1 shows the area of forest lost each year in three regions between 1990 and 2020. describe the trends shown in figure 1. explain the main causes of forest loss in south america and africa. explain why roads and railways cause more forest loss than the area they occupy. explain why softwood from scandinavia and canada is generally produced more sustainably than tropical hardwood. south america: high loss (5.1-5.5 mha yr-1) falling by about half by 2020 africa: loss steady at about 3.4 then rising to 3.9 by 2020 - now the highest europe: negative values - forest area increasing (afforestation exceeds loss), though the gain is shrinking south america: clearance for commercial cash crops (sugar cane, soya) and cattle ranching for export; hep reservoirs; roads improving access; mining africa: growing rural populations clearing land for subsistence farming; fuelwood and charcoal collection above regrowth (charcoal wastes much of the wood's energy); fodder cutting in dry areas both: hardwood timber (teak, mahogany) from clear-felled mixed forest often not replanted; urban expansion they create barriers fragmenting wildlife populations improved access makes it easier to remove timber and farm produce, so clearance spreads along them conifers grow quickly so plantations are replanted after harvest and yield a saleable crop within decades; slow-growing hardwoods (teak, mahogany) take too long for commercial plantations so they are taken from natural forest that is clear-felled and not replanted", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FOR-03\" data-qid=\"FOR-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/forests.html\">Forest resources &amp; deforestation</a> &middot; spec 3.5.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 367-368 &middot; FOR-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the area of forest lost each year in three regions between 1990 and 2020.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,72.8 220.0,61.7 380.0,111.7 540.0,142.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"72.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"220.0\" cy=\"61.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"380.0\" cy=\"111.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"142.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<polyline points=\"60.0,122.8 220.0,120.0 380.0,120.0 540.0,106.1\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2f5f73\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"122.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"220.0\" cy=\"120.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"380.0\" cy=\"120.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"106.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<polyline points=\"60.0,236.7 220.0,239.4 380.0,231.1 540.0,222.8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#8b5e3c\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"236.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"220.0\" cy=\"239.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"380.0\" cy=\"231.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"222.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1990</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">-2</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1996</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">-0.2</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2002</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">1.6</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2008</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">3.4</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2014</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">5.2</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2020</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">7</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Year</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Forest loss / million ha per year</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"24\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#22302a\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"33\">South America</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"38\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"47\">Africa</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"52\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"61\">Europe</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Describe the trends shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain the main causes of forest loss in South America and Africa.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain why roads and railways cause more forest loss than the area they occupy.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Explain why softwood from Scandinavia and Canada is generally produced more sustainably than tropical hardwood.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>South America: high loss (5.1-5.5 Mha yr<sup>-1</sup>) falling by about half by 2020</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Africa: loss steady at about 3.4 then rising to 3.9 by 2020 - now the highest</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Europe: negative values - forest area increasing (afforestation exceeds loss), though the gain is shrinking</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 4 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>South America: clearance for commercial cash crops (sugar cane, soya) and cattle ranching for export; HEP reservoirs; roads improving access; mining</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Africa: growing rural populations clearing land for subsistence farming; fuelwood and charcoal collection above regrowth (charcoal wastes much of the wood's energy); fodder cutting in dry areas</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Both: hardwood timber (teak, mahogany) from clear-felled mixed forest often not replanted; urban expansion</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They create barriers fragmenting wildlife populations</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Improved access makes it easier to remove timber and farm produce, so clearance spreads along them</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Conifers grow quickly so plantations are replanted after harvest and yield a saleable crop within decades; slow-growing hardwoods (teak, mahogany) take too long for commercial plantations so they are taken from natural forest that is clear-felled and not replanted</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 367-368; AQA spec 3.5.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FOR-04", "sub": "forests", "subname": "Forest resources & deforestation", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.3.3", "pages": "368-369", "url": "subtopic/forests.html#FOR-04", "snippet": "Explain the effects of deforestation on soil.", "text": " explain the effects of deforestation on soil. explain how deforestation affects hydrology and climate. give two ecological impacts of deforestation other than the direct loss of species. less dead organic matter / leaf litter so less humus and slower soil formation; fewer nutrients returned loss of canopy and litter exposes soil to rain splash and wind decomposing roots no longer bind soil so erosion, gullying and landslides increase (especially on slopes) reduced organic matter and biota lower infiltration and water retention reduced interception and transpiration so less water returned to the atmosphere and less rainfall downwind increased runoff: more flooding after rain, lower flows in dry periods; sedimentation of rivers increased albedo of cleared land (reflects more sunlight) reduced carbon sequestration and loss of the carbon reservoir (co2 released by burning / decay) contributing to climate change fragmentation of remaining forest into isolated patches with non-viable populations / edge effects loss of inter-species relationships (pollinators, seed dispersers) causing further declines loss of genetic resources / potential medicines before they are discovered", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FOR-04\" data-qid=\"FOR-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/forests.html\">Forest resources &amp; deforestation</a> &middot; spec 3.5.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 368-369 &middot; FOR-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Explain the effects of deforestation on soil.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain how deforestation affects hydrology and climate.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Give <b>two</b> ecological impacts of deforestation other than the direct loss of species.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Less dead organic matter / leaf litter so less humus and slower soil formation; fewer nutrients returned</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Loss of canopy and litter exposes soil to rain splash and wind</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Decomposing roots no longer bind soil so erosion, gullying and landslides increase (especially on slopes)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduced organic matter and biota lower infiltration and water retention</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduced interception and transpiration so less water returned to the atmosphere and less rainfall downwind</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Increased runoff: more flooding after rain, lower flows in dry periods; sedimentation of rivers</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Increased albedo of cleared land (reflects more sunlight)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduced carbon sequestration and loss of the carbon reservoir (CO2 released by burning / decay) contributing to climate change</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fragmentation of remaining forest into isolated patches with non-viable populations / edge effects</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Loss of inter-species relationships (pollinators, seed dispersers) causing further declines</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Loss of genetic resources / potential medicines before they are discovered</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 368-369; AQA spec 3.5.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FOR-05", "sub": "forests", "subname": "Forest resources & deforestation", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.5.3.3", "pages": "369, 386-387", "url": "subtopic/forests.html#FOR-05", "snippet": "The Rio Bravo Conservation and Management Area in Belize exploits mahogany under a sustainable management plan while conserving a rainforest of over 200 tree, 400 bird and 70 mamma", "text": "the rio bravo conservation and management area in belize exploits mahogany under a sustainable management plan while conserving a rainforest of over 200 tree, 400 bird and 70 mammal species. describe five features of the timber harvesting at rio bravo that make it sustainable. explain why involving local communities is essential to the success of the reserve. explain why selective logging is better for wildlife than clear-felling, and give one disadvantage. harvest rate below the maximum sustainable yield; only economically valuable species (mahogany) selectively logged, not clear-felled trees felled only where other mahoganies stand upwind so seeds recolonise the clearing; large seed trees and small trees left to grow buffer zones with no harvesting or machinery near rivers / wetlands restricted machinery and planned track routes re-used to avoid damaging roots of retained / rare / fruit trees each felled tree tagged and tracked to the retailer for fsc accreditation; rangers control illegal logging; tree nurseries replant over-exploited and ecologically important species the reserve removed traditional uses (fuelwood, timber, farming clearings, hunting, medicines) so people would otherwise resent / ignore it employment as rangers, forestry and tourism workers; ecotourists use local restaurants, produce and souvenirs; offcuts given to local carvers benefits give the community an incentive to protect the forest; carbon sequestration payments from companies fund management removing individual trees leaves small clearings so remaining populations survive nearby and the forest structure / microclimate is kept; timber resource used over a longer period disadvantage: more labour-intensive and expensive; more tracks needed per tree", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FOR-05\" data-qid=\"FOR-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/forests.html\">Forest resources &amp; deforestation</a> &middot; spec 3.5.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 369, 386-387 &middot; FOR-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>The Rio Bravo Conservation and Management Area in Belize exploits mahogany under a sustainable management plan while conserving a rainforest of over 200 tree, 400 bird and 70 mammal species.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Describe <b>five</b> features of the timber harvesting at Rio Bravo that make it sustainable.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain why involving local communities is essential to the success of the reserve.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain why selective logging is better for wildlife than clear-felling, and give <b>one</b> disadvantage.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Harvest rate below the maximum sustainable yield; only economically valuable species (mahogany) selectively logged, not clear-felled</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Trees felled only where other mahoganies stand upwind so seeds recolonise the clearing; large seed trees and small trees left to grow</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Buffer zones with no harvesting or machinery near rivers / wetlands</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Restricted machinery and planned track routes re-used to avoid damaging roots of retained / rare / fruit trees</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Each felled tree tagged and tracked to the retailer for FSC accreditation; rangers control illegal logging; tree nurseries replant over-exploited and ecologically important species</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The reserve removed traditional uses (fuelwood, timber, farming clearings, hunting, medicines) so people would otherwise resent / ignore it</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Employment as rangers, forestry and tourism workers; ecotourists use local restaurants, produce and souvenirs; offcuts given to local carvers</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Benefits give the community an incentive to protect the forest; carbon sequestration payments from companies fund management</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Removing individual trees leaves small clearings so remaining populations survive nearby and the forest structure / microclimate is kept; timber resource used over a longer period</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Disadvantage: more labour-intensive and expensive; more tracks needed per tree</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 369, 386-387; AQA spec 3.5.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "FOR-06", "sub": "forests", "subname": "Forest resources & deforestation", "topic": "Biological resources", "tslug": "bioresources", "papers": [2], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.5.3", "pages": "360-369", "url": "subtopic/forests.html#FOR-06", "snippet": "Which of the following features of a plantation would most increase its wildlife value? Tick one box.", "text": " which of the following features of a plantation would most increase its wildlife value? tick one box. describe four features of sustainably managed forests and explain how each benefits biodiversity or productivity. b harvesting no faster than the msy - resource never depleted mixed species plantations - more food choices, relationships and niches indigenous species - support indigenous insects, birds mixed age structure - varied light, temperature, wind so more species; continuous cover and yield selective logging - small clearings, less disturbance, long-term timber benefit retention of dead wood, buffer zones, corridors", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"FOR-06\" data-qid=\"FOR-06\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/forests.html\">Forest resources &amp; deforestation</a> &middot; spec 3.5.3 &middot; Genn pp. 360-369 &middot; FOR-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Which of the following features of a plantation would most increase its wildlife value? Tick one box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; Close planting of a single non-indigenous species</li><li>B&nbsp; Mixed indigenous species with a mixed age structure</li><li>C&nbsp; Clear-felling on a 40-year rotation</li><li>D&nbsp; Removal of dead wood</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Describe <b>four</b> features of sustainably managed forests and explain how each benefits biodiversity or productivity.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b></b>&nbsp; B</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Harvesting no faster than the MSY - resource never depleted</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mixed species plantations - more food choices, relationships and niches</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Indigenous species - support indigenous insects, birds</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mixed age structure - varied light, temperature, wind so more species; continuous cover and yield</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Selective logging - small clearings, less disturbance, long-term timber benefit</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Retention of dead wood, buffer zones, corridors</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 360-369; AQA spec 3.5.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HUM-01", "sub": "human-impacts", "subname": "How humans influence biodiversity", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.2", "pages": "20-21", "url": "subtopic/human-impacts.html#HUM-01", "snippet": "For each of the following forms of direct exploitation, give a named species whose population has been reduced.", "text": " for each of the following forms of direct exploitation, give a named species whose population has been reduced. explain why species are deliberately eradicated. give three categories with examples. explain why legal protection alone rarely prevents the decline of a species exploited for traditional medicine. food: cod, tuna, swordfish, sharks, turtles; extinct - dodo, great auk, passenger pigeon fashion: leopard, snow leopard, tiger, ocelot fur; fur seals; crocodile / alligator leather; kingfisher, parrot, ostrich feathers entertainment / pets: parrots, lizards, snakes, tortoises, tropical marine fish (most wild-caught), dolphins / orcas for marine parks, wild-collected air plants furniture / ornaments: mahogany, teak, ramin timber; ebony and ivory piano keys; turtle shell, shark teeth, coral and shells as souvenirs traditional medicines: tiger (bones, claws), rhinoceros horn, seahorses threat to humans: sharks, venomous snakes, crocodiles disease vectors: malaria mosquitoes, tsetse flies predators of livestock: wolves, puma, birds of prey, herons, seals agricultural / forestry pests and competitors: rabbits, deer, wood-boring beetles, squirrels, beavers high demand and prices make illegal trade / poaching profitable; laws are hard to enforce across borders belief in the medicine persists despite little scientific evidence; species become rarer and more valuable", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HUM-01\" data-qid=\"HUM-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/human-impacts.html\">How humans influence biodiversity</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 20-21 &middot; HUM-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>For each of the following forms of direct exploitation, give a named species whose population has been reduced.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain why species are deliberately eradicated. Give <b>three</b> categories with examples.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain why legal protection alone rarely prevents the decline of a species exploited for traditional medicine.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Food: cod, tuna, swordfish, sharks, turtles; extinct - dodo, great auk, passenger pigeon</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fashion: leopard, snow leopard, tiger, ocelot fur; fur seals; crocodile / alligator leather; kingfisher, parrot, ostrich feathers</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Entertainment / pets: parrots, lizards, snakes, tortoises, tropical marine fish (most wild-caught), dolphins / orcas for marine parks, wild-collected air plants</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Furniture / ornaments: mahogany, teak, ramin timber; ebony and ivory piano keys; turtle shell, shark teeth, coral and shells as souvenirs</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Traditional medicines: tiger (bones, claws), rhinoceros horn, seahorses</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Threat to humans: sharks, venomous snakes, crocodiles</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Disease vectors: malaria mosquitoes, tsetse flies</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Predators of livestock: wolves, puma, birds of prey, herons, seals</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Agricultural / forestry pests and competitors: rabbits, deer, wood-boring beetles, squirrels, beavers</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>High demand and prices make illegal trade / poaching profitable; laws are hard to enforce across borders</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Belief in the medicine persists despite little scientific evidence; species become rarer and more valuable</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 20-21; AQA spec 3.1.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HUM-02", "sub": "human-impacts", "subname": "How humans influence biodiversity", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.2", "pages": "22-23", "url": "subtopic/human-impacts.html#HUM-02", "snippet": "Explain how each of the following changes in abiotic factors caused by human activities can make a habitat unsuitable for species living there.", "text": " explain how each of the following changes in abiotic factors caused by human activities can make a habitat unsuitable for species living there. explain how increased water turbidity caused by ploughing, mining or dredging harms aquatic organisms. explain why species that were already rare are most likely to be lost when abiotic conditions change. drainage of wetlands / lowering water tables by groundwater abstraction dries out wetlands; hep schemes alter river levels flooding sandbank nests of turtles / birds; species with narrow tolerance (specialists) affected first; sundews lost when drained soil becomes aerobic / nitrogen-rich and taller competitors invade hot water discharges and organic wastes (sewage) reduce dissolved oxygen, killing aerobic organisms such as fish and insect larvae acid mine drainage and acid rain gases lower ph, denaturing proteins of exposed tissues (fish eggs, gills) and dissolving calcium exoskeletons (crayfish) reduces light penetration so submerged plants cannot photosynthesise blocks the gills of filter feeders such as bivalve molluscs; sediment smothers the bed rare species often have very specialised habitat requirements / narrow ranges of tolerance a small change moves conditions outside their range while generalists survive; small populations cannot recover", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HUM-02\" data-qid=\"HUM-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/human-impacts.html\">How humans influence biodiversity</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 22-23 &middot; HUM-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain how each of the following changes in abiotic factors caused by human activities can make a habitat unsuitable for species living there.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain how increased water turbidity caused by ploughing, mining or dredging harms aquatic organisms.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain why species that were already rare are most likely to be lost when abiotic conditions change.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Drainage of wetlands / lowering water tables by groundwater abstraction dries out wetlands; HEP schemes alter river levels flooding sandbank nests of turtles / birds; species with narrow tolerance (specialists) affected first; sundews lost when drained soil becomes aerobic / nitrogen-rich and taller competitors invade</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Hot water discharges and organic wastes (sewage) reduce dissolved oxygen, killing aerobic organisms such as fish and insect larvae</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Acid mine drainage and acid rain gases lower pH, denaturing proteins of exposed tissues (fish eggs, gills) and dissolving calcium exoskeletons (crayfish)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduces light penetration so submerged plants cannot photosynthesise</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Blocks the gills of filter feeders such as bivalve molluscs; sediment smothers the bed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Rare species often have very specialised habitat requirements / narrow ranges of tolerance</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A small change moves conditions outside their range while generalists survive; small populations cannot recover</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 22-23; AQA spec 3.1.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HUM-03", "sub": "human-impacts", "subname": "How humans influence biodiversity", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.1.2.2", "pages": "23-25", "url": "subtopic/human-impacts.html#HUM-03", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the estimated populations of sea otters, sea urchins and the area of kelp forest along part of the coast of California between 1960 and 2010.", "text": "figure 1 shows the estimated populations of sea otters, sea urchins and the area of kelp forest along part of the coast of california between 1960 and 2010. describe the relationship between the three variables shown in figure 1. explain the relationships shown in figure 1. give two other examples of human over-exploitation of one species affecting the population of another. explain why the loss of beavers or forest elephants can cause the decline of many other species. as sea otters declined (1960-1980) sea urchins increased (to 350) and kelp area fell (to 25) as otters recovered after 1990 urchins declined and kelp recovered otter and kelp trends are positively correlated; urchins negatively correlated with both sea otters are predators of sea urchins so fewer otters allowed the urchin population to rise sea urchins graze kelp so the kelp forest was over-grazed and destroyed kelp forests are habitat / food for many species including otters (keystone species / trophic cascade); otter recovery restored the balance over-fishing of sandeels reduced puffins over-collection of turtle eggs reduced turtles so jellyfish (their food) increased; over-fishing of sardines / herring (competitors) also increased jellyfish loss of pollinating bees (pesticides, wild flower loss) reduces plant reproduction; loss of elephants / large herbivores reduces seed dispersal of trees they control abiotic features: beaver dams create ponds colonised by aquatic species; elephants create clearings and keep water holes open species that depend on these features (light, water, minerals) cannot survive in their absence", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HUM-03\" data-qid=\"HUM-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/human-impacts.html\">How humans influence biodiversity</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 23-25 &middot; HUM-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the estimated populations of sea otters, sea urchins and the area of kelp forest along part of the coast of California between 1960 and 2010.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,204.7 156.0,230.8 252.0,256.9 348.0,253.7 444.0,230.8 540.0,211.3\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"204.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"156.0\" cy=\"230.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"252.0\" cy=\"256.9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"348.0\" cy=\"253.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"444.0\" cy=\"230.8\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"211.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<polyline points=\"60.0,204.7 156.0,152.5 252.0,41.5 348.0,54.6 444.0,139.5 540.0,191.7\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2f5f73\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"204.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"156.0\" cy=\"152.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"252.0\" cy=\"41.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"348.0\" cy=\"54.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"444.0\" cy=\"139.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"191.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<polyline points=\"60.0,204.7 156.0,224.3 252.0,253.7 348.0,250.4 444.0,227.6 540.0,211.3\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#8b5e3c\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"204.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"156.0\" cy=\"224.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"252.0\" cy=\"253.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"348.0\" cy=\"250.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"444.0\" cy=\"227.6\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"211.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1960</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1970</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">76.6</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1980</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">153</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1990</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">230</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2000</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">306</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2010</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">383</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Year</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Index (1960 = 100)</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"24\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#22302a\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"33\">Sea otters</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"38\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"47\">Sea urchins</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"52\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#8b5e3c\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"61\">Kelp area</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Describe the relationship between the three variables shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain the relationships shown in Figure 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Give <b>two</b> other examples of human over-exploitation of one species affecting the population of another.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Explain why the loss of beavers or forest elephants can cause the decline of many other species.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>As sea otters declined (1960-1980) sea urchins increased (to 350) and kelp area fell (to 25)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>As otters recovered after 1990 urchins declined and kelp recovered</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Otter and kelp trends are positively correlated; urchins negatively correlated with both</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sea otters are predators of sea urchins so fewer otters allowed the urchin population to rise</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sea urchins graze kelp so the kelp forest was over-grazed and destroyed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Kelp forests are habitat / food for many species including otters (keystone species / trophic cascade); otter recovery restored the balance</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Over-fishing of sandeels reduced puffins</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Over-collection of turtle eggs reduced turtles so jellyfish (their food) increased; over-fishing of sardines / herring (competitors) also increased jellyfish</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Loss of pollinating bees (pesticides, wild flower loss) reduces plant reproduction; loss of elephants / large herbivores reduces seed dispersal of trees</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>They control abiotic features: beaver dams create ponds colonised by aquatic species; elephants create clearings and keep water holes open</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Species that depend on these features (light, water, minerals) cannot survive in their absence</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 23-25; AQA spec 3.1.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HUM-04", "sub": "human-impacts", "subname": "How humans influence biodiversity", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.2.2", "pages": "24-25", "url": "subtopic/human-impacts.html#HUM-04", "snippet": "Explain why introduced species often have a greater effect on island communities than on mainland communities.", "text": " explain why introduced species often have a greater effect on island communities than on mainland communities. for each of the following, describe an example of an introduced species and explain its effect on an indigenous species. explain how hybridisation with an introduced species threatens the red deer and the scottish wildcat. island communities evolved from few colonising species in isolation, often without mammal predators or large herbivores so indigenous species lack adaptations (eg ground-nesting birds, flightlessness) to survive introduced cats, rats, pigs, goats competitor: grey squirrel (north america) out-competes red squirrel - digests acorns, larger, wins nest sites; rhododendron shades ground and releases toxins preventing native regrowth predator: american mink (escaped fur farms) reduced water vole range; cane toads in australia; cats / rats / pigs on islands eating ground-nesting birds; nile perch in lake victoria ate cichlids to extinction pathogen: squirrel pox virus carried by grey squirrels kills red squirrels; crayfish plague carried by signal crayfish kills white-clawed crayfish; dutch elm disease, ash dieback, sudden oak death imported on plants / soil closely related introduced species (sika deer; domestic cats) cross-breed producing fertile hybrids the natural gene pool is changed / diluted with genes it would not naturally contain, so the pure indigenous species may disappear", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HUM-04\" data-qid=\"HUM-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/human-impacts.html\">How humans influence biodiversity</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 24-25 &middot; HUM-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Explain why introduced species often have a greater effect on island communities than on mainland communities.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>For each of the following, describe an example of an introduced species and explain its effect on an indigenous species.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain how hybridisation with an introduced species threatens the red deer and the Scottish wildcat.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Island communities evolved from few colonising species in isolation, often without mammal predators or large herbivores</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So indigenous species lack adaptations (eg ground-nesting birds, flightlessness) to survive introduced cats, rats, pigs, goats</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Competitor: grey squirrel (North America) out-competes red squirrel - digests acorns, larger, wins nest sites; rhododendron shades ground and releases toxins preventing native regrowth</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Predator: American mink (escaped fur farms) reduced water vole range; cane toads in Australia; cats / rats / pigs on islands eating ground-nesting birds; Nile perch in Lake Victoria ate cichlids to extinction</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Pathogen: squirrel pox virus carried by grey squirrels kills red squirrels; crayfish plague carried by signal crayfish kills white-clawed crayfish; Dutch elm disease, ash dieback, sudden oak death imported on plants / soil</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Closely related introduced species (sika deer; domestic cats) cross-breed producing fertile hybrids</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The natural gene pool is changed / diluted with genes it would not naturally contain, so the pure indigenous species may disappear</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 24-25; AQA spec 3.1.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "HUM-05", "sub": "human-impacts", "subname": "How humans influence biodiversity", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 5, "flags": ["mcq"], "spec": "3.1.2.2", "pages": "20-26", "url": "subtopic/human-impacts.html#HUM-05", "snippet": "Which of the following is an example of habitat destruction rather than a change in abiotic factors? Tick one box.", "text": " which of the following is an example of habitat destruction rather than a change in abiotic factors? tick one box. give four human activities that cause complete habitat destruction. b deforestation ploughing of grassland / expansion of farmland reservoir creation / flooding mineral extraction, especially open-cast mining urban expansion", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"HUM-05\" data-qid=\"HUM-05\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"mcq\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/human-impacts.html\">How humans influence biodiversity</a> &middot; spec 3.1.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 20-26 &middot; HUM-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Which of the following is an example of habitat destruction rather than a change in abiotic factors? Tick one box.\n<ul class=\"mcq\"><li>A&nbsp; Thermal pollution of a river by a power station</li><li>B&nbsp; Flooding a valley to create a reservoir</li><li>C&nbsp; Lowering the water table by groundwater abstraction</li><li>D&nbsp; Acid mine drainage entering a stream</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Give <b>four</b> human activities that cause complete habitat destruction.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Correct answer only.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span><b></b>&nbsp; B</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Deforestation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ploughing of grassland / expansion of farmland</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reservoir creation / flooding</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mineral extraction, especially open-cast mining</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Urban expansion</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 20-26; AQA spec 3.1.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "LP-01", "sub": "life-processes", "subname": "Adaptation, terminology, succession & population control", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.1.3.1", "pages": "72-73", "url": "subtopic/life-processes.html#LP-01", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the percentage survival of two populations of a freshwater snail species (X and Y) at different water pH values.", "text": "figure 1 shows the percentage survival of two populations of a freshwater snail species (x and y) at different water ph values. explain what is meant by the term range of tolerance and use figure 1 to compare the ranges of tolerance of populations x and y. a stream is acidified by mine drainage from ph 7.0 to 6.0. use figure 1 to explain which population is more likely to survive and why. explain how an understanding of a species' range of tolerance helps in conservation management. give two biotic factors that affect the distribution of a species. the range of an environmental factor within which a species / population can survive (survival highest at the optimum, falling towards the limits) x survives from about ph 5.5 to 8.5 (wide range); y only from about ph 6.0 to 8.0 (narrow range) both have the same optimum (ph 7.0) x: 60% survive at ph 6.0 whereas only 5% of y survive x has a larger gene pool / wider range of tolerance so some individuals are adapted to the new conditions survivors breed and their offspring are adapted so the population rebuilds; y is likely to die out locally reveals the habitat requirements / which abiotic factor is near the edge of tolerance management can make small beneficial changes to that factor (eg liming, shading, water levels) to keep conditions within the range and increase survival food supply pollinators seed dispersal species disease / pathogens predators / competitors", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"LP-01\" data-qid=\"LP-01\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/life-processes.html\">Adaptation, terminology, succession &amp; population control</a> &middot; spec 3.1.3.1 &middot; Genn pp. 72-73 &middot; LP-01</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the percentage survival of two populations of a freshwater snail species (X and Y) at different water pH values.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,270.0 120.0,245.0 180.0,120.0 240.0,32.5 300.0,20.0 360.0,32.5 420.0,120.0 480.0,245.0 540.0,270.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"120.0\" cy=\"245.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"180.0\" cy=\"120.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"240.0\" cy=\"32.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"20.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"360.0\" cy=\"32.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"420.0\" cy=\"120.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"480.0\" cy=\"245.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<polyline points=\"60.0,270.0 120.0,270.0 180.0,257.5 240.0,120.0 300.0,20.0 360.0,120.0 420.0,257.5 480.0,270.0 540.0,270.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2f5f73\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"120.0\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"180.0\" cy=\"257.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"240.0\" cy=\"120.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"20.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"360.0\" cy=\"120.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"420.0\" cy=\"257.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"480.0\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"270.0\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">5</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">5.8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">20</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">6.6</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">40</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">7.4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">60</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">8.2</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">80</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">9</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">100</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Water pH</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Survival / %</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"24\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#22302a\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"33\">Population X</text>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"38\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2f5f73\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"47\">Population Y</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Explain what is meant by the term <b>range of tolerance</b> and use Figure 1 to compare the ranges of tolerance of populations X and Y.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>A stream is acidified by mine drainage from pH 7.0 to 6.0. Use Figure 1 to explain which population is more likely to survive and why.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain how an understanding of a species' range of tolerance helps in conservation management.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Give <b>two</b> biotic factors that affect the distribution of a species.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The range of an environmental factor within which a species / population can survive (survival highest at the optimum, falling towards the limits)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>X survives from about pH 5.5 to 8.5 (wide range); Y only from about pH 6.0 to 8.0 (narrow range)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Both have the same optimum (pH 7.0)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>X: 60% survive at pH 6.0 whereas only 5% of Y survive</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>X has a larger gene pool / wider range of tolerance so some individuals are adapted to the new conditions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Survivors breed and their offspring are adapted so the population rebuilds; Y is likely to die out locally</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reveals the habitat requirements / which abiotic factor is near the edge of tolerance</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Management can make small beneficial changes to that factor (eg liming, shading, water levels) to keep conditions within the range and increase survival</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Food supply</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pollinators</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Seed dispersal species</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Disease / pathogens</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Predators / competitors</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 72-73; AQA spec 3.1.3.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "LP-02", "sub": "life-processes", "subname": "Adaptation, terminology, succession & population control", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.3.2", "pages": "82-85", "url": "subtopic/life-processes.html#LP-02", "snippet": "Define each of the following ecological terms and give an example.", "text": " define each of the following ecological terms and give an example. explain the difference between an ecosystem and a biome. species: a group of closely related organisms that naturally interbreed to produce fertile offspring and are reproductively isolated from other groups (passer domesticus, house sparrow); the only 'real' taxon niche: the role a species plays in its habitat - how it uses resources and relates to other species (tawny owl: nocturnal carnivore nesting in tree holes eating small mammals); two species cannot share exactly the same niche in one habitat community: all the populations of all species (plants, animals, fungi, bacteria, protists, archaea) living in an area biome: a large geographical region with a specific climate and characteristic community, including all areas where it occurs (all tropical rainforests, tundra, savannah) an ecosystem is the biotic community plus abiotic factors of one area, relatively self-contained (a particular coral reef) a biome includes all separate ecosystems with the same climate and community type (all coral reefs); each reef is a different ecosystem within the same biome", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"LP-02\" data-qid=\"LP-02\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/life-processes.html\">Adaptation, terminology, succession &amp; population control</a> &middot; spec 3.1.3.2 &middot; Genn pp. 82-85 &middot; LP-02</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[8 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Define each of the following ecological terms and give an example.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Explain the difference between an ecosystem and a biome.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">8 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Species: a group of closely related organisms that naturally interbreed to produce fertile offspring and are reproductively isolated from other groups (Passer domesticus, house sparrow); the only 'real' taxon</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Niche: the role a species plays in its habitat - how it uses resources and relates to other species (tawny owl: nocturnal carnivore nesting in tree holes eating small mammals); two species cannot share exactly the same niche in one habitat</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Community: all the populations of all species (plants, animals, fungi, bacteria, protists, archaea) living in an area</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Biome: a large geographical region with a specific climate and characteristic community, including all areas where it occurs (all tropical rainforests, tundra, savannah)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>An ecosystem is the biotic community plus abiotic factors of one area, relatively self-contained (a particular coral reef)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A biome includes all separate ecosystems with the same climate and community type (all coral reefs); each reef is a different ecosystem within the same biome</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 82-85; AQA spec 3.1.3.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "LP-03", "sub": "life-processes", "subname": "Adaptation, terminology, succession & population control", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.1.3.3", "pages": "73-76", "url": "subtopic/life-processes.html#LP-03", "snippet": "A new pond was dug in a nature reserve. Table 1 shows the vegetation recorded at intervals over 60 years.", "text": "a new pond was dug in a nature reserve. table 1 shows the vegetation recorded at intervals over 60 years. name the type of sere shown in table 1 and identify the pioneer species. explain the changes shown in table 1. explain why a lithosere and a hydrosere in the same region end with the same climax community. describe the early stages of a lithosere. hydrosere single-celled algae (and floating plants) - arrive on birds / insects / from soil pioneers and later plants grow and die; dead organic matter plus sediment washed in fill the pond so water becomes shallower as depth falls rooted plants (pondweeds then emergent reeds with leaves above water) can anchor and colonise; emergents shade submerged plants which die out sediment / soil accumulates and becomes able to support larger plants; water-tolerant trees (willow, alder) colonise the waterlogged soil transpiration by trees and further sediment dry the soil and make it aerobic so oak can colonise; the denser canopy shades out smaller plants each community changes abiotic conditions (depth, light, nutrients, oxygen), making it suitable for the next colonisers which out-compete them; conditions become less extreme and biotic factors increasingly important starting conditions (bare rock vs open water) differ but each stage makes conditions less extreme and builds soil / organic matter the final community is controlled by the regional climate (temperature, water availability), which is the same for both - climatic climax (eg oak woodland in the uk) lichens and algae colonise bare rock (extreme temperatures, no soil, little water); rock fragments and dead organic matter accumulate; mosses, then grasses and ferns as thin soil forms; wind-dispersed trees (birch) then animal-dispersed (oak, beech)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"LP-03\" data-qid=\"LP-03\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/life-processes.html\">Adaptation, terminology, succession &amp; population control</a> &middot; spec 3.1.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 73-76 &middot; LP-03</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A new pond was dug in a nature reserve. Table 1 shows the vegetation recorded at intervals over 60 years.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Years after creation</th><th>Dominant vegetation</th><th>Water depth at centre / m</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Single-celled algae, floating duckweed</td><td>2.0</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Submerged pondweeds; reeds at margins</td><td>1.8</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Reeds and reedmace over most of pond; water lilies</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>Marsh plants, sedges; willow and alder at edges</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>60</td><td>Willow / alder carr; oak seedlings</td><td>0 (soil waterlogged)</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Name the type of sere shown in Table 1 and identify the pioneer species.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain the changes shown in Table 1.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain why a lithosere and a hydrosere in the same region end with the same climax community.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Describe the early stages of a lithosere.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hydrosere</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Single-celled algae (and floating plants) - arrive on birds / insects / from soil</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pioneers and later plants grow and die; dead organic matter plus sediment washed in fill the pond so water becomes shallower</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>As depth falls rooted plants (pondweeds then emergent reeds with leaves above water) can anchor and colonise; emergents shade submerged plants which die out</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sediment / soil accumulates and becomes able to support larger plants; water-tolerant trees (willow, alder) colonise the waterlogged soil</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Transpiration by trees and further sediment dry the soil and make it aerobic so oak can colonise; the denser canopy shades out smaller plants</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Each community changes abiotic conditions (depth, light, nutrients, oxygen), making it suitable for the next colonisers which out-compete them; conditions become less extreme and biotic factors increasingly important</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Starting conditions (bare rock vs open water) differ but each stage makes conditions less extreme and builds soil / organic matter</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The final community is controlled by the regional climate (temperature, water availability), which is the same for both - climatic climax (eg oak woodland in the UK)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lichens and algae colonise bare rock (extreme temperatures, no soil, little water); rock fragments and dead organic matter accumulate; mosses, then grasses and ferns as thin soil forms; wind-dispersed trees (birch) then animal-dispersed (oak, beech)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 73-76; AQA spec 3.1.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "LP-04", "sub": "life-processes", "subname": "Adaptation, terminology, succession & population control", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.1.3.3", "pages": "76-77", "url": "subtopic/life-processes.html#LP-04", "snippet": "Explain the difference between primary and secondary succession and why secondary succession is faster.", "text": " explain the difference between primary and secondary succession and why secondary succession is faster. explain what is meant by deflected succession and a plagioclimax, using a named uk example. explain why conservation of a plagioclimax requires continued human activity whereas conservation of a rainforest or coral reef does not. explain why biodiversity tends to be low in abiotically extreme environments and high in stable ones. primary: succession on a newly created habitat with no life / no soil (bare rock, new pond, sand) secondary: succession restarting after an existing community is disturbed (tree felling, ploughing, fire, end of grazing) faster because soil already exists and many seeds / roots / organisms are still present so there are fewer delays for colonisation human activity (grazing, mowing, burning, coppicing) regularly removes the later successional species so the climatic climax cannot form the stable community that develops under this repeated disturbance is a plagioclimax examples: chalk grassland / hay meadows maintained by grazing or mowing; lowland heath by burning; coppiced woodland; machair grassland; broads grazing marshes if the traditional activity stops, secondary succession returns the habitat to scrub / woodland and the plagioclimax species are lost rainforest and coral reefs are climax communities so conservation aims to minimise human impact extreme environments (deserts, polar) are dominated by abiotic factors; few species tolerate them; populations fluctuate and food webs are simple so changes in one food species have large effects stable environments allow many species to evolve inter-species relationships; populations are regulated by biotic factors; complex food webs give stability (rainforests, reefs)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"LP-04\" data-qid=\"LP-04\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/life-processes.html\">Adaptation, terminology, succession &amp; population control</a> &middot; spec 3.1.3.3 &middot; Genn pp. 76-77 &middot; LP-04</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Explain the difference between primary and secondary succession and why secondary succession is faster.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain what is meant by <b>deflected succession</b> and a <b>plagioclimax</b>, using a named UK example.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain why conservation of a plagioclimax requires continued human activity whereas conservation of a rainforest or coral reef does not.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Explain why biodiversity tends to be low in abiotically extreme environments and high in stable ones.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Primary: succession on a newly created habitat with no life / no soil (bare rock, new pond, sand)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Secondary: succession restarting after an existing community is disturbed (tree felling, ploughing, fire, end of grazing)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Faster because soil already exists and many seeds / roots / organisms are still present so there are fewer delays for colonisation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Human activity (grazing, mowing, burning, coppicing) regularly removes the later successional species so the climatic climax cannot form</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The stable community that develops under this repeated disturbance is a plagioclimax</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Examples: chalk grassland / hay meadows maintained by grazing or mowing; lowland heath by burning; coppiced woodland; Machair grassland; Broads grazing marshes</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>If the traditional activity stops, secondary succession returns the habitat to scrub / woodland and the plagioclimax species are lost</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Rainforest and coral reefs are climax communities so conservation aims to minimise human impact</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Extreme environments (deserts, polar) are dominated by abiotic factors; few species tolerate them; populations fluctuate and food webs are simple so changes in one food species have large effects</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stable environments allow many species to evolve inter-species relationships; populations are regulated by biotic factors; complex food webs give stability (rainforests, reefs)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 76-77; AQA spec 3.1.3.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "LP-05", "sub": "life-processes", "subname": "Adaptation, terminology, succession & population control", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.1.3.4", "pages": "78-81", "url": "subtopic/life-processes.html#LP-05", "snippet": "Figure 1 shows the population of red deer on an island after they were introduced in year 0. No predators are present.", "text": "figure 1 shows the population of red deer on an island after they were introduced in year 0. no predators are present. name the shape of the growth curve and explain the changes in growth rate shown. explain the difference between density-dependent and density-independent factors, with an example of each. explain why the deer population on the island may need to be culled even though it appears stable. explain how predator and prey populations regulate each other. sigmoidal (s-shaped) growth curve slow start (few breeding individuals), then rapid / exponential growth (years 4-10) as abundant food and space mean birth rate far exceeds death rate growth slows as density-dependent factors (intra-species competition for food, disease) raise the death rate population stabilises / fluctuates around about 920-950 - the carrying capacity - where birth rate equals death rate density-dependent: effect on survival increases with population density - food shortage / intra-species competition, disease spread, predation density-independent: chance of survival unaffected by density - drought, flood, volcanic eruption, severe winter without wolves (natural predators removed) the population is regulated only by starvation / disease at a high density at this density deer over-graze, preventing tree regeneration and damaging habitats / other species; culling keeps numbers below the damaging level (as in scotland) rising prey numbers give predators more food so predator numbers rise; high predation then reduces the prey population food shortage reduces predators, allowing prey to recover - cyclic fluctuations with predators lagging behind prey", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"LP-05\" data-qid=\"LP-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/life-processes.html\">Adaptation, terminology, succession &amp; population control</a> &middot; spec 3.1.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 78-81 &middot; LP-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the population of red deer on an island after they were introduced in year 0. No predators are present.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Figure 1</div>\n<svg class=\"chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\">\n<polyline points=\"60.0,265.2 108.0,260.4 156.0,248.4 204.0,222.1 252.0,169.3 300.0,102.2 348.0,59.1 396.0,44.7 444.0,54.3 492.0,42.3 540.0,49.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#22302a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n<circle cx=\"60.0\" cy=\"265.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"108.0\" cy=\"260.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"156.0\" cy=\"248.4\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"204.0\" cy=\"222.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"252.0\" cy=\"169.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300.0\" cy=\"102.2\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"348.0\" cy=\"59.1\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"396.0\" cy=\"44.7\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"444.0\" cy=\"54.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"492.0\" cy=\"42.3\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"49.5\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#22302a\"/>\n<line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"60.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"274.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"224.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">209</text>\n<line x1=\"252.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"252.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"252.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">8</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">417</text>\n<line x1=\"348.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"348.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"348.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">12</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"124.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">626</text>\n<line x1=\"444.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"444.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"444.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">16</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"74.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">834</text>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"274\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\">20</text>\n<line x1=\"56\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20.0\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"24.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">1.04e+03</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#000\"/>\n<text x=\"300.0\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Years after introduction</text>\n<text transform=\"translate(14,145.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Population</text>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Name the shape of the growth curve and explain the changes in growth rate shown.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Explain the difference between density-dependent and density-independent factors, with an example of each.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain why the deer population on the island may need to be culled even though it appears stable.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Explain how predator and prey populations regulate each other.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sigmoidal (S-shaped) growth curve</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Slow start (few breeding individuals), then rapid / exponential growth (years 4-10) as abundant food and space mean birth rate far exceeds death rate</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Growth slows as density-dependent factors (intra-species competition for food, disease) raise the death rate</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Population stabilises / fluctuates around about 920-950 - the carrying capacity - where birth rate equals death rate</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Density-dependent: effect on survival increases with population density - food shortage / intra-species competition, disease spread, predation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Density-independent: chance of survival unaffected by density - drought, flood, volcanic eruption, severe winter</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Without wolves (natural predators removed) the population is regulated only by starvation / disease at a high density</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>At this density deer over-graze, preventing tree regeneration and damaging habitats / other species; culling keeps numbers below the damaging level (as in Scotland)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Rising prey numbers give predators more food so predator numbers rise; high predation then reduces the prey population</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Food shortage reduces predators, allowing prey to recover - cyclic fluctuations with predators lagging behind prey</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 78-81; AQA spec 3.1.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "LP-06", "sub": "life-processes", "subname": "Adaptation, terminology, succession & population control", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.1.3.4", "pages": "79-80", "url": "subtopic/life-processes.html#LP-06", "snippet": "Table 1 compares two exploited species.", "text": "table 1 compares two exploited species. use table 1 to identify which species is r-selected and which is k-selected, and explain the difference between the strategies. explain why k-selected species are much more easily over-exploited than r-selected species. state the equation used to forecast a change in population size. explain what is meant by maximum sustainable yield and how it relates to carrying capacity. anchovy r-selected; blue whale k-selected r-selected: mature quickly, produce many young, disperse widely, short lives; adapted to respond rapidly to high death rates / unstable conditions (mice, locusts, greenfly, rabbits) k-selected: mature late, few young with parental care, long-lived; populations stable near carrying capacity (whales, elephants, rhinos) low reproductive rate means losses from hunting / habitat change cannot be replaced quickly an increase in death rate above the low birth rate causes a population crash recovery takes decades (eg blue whale still far below pre-whaling numbers after the 1986 moratorium); r-selected species rebound within a few years once exploitation stops new population = starting population + births + immigrants - deaths - emigrants the greatest rate of exploitation (harvest) possible without causing long-term population decline equals the surplus production: highest when the population is well below carrying capacity where growth rate is fastest (middle of the sigmoid curve) at carrying capacity net growth is zero so any harvest reduces the population; harvesting above msy causes decline", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"LP-06\" data-qid=\"LP-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/life-processes.html\">Adaptation, terminology, succession &amp; population control</a> &middot; spec 3.1.3.4 &middot; Genn pp. 79-80 &middot; LP-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 compares two exploited species.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Anchovy</th><th>Blue whale</th></tr><tr><td>Age at sexual maturity</td><td>1 year</td><td>10 years</td></tr><tr><td>Offspring per female per year</td><td>Thousands of eggs</td><td>1 calf every 2-3 years</td></tr><tr><td>Lifespan</td><td>3-4 years</td><td>80+ years</td></tr><tr><td>Parental care</td><td>None</td><td>Extensive</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Use Table 1 to identify which species is r-selected and which is k-selected, and explain the difference between the strategies.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain why k-selected species are much more easily over-exploited than r-selected species.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>State the equation used to forecast a change in population size.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Explain what is meant by <b>maximum sustainable yield</b> and how it relates to carrying capacity.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Anchovy r-selected; blue whale k-selected</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>r-selected: mature quickly, produce many young, disperse widely, short lives; adapted to respond rapidly to high death rates / unstable conditions (mice, locusts, greenfly, rabbits)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>k-selected: mature late, few young with parental care, long-lived; populations stable near carrying capacity (whales, elephants, rhinos)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Low reproductive rate means losses from hunting / habitat change cannot be replaced quickly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>An increase in death rate above the low birth rate causes a population crash</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Recovery takes decades (eg blue whale still far below pre-whaling numbers after the 1986 moratorium); r-selected species rebound within a few years once exploitation stops</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>New population = starting population + births + immigrants - deaths - emigrants</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The greatest rate of exploitation (harvest) possible without causing long-term population decline</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Equals the surplus production: highest when the population is well below carrying capacity where growth rate is fastest (middle of the sigmoid curve)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>At carrying capacity net growth is zero so any harvest reduces the population; harvesting above MSY causes decline</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 79-80; AQA spec 3.1.3.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "LP-07", "sub": "life-processes", "subname": "Adaptation, terminology, succession & population control", "topic": "The living environment", "tslug": "living", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.1.3", "pages": "72-85", "url": "subtopic/life-processes.html#LP-07", "snippet": "Explain how an understanding of ecological succession and population dynamics can inform conservation decision-making.", "text": " explain how an understanding of ecological succession and population dynamics can inform conservation decision-making. state what is meant by a taxon. succession: pioneers, seres (hydrosere, lithosere, psammosere), modification of abiotic conditions, climax communities controlled by climate; deflected succession and plagioclimaxes application: deciding whether to manage (plagioclimax habitats need grazing, mowing, burning, coppicing, pollarding continued) or leave alone (climax habitats); habitat creation planning the stages; controlling scrub / secondary succession; understanding that new habitats change over time population dynamics: birth and death rates, density-dependent and independent factors, carrying capacity, sigmoid growth, predator-prey cycles, r- and k-selection, msy application: setting sustainable harvest levels (fisheries, whaling, forestry); recognising k-selected species need strict protection and recover slowly; deciding when culling is needed (deer without wolves, introduced species) or when releases / captive breeding are needed (low breeding rate); predicting effects of reintroducing predators monitoring population size, distribution, survival rate and age structure to assess success; adaptation and range of tolerance guiding abiotic management evaluation: uncertainties (natural fluctuations, unknown carrying capacity); examples (yellowstone wolves, scottish deer, chalk grassland); judgement any taxonomic group of organisms classified together by similarity (eg species, genus, family)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"LP-07\" data-qid=\"LP-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/life-processes.html\">Adaptation, terminology, succession &amp; population control</a> &middot; spec 3.1.3 &middot; Genn pp. 72-85 &middot; LP-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Explain how an understanding of ecological succession and population dynamics can inform conservation decision-making.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>State what is meant by a <b>taxon</b>.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Succession: pioneers, seres (hydrosere, lithosere, psammosere), modification of abiotic conditions, climax communities controlled by climate; deflected succession and plagioclimaxes</li><li>Application: deciding whether to manage (plagioclimax habitats need grazing, mowing, burning, coppicing, pollarding continued) or leave alone (climax habitats); habitat creation planning the stages; controlling scrub / secondary succession; understanding that new habitats change over time</li><li>Population dynamics: birth and death rates, density-dependent and independent factors, carrying capacity, sigmoid growth, predator-prey cycles, r- and k-selection, MSY</li><li>Application: setting sustainable harvest levels (fisheries, whaling, forestry); recognising k-selected species need strict protection and recover slowly; deciding when culling is needed (deer without wolves, introduced species) or when releases / captive breeding are needed (low breeding rate); predicting effects of reintroducing predators</li><li>Monitoring population size, distribution, survival rate and age structure to assess success; adaptation and range of tolerance guiding abiotic management</li><li>Evaluation: uncertainties (natural fluctuations, unknown carrying capacity); examples (Yellowstone wolves, Scottish deer, chalk grassland); judgement</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Any taxonomic group of organisms classified together by similarity (eg species, genus, family)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 72-85; AQA spec 3.1.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SUS-05", "sub": "circular-economy", "subname": "The circular economy, biocapacity & ecological footprints", "topic": "Sustainability", "tslug": "sustainability", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.6.4.1", "pages": "374-375", "url": "subtopic/circular-economy.html#SUS-05", "snippet": "Describe the main principles of the circular economy.", "text": " describe the main principles of the circular economy. explain what is meant by 'optimum production rather than maximum production', using a comparison between a natural ecosystem and an agroecosystem. explain why international cooperation (eg through the eu or un) is needed to implement circular economy initiatives. cycling of materials (like biogeochemical cycles) - recycling, re-use energy from renewable sources human activities should support ecosystems / conserve biodiversity separation of technical and biological materials; connected systems where the waste of one process feeds another (industrial symbiosis) diverse systems are more resistant to change design of products for extended use and end-of-life re-use; optimum rather than maximum production; technologies that improve efficiency and enable dismantling natural systems over-produce so the surplus supports the processes the system relies on: plant products feed pollinators, seed dispersers, decomposers, mycorrhizae agroecosystems aim for maximum harvested yield, removing the surplus that would maintain soil biota, pollinators and pest predators, so they need artificial inputs costs of environmental measures could put companies in one country at a disadvantage against competitors in countries without them a common approach gives a level playing field, coordinated monitoring and support for less successful countries", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SUS-05\" data-qid=\"SUS-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/circular-economy.html\">The circular economy, biocapacity &amp; ecological footprints</a> &middot; spec 3.6.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 374-375 &middot; SUS-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Describe the main principles of the circular economy.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain what is meant by 'optimum production rather than maximum production', using a comparison between a natural ecosystem and an agroecosystem.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Explain why international cooperation (eg through the EU or UN) is needed to implement circular economy initiatives.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cycling of materials (like biogeochemical cycles) - recycling, re-use</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Energy from renewable sources</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Human activities should support ecosystems / conserve biodiversity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Separation of technical and biological materials; connected systems where the waste of one process feeds another (industrial symbiosis)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Diverse systems are more resistant to change</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Design of products for extended use and end-of-life re-use; optimum rather than maximum production; technologies that improve efficiency and enable dismantling</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Natural systems over-produce so the surplus supports the processes the system relies on: plant products feed pollinators, seed dispersers, decomposers, mycorrhizae</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Agroecosystems aim for maximum harvested yield, removing the surplus that would maintain soil biota, pollinators and pest predators, so they need artificial inputs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Costs of environmental measures could put companies in one country at a disadvantage against competitors in countries without them</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A common approach gives a level playing field, coordinated monitoring and support for less successful countries</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 374-375; AQA spec 3.6.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SUS-06", "sub": "circular-economy", "subname": "The circular economy, biocapacity & ecological footprints", "topic": "Sustainability", "tslug": "sustainability", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["ext"], "spec": "3.6.4.1", "pages": "375-379", "url": "subtopic/circular-economy.html#SUS-06", "snippet": "Evaluate the extent to which the principles of the circular economy can be applied to three of the following: water supplies, mineral supplies, waste management, pollution control,", "text": " evaluate the extent to which the principles of the circular economy can be applied to three of the following: water supplies, mineral supplies, waste management, pollution control, energy supplies, food production. state what is meant by industrial symbiosis. water: catchment management, conservation, metering, greywater, re-use of treated effluent, aquifer recharge, sustainable urban drainage - largely cyclical already; limits in arid regions minerals: exploiting low-grade ores with low-temperature processes (bioleaching), extending product lifespans, repair / re-use / repurpose / recycle, design for disassembly, reduced packaging; limits: dispersal, alloys, energy of collection waste management: repair, re-use, refurbish, recycle, composting and anaerobic digestion returning nutrients, minimal landfill, energy from residual waste; eu targets on packaging, batteries, weee pollution control: shift from post-production treatment to non-release - fuel desulfurisation not fgd, fuel cells / electric vehicles not combustion, industrial symbiosis (kalundborg: waste heat, gypsum, fly ash, yeast used by neighbours); limits for legacy pollution energy: renewables, storage, energy density technologies, low-temperature manufacturing, chp; limits of intermittency, transport fuels food: natural processes for nutrients (legumes, organic matter), pest control (ipm), soil biota, agri-environment schemes, imta aquaculture; tension with maximum yield for a growing population land uses supporting ecosystems: urban green infrastructure, gardens, permeable surfaces, habitat in redeveloped docks / lagoons judgement: high applicability in some sectors (water, waste), partial in others (minerals, energy for transport); needs policy support locating industries so that the wastes of one (heat, gases, by-products) become the raw materials of another, mimicking natural cycles (eg kalundborg eco-industrial park)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SUS-06\" data-qid=\"SUS-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"ext\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/circular-economy.html\">The circular economy, biocapacity &amp; ecological footprints</a> &middot; spec 3.6.4.1 &middot; Genn pp. 375-379 &middot; SUS-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[9 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Evaluate the extent to which the principles of the circular economy can be applied to <b>three</b> of the following: water supplies, mineral supplies, waste management, pollution control, energy supplies, food production.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>State what is meant by <b>industrial symbiosis</b>.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">9 marks</span></div>\n<table class=\"levels\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the answer must show</th></tr><tr><td><b>Level 3</b></td><td class=\"r\">7-9</td><td>Answer is comprehensive, relevant and logically structured. Shows detailed knowledge and understanding of the relevant scientific ideas and applies them accurately to the context. Evaluation/analysis is well supported by evidence and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 2</b></td><td class=\"r\">4-6</td><td>Answer is mostly relevant and shows some structure. Knowledge and understanding of relevant ideas is sound but application to the context or evaluation may be incomplete or lack detail. A judgement may be made but is not fully supported.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Level 1</b></td><td class=\"r\">1-3</td><td>Answer is basic, with limited structure. Some relevant knowledge is shown but there are omissions or errors. Little application to the context and little or no evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>&nbsp;</b></td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td>No relevant content.</td></tr></table><div class=\"lvl\"><b>Indicative content</b> - credit any of the following (and other relevant, accurate points):</div>\n<ul class=\"ind\"><li>Water: catchment management, conservation, metering, greywater, re-use of treated effluent, aquifer recharge, sustainable urban drainage - largely cyclical already; limits in arid regions</li><li>Minerals: exploiting low-grade ores with low-temperature processes (bioleaching), extending product lifespans, repair / re-use / repurpose / recycle, design for disassembly, reduced packaging; limits: dispersal, alloys, energy of collection</li><li>Waste management: repair, re-use, refurbish, recycle, composting and anaerobic digestion returning nutrients, minimal landfill, energy from residual waste; EU targets on packaging, batteries, WEEE</li><li>Pollution control: shift from post-production treatment to non-release - fuel desulfurisation not FGD, fuel cells / electric vehicles not combustion, industrial symbiosis (Kalundborg: waste heat, gypsum, fly ash, yeast used by neighbours); limits for legacy pollution</li><li>Energy: renewables, storage, energy density technologies, low-temperature manufacturing, CHP; limits of intermittency, transport fuels</li><li>Food: natural processes for nutrients (legumes, organic matter), pest control (IPM), soil biota, agri-environment schemes, IMTA aquaculture; tension with maximum yield for a growing population</li><li>Land uses supporting ecosystems: urban green infrastructure, gardens, permeable surfaces, habitat in redeveloped docks / lagoons</li><li>Judgement: high applicability in some sectors (water, waste), partial in others (minerals, energy for transport); needs policy support</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Locating industries so that the wastes of one (heat, gases, by-products) become the raw materials of another, mimicking natural cycles (eg Kalundborg eco-industrial park)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 375-379; AQA spec 3.6.4.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SUS-07", "sub": "circular-economy", "subname": "The circular economy, biocapacity & ecological footprints", "topic": "Sustainability", "tslug": "sustainability", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.6.4.2", "pages": "379-380", "url": "subtopic/circular-economy.html#SUS-07", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the ecological footprint and biocapacity per person for four countries and the world in 2016.", "text": "table 1 shows the ecological footprint and biocapacity per person for four countries and the world in 2016. define ecological footprint and biocapacity. explain what is meant by a global hectare and why it is used. use table 1 to calculate how many earths would be needed if everyone lived like the average us citizen. use table 1 to describe the relationship between development and ecological footprint, and explain the concept of earth overshoot day. suggest two ways a country such as the uk could reduce its footprint without reducing its hdi. ecological footprint: the area of the earth's surface (global hectares) needed to provide the resources used by a person / population / activity and to absorb the wastes produced biocapacity: the biological productivity of an area (forests, cropland, grazing, fisheries, built land) - its ability to supply resources and absorb wastes, expressed in global hectares a hectare of land / sea with the world-average biological productivity biocapacities of real areas vary widely so a standard unit allows footprints and biocapacities of different places to be compared 8.1 / 1.7 = 4.8 (accept 4.7-4.8) higher hdi is associated with a larger footprint (usa 8.1 vs bangladesh 0.8), though the uk achieves the same hdi as the usa with about half the footprint global footprint (2.8) exceeds global biocapacity (1.7) so humanity uses resources faster than they regenerate and co2 accumulates (overshoot) earth overshoot day is the date each year when a year's worth of resources / waste absorption has been used up; it falls earlier each year (8 august in 2016) renewable energy and energy conservation (carbon footprint is the largest component) reduced meat consumption / food waste; local seasonal food circular economy: repair, re-use, recycling; longer product lives public transport, efficient buildings", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SUS-07\" data-qid=\"SUS-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/circular-economy.html\">The circular economy, biocapacity &amp; ecological footprints</a> &middot; spec 3.6.4.2 &middot; Genn pp. 379-380 &middot; SUS-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the ecological footprint and biocapacity per person for four countries and the world in 2016.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th></th><th>Ecological footprint / gha per person</th><th>Biocapacity / gha per person</th><th>Human Development Index</th></tr><tr><td>USA</td><td>8.1</td><td>3.6</td><td>0.92</td></tr><tr><td>UK</td><td>4.4</td><td>1.4</td><td>0.92</td></tr><tr><td>China</td><td>3.6</td><td>1.0</td><td>0.75</td></tr><tr><td>Bangladesh</td><td>0.8</td><td>0.4</td><td>0.61</td></tr><tr><td>World</td><td>2.8</td><td>1.7</td><td>0.72</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Define <b>ecological footprint</b> and <b>biocapacity</b>.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain what is meant by a <b>global hectare</b> and why it is used.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Use Table 1 to calculate how many Earths would be needed if everyone lived like the average US citizen.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Use Table 1 to describe the relationship between development and ecological footprint, and explain the concept of Earth Overshoot Day.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.5</span>Suggest <b>two</b> ways a country such as the UK could reduce its footprint without reducing its HDI.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ecological footprint: the area of the Earth's surface (global hectares) needed to provide the resources used by a person / population / activity and to absorb the wastes produced</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Biocapacity: the biological productivity of an area (forests, cropland, grazing, fisheries, built land) - its ability to supply resources and absorb wastes, expressed in global hectares</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A hectare of land / sea with the world-average biological productivity</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Biocapacities of real areas vary widely so a standard unit allows footprints and biocapacities of different places to be compared</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for the correct answer.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>8.1 / 1.7 = 4.8 (accept 4.7-4.8)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Higher HDI is associated with a larger footprint (USA 8.1 vs Bangladesh 0.8), though the UK achieves the same HDI as the USA with about half the footprint</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Global footprint (2.8) exceeds global biocapacity (1.7) so humanity uses resources faster than they regenerate and CO2 accumulates (overshoot)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Earth Overshoot Day is the date each year when a year's worth of resources / waste absorption has been used up; it falls earlier each year (8 August in 2016)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Renewable energy and energy conservation (carbon footprint is the largest component)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Reduced meat consumption / food waste; local seasonal food</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Circular economy: repair, re-use, recycling; longer product lives</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Public transport, efficient buildings</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 379-380; AQA spec 3.6.4.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SUS-08", "sub": "circular-economy", "subname": "The circular economy, biocapacity & ecological footprints", "topic": "Sustainability", "tslug": "sustainability", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.6.4.2", "pages": "380-383", "url": "subtopic/circular-economy.html#SUS-08", "snippet": "Explain what a carbon footprint should include when calculated for a car.", "text": " explain what a carbon footprint should include when calculated for a car. describe what the wwf living planet index measures and how it is used. describe how the ol pejeta reserve in kenya combines commercial cattle ranching with wildlife conservation. all greenhouse gases expressed as co2 equivalent (allowing for different warming strengths) direct emissions from fuel combustion plus indirect: manufacture of components, maintenance items, final disposal (life-cycle analysis) a fair share of the infrastructure: roads, car parks, signals, street lighting compares the populations of over 18 000 populations of 3700 vertebrate species (terrestrial, freshwater, marine) with 1970 levels published in the living planet report as a measure of the state of the planet's ecosystems / biodiversity loss over time hardy, disease-resistant boran cattle; regular tick dipping reduces ticks for wild herbivores too mobile bomas (night enclosures) protect cattle from lions and leave dung that enriches soil and plant growth electric fences keep game in and poachers out; biological corridors let animals (except rhinos) move to other reserves; problem elephants relocated monitoring: radio-collared lions, drones, camera traps, track surveys; endangered species (grevy's zebra, northern white rhino) protected community links: ecotourism income funds health, education and water projects; local employment; cattle breeding help for local farmers", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SUS-08\" data-qid=\"SUS-08\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/circular-economy.html\">The circular economy, biocapacity &amp; ecological footprints</a> &middot; spec 3.6.4.2 &middot; Genn pp. 380-383 &middot; SUS-08</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Explain what a carbon footprint should include when calculated for a car.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Describe what the WWF Living Planet Index measures and how it is used.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[5 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Describe how the Ol Pejeta reserve in Kenya combines commercial cattle ranching with wildlife conservation.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>All greenhouse gases expressed as CO2 equivalent (allowing for different warming strengths)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Direct emissions from fuel combustion plus indirect: manufacture of components, maintenance items, final disposal (life-cycle analysis)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A fair share of the infrastructure: roads, car parks, signals, street lighting</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Compares the populations of over 18 000 populations of 3700 vertebrate species (terrestrial, freshwater, marine) with 1970 levels</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Published in the Living Planet Report as a measure of the state of the planet's ecosystems / biodiversity loss over time</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">5 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Hardy, disease-resistant Boran cattle; regular tick dipping reduces ticks for wild herbivores too</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mobile bomas (night enclosures) protect cattle from lions and leave dung that enriches soil and plant growth</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Electric fences keep game in and poachers out; biological corridors let animals (except rhinos) move to other reserves; problem elephants relocated</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Monitoring: radio-collared lions, drones, camera traps, track surveys; endangered species (Grevy's zebra, northern white rhino) protected</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Community links: ecotourism income funds health, education and water projects; local employment; cattle breeding help for local farmers</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 380-383; AQA spec 3.6.4.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "SUS-09", "sub": "circular-economy", "subname": "The circular economy, biocapacity & ecological footprints", "topic": "Sustainability", "tslug": "sustainability", "papers": [2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.6.4", "pages": "384-389", "url": "subtopic/circular-economy.html#SUS-09", "snippet": "The Isle of Eigg (area 31 km2, population about 100) is not connected to the national grid. Table 1 shows its electricity sources.", "text": "the isle of eigg (area 31 km2, population about 100) is not connected to the national grid. table 1 shows its electricity sources. explain why eigg uses a mix of three renewable resources rather than one, and why diesel back-up is still needed. describe three other ways in which the eigg community has increased its sustainability. explain why small self-contained communities often find it easier to adopt sustainable lifestyles than large cities. explain how large-scale dc electricity grids such as desertec could increase the use of renewable energy. diversity: hep (wet winters), solar (summer) and wind peak at different times so supply is more reliable - diverse systems resist change no single source could meet demand all year; battery storage on the island grid smooths fluctuations calm, dry, dull periods still leave gaps so diesel generators provide security (fossil fuel use minimised, not yet eliminated) community ownership (isle of eigg trust with scottish wildlife trust) managing the island for people and wildlife wood biomass heating; insulation such as straw-bale housing; some electric vehicles habitat protection and creation: fencing rare plants from grazing, planting indigenous woodland (rowan, birch, willow); eagles and hen harriers nesting recyclables shipped off; garden / food waste composted; low-impact food production; beach litter collection small groups can organise, agree and act quickly; decisions benefit people who see the results directly resources and wastes are local and visible so feedback is immediate, unlike cities supplied from far away renewable energy is abundant but geographically and temporally variable; a continental grid moves electricity from areas of surplus (desert solar, offshore wind) to areas of demand dc grids do not need every generator synchronised in phase as ac grids do, so coordination across many countries is easier", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"SUS-09\" data-qid=\"SUS-09\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/circular-economy.html\">The circular economy, biocapacity &amp; ecological footprints</a> &middot; spec 3.6.4 &middot; Genn pp. 384-389 &middot; SUS-09</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>The Isle of Eigg (area 31 km<sup>2</sup>, population about 100) is not connected to the national grid. Table 1 shows its electricity sources.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Source</th><th>Capacity / kW</th></tr><tr><td>Hydroelectric (3 generators)</td><td>110</td></tr><tr><td>Solar PV</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>Wind (4 turbines)</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>Diesel generators (back-up)</td><td>160</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Explain why Eigg uses a mix of three renewable resources rather than one, and why diesel back-up is still needed.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Describe <b>three</b> other ways in which the Eigg community has increased its sustainability.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Explain why small self-contained communities often find it easier to adopt sustainable lifestyles than large cities.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Explain how large-scale DC electricity grids such as Desertec could increase the use of renewable energy.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Diversity: HEP (wet winters), solar (summer) and wind peak at different times so supply is more reliable - diverse systems resist change</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No single source could meet demand all year; battery storage on the island grid smooths fluctuations</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Calm, dry, dull periods still leave gaps so diesel generators provide security (fossil fuel use minimised, not yet eliminated)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Community ownership (Isle of Eigg Trust with Scottish Wildlife Trust) managing the island for people and wildlife</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wood biomass heating; insulation such as straw-bale housing; some electric vehicles</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Habitat protection and creation: fencing rare plants from grazing, planting indigenous woodland (rowan, birch, willow); eagles and hen harriers nesting</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Recyclables shipped off; garden / food waste composted; low-impact food production; beach litter collection</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Small groups can organise, agree and act quickly; decisions benefit people who see the results directly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Resources and wastes are local and visible so feedback is immediate, unlike cities supplied from far away</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Renewable energy is abundant but geographically and temporally variable; a continental grid moves electricity from areas of surplus (desert solar, offshore wind) to areas of demand</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>DC grids do not need every generator synchronised in phase as AC grids do, so coordination across many countries is easier</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 384-389; AQA spec 3.6.4.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-12", "sub": "stats", "subname": "Specialist techniques & statistical analysis", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.7.2.3", "pages": "402-407", "url": "subtopic/stats.html#RM-12", "snippet": "Table 1 lists some techniques used in ecological monitoring.", "text": "table 1 lists some techniques used in ecological monitoring. complete table 1 by giving one application and one limitation of each technique. explain how satellite radar (microwave) sensors are useful for monitoring oil pollution and sea conditions. give two types of indirect evidence of an animal's presence and the information each can give. image databases: identifying individuals (tiger stripes, whale shark spots, dolphin fins) to find territory size, movements, lifespan, social groups - few species have unique recognisable features edna: detecting the presence of a species from dna shed into water (great crested newt, invasive carp) - shows presence not abundance; contamination / dna transport by water geolocator tags: tracking small birds on migration where transmitters are too heavy - only record light and time so position is approximate; animal must be recaptured to download data microwaves emitted by the satellite reflect off the surface giving data on wave height, wind velocity, sea surface altitude and oil slicks (which smooth the surface) microwaves pass through cloud so monitoring is not interrupted by weather droppings: diet, gender, territory size owl pellets: diet feeding marks (chewed hazel nuts - dormouse), tracks / footprints, nests / burrows, scratching posts (territory)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-12\" data-qid=\"RM-12\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/stats.html\">Specialist techniques &amp; statistical analysis</a> &middot; spec 3.7.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 402-407 &middot; RM-12</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 lists some techniques used in ecological monitoring.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Complete Table 1 by giving one application and one limitation of each technique.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Application</th><th>Limitation</th></tr><tr><td>Image databases of unique markings</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>eDNA sampling</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Geolocator tags</td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Explain how satellite radar (microwave) sensors are useful for monitoring oil pollution and sea conditions.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>Give <b>two</b> types of indirect evidence of an animal's presence and the information each can give.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Image databases: identifying individuals (tiger stripes, whale shark spots, dolphin fins) to find territory size, movements, lifespan, social groups - few species have unique recognisable features</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>eDNA: detecting the presence of a species from DNA shed into water (great crested newt, invasive carp) - shows presence not abundance; contamination / DNA transport by water</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Geolocator tags: tracking small birds on migration where transmitters are too heavy - only record light and time so position is approximate; animal must be recaptured to download data</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Microwaves emitted by the satellite reflect off the surface giving data on wave height, wind velocity, sea surface altitude and oil slicks (which smooth the surface)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Microwaves pass through cloud so monitoring is not interrupted by weather</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Droppings: diet, gender, territory size</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Owl pellets: diet</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Feeding marks (chewed hazel nuts - dormouse), tracks / footprints, nests / burrows, scratching posts (territory)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 402-407; AQA spec 3.7.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-13", "sub": "stats", "subname": "Specialist techniques & statistical analysis", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.7.2.3", "pages": "420-423", "url": "subtopic/stats.html#RM-13", "snippet": "Table 1 shows the mean dry mass of wheat plants grown with two fertiliser treatments (10 replicates each).", "text": "table 1 shows the mean dry mass of wheat plants grown with two fertiliser treatments (10 replicates each). explain what the standard deviation values tell you about the data. name the statistical test that should be used to decide whether the difference between the two means is significant, and justify your choice. the test gave a probability p = 0.30. state what this means and what the student should conclude. explain why a statistically significant correlation does not prove a causal relationship. suggest how the student could increase the chance of detecting a real difference if one exists. the scatter / variability of results around each mean; about 68% of values lie within one sd of the mean the sds are large relative to the difference in means (1.9 g) so the ranges overlap greatly t-test comparing two means of measured (continuous) data there is a 30% probability that the difference occurred by random chance above the 0.05 (5%) level so the difference is not significant; the null hypothesis (no difference) is accepted two correlated variables may both be caused by a third variable that was not investigated significance only shows the result is unlikely to be chance; a mechanism / controlled experiment is needed for causation increase the number of replicates (reduces the effect of variability) standardise other variables (soil, water, light) more closely to reduce scatter", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-13\" data-qid=\"RM-13\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/stats.html\">Specialist techniques &amp; statistical analysis</a> &middot; spec 3.7.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 420-423 &middot; RM-13</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>Table 1 shows the mean dry mass of wheat plants grown with two fertiliser treatments (10 replicates each).</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Treatment</th><th>Mean dry mass / g</th><th>Standard deviation / g</th></tr><tr><td>Organic manure</td><td>14.2</td><td>3.9</td></tr><tr><td>Inorganic NPK fertiliser</td><td>16.1</td><td>4.1</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Explain what the standard deviation values tell you about the data.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Name the statistical test that should be used to decide whether the difference between the two means is significant, and justify your choice.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>The test gave a probability p = 0.30. State what this means and what the student should conclude.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Explain why a statistically significant correlation does not prove a causal relationship.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.5</span>Suggest how the student could increase the chance of detecting a real difference if one exists.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The scatter / variability of results around each mean; about 68% of values lie within one SD of the mean</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The SDs are large relative to the difference in means (1.9 g) so the ranges overlap greatly</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>t-test</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Comparing two means of measured (continuous) data</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>There is a 30% probability that the difference occurred by random chance</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Above the 0.05 (5%) level so the difference is not significant; the null hypothesis (no difference) is accepted</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Two correlated variables may both be caused by a third variable that was not investigated</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Significance only shows the result is unlikely to be chance; a mechanism / controlled experiment is needed for causation</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Increase the number of replicates (reduces the effect of variability)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Standardise other variables (soil, water, light) more closely to reduce scatter</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 420-423; AQA spec 3.7.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-14", "sub": "stats", "subname": "Specialist techniques & statistical analysis", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.7.2.3", "pages": "422-423", "url": "subtopic/stats.html#RM-14", "snippet": "For each of the following investigations, name the most appropriate statistical test.", "text": " for each of the following investigations, name the most appropriate statistical test. explain why the chi-squared test cannot be used for data such as wind speed or plant height. a spearman's rank test on 12 pairs of data gave rs = -0.72. the critical value at p = 0.05 for n = 12 is 0.59. explain what can be concluded. explain the purpose of error bars on a graph of mean values. spearman's rank correlation coefficient chi-squared test (frequencies / counts in categories) t-test (two means of measured data) spearman's rank correlation coefficient chi-squared compares frequencies / counts in categories; it cannot be used for continuously variable measured data the value (ignoring sign) exceeds the critical value so the correlation is significant at the 5% level the negative sign shows a negative correlation: one variable decreases as the other increases there is less than a 5% probability the correlation arose by chance; reject the null hypothesis (but correlation is not proof of causation) show the standard deviation / range of the original values around each mean if the bars of two means overlap greatly the difference is probably not significant; little overlap suggests a significant difference", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-14\" data-qid=\"RM-14\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/stats.html\">Specialist techniques &amp; statistical analysis</a> &middot; spec 3.7.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 422-423 &middot; RM-14</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>For each of the following investigations, name the most appropriate statistical test.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain why the chi-squared test cannot be used for data such as wind speed or plant height.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>A Spearman's rank test on 12 pairs of data gave r<sub>s</sub> = -0.72. The critical value at p = 0.05 for n = 12 is 0.59. Explain what can be concluded.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.4</span>Explain the purpose of error bars on a graph of mean values.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Spearman's rank correlation coefficient</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Chi-squared test (frequencies / counts in categories)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>t-test (two means of measured data)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Spearman's rank correlation coefficient</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Chi-squared compares frequencies / counts in categories; it cannot be used for continuously variable measured data</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The value (ignoring sign) exceeds the critical value so the correlation is significant at the 5% level</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The negative sign shows a negative correlation: one variable decreases as the other increases</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>There is less than a 5% probability the correlation arose by chance; reject the null hypothesis (but correlation is not proof of causation)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Show the standard deviation / range of the original values around each mean</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>If the bars of two means overlap greatly the difference is probably not significant; little overlap suggests a significant difference</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 422-423; AQA spec 3.7.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-15", "sub": "stats", "subname": "Specialist techniques & statistical analysis", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.7.2.3", "pages": "404-406", "url": "subtopic/stats.html#RM-15", "snippet": "Describe how DNA databases are used to control the illegal trade in tropical timber and ivory.", "text": " describe how dna databases are used to control the illegal trade in tropical timber and ivory. explain the difference between radio / gps tracking and acoustic tracking and state when each is used. give three types of carrier system used to move sensors to study areas and one advantage of drones for ecological monitoring. explain how motion-sensitive cameras and cctv reduce disturbance to wildlife compared with direct observation. each regional population has its own unique gene pool / dna profile dna from seized timber or ivory is compared with the database of regional populations this identifies where the material came from, showing whether it was legally harvested and where poaching occurs radio / gps transmitters use radio waves - work in air and fresh water, giving positions (gps stores or transmits location) radio waves do not travel through seawater so acoustic transmitters (sound) are used for marine animals, giving real-time positions drones / rovs, auvs (eg autosub), balloons, aircraft, satellites, animals (tags) drones: cheap, low disturbance, high-resolution images of areas hard to reach (cliffs, canopy, ice), repeated surveys; infrared cameras detect animals at night cameras record automatically (visible light by day, infrared at night) without researchers being present so animals behave naturally and nests are not deserted; monitoring continues 24 hours", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-15\" data-qid=\"RM-15\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/stats.html\">Specialist techniques &amp; statistical analysis</a> &middot; spec 3.7.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 404-406 &middot; RM-15</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Describe how DNA databases are used to control the illegal trade in tropical timber and ivory.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>Explain the difference between radio / GPS tracking and acoustic tracking and state when each is used.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Give <b>three</b> types of carrier system used to move sensors to study areas and one advantage of drones for ecological monitoring.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Explain how motion-sensitive cameras and CCTV reduce disturbance to wildlife compared with direct observation.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Each regional population has its own unique gene pool / DNA profile</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>DNA from seized timber or ivory is compared with the database of regional populations</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>This identifies where the material came from, showing whether it was legally harvested and where poaching occurs</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Radio / GPS transmitters use radio waves - work in air and fresh water, giving positions (GPS stores or transmits location)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Radio waves do not travel through seawater so acoustic transmitters (sound) are used for marine animals, giving real-time positions</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Up to 3 marks in total; award marks in proportion to the completeness of each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Drones / ROVs, AUVs (eg Autosub), balloons, aircraft, satellites, animals (tags)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">up to 2</span><span>Drones: cheap, low disturbance, high-resolution images of areas hard to reach (cliffs, canopy, ice), repeated surveys; infrared cameras detect animals at night</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Cameras record automatically (visible light by day, infrared at night) without researchers being present</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So animals behave naturally and nests are not deserted; monitoring continues 24 hours</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 404-406; AQA spec 3.7.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-18", "sub": "stats", "subname": "Specialist techniques & statistical analysis", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 5, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.7.2.3", "pages": "402", "url": "subtopic/stats.html#RM-18", "snippet": "A student sampled the ground-flora of two woodlands. Table 1 shows the results for woodland A.", "text": "a student sampled the ground-flora of two woodlands. table 1 shows the results for woodland a. use the formula d = n(n - 1) / σn(n - 1) to calculate simpson's index of diversity for woodland a. woodland b had an index of 1.6. state what this shows and give one limitation of using the index alone to compare the woodlands. σn(n - 1) = (40 x 39) + (30 x 29) + (20 x 19) + (10 x 9) = 1560 + 870 + 380 + 90 = 2900 n(n - 1) = 100 x 99 = 9900 d = 9900 / 2900 = 3.41 (accept 3.4) woodland a has a higher biodiversity (greater variety and more even abundance) than woodland b the index does not identify which species are present / a rare or important species may be missed; depends on identification skill and sampling method", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-18\" data-qid=\"RM-18\" data-marks=\"5\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">5 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/stats.html\">Specialist techniques &amp; statistical analysis</a> &middot; spec 3.7.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 402 &middot; RM-18</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A student sampled the ground-flora of two woodlands. Table 1 shows the results for woodland A.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Species</th><th>Number of individuals (n)</th></tr><tr><td>Bluebell</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td>Wood anemone</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>Dog's mercury</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>Bramble</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>Total (N)</td><td>100</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Use the formula <i>D</i> = <i>N</i>(<i>N</i> - 1) / &Sigma;<i>n</i>(<i>n</i> - 1) to calculate Simpson's index of diversity for woodland A.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Woodland B had an index of 1.6. State what this shows and give <b>one</b> limitation of using the index alone to compare the woodlands.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>&Sigma;n(n - 1) = (40 x 39) + (30 x 29) + (20 x 19) + (10 x 9) = 1560 + 870 + 380 + 90 = 2900</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>N(N - 1) = 100 x 99 = 9900</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>D = 9900 / 2900 = 3.41 (accept 3.4)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Woodland A has a higher biodiversity (greater variety and more even abundance) than woodland B</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The index does not identify which species are present / a rare or important species may be missed; depends on identification skill and sampling method</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 402; AQA spec 3.7.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-19", "sub": "stats", "subname": "Specialist techniques & statistical analysis", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data"], "spec": "3.7.2.3", "pages": "420-423", "url": "subtopic/stats.html#RM-19", "snippet": "A student measured the wind speed at 10 sites inside a woodland and at 10 sites in a nearby clearing.", "text": "a student measured the wind speed at 10 sites inside a woodland and at 10 sites in a nearby clearing. name the statistical test the student should use to find out whether the difference between the means is significant. give two reasons for your choice. explain what the student should do if it is not possible to show that the data are normally distributed. the test gave p = 0.02. explain what this means. explain how the standard deviations in table 1 support the conclusion that the means are different. explain why a statistically significant result does not prove that trees cause the lower wind speed. t-test the data are measured (continuous) values and two means are being compared the data are (assumed to be) normally distributed use the mann-whitney u test instead (compares medians) there is a 2% probability that the difference occurred by random chance as this is less than 0.05 the difference is statistically significant / the null hypothesis is rejected the ranges of values (mean plus or minus sd) do not overlap: woodland up to 1.6 and clearing down to 2.4 about 68% of values lie within one sd of each mean so the two data sets are clearly separated significance shows the difference is unlikely to be due to chance, not that a causal relationship exists another variable (eg topography / aspect of the sites) could cause both; a controlled experiment or further evidence is needed", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-19\" data-qid=\"RM-19\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/stats.html\">Specialist techniques &amp; statistical analysis</a> &middot; spec 3.7.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 420-423 &middot; RM-19</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A student measured the wind speed at 10 sites inside a woodland and at 10 sites in a nearby clearing.</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Location</th><th>Mean wind speed / m s<sup>-1</sup></th><th>Standard deviation / m s<sup>-1</sup></th></tr><tr><td>Woodland</td><td>1.2</td><td>0.4</td></tr><tr><td>Clearing</td><td>3.5</td><td>1.1</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Name the statistical test the student should use to find out whether the difference between the means is significant. Give <b>two</b> reasons for your choice.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain what the student should do if it is not possible to show that the data are normally distributed.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>The test gave p = 0.02. Explain what this means.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Explain how the standard deviations in Table 1 support the conclusion that the means are different.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.5</span>Explain why a statistically significant result does not prove that trees cause the lower wind speed.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>t-test</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The data are measured (continuous) values and two means are being compared</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The data are (assumed to be) normally distributed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Use the Mann-Whitney U test instead (compares medians)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>There is a 2% probability that the difference occurred by random chance</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>As this is less than 0.05 the difference is statistically significant / the null hypothesis is rejected</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The ranges of values (mean plus or minus SD) do not overlap: woodland up to 1.6 and clearing down to 2.4</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>About 68% of values lie within one SD of each mean so the two data sets are clearly separated</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Significance shows the difference is unlikely to be due to chance, not that a causal relationship exists</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Another variable (eg topography / aspect of the sites) could cause both; a controlled experiment or further evidence is needed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 420-423; AQA spec 3.7.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-20", "sub": "stats", "subname": "Specialist techniques & statistical analysis", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.7.2.3", "pages": "422-423", "url": "subtopic/stats.html#RM-20", "snippet": "Complete Table 1 by naming the most suitable statistical test for each hypothesis.", "text": " complete table 1 by naming the most suitable statistical test for each hypothesis. explain why the chi-squared test cannot be used to compare the dissolved oxygen concentrations of two rivers. explain why a scientific investigation never 'proves' a hypothesis. describe how error bars are added to a graph of means and how they are interpreted. spearman's rank correlation coefficient mann-whitney u test chi-squared test t-test chi-squared compares frequencies / counts in categories dissolved oxygen is continuously variable measured data, so a t-test or mann-whitney u test is needed there is always some remaining uncertainty / a probability that the result was produced by chance statistics only give a level of confidence (eg 95%, 99%) that the result is significant bars drawn above and below each mean showing plus and minus one standard deviation if the bars of two means overlap a lot the difference is probably not significant; if they do not overlap it probably is", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-20\" data-qid=\"RM-20\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/stats.html\">Specialist techniques &amp; statistical analysis</a> &middot; spec 3.7.2.3 &middot; Genn pp. 422-423 &middot; RM-20</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Complete Table 1 by naming the most suitable statistical test for each hypothesis.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Hypothesis</th><th>Statistical test</th></tr><tr><td>Noise levels decrease as distance from a road increases</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Fewer cars use a road after road charging is introduced (counts on 12 days before and after)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Woodlands managed in different ways have different numbers of dormice</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Mean lobster mass is higher in areas where collection is banned</td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Explain why the chi-squared test cannot be used to compare the dissolved oxygen concentrations of two rivers.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Explain why a scientific investigation never 'proves' a hypothesis.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>Describe how error bars are added to a graph of means and how they are interpreted.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Spearman's rank correlation coefficient</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Mann-Whitney U test</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Chi-squared test</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>t-test</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Chi-squared compares frequencies / counts in categories</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Dissolved oxygen is continuously variable measured data, so a t-test or Mann-Whitney U test is needed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>There is always some remaining uncertainty / a probability that the result was produced by chance</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Statistics only give a level of confidence (eg 95%, 99%) that the result is significant</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Bars drawn above and below each mean showing plus and minus one standard deviation</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>If the bars of two means overlap a lot the difference is probably not significant; if they do not overlap it probably is</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 422-423; AQA spec 3.7.2.3.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-05", "sub": "sampling", "subname": "Sampling techniques & measuring abiotic factors", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc", "data"], "spec": "3.7.2.1", "pages": "394-395, 401-402", "url": "subtopic/sampling.html#RM-05", "snippet": "A student used 0.5 m x 0.5 m grid quadrats to survey ground flora in two meadows, one grazed and one ungrazed. Table 1 shows the results for the grazed meadow (10 quadrats).", "text": "a student used 0.5 m x 0.5 m grid quadrats to survey ground flora in two meadows, one grazed and one ungrazed. table 1 shows the results for the grazed meadow (10 quadrats). explain why a 0.5 m x 0.5 m quadrat was chosen rather than a 10 cm x 10 cm or 100 m x 100 m quadrat. calculate simpson's index of biodiversity (d) for the grazed meadow using d = n(n-1) / σn(n-1). show your working. the ungrazed meadow had d = 1.8 with the same number of species. explain what this shows. explain why a point quadrat would be better than a frame quadrat in tall vegetation. give one limitation of using the dafor abundance scale. quadrat size depends on the size / spacing of the organisms: 0.5 m x 0.5 m is typical for ground flora 10 cm x 10 cm is for lichens (too small to contain a representative sample of plants); 100 m x 100 m is for trees (impractical, too much work) n = 240; n(n-1) = 240 x 239 = 57 360 σn(n-1) = 48x47 + 120x119 + 36x35 + 24x23 + 12x11 = 2256 + 14280 + 1260 + 552 + 132 = 18 480 d = 57 360 / 18 480 = 3.10 (accept 3.1) lower d means lower biodiversity: the ungrazed meadow is dominated by one or a few species (eg tall grass) with the others rare (less even) grazing (plagioclimax management) prevents dominance by vigorous species so abundance is more evenly spread a frame quadrat flattens tall plants, making percentage cover estimates inaccurate and hiding shorter plants point quadrat pins are lowered through the vegetation; each touch counts (10 pins = 10% each) without flattening subjective judgement varies between recorders; results are not quantitative so statistical analysis is not possible (but quick for many areas)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-05\" data-qid=\"RM-05\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc data\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 1</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/sampling.html\">Sampling techniques &amp; measuring abiotic factors</a> &middot; spec 3.7.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 394-395, 401-402 &middot; RM-05</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A student used 0.5 m x 0.5 m grid quadrats to survey ground flora in two meadows, one grazed and one ungrazed. Table 1 shows the results for the grazed meadow (10 quadrats).</p>\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Species</th><th>Number of individuals</th></tr><tr><td>Ribwort plantain</td><td>48</td></tr><tr><td>Perennial ryegrass</td><td>120</td></tr><tr><td>White clover</td><td>36</td></tr><tr><td>Daisy</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>Creeping buttercup</td><td>12</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.1</span>Explain why a 0.5 m x 0.5 m quadrat was chosen rather than a 10 cm x 10 cm or 100 m x 100 m quadrat.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.2</span>Calculate Simpson's Index of Biodiversity (D) for the grazed meadow using D = N(N-1) / &Sigma;n(n-1). Show your working.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.3</span>The ungrazed meadow had D = 1.8 with the same number of species. Explain what this shows.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.4</span>Explain why a point quadrat would be better than a frame quadrat in tall vegetation.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[1 mark]</span><span class=\"pn\">01.5</span>Give <b>one</b> limitation of using the DAFOR abundance scale.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Quadrat size depends on the size / spacing of the organisms: 0.5 m x 0.5 m is typical for ground flora</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>10 cm x 10 cm is for lichens (too small to contain a representative sample of plants); 100 m x 100 m is for trees (impractical, too much work)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>N = 240; N(N-1) = 240 x 239 = 57 360</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>&Sigma;n(n-1) = 48x47 + 120x119 + 36x35 + 24x23 + 12x11 = 2256 + 14280 + 1260 + 552 + 132 = 18 480</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>D = 57 360 / 18 480 = 3.10 (accept 3.1)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Lower D means lower biodiversity: the ungrazed meadow is dominated by one or a few species (eg tall grass) with the others rare (less even)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Grazing (plagioclimax management) prevents dominance by vigorous species so abundance is more evenly spread</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A frame quadrat flattens tall plants, making percentage cover estimates inaccurate and hiding shorter plants</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Point quadrat pins are lowered through the vegetation; each touch counts (10 pins = 10% each) without flattening</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>01.5</b> <span class=\"mtot\">1 mark</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Subjective judgement varies between recorders; results are not quantitative so statistical analysis is not possible (but quick for many areas)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 394-395, 401-402; AQA spec 3.7.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-06", "sub": "sampling", "subname": "Sampling techniques & measuring abiotic factors", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.7.2.1", "pages": "395-396, 415-416", "url": "subtopic/sampling.html#RM-06", "snippet": "A student compared the invertebrates in a stream above and below a sewage outfall using kick sampling.", "text": "a student compared the invertebrates in a stream above and below a sewage outfall using kick sampling. describe how kick sampling is carried out so that the samples from the two sites are comparable. give three limitations of kick sampling. explain how a surber sampler improves on kick sampling. explain why the invertebrates found give a better indication of long-term sewage pollution than a single measurement of dissolved oxygen. net held with its straight edge on the river bed facing the current (open to the flow) the bed in front is disturbed by kicking / scraping a boot for a standard number of kicks and time (eg 30 s) same substrate type, water depth and flow rate at both sites; same net mesh; repeat samples; invertebrates identified with a key and counted difficult to standardise the vigour / area kicked buried organisms or those fixed to stones are not dislodged some organisms swim and avoid the net flow rate varies and affects capture fragile organisms destroyed a fixed area of bed is enclosed by a frame so the sampled area is standardised; side nets reduce escapes but only works in fast enough flow and is hard to set on rough substrates / deep water pollutant / oxygen levels fluctuate (eg with flow, time of day) so one reading may miss a pollution event sensitive taxa (stonefly, mayfly larvae) disappear and tolerant ones (midge larvae, hoglouse) remain, integrating conditions over weeks / months (biotic index)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-06\" data-qid=\"RM-06\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 2</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/sampling.html\">Sampling techniques &amp; measuring abiotic factors</a> &middot; spec 3.7.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 395-396, 415-416 &middot; RM-06</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A student compared the invertebrates in a stream above and below a sewage outfall using kick sampling.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.1</span>Describe how kick sampling is carried out so that the samples from the two sites are comparable.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.2</span>Give <b>three</b> limitations of kick sampling.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.3</span>Explain how a Surber sampler improves on kick sampling.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">02.4</span>Explain why the invertebrates found give a better indication of long-term sewage pollution than a single measurement of dissolved oxygen.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Net held with its straight edge on the river bed facing the current (open to the flow)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The bed in front is disturbed by kicking / scraping a boot for a standard number of kicks and time (eg 30 s)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Same substrate type, water depth and flow rate at both sites; same net mesh; repeat samples; invertebrates identified with a key and counted</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 3 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 3.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Difficult to standardise the vigour / area kicked</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Buried organisms or those fixed to stones are not dislodged</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Some organisms swim and avoid the net</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Flow rate varies and affects capture</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fragile organisms destroyed</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A fixed area of bed is enclosed by a frame so the sampled area is standardised; side nets reduce escapes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>But only works in fast enough flow and is hard to set on rough substrates / deep water</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>02.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Pollutant / oxygen levels fluctuate (eg with flow, time of day) so one reading may miss a pollution event</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sensitive taxa (stonefly, mayfly larvae) disappear and tolerant ones (midge larvae, hoglouse) remain, integrating conditions over weeks / months (biotic index)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 395-396, 415-416; AQA spec 3.7.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-07", "sub": "sampling", "subname": "Sampling techniques & measuring abiotic factors", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["data", "table"], "spec": "3.7.2.1", "pages": "397-399", "url": "subtopic/sampling.html#RM-07", "snippet": "Complete Table 1 to show a suitable sampling technique for each group of organisms and one limitation of the technique.", "text": " complete table 1 to show a suitable sampling technique for each group of organisms and one limitation of the technique. explain why a light trap cannot be used to prove that a moth species is absent from a site. explain how a motor-driven suction sampler can give a population density, whereas a pooter cannot. ground surface: pitfall trap (container sunk level with the surface, cover, checked after 24 h) - only mobile surface animals; trapped predators eat others; preservative attracts / repels; escapes leaf litter: tullgren funnel (sample on mesh under a light; heat / light drive invertebrates down into a container for a standard time) - only species repelled by heat / light; some die or stop moving; large ones cannot pass the mesh branches: beating tray (branch beaten a set number of times over a sheet) - hard to standardise vigour; some species not dislodged / fly away; high branches unreachable moths only fly as adults for part of the year and activity depends on weather / season, so a species may be present but not caught brightness / wavelength of the lamp affects attractiveness so catches vary between traps the area of ground covered by the suction pipe is known so invertebrates per m2 can be calculated a pooter is only a handling / collection device for individuals already found, not a sampling method", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-07\" data-qid=\"RM-07\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"data table\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 3</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/sampling.html\">Sampling techniques &amp; measuring abiotic factors</a> &middot; spec 3.7.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 397-399 &middot; RM-07</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[6 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.1</span>Complete Table 1 to show a suitable sampling technique for each group of organisms and one limitation of the technique.\n<div class=\"cap\">Table 1</div><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Organisms</th><th>Technique</th><th>One limitation</th></tr><tr><td>Invertebrates active on the ground surface at night</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Invertebrates living in leaf litter</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Invertebrates on tree branches</td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.2</span>Explain why a light trap cannot be used to prove that a moth species is absent from a site.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">03.3</span>Explain how a motor-driven suction sampler can give a population density, whereas a pooter cannot.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">6 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">2 marks for each point (partial credit for a partly correct point).</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Ground surface: pitfall trap (container sunk level with the surface, cover, checked after 24 h) - only mobile surface animals; trapped predators eat others; preservative attracts / repels; escapes</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Leaf litter: Tullgren funnel (sample on mesh under a light; heat / light drive invertebrates down into a container for a standard time) - only species repelled by heat / light; some die or stop moving; large ones cannot pass the mesh</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">2</span><span>Branches: beating tray (branch beaten a set number of times over a sheet) - hard to standardise vigour; some species not dislodged / fly away; high branches unreachable</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Moths only fly as adults for part of the year and activity depends on weather / season, so a species may be present but not caught</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Brightness / wavelength of the lamp affects attractiveness so catches vary between traps</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>03.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>The area of ground covered by the suction pipe is known so invertebrates per m<sup>2</sup> can be calculated</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A pooter is only a handling / collection device for individuals already found, not a sampling method</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 397-399; AQA spec 3.7.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-08", "sub": "sampling", "subname": "Sampling techniques & measuring abiotic factors", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": ["calc"], "spec": "3.7.2.1", "pages": "401-402", "url": "subtopic/sampling.html#RM-08", "snippet": "A student estimated the population of ground beetles in a field using the Lincoln Index. 60 beetles were caught in pitfall traps, marked with a spot of paint and released. Three da", "text": "a student estimated the population of ground beetles in a field using the lincoln index. 60 beetles were caught in pitfall traps, marked with a spot of paint and released. three days later 45 beetles were caught, of which 9 were marked. calculate the estimated population size. state four assumptions of the lincoln index method. explain how the estimate would be affected if the paint made the beetles more likely to be eaten by birds. suggest why the second sample was taken after three days rather than the next day or after a month. (60 x 45) / 9 = 300 marked individuals mix freely / randomly with the rest of the population no births, deaths, immigration or emigration between samples marking does not affect survival or behaviour (eg make them more visible to predators) marks do not wear off / fall off all individuals have the same chance of being caught (traps do not attract or repel marked animals) fewer marked beetles would survive to be recaptured so the proportion marked in the second sample would be too low and the population would be overestimated enough time for marked beetles to mix with the population not so long that births / deaths / migration or loss of marks change the population", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-08\" data-qid=\"RM-08\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"calc\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 4</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/sampling.html\">Sampling techniques &amp; measuring abiotic factors</a> &middot; spec 3.7.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 401-402 &middot; RM-08</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A student estimated the population of ground beetles in a field using the Lincoln Index. 60 beetles were caught in pitfall traps, marked with a spot of paint and released. Three days later 45 beetles were caught, of which 9 were marked.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.1</span>Calculate the estimated population size.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.2</span>State <b>four</b> assumptions of the Lincoln Index method.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.3</span>Explain how the estimate would be affected if the paint made the beetles more likely to be eaten by birds.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">04.4</span>Suggest why the second sample was taken after three days rather than the next day or after a month.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">A correct final answer (with unit where asked) scores full marks. If the answer is wrong, award 1 mark for each correct step of working shown.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>(60 x 45) / 9</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>= 300</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 4 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 4.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Marked individuals mix freely / randomly with the rest of the population</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No births, deaths, immigration or emigration between samples</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Marking does not affect survival or behaviour (eg make them more visible to predators)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Marks do not wear off / fall off</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>All individuals have the same chance of being caught (traps do not attract or repel marked animals)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Fewer marked beetles would survive to be recaptured</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So the proportion marked in the second sample would be too low and the population would be overestimated</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>04.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Enough time for marked beetles to mix with the population</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Not so long that births / deaths / migration or loss of marks change the population</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 401-402; AQA spec 3.7.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-09", "sub": "sampling", "subname": "Sampling techniques & measuring abiotic factors", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.7.2.1", "pages": "407-410, 413", "url": "subtopic/sampling.html#RM-09", "snippet": "Explain why electronic meters must be calibrated before use and describe how a pH meter is calibrated.", "text": " explain why electronic meters must be calibrated before use and describe how a ph meter is calibrated. describe how wind velocity measurements with an anemometer should be standardised. describe how a whirling hygrometer is used to measure humidity. describe two methods of measuring nitrate concentration in water. readings drift so they may not be accurate; calibration adjusts the meter to match a known standard so results are accurate and comparable between meters / occasions ph meter placed in buffer solutions of known ph (eg 4, 7, 10) and adjusted to read correctly if a meter cannot be adjusted, record the percentage error and correct the results same height above the ground no obstacles to air flow; impeller type facing into the wind axis of rotation horizontal (impeller) or vertical (cup); same time period / averaging two thermometers, one with a wet bulb, are spun until the wet bulb stops cooling evaporation cools the wet bulb more when humidity is low; the temperature difference is compared with a table to find humidity test strips with reagent bands compared with a colour chart (semi-quantitative) electronic colourimeter: reagent added and colour intensity measured at a specific wavelength ion-selective electrode measuring electrode potential (other ions can interfere)", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-09\" data-qid=\"RM-09\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 5</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/sampling.html\">Sampling techniques &amp; measuring abiotic factors</a> &middot; spec 3.7.2.1 &middot; Genn pp. 407-410, 413 &middot; RM-09</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.1</span>Explain why electronic meters must be calibrated before use and describe how a pH meter is calibrated.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.2</span>Describe how wind velocity measurements with an anemometer should be standardised.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.3</span>Describe how a whirling hygrometer is used to measure humidity.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">05.4</span>Describe <b>two</b> methods of measuring nitrate concentration in water.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Readings drift so they may not be accurate; calibration adjusts the meter to match a known standard so results are accurate and comparable between meters / occasions</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>pH meter placed in buffer solutions of known pH (eg 4, 7, 10) and adjusted to read correctly</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>If a meter cannot be adjusted, record the percentage error and correct the results</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Same height above the ground</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>No obstacles to air flow; impeller type facing into the wind</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Axis of rotation horizontal (impeller) or vertical (cup); same time period / averaging</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Two thermometers, one with a wet bulb, are spun until the wet bulb stops cooling</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Evaporation cools the wet bulb more when humidity is low; the temperature difference is compared with a table to find humidity</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>05.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">Any 2 from the points below - 1 mark each, maximum 2.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Test strips with reagent bands compared with a colour chart (semi-quantitative)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Electronic colourimeter: reagent added and colour intensity measured at a specific wavelength</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Ion-selective electrode measuring electrode potential (other ions can interfere)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 407-410, 413; AQA spec 3.7.2.1.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-10", "sub": "sampling", "subname": "Sampling techniques & measuring abiotic factors", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.7.2.1-3.7.2.2", "pages": "410-413, 417", "url": "subtopic/sampling.html#RM-10", "snippet": "Describe how soil texture is determined using a stack of sieves.", "text": " describe how soil texture is determined using a stack of sieves. explain why a soil auger gives a more representative sample than a trowel. explain why the organic matter content will be overestimated if a soil sample is heated above 550 °c. describe how a turbidimeter measures water turbidity and explain how quantitative results are obtained. sieves assembled with the coarsest mesh on top; a dried, crushed soil sample is placed in the top sieve stack shaken (about 2 minutes) until no more particles pass; each fraction weighed percentages of sand (0.02-2 mm), silt (0.002-0.02 mm) and clay (base pan, below 0.002 mm) calculated; material over 2 mm (stones, twigs) is not soil; plotted on a soil triangle auger collects a core of known cross-sectional area and depth so samples from different sites are directly comparable (also needed for bulk density = dry mass / volume) minerals such as calcium carbonate break down releasing carbon dioxide so mass is lost that is not organic matter a beam of light passes through the sample in a dark chamber; a light sensor at 90° measures light scattered by suspended particles - more turbid, more scatter standardise bulb brightness / colour, distance and container a calibration curve is produced using samples with known amounts of suspended matter so readings convert to concentrations; secchi discs / turbidity bottles give only semi-quantitative results", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-10\" data-qid=\"RM-10\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 6</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/sampling.html\">Sampling techniques &amp; measuring abiotic factors</a> &middot; spec 3.7.2.1-3.7.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 410-413, 417 &middot; RM-10</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.1</span>Describe how soil texture is determined using a stack of sieves.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.2</span>Explain why a soil auger gives a more representative sample than a trowel.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.3</span>Explain why the organic matter content will be overestimated if a soil sample is heated above 550 &deg;C.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[3 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">06.4</span>Describe how a turbidimeter measures water turbidity and explain how quantitative results are obtained.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Sieves assembled with the coarsest mesh on top; a dried, crushed soil sample is placed in the top sieve</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Stack shaken (about 2 minutes) until no more particles pass; each fraction weighed</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Percentages of sand (0.02-2 mm), silt (0.002-0.02 mm) and clay (base pan, below 0.002 mm) calculated; material over 2 mm (stones, twigs) is not soil; plotted on a soil triangle</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Auger collects a core of known cross-sectional area and depth</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So samples from different sites are directly comparable (also needed for bulk density = dry mass / volume)</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Minerals such as calcium carbonate break down releasing carbon dioxide</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>So mass is lost that is not organic matter</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>06.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">3 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A beam of light passes through the sample in a dark chamber; a light sensor at 90&deg; measures light scattered by suspended particles - more turbid, more scatter</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Standardise bulb brightness / colour, distance and container</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>A calibration curve is produced using samples with known amounts of suspended matter so readings convert to concentrations; Secchi discs / turbidity bottles give only semi-quantitative results</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 410-413, 417; AQA spec 3.7.2.1-3.7.2.2.</div></details></div>"}, {"id": "RM-11", "sub": "sampling", "subname": "Sampling techniques & measuring abiotic factors", "topic": "Research methods", "tslug": "research", "papers": [1, 2], "marks": 10, "flags": [], "spec": "3.7.2.2", "pages": "416-418", "url": "subtopic/sampling.html#RM-11", "snippet": "A student investigated the effect of pH on the germination of cress seeds.", "text": "a student investigated the effect of ph on the germination of cress seeds. explain why cress is a suitable species for this investigation but the results may not apply directly to natural ecosystems. describe how the student should decide the range of ph values to test. give three variables that should be standardised and explain how long the experiment should continue. the student wants to compare the effect of nutrient concentration on the growth of duckweed. explain why leaf area is a better measure of growth than fresh mass. small seeds, genetically uniform, high and rapid germination rate so results are quick and reliable wild species have different ranges of tolerance so cress results cannot be applied directly preliminary study with widely spaced ph values to find where germination occurs final range from below the lowest ph giving germination to above the highest, with closer intervals temperature (constant, natural germination temperature) light, water volume, number of seeds per dish, seed source growth medium (filter paper; soil would alter ph); nutrients not needed for germination continue until it is clear that ungerminated seeds will not germinate, so that a delay in germination is not mistaken for failure surface water affects fresh mass and drying damages the plants; dry mass requires killing the plants so the same plants cannot be re-measured leaf area is a reasonable estimate of biomass that allows repeated measurement of the same plants over time", "html": "<div class=\"q\" id=\"RM-11\" data-qid=\"RM-11\" data-marks=\"10\" data-flags=\"\"><div class=\"qh\"><span class=\"num\">Question 7</span><span class=\"meta\">10 marks &middot; <a href=\"subtopic/sampling.html\">Sampling techniques &amp; measuring abiotic factors</a> &middot; spec 3.7.2.2 &middot; Genn pp. 416-418 &middot; RM-11</span><span class=\"st noprint\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"lnk\" data-copy title=\"Copy a link to this question\">&#128279;</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"1\" title=\"Attempted but needs more work\">Needs work</button><button type=\"button\" data-s=\"2\" title=\"Confident with this question\">Secure</button></span></div>\n<p>A student investigated the effect of pH on the germination of cress seeds.</p>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.1</span>Explain why cress is a suitable species for this investigation but the results may not apply directly to natural ecosystems.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.2</span>Describe how the student should decide the range of pH values to test.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[4 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.3</span>Give <b>three</b> variables that should be standardised and explain how long the experiment should continue.\n</div>\n<div class=\"part\"><span class=\"marks\">[2 marks]</span><span class=\"pn\">07.4</span>The student wants to compare the effect of nutrient concentration on the growth of duckweed. Explain why leaf area is a better measure of growth than fresh mass.\n</div>\n<details class=\"ms\"><summary>Show mark scheme</summary>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.1</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Small seeds, genetically uniform, high and rapid germination rate so results are quick and reliable</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Wild species have different ranges of tolerance so cress results cannot be applied directly</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.2</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Preliminary study with widely spaced pH values to find where germination occurs</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Final range from below the lowest pH giving germination to above the highest, with closer intervals</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.3</b> <span class=\"mtot\">4 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Temperature (constant, natural germination temperature)</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Light, water volume, number of seeds per dish, seed source</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Growth medium (filter paper; soil would alter pH); nutrients not needed for germination</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Continue until it is clear that ungerminated seeds will not germinate, so that a delay in germination is not mistaken for failure</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mspart\"><div class=\"msh\"><b>07.4</b> <span class=\"mtot\">2 marks</span></div>\n<div class=\"lvl\">1 mark for each point.</div><ul class=\"pts\"><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Surface water affects fresh mass and drying damages the plants; dry mass requires killing the plants so the same plants cannot be re-measured</span></li><li><span class=\"mk\">1</span><span>Leaf area is a reasonable estimate of biomass that allows repeated measurement of the same plants over time</span></li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"src\">Source: R. Genn, Environmental Science A level AQA (Insight &amp; Perspective, 2018), pp. 416-418; AQA spec 3.7.2.2.</div></details></div>"}];
